            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-fantasy-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Fantasy · 02/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPagaeah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the outer scene first: who is telling, who is listening, where the telling is happening, and what immediate need the telling serves. The teller may want time, money, shelter, permission, cooperation, or a change in someone's decision. Keep that need present during the embedded story. The tale is not an ornament; it is an action taken under pressure.\n\nWhen the teller introduces the fantastic premise of the embedded story, give it an ordinary consequence that reaches the teller's present situation. A city that rearranges its streets overnight should create delivery disputes, missed appointments, and a municipal office that sells yesterday's maps. A speaking animal should have opinions about its food and a position on the household budget. Let the large claim produce small logistical problems, and let those problems interrupt or redirect the telling.\n\nUse the listeners' reactions to shape the nested account. A listener may interrupt to ask how a character paid for something, where a door led, or why a witness did not simply leave. The teller can answer, revise, or become distracted by the practical difficulty the question raises. These interruptions are not proof that the tale is false; they are moments when the outer scene's needs press against the inner story's claims.\n\nRepeat a phrase, object, or gesture across the narrative levels so that its meaning changes with use. The same phrase may begin as a formal opening, become a bargaining chip, and later mark a debt the teller has incurred. Do not make the repetition certify truth. Let it show how the telling has altered the relationship between teller and listener.\n\nAllow the embedded story to delay or redirect the outer problem without resolving it completely. The teller may gain a meal, a night's shelter, or a listener's hesitation. The outer situation can continue after the tale ends, with the listeners acting on what the tale changed in them. Preserve the fantasy premise's intelligible consequences throughout, but do not require every nested account to close with a magical bargain or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modernist, absurdist, or satirical construction route. Treat the fantastic premise as a rule, institution, or procedure that people must administer, comply with, or work around. Do not begin with wonder as an emotional state. Begin with the premise's practical requirements: forms, schedules, measurements, permissions, or maintenance.\n\nLet the narration maintain a dry, precise distance from the absurdity. State the procedure's steps in order, as if they were ordinary, and let the discrepancy between the procedure's language and its actual effects carry the satire. The narrator may describe a catastrophic consequence in the same tone as a filing error. Do not translate this distance into a single national temperament or bureaucratic stereotype; let it follow the particular teller and situation.\n\nExpose the absurd consequences through implementation. A rule that seems reasonable in the abstract should create specific problems for the people who must follow it. Show the forms, queues, exceptions, and workarounds. Different characters should respond differently: one may obey scrupulously, another may improvise, a third may treat the whole arrangement as normal because they have never known anything else.\n\nAllow digressions to arise from the current problem and return to it. A digression about the history of a permit, the design of a waiting room, or the career of a minor official should eventually supply a detail that matters to the scene. Do not let the digression become a universal joke about all institutions. Keep it tied to the specific fantastic premise and the specific people affected by it.\n\nGive the fantasy premise intelligible consequences that accumulate. The procedure may produce a result no one intended, and that result may become the next problem. Wonder can appear when a character notices the gap between what the procedure claims and what it has done, but the noticing is not the end of the scene. The character must then decide what to do with the consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the scene move between two scales: the inherited authority's large claims and the decaying material conditions in which that authority actually operates. A lineage may claim dominion over a district while its members argue about a broken lift, a leaking roof, or a ritual that requires a permit no one can find. Keep the collision concrete. The authority is not only menacing; it is also inconvenient, underfunded, and staffed by people with private reasons to stay.\n\nGive the fantastic premise a practical cost. If the old power works, it works through procedures, maintenance, and negotiation. Show the physical detail of those procedures: the worn key, the outdated register, the room that must be prepared before a rite can begin. Let the absurdity arise from the gap between what the authority claims and what its machinery can actually do.\n\nUse intimate relationships to carry the political atmosphere. A character may obey an old rule to protect someone, break it to keep a promise, or pretend compliance while arranging an exit. Private desire and social masks should matter in the same scene as the absurd procedure. When a mask falls away in trusted company, the moment should be shaped by the specific relationship, not by a compulsory confession or betrayal.\n\nLet different characters respond differently to the same old authority. One may treat its rituals as sacred, another as a scheduling problem, a third as a source of income. Do not make everyone equally witty or equally reverent. The humor belongs to the situation and to particular characters, not to a universal narrator.\n\nEnd the scene where the practical and intimate pressures intersect, without requiring a completed escape, a revealed conspiracy, or a moral resolution. The character may still be waiting for a form, avoiding a relative, or deciding whether to attend a rite. The fantasy premise's consequences remain intelligible, and the atmosphere remains personal rather than decorative.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Establish a teller, an audience, and an occasion that matters to both. The teller may be recounting a family episode, a community dispute, a journey, or a promise. Keep the telling situated in a specific place, language, and community rather than a generic family or village.\n\nLet the fantastic premise enter through the tale's practical consequences. A wonder should affect food, rent, marriage arrangements, travel, work, or inheritance. Do not treat the premise as a distant marvel. Show how it changes what people must do next. The Adams profile's scale collision appears when the tale's large claims meet the teller's small logistical problems: a miraculous object must be carried, stored, divided, or explained to a neighbor.\n\nAllow the audience to interrupt, question, or correct the teller. These interruptions should arise from the audience's own social and domestic concerns. A listener may ask who paid for something, what a relative said, or why a journey took so long. The teller's answers can expose further absurd consequences of the premise. Keep the interruptions tied to the current problem rather than turning every listener into the same comic voice.\n\nUse repetition to connect the frame and the embedded tale. A phrase, object, or obligation may appear in the outer scene and then accrue meaning inside the tale. The repetition should show how the telling changes the listeners' understanding of their own situation. Do not make the repetition certify that the tale is true.\n\nLet the telling end with a practical consequence for the outer scene. The audience may change a decision, offer help, refuse a request, or simply understand a problem differently. The embedded tale need not resolve completely. Preserve the fantasy premise's intelligible consequences and the specific social texture of the teller's world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the early-modern comic/theatrical telling route. Stage the scene as a direct, energetic performance: the narrator announces entrances, shifts location clearly, and lets characters react aloud to the fantastic premise. Keep the telling immediate and audience-facing rather than buried in reflective interiority.\n\nIntroduce the fantastic premise through its practical requirements. A wonder should need preparation, transport, payment, or explanation. Let the narrator describe these requirements with comic precision. The Adams profile's scale collision appears when a character makes a grand claim and then must deal with a small, concrete obstacle: the wrong key, the missing ingredient, the door that opens onto the wrong place.\n\nLet characters respond differently to the absurdity. One may shout, another may bargain, a third may treat the whole matter as an ordinary nuisance. Do not make every character equally witty. The humor should come from the specific situation and the specific person's reaction. Direct feeling is available where appropriate, but it belongs to the character, not to a universal comic narrator.\n\nUse spatial changes and entrances to keep the scene readable. When a character arrives, say where they are and what they are carrying. When the scene moves, mark the move clearly. The theatrical route favors visible action and spoken reaction over long private deliberation. Keep required private thought in its active presentation and connect it to the character's present experience. A brief practical thought may fit, while memory, direct feeling, or developed reasoning remains available.\n\nLet the scene end on an unfinished practical difficulty or a new arrival. The fantastic premise's consequences should remain intelligible, but the scene need not resolve. A character may still be holding the wrong object, waiting for a reply, or arguing about what to do next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic or metaphysical construction route. Treat the impossible premise as a real condition of the world, not as a metaphor or a dream. The narration should accept the premise and move directly to its consequences: who is affected, what they must do, and how the condition changes ordinary life.\n\nLet the Adams profile's absurdity emerge from the premise's social and bureaucratic consequences. A metaphysical condition should produce forms, queues, disputes, and workarounds. A character may need to prove that a room is larger on the inside, register a talking animal, or negotiate with an office that exists only on certain days. Keep the large claim tied to the small logistical problem.\n\nPreserve the specific language, community, and history of the scene. The fantastic condition should affect a particular place and particular people, not a generic landscape. Do not manufacture national speech patterns or a generic regional temperament. The absurdity belongs to the situation, not to a stereotype.\n\nLet different characters respond differently to the same impossible condition. One may treat it as normal, another as a bureaucratic nuisance, a third as a philosophical problem. Do not make everyone equally witty or equally resigned. The humor should come from the gap between the condition's scale and the practical response it demands.\n\nEnd the scene where the practical consequence and the metaphysical condition intersect, without requiring a completed explanation or a moral resolution. The character may still be waiting for a form, arguing with a neighbor, or deciding how to live with the condition. The impossible premise remains real and intelligible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaean::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. In the first lines, establish where the character is, what they are trying to do, and what fantastic condition or object is already causing trouble. Do not begin with a long explanation of the world. Let the problem appear through action or dialogue.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When the character moves, say where they are now and what they can see or touch. Use short, readable paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response. A reaction beat should follow a new piece of information or a change in the situation, and it should belong to the specific character: serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to who they are.\n\nIntroduce the fantastic premise through its practical requirements. A wonder should need a procedure, a payment, a key, a schedule, or a repair. Let the Adams profile's scale collision appear when a character makes a large claim and then must deal with a small, concrete obstacle. Show the obstacle clearly: the wrong form, the locked door, the object that works only when held at an awkward angle.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when needed. If a term or rule matters to the scene, explain it in one or two plain sentences at the point of use. Do not pause the scene for a glossary. Let the explanation itself create the next practical problem.\n\nLet different characters react differently to the same absurdity. One may try to fix it, another may argue about who is responsible, a third may accept it as normal. Do not make everyone equally witty. The humor comes from the specific person's reaction to the specific inconvenience.\n\nEnd the scene where the practical difficulty and the character's reaction intersect, without requiring a cliffhanger or a completed solution. The character may still be holding the wrong object, waiting for a reply, or deciding whether to follow a new instruction. The fantastic premise's consequences remain clear and immediate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the established fantastic premise enter the prose as lived logistics rather than announced awe: the queue it creates, the maintenance it requires, what a person now packs, waits for, or explains to a neighbor. Let one such precise detail become the image the prose carries, returned to whenever the scene's attention shifts, so that each recurrence carries a changed meaning. When a large claim meets a small arrangement, hold both in the same sentence long enough for the contrast to become audible.\n\nShape that contrast with rhythm rather than adjective accumulation. A long clause can unspool the grand version of the premise while a short plain clause registers its inconvenience, so the syntax itself performs the meeting of scale and schedule. Let a comparison unfold across several clauses when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present — an explanation, procedure, or apparently reasonable premise opening into its absurd consequence — and set a plain statement beside it for contrast. Keep every image traceable to the scene or the teller's associations.\n\nTreat digression as a movement of attention rather than an interruption to apologize for: it follows the association, exposes the absurdity, and returns through the problem currently at hand. Different people respond differently to that absurdity — one treats it as routine, another as an outrage, another as a scheduling difficulty — and they need not speak in the narrator's figurative register; a plainly spoken line against lyrical narration sharpens both. Preserve the active narrator's access and the selected perspective. You may stop while the practical consequence is still unfolding, the next inconvenience visible but not yet resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the fantastic premise age. A wonder that was once new now has a maintenance schedule, a waiting list, a discontinued part, a generation that remembers when it worked differently. Attend to those small irreversible changes — the route no longer used, the procedure simplified past recognition, the person who knew the old method and is no longer there — and let the reflection arrive either through a remembered occasion or through a present detail that carries the association. It should sit beside the scene's ordinary business rather than replace it, and ordinary pleasure remains pleasurable even when it is no longer exactly what it was.\n\nKeep the absurdity precise inside the memory. A large claim can meet a small logistical problem in the middle of a recollection, so the promise is remembered along with the queue it produced, and the inconvenience is funny without canceling the loss. Let different people remember the same wonder differently: one its inconvenience, another its promise, another only the argument it caused. The passing of the thing is then felt through disagreement and detail rather than through a declared lament; do not end every recollection by announcing impermanence.\n\nMake the connection between present experience and what is no longer available specific: what the current procedure cannot do, whom the new arrangement no longer includes, what the old way required that no one alive still knows. The narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry it. Preserve the active perspective and the actual world facts; reflection grants no access to other minds and need not resolve into a lesson. You may stop with the present moment still in progress, the remembered thing still absent, and the next small consequence approaching.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell it as a situated oral storyteller: one particular person addressing a particular listener, moving through ordinary social and domestic life — the errand, the kitchen, the debt, the relative who must be explained to. The established fantastic premise belongs to that ordinary life; it changes how people queue, cook, travel, settle accounts, or account for themselves, and it is a settled fact of the world, not a nested marvel the telling must justify. What the teller reports about others is report: speech, action, and reputation, never direct knowledge of another person's private mind.\n\nLet the account move picaresquely and absurdly. A large claim meets a small logistical problem — a delayed permit, a mislabeled container, a neighbor's disputed memory — and the resulting inconvenience sends the telling in a new direction before it returns through the current problem, so that every digression earns its return: the story about the paperwork becomes the reason the main errand fails or succeeds. The teller may interrupt the story to explain a local arrangement, complain about a delay, or admit that a detail is disputed, and that voice should be particular — one person's irritation, humor, or precision — rather than a generic comic register. The figures in the tale respond to the absurdity each in their own way.\n\nUse the setting, language, period, and community actually supplied, and keep Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Hebrew, Kurdish, and other traditions distinct where the story names one; do not manufacture a regional temperament, antiquity, mysticism, or a single honor code. Recurrence and delayed revelation may shape the telling where the current story supports them. You may stop with the teller pausing mid-account, the next inconvenience already visible and the journey not yet concluded.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaear::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose the few exact details that make the impossible administratively real, and stop there: the queue it creates, the form it requires, the replacement part that no longer fits. One such detail carries the premise's consequence; once the first has made it legible, do not add a second example. Set a large claim and its small logistical problem in the same sentence, or in the space between two adjacent sentences, and let that gap do the work — the reader hears the absurdity without elaboration.\n\nKeep only the explanation or inward connection that materially contributes to the present account. An apparently reasonable premise can expose its absurd consequence through a single observed fact or a single line of dialogue; omit the rest, but leave it inferable, and never cut the step that connects the wonder to the inconvenience. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several performed fragments; shortness is not a quota.\n\nLet people respond differently to the absurdity and show the difference through what they do or say — the one who queues, the one who leaves, the one who demands to see whoever is in charge — rather than through a summary of attitudes. Keep the voices individual, and preserve the active narrator's access and the selected perspective. You may stop after one consequence is registered, the next problem visible but not yet stated.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which the established fantastic premise appears through its effect on the present moment: what someone is doing, waiting for, explaining, or trying to repair. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and whatever inner experience the active contract authorizes, and use paragraphing and transitions to orient — a changed room, a new speaker, a step to later the same day — without recap machinery. Let the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm rather than a uniform comic tone laid over everything.\n\nFold the contrast between large claims and small logistical problems into that movement as scene events. A character makes a grand statement and then discovers the queue, the missing key, the form that requires a different form; an explanation or apparently reasonable premise exposes its absurd consequence in front of the people it inconveniences. A digression can leave the scene — into a memory, a procedure, the history of an arrangement — provided it returns through the current problem instead of floating as isolated anecdote. The cast responds in its own mixed ways: routine, personal insult, a reason to leave early, or genuine unease the joke does not touch.\n\nKeep the premise intelligible without requiring every mystery to become an explained system. Contemporary presentation does not require a contemporary setting, understated emotion, realism as world physics, or a lesson at the close. Preserve the active perspective and the world facts. You may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup, with the next practical consequence in view and unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through the scenes you select, the private language you repeat, and the consequences that fulfill its particular promise. Choose moments where the world's practical arrangements press on the relationship: a shared errand complicated by the impossible, a comfort offered after an absurd delay, a rivalry sharpened by a rule that makes no sense to either of them. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space it earns where it belongs, and let each emotional consequence follow from the particular premise rather than from a borrowed trope.\n\nBuild the intimacy out of the contrast between large claims and small logistical problems. A grand promise can be interrupted by a queue; a serious confession can wait while a form is found; a moment of comfort can take the shape of the inconvenience they have both learned to expect. Let the repeated thing — the same phrase, the same small task, the same shared complaint — return with a new application because of what has happened between them, so the callback pays off this relationship's specific history instead of announcing it. Keep the two voices distinct and unevenly witty; one may treat the absurdity as routine while the other takes it personally.\n\nUse only the relationship the supplied premise actually establishes, without stacking a roster of familiar tropes, and without requiring every scene to end in comfort or in collapse. One character's private experience remains private from the other unless the active contract permits more. You may stop with the relationship's next consequence still unfolding, the private language still working, and the practical inconvenience still in the way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling: deliberate openings, compact declarations, recurring phrasing, and the question of what will be remembered. Establish a person or an act in a declaration, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Let the established fantastic premise appear as what memory is made of — the wonder that changed a journey, the rule behind a feud, the gift that created an obligation, the public deed that turned out to involve a great deal of waiting. What the telling claims about others is reputation and report, not knowledge of their private minds.\n\nMake the meeting of large claims and small logistical problems the chronicle's engine. Follow the compact declaration with the practical detail that complicates it: a hero of the celebrated deed who is also remembered for losing the receipt; an oath sworn with ceremony and then delayed by a missing witness; a journey celebrated in song and mostly spent looking for somewhere to sleep. Let dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority — the subject of an oft-told incident objects to one detail, or answers an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern — without the telling collapsing into parody; household work is not mock combat, and familiar inconvenience is not heroic doom.\n\nReturn to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application, and keep open the question of which version will be remembered. Keep the particular source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention; do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into speakers who would not use them. The account may close with business concluded or resumed, another modest act left available to be remembered, and no legend declared on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the tradition's social and domestic comic register: the mode where communal life, official procedure, and private feeling are treated with equal seriousness. Give the scene the texture of an actual household, workplace, or housing community — meetings, notices, tenancy rules, shared duties, family visits, practical talk about weather and work. The fantastic premise is an established fact of that place, handled matter-of-factly rather than with awe: it appears in a municipal form, a neighbor's complaint, a clause in the building's rules, a chore it has created. Do not reach for kingdoms, prophecies, or ancient bloodlines; the impossible thing changes what people must do on an ordinary Tuesday.\n\nLet understatement carry the comedy. An official explanation can be perfectly coherent on its own terms and still fail to account for what is standing in the yard. A form asks for information the premise makes impossible to supply. Someone accepts the wonder without comment but is genuinely upset about parking, noise, or the laundry rota. Deliver these absurdities in the same plain, dutiful voice the community uses for everything else; the joke is the mismatch, not anyone narrating it.\n\nGive different people different, stable responses: one treats the impossible thing as a nuisance to be scheduled around, another as an opportunity, another as a matter for the board, another as a private embarrassment to be kept from a visiting parent. Their speech fits their age, role, and relationship; reserve and evasion sit beside open argument and plain kindness.\n\nUse the place the story actually supplies. Do not import snow, isolation, or bleakness unless the setting and season provide them, and keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts distinct when which one it is matters — the institutions, forms of address, and social expectations differ. Let digressions about local rules or previous incidents return through the current problem, each return exposing a further absurd consequence rather than delaying the scene.\n\nKeep private experience available in ordinary prose: a character silently rehearsing what to say at the meeting, noticing that the official account cannot be true, or feeling relief that someone else has finally seen the thing. No one else knows these thoughts, and they need not produce an immediate decision. The scene may close on a practical result, an unresolved obligation, or plain acknowledgment of the changed situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nWrite readable scene blocks with clear speaker cues and a scene heading whenever location or time changes. Keep action lines to what the audience can see, hear, or reasonably infer: entrances, objects, sounds, what people do with their hands. Do not fill the page with camera commands; use a cut, an offscreen sound, or a held quiet reaction only when the sequence needs it to establish a change, a consequence, or an absence.\n\nMake the fantastic premise a visible or audible production fact rather than a topic. A character enters carrying something impossible; a room contains an object that should not be there; an announcement refers to a condition everyone has stopped questioning. Show its effect on behavior — which door people avoid, which form they fill in, whose shift it has become — and let the cause stay partly unexplained while its consequences stay legible.\n\nPlay the comic gap between dialogue block and action line. A speech describes the situation in grand or perfectly reasonable terms while the action line records the queue, the dripping, the delivery slot, the smell, or an official account of an event the audience has just watched happen differently. Let an explanation be internally consistent and visibly false at the same time. A digression about a past incident or a regulation should land on the immediate scene, and a cut can deliver the landing: the tangent ends, and the new location shows what it actually produced.\n\nGive each speaker a distinct relationship to the absurdity — routine, crisis, inconvenience, opportunity — in their own diction and rhythm rather than interchangeable polished banter. Two people can disagree about what kind of problem it is while both are wrong. A quiet or emotional beat may stand without a follow-up joke.\n\nKeep required private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not as dialogue and not as knowledge the cast possesses. Use voice-over only if the sequence specifically needs it and the active contract permits it. End a scene on a visible result, an unfinished exchange, or a quiet acknowledgment; no forced reveal or punchline is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script: clear speakers, entrances and exits, economical directions, blocking that a cast and crew can actually stage. No film-camera instructions. Let physical business and objects carry as much of the public scene as the lines do.\n\nPut the fantastic premise inside the shared stage space. The impossible thing can be seen, touched, avoided, wheeled through, apologized to; a character enters with evidence of it, or the room changes in a way everyone must respond to. Let it constrain movement — a doorway people file through one at a time, a prop nobody wants to be left holding — so it affects what people can do, not merely what they discuss. Its consequences remain legible even if its cause is never explained.\n\nBuild the comedy from lines against business. A character delivers a grand explanation while trying to close a window, right a chair, or keep the thing from rolling away; a sensible procedure is recited while the audience watches it visibly not apply to the object in front of everyone. Entrances and exits distribute the responses: one person treats the condition as normal, another as a personal insult, another as a scheduling problem, another leaves to fetch someone more senior. Each speaks in their own register; none of them is the narrator.\n\nGive a monologue or aside a dramatic purpose when used — winning a room, covering a mistake — and treat it as performed speech. Required private experience stays nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation; do not disguise it as words another character could overhear.\n\nLet a digression about a past incident or an official rule return through the physical problem of the moment. Close the scene on a staged result, an interrupted exchange, or one character's silent look at what has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through one authorized mind, letting perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern braid together as the scene proceeds. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations — a sentence broken by a sound, a memory arriving mid-clause — but keep orientation firm enough to tell what is happening now from what is remembered, imagined, or dreaded. A tidy linear report is not this mode; the movement is associative.\n\nLet the fantastic premise be a fact of the world this mind keeps colliding with. It announces itself through practical effects — the form with no field for it, the detour everyone now takes, the physical consequence the body has learned to expect — and through memory of when it began. Attention drifts to ordinary things and returns by unexpected routes: a shopping list calls up the committee incident; the taste of coffee recalls the morning it arrived. Do not let it shrink into a metaphor for mood; it stays real and consequential.\n\nThe comedy is experienced from inside, as the strain of holding a reasonable account together. An explanation sounds solid until, midway through a digression about something else, its absurd consequence surfaces; the mind attempts a correction, gets interrupted, files it under tomorrow. A large claim sits in memory beside the small logistical wreckage it left behind. Let digressions return through the current problem with new practical weight rather than wandering off, and let stretches of plain attention stand so the next absurdity lands harder.\n\nKeep other people distinct as the focal mind encounters them: patient, irritated, practical, frightened, entirely uninterested. Their speech does not share the focal comic interiority, and the gap between what someone says and what the situation shows is this character's private perception, not shared knowledge.\n\nWrite the mind's actual thought, not a summary of it; no other character has access. End where the attention genuinely is — mid-association, mid-task, on a shift of focus — without a manufactured insight or closing line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as a chat transcript in which an established fantasy premise has become a practical coordination problem. The impossible thing is not merely mentioned; it must be handled through messages. Senders arrange access, request confirmation, report observations, dispute procedure, or try to get someone else to deal with it. Let the premise's consequences appear in the exchange itself: a meeting time becomes impossible because of a condition the premise creates, a request requires a step no one can describe clearly, or a sender stops replying because the situation has absorbed their attention.\n\nGive each sender a distinct digital habit that reflects their relation to the premise, not a uniform comic voice. One may treat the impossible thing as an ordinary work order and write in complete procedural sentences. Another may send fragments while actively dealing with it. Another may correct a word or revise a message because the situation has made precision suddenly important. Another may leave a message unfinished when the practical problem overtakes the attempt to explain it. Let these habits carry characterization and information, not just texture.\n\nUse the gap between message conventions and the actual situation as the main comic engine. A sender may describe a step-by-step procedure that is internally consistent but absurd for the thing it is supposed to address. A calm, official-sounding message may be followed by a timestamp that shows it took far too long to compose. An attachment or link may be offered as proof and then require a second message admitting it does not show what the sender expected. The humor comes from the collision between the format's implied competence and the situation's resistance to that format.\n\nTreat timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, and channel changes as information-bearing events. A delay is meaningful when the next message reveals what the sender was doing during it, or when the sender returns with a different tone. An edit is meaningful when the revised version changes what another sender can act on. A read receipt is meaningful when it changes the sender's next message. Do not insert interface events merely to create subtext; each one must affect the exchange or reveal something about the sender's practical situation.\n\nDistinguish transmitted text from private experience. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not the writer's live thought; it may show what a sender considered saying, but it does not grant access to their mind. When private experience is required, place it in clearly separated context outside the transcript, marked as narrative context rather than as a message. Keep that context focused on what the sender is actually experiencing while composing, waiting, or acting, and do not let other senders read it.\n\nLet digressions enter as things a sender brings up while trying to manage the current problem: a past incident, an official rule, an unrelated errand, or a remembered procedure. The next message should show how that digression bears on the immediate situation, either by changing what a sender proposes or by revealing why the current exchange is stuck. The transcript can end on a sent message, a delay, a read receipt, or a separated contextual moment. Do not force a punchline, cliffhanger, or completed resolution; the exchange may simply pause where the practical situation has paused.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in the rhythm of an illustrated scene: a concise line or two of scene-setting, attributed dialogue, a focal reaction, then the next beat. Use background and expression cues as purposeful references that orient the moment — what changed in the room, what a face is doing — not as engine code or a mandatory label on every line. Keep scene changes legible.\n\nLet the fantastic premise be part of what the focal character is looking at. The impossible object sits where it sits, the street has adjusted around it, a change it causes registers in the scene. It shapes what people can do and say — which door is usable, which errand has become strange — not only what they claim about it, and its consequences stay intelligible even when the full explanation does not arrive.\n\nMake the focal reaction the comic instrument. A speaker lays out the situation in grand or reasonable terms; the focal narration quietly registers the small practical contradiction — the object in the wrong place, the procedure requiring something impossible, the official account omitting the thing in plain view. Let that private registration accumulate across beats while the conversation continues politely. A digression about a past incident or a rule should land back on a visible detail of the current scene.\n\nGive the cast distinct stances — normal, nuisance, opportunity, reason to leave — in speech fitted to role and relationship, not interchangeable banter. The focal character's inner response is actual private experience: noticing an implication, rehearsing a reply, feeling the strain of pretending things are ordinary. Others do not know it. No branching menus, player choices, romance routes, or images are activated by the style alone. A scene can end on a focal reaction, an unfinished exchange, or a visible change in the situation, without a choice screen or forced reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaebb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nWrite installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line: what is underway, what changed locally, what remains open. Recover earlier context in a sentence when it helps the current scene, and otherwise let cumulative development carry it. Earn each hook; do not close every turn as a complete miniature chapter or lean on an artificial cliffhanger.\n\nLet the fantastic premise accumulate practical consequences across replies. One installment establishes a new limitation; a later one shows how people have adapted; a later one still reveals that an earlier explanation was incomplete — the old fact qualified, not erased. Progress can be knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. The premise is not a leveling system or a ranking of powers.\n\nPlay the comic contrast across time. A grand solution announced in one reply meets its queue, paperwork, cleanup, or neighborly dispute in the next; an explanation holds until an installment shows what it left out. Let a digression — a past incident, an official rule — become newly relevant later rather than being repeated as filler, and briefly recover the particular it rests on instead of retelling its history.\n\nGive the cast responses that persist and shift: one grows used to the condition, another keeps trying to fix it, another exploits it, another refuses to discuss it, and each new installment may move that position slightly. Their speech fits role and relationship; they are not all one comic narrator.\n\nKeep private experience available in ordinary prose — privately reconsidering an earlier explanation, noticing a consequence no one has mentioned yet, feeling the strain of a public position. It need not produce an immediate decision, and others do not know it. Close an installment on completed work, a pending request, a newly visible consequence, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the hurt through what the character handles, avoids, repeats, or fails to finish, and let the established fantastic premise give that hurt a working body: a healing that closes the wound and leaves the scar tender, a binding that must be renewed each morning by whoever set it, a door in the house that will not stay shut. The impossible fact is not a metaphor to retreat behind; it has consequences the character works around daily, and the working-around is where the feeling shows.\n\nPut the strain inside the exchanges themselves. A direct question gets an answer about the wrong subject; a task is taken up again at the moment an apology is expected; a true thing is said in a tone that makes it harder to hear. Repetition does the admitting the speaker will not do: the cup set down twice, the same stretch of road walked past the same marker, the same promise offered in the same words because the first offering failed. Use the narrator's actual distance — a close narrator registers the body's signals before the mind consents to read them; a cooler one reports only what could be observed and leaves the strain sitting in the gaps between actions.\n\nWhen warmth comes, keep it partial and awkward: a dry coat held out without a word, a fire built too hot, a question asked and then abandoned before it is answered. Nothing here requires a confession, a repair, or a collapse. The exposure can remain unfinished, and the next scene can carry its weight without naming it, because the objects and the repeated acts have already carried it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nRun the shared interval in short, readable paragraphs, each anchored to one speaker's action or one practical exchange, with attribution no reader can mistake. Ground the scene in objects and movements a participant could point to — the lantern set down on the ledge, the cloak shaken dry, the coin slid across the table — and treat the fantastic as one more working fact of the place, handled as plainly as rope or weather. When the impossible enters, show one consequence in the scene instead of stopping the action for its history.\n\nGive the dialogue the weight of the unsaid. A single word can answer a long question; a phrase repeated under a new load can carry a refusal to explain; a silence between two speeches can do the work of a paragraph. Keep the world and its people active on their own aims: an NPC continues a task already in progress, offers or withholds help for reasons of their own, and does not wait on the players to exist. Where the active contract allows private thought, keep it concrete and readable in the same flow. A quick exchange may contain brief thought, but it should not crowd out developed experience that belongs to the character.\n\nEnd where another participant can naturally take the next beat — mid-task, with a tension unspoken, or with a practical problem still in motion. No question, cliffhanger, or forced choice is owed at the boundary; the scene simply stays live, in whatever person, voice, and action-marker conventions the active contract sets, with the players' control over their own characters intact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the vernacular episodic route: tell the story as a chain of settled episodes, each one a unit of practical business — a stage of a journey, a transaction at the market, a task owed to a household, a dispute over what one party promised another. The fantastic premise lives inside that business as a condition of the community: a spirit touches the harvest and the family answers with the measures their neighbors have always used; a talisman fails at the worst hour and the failure becomes a debt; a stranger repays an obligation in a form that changes what the household now owes. Within each episode, write with concrete economy — name the thing handled, the act done, what it cost, what follows — and keep the speech practical and economical.\n\nHold the language, period, and community of the setting actually supplied. The episodes take their weight from who owes whom, what must be finished before anyone travels, how a house receives someone it does not know — social texture, not a manufactured landscape or a borrowed national temperament. Withholding works through omission: a question about the past answered with a question about the present, a courtesy repeated past its natural life because a grudge sits under it, a name spoken and then not spoken again. Repetition marks the return of an unresolved matter, and each return adds something — a new fact, a harder question, one fewer excuse.\n\nClose an episode where its practical matter rests, with the larger business unfinished, so the next episode picks up what remains. The telling accumulates through these units rather than through one continuous scene, and the fantastic stays intelligible through its consequences, not through an explanatory system.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the fantastic as a hard condition with costs. A ward burns out and someone sleeps cold; a road shifts underfoot and the detour runs through country that is dangerous on foot; a beast leaves tracks that can be measured and wounds that take time. Compress the transitions — cut the connective explanation and let one concrete act hand the next its place — and force the diction toward verbs and nouns that do their own work. The material world shows wear: cracked leather, a blade nicked where it met something harder, a door scarred at the latch.\n\nKeep clauses short where the stakes are physical, and let a thought run longer only when the character is actually reasoning something through — a judgment of risk, an account that has to add up — so length follows need rather than decoration. Register the impossible in what it does to bodies, tools, shelter, and supply, and let repetition be routine with a reason: the same check made at the same place each time, a failing method kept past its point because its keeper will not put it down.\n\nShift rhythm deliberately. A run of quick actions can stop on one detail that matters — the exact sound of the latch, the weight of wet rope, the color of a burn — and that stop is where the pressure lands. Speech stays rough when the speaker is rough and plain when the speaker is plain, grounded in who is talking. The world's dangers and its ordinary work are both real; the compression serves clarity and consequence, and the impossible belongs to this world as a fact that changes what its people can do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence in documents, each with a definite author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, and let that situation govern what the page holds. A field report lists the damage from a failed summoning and does not mention the fear; a letter home describes the strange market and omits the debt that made the journey necessary; a ledger records a payment in a currency no one at the house can identify. Inside each document write with concrete economy — objects named, actions stated, exchanges counted — so the fantastic appears as a practical fact the writer must handle: a road closed by something that left no tracks, a crop that ripened overnight, a healer whose price is memory.\n\nLet omission be legible on the page itself. A report that never names a companion, a reassurance repeated once too often, a record that stops the line before the event it was kept to document — these carry what the writer would not say. A document is a made thing, not a whole mind: it shows what its author chose to write, struck out, or rewrote, and later entries can change what earlier ones meant. Material traces count — the ink, the seal, a page water-stained, a line crossed through — and gaps between documents can reveal events without an omniscient voice arriving to settle them.\n\nUse the form the scene actually requires — letter, report, ledger, diary, order, note — rather than rotating formats for their own sake, and let each next document follow from its author's situation. Conflicting accounts can stand. Readable continuity comes from dates, names, and repeated practical threads, not from explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend the attention on chosen care made visible in practical acts. A cloak is warmed at the fire before it is handed over; the best portion is kept back without explanation; someone remembers which charm keeps the draft out of that one room. Write these acts with precise objects and plain statements — the thing given, the task done, the preference remembered — so the warmth sits in what is done rather than in what is declared. The fantasy premise supplies this world's particular pleasures and its share of the work: a tea that takes its flavor from the drinker's mood, a lamp that burns without oil through the long nights, a small garden spirit that clears the stones and expects its bowl of milk.\n\nLet affection be sincere without demanding a confession or a repair. A character shows care by mending a strap, leaving the door open, taking the longer road past the place the other one dislikes; a practical question — whether the boots were oiled, whether the packs are light enough — is really an offer, and the silence after it can mean company. Repeat the small rituals until the reader knows them; the repetition is how these people say what they do not say. Where the active contract grants private thought, let a short inward line register the pleasure of a routine or the wish to get it right, unannounced.\n\nKeep the people intact. Someone cared for can still be difficult; two people who trust each other can disagree about the route or the money; the season outside stays what it is, and the world's established dangers and obligations continue. The comfort is not the removal of the world but the way these particular people meet it together, and it needs no crisis to justify itself — an ordinary evening doing its ordinary work is scene enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBuild the scene in deliberate, sustained paragraphs. Open on the practical matter at hand, move through its action, and close on what the event means for the household, the trade, the boundary, or the season's work, so each paragraph carries both a happening and its social weight. Inside that broader architecture keep the concrete economy: objects and actions named precisely, sentences that stay lean even when the paragraph runs long, and narration held at a composed level above ordinary speech while the dialogue itself stays plainly spoken.\n\nSituate the impossible in institutions and history rather than presenting it as a solitary marvel. A standing stone marks an old boundary dispute as well as a source of power, and the dispute has heirs; a guild sets the terms under which enchanted goods may be sold; a family has held one charm for generations, and its failure this winter carries the weight of everything that holding meant. Let the narrator's permitted distance frame what a community believes, what a record claims, or what a silence suggests — judgment is available where this narrator can honestly make it; omniscient settlement is not.\n\nRepetition can mark a social pattern or a family habit rather than only an individual fixation, and consequential omission still governs: what the narration declines to say can carry as much as what it reports. Hold the chosen narrator's limits and the active authorship contract. The formality is the architecture and the social framing — not an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or speech made uniformly elaborate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the scene around someone telling a story to a listener who needs something from the telling: passage, payment, permission, a decision, a reason to stay or leave. The outer frame should show the immediate practical situation in concrete terms—what work is being done, what object changes hands, what the listener is waiting to hear before acting.\n\nMake the embedded tale demonstrate the story's fantastic premise through a specific person's experience. Do not explain the impossible thing in general. Show what someone could and could not do with it, what it cost them, what they learned to avoid, or how it altered a familiar task. The teller selects and omits details for a purpose. A repeated phrase or object should connect the inner tale to the outer occasion without becoming a guarantee that the tale is true.\n\nKeep the prose economical. Use short declarative sentences for physical actions and exchanges. Let repetition mark what the teller refuses to pass over or what the listener keeps returning to. When the tale ends, return to the outer scene and let the listener act, ask, or remain silent. The telling may change the listener's understanding of the premise, but it does not need to resolve the outer situation or force an immediate decision. Preserve what the teller does not say as a meaningful absence, not as a hidden clue to be decoded.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic-uncanny route. Let the fantastic premise enter the story as a disturbance in a familiar place: a room, a road, a workshop, a household task, a journey. Do not begin with an explanation of the impossible thing. Begin with someone encountering its effects through ordinary activity—a door that should not be open, a tool that works without being touched, a voice that answers from an empty room.\n\nRender the disturbance through concrete objects and actions. Name the thing that is wrong, what the character does about it, and what happens next. Keep sentences short and direct. Let the uncanny feeling come from the gap between what the character knows should be true and what the senses report. Do not fill that gap with lengthy interpretation. A character may notice the discrepancy and act anyway.\n\nLet the fantastic premise have intelligible consequences. Show what it changes about the character's work, safety, or choices. The uncanny is not only mood; it alters what can be done. Repetition can mark a recurring disturbance or a refusal to accept what has happened. End the scene at a point where the character has acted or chosen, without resolving the larger mystery or explaining the premise away. Keep the telling restrained and physical, with the strangeness carried by what is seen and handled rather than by adjectives.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the scene in a present urban environment where the fantastic premise is tied to old authority: a building, a family, an institution, a contract, a debt, a rite that still controls access or obligation. Show the premise through its practical effects on people who must live with it. Do not begin with a general explanation of the power. Begin with someone entering a room, waiting for someone, handling an object, or making a request that brings the old arrangement into the present.\n\nUse concrete urban detail: the condition of a stairwell, the sound of traffic outside, the way a door is locked, the weight of a key or a document. Let the atmosphere come from these details and from what the characters do, not from abstract dread. Keep sentences direct. Show the collision between inherited power and present need through exchanges: someone asks for something, someone refuses or grants it, someone pays a cost or avoids one.\n\nLet intimacy be morally complicated. A character may trust someone who holds power over them, or protect someone while still using them. Do not make every relationship a betrayal or every private moment a trap. Show private desire and social masks falling away only in specific, earned encounters. The fantastic premise should have intelligible consequences for who can act and what it costs. End at a point where the encounter has changed the immediate situation, without resolving the larger arrangement or forcing a revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route. Place the fantastic premise inside a specific household, workplace, or community setting where people have obligations to one another: a family member who must be cared for, a job that must be done, a neighbor who depends on help, a debt or promise that shapes the day. Keep the language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them. Do not impose a generic family structure or force code-switching.\n\nShow the premise through its effect on these obligations. A person may use the impossible thing to finish work, to protect someone, to keep a promise, or to avoid a difficult choice. Let the consequence be concrete: what is gained, what is lost, what someone must now do differently. Use Hemingway's direct sentences for actions and exchanges. Let characters speak plainly about practical matters. Repetition can mark a recurring obligation or a refusal to let go of a task.\n\nKeep wonder beside the ordinary. The impossible thing does not need to be hidden or dramatic. It can be as familiar as a tool or a household routine, but it changes what people can do. Let the scene end at a point where the character has acted within their obligations, without resolving every tension. Do not turn the premise into a moral lesson or a spectacle. The story's weight comes from the specific human cost and care around the impossible thing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tale route. Introduce the impossible premise as a strange occurrence inside a recognizable social world: a village, a household, a shop, a road, a place where people know one another and have practical business. Do not begin with an explanation. Begin with someone noticing that something has happened which should not be possible—a person returned, an object moved, a season changed, a debt settled without payment.\n\nShow the occurrence through concrete actions and exchanges. Let characters respond practically: they ask questions, check the place, handle the object, tell someone what they saw, or decide to keep quiet. Use short, direct sentences. Let the wonder remain strange without forcing the characters to solve it. The premise should have intelligible consequences: it changes what someone can do, what they owe, or what they fear.\n\nLet repetition mark what the characters cannot ignore or what they keep returning to. Omission carries meaning: what a character does not say about the occurrence can matter as much as what they report. End the scene at a point where the occurrence has altered the immediate situation, without resolving its origin or forcing a moral. Keep the telling plain and physical, with the strangeness arising from the event itself rather than from elaborate description.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the marvelous-treated-as-ordinary route. Treat the fantastic premise as an accepted fact of the world, present in daily life without requiring astonishment or explanation. A character may know that a particular person can do something impossible, that a place behaves strangely, or that an object works in a way others do not. They do not need to question it. They need to live with it.\n\nShow the premise through concrete actions and practical exchanges. Someone uses the impossible thing to complete a task, avoid a danger, keep a promise, or settle a dispute. Let the consequence be specific: what is gained, what is lost, what someone must now do. Use Hemingway's direct sentences for physical movement and speech. Keep the prose economical. Do not decorate the wonder with adjectives or turn it into a symbol.\n\nLet repetition mark what the characters accept without discussion or what they return to because it still costs them. Omission can carry the weight of what everyone knows and does not say. The premise should change what people can do, but it does not need to be dramatic. End at a point where the character has acted within this ordinary strangeness, without resolving the larger world or explaining the impossible thing away. Preserve the specific language, community, and history the story supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. In the first lines, show where the character is, what they are doing, and what practical problem the fantastic premise creates. Use concrete spatial details: the layout of the room, the position of an object, the distance to a door, the condition of the ground. Keep spatial changes easy to follow by naming the movement and its result in short, direct sentences.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. When a character acts, show the physical movement and its immediate consequence. When they speak, keep the exchange practical and direct. When they react privately, give a brief focused beat: what they notice, what they decide, what they refuse to say. Do not let the reaction become a long explanation of the premise. Let the premise become legible through what the character does and what happens as a result.\n\nLet the fantastic element affect the character's choices and the scene's stakes. It may make a task possible, create a new danger, or change what someone can ask for. Repetition can mark a recurring obstacle or a phrase the character returns to under pressure. End at a natural pause in the immediate situation: after an action, a decision, or a response that leaves the next step open. Do not force a cliffhanger or a completed arc. Keep the prose fast and concrete, with the wonder carried by the event rather than by description.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the impossible as a physical fact among other physical facts. When a character encounters the fantastic, give it weight, texture, temperature, and consequence through exact objects and actions: the stone that stays warm after the spell, the door that must be lifted rather than pushed, the river that runs the wrong way and carries the smell of a place no one has seen. Do not explain the mechanism. Attend to the thing itself, and show ordinary life bending around it: the route changed, the task altered, the price paid in work or waiting.\n\nBuild rhythm through recurrence of a concrete image or phrase tied to the character's repeated action. A repeated detail can carry feeling without naming it: the same cracked cup set down each morning, the same wind moving the curtain before a departure. When the image returns, place it in a slightly different position or light so the repetition accumulates rather than restates. Use short declarative sentences for action and consequence. When attention shifts from the immediate task to what the task has begun to mean, let the syntax lengthen with that shift: the sentence can follow the character's eye from the object in hand to the thing it now recalls or anticipates.\n\nRender the unspoken through what the character does. If the mountain moves at night and they will not say what it means to them, show the window checked, the route chosen, the pause before the answer. The omission leaves the feeling inferable because the behavior makes it present. Keep figurative language traceable to the scene. When a comparison opens, let it change what the narrator understands or makes present rather than decorate the sentence. Plain statements provide contrast and keep the lyricism from becoming permanent solemnity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the fantastic premise mark time. A character may remember when the floating islands were close enough to walk between, or when the hearth-spirit still answered, or when the healing spring worked. The present scene holds the changed version: the islands now too far, the hearth cold, the spring ordinary water. Write both states through concrete detail. The memory should be specific—what the light did, what someone said, what was carried—not a general statement that things were better.\n\nUse Hemingway's economical clauses to keep reflection from becoming rumination. State the present fact. State the remembered fact. The unstated difference between them carries the loss. A character may return to a familiar object or action and find it altered in one small way: the same road, but the gate no longer needs oiling; the same festival, but the fire is kept in a metal cage now. Make the alteration a particular sensory or functional change, not an announcement of decline.\n\nAllow ordinary pleasure to remain pleasurable. A meal can be good, a morning can be clear, a companion can be funny, even while the narrator notices what is gone. Do not require every scene to end by declaring impermanence. Carry the attachment through what the character does: the empty shelf checked, the old key kept, the pause at the place where the path used to turn. When such an action repeats, show it again in its specific circumstances; the repetition itself exposes the refusal to move on without the narrator explaining it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling. A situated teller speaks to a particular audience: a traveler at a table, a grandmother by the oven, a guide resting with the mule. The frame is concrete—who is present, what they are doing, what prompts the telling. The tale within the frame carries the fantastic premise, but it is told by someone with a reason to tell it now and to this company. The teller may pause to drink, answer a question, or correct a detail. The listeners are not passive; their interruptions and silences shape what gets told.\n\nKeep the teller's language direct and economical. The tale should move through precise objects and actions: the jar that must be filled before the door opens, the coin that changes hands twice, the horse that refuses the bridge. Do not let the teller explain the mechanism of the impossible thing. They report what happened, what was seen, and what was said. Select the detail that carries the unstated meaning: the jar left empty, the coin returned, the horse led the long way around. A listener may ask the wrong question, and the teller may answer with a practical correction rather than a revelation.\n\nLet the frame return at the end. The telling may be interrupted by a practical need, or conclude with the teller returning to the immediate task. The tale's consequence may be visible in the frame: someone checks a door, a child repeats a phrase, a listener decides to take a different road. Do not force a moral. The fantastic element should have intelligible consequences within the tale—what it costs, what it changes, what it leaves behind—without the teller turning it into an explained system.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nEstablish the fantastic premise through a small number of exact details and their practical consequences. If the dead can be spoken to at the river, show the character walking there with a specific question and returning with a specific answer or no answer. If a charm keeps the house safe, show the charm being checked, replaced, or failing. Do not explain the mechanism. Show the impossible thing through what the characters do because of it.\n\nUse direct clauses and concrete nouns. Choose the detail that does the most work: the locked drawer no one opens, the salt line worn thin at the threshold, the second bowl set out at meals. Omission should leave something inferable, not delete causality. If a character refuses to enter a room, the refusal itself is the evidence; the history of the room need not be explained. Connect the refusal to a legible consequence—what the character does instead, what they avoid, what it costs them.\n\nLet repetition carry emphasis without becoming ornament. A phrase or action may return when it has a new application: the same question asked at the river each year, the same coin left on the same stone. Keep any inward connection brief and material. A thought may be long enough to make sense, but it should contribute to the present account. End the scene when the action or exchange completes its immediate movement. Let the fantastic premise remain present through the next practical detail—the bowl set out again, the drawer still locked—rather than through a narrator's summary of the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite scene-first prose in which the fantastic premise is part of the world's ordinary operation. A scene may open with a practical task that the impossible element complicates or enables: repairing a roof while the house shifts its rooms, bargaining for grain in a market where some coins are not what they seem. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. The narrator's voice should stay direct and concrete, with Hemingway's economy governing sentence length and what goes unsaid.\n\nLet the fantastic element affect decisions and institutions without becoming the subject of every sentence. A character may think about the cost of the charm, the route that avoids the changed forest, or the official who must be told about the new door. Their inner experience is available under the active contract, but it should be particular and tied to the scene: what they notice, what they decide, what they do not say. Dialogue should be practical and individual. Characters speak about what they need, what they saw, what they will do, not in polished exposition.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery. A recollection may enter when the present scene calls it up: a smell, a phrase, a familiar object. Let the recollection be specific and brief enough to serve the present. Repetition can mark a refusal to move on or a changed understanding, but it should arise from the scene rather than be announced. The scene may end with business concluded or still in motion. Do not require a lesson or a completed arc. Keep the fantastic present through the next practical detail or concern—the coin counted again, the route reconsidered, the official still to be told—rather than through a closing statement about the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nOrganize the scene around the established relationship premise. Whatever the specific promise is—affection, rivalry, comfort, discovery, jealousy—let the scene select and arrange its details to make that premise legible. Use the fantasy element as part of the relationship's practical texture: the charm one of them made, the route they take together, the room that only opens for both. The impossible thing should affect how they meet, what they risk, or what they can do for each other, not merely decorate the background.\n\nBuild private language and repeated moments from the actual supplied premise. A phrase, an object, or a small ritual may return with new weight. The callback should fulfill the promise of the earlier moment rather than merely restate it. Use Hemingway's direct clauses for the exchanges that matter: what one says, what the other does, what is left unsaid. Affection or hurt should be carried by concrete action—the cup filled, the door held, the letter not sent—rather than by a paragraph announcing the feeling.\n\nGive the emotional payoff enough space where it belongs. A scene of comfort or discovery may slow down and attend to the particular actions and perceptions that constitute it. Do not force wit, banter, or confession. Characters may say exactly what they mean, or they may not. The unsaid should remain inferable through what they do and what they avoid. Let the fantasy premise create the conditions for the relationship's consequence: a spell that requires trust, a journey that changes what they can promise, a danger that makes one of them choose. End the scene at the moment the relationship's particular promise has been fulfilled, complicated, or left open, and keep that promise as the organizing center throughout.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the story as an account of deeds and what will be remembered of them. Open with a compact declaration that establishes a person, place, or act: the smith who first worked the star-iron, the bridge that was built in one night, the winter the dead walked. Then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. The fantastic premise should be part of the deed's consequence: the star-iron held an edge but sang in the forge; the bridge stood, but the builders' names were not all remembered; the dead walked, and the living learned which doors to bar.\n\nUse recurring phrasing with a new application at each return. A designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not an ornamental epithet. A phrase may return when a later occurrence changes its meaning: the same oath sworn under different stars, the same gift given twice with different intent. Keep the chronicle's language direct and concrete. State the act, its cost, and its consequence in economical clauses. Omission should leave the meaning inferable: the chronicle may record that a person never spoke of the river again, without explaining what they saw there.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle. A participant may correct a detail or answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern. This need not become parody. The subject of an oft-told deed may object that the horse was borrowed, not given, or that the door was already open. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account may end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf. Let the chronicle return to the fantastic deed's cost or change through a later practical detail—the door still barred, the forge still singing, the name still unrecorded—rather than through a closing statement about the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantasy as folktale-inflected telling: the impossible is already part of the world, and the narration treats it as a condition people live with rather than a puzzle to explain. Use a definite Scandinavian context only when the story supplies one; otherwise keep the method regional in shape without inventing national scenery. The route is the folktale's matter-of-fact relation to the strange, not a catalogue of snow, isolation, or reserve.\n\nLet the fantastic premise appear through precise domestic and working actions. A person salts a threshold, feeds a house spirit, bargains with a river, or performs the same small task on the day the dead are said to walk. Show the object, the movement, and the practical exchange. Do not stop the scene to explain what the being is or how the magic works. The consequence belongs to the action: the bread is left, the boat is not taken, the door is opened or kept shut.\n\nKeep clauses economical and concrete. Name the tool, the food, the weather, the exact thing someone carries or refuses. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let dialogue stay direct and practical. A character can say what must be done, ask what was seen, or answer with a refusal. Repetition should carry a change in emphasis: the same task done twice may mean obligation the first time and grief or resistance the second. What remains unsaid does the emotional work. If a character is afraid, show the fear in the way they handle the object or avoid the question; do not announce the feeling or replace it with a speech about belief.\n\nUse the folktale narrator's plain relation to consequence. The strange event does not require wonder from every witness. Some people accept it, some bargain with it, some ignore it until it cannot be ignored. Let the impossible affect decisions and institutions through ordinary channels: a household rule, a seasonal task, a debt, a journey, a marriage, a burial. The narration may report what people say happened as well as what the scene shows, but keep the report concrete and attributed. A story told by a neighbor is not proof and not the same as the focal character's knowledge.\n\nPreserve required interiority in the active presentation. A character's private reasoning can be brief and physical: they think of the cold water, the cost of the cow, the sound the door made. Do not turn every thought into a lyric passage or an explained motive. The style's inward route is available through restraint: a repeated action, a delayed answer, or a precise memory can carry the private weight. Keep access limited to the authorized mind. Another character does not know what the focal character has decided merely because the reader does.\n\nLet the scene stop on completed work, a pending obligation, or a plain acknowledgment. The fantastic premise may remain partially unexplained. What matters is that it has intelligible consequences for the people in the scene, and that the narration has shown those consequences through action, speech, and the particular things of the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nWrite the fantasy through what can be seen, heard, and handled. Use scene headings when location or time changes. Keep action lines concise and concrete: name the object, the movement, the surface, the sound. If a door opens by itself, show the door opening and the person's response in action, not in a description of the mechanism. If a charm works, show the result: the wound closes, the fire changes, the animal turns away.\n\nEstablish the fantastic premise through repeated practical action rather than exposition. A character performs a task the same way each day, and the action line records the exact detail that matters. The repetition can change emphasis: the first time it is routine, the second time it is refusal, the third time it is a bargain. Do not add a scene explaining what the magic is. Let the consequence appear in the next action or the next line.\n\nKeep dialogue direct and economical. Characters speak about what they need, what they saw, what they will do. A line can carry the unsaid: \"You opened it.\" \"I did not.\" \"Then who did?\" Let the exchange stop before it becomes explanation. Do not use dialogue to summarize the fantasy premise for the audience. If a character does not understand what happened, show the uncertainty in what they do next, not in a speech about confusion.\n\nUse cuts and offscreen sound only when the chosen sequence needs them. A cut can move from the closed door to the person waiting outside. An offscreen sound can be the knock that should not be there. Do not fill the page with camera commands or shot descriptions. The action lines carry the scene; the cut is a transition, not a style.\n\nPreserve required private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation when the contract requires it. Keep it separate from dialogue. Another character does not know what the focal character thinks merely because the note appears. The note can be brief and physical: the memory of the cold handle, the cost of the promise, the sound the floor made. Do not turn it into voice-over unless the chosen sequence needs that device.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed action, a pending task, or a plain acknowledgment. The fantasy premise may remain unexplained. What matters is that the visible action and dialogue have made its consequence intelligible, and that the next scene can begin from what the characters now know or must do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nWrite the fantasy as a playable script. Use clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. The impossible belongs to the shared stage space: what the audience can see and what the characters can reach, carry, open, or refuse. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nEstablish the fantastic premise through staged physical business. A character sets a bowl by the door, turns a key that is not there, or steps around a space another character will not enter. Show the object and the movement in the stage directions. Keep the directions economical: name the prop, the action, the pause. Do not describe the magic in the directions. Let the consequence appear in the next action or the next line.\n\nLet dialogue carry the practical exchange. Characters speak about what they need, what they saw, what they will do. A line can carry the unsaid: \"You left it out again.\" \"It was empty this morning.\" \"Then it was not empty.\" Let the exchange stop before it becomes explanation. Do not use dialogue to summarize the fantasy premise for the audience. If a character does not understand what happened, show the uncertainty in what they do next, not in a speech about confusion.\n\nUse repetition to change emphasis. The same staged action may appear twice: the first time routine, the second time refusal or grief. The stage direction records the difference in the handling of the object or the pause before the action. Do not announce the emotional change in the dialogue. Let the repeated business carry it.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation when the contract requires it. Keep it separate from the spoken lines. A monologue or aside may be used when the scene needs a performed speech, but it is not the default destination for thought. Another character does not hear what the focal character thinks merely because the reader has access to the note. The note can be brief and physical: the memory of the cold handle, the cost of the promise, the sound the floor made.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed action, a pending task, or a plain acknowledgment. The fantasy premise may remain unexplained. What matters is that the staged action and dialogue have made its consequence intelligible, and that the next scene can begin from what the characters now know or must do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the fantasy through a particular authorized mind's associative movement. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. The impossible appears as part of that movement: the mind notices the door that was not open, remembers the cold handle, feels the draft, returns to the cost of the promise. Do not stop the flow to explain what the magic is.\n\nKeep the associations anchored in concrete objects and actions. Name the tool, the food, the weather, the exact thing someone carries or refuses. A thought can move from the sound of the floor to the day the floor was mended to the person who did not come back. The syntax may follow that movement, but each step remains a particular thing, not an abstraction. Do not let the stream dissolve into general feeling or lose the distinction between what happened, what is remembered, and what is imagined.\n\nUse repetition to expose a refusal to move on. The same perception or phrase may return with a change in emphasis: the first time it is routine, the second time it is grief, the third time it is a bargain. The repetition belongs to the mind's movement, not to a narrator's summary. Show the return through the concrete detail: the bowl, the key, the cold water, the sound the door made.\n\nLet the fantastic premise affect the mind's practical attention. The character may think about what must be done, what was promised, what the neighbor said, what the body feels. The impossible is not a separate wonder to be decoded; it is part of the same attention that notices the cost of the cow and the weight of the bucket. Keep the consequence intelligible through that attention. If the mind cannot explain what happened, show the uncertainty in the repeated perception or the unfinished thought, not in a speech about confusion.\n\nPreserve orientation. Enough scene facts must remain distinct from recollection and fantasy. The reader should know whether the door is open now or was open then, whether the voice was heard or remembered. Do not make every scene fact indeterminate. The stream of consciousness is a method of attention, not a license to blur events.\n\nKeep access limited to the authorized mind. Another character's thought does not enter the stream merely because the style is selected. The focal mind may observe another person's action and interpret it, but the interpretation remains the focal character's own. Let the passage end on a completed perception, a pending concern, or a plain acknowledgment. The fantasy premise may remain partially unexplained. What matters is that the mind's movement has made its consequence intelligible through concrete particulars.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the fantasy through readable sender-attributed messages. Each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: some write short practical lines, some delay, some edit, some leave a draft unsent. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter.\n\nEstablish the fantastic premise through what people transmit and what they do not. A message says the door was open. Another says it was not. A photo shows the bowl empty. A delay before the answer carries the unsaid. The consequence belongs to the exchange: someone asks what must be done, someone refuses, someone sends the same instruction twice. Do not add a message explaining what the magic is. Let the next message or the next interface event show the result.\n\nKeep the messages economical and concrete. Name the object, the task, the time, the place. A message can be a single line: \"The key is not there.\" \"I left it on the table.\" \"Then who moved it?\" Let the exchange stop before it becomes explanation. Do not use messages to summarize the fantasy premise for the reader. If a character does not understand what happened, show the uncertainty in what they type, delete, or do not send.\n\nUse repetition to change emphasis. The same message may appear twice: the first time routine, the second time refusal or grief. The interface records the difference: the timestamp, the edit, the read receipt, the unsent draft. Do not announce the emotional change in the text. Let the repeated message or the delay carry it.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. Preserve required interiority in the active presentation when the contract requires it. Another character does not know what the focal character thinks merely because the reader has access to the note. The note can be brief and physical: the memory of the cold handle, the cost of the promise, the sound the floor made.\n\nLet the exchange end on a completed task, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment. The fantasy premise may remain unexplained. What matters is that the messages and interface events have made its consequence intelligible, and that the next exchange can begin from what the characters now know or must do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the fantasy as a scene-oriented visual novel passage. Use concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Background or expression cues are purposeful references, not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nEstablish the fantastic premise through what the scene shows and the focal character handles. The scene-setting names the object, the surface, the light, the exact thing someone carries or refuses. The dialogue carries the practical exchange. The focal narration records the concrete perception: the cold handle, the sound the floor made, the cost of the promise. Do not stop the scene to explain what the magic is. Let the consequence appear in the next action or the next line.\n\nKeep the dialogue direct and economical. Characters speak about what they need, what they saw, what they will do. A line can carry the unsaid: \"You left it out again.\" \"It was empty this morning.\" \"Then it was not empty.\" Let the exchange stop before it becomes explanation. Do not use dialogue to summarize the fantasy premise for the reader. If a character does not understand what happened, show the uncertainty in what they do next, not in a speech about confusion.\n\nUse repetition to change emphasis. The same scene detail may appear twice: the first time routine, the second time refusal or grief. The focal narration records the difference in the handling of the object or the pause before the action. Do not announce the emotional change in the dialogue. Let the repeated detail carry it.\n\nUse background or expression cues only when they orient the moment. A changed expression can draw attention, but it does not certify what a character feels or knows. The scene-setting and focal narration carry the interpretive weight. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits.\n\nPreserve required interiority in the active presentation when the contract requires it. The focal narration can include brief private reasoning, memory, or bodily sensation. Keep it separate from dialogue. Another character does not know what the focal character thinks merely because the reader has access to the narration. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed action, a pending task, or a plain acknowledgment. The fantasy premise may remain unexplained. What matters is that the scene-setting, dialogue, and focal narration have made its consequence intelligible, and that the next scene can begin from what the characters now know or must do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nWrite the fantasy as an installment-oriented web serial. Maintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. The fantastic premise is an established condition, not a puzzle to be solved. Let it affect practical work, relationships, resources, and decisions across installments.\n\nRecover context briefly only when it helps. When an earlier particular becomes relevant, name it exactly: the door that was left open, the promise made at the river, the bowl that was empty. Do not retell the whole history. The recovered detail should be enough for re-entry and should carry its consequence into the current installment.\n\nKeep the prose concrete and economical. Name the tool, the food, the weather, the exact thing someone carries or refuses. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let dialogue stay direct and practical. A character can say what must be done, ask what was seen, or answer with a refusal. Repetition can change emphasis: the same task done twice may mean obligation the first time and grief or resistance the second. What remains unsaid does the emotional work.\n\nUse earned developments. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A new fact can qualify an old understanding without replacing the old event. A relationship consequence may remain unsettled after a practical question is answered. Do not invent a reversal or a cliffhanger merely to end the installment. The scene can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.\n\nLet ordinary life continue around the fantastic condition. People still eat, work, argue, travel, and keep promises. The impossible belongs to that life rather than interrupting it as a separate wonder. Show its consequence through the practical exchange: the debt is paid, the journey is delayed, the household rule is kept or broken.\n\nPreserve required interiority in the active presentation when the contract requires it. A character's private reasoning can be brief and physical: they think of the cold water, the cost of the cow, the sound the door made. Keep access limited to the authorized mind. Another character does not know what the focal character has decided merely because the reader does.\n\nLet the installment end on a completed action, a pending task, or a plain acknowledgment. The fantasy premise may remain partially unexplained. What matters is that the serial has given selected facts room to acquire meaning, and that the next installment can begin from what the characters now know or must do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nMake the impossible element a debt that already exists between the characters when the scene begins. It may be a power one of them used, refused, or failed to control, and the other may have paid for it, witnessed it, or been left to explain it. Let the wonder change what they owe each other, not only what they can do. The scene should return to that debt through the present exchange: a need that cannot be asked for cleanly, an apology that arrives too late or in the wrong form, a withheld fact the other person already suspects.\n\nBuild the scene around exposure that costs something. Do not stage a general confession. Let one character risk a particular admission because the conversation has made silence harder, then watch the other respond with anger, care, fatigue, or a question that exposes a second wound. The strain should alter the actual talk: a speaker may interrupt, reverse a claim, offer sudden tenderness, or realize they have said more than they intended. Afterward, let the behavior show the cost. Someone may stay close but stop speaking, perform a small service with too much force, or leave a door open while refusing to look back.\n\nUse thought that argues with itself about the debt. A character may defend what they did with the wonder, then accuse themselves of the defense, then notice that their accuser is also afraid. Keep the self-argument specific to the fantasy situation: what the power took, what it proved, what the other person now sees. A plain remark from the other character can cut through the elaborate justification without resolving it. The private thought may be visible or hidden according to the active contract, but it must remain the character's actual nonspoken experience, not a summary of the argument.\n\nLet the impossible remain partly unexplained. The characters may know what the wonder does without agreeing on what it means, and that disagreement can become the wound itself. Comfort may arrive as a partial, awkward gesture that leaves the deeper hurt untouched. Warmth and pain should occupy the same room without canceling each other. The style does not require a tragic ending, but it does require that the emotional cost remain present even when someone chooses to stay.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nKeep the scene to a coherent shared interval with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Give each significant speech a clear speaker and follow it with a readable focal reaction: a pause, a gesture, a shift in posture, a line of private thought where the active contract permits it. The fantasy premise should be a concrete fact in the exchange, not a background decoration. Let it appear as something one speaker used, refused, owes, or fears, and let that fact shape what they are willing to say.\n\nUse the Dostoevsky profile as pressure inside the exchange, not as a lecture. Choose one specific point of self-justification and let the dialogue work on it. A character may defend a choice in two or three sentences, then reverse themselves when the other speaker answers with a plain objection. The reversal should be visible in the next line or focal reaction: the speaker may accuse themselves, shift from pride to shame, or notice that the justification sounded weaker aloud than it did in their head. Keep the movement short enough for collaborative reading. One self-accusation or one sudden shift can carry the profile's weight without a long philosophical speech.\n\nPreserve the active narrator and player-control settings. Write focal thought only where the contract permits it, and keep other characters' minds outside the narration. The fantasy premise may create a practical pressure, such as a cost already paid or a choice still open, but it should not force the scene into a decision prompt. Let the continuation point follow from the scene's actual pressure: a question left hanging, a gesture not yet answered, or a character about to speak. The scene may end while a character is still choosing what to say, and the next turn can enter that opening naturally.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Organize the story as a sequence of linked incidents in which the same impossible element or its consequence reappears. Each episode should advance one practical situation: a debt, a journey, a quarrel, a household obligation, a public accusation. Let the fantasy premise enter through that ordinary pressure rather than through a separate mythic frame. In each episode, show one concrete way the wonder changes what the characters can do or what they owe, and let that change create the next practical problem.\n\nGive the narrator a storyteller's connective voice between episodes. The narrator may mark the passage of time, recall an earlier incident, or point to a change in a character's reputation. Keep this voice concrete and local to the supplied setting and community. Do not import a fixed period, cast, or national temperament. The connective passage should not explain the moral of the episode; it should carry the reader from one incident to the next while making the accumulating consequence legible.\n\nPlace Dostoevskian pressure inside the episodes. In each episode, give the focal character one specific self-justification tied to the fantasy element: what it permitted, what it cost, what it proved about them. Let a plain neighbor, servant, companion, or creditor unsettle that justification with a concrete objection. The character may reverse their own argument within the episode or discover that their pride has become visible to others. Do not repeat the same self-accusation in every episode. Instead, let each incident expose a different layer of the same underlying claim, so the character's defense changes as the practical situation changes.\n\nLet the episodic structure accumulate consequence. A choice made in one episode can return as a reputation, a debt, or a changed relationship in a later one. The final episode need not resolve every question, but it should make the accumulated weight of the wonder and the character's self-argument felt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move from one concrete detail to the next without explanatory padding. Let the fantasy premise appear as a physical fact with a visible cost: a marked door, a burned hand, a tool that works only after a payment, a substance that leaves a stain. Show the cost in material terms before the character names it. The impossible element should have practical consequences for work, shelter, travel, or safety, and those consequences should remain visible in the scene.\n\nGround Dostoevskian pressure in material conditions. Tie the character's self-justification to what they own, owe, broke, or cannot afford to lose. Let a longer thought appear only when the character's reasoning needs room: a decision about whether to use the wonder, a memory of what it cost before, or an argument with themselves about what they deserve. Keep the thought concrete. Instead of abstract shame, show the character looking at the damaged object or the unpaid debt while the accusation forms. Let the thought reverse itself by returning to the physical evidence: the character may defend the act, then see the mark it left and accuse themselves of the defense.\n\nUse high contrast and changes of rhythm. A short, hard sentence can follow a longer passage of reasoning. A plain speaker can cut through an elaborate defense with one concrete objection. Let abrasive or rough speech belong to a specific speaker and situation, not to every character. The rhythm change should do narrative work: it can mark the moment a justification collapses, a decision becomes final, or a speaker stops pretending.\n\nThe fantasy element may be dangerous, but the style does not turn every scene into survival horror. The wonder can also be useful, familiar, or necessary. The pressure comes from what it costs and what the character believes about themselves for using or refusing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the story through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene requires: a letter to a relative, a journal entry written after a quarrel, a report filed to an institution, a note left beside an object. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Each document should carry one specific moment in the story, and the choice of form should affect what the writer can say.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure live inside the documents. A letter may begin as a justification and reverse into self-accusation. A journal entry may record what the writer did with the impossible element, then argue about what the act means, then stop before admitting the worst part. A report may conceal the writer's fear behind official language while a later note reveals it. The intended recipient shapes what the writer dares to say: a letter to a superior may be guarded, a letter to a sibling may be reckless, a private journal may be the only place a confession can begin. Do not make every document confess. Some writers may defend themselves to the end, and the pressure may appear in what they omit or revise.\n\nMake the fantasy premise a fact the documents circle. One writer may describe the wonder as a gift, another as a curse, another as a debt. Let gaps and conflicting accounts reveal the event across entries. A later document may correct an earlier one, or may show that the writer misunderstood what they saw. Preserve what each document's author knew at the time of composition; later knowledge cannot be retroactively inserted into an earlier entry. Use the conflict between documents to show the fantasy premise's emotional weight: the same act may become proof of worth in one account and an accusation in another.\n\nKeep readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. The reader should assemble the sequence from the documents themselves. A material trace, such as a crossed-out line or a letter never sent, can carry as much weight as a full confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness and practical care. Show a character preparing food, mending something, remembering a preference, or offering a small comfort that fits the fantasy world. Choose one concrete act of care and let it occupy the scene's attention. The impossible element should be present as a shared fact tied to that care: a wonder that lights the room, a creature that needs feeding, a charm that eases a chore, a burden one character carries and another helps bear. Let the care respond to the fantasy premise's actual weight rather than ignoring it.\n\nLet Dostoevskian pressure appear as private doubt or a small disagreement, not as a crisis. A character may believe they do not deserve the care they are receiving, or may argue with themselves about whether to accept help. Keep the doubt specific to the fantasy situation: what the character did with the wonder, what they fear it made them, or what they believe the other person now sees. Another character may answer that doubt with a plain, sincere gesture rather than a philosophical rebuttal. The doubt need not be fully resolved. A character may remain proud or uncertain while still accepting a cup of tea or a steadying hand.\n\nLet affection be sincere without erasing the fantasy premise's weight. The wonder may still be dangerous, costly, or strange, but the scene can hold that weight beside warmth. A character may be afraid of what they can do and still be glad someone stayed. The style does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. It lets particular people care for each other inside those problems.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration move from a public scene to a private one, from a remembered event to its present consequence, or from one character's account to another's. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language: the sentences may be more composed, the judgments more measured, the social context more fully drawn. The transitions should do narrative work, carrying the reader from the public interpretation of the fantasy premise to the private cost it imposes.\n\nFrame the fantasy premise through social context, history, or judgment where the narrator permits it. The impossible element may be a matter of public rumor, institutional record, family memory, or civic dispute. The narration can show how different people in the community interpret the wonder: as a blessing, a scandal, a sign, or a burden. Let one public interpretation create a concrete social consequence: a reputation changed, an obligation imposed, a door closed, a debt called in. Keep the setting and community actually supplied; do not import an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary automatically.\n\nPlace Dostoevskian pressure inside the characters' private thought and speech. A character may defend an act in public and accuse themselves in private. Let a plain speaker unsettle an elaborate argument. The narration may enter a character's mind to show the self-argument turning, reversing, and entangling itself, while other characters remain outside that access. The private thought should respond to the public consequence: the character may argue that the community misunderstood the act, then admit that the community saw something true, then accuse themselves of caring about the misunderstanding. The social frame should not replace the private pressure; it should give the pressure a public consequence.\n\nLet the fantasy premise remain partly unexplained. The narration may judge what people do with the wonder without explaining every mechanism. A scene may close on an unresolved moral question, a relationship changed but not settled, or a character still arguing with themselves.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen on the occasion, not the tale: a specific room, hour, and relationship in which one character needs something from another — time, shelter, money, permission, a verdict delayed — and telling a story is the available instrument. Let the world's fantastic premise already shape that need: the debt may be owed to something impossible, the shelter necessary because of a wonder, the verdict delivered by an institution the premise created. The teller selects, omits, and arranges; the telling is an intervention, not neutral entertainment.\n\nInside the embedded tale, keep the teller's voice present as an arguing conscience. A figure in the tale may voice the conviction the teller is trying to disown, or an episode can repeat the outer dilemma in another costume without becoming clean allegory. Let the tale leak: an excuse that sounds weaker aloud, a victim described with too much precision, a generous act that also served the teller. Where the narration reaches a mind, give teller or listener actual private experience — the listener registering what slipped out while keeping a courteous face, the teller hearing the story turn against them — rather than converting all awareness into performance.\n\nKeep the levels legible and porous. Mark entry into the tale by a shift in address or rhythm, and let the outer world interrupt it: a practical question about timing or sequence, a competing interpretation of the inner events, a silence the teller has to fill. Carry one object, phrase, or gesture across the levels so it accrues meaning as evidence of what the telling costs — never as proof the tale is true. If a wonder appears in the embedded story, show the choice or institution it altered, and let the outer characters weigh that consequence against their present need.\n\nStop where the scene's own boundary falls — mid-telling, mid-question, or in the breath after an unintended admission — rather than holding the reply open for a confession, a completed tale, or a settled decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the Romantic-uncanny route. Ground the fantastic premise in one specific place — a house, a workshop, a stretch of road, a civic institution, a natural feature — whose behavior is impossible but whose local rules the people around it have learned to live with. Let the place be known through use rather than exposition: who avoids the road and at what hour, which tasks the workshop makes possible, what the institution now requires because of what lives there.\n\nNarrate at a distance that shifts. Composed observation of the place can give way to close, agitated rendering of a focal mind and return, so that the uncanny object and the self-argument press on each other and each makes the other harder to dismiss. Give the place moral weight without turning it into a master symbol: a room can be cold because the heating fails and because someone has stopped caring for it, and both readings can stand in the same sentence. Set one character who treats the wonder as ordinary against another who treats it as a judgment on their life; the disagreement is a collision of convictions about what is owed and what is deserved, not superstition against reason.\n\nWhen a character argues with themselves, let the syntax carry the motion: a confident sentence, a subordinate clause that reverses it, a remembered accusation inserted into a present plan, a plain practical observation that undercuts an elaborate justification. Not everyone speaks in paragraphs — a quiet sentence from a listener can be the thing a long defense cannot answer.\n\nKeep the uncanny material. Sounds, light, and the condition of objects earn their place by affecting a choice or exposing neglect. Let the reply end where the scene's turn falls — mid-errand, mid-conversation, or in the middle of a thought that has just become dangerous — without waiting for the wonder to be explained or the conflict to stabilize.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the scene where an old authority — a family, guild, temple, civic order, or inherited obligation — still operates inside a present urban world that has grown around it and partly decayed it. Make the fantasy premise a fact of that authority: a power, prohibition, or condition that decides who may live where, who owes what to whom, and what people hide to keep their place. The old authority here is a social and moral structure this world's own premise permits; do not import vampire physiology, faction politics, or blood-bond mechanics.\n\nLet urban material detail carry the emotional pressure. Describe the specific condition of a stairwell, a shared kitchen, a locked room, a repaired object, a public notice, and let each detail register shame or pride: the worn step someone cannot afford to fix, the room kept spotless as the only remaining proof of standing. Atmosphere should change how this particular encounter is experienced, not decorate it — the same conversation in a warm kitchen and a freezing corridor is a different event.\n\nUnmask unevenly. In trusted company a character may confess a desire while concealing the act it produced; another may defend an institution they privately despise because their bread depends on it. Let self-argument accuse, reverse into justification, and then expose a simpler motive by accident. A plain speaker's practical question can stop a long rationalization cold.\n\nKeep intimacy morally complicated: affection, loyalty, and dependence coexisting with resentment, debt, and the fear of exposure. When talk turns toward confession, keep the material stakes in the room — what would be lost, who else would suffer, what the old authority would do with the knowledge. Let the reply stop at the scene's own boundary — mid-negotiation, mid-accusation, or in the silence after a mask has slipped — without holding out for full confession, betrayal, or settlement of the inherited conflict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route, told at a specific community occasion: a household gathering, a journey's halt, a festival, a work site, a dispute being settled, a vigil. Keep the language, region, and community exactly as the story supplies them; do not force English, code-switching, or a generic family structure onto the scene. The teller speaks from a known position — age, kinship, trade, debt — and the listeners attend for practical reasons: a decision to make together, an obligation to honor or dodge, a reputation to protect.\n\nLet the tale behave like recited epic rather than private anecdote. Genealogy, repeated epithets, formal praise and blame, and recognized openings give the telling a shape the community already trusts, and the teller can lean on that shape to make an old episode sound like destiny. Inside the tale, the fantastic premise is an established fact with social consequences: a wonder that changed a marriage, a harvest, a boundary, or a legal duty, and the accommodations people have lived with since. Let one episode cut against the teller's stated purpose — a debt omitted, a generous act that also served the teller's household, a wonder whose cost the community is still paying.\n\nRender the teller as an arguing conscience. Confidence, reversal, an accusation aimed at an absent party, self-accusation, a sudden awareness of the listeners' faces. A single plain question from an elder, a child, or a laborer can expose what the ornate telling was avoiding. Where the narration reaches a mind, give teller or listener real private thought; the tale is spoken, but the thinking around it is not a performance.\n\nBind the levels with a repeated phrase, object, or name the community already owns, so recurrence shows what the telling costs rather than proving the tale true. Stop where the occasion's own boundary falls — mid-tale, mid-question, mid-deliberation — without waiting for the dispute to settle, the tale to conclude, or the teller to confess.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tales route. Present the impossible element as something the world's ordinary life already accommodates: a spirit, transformation, curse, or uncanny occurrence that people have practical ways of living with, avoiding, propitiating, or serving. Do not explain the wonder into a system. Show its consequences in work, family, travel, or local obligation — if it changed who may enter a place or what a household owes, show that rule operating in the present scene, not only remembered.\n\nLet the narration move between direct observation and a focal character's inward argument. State a feeling plainly, then let the character question it, accuse someone, reverse the accusation, or notice a small detail that undoes a long justification. Treat omission as an active tool: the character circles a debt, betrayal, or desire without naming it, and the circling becomes visible in the rhythm of the thought — what gets repeated, what gets dropped, where the sentence turns away. Another person's plain sentence can cut through the spiral without ending it.\n\nKeep the telling shaped rather than exhaustive. Choose a few concrete particulars — a doorway, a meal, a task, a change in the light — and let them carry the weight, each one affecting a choice or marking what has been neglected. No required quietness, seasonal imagery, or fixed landscape: direct feeling, anger, blunt argument, and comic exaggeration are all available where the character and scene permit.\n\nThe reply can end while the character is still circling the avoided thing, or while the wonder is visibly altering the terms of a relationship on the page. Do not hold the ending for recognition, confession, or a completed account of the impossible; stop at the scene's natural boundary, even mid-thought.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological or urban fiction route. Set the scene in a specific city, town, or neighborhood whose material conditions are present in the prose: rent, work, transport, food, noise, the state of a building, the history of a street or family. Keep the language, community, and history particular as the story supplies them. The fantasy premise is an established fact of this world, and it presses directly on those conditions — who can work where, what a household owes, how a journey is made, what an institution demands.\n\nFocus the narration through one character's inward life while the social world keeps pressing on it. Let the self-argument move: a confident justification, a reversal, a remembered accusation inserted into a present plan, shame turning into pride and then into a simpler fear. Keep it grounded rather than detachable monologue — a neighbor watching from a doorway, an unpaid bill on the table, a child's question, the smell of a particular street can each redirect the thought, because the argument is about this life, not about the world in general.\n\nGive the other characters distinct moral voices and specific knowledge. A plain speaker's practical observation can expose the flaw in an elaborate defense. Let affection, dependence, resentment, and the fear of exposure coexist in the same relationship without every intimacy curdling into betrayal; what each person conceals should be particular — an amount, an act, a name — not a general condition.\n\nTreat the fantastic as ordinary in the sense that people have learned to live with it, and never let that ordinariness mute its consequences: a wonder that changed a family's history or a block's rules is still felt in today's rent, shifts, and favors. Let the reply end where the scene breaks — mid-errand, mid-exchange, mid-argument — without waiting for a confession, a reversal, or the pressure to resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Within the first lines, establish where the characters are, what they are doing, and what the fantasy premise is already doing to their practical lives — a rule that decides who may enter the building, a power that makes the task possible or dangerous, a condition that must be met before nightfall. Do not open with a sustained explanation of the world; show the impossible through a concrete effect on today's problem. Keep spatial movement easy to follow by naming each change of place, and break paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader always knows who spoke and what changed.\n\nUse short, focused reaction beats for Dostoevskian interiority. After a line of dialogue or a visible event, give the focal character a compact burst of thought that defends, accuses, reverses, or entangles itself — pride that turns to shame, shame that re-justifies the original act, justification that finally notices a simpler fear. Anchor each beat to the immediate scene: a specific object, a specific debt, a specific person watching. The thought stays a beat, not a lecture, and a plain sentence from another character can cut a long rationalization short.\n\nWhen a mechanic or unfamiliar name matters, present it in the moment, through what the character must do or avoid — no glossary pause. If the wonder has reshaped an institution or a relationship, show that change operating in the present action rather than only in backstory.\n\nVary the register. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner; the cast is not uniformly witty and the scene is not obliged to end on a hook. Stop where the scene's turn falls — a question hanging, a move half-made, a new pressure visible — and leave the next action open without a completed test, confession, or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the established fantastic premise press on a character's self-justification, and carry that pressure as rhythm rather than declaration. When a magical fact changes what someone can do or know, follow the thought that tries to absorb it, deny it, or turn it into proof of worth. Write sentences that perform the entanglement: one can open with the impossible thing observed plainly, turn toward the character's defense of a decision, and end in a smaller admission the character did not plan to make. A clause that begins as accusation may slide into self-accusation; a sentence launched with certainty may trail into a question.\n\nDraw recurring images from the immediate scene—light on water, a locked door, a stain that will not wash out—and return to one when the character's reasoning reverses, so the image changes meaning without your explaining the change. Keep every figure traceable to what this character actually sees, remembers, or cannot stop noticing; the fantastic fact is not a metaphor for their feelings but a condition they must think inside. Let plain statements provide contrast and rest within the rhythm, and do not let every character speak in the narrator's figurative register.\n\nAllow plain speech to interrupt the lyrical movement. Another character's short, practical sentence can break an elaborate inward passage; register the interruption as a change in the prose—a shortened line, a held beat—rather than absorbing it into the same elevated tone. The argument need not be resolved when the scene ends: the passage may close on the changed image, on a question the character has finally asked aloud or privately, or on the ordinary world continuing while the thought still turns.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTreat the established fantastic premise as a boundary between what was possible before and what is possible now. A character may remember when the magical fact first entered their life, or return to a place where its consequences are still visible. Make the reflection an examination rather than a mourning: what the character believed about themselves then, what they believe now, with self-reproach surfacing through remembered details instead of through a present argument.\n\nUse small irreversible changes as the material of reflection: a familiar object altered by the premise, a relationship that shifted after someone gained or lost an ability, a phrase that once meant one thing and now means another. When the narrator reflects directly, attach the reflection to a specific occasion so the coexistence of present experience with what is no longer available stays particular rather than becoming an announcement about impermanence. Let ordinary pleasure remain pleasurable—a remembered kindness, a warm room, a moment of unexpected ease can stand beside regret without being undercut.\n\nThe Dostoevskian pressure is the character's inability to leave the past alone: a remembered justification gets reexamined, a former certainty begins to look self-serving, and the argument with the younger self can reverse more than once. The fantasy element remains a fact with consequences, not a figure for loss. A passage can end on a detail or memory that has shifted meaning, with the character's self-assessment unfinished—but it does not have to; the present moment may simply continue, still worth having.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the framed oral telling as the definite route. Situate a teller recounting an event that involved the established fantastic premise to a particular audience, and make the telling itself the scene: where it happens, who is present, what prompts it. Let the teller's self-justification emerge through the craft of the account—the order in which details are revealed, the digressions the teller permits themselves, the phrases repeated, the pauses where a response was expected. The audience supplies social pressure: a question, an expression of doubt, or a silence at the wrong moment can redirect the confession.\n\nLet the nested or delayed structure carry the Dostoevskian argument. The teller may open with a version of events in which they appear justified, then complicate it with a remembered detail that does not fit; a plain question from a listener can unsettle an elaborate explanation more effectively than an accusation would. Inside the told story the fantasy element stays a fact with consequences—what it let someone do, what it cost, who else knows—never a metaphor for the teller's guilt.\n\nKeep the frame distinct from the told account. The frame remains brief and concrete; the told account may grow more figurative or philosophical, but it stays traceable to this teller's memory, situation, and stake in being believed. Use the tradition's resources—recurrence, delayed revelation, an audience that can answer back—without importing a fixed setting, period, or religious framework, and keep the specific language or tradition distinct where the supplied world names one. The audience need not resolve the matter; the telling may stop while the teller's self-assessment is still unfinished, the frame resuming or not as the scene allows.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nState the established fantastic premise and its immediate consequence in direct clauses. Then let a character's self-justification move in short, exact steps: a claim, a remembered fact that contradicts it, a smaller admission. Select the few details that do real work—a changed object, a repeated gesture, a word someone will not say—and let one carry the weight of a longer argument. Keep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the present account.\n\nUse omission to leave something inferable. If a character avoids a subject, show the avoidance through what they say instead, not through a paragraph explaining what is being avoided. Do not delete causality: the reader must still be able to infer why a reversal or a silence happens. Let an essential thought run long enough to make sense—one precise extended sentence may serve better than several performed fragments—and keep voices individual. A plain speaker's short sentence can unsettle an elaborate self-defense; a single direct clause can register that it landed.\n\nThe fantasy element is a fact with consequences, not a symbol. A passage may close on a detail or a short admission that leaves the next move inferable rather than announced, and it may equally stop mid-exchange or mid-action at the scene's actual boundary, with nothing summed up.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which the established fantastic premise is a material condition of the world, as concrete as rent, weather, or a family obligation. When a character encounters or uses that premise, treat the encounter as an event with practical consequences: it may alter a schedule, create a debt, expose a difference in status, or force a choice about what to tell someone else. Do not let the magic become a metaphor or a decorative backdrop. Its consequences should be legible in the scene's actions and decisions.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure arise through the character's effort to justify, explain, or disown those consequences. When the narration moves from outward action to inward experience, anchor the shift in a specific scene detail: a look, a delay, a familiar object, a phrase someone has just spoken. The inner experience should be the character's actual private thought, rendered as self-argument rather than summary. A character may defend a decision, accuse themselves, reverse their own reasoning, or entangle a practical matter with a larger claim about who they are. Another character's plain sentence can interrupt that elaborate justification, and the scene should register the interruption: the speaker may answer, fall silent, or redirect the conversation while the private argument continues.\n\nUse the contemporary novel's flexible attention to move among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience without recap machinery. Paragraphing and transitions should orient the reader through the scene's immediate movement. When a conversation begins with a practical matter, let a single remark turn it toward self-defense or accusation, but do not require every exchange to become a philosophical speech. The register may shift with the scene: a formal narrator can describe an ordinary errand, or an intimate narrator can observe a public confrontation. The fantasy element remains a fact with consequences throughout, even when the characters are not discussing it.\n\nClose the scene according to the actual moment, not a required lesson or reversal. Business may conclude, a question may remain open, or a character may still be arguing with themselves while the ordinary day continues around them. The response boundary can occur during continuing speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup. Do not require the character to reach a resolution, change their plan, or produce a result before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through the scenes you select and the moments you return to. Treat the fantastic premise as one of the conditions the relationship lives inside: an ability one person has, a consequence they share, a debt or promise the magical fact has created. Let affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery open a Dostoevskian self-argument—a character offers comfort and then wonders whether they offered it for the other person's sake or their own; a moment of closeness is followed by a remembered slight they cannot stop reexamining.\n\nBuild the relationship's emotional grammar from private language and repeated moments. A phrase, a gesture, or a shared reference can return at a later point with changed meaning, earned by what has happened between the two people; use callbacks and their consequences to fulfill the particular promise of the supplied premise rather than inserting a roster of familiar tropes. Give the emotional beat enough space to land—slow the scene, let the reaction arrive in stages—while keeping the self-argument attached to the relationship's actual history.\n\nDo not make the cast uniformly witty or combative; a plain sentence can carry more weight than a polished retort, and cooperation is as available as friction. The fantasy element remains a fact with consequences, not a metaphor for the relationship. A scene may end with a confession unfinished, a comfort accepted or refused, or a private thought that changes what the character will say next, and none of these needs to be resolved within the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling: deliberate openings, compact declarations, recurring phrasing. Introduce a deed involving the established fantastic premise by what it will be remembered as, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. The Dostoevskian pressure lives in the gap between the public account and the private self-assessment: the chronicle says what was done, while the focal character knows what they believed, feared, or refused to admit at the time, and the narration can show that self-argument without overturning the public record.\n\nUse recurrence to weigh consequence. A name, a phrase, or a place returns when a later event gives it a new application, and the relation of an act to what will be remembered stays concrete: who still tells the story, who disputes a detail, who will not speak of it. Keep shared reputation distinct from private experience—being remembered as steadfast does not mean the character felt steadfast. Let dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority: a plain speaker may object to one detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, without the exchange becoming parody or ceremonial speech.\n\nThe fantasy element remains a fact with consequences, not a metaphor for glory or doom, and the telling need not import a Nordic pantheon or heroic fatalism. The account can end with the deed concluded or its consequence still unfolding, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story in a folktale-inflected Scandinavian narrative voice: a composed, slightly formal teller who knows the place, its weather, its work, and the inherited sayings that surround the fantastic premise. Establish the impossible thing early as an ordinary local fact — something the community names, bargains with, avoids, or carries into everyday tasks — so the wonder sits inside work seasons, debts, and household obligations rather than arriving as an intrusion. Let the teller report what is known and what is remembered, give the community's proverbs and rules their full weight, and stop at the edge of private minds.\n\nBeneath that calm telling, give the focal character a live inward pressure. When the narration draws close to the character, write the self-argument as present nonspoken interiority in the active presentation: the character defending a choice, accusing themselves, reversing a judgment, or entangling two convictions until neither can be held cleanly. Let the tale's frame open and close the episode in its own voice, but never summarize the inner argument away. The teller may record what the argument left visible — words said aloud, an act done or refused, what the neighbors remember — while the interiority carries the pressure as it happens, and the space between those two layers is where the story lives.\n\nGive the fantastic premise practical consequences the community partly knows how to manage and can never wholly master. A bargain touches a household debt, a haunting changes a work route, a changed animal complicates an inheritance. Do not explain the impossible thing into a system; let it stay strange while its effects remain concrete and enforceable. Let the focal character use the community's rule to justify themselves in one breath and despise it in the next, and let another character's plain practical remark cut through an elaborate self-defense without settling the moral question on anyone's behalf.\n\nRoot the telling in the particular Scandinavian setting the story actually supplies — its seasons, food, labor, legal or parish expectations, domestic arrangements — and keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts distinct where they differ rather than blending them into one generic north. Do not lean on snow, silence, reserve, or isolation as automatic atmosphere. The telling may be slow and cumulative, with a repeated phrase or image gathering new weight as the character's understanding changes; repetition should deepen the obligation rather than restart the same argument. The episode may close with the character still carrying the unresolved debt, or with a small completed action that leaves the larger question open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nWrite in screenplay form with concise scene headings, readable action lines, and speaker-led dialogue blocks. Establish the fantastic premise through what each scene shows and what the cast says about it — a changed animal in the yard, a door that opens onto the wrong season, a debt owed to something with no body — and let its consequences appear in practical arrangements: a payment set out, a route changed, a room no one enters. Keep the impossible thing visible or audible whenever it is present; do not explain it in speech when a staged fact can carry it.\n\nBuild the focal character's pressure into the exchanges themselves. Give the character a justification to defend; a plain practical question from another speaker can strip it, and the character can take up the opposite justification without acknowledging the reversal. Let self-image tangle with material need, so the character argues principle while counting what principle costs. Do not let every exchange become a philosophical speech — the argument should grow out of the work at hand and the relationships in the room, and a short flat line can unsettle a long one.\n\nUse action lines to disagree with dialogue. While the character argues, show what the hands do: feeding what must be fed, keeping the payment ready, refusing to look toward the impossible thing, turning a task into a delay. Let a pause, a turned back, or a repeated physical act carry private pressure without voice-over. When private thought is required, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation — not as dialogue another character can hear, and not as knowledge the cast shares.\n\nKeep transitions selective: a cut when location or time changes and the new scene must be understood at once, offscreen sound only when it changes what the characters know or do, and no page-filling camera commands. The sequence may end on a completed action, a pending question, or a character left alone with the impossible thing; do not require a reversal or a confession at each scene boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speaker names, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions a stage can perform. Make the fantastic premise a practical condition of the stage world — a door that must stay shut, a payment left on the threshold, a person who returns changed — handled through ordinary business: setting a table, mending a coat, laying out coins, refusing to cross a marked line. Let what the audience can see, reach, and hear define what is possible in the room; the impossible thing works through its staged effects.\n\nGive the focal character pressure through public speech against ongoing business. Let the character defend an action to one person, accuse themselves to another, and reverse a justification within a single exchange without acknowledging the turn, while the practical task continues underneath. Let self-image collide with material need in front of witnesses: what the character claims to owe beside what the ledger, the larder, or the marked line says. A plain practical line from another character can cut through an elaborate justification mid-task; do not let it settle the moral question on that speaker's behalf, and do not hand every character a philosophical monologue — the argument grows from the immediate relationship and the work of the scene.\n\nUse pauses, silences, and repeated actions to register private pressure without converting every thought into an aside. Reserve monologue and aside for moments with a dramatic purpose in the scene — a public confession, a deliberate address — and keep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, never disguised as speech another character can hear.\n\nKeep stage directions economical and stageable, with no film-camera instructions. The scene may end on a completed action, an entrance, an exit, or a character left alone with the impossible thing; do not require a gathering of the whole cast or a theatrical revelation at each boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind, braiding perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Let the fantastic premise enter the flow as something already lived with rather than discovered: a remembered bargain, a changed body, a debt that returns in dreams, an object that no longer behaves. Do not stop the flow to explain it. Let it surface through association — a smell, a phrase, a task, a sudden silence pulls it into the present — and let its terms arrive in fragments whose combined weight the reader assembles.\n\nMake the self-argument move the way the mind moves. The character defends a choice, accuses themselves, reverses a judgment, entangles two convictions until neither can be held cleanly — but by interruption rather than orderly debate: a memory cuts off a justification, a bodily sensation overturns a resolution, a recurring phrase returns with its meaning changed. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow these turns — a sentence that argues itself into a dash, a resolution that restarts from its own first word. Keep enough orientation to separate what happens now from what is remembered or imagined: anchor each movement in a concrete perception, a place, or a task the reader can track.\n\nLet the premise's practical consequences appear as what attention keeps returning to: the debt counted again, the wound checked, the changed animal at the edge of the yard, the person who will not meet the character's eyes. The impossible thing remains strange while its effects stay concrete; it need not resolve into a system. Another character's plain remark may enter the flow and cut through an elaborate self-defense — and keep working on the mind afterward — but that character's private mind stays closed unless separately authorized. The technique belongs to this one focal mind; do not spread it across the cast or blur all scene facts into dream. The passage may end with the character still caught in the recurring concern, or with a small completed action that leaves the larger question open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as a readable, sender-attributed message exchange. Establish the fantastic premise through what people send and what they avoid sending: a photograph that should not exist, a voice message in the wrong voice, a payment request from a number not in the contacts, a repeated warning about a place or a person. Let the impossible thing be handled matter-of-factly through the logistics of the exchange — amounts, times, pickups, arrangements — so it stays strange while its effects stay practical. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter, and never fabricate an interface event just to add subtext.\n\nLocate the focal character's pressure in the gap between sent text, unsent draft, and withheld thought. The character defends themselves in one sent message, drafts an accusation or confession and deletes it, sends something deflectionary instead, then reverses the justification hours later in a follow-up. Let visible traces carry the reversal where they honestly exist: a long delay before a short reply, an edit that softens a line. Let self-image tangle with material need — what the character claims about the debt beside what the payment record shows. A plain practical message from another person can cut through an elaborate self-defense in one line.\n\nLet each person's actual digital habits shape their messages: one writes long paragraphs, another sends fragments, another leaves messages unread for hours, another replies only in voice notes. Distinguish a sent message from an unsent draft, and an unsent draft from live private thought: a draft is a composed artifact the character chose not to transmit, and it does not give the reader the whole working mind. When required private experience must appear, write it as clearly separated nonspoken context outside the transcript, not as a message anyone receives; no character knows what another only drafted or thought.\n\nShow the premise's consequences in the exchange itself — a missed appointment, a changed plan, a debt, a threat, a person who stops replying. Context outside the exchange may appear when needed, clearly separated from the transcript. The exchange may end on a sent message, an unsent draft, a delay, or a pending question; do not require a confession or a reversal at every boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in scene-oriented visual novel form: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Establish the fantastic premise through what the scene shows and what the cast says about it — a changed room, a person who returns different, an object that moves untouched, a debt owed to something with no face — and keep scene changes legible so the reader always knows where and when the moment sits.\n\nWork the focal character's pressure into the rhythm itself. Let a spoken exchange run in short readable lines, then let a focal reaction beat carry the private turn: the character hearing a line and privately refining a defense, or launching an accusation against themselves, or reversing the meaning of what they just agreed to. Let the inner response interrupt the dialogue where a real mind would — between one line and the next — while remaining the focal character's private experience, never knowledge the other characters hold. Let self-image collide with material need inside the scene's own stakes, and let another character's plain practical line cut through an elaborate self-defense. Not every exchange becomes a speech; the argument grows from the immediate relationship.\n\nUse background and expression cues as purposeful references that orient the moment — where the character is looking, what the room now contains, who has entered — not as automatic truth indicators and not as a mandatory label on every line. A changed expression can raise the focal character's suspicion or hope without certifying what anyone feels. Let the premise's consequences be visible in the scene: a household obligation, a changed work route, a person who will not enter a room, a payment left on a threshold; the impossible thing remains strange while its effects stay concrete.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene may end on a completed action, a pending question, or a character left alone with the impossible thing; do not require a reversal or a confession at every scene boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Establish the fantastic premise early — a bargain, a changed person, a place that takes something in return, a debt that follows the family — and let its consequences accumulate through earned developments in knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. Do not add leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules.\n\nLet the focal character's self-argument develop across installments rather than resolve inside each one. A justification defended in one reply can be quietly abandoned in a later reply under a new condition; an accusation made in private can return much later as a plea. Let ideology and self-image work as personal problems inside relationships and material conditions — what the character claims to owe, beside what the household, the creditor, or the thing itself actually collects. Keep the private side of the argument available in the narration as it forms, so a later admission or action reads as the result of pressure the reader has already followed. A plain practical remark from another character can cut through an elaborate self-defense in any installment.\n\nRecover context briefly, and only when it helps the reader re-enter: a particular object, debt, promise, or earlier conversation recalled in a sentence or two rather than a retelling of the whole history. Let a new installment change what an earlier fact suggests without replacing the earlier event or forcing the character to forget what has already been admitted. If a promise was made under one condition, the changed condition belongs to the record, and the next installment argues from it rather than erasing it.\n\nClose installments on earned developments and occasional hooks — completed work, a pending request, a changed understanding, plain acknowledgment of what is now known — without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Do not require a confession, a reversal, or a new discovery at every boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the established fantastic premise as a familiar fact people discuss, avoid, or half understand the way they half understand one another's family trouble. A single sentence may gather years of rumor, weather, and household consequence around the wound, but keep the wound particular: the exact words someone did not say, the apology that arrived too late, the need still refused a name. Let the narrator's own distance and diction carry the feeling — flat where it is worst, circling where it cannot settle — rather than a borrowed vocabulary of suffering.\n\nLet the turbulence live in actual exchange. Show what the conversation costs: a question that lands as an accusation, an offer that arrives as a condition, a silence the other person reads correctly. Private thought may run ahead of speech or trail behind it, carrying what speech cannot manage, but it should not simply restate the dialogue in cleaner words. Let the strain continue into behavior afterwards — what gets done, refused, or left half-finished — and let comfort, when it comes, be partial or awkward, warmth sitting beside the pain without dissolving it.\n\nUse the community as pressure rather than chorus. Neighbors hold the public version of the marvel and the public version of the grievance, and their retellings harden or soften what the characters believe about themselves. A recurring phrase can pass through several mouths and change meaning without becoming a verdict. Make the fantastic premise alter the practical conditions of the relationship — what can be hidden, what can be sent, what can be waited for, what cannot be undone — so the impossibility carries the hurt instead of decorating it. Do not require a confession, reconciliation, or final rupture; if the strain is still active where the scene stands, end there, with the next move uncertain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution, so any line can be traced to its speaker at a glance. Let each spoken line carry a small piece of the world's accumulated knowledge — a neighbor's version of the marvel, a family saying, a public event everyone remembers differently — kept brief enough to stay scannable and anchored in what the speaker wants from the present exchange, not in a lecture about the past.\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as ordinary local fact. NPCs may mention it while working, bargaining, or giving directions, and their behavior should show its practical consequences without stopping the scene to explain any mechanism. A recurring phrase can pass between characters and change meaning as it moves, but it is an available device, not a required tag on every reply.\n\nMove the scene through actual interaction: one character asks for something, refuses something, offers partial help, or acts on a decision already made, and the others respond from their own concerns. Keep the world active around them — someone arrives, a task continues, a rumor reaches the room — and follow the active selections for narrative person, action markers, and player control. End where the exchange naturally leaves off, following from what has just happened. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice screen; the next move may simply be the next ordinary action in a day that already contains the impossible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the story as a sequence of connected episodes, each with a clear occasion — something happens, is owed, is remembered, is demanded — and a human consequence that carries into the next. Let the fantastic premise be a known local fact that people discuss, avoid, bargain with, or remember differently, and show its effects on decisions, institutions, and everyday life through the events of the episode rather than through explanation of any system.\n\nWithin each episode, let a sentence or two gather across time: what the marvel did to a character's parents, what the neighbors said afterward, what the character has never admitted, held together by the present occasion so that the recollection shapes what happens next instead of decorating the narration. Keep the language, period, and community of the actual story; the episodic route supplies the method of telling, not a relocation of the cast or an imported town, and the communal-memory technique works inside whatever community is actually present.\n\nUse recurrence as the episodic method. A phrase, object, or place returns in a later episode with its meaning changed by intervening events, the change earned by what the characters actually did or learned. Preserve the difference between what the original participants knew and what the teller or community has learned since. An episode may close on a settled consequence or an unfinished one, but the next episode should follow from that consequence rather than restart the situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions, moving from one concrete detail to the next without smoothing the joins. Let the fantastic premise show itself in material wear and practical stakes: a door sealed too long, a tool kept for a purpose no one names, a debt measured in what the marvel took. Communal knowledge lives in the objects and in the way people handle them — compressed to a line or two of what the neighbors said — not in extended social commentary.\n\nAnchor memory in physical consequence. A character remembers the marvel through a scar, a broken lock, a stain that will not wash out, or the weight of something carried for years; the recollection arrives in one hard sentence and returns the character to the present task. When a sentence must gather years, let it do so in a single arc and cut it off; give a thought more room only when the character's reasoning actually needs it. Keep the contrast high — one bleak fact set against one image that stays.\n\nChange rhythm deliberately. Short, hard sentences carry action and damage; a longer sentence gathers two or three related facts before the next impact. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker, so harshness comes from the situation and the person rather than from a uniform tone. The fantasy stays ordinary and consequential — something people work around, repair, or refuse to discuss — never a wonder held up for admiration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Let the fantastic premise enter the record the way it enters local life: a letter mentions it as an old fact, a report tries to fix its date, a journal records what the writer dared not say aloud. What each document knows or conceals follows its author's situation, never the reader's need for explanation.\n\nUse gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces to carry the movement between personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor. One writer records the marvel as a blessing, another as a debt, a third as something their parents misunderstood; a page is removed, a hand changes, ink blots over the line that mattered. A recurring phrase can appear in different documents and change meaning as it passes through different hands. Do not add an omniscient explanation after any record; let the next document correct, complicate, or simply ignore the one before it.\n\nAnchor each document in a human consequence. Its writer should be trying to persuade, remember, warn, confess, or set the record straight, and the form should follow that purpose: a letter may omit what a journal later admits, a report may be precise about the wrong thing. Let the sequence leave the reader able to follow the events and their changing meaning without resolving every uncertainty, and use the specific document form the scene requires rather than a fixed rotation through formats.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures, and let the fantastic premise be part of the comfort rather than a threat to it. Someone knows exactly how the marvel behaves in this weather, what to bring when it has been restless, which old saying still holds true; show affection through these particular acts of knowledge rather than through general warmth.\n\nUse communal memory to make the care specific. A character remembers who first taught them to prepare the remedy, what the neighbors used to say, how a small ritual changed after a marriage or a death, and the recollection gathers detail across time while staying anchored in the present act of looking after someone. A recurring phrase can return as a private joke or a family saying — an available pleasure, not a required tag.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world: a character may accept care while keeping a secret, or offer help while withholding a confession, and manageable friction can pass through the scene without becoming a rupture. Affection should be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. End wherever the enjoyment actually stands — the small pleasure still being savored, the task still in hand, the particular people simply present to one another — rather than at a forced resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let a paragraph gather personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor around a single human consequence, moving across years without losing its anchor in one person's experience. Keep the narration composed rather than conversational, distinct from ordinary spoken language, with enough distance to frame the scene through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it; the contrast between what a household shows in public and admits in private may organize a passage when it matters.\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as an ordinary public fact, discussed in the same breath as weather, work, marriage, or debt. Show its effects in institutions and everyday decisions: a household arranges its hours around it, a public official records it without surprise, a child learns the old precautions before learning any explanation. Do not convert the marvel into a system to be solved; keep its consequences intelligible while its deeper cause remains as accepted as the world's other given conditions.\n\nUse recurring language as a communal memory device. A phrase, name, or image returns in different mouths and different years, its meaning changed by what the characters have actually done or learned in between, and the difference between what the original participants knew and what the community has learned since stays visible. Close on a settled consequence or an unfinished one as the scene dictates, but let the prose leave the reader with the sense that time has moved and the world has registered it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the occasion of telling. Before any tale begins, establish who is speaking to whom, where the listeners sit or wait, and what the telling is for: persuading a neighbor, preserving a memory, delaying a departure, warning someone without naming them. The storyteller's emphasis and omissions are personal knowledge in action — a generous account can be sincere and still leave out what the teller never understood or cannot afford to say. An embedded account may hold something back, a name, a date, a debt, and release it later so that it changes how the listener reads the outer scene.\n\nKeep the outer scene and the embedded tale one continuous world. A listener may interrupt with a correction, serve a drink, or answer someone at the door; the teller may pause and let the present re-enter before resuming. Anchor each shift between levels in a fresh gesture, a new speaker, or a change of light, so the reader always knows which time they are in. Let the routine and the marvelous share the same domestic gravity: if the established fantastic premise has produced a strange custom, the teller mentions who first noticed it, who adapted, and how the neighborhood has treated it since. The wonder arrives as inherited fact, not spectacle.\n\nRepeat one physical object or phrase across two or more levels so it accrues meaning — a shawl, a street's name, a formula like 'the house knew before anyone said it' — connecting the remembered past with the pressure inside the outer frame. When time needs to thicken, build a gathering sentence that accumulates the same house across years or the same rumor across several households while staying anchored in one life. Keep exact statement available beneath the recursion.\n\nYou may close where the telling's present consequence begins to show — a listener softening, an avoided question finally asked, a departure postponed — and you may equally close inside the telling, at a silence or an interruption, with no decision yet rendered. Do not force a completed moral, a settlement of every reported deed, or a cliffhanger before each return. The tale sends the fantasy fact back into ordinary life, where it stays available for further consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite from the Romantic-uncanny route. The impossible occurrence is real and has consequences, but the narration keeps it half-perceived — arriving through weather, architecture, local speech, and the effort of witnesses to bring the event into common memory. Open with an outer situation that needs the strange event as explanation or caution: a town official, a family member, or a grown child now reconsidering what was said decades earlier. Establish the uncanny through linked perceptual details — cold air in a hallway, an index changed in a ledger, a field never described the same way twice. These are not clues to be solved and closed; they are the surface of a fact the community treats as normal enough to discuss around a table.\n\nModel narration on deliberative recollection rather than adventure. Let sentences hold several times at once: a mother's warning, the yearly market, the mayor's report, the same empty bench. Give one character the habits of a local chronicler; when the chronicler speaks, let the prose turn rounded and public, and when a private witness remembers, narrow the syntax and let that remembering carry the witness's own doubt, dread, or reluctance. Keep the narrator at a hearing distance — reporting what was said, seen, and recorded without entering minds the story has not opened. Do not let repeated witness accounts certify the event; show instead how the community has made room for it in schedules, debts, fears, and celebrations.\n\nShape each scene around an unfinished explanation that is nevertheless causally active. The uncanny thing might be a recurring apparition that makes a family delay a sale, or a house that produces its former inhabitants' voices during rain. Give it specific present effects — a changed inheritance, a school timetable, a prayer before a locked room. Register its oddity through ordinary diction, so the uncanny rests inside a phrase like 'after the mirror warmed' rather than in exclamation, and give it no origin myth or taxonomy of powers.\n\nAdvance time sometimes by family event, sometimes by public record, sometimes by rumor: a marriage revises what the town accepted; a court record adds detail without certainty; a rumor names a person who never lived there. Keep the sentences dense but not encrypted. Close a scene where the next layer of remembrance would begin, not at definitive proof, leaving the fantastic carried forward inside social life with both its daily weight and its unexplained excess.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nTreat the fantasy premise as an inherited condition of the neighborhood, not a hidden force awaiting discovery. Establish early that an old family, building, or tradition still exerts practical control through resources, debts, keys, passwords, or medical knowledge, and present this as both intimate and political: a grandmother who can open any door in the tenement, a pawnshop that takes stories as interest, a garden in a drained factory where the dead continue their former occupations. These are confirmed facts of the world and need no masquerade. Write their consequences through the bodies and schedules of the people who depend on them.\n\nMove between biography, gossip, public record, and family memory. Begin a scene with a room's practical detail, then let one image gather decades: the same silk curtain, the same unpaid bill, the dates marked on a wardrobe door. Let characters inherit roles they did not choose — taking messages for the dead, executing a bond signed before their birth, eating the same memorial meal for an anniversary no one can describe accurately anymore. This builds gothic weight without inventing a villain or a ruling institution.\n\nStage the intimate encounter where old power and present desire touch: a caretaker who loves the locked wing of a hostel; a young person who sells a modern device to an ancient creditor and learns it carries their name; an employee who discovers the owner's watch keeps the city's obsolete hour. Make the moral complication specific and proportionate — the caretaker delays a repair so a bedridden resident stays near; the creditor forgives interest for a song remembered from childhood; the employee stays silent because the obsolete hour shortens her shift. Let private want and the social mask show when company is trusted, and let damp, darkness, and modern decay alter how a particular encounter is felt rather than serve as wallpaper.\n\nWork one recurring phrase — 'the house keeps its side of the bargain' — across generations and speakers, so that it is true, false, and coercive by turns depending on who gains. Keep the urban material detail exact enough to carry economic pressure: bus tickets, tile repair, shared bathrooms, mail, shift payments, a refrigerator's hum. Close wherever the current beat leaves the arrangement visible — a choice in progress, a meal served a few minutes late, a silence after an offer — without requiring the bargain to be kept, broken, or avenged on the page.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the oral, epic, and frame-tale route as household recitation. Open each chapter in a present scene where someone is telling, challenging, or re-telling a story, and make the teller partisan rather than archival: a grandmother may correct a daughter's version, an uncle may break off where a debt would surface, a child may demand the section the adults agreed to skip. Give the listeners small practical stakes — a pending engagement, a property dispute, a relative's arrival — so the recitation intervenes in the present. Keep the established fantastic premise active and inherited, never a secret: the never-locking family door, the field that returns whatever is buried in it, the festival that cannot begin before a certain borrowed voice speaks.\n\nMark every shift between narrative levels with a physical gesture or a change of address, so the reader always knows where they stand. Rise to a ceremonial register inside the embedded tale only if this particular speaker would use one; otherwise keep the same syntax with a new subject and an older time. Hold generations together in gathering sentences built from the household's own repeated things — the same garments folded the same way, the same seasonal argument, the same set of keys — and let a phrase first heard as praise return later as accusation or compromise. Fit every image to the setting, language, and community the story actually supplies rather than dressing a generic village.\n\nShow the teller's memory at work even when the events are marvelous. Describe how the impossible fits calendars and kitchens: a widow who hears her dead husband advise her at the exact hour the milk boils, a tailor whose needle mends a torn shadow, unpaid promises growing as white flowers on a debtor's roof. These wonders need no explanation, but they need consequence — the daughter expected to speak the dead man's verdict before the family can act, the tailor's skill forcing a creditor into a strange public obligation, the flowers deciding who may marry. Write those consequences as intimate family drama, not epic combat.\n\nLet a phrase in another language enter only when it changes a relationship or the legal force of a statement, and match dialogue to each speaker's age, role, and situation. A chapter can close as the listeners accept, resist, or postpone what the tale implies — or while the recitation is still unfinished, broken off by an arrival or by exhaustion. The ending is a new present condition, not a completed moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite modern experimental inward fiction. The primary narrator is one person returning again and again to a single impossible event through diary-like entries, remembered fragments, and pieces of what neighbors say, moving between direct present-tense perception and retrospective musing without announcing a strict chronicle. Open each unit with a concrete time marker — a seasonal change in food, a new train schedule, the feel of a particular floor mat, the first night of a local sale — and let one small change stand for the passage of years. Do not accumulate scenery.\n\nMake the fantasy premise a standing feature of social life: the narrator might live above a bathhouse whose water shows the back of one's head at three in the afternoon, or work in a shop where items bought on rainy days return to their former owners. State such facts plainly, without a mythology lecture. The story's work is to show how the fact gets misremembered, commercialized, feared, or privately needed — a visitor asks a practical question, an older neighbor offers a contradictory explanation, a child discovers the thing as though new — while the narrator's own mind registers each encounter through omission, correction, and abrupt feeling. Fit the telling to the story's actual narrator and person; if a first-person participant does not fit, take the nearest consistent stance, such as a child watching adults argue or someone collecting neighbors' testimony in a notebook.\n\nUse a gathering sentence when memory condenses: a single paragraph can hold a grandmother's warning, a school photograph, a lost umbrella, and the city's new glass tower, anchored in a human consequence — the decision to move, to apologize, to finally ring a bell that had been forbidden. Vary sentence length: short sentences carry surprise, direct speech, or sensory break; long ones carry the pressure of unspoken family history and public rumor. Let private thought remain private prose — the narrator can remember a mother's exact phrasing and then say nothing in the following scene.\n\nLet a unit occasionally skip into another shape — a radio announcement, a litany of missing objects, a single quoted sentence — so the inward method stays inventive without losing the narrator's particular voice, which repeats rumor with doubt, envy, or tenderness. Close a unit at a shift in attention or an unfinished observation, not at a complete explanation, leaving the impossible fact active in daily decisions and a present-tense mind still absorbing the community's long inheritance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaham::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the marvelous treated as ordinary, through overlapping community testimony. Begin with a present local problem — a disputed business, a delayed return, an inheritance, a decision about a public building — and let people cite the established fantastic fact as one more part of that problem. The wonder has no dramatic entrance; it is already woven into contracts, jokes, prayers, and household complaints: a woman may be paying rent to a house that answers her by rearranging its shutters, a butcher may owe for a goose that walked out of a closed oven during a land sale. Narrate these facts from several partial positions.\n\nMove as a roving but controlled recounting among a shopkeeper's denial, a notary's notation, a younger relative's memory, and a mother's bedside explanation, and let gathering sentences show how the years carried one version into many households. Keep each account distinct in syntax and stake: the notary speaks in official conditions and dates; the relative in short, hurt sentences; the mother mixes tenderness, theology, and practical arithmetic. No single version becomes the authoritative proof of the marvel; the fact grows more concretely real through the way it changes obligations. Preserve the language, community, and history the story supplies, and add no supernatural occurrence beyond what its world already permits.\n\nGive the marvelous event the same emotional weight as weather, debt, or the arrival of a train. If a dead woman appears at the corner every day to check the price of sugar, show the traffic and the buyer's ordinary focus; let a vendor change a price for unrelated supply reasons. Let the premise's consequences stay social and cumulative — the neighborhood adjusts, avoids, or profits — without ever needing to understand its physics.\n\nBetween testimonies, set brief public-language passages that carry the same premise: a radio bulletin, a market list, a gravestone inscription, a legal clause, a song lyric through a half-open window. Let repeated phrases pass from mouth to mouth and change slightly — 'the town keeps what it is owed' said by a creditor, a bride, and a television announcer. Close on any everyday beat of the present problem — a paper signed, a debt washed, a name added to a register — or while the matter is still being argued; either way the marvel carries the same gravity as the paperwork, and the impossible remains one more intimate, negotiable part of the community's long life, with no moral epilogue required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nOpen each scene in a concrete space and moment — a kitchen before a repair appointment, a classroom at the end of a test, a shop during the hour when the grandfather's clock runs backward — so the reader always knows who is present, what they are doing, and what the established fantastic premise is doing to the routine. Let the strangeness show through one visual or social detail the characters accept: unpaid library books returning to the wrong borrower on full moons, a street that appears on the city map for one hour each autumn, a grandmother's voice arriving with the postman's bicycle bell. Take it lightly in tone if the characters do, without emptying it into comedy.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. After a line of dialogue or a shift of attention, give a short beat of interiority — the protagonist notices a family reason, a debt, a rumor, an old warning — and let that thought direct the next action, keeping each beat to a sentence or two so the pace stays quick. When memory needs condensing, let one longer gathering sentence hold a person's story across three years, a room's history across three tenants, or a local phrase across three speakers, then return to the immediate moment.\n\nCarry communal memory through repetition rather than long reflective passages. Let the same phrase surface in a street conversation, a handwritten note, and a mother's scolding, its meaning shifting slightly each time; let rumors move through quick exchanges — a neighbor at a window, a cashier, a phone message. Make the fantasy premise alter a practical decision inside the scene — the protagonist takes a different route, leaves a chair empty, answers with an old formula, pays a debt no one remembers making — so the impossible fact has causal weight without an explained system.\n\nMake spatial changes explicit: when the scene moves, one short transition names the new place and the changed time, as in 'By the time they reached the clinic, the clock had returned to its ordinary direction.' Introduce an unfamiliar name, custom, or registered obligation through a small sensory anchor plus one consequence. A chapter's last beat can be a reaction, a newly understood implication, or an ordinary cut in the middle of speech or motion; a cliffhanger is available but never owed. End wherever the protagonist registers a fresh consequence of the familiar wonder — or simply where the scene's business stops — and leave the next move open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the fantastic premise be as ordinary as weather in the narration: it enters the sentence without announcement, and its consequences are felt through work, family obligations, and daily decisions. Establish the impossible fact by showing how people accommodate it—how they alter a route, store a certain object, keep a particular silence, teach a child to manage it. Do not explain its mechanism unless a character would naturally need to explain it to another person, and even then let the explanation remain partial and shaped by that speaker's interest.\n\nCompose sentences that gather detail across time while staying anchored in a human consequence. A long clause can move from a remembered morning to the object in a character's hands, provided the connection belongs to the narrator's or the focal character's associations rather than to a summary offered to the reader. Repeat a sound or an image so it recurs like a family phrase, not a symbol waiting to be decoded; when a comparison unfolds, let it change what the narrator understands or makes present instead of decorating the sentence. Give the shaped sentences plain ones to lean against: a flat declaration of what happened, a line of task or talk in its own voice, so the lyric rhythm registers as rhythm rather than constant elevation. Characters keep their own registers, and an exasperated daughter need not inherit the narrator's figurative habit.\n\nMove easily among personal life, family memory, public rumor, and recurring language. A rumor can be reported as fact by one person and doubted by another, each version carrying its own manner of telling, and the narration may leave both standing. Respect who knows what: a character may accommodate a marvel without understanding it, and no teller is granted another character's private thought. End when the scene has made its consequence felt, rather than by resolving the impossible or announcing its meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the remembered occasion drive the passage. Bring present experience and recollection together so that the same sensory detail—a room's smell, a gate's hinge, a voice recognized before it is named—carries both the current moment and the earlier one. The fantasy element may appear in either time, but its weight comes from what it has already changed: a custom, a debt, a family story, a way of entering a house. Do not stop to explain the marvel; show how people have arranged their lives around its absence or its persistence.\n\nUse the reflective sentence to trace an unfinished attachment, not to pronounce it finished. Let ordinary pleasure remain pleasurable while the character notices what is no longer available—a good meal, a successful errand, easy company—and let the loss stay specific: a particular regret, a particular kindness now impossible, a particular person who used to do something differently. The narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry the association. Do not close scenes by announcing impermanence, and do not lift one loss into a universal lesson.\n\nKeep the gathering sentence in service of memory. A single sentence may carry several years within one action, but it must arrive at a consequence the character still feels in the present scene. The impossible can itself be a form of memory—a preserved thing, a repeated farewell, a weather that returns on anniversaries—treated with the same domestic seriousness as a widow's habit or an unpaid courtesy. Keep the teller within their access: they may remember what someone did and said, not what that person privately intended. End with the character still inside the reflected occasion, the feeling available and unresolved rather than summarized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the reply as a situated telling: one storyteller, present in the scene or clearly remembered by the listeners, delivering an account in a distinct voice. Establish briefly who is speaking and to whom, then let the telling proceed without repeated frame interruptions. Take the teller from the cast and setting the story actually supplies, and let idiom, formulas, and social world belong to that language and community—Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Hebrew, Kurdish, or another as supplied—kept distinct rather than blended into a generic regional flavor. Neither antiquity, mysticism, exile, religion, nor a single code of honor is required; a modern kitchen table serves this form as well as an older gathering does.\n\nLet the fantasy element enter as ordinary material in the account, its consequences shown through work, mourning, travel, or family obligation. The teller need not name it as a wonder; they notice who refuses to discuss it, who has changed a habit because of it, which object is now kept covered. Build recurrence from phrases and comparisons the teller would actually use—a measure, an animal, a door always described before it is admitted to be closed—and let one of these return with a changed application when the listeners' question reaches it. Do not explain the impossible mechanism unless this teller would attempt an explanation for this audience, and then let it be partial and self-interested.\n\nHold the teller to honest access. What they did not see or hear must be attributed—'my cousin said,' 'the letter from the coast claimed,' 'the child told me later'—and what no one will confirm stays unconfirmed, so that rumor stands beside memory, each with its own manner of telling. A listener may interrupt with a correction or a demand, and the teller answers or evades in character. End where the teller would naturally pause, with the account's consequence still open and no lesson drawn on the tale's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small number of exact details and let them carry the scene's consequences. When the fantasy element appears, report it in the same tone as an errand or a household task; one direct clause can establish that the impossible has happened, and the next can show what someone did about it. Keep explanation brief and rare—a mother tells a child to stay away from the well at noon; no narrator explains why the water remembers—and let what is withheld remain inferable rather than deleted: if a neighbor stops visiting after the bird returns, the reader can understand why without being told, provided the changed behavior is on the page.\n\nAn extended sentence may gather years when it earns its length: it can move from a remembered morning to the present object, every clause necessary, arriving at a human consequence. When nothing needs that reach, prefer short sentences with strong verbs and concrete nouns. Family memory and rumor arrive as brief recollection rather than elaborated history—one detail from a wedding, one phrase from a letter—and that detail becomes the unit of memory, repeated without commentary so the reader assembles its meaning from the scenes that use it.\n\nKeep dialogue exactly as spoken and economical; no character becomes eloquent to fill a silence, and voices stay individual rather than uniformly clipped. End with the scene's business concluded or resting at a natural pause, not with a summarizing reflection on what the wonder meant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite scene-first: a clear physical location and present action, with paragraphs free to move from present perception into memory or rumor when the transition is anchored in something the character sees, hears, or handles. A woman scrubbing a floor notices a water stain shaped like a map, and the sentence moves to her father's warning about that stain, given the year the river changed course—without pausing to explain the river's change. Let the fantasy element live inside the scene's practical economy: its consequences appear in the work being done, the errand being run, the obligation being fulfilled, and the narration gives it the same unastonished attention it gives weather.\n\nUse paragraphing to orient without recap machinery. A new paragraph may open on a simple action or a line of speech, then gather the relevant recollection; do not begin scenes with thematic summary. Keep the narrator's voice plausible for a contemporary novel—plain, gently ironic, or quietly knowing—and remember that this presentation carries the marvelous without requiring a modern setting, realism as world physics, or a lesson at the close of every reply.\n\nGive rumor a specific, contested shape: one character states a version of an event, another corrects a detail, and the narration may record both without deciding, provided the focal character's knowledge stays distinct from communal claim. Inner experience belongs to the characters the active contract permits, and it is actual reasoning, sensation, or uncertainty rather than a label such as 'he felt sad.' Recurring phrases may rise out of family speech—a nickname, a proverb, a way of referring to the impossible occurrence—without forcing one into every conversation. End at the scene's natural boundary: an action completed, a question left unanswered, or a character still caught in the middle of the day's work, with no concluding paragraph that interprets the marvel for the reader.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nEstablish the relationship's premise through scene selection rather than explanation. Choose a domestic or public moment—a shared task, a room entered after an absence, a message delivered through another person—and let the points of contact reveal what the two mean to each other, what they remember, and what they cannot yet say. Make the fantasy element part of that contact: a creature that accompanies one of them, a place they visit only together, a task only one can perform and the other must wait through. Do not stop the scene to explain the world's rules; let the rules be felt through inconvenience, longing, or relief.\n\nBuild private language and recurring moments across the reply. A phrase used early may return with a changed application: a joke about a door becomes a promise made at that door; a nickname first spoken in mockery is used in a hard hour. A callback should fulfill this relationship's particular promise rather than decorate the scene, and nothing enters that the supplied premise does not include—no imported rivals, no engineered miscommunication, no forced proximity, no confession unless the premise makes one available. Where affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery belongs, give it the space the moment requires.\n\nLet communal memory press on the pair from outside. Relatives remember how they met; neighbors pass on rumors; an old letter or a mother's saying returns when it can do work. A single gathering sentence may carry that history and land on a present consequence, such as the exact way one of them now waits for the other's knock. Keep interior experience focal and private, so the reader holds what each character feels alongside what each wrongly assumes about the other. End at a moment of emotional consequence, the relationship's promise advanced or newly complicated rather than resolved by a closing summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a deliberate declaration that marks the account as a retelling: a birth, a departure, a first return, a deed whose consequences are still spoken of in the house. Keep the declaration compact, then expand into how the event was lived—who was present, who refused to speak of it, what daily work was interrupted. Treat the fantasy element as an established fact of the family's history or a recurring feature of their land: a well that gives back a voice, a bird that arrives before every marriage, a road that walks longer for one branch of the family. It needs no heroic spectacle; its importance is what the household has built around it.\n\nBind separate moments with recurring phrasing. 'That was the winter of the third return,' or 'as her mother had done before her,' may reappear when a later action gives the words new weight. The telling may gather years into one sentence, but the sentence must end in a human consequence the present scene still carries—a debt, a prohibition, a child's name, a door left unlocked. Name people by deed and manner rather than ornamental epithet, and let a returning designation come from something the community actually did or said.\n\nKeep the chronicle oral, not omniscient. The teller reports hearsay as hearsay, separating what they saw from what they were told from what no one will confirm: 'they say,' 'the letter said,' 'my grandfather would not discuss it.' Where the marvelous has hardened into custom—the firstborn walking the boundary at dawn, the covered mirror in the guest room—present the custom's use and its cost without moralizing about it. End on a compact sentence that leaves another act available for retelling, rather than closing the family's account on their behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in continuous literary prose whose definite Scandinavian route is social and domestic realism, with folktale-inflected memory as its secondary texture. Treat the established fantastic premise as a settled condition of the community, and make its consequences legible through practical accounting: who can leave in winter, how bread is kept, what debt may be repaid in kind, which household must host the stranger the road has already accepted.\n\nNarrate through a collective, geographically situated voice that remembers years in the same breath and attributes wonder without ever calling it wonder. A sentence may hold a birth, a long quarrel, and a changed shoreline. Keep the collective voice's knowledge permissions concrete: it reports what is said, remembered, recorded in a parish book, or visible in shared domestic life. When the narration narrows to one person, move into that consciousness and restrict knowledge to what the character has seen, been told, or privately felt. Do not let the collective voice read minds.\n\nShow institutions, domestic arrangements, and neighborly speech accommodating the supernatural as ordinary work. A tax may be paid in honey and a day of dreaming; a road may be avoided after a naming; a drowned man's promise may be entered in the account book beside a living neighbor's debt. Folktale material enters as remembered fact with a social consequence attached—a house that answers before its owner creates an obligation about how visitors announce themselves, a loom that remembers its weaver changes who may finish the cloth—rather than as an advertised marvel or an explained origin.\n\nUse communal memory to layer time. A recent event can stand alongside an old scandal or a family habit that began three generations ago. Let a familiar object—a church bell, a loom, a boat—return so that its repeated presence accumulates a different meaning each time. The setting supplied by the active story governs geography, language, and season; do not invent a unified Nordic terrain of snow and silence, and do not silently relabel a Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context as generic Scandinavian.\n\nPreserve required interiority in the operative presentation, but let social embarrassment or the habit of public accounting keep private thought distinct from what is spoken. A reserved person may think more than they say; no general national temperament applies. End on a scene that has completed its local work or opened a fresh obligation; the fantastic premise remains part of continuing life, not a climax to be resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nWrite a screenplay with scene headings where location or time shifts, concise action lines, and attributed dialogue. Treat the established fantastic premise as part of the physical world by stating it in the same concrete register as domestic work: a fish becomes a ferry when asked, a chair is always warm, a widow's shadow does not follow her into the church. The action line records the fact without pausing to explain it, exactly as it records bread being set on a sill or a boat being turned over.\n\nUse cuts to gather time in the manner of communal memory. A scene may begin in the morning and cut to a funeral; the intervening years are carried in a changed object or a line of dialogue, not in a narrator's summary. When a cut jumps forward, show one visible particular that has altered: the cradle now stands empty beside a taller child, the shop's account book has a new column, the road that was open is now roped with laundry. Let the audience read the elapsed time from the scene itself.\n\nUse offscreen sound for rumor and continuity. A neighbor's voice reports a birth, then the action shows the baby older and quietly levitating above its cradle. The offscreen line may be corrected or repeated by another speaker in a later scene, so that public memory arrives in fragments rather than as an authoritative voice. Voice-over is permissible only when the active perspective requires a definite character's private memory, and it must remain that character's recollection, not a general narrator's explanation of the world.\n\nDialogue carries personal consequence, not universal wisdom. A merchant explains that the dead man still pays his debts because the shop remembers; a child asks why the river runs upstream and is told it changed direction after the wedding. Required private thought that the screenplay must retain remains a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the script; it is not spoken and the cast cannot hear it.\n\nKeep scenes readable. Avoid camera commands as prose ornaments; action lines state what is visible and audible. End within a scene's natural boundary: after a line of dialogue that changes the practical situation, or a silent action that brings the sequence to a close. Do not force a reveal; the fantastic continues beyond the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speaker names and economical stage directions. The established fantastic premise must be present in what can be staged: a lamp that lights when a certain name is spoken, the smell of the sea during a landlocked scene, a table that has an extra place no one uses. Characters accept these facts through their ordinary conversation and work, not through a scene that stops to explain them.\n\nUse entrances and exits to deliver communal memory. A neighbor enters carrying a bowl of fish that was supposedly eaten twenty years ago; a daughter exits because the house is in a mood. Let a later entrance correct or complicate an earlier account through a prop or a practical action: the bowl is set down with a different number of fish than the first speaker claimed, or the daughter returns wearing the coat her mother said was lost. The cast collectively recounts the past through what they carry, refuse, or set aside, and their accounts may contradict without anyone stepping forward to explain the wonder.\n\nRepetition is theatrical: an object, a phrase, or a gesture returns with changed meaning in a later scene. The same sheet is folded twice, the same threshold is crossed by one character and avoided by another, the same name is spoken with a different consequence. Stage directions establish the observable conditions that make the repetition legible; they do not certify hidden guilt or hidden truth.\n\nDialogue carries the consequences of the impossible: a mother tells her son he cannot marry the river woman because the bridge has agreed; a shopkeeper refuses to sell salt to a man whose shadow has not come home. Monologues or asides have a dramatic purpose tied to a specific occasion, such as a funeral recollection or a petition to an invisible neighbor, and they are performed in the scene. Required private experience that cannot be spoken lives in a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the script, not as a line another character hears.\n\nBlocking shows practical care with fantastic matter. A character avoids crossing the threshold without an invitation; two women fold a sheet that remains damp for three days. Stage directions should be actionable, not cinematic. End the play at a natural pause in the public action; the supernatural premise persists offstage and into later acts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through a particular authorized mind, letting syntax follow association rather than a linear report. The established fantastic premise appears as familiar sense: skin that tightens near the church door, the unaccountable weight of a loaf, a grandmother's voice that continues after her death because the house remembers it. The character does not pause to explain the wonder; it belongs to the texture of a morning.\n\nBraid perception with memory and communal rumor. A smell of wet wool brings back a public scandal, which brings back a neighbor's sentence, which opens into the character's own fear or hunger. Use recurring phrases that shift meaning by returning attached to a different remembered scene or a different speaker's voice. 'They always left the window open' first arrives as a mother's warning, then as a neighbor's account of a death, then as the character's own present decision to close it; the shift is registered through the focal character's sensation or action, not announced as a theme.\n\nWhen the mind moves to a public account, attribute it: 'my father said,' 'the women at the bakery believed.' Enough orientation must remain to distinguish an event from a daydream or a rumor. Private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and concern may interrupt each other without becoming a uniform fragmentary voice. A child's shout, a pain in the knee, a remembered debt all change the direction of thought.\n\nThe fantastic is consequential: the mind calculates how to pay the ferryman who works for dreams, or decides which water is safe after the wells woke up. The narrative should not make every character share this consciousness; other minds remain inaccessible, reported only through what the focal character observes or has been told.\n\nLet the associative movement settle on a human consequence rather than a puzzle. A decision to visit a cousin, an apology never sent, or a quiet recognition of how the river has been walking beside him are sufficient endings. The style is selection and rhythm, not a refusal to know anything. Keep the active perspective and world facts intact; the character may misunderstand, but the world's events are not made indeterminate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through sender-attributed messages. The established fantastic premise influences what people text: a daughter warns her mother not to buy river fish on account of the new drowned man's mood; a work group announces that the office will close early because the building remembers a death and will not open its doors.\n\nLet the transcript function as a communal record through the actual movement of messages. A sender repeats an earlier claim with a small correction; a new sender confirms only part of it; a third asks a practical question that shows which part was believed. The accumulation is visible in the transcript itself: who repeats the phrase, who changes a number, who stops replying after a particular detail. Do not add a hidden narrator's summary of what the community thinks.\n\nUse only the interface events that matter: a timestamp for a long delay, an edit that changes a claim, a read receipt when someone chooses not to reply. Do not invent receipts or channel changes for subtext. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript; it should state what is visible or known, not supply a hidden narrator's explanation of the supernatural.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not the writer's live private thought; it may be shown once when the character's digital habit makes the composition itself meaningful. Required private experience that must be retained remains clearly marked nonspoken material outside the transcript. Do not let recipients know an unsent draft's contents. When a character says 'yes, I was there,' other senders can respond only to what was sent, not to the fear behind it.\n\nLet recurring language accumulate across messages: a phrase from an old family chat ('the house answers before you knock') returns in a new context, gaining weight because the transcript shows who keeps repeating it. The fantastic is ordinary: a mother asks if the bread has stopped glowing before she will serve it; a neighbor texts that the dead man's debt is now considered paid. End at the natural boundary of an exchange—after a message that closes a practical matter, opens a new plan, or leaves a reply pending—not on an artificial cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in scene-oriented visual novel prose: a concise scene heading, clear character dialogue with attribution, and short narrative passages for focal perception or inner response. The established fantastic premise appears through repeatable visual details—the same green scarf drying on the fence, the kitchen door that opens for no one, a cracked plate that sounds like the sea when touched. These cues are purposeful references to place and condition, not labels on every line.\n\nLet a scene contain the pressure of communal time by pairing a recent object with an older one in the same visual field. A new lantern hangs beside the saints' niche that has been dark since a grandfather insulted the river. The focal narration registers the pairing as a change in the character's understanding: the lantern is not decoration but a payment, or the dark niche is not neglect but a standing refusal. Dialogue carries the practical consequences of the miraculous without explaining it. A character says the ferryman works for dreams, so the town collects new dreams each market day. Another says the priest will not enter the house because the floor remembers a promise and is waiting.\n\nGive focal narration enough room to show private understanding. The viewpoint character may notice that a neighbor never turns her back to the sea; this can deepen, complicate, or be turned to another concern in the next exchange. Required interiority remains nonspoken material available to the reader, not dialogue overheard by the cast. Use background or expression cues to orient a scene, but never treat them as truth indicators: a smile need not signal honesty, and a turned head need not mean guilt. The focal character may infer from a cue, but the inference is that character's own and may be wrong.\n\nPreserve development across scenes. A revisit can make an earlier object meaningful: the green scarf, first seen as laundry, later belongs to a woman whose drowned husband sends messages on the wind. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images without explicit feature authorization. Keep transitions clear and stop at a private or public moment that completes a local beat, leaving the fantastic premise alive in the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagahbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / García Márquez / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installments that carry a readable through-line, each opening with just enough recovery to orient a reader who has been away. Remind the reader of the established fantastic premise only when it matters: the ferryman who takes payment in dreams, the house that pays the dead man's debts, the baker's dough that sings when the river is pleased. Do not re-explain the entire world in every installment.\n\nLet an installment advance a specific consequence of the impossible by opening with that consequence already in motion and following one practical attempt to address it. A character tries to settle a debt with a dream, or visits a neighbor to learn why the well has started naming people. The attempt may succeed, fail, or remain unfinished; what matters is that the installment shows the consequence changing locally through work, family, travel, or personal discovery. The wonder remains ordinary.\n\nUse recurring language and familiar objects as anchors: the same phrase a grandmother used to close a bargain, the same cracked bell that rings when a promise is broken. Across installments, a detail may change meaning without altering the remembered event. When a new fact matters, recover only the needed particular: 'The same boat that had refused to carry her mother now floated toward the dock.' Keep gossip and public memory in the voice of the community—neighbors repeat, correct, or refuse to speak—without a universal narrator's summary. A focal character's private thought may be shown as clearly attributed nonspoken prose; other characters know only what is spoken or observed.\n\nEnd where the scene completes its local movement: a request made, a debt refused, a task begun, or an understanding that has just arrived. A hook may open the next installment, but do not force a cliffhanger or a finished miniature chapter. The fantastic premise remains available, not a puzzle that must be solved by the serial's end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the story in lean, white-spaced movements where a fantastic element has become inseparable from an intimate hurt. Establish the impossible fact briefly and concretely—a changed body, a binding, a price, an unbreakable bond—and let it rehearse itself through ordinary actions and small gestures. The premise is not explained in full; its effects are shown through what characters notice and decline to say.\n\nLet the cost of naming a feeling appear in the actual conversation and subsequent behavior. When a character admits, needs, withholds, apologizes, or returns to a difficult relationship, show what that exposure costs them: a pause, an averted gaze, an unfinished sentence, a performed task, or a sudden directness that surprises both people. A character may hand over an object, refill a cup, or turn away before a sentence completes, but the gesture is not a universal substitute for speech. If the scene supports an admission, let it land with its full weight and let the aftermath show the strain of having spoken.\n\nUse the fantastic element to make emotion concrete. A promise that binds like a scar, a power that burns when the user is afraid, a spell that slips when the caster is distracted—the impossible detail records strain that dialogue cannot. Describe the body's reaction to the magic briefly and without purple prose; the work of carrying it is visible in posture, hesitation, or small failures. Let the magic's behavior follow the character's actual state rather than a fixed emotional script.\n\nAllow partial comfort. Warmth can arrive as a shared meal left untouched, a blanket placed nearer, a word spoken too late, or a direct answer that eases one wound while opening another. Affection need not resolve the central pain. Show that relief and hurt can coexist in the same room, and that a gesture of care may be accepted awkwardly or refused without becoming a new injury.\n\nLet the cause of the hurt surface when the character's attention or the scene's pressure makes it unavoidable. A character may recall a moment of injury after several restrained exchanges, or may blurt it out under strain. When the revelation comes, let it change the preceding stillness rather than open a full explanation. The story can end before repair, or it can end with a partial repair that leaves the deeper wound untouched.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite a coherent shared interval in short, scannable paragraphs. Put dialogue first, with unmistakable attribution before or after each line. Use direct speech markers suited to the active roleplay convention: quotes or plain tags, no ambiguity about who is speaking.\n\nChoose a single fantastic element from the current scene—an object, a spell, a physical limitation, an altered place—and let it shape the dialogue. Characters respond to what the magic does right now, not to a full explanation. One line may name the effect; the next treats it as a practical fact to ignore, test, or suffer. Let the magic remain an active condition in the scene: a charm that rattles, a mark that darkens, a flame that burns cold. The next speaker can engage that condition directly without needing a question to prompt them.\n\nUse Gu Long spacing in the narrative beats between speeches. A beat may be one stripped sentence: a look down, a hand opened, a cup arrested halfway. Do not elaborate. The gap tells the reader that the speaker has noticed something and chosen not to say it yet, or that they are deciding whether to speak at all.\n\nKeep NPCs active. When a character speaks, they also do something in the world: adjust a lamp, coil a rope, place a coin. Their action advances the scene even if no dialogue turn completes immediately. The magical element remains present through these concrete details, and an NPC's action may reveal their relationship to it without an explanatory line.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point. Stop where the scene's actual pressure pauses—after a reaction, a new line of talk, a continuing action, or an unfinished setup that leaves the next speaker room to move. Do not force a question, choice branch, or cliffhanger. The next speaker can simply continue from what was just said or from the unfinished gesture the narration has named.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the martial adventure route. Build each scene around a compact encounter: a duel, a debt, a delivery, a refused hospitality, a message carried too late. The fantastic element is a single impossible premise that affects the martial world—an oath that compels, a blade that remembers, a technique that exacts a price. Never explain the magic fully; let it appear through its consequences.\n\nWrite in Gu Long's short declarative sentences. A scene may open with a concrete observation: smoke rising, an empty seat, a bell that does not ring. Dialogue is terse; what matters is often the word not spoken. Characters may reveal relationship through what they leave unsaid and through small courtesies—pouring wine, clearing a threshold, sheathing a blade before entering—but a direct question or a plain answer can carry equal weight when the encounter demands it.\n\nStructure the telling episodically. Each scene can stand as a moment with its own shift, but across scenes a delayed revelation accrues. A name mentioned once becomes important later; a favor offered early is repaid with surprise; a wound mentioned in passing determines a later choice. Keep causal links light, not forensically explained.\n\nLet social and ethical conventions belong to the current fantasy world. Use the oaths, hospitality, reputation, face, and limits of courtesy already present in the story, and let them shape how characters enter a room, accept a gift, or refuse a debt. The martial adventure route supplies the encounter structure and the emphasis on obligation, not a transplanted historical setting or a fixed cast identity.\n\nAllow the fantastic element to feel dangerous and personal. A promise enforced by magic costs physical strength or memory. A martial technique that borrows power alters the user's perception of truth. When the revelation arrives, let it reorder the preceding scenes: the reader now sees why a certain gift was refused or why a route was changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite hard, concrete sentences with few connective words. Let each paragraph move from one physical fact to the next: a cracked handle, a boot sole separating, a light failing at the edge of the room. The fantasy premise shows itself as a material condition—a mark on skin, a tool that grows heavier, a room that stays cold. Do not explain the magic; show it working through wear.\n\nUse short scenes with abrupt transitions. A chapter break may leap over hours. A paragraph break may skip a step between intention and result. When a character loses something, name the loss plainly. When a spell misfires, show the consequence in burned fingertips or a broken container before anyone comments.\n\nKeep dialogue stripped. Speakers give instructions, report damage, or refuse help in few words. Abrasive or rough speech must fit the individual; it is not a default voice for every character. Let a longer thought appear only when the character's reasoning requires it—assessing a route before travel, deciding which tool to sacrifice, working out why a mark will not fade.\n\nMake the fantastic cost physical. A power use leaves a residue: dust, heat, a twitch, a stutter. Magic can fail when the user is tired or bleeding. The story should feel like work, but not survival horror: the danger is ordinary, practical, and possible to outlast.\n\nLet the cause behind the magic surface under the pressure of the strain. The reader may see the marks and costs before learning what caused them, or a character may name the cause in a stripped sentence when the damage forces it out. When the fact behind the magic arrives, let it be an action or a concrete object—a broken seal in a bag, a name scratched on a door—rather than an explanatory speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Epistolary / Documents\n\nConstruct the story as a sequence of documents, each with a clear author, intended reader or purpose, and moment of writing. Use only the forms the scene actually needs: a consignment list, a found letter, a report after an incident, a personal journal entry. Do not rotate through all available formats.\n\nTreat each document as a partial view. A writer may set down exactly what was ordered but omit who gave the order. A worried letter may describe a symptom without naming its cause. A report may list observed effects and refuse to speculate. The gap between what a document knows and what it says is the engine; the next document may contradict, correct, or silently confirm it.\n\nLet the fantasy element appear as a trace. A hot coin described in an inventory, a stain that reappears in a second account, a date that does not match the moon. Later documents may reinterpret the same material detail without explaining the magic. The reader assembles the consequence from these partial views.\n\nUse Gu Long's brevity inside the documents. A sentence stands alone and does heavy work: \"The door was open.\" \"He did not return my look.\" Do not let the document style inflate into long rhetorical essays unless the document's author would actually write that way.\n\nLet the key revelation follow the document authors' actual knowledge and situation. One document may plant a detail that a much later one makes significant, or a writer may state the central fact plainly in a single line because they are the one person who knows it. When the revelation comes, let it change the meaning of the earlier documents rather than adding a new authority. The final entry may remain unfinished or evade the central event.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite tenderness through small, practical actions, not declarations. A character warms a bowl, shakes out a blanket, mends a strap, leaves tea where it will be found. Keep these actions brief and concrete: one or two sentences, then move on. Affection is present in what is done and what is not said.\n\nLet the fantasy element be a comfort or a small protection. A charm that warms a cold room, a light that softens before dawn, a mark that eases pain. It should work quietly and imperfectly. It does not solve the larger problem, but it makes the current moment gentler. The narration shows its effect through sensation: warmth spreading, a lamp steadying.\n\nUse Gu Long's white space to preserve privacy. A character may accept comfort without explaining themselves. A kind gesture may be acknowledged with a nod or a pause. Do not force a confession or emotional repair. Personality and disagreement stay intact; sweetness can sit beside stubbornness.\n\nCreate small pleasures specific to the characters: a familiar food, an old song hummed, a game of stones, a particular chair. The fantasy setting gives these details texture without making them exotic. Let the reader enjoy the particular people in the scene.\n\nKeep manageable friction. A ruined meal, a missed meeting, annoyance at a borrowed tool—these can occur without becoming harm. The warmth need not be interrupted by a crisis, and it need not erase the friction. End where the scene's actual pressure pauses: on a note of ease or resumed routine, or during a continuing task or an unresolved small annoyance that the characters can carry without losing the warmth between them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed paragraphs with deliberate transitions. Open a scene with a wider frame: the season, a house, a custom, the state of a road. Let the narrator, where permitted, supply social context or judgment. Then narrow to a small, concrete exchange. The paragraph movement should feel like a camera drawing in from a courtyard to a cup on a table.\n\nMake the fantastic element part of institutions and habits, not an isolated marvel. A city may keep time by a magical bell that tolls when a particular family lies. A market may use a charm to bind a contract. Characters move through these facts as ordinary conditions; the narration may frame them briefly but does not explain them as a system.\n\nUse Gu Long compression for pivotal moments. Within a longer paragraph of social observation, a short declarative sentence can land like a blow: \"The door stayed shut.\" \"No one touched the bread.\" \"She did not answer.\" Let the white space after such a sentence carry the implication before the longer movement resumes.\n\nLet the revelation follow the narrator's actual distance and the scene's structure. The narrator may know more than the characters and withhold it until a later perspective permits, or may state the public fact plainly and leave the private significance to emerge through the characters' exchanges. A detail mentioned in passing—a locked room, a repeated name—becomes significant only when later perspective permits. The revelation should reorganize the social frame, showing how a public custom concealed a private fact.\n\nKeep speech distinct from narration. Dialogue may be terse and indirect while the surrounding narration is elaborate. The contrast is itself a technique: in a formal world, the important words are often the shortest. The novelistic style should not make every character speak in polished periods; let the simplicity of direct speech stand out against the composed prose.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen in the outer scene at the moment a telling begins. Make the occasion concrete: who speaks, who listens, and what the telling is for — to persuade, to delay, to warn, to comfort, to move a decision that is live right now. The fantastic premise already belongs to this world; the embedded story does not announce it, but shows how one person has lived inside its consequences.\n\nLet the teller use Gu Long's compressed movement. A distilled image or a short declarative sentence opens the tale; then a gap; then the next detail, chosen. Omissions should feel deliberate rather than accidental — a name withheld, a season skipped, a witness never described. Let what the listener notices but does not yet say carry the frame's uncertainty.\n\nPlant one concrete detail early in the telling and let it return late, changed by everything in between. The return is the turn: it can reveal that the teller was present at the event, that the cost was not what the frame implied, or that the listener is already inside the story being told. Keep it a recognition, not a solved puzzle.\n\nRepeat with purpose. A formula of address, a gesture, an object may appear in both frame and tale; each recurrence should add one new piece of information rather than echo. Keep the two levels legible: when the embedded tale closes, come back to the room and show what the telling has done there — a silence, a question, a changed attention. The scene can end inside that return, before the listener's reaction settles. No cliffhanger, bargain, or royal court is required; the Arabian Nights is a craft source here, not a setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the Romantic and uncanny mode. Treat the fantastic premise as woven into the world's ordinary texture — it is not news — and let the scene's attention slowly estrange one familiar thing: a known place turning briefly unreadable, a routine act disclosing a consequence no one accounted for. Keep the prose in the story's own natural language and register; severity, bureaucracy, and imitated translated word order are not markers of this tradition.\n\nMove from the settled world inward. Narrate a room, a walk, a working day with patient concrete attention and longer composed sentences; when perception turns strange, cut to short declaratives. Hold a composed narrative distance while narrowing to what one person notices and cannot yet explain; that private noticing is the scene's center of interiority, kept unconfessional in tone.\n\nWithhold one concrete fact until the stillness can bear it. The fact must be specific and already possible in this world — what was actually seen in the corner of the room, what the letter really said, why the familiar road stood empty. Deliver it in a single brief sentence and let it change the meaning of everything before it, without adding a new supernatural rule.\n\nKeep consequence attached to atmosphere. The premise should touch a decision, a relationship, or a practical task and leave a trace proportioned to the scene: a hesitation, a revised task, a question someone decides not to ask. End where the altered attention naturally rests — mid-action or mid-thought, the estrangement unresolved, no moral supplied.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Gothic-Punk\n\nTreat the fantasy premise as inherited infrastructure. An old authority may administer it, a modern system may regulate or exploit it, and ordinary people live inside its consequences without fully understanding them. Show the collision in urban material detail: a converted building, a maintenance schedule, a permit, a family obligation, a service performed after hours.\n\nLet atmosphere arise from that collision rather than from decoration. A room reads Gothic-Punk when its present use sits awkwardly against an older function — fluorescent light over carved stone, a payment terminal beside a ritual object, a locked door that was once the main entrance. Describe such details economically, then let one character's attention linger on one of them, so the collision presses on how the present moment feels; what that person notices and does not explain can carry the unease.\n\nUse the meaningful gap around the social mask. In trusted company a mask falls not through confession but through a single withheld detail finally spoken: the real reason someone stayed, the price already paid, the name no one uses. Deliver it in Gu Long's compressed movement — stillness, then one short declarative — and let it change what the preceding quiet meant. The intimacy that follows should be morally complicated rather than comforting.\n\nKeep private desire and practical life entangled. A character may want safety, recognition, escape, or closeness while still performing a duty, paying a debt, or protecting someone; neither cancels the other. The fantasy premise remains the one this world established — no borrowed vampire physiology, faction, or power system enters to explain anything. End where the scene's attention naturally rests: mid-task, mid-conversation, or in a private realization that has not yet become action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nTell in the oral, epic, and frame-tale mode. Make the telling a situated event with a specific audience, a language and register drawn from the supplied world, and a purpose: the tale answers something these listeners have asked, need, or dread. Let it feel addressed to them rather than recited into empty air; where the moment allows, listeners may interject, prompt, or answer, and the teller can adjust to them.\n\nThe fantasy premise enters as an established fact of this community's world. The teller does not argue that the impossible thing exists; the tale assumes it and shows how it shaped a decision, a debt, a marriage, a departure, a return. Keep the consequence specific to the people involved.\n\nInside the telling, use Gu Long's compressed movement: a brief declarative opening or a distilled image, then a gap, then the next chosen detail. The teller may omit what the listeners already know, what cannot yet be said, or what would make the tale too costly to finish. Let one such omission become visible to a listener without being named aloud.\n\nBring a detail from the embedded tale back near the end and let it change the outer gathering — what the listeners understand about the teller's motive, about the request being made, or about their own part in the story. The recognition need not be supernatural; responsibility, kinship, or prior knowledge serves as well. Keep language, region, community, and period specific to the supplied world, forcing no English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or generic family structure onto the scene. End at a natural pause in the telling or its aftermath; the tale need not conclude, and the listeners need not agree.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaial::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite a fantastic tale: a strange event that has already happened or is happening now, carried by a witness's account. Give the account a teller with an actual relationship to the event — speaking to a listener, recalling it, or narrating at a slight remove — and let the language, pace, and distance belong to that teller rather than to an impersonal chronicle.\n\nLet attention do the selecting. A seasonal detail, a particular object, a sound, a moment of hesitation can carry more weight than an explanation. Render the teller's private experience where it belongs — what they attended to, what they refused to think about — as the account's real substance. The premise must leave an intelligible consequence: a plan changed, a place avoided, a promise kept, something understood too late.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation as the account's turn. Hold back one concrete, previously possible detail until the account is still enough to bear it — what the witness actually saw, who else was present, what was said just before. Release it in a single short sentence and let it change what the whole account has meant, without explaining the premise into a system.\n\nDo not impose uniform quietness or a stock inventory of scenery. Seasonal attention, omission, exuberance, direct feeling, and plain argument are available as this teller would use them. End at a natural boundary of the telling or its aftermath — mid-speech, mid-thought, or in a listener's unspoken reaction — with the strange event unresolved and no moral attached.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the marvelous as ordinary, where this world's canon permits. Let the impossible fact sit inside daily life without announcement: a household task, a market transaction, a family obligation, a journey may already include it. No one pauses to justify it; the characters treat it as part of how things are, and the narration does the same.\n\nAnchor everything locally. The setting, language, community, and history come from the supplied world, and the marvelous detail belongs to a particular place, practice, or relationship rather than serving as decoration. Add no supernatural facts beyond what this world already establishes.\n\nUse Gu Long's compression to hold the premise. Open on a distilled image or one brief declarative that contains the impossible thing; then move into ordinary action without explanation, leaving a gap between what the reader sees and what the characters say about it. What a person declines to remark on can carry the story's real weight.\n\nMake the delayed revelation disclose cost. A detail that read as ordinary earlier — an object, a habit, an absence — returns near the end and shows what the premise has already required: a debt, a silence, a separation, a promise kept at too high a price. Delivered in stillness, it turns the marvelous fact intimate rather than spectacular. End inside that altered understanding — mid-work, mid-conversation, or mid-thought — with the cost neither resolved nor explained away.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaian::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly: where the characters are, what they are doing, and what the fantasy premise means for this moment — no preamble. Show the premise through a concrete particular in use: a mechanism working, a rule being followed, a consequence someone is steering around.\n\nKeep staging legible. Mark each movement, action, or shift of attention with a readable paragraph break; let dialogue, action, and private response each get room to land. When an unfamiliar name or mechanic appears, present it clearly once, through use or brief context, without turning the scene into a glossary.\n\nRun Gu Long's compression inside the reaction beats. One distilled image or one short declarative line, then a gap before the next beat: a pause in the dialogue, a glance, a private thought that does not yet name what it means. The reaction beat is where this style lives — serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to whoever owns it, never a required tone.\n\nHold one concrete detail back until it can pivot the scene — what the character saw in the corner of the room, what the message actually said, why the other person stopped talking — and let it land inside a reaction beat that carries the weight. First person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are all optional, none required. End at a natural continuation point: mid-action, mid-exchange, or in a realization that has not yet become speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the established impossible thing arrive through perception before any explanation. Choose one or two sensory details through which it first registers — the direction a shadow falls, a sound reaching the ear before its cause, warmth lingering where no one has stood — and let the viewpoint's attention dwell there. The narration may know what the strange thing does without knowing how it does it; do not stop the scene to define a mechanism.\n\nBuild each paragraph as rhythm around a plain fact. State the impossible in one short declarative sentence, then let the surrounding sentences extend it, repeat a sound or an image, and follow the viewpoint's association — memory, fear, habit — wherever it actually leads. A comparison earns its length only when it changes what the viewpoint understands or makes an absent thing present; when it merely decorates, cut it. Bring the chosen image back later with its meaning shifted by what has happened in between, so the premise accumulates weight instead of being redescribed. Let a flat, practical sentence break the music whenever work, danger, or another person demands attention; the contrast is part of the method, and a character's spoken register need not match the narration's figurative one.\n\nKeep every association traceable to this teller in this place: a remembered kitchen, a feared color, a song half-heard — whatever the scene or the viewpoint's own history supplies, not a borrowed poetic inventory. The strange thing's effect on bodies, decisions, and ordinary labor stays concrete even while its cause stays unstated. Stop where the attention of the scene naturally rests — mid-task, mid-glance, or in the small quiet after a line of dialogue — rather than forcing the image to resolve into an answer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the impossible thing be felt as what it has already changed. Keep the scene in the present, among familiar objects and ongoing work, and let one of them behave differently than it did before the premise touched this life: a gate that no longer knows its owner's hand, lamplight holding a color from a year no one names. State the change in a compact declarative sentence, then let the reflection dwell on the particular alteration rather than on sorrow in general.\n\nMove between the present and what is no longer available through small irreversible details. A sound, a gesture, a tool left where it was last used can summon a remembered occasion; unfold that association only as far as it changes what the narrator or viewpoint understands about now, then return to the room. Ordinary pleasure stays pleasure — the meal is still good, the joke still lands — alongside the knowledge that an earlier version of this life is closed. Let the detail carry the association when that is enough; no scene owes a closing announcement of impermanence.\n\nGive the reflection its shape by delaying the cause. Circle the memory or the absence without naming what made it, letting the gaps ask the question. A quiet action — something taken down from a shelf, something deliberately not said — or a single spoken line can disclose the cause when the story reaches that point, and the disclosure should revise what the earlier details meant rather than replace them with a history or a system. The premise remains a fact of this world; its weight is the particular past it has made unreachable, carried in what this person still does every day.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell it as a telling. Set a teller among listeners who have their own stake in the account — a courtyard, a workshop, a night watch — and make the occasion legible even if brief: why this story, why now, for whom. The frame should stay present enough that the inner tale reads as one person's shaped account rather than an unmediated report. Keep the idiom, imagery, and tradition specific to the supplied setting and language; Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Hebrew, Kurdish, and neighboring traditions remain distinct, and none of them owes a generic honor code, mysticism, or antiquity.\n\nInside the tale, let withholding do the work. The teller states an impossible fact in a compact declaration — what the river did, who came back wrong — and leaves a gap where explanation would ordinarily stand. What the teller declines to explain carries uncertainty for the listeners and the reader alike, and where the telling lingers or hurries can show a private stake without opening anyone's mind. When a disclosed detail or an acted consequence finally lands, it changes what the earlier stillness meant. The fantastic premise is an established fact of this world; the tale reveals its significance by degree and never owes its mechanism.\n\nMake the frame part of the information flow rather than a wrapper. A listener's practical question, a pause where the implication cuts too close, a correction from someone who was there — these shape what gets told and when, staying occasional and in their own register rather than turning into running commentary on storytelling itself. End inside the tale or return for a moment to the teller and the room; no nested thread must be closed, and no impossible device explained, before you stop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Minimalist Prose\n\nMake the impossible present with a few exact details and leave the rest out. One may be enough: a door that opens onto the wrong street, a wound closing from the wrong end, a fire that gives no heat. State the detail in direct clauses and follow it with what a person actually does next. Do not pause to define the strange thing or to reassure anyone that a system stands behind it.\n\nOmit the explanation, never the connection. If a character will not enter a room, the preceding detail should make the refusal legible on its own; the reader needs the result in behavior, not the reasoning spelled out. Keep an inward thought when it materially changes what is understood — one sentence of fear, habit, or calculation can be the hinge of a scene — and otherwise let action and withheld speech carry the weight. Shortness is not the goal: a precise long sentence that tracks one movement of attention is worth more than a run of performed fragments, and each voice keeps its own shape.\n\nLet a single changed element do the turning. One line of dialogue, one small movement, one detail noticed a second time can reorder what the earlier observations meant, with no summary after it. Stop there, or in the middle of the next ordinary task, with the following consequence left inferable. The premise stays a fact of this world; the prose earns its effect by what it chooses to show and what it trusts the reader to supply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaias::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene as continuous novel prose, moving as needed among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and whatever inner experience the active contract authorizes. Orient with paragraphing and transition rather than recap. Let the established fantastic premise sit inside ordinary life the way weather or debt does: a person negotiates a magical condition while also handling an errand, or notices something impossible in the middle of a conversation and decides what to do about it — or whether to say anything at all. Its consequences emerge through what people do, avoid, and remember, never through an explanatory stop.\n\nPlace the compact declarations where the prose gathers itself. A short sentence states a strange fact or a withheld feeling; the sentences around it expand into the particulars that make the statement matter — who is in the room, what has been risked, what the viewpoint notices and does not say. Then leave a meaningful gap: a question unanswered, an explanation declined, a silence the narration declines to fill. When a revelation or an action does arrive, let it revise the reader's understanding of earlier details rather than replace them with a revealed system.\n\nKeep access consistent with the active perspective: authorized thought may include reasoning, uncertainty, sensation, and memory, but no knowledge of closed minds and no access the contract withholds. Register and rhythm follow the narrator, the scene, and the people in it. Stop where the scene's attention rests — mid-errand, mid-exchange, or on an open question — without owing a beat of completion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose scenes in which this relationship's particular promise is at stake, and let the choice itself make the premise legible: an affection that has to cross a practical barrier, comfort offered under another name, a rivalry that turns on one withheld admission. Use only what the supplied premise establishes between these particular people; no borrowed roster of familiar tropes, no beats inserted for recognition alone. Let the fantastic premise act on the relationship — change what one of them can safely say or do, or what the other must not yet know — without becoming its only subject.\n\nBuild the private language and let it recur. A phrase, a gesture, an object carried or mended can return later with its significance altered by what has happened between the returns. Restraint is the engine of the payoff: these people may decline to explain what they feel, and a small said thing — two words, a question about food — carries its larger meaning precisely because the two of them share what it stands on. Where a long passage would explain, a short declarative sentence often does more.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space it actually needs in this scene, and no fixed amount more. The narration may enter whichever mind the active contract authorizes — wanting, calculating, misreading — while other minds stay closed and nobody becomes perfectly perceptive. When a disclosure or a quiet act changes the preceding stillness, its consequence can be felt without forcing a confession or closing off the relationship's next step. Stop where the two of them are: mid-exchange, mid-refusal, or just after what was finally said.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the feel of retelling. Open with a deliberate declaration that sets events into memory: what was done, who was there, what later became of the deed or of the people who saw it. Let the established fantastic premise enter as an act or condition with public consequence — a thing done before witnesses, a price a household still pays, a marvel the district has had to live around. It is not a private wonder awaiting explanation; it is part of what this community remembers and manages.\n\nShape the chronicle with gaps. State a strange fact in a compact declaration, then withhold its full significance; what the account declines to explain can leave even known events uncertain, and a later action or a single disclosed detail can change what the earlier stillness meant. Let recurrence do structural work: a phrase or a designation earned by an established deed or a way of speaking returns with a new application as the telling moves forward, never an ornamental epithet pinned to every entrance. Keep the source tradition coherent — oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, and public deeds organize the account where the supplied setting establishes them, and nothing imports a foreign pantheon or stirs every epic convention into one voice.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority. A participant may object to a detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, and the telling absorbs it without turning to parody. The teller's access follows the selected perspective, and shared reputation stays distinct from any focal character's private account of the same events. End with the deed still unfolding or its memory still contested, another act left available to be remembered, rather than declaring legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
