            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-fantasy-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Fantasy · 03/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPagaiav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantastic as an established condition inside a specific Scandinavian domestic world: a particular place, season, household, and form of work or civic life. Let the impossible thing already belong to that world through practical arrangements—a household rule, a seasonal task, a local office that keeps a record, a family obligation timed around the condition. Show its consequences in wages, travel, meals, childcare, property, or public procedure. Do not make the condition a secret the narrative must gradually expose, and do not import generic reserve, snow, or isolation.\n\nUse the myth-inflected domestic-realist route. The narration attends to the household's material life and social relationships with plain precision: who does which work, what is owed, what is said at table, what the neighbors know. Folktale logic enters through the way people explain, remember, and negotiate with the established impossibility. A grandparent's account may supply a rule whose origin no one now verifies; a local official may record the condition in bureaucratic language that misses its older meaning; a child may repeat a warning as a household fact. Keep these explanations partial and situated. The narrator may speak with the composed clarity of someone who knows this community, but should not inflate the prose into epic declamation.\n\nApply Gu Long's brief declarative movement and meaningful gaps. Write short, direct sentences that state what happens or what a person notices. Leave out the explanation a character would not give, especially when the explanation is the very thing the scene is circling. Distill a moment into one or two concrete images: a door left open, a lamp carried across a yard, a name written in a ledger, a bowl set out for something that is expected. What a person declines to explain can carry as much weight as what they say.\n\nDelay one revelation until it revises the community's practical understanding of the established condition. An early scene may show an accommodation—a locked room, a repeated errand, a child's question, an item missing from a table—without naming its full significance. The later revelation should be a particular fact or recognition that changes how the reader understands that ordinary arrangement and the people who maintain it. It may correct a household's inherited explanation, expose a gap in an official record, or show that a rule once thought protective has a different practical consequence. It should not be a solved system or a complete explanation of the fantastic premise.\n\nKeep interiority available where it contributes feeling, attention, or the private reasons behind a choice. A character may know more than they say, or may simply not yet have found words for what they sense. Do not make every silence a concealment. The narration may reveal a focal thought while another character remains unaware of it. Let the scene end on a completed action, a quiet acknowledgment, or the moment after recognition, without forcing a moral, a confrontation, or a full resolution of the impossible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Screenplay\n\nWrite the fantastic as an established condition visible through how people live and work. Show the impossible through practical behavior: a room no one enters without a particular gesture, a household task performed at a fixed hour, an official who records something the audience can see but not yet understand, a child taught a rule as casually as a chore. The action lines should state these behaviors plainly without explaining the premise. Let the consequences be intelligible through their effect on work, travel, family, or local authority.\n\nUse concise, readable action blocks with clear speaker cues. Scene headings mark changes in location or time. Keep action lines to what is visible or audible: movement, objects, sound, light, weather, the physical result of a decision. Do not fill the page with camera commands. When a cut or an offscreen sound is used, make it serve a narrative purpose—usually to withhold something Gu Long's method would leave unstated. A scene may end just before an explanation, or cut away from a question no one answers.\n\nLet dialogue be compact and indirect. Characters speak in short exchanges that circle the established impossibility without naming it fully. A question may receive a practical answer that does not satisfy the underlying uncertainty. A person may decline to explain, and the scene should let that refusal stand rather than forcing a follow-up line. What a character notices can be conveyed through a look, a pause, or a small action described in the action lines, not through dialogue that announces the observation.\n\nDelay one revelation so that an earlier visible detail returns with changed meaning. Show a familiar detail in an early scene—a covered object, a locked door, a repeated errand, a name on a list—without explaining its significance. In a later scene, a line of dialogue or a visible action recontextualizes that detail. The recontextualization should be something the audience can see or hear: a character performs the earlier gesture with a different object, an offscreen sound is now identified, a record is opened to a page the audience previously saw closed. The revelation should be a particular fact or recognition, not a complete explanation of the fantasy premise. It should change how the audience understands the stillness of the earlier scene.\n\nKeep required private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note when it is needed, separated from dialogue and action lines so it is not mistaken for something the cast can hear. Do not use voice-over merely to replace private thought. Let a scene close on a completed action, a held silence, or the moment after a recognition, without an obligatory cliffhanger or explanatory speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Stage Play\n\nWrite the fantastic as part of the stage's observable world. The impossible thing should be present through playable elements: an object handled with a particular ritual, a door or window treated as significant, a household task performed at a fixed time, an official record consulted with visible care. Stage directions should state these actions economically so a director and cast can perform them. The audience sees the accommodation; the characters do not need to explain it. Let the consequences affect entrances, exits, work, and relationships in ways that can be staged.\n\nUse clear speakers and let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the scene. Dialogue should be compact and indirect. Characters speak in short exchanges that circle the established impossibility without naming it fully. A question may receive a practical answer that does not resolve the underlying uncertainty. A pause, a look at an object, or a small piece of business can carry what a character declines to say. Do not force every silence into a confession or every pause into a dramatic beat.\n\nUse entrances and exits to create Gu Long's meaningful gaps. A character may leave just before an explanation, or enter after a significant action has occurred offstage. The audience shares the stage space and can see who is present for each exchange. What one character knows but does not say should remain private unless the play gives them a performed reason to reveal it. Monologues and asides need a dramatic purpose; they are not the default destination for private thought.\n\nDelay one revelation so that an earlier prop or routine returns with changed meaning. Show a familiar object or routine in an early scene—a covered dish, a locked drawer, a repeated errand, a name written in a book—without explaining its significance. In a later scene, the recontextualization should be playable: a character performs the earlier routine with a different object, opens the locked drawer in front of someone who was not present before, or enters carrying the covered dish at an unexpected hour. The revelation should be a particular fact or recognition, not a complete explanation of the fantasy premise. It should change how the audience understands the stillness of the earlier scene.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material when it is needed, clearly separated from dialogue and stage directions so it is not disguised as something another character can hear. Do not add film-camera instructions. Let the scene end on a completed action, an exit, or the moment after recognition, without an obligatory gathering of suspects, theatrical revelation, or new clue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind as it moves through an established fantastic world. The impossible thing should already belong to that person's ordinary experience: a household rule, a work routine, a civic obligation, a seasonal change, a familiar object with known consequences. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, and interruption braid together around it. Keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered, imagined, or feared. The fantastic premise is not a hallucination or a mystery to be solved; it is a settled condition the mind keeps returning to.\n\nUse Gu Long's brief declarative movement within the associative flow. Even when syntax follows the mind's jumps, individual perceptions and memories should arrive as short, concrete units: a door, a smell, a name, a hand on a rail, a sentence someone said years ago. Distill each return to the premise into one or two sharp images rather than explanatory reflection. Let the mind circle a question without resolving it. What the person notices but cannot yet name can carry uncertainty.\n\nLet the associations follow a particular person's actual habits of attention. One mind may return to a bodily sensation; another may interrupt itself with a remembered instruction; another may keep reaching for a word that will not come. Do not make every character share the same associative style or grant the narration access to minds outside the active contract. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this mode; the movement should be perceptible as associative, not merely a linear report with occasional memories inserted.\n\nDelay one recognition until a later association collides with an earlier image the mind registered but did not complete. The mind may notice a detail early—a mark on a wall, a repeated phrase, an object out of place, a particular silence—and pass on without finishing its significance. Later, a new perception or memory forces that earlier image back into attention, and the recognition arrives suddenly: the mark belongs to a task performed at the wrong hour, the repeated phrase was a warning the person now understands, the silence was the absence of a sound the household rule should have produced. The revelation should be a particular fact or private understanding, not a complete explanation of the fantastic premise. It should change how the person understands the stillness they have been circling.\n\nKeep the telling honest about access and knowledge. The narration may reveal this mind's private experience without making it available to other characters. A later realization cannot be retroactively inserted as an earlier motive. Let the passage end on a completed thought, an interruption, or the moment after recognition, without forcing a decision, a confession, or a full resolution of the impossible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the fantastic as an established condition visible through the senders' digital habits. The impossible thing should already shape their messages: a household rule about when to reply, a work schedule built around a recurring event, an errand tied to a civic obligation, a family member who sends the same practical reminder each season. Use readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution—one sends short practical notes, another types long messages and edits them, another leaves long delays between replies. Do not make every sender sound the same.\n\nUse only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. A delay may carry meaning; a read receipt without a reply may carry more. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. When context outside the exchange is required, present it clearly separated from the transcript. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet the messages be compact and indirect. Senders write in short exchanges that circle the established impossibility without naming it fully. A question may receive a practical answer that does not resolve the underlying uncertainty. A person may decline to explain, and the transcript should let that refusal stand rather than forcing a follow-up message. What a sender notices but does not transmit can be shown through an unsent draft, a delay, or a message typed and then replaced with something safer—when the active presentation permits it.\n\nDelay one revelation so that an earlier transmitted detail returns with changed meaning. Show a familiar detail early—a repeated instruction, a time mentioned in passing, an attachment no one opens, a question left unanswered—without explaining its significance. In a later exchange, the recontextualization should be something the reader can compare against the earlier transcript: a sender repeats the instruction with one word changed, opens the attachment and describes what it shows, answers the old question under a new condition, or sends a message at the hour the earlier rule forbade. The revelation should be a particular fact or recognition, not a complete explanation of the fantasy premise. It should change how the reader understands the stillness of the earlier exchange.\n\nKeep private thought distinct from transmitted text. A sender may know more than they type, or may not yet have found words for what they sense. Do not make every silence a concealment. Let the transcript end on a sent message, a delay, an unsent draft, or the moment after a recognition, without forcing a confession, a cliffhanger, or a full resolution of the impossible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaiba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the fantastic as an established part of the scene's observable world. The impossible thing should already shape the setting and the focal character's practical attention: a room arranged around a particular rule, a household task performed at a fixed hour, an object handled with familiar care, a civic notice visible in the background. Use concise scene-setting and readable dialogue attribution. Let the focal narration or inner response show how the character experiences the established premise without explaining it as a system.\n\nUse background or expression cues as orienting references, not automatic truth indicators. A changed expression can attract attention, but a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the evidentiary work if the scene concerns uncertainty. Keep the cues purposeful: a familiar background detail may anchor a return, a character's expression may show attention or discomfort without certifying what they know. Do not label every line with a cue or turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits.\n\nLet dialogue be compact and indirect. Characters speak in short exchanges that circle the established impossibility without naming it fully. A question may receive a practical answer that does not resolve the underlying uncertainty. A person may decline to explain, and the scene should let that refusal stand. What the focal character notices but does not say can appear in the focal narration or inner response, clearly separated from dialogue so it is not mistaken for something another character can hear.\n\nDelay one revelation so that a familiar visual detail returns with changed meaning. Show a background or expression cue early—a covered object, a repeated gesture, a mark on a wall, a character's particular look—without explaining its significance. In a later scene, a new fact or a line of dialogue recontextualizes that earlier detail: the covered object is opened and shown to contain something the earlier scene implied it could not, the repeated gesture is performed by a different character at a different hour, the mark on the wall is revealed to be a record of the household rule. The revelation should be a particular fact or recognition, not a complete explanation of the fantasy premise. It should change how the reader understands the stillness of the earlier scene.\n\nKeep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected. Let the scene end on a completed action, a held silence, or the moment after recognition, without forcing a choice screen, a confession, or a full resolution of the impossible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaibb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Gu Long / Web Serial\n\nWrite the fantastic as an established condition that accumulates across installments. The impossible thing should already shape the ongoing situation: a household rule, a work obligation, a civic institution, a seasonal event, or a personal accommodation. Each installment shows a local change or development in how that condition affects the characters' practical lives. Maintain a readable through-line: what is underway, what changes now, and what remains available to continue. Do not turn the premise into a leveling system, a ranking, or a mystery that perpetually recedes.\n\nUse Gu Long's brief declarative movement and meaningful gaps within the serial form. Write short, direct sentences that state what happens or what a person notices. Leave out the explanation a character would not give. Distill a moment into one or two concrete images. An installment may end on a completed action, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known, without forcing a cliffhanger or a complete miniature chapter. Let some questions remain unanswered across installments.\n\nRecover context briefly only when it helps. When an earlier observation becomes relevant, restate the necessary particular—the door previously said to be locked, the object another character described, the time a familiar task is performed—rather than retelling the whole history. Preserve changes that matter. If a repair or a new arrangement occurred between installments, include it so the later scene does not falsely contradict the earlier one.\n\nDelay one revelation so that an earlier observation returns with changed meaning in a later installment. Show a familiar detail early—a repeated errand, a name in a record, an object out of place, a particular silence—without explaining its significance. In a later installment, a new fact or a line of dialogue recontextualizes that earlier detail: the repeated errand is revealed to serve a different purpose than the household claimed, the name in the record is shown to belong to someone the reader has since met, the object out of place is found in the location the earlier installment said was locked. The revelation should be a particular fact or recognition, not a complete explanation of the fantasy premise. It should change how the reader understands the stillness of the earlier installment.\n\nKeep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. A character may know more than they say, or may not yet have found words for what they sense. Do not make every silence a concealment. Let ordinary life continue around the established impossibility, and let the serial duration give selected facts room to acquire meaning without making a fair understanding perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite a difficult relationship inside a world where the impossible is settled fact. Let the fantastic premise bear on the trouble between these people rather than decorate it: it can lengthen a separation, make a return possible but expensive, put a private feeling where someone can stumble over it, or remove the excuse a character was hiding behind. Whatever it does, give it the same unhurried, concrete attention as the cooking, the errand, the walk to the station. The strange thing is neither a special effect nor a puzzle to be decoded; it is one more condition the relationship has to be conducted under.\n\nPut the strain in behavior rather than in a vocabulary of suffering. One person asks the ordinary question instead of the real one; the other answers a confession with a practical instruction, or does the helpful thing while refusing to name it. When a need is finally admitted, it can still go unmet; when an apology is offered, the injury can stay. Track what the exchange cannot settle, and let that unfinished business bend what happens afterward — an errand taken too slowly, a message reread, a door left open that used to be shut. Give the private version of the scene as real interiority under the active contract, with a specific reason for what goes unsaid — shame, habit, the wish to keep one thing intact — so the reader holds that reason even when no one else in the scene does.\n\nKeep the narrator's even distance working for the turbulence instead of against it. The character notices a familiar object at the wrong moment, registers the impossible thing with the same attention given to a late bus, and continues the routine after a wounding line because the routine is what there is. Warmth may arrive, awkwardly, through a remembered preference or a shared task, without repairing anything underneath. You can stop while something is still going on — mid-task, mid-wait, mid-unsettled silence — as long as the pressure is legible; the hurt does not need to be cured before the reply ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Chat Roleplay\n\nTake what the other participant has already established — the last action, question, or arrival — and continue from inside it, in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Each speaker's words and gestures stay distinct on the page; when two people talk, the reader can always tell who is doing what. Honor the active contract's division of control: write your characters and the world as far as it permits, and leave the other participant's character to them.\n\nLet the impossible be a normal condition of this shared interval. Someone mentions it while doing something ordinary, passes it on the way to a task, or folds its effects into the day's practical arrangements the way weather or a delivery schedule gets folded in. Do not stop the scene to explain the system. Keep the pacing grounded in concrete, unhurried activity — making tea, checking a route, carrying something, waiting out weather — and let the strange detail carry a practical consequence for what can happen next without dominating every exchange. A character can notice something odd and say nothing about it.\n\nLet the people around the participant keep living: NPCs make choices, pursue their own errands, offer help or friction as their situation actually supports. Conversation can be indirect, practical, or quietly revealing; it does not need wit, and it does not need to end on a question. Stop at whatever boundary the scene reaches on its own — a task half-done, a reply half-answered, someone turning to the next ordinary thing — and let the continuation follow from what is now on the table rather than from a manufactured cliffhanger or forced choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as intimate urban and domestic narrative: the story stays with a household, a shop floor, a neighborhood, a small circle of people bound by debts, promises, shared rooms, and family obligation. The fantastic premise is felt where these people actually live — it changes how a family divides its hours, how a shop receives its goods, how a worker explains an irregularity to a supervisor, how someone keeps a departed relative's place at a table. Keep the language, period, and community exactly as the active setting supplies them: no transplanted historical scenery, no invented period speech, no generic temperament laid over the cast.\n\nRender the impossible with unhurried practical attention, through the route's own economy of detail — meals, errands, cleaning, accounts, visits, small exchanges between people who know each other too well — giving the strange occurrence the same care as the ordinary ones. Strangeness often arrives at one remove: a neighbor's account, a household rule nobody explains, a familiar routine quietly altered. Its meaning can stay partly unresolved without the scene facts becoming unreliable.\n\nKeep the focal character's attention close and practical, and let feeling travel through arrangements rather than declarations — what is served, who is humored, what gets paid and what gets deferred. Private concern attaches to ordinary objects and postponed duties; give the character's unspoken version as real interiority under the active contract. The scene can settle back into the household's ordinary rhythm with the strange thing now one more condition these people must live with.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nKeep the prose tight and load-bearing. Cut connective tissue between beats: a character finishes one practical task and is already into the next, and the impossible thing moves through the scene as a material condition — it costs time, damages property, limits where someone can go, changes what can be safely carried — never as a pause for wonder. Where the scene's physical facts include wear, cold, fatigue, bad light, or a route that no longer works, name them in hard, concrete strokes and let forceful diction carry real weight. Rough or clipped speech belongs to its speaker and situation, not to a general atmosphere of hostility.\n\nPreserve the even, exact attention underneath the compression. The character notices the impossible thing while doing something ordinary, and the narration gives it the same flat precision as a cracked cup or a missed connection — one strange fact stated plainly, then the work continues. The strangeness does not need explaining, but its practical consequences must be legible: what it broke, what it cost, what it now blocks. When the character has to reason something through, let the thought run longer and plainer; compression serves pressure, not amnesia about thinking.\n\nUse rhythm deliberately. A compressed stretch of action can break against a single hard observation; a long physical job can be interrupted by one flat sentence of fact. The grit comes from material consequence — what the body and the objects around it endure — not from making everyone abrasive or every relationship hostile. You can leave the character still working when the reply ends, the strange thing absorbed into the day's cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story in documents — whatever the scene actually calls for: a letter, a journal page, a work log, a notice, a receipt, a message left for a housemate — each with a specific author, a specific recipient or purpose, and a specific hour of composition. Do not rotate formats for variety's sake. Every document holds only what its writer would record or allow at that moment; a diary entry or an unsent draft is still something composed, shaped by what its author can face, wants, or refuses to say, not a transcript of a whole live mind. Where the active contract supplies interiority, keep it as genuine private experience beside the documents rather than inflating a record into one.\n\nLet the fantastic premise enter as practical detail inside each entry. While listing the day's tasks, a writer notes an impossible occurrence; a changed route, an unaccountable charge, an object where it cannot be — these sit in the margins of ordinary bookkeeping, described with calm, concrete precision. What the writer knows at the hour of composition is all that entry can know. Later documents can correct, complicate, or quietly confirm earlier ones without any of them explaining the whole system.\n\nBuild the story's revelations out of gaps and revisions: the sentence a letter never finishes, the entry rewritten before sending, the log fact recorded by someone who does not yet understand it, the detail a later note returns to with new practical meaning. Between documents, trust the reader to carry the continuity — no omniscient voice steps in after a record to explain it. End where the paper naturally stops: a message sent, a page left mid-sentence, a form filed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the impossible active as settled fact, and let it make care specific rather than replacing it. The strange thing can complicate a routine, lengthen an errand, change what a household needs stocked or mended — and the scene's real attention goes to how particular people look after one another inside those conditions. A small practical adjustment made for someone's sake carries the genre; do not dissolve the fantasy into a cozy backdrop, and do not dissolve the care into a generic warm mood.\n\nRender comfort with unhurried practical attention: a meal cooked to someone's exact preference, a room made easier to come back to, a task taken over before it is asked for, a familiar object put back where its owner will find it, an errand run in bad weather because it matters to someone. Affection is sincere without requiring a confession, a repair, or a settled future; people stay private, awkward, or stubbornly wrong about some things while still acting with care. Small frictions are allowed to stay small.\n\nLet the chosen people be enjoyable on the page — their habits, their jokes that are not quite jokes, what each one reliably notices or forgets. Established problems in the world and between these characters remain real; comfort does not make anyone agreeable or erase what is difficult, and accepting help does not settle the larger trouble. A small pleasure can complete itself — a shared meal, a warm room, a job finished side by side — while the rest of the situation keeps going.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose: deliberate transitions, sustained paragraph movement, narration distinct from ordinary spoken language even when the dialogue stays plain. Let a paragraph travel — from a practical activity into a social observation, a remembered detail, a quiet judgment — without losing the scene's forward motion. Use the narrative distance the chosen narrator actually permits to frame events through their context: who these people are to each other, what has happened here before, what the neighborhood or the office makes of it.\n\nFrame the impossible through the world's ordinary institutions and relationships: how a workplace books it into the accounts, how a family explains it to a child, how a public record leaves it half-described, which households it touches and which it passes by. The strange fact can remain partially unresolved — a threshold the narration approaches evenly, without insisting on an explanation — while every scene-level fact stays stable. Inside the formal register, keep the calm practical attention: preparing food, walking a familiar route, waiting for a delivery, sorting papers, each given the same even precision as the impossible thing, and each allowed to carry its association with a private concern.\n\nCeremony, correspondence, and the contrast between public presentation and private life may organize a passage where the material supports it, but formality here is architecture, not costume: no invented aristocracy, no archaic diction, no elaboration laid uniformly over everyone's speech. The scene can return its character to an ordinary task, the fantastic premise now part of the larger arrangement they live within.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as a specific occasion: a listener has asked for a story, needs a delay, wants an explanation, or is being persuaded. Give the teller a practical reason to speak now, and let the telling itself become an event in the outer world. The listener's attention, interruptions, and changing understanding should be visible around the embedded story.\n\nWhen the teller begins the inner account, keep the narration unhurried and concrete. Describe the fantastic occurrence with the same calm attention given to a meal, a walk, a household task, or a familiar object. Do not signal the impossible moment with heightened prose. Let the strangeness arrive through ordinary details: a door that opens onto the wrong street, a phone that rings with a voice already known, a room that remembers a conversation no one had. The teller may report these events without insisting on their meaning.\n\nUse the embedded story to change how the listener reads the outer scene. The tale may explain a silence, justify a decision, warn of a consequence, or make the listener notice an object or phrase they had ignored. After the inner story ends, return to the outer occasion and show the listener's altered attention. They may ask a question, touch an object, revise a plan, or simply sit with the new possibility. The telling does not need to resolve the fantastic event; it needs to make the listener's relation to the present world different.\n\nLet a repeated phrase or object cross the narrative levels. A color, a sound, a small action, or a sentence can appear in the outer scene, recur inside the tale, and return with changed weight. The recurrence should feel like an association the characters themselves notice, not a hidden key. It may suggest that the fantastic premise has entered the outer world, or only that the listener has begun to see the ordinary world through the story's terms. Keep that ambiguity available.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the teller experienced and what the teller now believes. The inner account may be shaped by memory, hope, or the needs of the present audience. The listener may suspect this without being certain. Do not make every embedded story a confession or a trick. The teller can be sincere and still be unreliable about the meaning of what happened. The outer scene should hold both the story's power and the listener's awareness that it is a story being told by someone with reasons to tell it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic-uncanny route. The narrative should stay close to a particular consciousness while keeping the social world around it legible: a workplace, a household, a journey, a small community, or a family obligation continues in recognizable form. The fantastic premise enters this world as a threshold experience that the focal character cannot fully explain but cannot dismiss.\n\nDescribe the ordinary setting with Murakami's unhurried attention. Meals, errands, weather, rooms, and small tasks should feel concrete and calm. Let the impossible event arise from within that texture rather than interrupting it: a sound heard at the wrong hour, a face seen in a place where no one should be, an object that returns after being lost, a message that answers a thought before it is spoken. Do not raise the prose temperature. The uncanny effect comes from the character's quiet recognition that something familiar has become strange.\n\nKeep the fantastic event's consequences intimate and psychological. The character may change a routine, avoid a place, seek out a person, or begin to watch for a recurrence. Other people may notice a difference without understanding it. The impossible event does not need to become a public crisis or an explained system. It should alter how the character experiences time, memory, or desire.\n\nLet the narration distinguish between the character's social self and their private relation to the uncanny. In conversation they may speak plainly, even casually, about ordinary matters. Inwardly they may return to the threshold experience, test it against memory, or wonder whether it has happened before. This split is not concealment for its own sake; it reflects the difficulty of fitting an impossible private experience into a world that continues to make practical demands.\n\nPreserve unresolved uncertainty. The character may come to believe the event was real, or may suspect their own perception, or may accept that they will never know. The world itself should remain stable enough that the reader can see both possibilities. The story's power lies in the character's changed relation to the ordinary, not in a final proof that the impossible thing happened.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the fantastic premise as an old arrangement pressing against present life. It may be an inherited obligation, a building with its own rules, a family practice that still works, a debt that survives its original parties, or a place where an old authority remains active. Give that arrangement concrete modern consequences: it affects a job, a lease, a commute, a relationship, a payment, or a decision the character must make now.\n\nUse urban material detail to make the collision specific. Describe the surfaces of the present world with Murakami's calm exactness: the sound of a train, the light in a stairwell, the contents of a bag, the routine of a shop or office. Let the old arrangement show itself through those surfaces rather than through dramatic spectacle. A door that will not stay closed, a name that appears on a list, a room kept for someone who no longer comes, a rule everyone follows without saying why.\n\nKeep the atmosphere personal. The character should experience the old authority through private desire, fear, loyalty, or shame, not only through external threat. Let a social mask fall in trusted company: a conversation where the character admits what they have begun to believe, or where another person reveals the cost of compliance. These moments should feel earned by the relationship and the pressure of the situation, not forced as a beat.\n\nShow how the fantastic premise changes the experience of a particular encounter. A meeting, a walk home, a meal, or a small errand can become charged because the character now knows something about the old arrangement. The prose should register that change in attention and sensation without making every object symbolic. The horror, when it comes, should feel like an extension of the character's own implication in the arrangement, not an external monster.\n\nDo not resolve the old authority into a system. The character may learn part of its history or its cost, but the premise can remain partially unexplained. What matters is that the collision has altered the character's relation to the present world: they can no longer treat the old arrangement as mere background. End on that changed relation rather than on a completed confrontation or a full explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the lyric-philosophical inward route. Keep the narrative close to a focal consciousness that notices the world with patient attention. The prose should move between precise sensory detail and reflection on what that detail means: a particular hour, a repeated sound, the weight of an object, the way light falls in a room, the feeling of waiting.\n\nLet the fantastic premise arrive as a shift in perception rather than a public event. The character may see something impossible, hear a voice, remember an event that has not happened, or find that a familiar place no longer matches their memory of it. Describe the occurrence with the same calm exactness used for ordinary things. Do not explain it immediately. Let the character's inward response become the story: they may test the memory, compare it with what they know, or ask what it would mean if it were true.\n\nUse reflection to give the fantastic event consequence. The character may think about time, loss, love, work, or the difference between what they expected and what they have. These thoughts should arise from the specific situation, not from abstract themes. The impossible event becomes a way of asking a question the character already needed to ask: whether a choice can be undone, whether a person can be known, whether the self continues through change.\n\nKeep the outer world present but secondary. Other people may appear in conversation or memory, and practical tasks may continue. The narration should register them clearly, but the main movement is inward. A conversation can be ordinary on its surface while the character privately returns to the threshold experience. This is not concealment; it is the natural shape of a mind that cannot stop turning something over.\n\nAllow the fantastic premise to remain unresolved. The character may reach a new understanding of their own feeling without proving what happened. The story's resolution is a change in how they see the world or themselves, not a closed explanation. End at a point where that inward change is legible, even if the impossible event remains uncertain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tale route. Tell the story in a plain, personal voice that stays close to the focal character's experience. The narration should move through ordinary particulars with calm attention: a commute, a meal, a household task, a visit, a small errand. Let the character's practical life remain visible and unhurried.\n\nIntroduce the impossible event as a quiet intrusion. It may happen in a familiar place, through a familiar object, or during a routine the character has performed many times. A cat speaks once and then does not speak again. A room contains an extra door. A person the character knows appears in a photograph taken before they met. A song plays from a radio that is not plugged in. Describe the event without exclamation. The character may notice it, pause, and then continue with what they were doing.\n\nLet the strange occurrence alter the character's relation to the ordinary world. They may begin to watch for it, avoid a place, return to an object, or ask someone a careful question. The event does not need to become a quest or a public crisis. Its consequence is private: a changed habit, a new association, a memory that now seems different, a decision made for reasons the character cannot fully explain.\n\nUse conversational distance even in moments of strangeness. The character may speak about the event to another person in an offhand way, or decide not to speak about it. Their private thought should remain available but not overwrought. The narration can register fear, wonder, or uncertainty through small physical details: a pause before answering, a hand resting on a table, a window checked twice.\n\nAllow the fantastic event to remain unresolved. The character may never learn its cause or its full meaning. What matters is that it has entered their life and changed how they see something familiar. End at a point where that change is clear, without forcing a final explanation or a dramatic confrontation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-metaphysical route. Present the impossible event as a concrete fact within the story's world. The character may be surprised, but the narration should not treat the event as a hallucination or a symbol. A man finds that his shadow arrives before he does. A woman receives letters from a house she has never visited. A street appears on the map only after it has been walked. The event is real within the fiction, and the story proceeds from that reality.\n\nKeep the telling calm and exact. Use Murakami's unhurried attention to describe the event's practical details: what time it happened, what the character was carrying, what the light was like, what they did next. The strangeness should sit inside ordinary activity rather than interrupting it. The character may continue a conversation, finish a meal, or complete a task while privately absorbing what has occurred.\n\nLet the fantastic event open a metaphysical question. The character may wonder what the event means for time, identity, memory, or the relation between the self and the world. These questions should arise from the specific situation: if a shadow can arrive first, what else is already waiting? If a letter comes from a place one has never visited, what does it mean to know a place? Do not turn the prose into abstract argument. Let the question live in the character's attention to objects, places, and other people.\n\nShow the event's consequence in the character's decisions and relationships. They may change a plan, seek out someone, return to a place, or begin to notice a pattern. Other characters may accept the event, doubt it, or respond with their own strange knowledge. The fantastic premise should affect how the character acts, not only how they feel.\n\nAllow the metaphysical question to remain open. The story does not need to explain the event's origin or prove its meaning. The resolution is the character's changed understanding of the world's possibilities, made concrete in a final image or action. End there, with the impossible fact still standing and the character still living beside it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Light Novel\n\nOpen the scene inside a specific situation. The character is already doing something ordinary: walking to a station, waiting for a delivery, cleaning a room, finishing a shift, or looking for something they misplaced. Establish the place with a few concrete details so the reader can follow movement through it. Keep the opening clean and immediate; do not begin with background explanation.\n\nIntroduce the fantastic premise through a clear, observable event. A door opens onto the wrong corridor. A phone shows a message from a number the character has not saved. A vending machine dispenses an item no one ordered. A familiar building has an extra floor. Describe the event in one or two short paragraphs, with the same calm attention given to the ordinary details. The character may pause, check the surroundings, or compare the event with what they expected.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. When the character moves, make the spatial change easy to follow: they leave the room, cross the street, enter the shop, climb the stairs. When they speak, keep the exchange focused and let the other person's reaction be visible. When they think, give the private response its own short beat. The reaction can be uncertain, curious, frightened, or quietly accepting, according to the character's actual situation.\n\nLet the fantastic event affect the character's next practical choice. They may decide to follow the wrong corridor, call the number, keep the unexpected item, or ask someone about the extra floor. The choice should feel natural to the character, not forced by the need for a cliffhanger. The event's strangeness should remain present in the character's attention even as they continue with ordinary tasks.\n\nKeep the premise unresolved. The scene may end while the character is still deciding, still walking, still waiting, or still holding the strange object. Do not require a completed discovery or a dramatic reversal. The ending should leave the reader with the character's changed situation clearly established and a natural reason to continue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the fantastic as a change in the texture of ordinary attention. Begin with a practical activity or familiar surface, then let one exact image carry the impossible fact into the scene: a shadow that arrives before its owner, a door that remembers being opened, a household object that keeps a schedule of its own. Describe the thing in the same unhurried register as the surrounding work, so the strangeness appears as a condition of the world rather than an interruption of it.\n\nDevelop one comparison across the paragraph when it changes what the narrator understands. If a sound is like rain, stay with the likeness long enough to find where it departs from rain; if a room is like a held breath, let that image shape the next perception. Return to a recurring image or sound when a later occurrence gives it a new application, not as decoration. Keep plain statements available as contrast, so the shaped sentence lands against a direct one.\n\nLet the established fantastic premise affect decisions, routines, or institutions through concrete consequences. A worker may adjust a task to accommodate a strange condition; a family may keep a practice that makes sense only because the impossible thing is real. Do not explain the mechanism. Let the narrator's private associations connect the ordinary object to a concern, but keep other characters' interiority outside the focal access. The scene may end with the activity resumed or left unfinished, the altered world still present in the next small perception.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTreat the established fantastic condition as something the characters have already learned to live with, and let memory show what that learning cost. A present routine may accommodate the impossible thing so smoothly that its strangeness appears only in the difference between now and a remembered time when it was new, feared, or absent. Use a small concrete detail to carry that difference: the way a room is arranged now, a task done differently, a phrase no longer spoken.\n\nAllow reflection to arise from an ordinary object or repeated action rather than from a general announcement about loss. The narrator may recall an earlier occasion when the same object meant something else, or notice that a familiar practice continues after the person who began it is gone. Keep ordinary pleasure available; a remembered meal or a working afternoon may remain satisfying even while the narrator notices what has changed.\n\nLet the fantastic premise have irreversible consequences that shape the present without being restated as exposition. A journey, a bargain, or a change in the world may have closed off an earlier possibility, and the scene can register that closure through a detail the character now accepts. Do not require every scene to end by announcing impermanence. The reflection may conclude with the present task resumed, the remembered thing still absent, and the impossible condition continuing as a settled part of life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the concrete route. Open with a situated teller and an occasion for the telling: someone asks about a place, an object, or a past event, and the teller begins an account that has been shaped by repetition. Keep the frame in the Murakami profile's unhurried register: the room, the tea, the practical reason for the visit, and the teller's conversational distance all remain present.\n\nPresent the fantastic occurrence as a received account rather than a witnessed scene. The teller reports what was said to have happened, what someone saw, or what the community came to accept. Let the impossible fact have intelligible consequences in the tale: a road that shortens for some travelers, a well that returns what is thrown into it, a house that keeps one room for a guest who never arrives. Do not explain the mechanism, and do not require the teller to prove the account true.\n\nLet the teller's private associations shape the telling. A detail may be repeated because it belongs to the teller's own memory, and a pause may mark the place where the account touches a personal concern. The frame may return at the end with the audience's response or the teller's ordinary business resumed. Keep the source tradition coherent rather than mixing every regional convention, and do not import a roster of antiquities or a single honor code. The telling itself is the story's present action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Minimalist Prose\n\nEstablish the fantastic fact through its practical consequences, using a small number of exact details. Choose one or two concrete signs that the world works differently: a task done in a way that makes sense only under the strange condition, an object placed where it must be placed, a schedule kept for a reason no one states. Let the detail sit in a direct clause without explanation, so the reader infers the premise from what the character does.\n\nKeep the prose economical but causal. If a thought or inward connection is essential to the present account, give it one precise sentence; otherwise let the action and the selected detail carry the meaning. Do not delete the step that connects the strange condition to the character's decision. A character may avoid a door, set out water, or wait an extra hour, and the omission of the reason should leave the reason inferable rather than absent.\n\nLet the narrator's private association appear only when it materially contributes. A single remembered fact may explain why the character notices one detail rather than another. Keep other characters' interiority outside the focal access. The scene may end with the practical activity concluded or left unfinished, the strange condition still present in the next exact detail. Do not pad the prose to create atmosphere; the strangeness should be in what is selected and what is left out.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene as continuous contemporary-novel prose in which the fantastic condition is one settled fact among the character's other practical concerns. Let action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience move naturally within the paragraph. A conversation may turn to the strange condition and then back to an errand; a perception of the impossible thing may sit beside a thought about dinner or a remembered obligation.\n\nUse transitions and paragraphing to orient the reader without recap machinery. When the scene moves from a room to a street or from a present exchange to a recollection, let a concrete detail carry the shift. Keep the narration in the Murakami profile's unhurried register: describe the strange thing evenly, without raising the prose temperature, and let its oddness appear through the character's calm accommodation of it.\n\nLet the established fantastic premise affect the scene's practical decisions and institutions. A workplace may have a rule that exists because of the strange condition; a family may plan a journey around a fact no one needs to explain. Do not pause the scene to explain the mechanism. The narrator's private associations may connect an ordinary object to a concern, but keep other characters' interiority outside the focal access. The scene may end with the present business continuing, the impossible fact still present as part of the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect scenes that make the established relationship premise legible through the couple's or pair's particular habits. Choose moments where the fantastic condition touches their shared routine: one person knows how the other accommodates the strange fact, or a private phrase has acquired a meaning tied to an impossible occurrence. Let the scene's practical activity carry the relationship; cooking, repairing, waiting, or walking together can show the attachment without announcing it.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments as callbacks. A phrase, a gesture, or an object may return when a later occurrence gives it new emotional weight. Keep the Murakami profile's conversational distance: the characters need not declare their feelings in polished speeches, and the narrator need not inflate the prose to prove the emotion. Let a withheld sentence or an ordinary question carry more than an explanation would.\n\nGive the relationship consequences that fulfill its particular promise. Affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery should have enough space where it belongs, and the fantastic premise should affect what the characters can offer or ask of one another. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; use only the actual supplied premise. Keep other characters' interiority outside the focal access. The scene may end with the shared routine resumed or interrupted, the emotional consequence still settling rather than resolved on command.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the fantastic as a deed that entered the community's memory. Open with a deliberate, compact declaration of what was done or what changed, then expand into the particulars that make the remembered account incomplete. The impossible fact should have public consequence: a road opened, a bargain kept, a household saved or altered. Keep the chronicle's scale at the present story's size; an errand or a family task can be the deed remembered.\n\nUse recurring phrasing and designations that come from established acts rather than ornamental epithets. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Let the Murakami profile's practical attention keep the telling unhurried: describe the work of the deed, the ordinary objects involved, and the calm way the strange thing was accepted. Do not inflate the prose into ceremonial speech or heroic doom.\n\nLet the teller's private association temper the public account. The chronicle may record what the community remembers, while the focal character's inward attention registers what the deed cost or left unfinished. Keep shared reputation distinct from private experience. Dialogue may interrupt the authority of the chronicle when someone objects to a detail or answers an elevated introduction with a practical concern. The account may end with the deed's consequence still settling, another modest act available to be remembered rather than declared legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantasy through a definite Scandinavian folktale-inflected route: an impossible premise enters ordinary social and domestic life, and its consequences become legible through work, housing, family, neighborhood, or local institutions. Use the actual setting, season, and community supplied by the story; do not treat the region as a landscape of snow and silence.\n\nLet the telling move between the community's shared account and the focal character's private attention. A neighbor may report the strange thing as a known local occurrence, a family member may treat it as a practical problem, and the focal character may notice the one detail that does not fit the shared version. Keep these layers distinct: what people say, what they believe, what the focal character observes, and what remains unresolved. The community's account can be wrong, partial, or accurate without being certified by the narration.\n\nUse Murakami's calm exact-surface manner. Describe the strange thing with the same unhurried attention given to making coffee, repairing a window, waiting for a bus, or completing a shift. Let the narrator notice ordinary particulars precisely, then let one detail carry the impossible fact without raising the prose temperature. The threshold may remain unresolved: a door that opens onto the wrong street, a person who remembers a meeting that never happened, an animal that returns with knowledge it should not have. Do not explain the mechanism or make every scene fact unstable.\n\nLet the fantastic premise have practical consequences that recur and accumulate. Show how it affects a household budget, a workplace schedule, a neighbor's obligation, a municipal form, a family meal, or a long-standing local custom. The wonder should change what people can do or must do, not merely decorate a mood. Let characters respond with practical concern, quiet acceptance, avoidance, or ordinary fear, and let their responses differ according to their actual positions and relationships.\n\nAllow private concern to attach to ordinary objects and routines. A character may associate a particular coat, timetable, radio, or stairwell with an unresolved question, and the narration may return to that object when the concern returns. Keep the association available to the focal character without making other characters know it. The interiority should be composed and specific, not a sudden explanatory monologue.\n\nLet the narration move between the social surface and the inward account without switching into essayistic commentary. When a character reflects, ground the reflection in a concrete moment: what they were doing, what they could see, what they chose not to say. The fantastic element may remain unexplained, but its consequences should remain legible. A scene can end after a practical result, a small change in understanding, or a quiet acknowledgment that something has altered, without a revelation or a completed explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Screenplay\n\nWrite the fantasy in screen-oriented scenes. Use scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines for visible and audible events, and clear speaker cues for dialogue. Keep action lines concrete: what the camera can see or the audience can hear, not what a character privately feels.\n\nLet Murakami's calm attention live in the arrangement of ordinary action. A character washes a cup, checks a timetable, waits at a crossing, or folds a shirt while discussing something strange. Describe the action in even, unhurried blocks. The impossible event should appear with the same plainness: a second shadow on the wall, a train arriving with no passengers, a cat that speaks once and then does not. Do not signal the fantastic with sudden stylistic elevation or explanatory dialogue.\n\nShow private concern through repeated visible attention rather than announcing it. A character may return to the same coat, timetable, radio, or stairwell across scenes, touching or checking it without comment. Let the repetition accumulate: the audience sees the character's attention settle on the object, then sees the object later in a changed context. The association remains visible without becoming a truth indicator or a confession.\n\nUse dialogue to carry social distance and practical response. Characters may discuss the strange thing as they would discuss a delayed delivery or a neighbor's illness: with concern, avoidance, matter-of-fact acceptance, or quiet disagreement. Let them not say everything they think. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge.\n\nWhen the active contract requires private interiority, present it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not as dialogue or knowledge available to the cast. Use voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs it, and do not let voice-over replace required private prose. An offscreen sound may orient a scene, but it does not certify what caused it.\n\nUse cuts and transitions selectively. A cut may move from a domestic moment to the place where the strange consequence appears, but do not fill the page with camera commands. The scene can end after a visible result, an unanswered question, or a quiet continuation of routine. The fantasy may remain unexplained as long as its visible consequences remain intelligible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Stage Play\n\nWrite the fantasy as a playable script. Use clear speaker names, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Keep stage directions economical: they establish what the audience can see and what the actors do, not what a character privately feels.\n\nLet Murakami's calm attention live in the relationship between lines and physical business. A character enters with a shopping bag, sets a kettle on a burner, folds a newspaper, or stands at a window while speaking. The strange thing should enter through the same ordinary stage world: a door that should not be there, a telephone that rings with no caller, a coat that returns itself to the hook. Stage the impossible event plainly, without a sudden shift in tone or an explanatory speech.\n\nShow private concern through repeated staged attention rather than announcing it. A character may return to the same coat, timetable, radio, or stairwell across scenes, touching or checking it without comment. Let the repetition accumulate: the audience sees the character's attention settle on the object, then sees the object later in a changed context. The association remains visible without becoming a truth indicator or a confession.\n\nUse dialogue to carry social distance and practical response. Characters may discuss the strange thing as they would discuss a household problem or a work schedule: with concern, avoidance, matter-of-fact acceptance, or quiet disagreement. Let them not say everything they think. A pause can carry more than a confession.\n\nWhen the active contract requires private interiority, preserve it as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, not as an aside or monologue another character can hear. Use a monologue or aside only when it has a dramatic purpose in the scene. Another character does not know a focal conclusion merely because the audience has followed its formation.\n\nLet entrances and exits shape the scene. A character may leave before a question is answered, or arrive after a strange event has already altered the room. The scene can end after a practical result, an unanswered question, or a quiet continuation of routine. The fantasy may remain unresolved as long as its staged consequences remain legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the fantasy through one authorized mind's associative movement. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish an actual event from a recollection, a wish, or a private fantasy.\n\nUse Murakami's calm attention as the mind's habitual texture. The character notices a particular sound, the weight of a bag, the order of objects on a shelf, the time on a station clock, and then drifts toward a memory or an unresolved question. Let the drift return to the ordinary detail rather than resolve into a conclusion. The strange thing may arrive in the middle of this movement: a remembered street now leading somewhere else, a familiar voice speaking from an empty room, a train that passes without sound. Describe it with the same even attention given to the ordinary detail.\n\nLet the stream carry the fantasy's consequences through recurring details and legible changes. A route that no longer works, a missed appointment, a household object that behaves differently, or a phrase from a conversation that returns with new weight can surface when the mind circles back. The character may notice the change without naming it, or may connect it to an older concern. The consequence remains a world fact even when the character doubts or misremembers the event.\n\nKeep the impossible event a world fact, not a symptom of the telling. The character may doubt it, misremember it, or connect it to an older concern, but the narration should not make every scene fact indeterminate. The fantasy may remain unexplained, yet its consequences should remain legible.\n\nLet recurring concern attach to particular objects and routines. A coat, a timetable, a radio, a stairwell, or a phrase from a conversation may return when the mind circles back to the same question. Do not require the character to resolve the question or announce an insight. The mental movement can stop after a small perception, an interruption, or a quiet acknowledgment that something has altered.\n\nDo not make every character share a mind or grant access to excluded minds. The stream belongs to the authorized focal character. Other people remain known through what the focal mind perceives, remembers, or imagines about them, and their actual private experience stays outside that access unless separately authorized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the fantasy as a story carried by readable sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in short practical lines, another in longer careful sentences, another leaves a message unfinished.\n\nLet Murakami's calm attention live in what people choose to send and what they leave out. They may report an ordinary task, ask about a timetable, confirm a meeting, or describe a household object while the strange thing enters through the exchange itself: a message that arrives before it was sent, a contact who answers from a number that no longer exists, a photograph showing a room that has changed. Describe the interface event plainly, without making it a dramatic system or an explained mechanism.\n\nShow private concern through repeated message content rather than announcing it. A character may mention the same coat, timetable, radio, or stairwell in different exchanges, asking about it or reporting it without comment. Let the repetition accumulate: the reader sees the character's attention settle on the object, then sees the object later in a changed context. The association remains visible in what the character chooses to send, without becoming a truth indicator or a confession.\n\nUse conversational distance in the messages. Characters may discuss the strange thing as they would discuss a delayed delivery or a work schedule: with concern, avoidance, matter-of-fact acceptance, or quiet disagreement. Let them not say everything they think. A delay or a read receipt may matter, but do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. When the active contract requires private interiority, present it in the active presentation clearly separated from the transcript, not as a message another character can read. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript.\n\nLet the fantasy remain unresolved while its consequences stay legible. A changed plan, a missed connection, a repeated question, or a quiet acknowledgment that something has altered can carry the effect. The exchange can end after a practical result, an unanswered message, or a sender's decision to stop typing, without a revelation or a completed explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the fantasy in scene-oriented visual-novel presentation. Use concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nLet Murakami's calm attention live in the scene-setting and focal narration. Describe the room, the street, the station, or the kitchen in a few exact details, then let the focal character notice one ordinary thing with particular care: the way a curtain hangs, the sound of a refrigerator, the order of shoes by the door. The strange thing should enter through the same composed attention: a door that opens onto the wrong hallway, a reflection that moves a moment late, a cat that speaks once and then returns to grooming. Describe it evenly, without raising the prose temperature.\n\nShow private concern through repeated focal attention rather than announcing it. A character may return to the same coat, timetable, radio, or stairwell across scenes, noticing or checking it without comment. Let the repetition accumulate: the reader sees the character's attention settle on the object, then sees the object later in a changed context. The association remains visible in the focal narration without becoming a truth indicator or a confession.\n\nUse dialogue to carry social distance and practical response. Characters may discuss the strange thing as they would discuss a household problem or a work schedule: with concern, avoidance, matter-of-fact acceptance, or quiet disagreement. Let them not say everything they think. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge.\n\nUse background or expression cues as purposeful references, not automatic truth indicators. A changed expression may attract attention, but a contradiction in what a character says or does must do the interpretive work. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals what they feel.\n\nLet the fantasy remain unresolved while its consequences stay legible. A changed route, a missed appointment, a household object that no longer behaves as it should, or a quiet acknowledgment that something has altered can carry the effect. The scene can end after a practical result, an unanswered question, or a continuation of routine, without a revelation or a completed explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagajbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Web Serial\n\nWrite the fantasy as installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line. Keep clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps, and let the recovered particular be the one that matters to the current moment rather than a retelling of everything that came before.\n\nLet Murakami's calm attention shape each installment. Describe an ordinary task, a walk, a meal, or a household repair with unhurried precision, then let the strange thing enter through the same even attention: a street that now leads somewhere else, a train that arrives with no passengers, a person who remembers a meeting that never happened. Describe the impossible event plainly, without raising the prose temperature or forcing a dramatic turn.\n\nShow private concern through recovered particulars rather than announcing it. A character may return to the same coat, timetable, radio, or stairwell across installments, noticing or checking it without comment. When the concern returns, recover only the specific detail that matters: the object's position, its sound, its weight, or the last time the character saw it. Let the repetition accumulate across installments without becoming a truth indicator or a confession.\n\nUse conversational distance and practical response in dialogue. Characters may discuss the strange thing as they would discuss a delayed delivery or a work schedule: with concern, avoidance, matter-of-fact acceptance, or quiet disagreement. Let them not say everything they think. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge.\n\nLet the fantasy remain unresolved while its consequences stay legible across installments. A changed plan, a missed connection, a household object that no longer behaves as it should, or a quiet acknowledgment that something has altered can carry the effect. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger.\n\nDo not create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules merely because the style is selected. The serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make the explanation perpetually recede. An installment can close after completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet the established fantastic premise create a practical social consequence that the viewpoint character cannot discuss without exposing need, fear, or resentment. The impossible thing is not a distant marvel; it alters an obligation, a visit, an inheritance, a debt, a household arrangement, or a reputation. Write the strain through what people actually say and do, not through a vocabulary of anguish.\n\nUse free indirect judgment to keep turbulent feeling inside a clear owner. The narration may compress a character's reasoning, notice the gap between a polite phrase and its probable meaning, or register how an offer sounds like an accusation. Do not let the narrator supply feelings the character has not reached. Another person's calm can be genuine, mistaken, or self-protective; the viewpoint may not know which.\n\nMake admission costly in the scene's own terms. A character may reveal a fear and receive only practical help, or apologize and discover that the apology does not repair the original harm. Let the conversation continue under that unresolved weight. Affection, when it appears, may be awkward, partial, or offered through an ordinary service rather than a declaration. The story may end with the relationship still strained, but it must not be pushed toward automatic tragedy or compulsory reconciliation.\n\nGround the fantasy in the same social attention as the rest of the scene. If a magical object, creature, place, or condition affects the household, show who must manage it, who is blamed for it, who profits from it, and who avoids mentioning it. A change in magical circumstance should alter those social positions rather than merely decorate them. Keep the viewpoint's knowledge limited to what the active contract permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Each speech should belong to a clear speaker, and each narrative paragraph should stay close to what the active viewpoint can observe, know, or infer. Do not merge two characters' reactions into one unattributed paragraph.\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as part of the scene's social machinery. A magical condition may affect who may speak first, who is owed a courtesy, what a household can afford, or what a visitor is permitted to know. Let conversation carry those pressures. A polite phrase can acknowledge an obligation, refuse an intimacy, or conceal a practical fear. Show the viewpoint interpreting such exchanges without making every inference correct.\n\nKeep NPCs and world behavior active. A servant may continue a task, an animal or magical presence may react to the conversation, or a household rule may constrain the next move. These details should follow from the established world rather than interrupt it. The viewpoint may act on a partial understanding and discover the result in the next turn.\n\nEnd the response at a natural continuation point. The scene may pause during continuing speech, thought, action, or an unfinished arrangement. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or decision prompt. Preserve the active narrative person, action markers, and player-control settings. A private thought may accompany the outward exchange, but it must remain the viewpoint's own and must not grant knowledge of other minds.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route: advance the story through connected scenes or chapters that each have a clear occasion, exchange, or change in circumstance. Let the episode close with a settled development that also opens the next social question. Do not present the other available routes as a menu; this recipe commits to episodic telling.\n\nWithin each episode, apply Austen-style social interpretation to the fantastic premise. A spirit, talisman, magical inheritance, altered body, or uncanny place should affect who is respected, who is burdened, who may marry or travel, and who is believed. Conversation and courtesy carry these consequences. A character may be praised for a magical gift while being excluded from ordinary trust, or may receive help that also lowers their standing.\n\nUse free indirect judgment to keep the viewpoint's social reading distinct from the narrator's. The narration may notice that a host's generosity resembles a demand, or that a family's gratitude sounds like fear. Let the viewpoint misunderstand another person's motive and discover the error through a later exchange or a practical result. Keep the language and community consistent with the supplied setting rather than importing an English manor or a generic imperial court.\n\nMake the fantastic element legible through its social effects. An episode may show how a magical object is stored, lent, hidden, or argued over. The story need not explain the object's full origin or mechanism. Recurrence should deepen the reader's understanding of a relationship or household, not restart the same suspicion. Keep each episode's information available to the active viewpoint and contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move from one concrete fact to the next without explanatory padding. Let a worn threshold, a cracked charm, a stained sleeve, or a cold hearth carry the scene's pressure. Physical wear should reflect the actual fantastic premise: a magical working may damage its user, a household may ration its enchanted resource, or a journey may leave visible marks on people and gear.\n\nKeep Austen-style social interpretation inside the concrete action. A character may notice that a host's courtesy costs nothing because someone else does the work, or that an offer of shelter comes with a claim on future service. Show these readings through what the viewpoint sees, handles, or avoids, not through abstract commentary. The narration may state the inference plainly, but it must remain the viewpoint's judgment.\n\nVary rhythm by cutting between short physical beats and a longer passage when the character's reasoning needs room. A sudden magical failure, a slammed door, or a dropped object can interrupt a polite exchange. Let the consequence stay practical: a debt increases, a route closes, a tool breaks, or a person must choose between two costly obligations. Do not inflate every setback into mortal danger.\n\nGround abrasive or rough speech in its speaker. A frightened person may snap, a servant may speak bluntly when no guest is present, or a character may use courtesy as a weapon. The fantasy element remains part of the material world: show its weight, residue, upkeep, or failure. Do not turn the story into survival horror or erase the social meanings that the Austen profile supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. A letter to a sibling, a household account, a report to a patron, or a private journal entry knows only what its author knows and reveals only what its author chooses or cannot help revealing. Do not add an omniscient explanation after each record.\n\nLet the fantastic premise enter through the document's practical concerns. A correspondent may describe an enchanted object as a household expense, a dangerous journey as a social obligation, or a magical change as a threat to reputation. The document's tone should follow its purpose: a letter seeking money may flatter, a report may minimize blame, and a journal may admit a fear the writer would not speak aloud.\n\nUse gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts to expose Austen-style social misreadings. One writer may interpret a host's courtesy as friendship while another records the same visit as a demand for payment. A later document may correct an earlier account without the earlier writer ever learning the truth. Let the reader compare what each author noticed, omitted, or misunderstood. Keep the sequence readable by giving each document a clear place in the developing situation.\n\nPreserve the distinction between a composed document and live private thought. A journal entry records what its author decided to set down; it is not automatic access to the character's complete mind. The document's material form may matter: a torn page, a delayed letter, an unsent draft, or a copied record can carry information. Use the specific document form required by the scene rather than rotating through every available format.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness and familiar preferences. A character may remember how another likes a room warmed, which food is safe, what route avoids a difficult neighbor, or how a magical tool must be handled. Let the fantasy premise supply the practical care: an enchanted household may need tending, a traveling companion may need a charm renewed, or a magical creature may respond to a particular person's voice.\n\nKeep affection sincere but not compulsory. A character may show care through a small service, a moment of patience, or a private observation they do not speak aloud. No one must confess love or complete an emotional repair. Let personality and privacy remain: a kind person may still be reserved, a grateful person may still disagree, and a comfortable room may still contain an unresolved worry.\n\nUse Austen-style social interpretation to make the comfort specific. A courtesy may be meaningful because it costs the giver something, or because it recognizes a preference others ignore. The viewpoint may notice that an offer is kind without being intimate, or that a family's ease depends on someone's quiet labor. These observations should deepen the warmth rather than turn it into suspicion.\n\nKeep manageable friction inside the scene. A minor misunderstanding, a household inconvenience, or a difference in taste can create room for care without becoming a crisis. The fantasy element should remain part of the world: show its small pleasures and ordinary upkeep. Do not remove established problems or make everyone agreeable. The story may end in a moment of shared ease that does not resolve every difficulty.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. A scene may open with the household's history, the season's demands, or the public meaning of a visit before narrowing to a particular exchange. Let the narration frame the fantastic premise through social context: how a magical inheritance is discussed, which families may use a certain power, what a village believes about an uncanny place, or how an institution records an impossible event.\n\nKeep Austen-style free indirect judgment with a clear owner. The narration may compress a character's reasoning, notice the difference between a public courtesy and a private motive, or register the irony of a reputation built on a misunderstood magical accident. Do not let the narrator supply feelings or knowledge the viewpoint does not possess. Measured irony should belong to the narrator or the focal character as the active contract permits.\n\nUse public and private contrast to organize a passage. A formal dinner may present one account of a magical event while a later private conversation reveals another. A letter may be read aloud with one emphasis and understood differently by its recipient. The fantastic premise should change social positions: who is invited, who is blamed, who is owed an explanation, or who may travel safely. Show these changes through the novel's social texture rather than explaining them as a system.\n\nDistinguish narration from ordinary spoken language. The prose may be more periodic and composed than dialogue, but it need not become archaic or uniformly elaborate. Ceremony, correspondence, and reputation may organize a passage when relevant. Keep the story's actual setting, language, and community distinct; do not import an aristocratic English estate or a marriage plot merely because the author profile supplies social interpretation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen in a gathering whose members have interests in one another, and give the telling an occasion and a purpose beyond entertainment: to advise, delay, warn, flatter, or change how someone present reads an offer, obligation, or family question already forming. Treat the performance itself as a social exchange — an offer, a courtesy, a bid for standing, a slight managed by omission — so that who tells, who must listen, who is permitted to interrupt, and what the teller hurries past all carry meaning. Let the established fantastic premise bear directly on this exchange: it may set what such a telling costs or obligates, whom it endangers or dignifies, or why this listener needs the tale now, so that the wonder does social work rather than decorating the evening.\n\nInside the embedded tale, carry the fantasy in social terms. Its marvels need no explained system; make them intelligible through material and reputational consequence — what a character may now do, owe, claim, inherit, or admit to — and give the tale's people their own self-belief, the one certain of her prudence, the one flattered by his own generosity, shown through what their exchanges reveal rather than through announcement. Treat the told tale as a performance composed for this audience: its polish is craft, not a window onto the teller's mind. Whatever calculation, affection, or unease runs beneath it is the teller's actual private experience; keep it present as nonspoken experience under the active interiority contract, not merely implied by the performance's finish.\n\nKeep the two levels legible and give every judgment an owner. Mark the passage into the tale with an opening formula, an address, or the audience's settling, and return to the frame through interruption, question, or reaction. The listeners' small contributions — a question about money or precedence, a glance between rivals, a held breath at a particular name — reveal their interests even when they settle nothing about the tale. Free indirect judgment may operate on both levels, but always attributed: the teller colors a character's motives; the frame's narration measures the teller; a listener privately misreads either. Whatever narrator, person, and ownership the active contract supplies, carry the nested tale in the teller's spoken voice within it, and treat a listener's private reading as that listener's own experience.\n\nLet one phrase, form of words, or object pass between the levels and accrue meaning. The second appearance — a proverb the tale taught, or a formula for refusing a gift, now spoken in the frame — is where the tale's bearing on the outer situation can become plain, so that the matter in the frame can read differently afterward, whether or not anyone says so. Work delayed revelation through this changed hearing rather than through a withheld announcement, and let repetition deepen understanding without certifying that the tale is true.\n\nEnd where the scene's own boundary falls: mid-tale, mid-question, or in the quiet after a phrase lands. An altered reading may stay private and unspoken, with no confession, lesson, or cliffhanger owed. Take the Arabian Nights as craft source only — situated teller, nesting, meaningful recurrence — without importing its palaces, djinn, deserts, or any particular religion; use the setting, cast, and social world actually supplied, let speech be as plain or as warm as those people warrant, and preserve the active narrator, perspective, and output contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the Romantic and uncanny route as the governing method. Treat the fantastic premise as an established condition of ordinary life: it has already changed what people can own, owe, inherit, promise, hide, travel past, or ask of one another. Show those consequences in household arrangements, work, reputation, law, ritual, and family expectation. Let wonder sit beside errands, meals, negotiations, and fatigue. Leave some uncanny effects unexplained, with characters encountering them through partial knowledge, local custom, and practical consequence rather than through a complete system.\n\nAnchor the telling in a viewpoint whose social position is concrete and material. That character notices who can afford courtesy, who depends on whom, what an offer costs, what an omission protects, and how family or reputation narrows a choice. Use conversation and circumstance to give a small exchange several possible meanings at once: a kindness may be genuine and strategic, a refusal prudent and cruel, a marvel welcomed as advantage by one person and read as bad blood by another. Let characters interpret the impossible through their own habits, interests, and limits; do not make everyone equally perceptive or equally ironic.\n\nCarry Austen's free indirect judgment and measured irony with a clear owner. Let private assessment arise in the idiom of the character who thinks it, then allow later speech, evidence, or arrangement to confirm, complicate, or overturn that assessment. The narrator may see more than the character, but the irony should land on a particular assumption rather than spray over the cast. Keep nonspoken interiority active—sensation, calculation, memory, dread, hope, self-correction—so the fantastic is experienced as well as observed; do not let letters, reported speech, or documents stand in for the viewpoint's private experience. Do not force polished speech, a marriage plot, or a period setting; the social pressure comes from the story's world, not from imitation furniture.\n\nLet syntax and distance follow the narrator's relation to the viewpoint. In composed social passages, use balanced or suspended sentences that hold several obligations in view. When the impossible presses close, let the sentence loosen, delay its main clause, or fix on a concrete detail whose familiarity has become strange. Move between intimacy and remote summary when the viewpoint's composure changes, but keep the grammar readable and the distance motivated by character and scene rather than by an idea of German-language severity or translated word order.\n\nAdvance through scenes in which the fantastic premise alters ordinary information flow. A visit, a negotiation, a shared task, a letter, or a ritual can reveal a new consequence of the premise and change what earlier behavior meant. Revisit a place or object only to deepen its significance, not to restart the same wonder. Let practical decisions remain contingent: someone may cooperate, refuse, delay, or choose wrongly. Uncertainty about the premise can persist, and a social consequence may remain unsettled. End where the current attention, speech, action, or thought rests, without requiring a revelation, a proposal, or a restored order.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the fantastic premise as a social fact. Whatever the world already establishes — a gift passed down, a debt owed to an unseen authority, a street where an older law still answers — let it act through money, family, reputation, and obligation, not through explained mechanics, and let it genuinely change what people can do: whose promise binds, which debts can be collected, who must be obeyed. Enter each scene at the level of ordinary business — an errand, a payment, a visit, a refusal — and let the premise surface as a term of that business rather than as spectacle. Then a small exchange can carry several meanings at once: an offered escort, a loan quietly made, a name left off a list may be protection, claim, courtship, or surveillance, and the scene's work is to let that reading shift as people speak, not to settle it on cue. An exchange may close with its meaning still unresolved, to be revised by later circumstance.\n\nGive the collision of old authority and present life a material address. Set the inherited charter, the fading institution, the ancestor's unrevoked standing against rent, transit, work, and the specific decay of the district, so that urban detail sharpens the social reading instead of decorating it: who holds keys, whose name still opens doors, which rooms the wiring no longer reaches. Courtesy itself can be an artifact of the old order — polished forms kept up as a claim of standing — while others speak plainly, and the contrast shows who believes what about themselves. Let conversation and circumstance reveal the distance between that self-regard and actual conduct.\n\nNarrate through a focal character's judging intelligence, free indirect and clearly owned. The irony may be sharp, generous, or mistaken; it belongs to this narrator, whose private interpretations, desires, and calculations appear in the prose as living thought rather than summary. Other minds stay closed, read through speech, action, and omission, and give the reader the same evidence the narrator weighs, so a misreading can be recognized as well as felt. Let atmosphere change how an encounter is experienced — the same offer made in a bright café and on a wet back stair is not the same offer — without making every kindness a trap or every dim corridor an omen.\n\nLet a mask fall only when a particular character has earned reason to lower it — trusted company, plain exhaustion — and let what is then admitted, whether desire, resentment, or fear of the inherited thing, diverge from public courtesy for reasons specific to that person. The gap is where intimacy turns morally complicated: affection across a debt, loyalty to a house that constrains, gratitude shading into obligation. Keep the premise's consequences intelligible in daily life — what it changes about standing, options, and safety — while leaving its mysteries unexplained, and take no vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules from any other setting; the old authority here is whatever the active world already establishes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the domestic social-realist register of this tradition: a world of households, reciprocal duties, and reputation, where the family rather than the solitary person is the unit that holds fortune, and the community's talk presses on everyone like weather. Let the fantastic premise enter that world the way property and income do — established, unmysterious to those who live with it, practical in its terms. Whether the supplied premise is a season-bound power, a debt owed across a threshold, or an entitlement that passes with the house, treat it as the household's asset and burden at once. What it costs to keep, who may draw on its benefit, which kin it binds — settle these as facts of dependence, and attach them to the whole family, so that one member's errand or spoken word commits the rest and a single offer of help is felt as touching everyone's standing.\n\nGive small exchanges several meanings. A courtesy, an omission, a delicately withheld question, a neighbor's proud account of carrying the same burden in another house — each can read as kindness, condescension, courtship, or ambition, judged by the categories the community already uses for reciprocity: its forms of hospitality and address, its sense of what may be asked and what must be offered. Carry the focal character's appraisals in free indirect style, owned by that one consciousness in whatever narrator and person the active contract supplies — mortification at needing help, private satisfaction in having managed better, the silent retelling of another's speech as vanity. Let irony arise there, from what someone believes about themselves while the exchange quietly shows otherwise. No other mind is entered; the reader may know more than any single figure does because two estimates of the same person sit side by side, never because the narration certifies either. Do not explain the premise's mechanism; let it act as dependence acts — an errand declined, an invitation not repeated, a visit shortened — so that the unfamiliar keeps an intelligible social weight beside work, family, and personal disappointment.\n\nLet private thought take the reflective turn this realism allows: between exchanges, the focal mind may dwell on duty, self-respect, or what one generation owes another, until a question about the house becomes a question about the self; such an interval needs no practical task to justify it and may end unresolved. Where characters misread one another, let partial knowledge and self-regard do the work — an omission taken for disdain, a courtesy for a claim — and let the story confirm, complicate, or overturn the reading without announcing a verdict. Keep language, region, community, and period exactly as supplied; invent no speech patterns, religious or caste detail, or generic family structure, and let speech be as plain or as formal as these people actually are. Stop wherever the social moment actually stands — mid-visit, an arrangement half-made, a remark whose consequence is still open — and let what courtesy, pride, or prudence declines to say remain living private thought beside the spoken surface.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as a personal record of occasions—scenes, remarks, and reflections kept by someone with a reason to keep them—grouped like a reflective miscellany rather than a single plot that must close. Let intervals pass through gaps: a later entry shows what has changed in the household, the arrangements, or the teller's estimate of someone instead of summarizing the time between. The record may speak as I or hold the teller at a small distance; in either case, selection and grouping carry the method. The record is a composed artifact, not automatically the teller's complete live mind; when private experience matters, write the actual nonspoken thought, sensation, and reasoning as it occurs, not only what the entry would choose to set down.\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as a working condition of ordinary life. Establish early and lightly who can do the impossible thing, what it costs them and others, who is entitled to ask, and how households, employers, parishes, guilds, leases, or inheritances have adjusted around it. Let those adjustments carry the wonder: a fee paid in advance, a favour that cannot decently be refused, an inheritance written around a capability, an invitation sent for that reason alone. Its limits appear as things people plan around rather than as rules recited. Because the capability is known, it is also read socially, like wealth or connection: characters estimate one another by it, resent it, conceal it, or make a performance of not needing it. An uncanny act shows what someone can do and proves nothing reliable about what they mean; a demonstration, a rescue, or a refusal does not settle anyone's character.\n\nKeep the irony owned and the money visible. Render the focal character's self-account from inside—how she explains her own motives, what she takes her courtesy to be doing, what she hopes an offer meant—while the arrangements the entry describes sit slightly askew of that account. Let the reader measure the distance; the narration need not announce the verdict. Manners sharpen against dependence: track who pays, who must travel or be stayed with, whose reputation an association exposes, and what a refusal costs. The premise can enter this accounting on either side, as an asset that raises a family's expectations, a liability that narrows a choice, or a service that obliges both parties. A decision made under those conditions shows its calculation, and the private reasoning may be considerably less polite than the speech.\n\nUse omission deliberately. What the record leaves unspoken—an amount, a connection, the reason a visitor did not return—reads as a choice made under social pressure, and the missing fact can arrive later in the teller's own terms and revise what the earlier entry meant. Keep access and knowledge separate: the record may render the focal character's actual private experience in full while another person's mind stays closed. Where speech and thought diverge, give the divergence a local cause—propriety, dependence, a self-flattering habit, a wish not to look—and let them agree completely when nothing presses. Let the language follow the teller rather than a period manner: a blunt aunt, a careful dependent, someone writing some years after the events, and a servant with a good ear each keep a different record, one stating feeling plainly and another arranging facts so that feeling must be inferred. Season, weather, and occasion may carry attention and shifts of mood, but no inventory of scenery is owed and no universal quietness applies; a comic, exasperated, or theatrical entry is as available as a subdued one. If the teller already knows how things turned out, let that knowledge colour the telling without being installed as the earlier motive.\n\nStop where the record naturally stops: mid-visit, mid-negotiation, on a decision not yet acted on, or in a private interval of thought. The account need not conclude a courtship, discharge a debt, explain the premise, or settle what a relationship is. The boundary is where the current occasion reaches its edge, not where every question closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as an intimate social chronicle of a particular place and its people, rendered in the register of weather, prices, illness, roads, and local gossip. An established fantastic premise is already ordinary fact, not a spectacle and not a puzzle to be solved. It shapes what people can do, ask, owe, inherit, hide, or refuse. Its limits, costs, seasons, and refusals are known unevenly and locally; the account may leave genuinely unresolved what remains unexplained. Keep the supplied language, place, period, and community; do not replace them with generic regional flavor or an inventory of landscape.\n\nGive the narration a clear owner. Let one focal character's private judgment run through the prose in their own vocabulary of propriety, worth, obligation, good sense, and embarrassment. Free indirect movement measures other people's conduct against their standards and exposes motives they would not say aloud. Chronicle habit and focal irony can work together: the telling may report what a household or neighborhood conventionally repeats, while the focal character's assessment stays an assessment rather than a verdict. Irony belongs to someone. Do not make the whole cast polished, perceptive, or quietly amused, and do not let the narration grade every character from above.\n\nLet small exchanges carry several meanings at once. An offer, a polite refusal, an introduction, an omission, an arrangement about money or inheritance, a favor asked on someone else's behalf: surface courtesy and practical negotiation can both be running. Where the fantastic premise lets one person do what another cannot, or makes someone depend on another's access to it, that asymmetry enters the ordinary traffic of family, neighborliness, service, and reputation. Nothing requires a ballroom, a proposal, or a marriage plot; a market transaction, a debt, or a request between relatives does the same work.\n\nKeep nonspoken life as lived thought: attention, calculation, embarrassment, fear, hope, and the reasoning that connects an event to a choice. Do not replace it with summary, a letter the character might compose, or an aside they might perform. Where the active presentation uses hidden interiority, place actual private experience in that block rather than summarizing it; visible interiority belongs in the narration. The difference between what is thought and what is said should have a character-specific cause, such as dependence, courtesy, hope of advantage, concealment, or simple habit, and the two may also agree. Private thought stays with its owner; others act on what they observe, infer, or are told, and may infer wrongly.\n\nLet time move as a chronicle moves. Later knowledge may frame earlier events, but what a character knew then is not quietly replaced by what the narrator has learned since, and a recollection gains nothing from agreeing with a reputation. If written records or secondhand accounts enter the telling, keep them distinct from what the people living through the events understood at the time. The scene may close during ongoing conversation, work, thought, or an unfinished arrangement; no settlement is required. The matter need not be resolved, and no one must be married, exposed, or improved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Light Novel\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as an established fact of dependence, the way money, land, and family connection are facts of dependence elsewhere: it can be called on, withheld, lent, inherited, or quietly resented, and it changes what a household can do and what its neighbours make of them. The premise need not be kingdom-scale; a talent that runs in one family, a household spirit, or a binding that lasts a season works by the same method, and wonder can sit beside ordinary work, family, travel, or private discovery. Stage the impossible where it does work — the light under a door, the shape the air takes when a name is spoken, the weariness the worker carries afterward — with the same plain clarity you would give a drawing room or a doorway. The first time an unfamiliar term or mechanic changes what someone can do, give it one brief, ordinary line of presentation and keep moving; the scene does not have to explain how the whole system runs.\n\nRead each exchange on two levels: the courtesy on the surface, and the negotiation of cost, obligation, family, and reputation underneath. A question about what a working took from the person who performed it may be concern, curiosity, a probe for advantage, or the opening of an offer; let the viewpoint character settle on one reading and be wrong about it. Keep the price visible in the scene's arrangements — if a gift draws on strength, time, standing, or another person's goodwill, that cost stays in what people can and cannot do, even when nobody names it. Let people's accounts of their own kindness, forbearance, or good judgement be tested by what circumstance actually shows.\n\nGive the irony a clear owner. A judgement about someone else's motive belongs to a specific mind: a quick beat of private assessment in the focal character's own register, or a single measured narrative line that plainly comes from the viewpoint rather than from an omniscient verdict. Let each speaker keep a register of their own — blunt, circumspect, or over-talkative when nervous — rather than one polished varnish over the whole cast. A smile, a flush, or a pause attracts interpretation but does not certify it; the meaning sits in what was offered, what was refused, and what went unmentioned.\n\nCarry all of this at the light novel's pace. Enter the scene at the moment of contact — an arrival, an offer, a working already underway — then keep rooms, bodies, and positions easy to follow. Break the paragraphs so the eye moves: a line of dialogue, a beat of action, a beat of private response, then more dialogue. The reaction beat is where the social reading lands, and it belongs to its owner — serious, uncertain, irritated, amused, or quietly hurt — and it may sit between two lines of talk or hold a silence on its own. When an unfamiliar term returns, keep the same clarity, so a second binding or working is recognised rather than reintroduced.\n\nRender the focal character's actual private experience as it happens — the judgement, the doubt, the calculation, the flare of resentment or pleasure — in the presentation the scene already uses, not only as what the character chooses to say aloud. Speech and thought may diverge for tact, pride, dependence, or uncertainty, or they may match exactly; let the particular person decide.\n\nStop where the scene's business naturally rests, whether an exchange settles or a remark is still hanging. No reply owes a cliffhanger, a proposal, a reconciliation, or a reversal, and none of those is forbidden when the moment reaches one. Keep the narrator, person, and cast the scene has established, and let the impossible remain something people live with rather than a puzzle the scene must solve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild the cadence around a single social turn: the sentence moves from the ordinary surface of an occasion toward the moment at which an established fantastic fact changes what someone may say, offer, or expect. Use the periodic sentence as the main instrument. Hold a courtesy, a financial arrangement, or a family obligation in suspension through subordinate clauses, then let the main clause release the social consequence the wonder has made possible. The turn of the syntax is the turn of the judgment.\n\nChoose images from what the social observer would actually notice: the arrangement of a room, the condition of a garment, the timing of a visit, the way a gift is placed or withheld. A comparison may unfold only when it revises the observer's estimate of a person. If a simile begins as decoration, let it end as interpretation: the likeness should reveal something about risk, dependence, or attachment that the character had not yet admitted. Keep the figurative language owned by the viewpoint or the narrator's free indirect judgment, not distributed to every speaker.\n\nLet the fantasy enter as a condition already woven into the social fabric. A binding promise, an inherited capacity, or a household wonder should appear through its practical effect on manners and consequence rather than through an explanatory pause. When the cadence rises toward a perception, let the perception remain fallible: a plain statement after an elaborate sentence can correct or complicate the lyrical impression. The rhythm may measure the gap between what the character believes and what the occasion demonstrates, but the demonstration itself stays social and particular.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse a present fantastic fact as the hinge between a current social moment and a past occasion that can no longer be returned to. The movement is specific: begin with the object, place, or recurring wonder as it now stands, move to the remembered scene in which it meant something else, then return to the present with the difference made visible. Do not describe the wonder as scenery. Show what it has made unrepeatable: a promise that cannot be kept again, a courtesy that now has no recipient, a capacity that has outlived its occasion.\n\nKeep the remembered scene in Austen's social register. The character recalls the precise words, the unkept promise, the performance of courtesy, or the material arrangement that accompanied the magical occurrence. Let free indirect narration trace how the person accounts for the changed attachment: they may blame circumstance, manners, or another's failure before allowing the truth. The reflection should reveal what the character believes about the past and what the present fact contradicts, without the narrator announcing the lesson.\n\nLet ordinary pleasure remain real. A present kindness may be noted alongside the memory of a past one, and the reflective tone may occupy one paragraph without demanding that the scene end by announcing loss. Use small irreversible details to carry the weight: a mended enchantment that can no longer be worn, a season when a familiar spell no longer works, a household custom whose magical object has been put away. If the fantasy permits an impossible return, render its limitations so the longing arises from those limits rather than from mandatory sorrow.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the framed tale as the mechanism of Austen's social judgment. A situated teller—a household member, a merchant, a guest, or a servant—recounts an incident in which an established wonder disturbed or illuminated the social world. The frame must do interpretive work: the teller's need for patronage, reputation, or advantage shapes what is emphasized and what is omitted, and the audience's interruptions test the account. A listener may raise a practical question, doubt a claim, or ask what courtesy required, and the teller's answer revises the tale's meaning. The frame is not decoration; it is where the social judgment is performed and contested.\n\nLet the inner tale show daily consequence through oral arrangement. A magical object may surface during a marriage negotiation, a financial agreement, a visit, or a quarrel over inheritance. Use lists and repetitions from oral style to arrange the scene: the exact order of the guests, the phrases repeated in a courtesy, the sequence of rooms or goods. The wonder should not exempt anyone from manners. Its effect may be absurd, exposing pretense in the manner of satire, but the narrator's irony should remain precise rather than broad. Include ordinary details—food, clothing, housework, money—so the marvelous occurrence is weighed against practical life.\n\nKeep the teller's and characters' knowledge separate. The outer frame may supply a free indirect commentary that a character in the tale does not possess. The narrator of the inner episode may be limited to what the viewpoint observed or heard, while a later moral or application belongs to the frame teller. If a wonder has uncertain authenticity, let the audience's skepticism or belief be a social fact, not evidence that the fantasy is merely superstition. The style does not require a Thousand and One Nights structure, a religious frame, or an exoticized landscape; it requires that the telling itself have an occasion, an audience, and a consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Minimalist Prose\n\nPlace the fantastic fact between two social details so the contrast does the interpretive work. A room is described in three exact observations; the wonder appears as the fourth, altering the meaning of what came before and what follows. The operation is positional, not explanatory: show who carries the enchanted object, who does not, whose chair is moved because of it, or what offer is made possible by it. Do not provide a history or a rule. The social consequence must be inferable from the arrangement.\n\nKeep the narrator's judgments short and owned. A single sentence may slide into free indirect discourse to show a character's surprised acceptance or resentment. Do not spell out the insight in a second sentence. The precise material fact often does the interpretive work: the money not offered, the invitation repeated, the object handed back without thanks. Let the syntax stay simple, but allow one careful longer sentence when a social implication is complex and would otherwise be lost.\n\nDistinguish inference from stated knowledge. A character may notice a change; the narration should not then tell the reader what it means. Contrast a plain remark with what is left unwritten, but do not withhold necessary causal information. If the fantasy causes the situation, the minimal presentation should make that connection apparent through positioning, not through a general statement. The discipline is in the exactness and economy of the selected detail.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite scene-first narrative in which the fantasy appears as a continuing condition of the world, not an inserted wonder that pauses the story for an explanation. Begin with ordinary movement and conversation: a call, a purchase, a family negotiation, or an errand. Let the fantastic fact influence the social situation through practical consequences—who may speak, what debt is owed, what courtesy is altered—rather than through a sudden display. The narrative may move freely among action, dialogue, perception, and recollection, using paragraph transitions to mark a change of focus without recap machinery.\n\nKeep Austen's social attention in the narrator's selection and free indirect judgment. The prose may show a character estimating another's income, reputation, or motives, but the narrator must not overload every exchange with commentary. Let a single moment of free indirect thought reveal a misconception without a lengthy internal lecture. Use dialogue to expose mismatch: a polite phrase may carry an insult, a generous offer a hidden condition, or a magical obligation an unspoken social cost. The narrator may notice gestures and room details only when they serve the present interpretation.\n\nTrigger free indirect thought from a present stimulus and return it to the scene's action. When a character estimates another's motive, the thought should arise from a gesture, a phrase, or a material fact just noticed, and it should end by returning the character to the ongoing exchange or task. A recollection may be introduced only when a present detail or decision calls it up, so the scene does not become an essay. Let the fantasy create ordinary complications—a household object with a temper, a binding promise inherited with an estate, a magical service that must be paid for in social credit—but render each through its effect on the present scene. The response may end in progress, with the situation continuing, rather than requiring a full resolution or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nAnchor the story in the established relationship and make the fantasy serve its particular tensions and rewards. Each scene should contribute to the relationship's development through dialogue, small shared history, or a choice that affects the two people differently. Use private language and repeated moments with care: a recalled phrase, a gesture, an object, or a place can return at a later turn, but the callback must arise from the current scene and carry new weight. The callback's new weight is social: the same phrase now means something different because the family expectation, financial dependence, or public reputation has changed. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes; use only the actual supplied relationship premise.\n\nLet Austen's social world shape the obstacles and opportunities. The attraction may be complicated by family expectations, financial dependence, reputation, or a misunderstanding carried in courtesy. Magic can alter these stakes without becoming a deus ex machina. A love confession may be impossible under the enchantment until a condition is met; a magical obligation may force a choice between duty and desire; a small private spell may be the only evidence of a feeling that public manners forbid. The narrator should stay close enough to the focal character to render the inner emotional experience while preserving the other character's opacity where appropriate.\n\nShow emotional events as moments with real duration. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, or rivalry enough space to avoid a rushed summary, but do not inflate minor incidents into crisis. The enchantment may heighten the risk, but the prose must not resolve every turn by magic alone; the relationship's consequences should emerge from recognizable human motives and social conditions. End at a point of emotional significance or available continuation without requiring a happy ending, a confession, or a complete reconciliation in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nPresent the episode as an account that is being passed on about these particular people. Use a deliberate opening, such as naming the season, the household, or the difficulty, that indicates the situation is already known to the audience and worth retelling. Let the central action be a modest deed shaped by the fantasy: a kept promise, a refused bargain, a repaired enchantment, or an inheritance handled unusually. After the deed, let a compact declaration state what the community will remember. That declaration is a social claim, not a settled truth: the dialogue that follows may correct, complicate, or undercut it.\n\nUse recurrence and designation from established social facts. A title like \"the one who paid the fairy debt\" or \"the woman who spoke to the house-spirit\" may be earned by the actual episode and repeated only when a later occurrence gives it new application. Keep dialogue grounded in Austen's social register: disputes about duty, money, reputation, and politeness may interrupt the elevated chronicle style. The subject of a remembered tale may correct a detail, offer a practical objection, or answer a formal introduction with an ordinary concern about dinner. This contrast need not become parody; it can show the difference between public memory and lived experience.\n\nDo not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial formula. The fantasy may be domestic, civic, or institutional while remaining part of the world's known conditions. Let the telling style use compact sentences and periodic recurrence to give shape, but allow the scene's scale to stay human. The response may end at a natural pause in the telling—an act completed, a question left for another day, or an audience turning to the next matter—without requiring the deed to be declared legendary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the third person from one focal character's mind, using the essayistic inward movement: a present perception or obligation opens a paragraph, then gives way to memory, self-justification, or a private calculation before returning to the room. Let the syntax carry that return rather than announcing it. When the character believes they are being reasonable, the free indirect voice may adopt their terms closely enough that the reader can measure the belief against what the scene has already shown.\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as a settled household condition with a cost. Show it through the practical texture of the day: a debt that renews itself, a room that keeps an agreement, an object that must be fed or placated before a visitor arrives. The character may think of it as an inconvenience, a family duty, or a proof of their own prudence. Do not pause to explain its origin; let its rules appear only where they touch money, work, promises, or courtesy.\n\nMake social exchanges small and exact. An offer, a refusal, a letter, or a silence can carry several meanings because each person's material position differs. Before a line is spoken, give the focal character's reasoning about what the other person can afford, expect, or overlook. Afterward, let the same mind revise that reasoning without the narrator correcting it aloud. The irony belongs to the gap the prose leaves between the character's account and the observable facts, not to an omniscient comment.\n\nKeep the setting concrete and particular: a specific sum, a specific errand, a specific room or season. Reserve is a method of emphasis, not coldness. A character may be grieved or relieved without declaring either; the essayistic pause can hold that feeling while the outward business continues. End when the focal mind reaches a new understanding or settles back into an old one, not when a threat or revelation arrives.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Screenplay\n\nWrite each scene as a readable action block, not as a camera script. Open with a scene heading when location or time changes, then give concise actions and objects the audience can see or hear. Dialogue blocks carry public speech and the social exchange Austen's mode notices: who offers, who refuses, who uses courtesy as evasion, and which terms are not spoken.\n\nPlace the focal character's private interpretation in a clearly attributed nonspoken authoring layer, separate from dialogue and not known to other characters. Write it as actual thought: what a person hopes, fears, or misjudges while a public conversation continues. When a line is spoken, the nonspoken layer may show a different thought; when the two align, show the character's reason for that alignment. Do not let an expression or gesture stand as proof of feeling or truth.\n\nLet the fantastic premise affect action and setting directly. An object is present and produces a visible result; an ability changes what a character can do; a household rule alters who may enter or what may be touched. Show the consequence in the action block rather than explaining it in dialogue. Characters may discuss the premise as a matter of property, convenience, or obligation without treating it as wonder.\n\nUse a cut only when it moves between related places or moments that the sequence needs; otherwise stay in the scene. Offscreen sound or voice-over appears only when the chosen sequence calls for it. The scene can end on an exchange, a visible result, or the recognition that an offer has failed, without a cliffhanger or a final zoom.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Stage Play\n\nLet the stage show the impossible condition in physical business. Choose one playable effect and make it visible: a door that will not stay locked, a letter that arrives without a messenger, a piece of furniture that changes when touched. Block the action so the audience can watch the effect occur and watch the characters respond to it as a household fact.\n\nWrite dialogue as social negotiation. Each line should carry an offer, a claim, a silence, or a term one person uses while meaning another. Let the speakers' material positions differ: one may need the other's help, money, or consent, and the courtesy or evasion in their lines should reflect that difference. When the fantastic premise is discussed, the characters may treat it as a matter of convenience, property, or obligation rather than as wonder.\n\nKeep the focal character's private judgment in nonspoken authoring material clearly separated from the lines. Write what the character thinks while another person speaks, or what they decide not to say. An aside or monologue is used only for a dramatic public moment with its own audience and purpose, not as the default destination for thought. Other characters do not know the nonspoken material merely because the reader does.\n\nBlock entrances, exits, props, and pauses so the audience can reason about the premise's effects. A character's inability to perform an ordinary act can be a staged event. Do not add film-camera instructions or make the stage directions certify hidden guilt. Pauses and exits can end a scene when the immediate social meaning is complete, without requiring a plot reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized mind. Begin with a present perception—a sound, an obligation, a touch—and let it pull memory, worry, or a private phrase from the same character. Syntax may falter, repeat, or list; but each sentence remains somebody's actual thought and preserves that person's ownership. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this mode; the movement should follow what the mind returns to rather than a task list.\n\nLet the social world enter through the stream as remembered exchange. A debt, a family claim, an offer, or the memory of a half-spoken apology can surface in the middle of an unrelated perception, then pull the mind back to the person who spoke or failed to speak. Give the remembered words their own texture: a phrase the character repeats, a courtesy they now hear differently, a sum they cannot stop recalculating.\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as an experienced condition, not a described system. Let a recurring object or physical sensation carry it: the character may notice again that the card has moved, that the hour feels the same, that no one has entered. Do not explain the magic; let the mind's return to it be the evidence. The stream may circle the condition several times, each return adding a small new fact or a sharper fear without resolving it.\n\nKeep the reader oriented enough to distinguish event from recollection. If a remembered scene is intercut, mark the shift by tone, sensory detail, or a clear break. Let the irony emerge from the stream's own repetitions and omissions: the character may insist on a generous motive while the remembered details show a narrower one, or may call an act prudent while the body remembers its cost. End when the stream naturally forgets or settles, not when a plot beat imposes resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the exchange as concise sender-attributed messages. Let each message carry the social weight Austen's register notices: an offer made, a delay explained, a question avoided, a courtesy that asks for something. Timestamps or read receipts appear only when the waiting itself matters to the characters. Edits or deletions are allowed when they change what the receiver can know.\n\nPlace private interiority in a clearly separated authoring layer outside the transcript. Write what a character thinks before sending, what they delete, what they wish the message said. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not the writer's live private thought, and private thought is not transmitted text. The reader may know the gap between what a person feels and what they send; other characters do not.\n\nLet the fantastic premise emerge through the channel's limits and affordances. Choose one disturbance and make it matter to the exchange: a message arrives too early, fails to exist in the log, appears on only one device, or is received before it is sent. Show the characters responding to that disturbance as a practical problem—checking the log, resending, asking what the other person saw—rather than as a wonder to explain. Do not invent an interface event merely to add subtext; use it only when the premise or the relationship actually calls for it.\n\nKeep each sender's digital habits distinct: one person replies immediately, another drafts and never sends, another changes the topic by sending an image. Let those habits reveal social position and feeling without a narrator's comment. End the exchange when an offer is accepted, postponed, or left on read, without requiring a reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Visual Novel\n\nWrite each passage as a compact illustrated scene: a background cue, then attributed speech, then focal narration or inner response. The visual element should orient the reader; it is not an executable engine command or a label on every line. A character's posture or expression may accompany an exchange, but it cannot show what they think or whether they are truthful.\n\nLet the social reading follow the dialogue and the private response. After a line, the focal narration can show what the speaker meant, hoped, or concealed; another person may respond from a different social position. When the same scene is revisited, use a familiar detail—a chair, a window, an empty cup—to anchor memory, but let its meaning grow from actual facts rather than from suspicion. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge.\n\nGive the fantasy condition material presence in the scene. Choose one object, message, or household prohibition and let it appear in the background cue or the focal narration: an object that cannot be lifted, a message delivered before it is written, a room that keeps a promise. Discussions may treat it as an ordinary inconvenience or a family secret without requiring a mythic framework. Show its consequence in the scene's action or the character's response rather than explaining it in dialogue.\n\nNo branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images are activated by this script style. If the scene closes on an unsent letter or an unanswered question, the visible moment is enough; the next panel can continue in a later reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagakbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jane Austen / Web Serial\n\nMaintain the through-line in each installment by keeping the fantastical premise materially present. Begin with the latest development and only recover earlier context when a reader needs to recall a particular object, promise, or event. A magical rule should have consistent consequences across installments: if a door refuses to stay locked, that remains true until a prior world fact changes it, and the change itself is a development, not a retcon.\n\nLet social interpretation grow through repeated encounters rather than isolated scenes. An earlier act of courtesy, a half-truth, or a failure to assist can acquire new meaning later because a different character's circumstances or the premise's effect emerges. The narration may revisit this with Austen's free indirect irony, but it must remain a single focal mind's judgment unless the perspective changes. Do not reinterpret a past event to manufacture a reversal; instead, show what the past act has since produced.\n\nClose a reply with a local outcome: an overture accepted, a condition discovered, a cost measured, or a question that the next installment can pursue. Avoid fake cliffhangers and avoid summarizing the entire story. Uneventful intervals may be compressed, but the result of time passing—a repair, a visit, a payment—belongs to the history and must be available when it matters. Serial duration should give the premise's consequences room to accumulate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the fantastic element as a specific recurring cost the characters have had to live with, not a spectacle. Choose one concrete burden: a vow that must be renewed at a price, an ability that helps others while injuring the user, a debt whose terms force a choice between two people. Let that burden sit inside practical choices and interruptions that make a relationship hard. When private attachment conflicts with an outward obligation, make the obligation concrete: an oath, a trusted person's need, a public role with real consequences. The strain appears in what a character does and does not say, and in what they notice they have already decided.\n\nRender interiority without a stock vocabulary of suffering. Show the cost through hesitation, changed priorities, small failures of attention, or actions that can be read two ways. Do not force a confession in every scene; a silent choice can expose more than a speech. When comfort does come, keep it partial, awkward, or bought at a price. Let warmth and pain share the same room without one canceling the other.\n\nUse scenic movement to hold the turbulence: a conversation, a journey, a public reckoning, then the private aftermath. The viewpoint may think it understands the other person's reasons, but leave gaps and the risk that the explanation is partly self-serving. The style's intimacy means the reader sees the wound, not a dramatic diagnosis of it. Advance the story through concrete decisions, allowing the characters to act while their hurt remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Chat Roleplay\n\nMaintain a coherent shared interval with clear speakers. Each paragraph can carry a small scene beat: movement, a practical problem, an exchange, or a described reaction. Attribute dialogue unmistakably; avoid floating lines. Let the fantastic element appear as a repeatable scene-level detail: a tool that needs mending, a sign that changes a plan, a debt that compels a detour, a ward that flickers at the wrong moment. Show it in one concrete sentence rather than explaining it.\n\nTranslate Jin Yong's particular decisions into roleplay's short movements. Show loyalty and consequence through what a character offers or refuses, and let reputation or technique affect how others respond. Distribute these decisions across player and NPC actions: an NPC may refuse help because of a past slight, while the player character may choose between two obligations in a single reply. Do not compress these into a succession of attacks. The scene may include explanation, travel, or a relationship's pressure without requiring a question or cliffhanger at the end.\n\nReplace forced choices with hooks that follow naturally from recent speech or action. A character may notice something they have not yet acted on; an NPC may begin a practical task that will matter next turn. Instead of closing the interval, end at a point where the next reply can continue from the last spoken or observed beat. Keep the style scannable: direct language, lively but not padded action, and each paragraph focused enough to be read at a glance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the martial-adventure route. Write the tale in a Chinese vernacular episodic register, with each section moving from a concrete encounter to a changed situation. Let the fantasy element operate through discipline, ritual, materials, or a pact that helps one party and burdens another. Make that element the engine of episode-level change: a pact's terms expire at the worst moment, a discipline's cost forces a character to seek help from an enemy, a material's scarcity turns a simple errand into a negotiation. Structure events around reversals, alliances, and debts that arise from these fantastic pressures, not just a sequence of fights.\n\nFollow the characters as they travel between the demands of home, sect, ruler, or traveling companions. Technique matters: show how a practice looks, what it costs, and why someone restrains it. Reputation is particular and social. Someone may be honored in one town and blamed in another; what they actually did is often less legible than the story being told about them.\n\nAllow a generous amount of setting and conversation so each episode feels like a complete chapter. The voice may know the region, the season, the way a hall is arranged, or the pressure behind a polite exchange. Fantasy does not cancel these details; it makes them heavier. End each episode at a place where a real choice has been made or a relationship has shifted, even if the larger journey continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse hard, concrete sentences with abrupt transitions. Name the materials: a worn strap, a cracked talisman, a muddied hem, a bruise someone hides. Give the fantastic element a specific recurring material cost: a seal that cracks further each time it is used, a debt that must be paid in labor, a tool that works only when fed something precious. Show that cost in the physical details, never explain it away. Let an oath, debt, or public allegiance press like weather on the body and the schedule.\n\nGive the viewpoint character finite strength and attention. Restraint is practical as well as moral. A technique that could end a fight may ruin a wagon, tire a companion, or break a seal the party still needs. Let consequences accumulate through the scene: a rushed meal, a failed rest, an unspoken irritation that makes a later decision harder. When loyalty and the fantastic burden pull against each other, show the choice in what the character does with their hands, their time, or their remaining strength.\n\nKeep dialogue rough when the speaker is rough, but specific to the character. Not everyone is gruff. Long thoughts may appear when the protagonist is genuinely reasoning through a pressing problem; otherwise cut them short. Use changes of rhythm on purpose: a quick action, then a still moment to see the damage. Do not make cruelty cheap or turn the world into ceaseless horror. The prose's pressure should heighten the moments that matter rather than flatten them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the story through dated documents with specific authors and purposes: a letter seeking help, a supply list with an anxious postscript, an order rewritten after a disagreement, a fragmentary report from the field. Keep each document partial. The writer knows only what they have seen or been told, and their loyalty, fear, or ambition shapes what they include.\n\nLet the fantasy element be the source of the central documentary uncertainty. Choose one impossible thing whose terms, cost, or effects different writers record differently: a pact whose wording was transcribed differently by two witnesses, a medicine that works but must be hidden, a ward that fails at dawn, a debt to a creature whose demands were heard differently by each party. Compare accounts across entries, not through an omniscient narrator. A contradiction can change the reader's understanding without anyone in the documents solving the mystery.\n\nMake private attachment and public obligation visible in what is written and what is omitted. A commander may give clear orders and write a separate private note that warns differently. A subordinate may report success while concealing exhaustion for fear of being sent home. Use revision marks, crossed-out lines, or a later addendum selectively. Keep continuity readable by attaching each document to a moment and a real concern, letting the story advance through what the documents accomplish or fail to accomplish.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet care be specific and grounded in the world. A character remembers how a companion takes tea, mends a torn sleeve because it matters to someone, or sets aside a portion of food before a long day. Tie the fantasy element directly to the act of care: a comforting spell that takes effort to renew, a creature whose favorite resting spot must be prepared, a tradition around first snow or harvest that gives the kindness its occasion. Show the impossible thing as part of what makes the comfort possible, effortful, or meaningful, not as a separate wonder.\n\nAllow manageable friction from Jin Yong's loyalties and reputations. Someone may be tired of being the dependable one; another may argue about whose turn it is to keep watch. These small disagreements do not make the cast unkind. They sit beside affection and give the scene its shape. Preserve privacy and a character's right to not want company sometimes.\n\nWhere a public duty or external danger exists, do not pretend it is gone. The danger may be distant, the duty waiting outside the room, or the worry mentioned only once. What the scene offers is not removal but ease: a pause for a shared meal, an unexpected gift, a quiet reassurance. End when the comfort has been felt, even if the larger problem remains.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose chapters in sustained, deliberately paced prose. Each scene receives enough narration to establish its social setting: who is present, what their presence means, and what recent history presses on the exchange. Transitions should carry the reader from one concern to another with visible logic, not jump cuts. Let the narrative voice preserve a degree of distance, so it can register how a decision looks from outside even when the point of view is close.\n\nTreat the fantasy element as an institution or a circumstance with public meaning, and make it the engine of the public/private contrast. Choose one impossible thing that exists differently in public and private spaces: a custom that arranges labor, law, courtesy, or obligation in the hall but becomes a private burden in the room; an ability or burden that has an official story told about it and a different truth known only to a few. If a person carries that ability or burden, consider who knows of it, who resents it, and what official story is told about it. Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation becomes part of the novel's architecture: a private letter may be answered in a public hall; a moment of tenderness can be shadowed by what the witnesses will say.\n\nAvoid substituting ornate vocabulary for formality. The period and community can be modest, even poor. Ceremony may be as small as a farmer's harvest rite or a clerk's recorded oath, but let its structure do work: the order of speakers, the difference between what is said to the room and what is said later. This frame does not turn every scene into a ball or audience; it gives the world's pressures a composed, readable shape. End passages at natural closures rather than forcing periodic revelations.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as a present negotiation in which one character tells a story to a specific audience. The fantasy premise is already real in the world; the teller does not prove it but uses it. Choose the embedded tale because it will change what a listener is willing to do: it may persuade a hesitant ally, teach a practical technique, warn against a known cost, or expose a loyalty the listener did not share. Show the teller selecting, emphasizing, and omitting material for that purpose. The choice of episode is an act of relationship, not neutral entertainment.\n\nInside the embedded tale, render the premise through consequence. When a wonder appears, show what people did with it, what they refused, or what it cost them. Move between travel, work, negotiation, and private feeling because the premise has already altered those activities. Let the teller's loyalty shape the account: a generous description of a companion may be sincere while leaving out something the teller did not understand. The teller may pass on a warning learned later or still misunderstand part of the event. Keep the difference between what the participants knew then and what the teller knows now.\n\nReturn to the outer frame through concrete interruption and response. A listener may ask a practical question, compare the tale with their own memory, or act on a detail while the telling continues. The teller may answer or deflect according to their bond with that listener. Repeat a phrase, object, or gesture across narrative levels so it gathers meaning slowly, but never let recurrence certify truth by itself. End where the current interaction naturally pauses: an unfinished tale, a listener's decision to speak, or an action already launched may all be legitimate stopping points. Do not import a royal court, magical bargain, or compulsory cliffhanger unless the current world already contains them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and developmental realist route. Write from the perspective of a narrator who observes individual decisions inside a specific institution, trade, household, or civic situation. The fantasy premise is established in the world and appears through its ordinary consequences: work schedules, property, access to services, family arrangements, or local regulation. Each new place should catch a different part of one social tangle rather than decorate.\n\nRender technique and restraint through those consequences. When a character's skill brings practical advantage, show the debt or obligation it creates to a master, a sponsor, a family member, or a public body. Make loyalty visible in small decisions about who is taught, who is protected, and who is left to manage without help. Private attachment and public duty may pull in opposite directions, but the difficulty can resolve through negotiation, delayed help, or a clear refusal with its cost shown. Do not require a betrayal or a collapse.\n\nThe narrator may summarize elapsed periods, compare present and past, or state a social rule, but the immediate experiences of the focal character remain concrete. Use fully composed syntax with subordinate clauses for actual causal or temporal relation. Avoid pseudo-translated word order and ethnic landscape inventories. The response may stop after a meaningful exchange, an unfinished negotiation, or a scene boundary that leaves the larger problem open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Gothic-Punk\n\nShow the fantasy premise as an inheritance that arrives in a present, imperfect urban setting. Establish who benefits from old authority, who must work within its limits, and how the premise has changed their daily access. Use concrete material detail—worn stairs, pay slips, rooms kept by old rules, public systems that only partly accommodate the premise—to make the atmosphere inseparable from the choices available. Do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules.\n\nRender loyalty as earned through instruction and choice rather than inherited by blood. A teacher, sponsor, or companion may grant access, refuse it, or make a demand that tests private attachment. In public company, characters may mask their need for help or their resentment of authority; in a scene of trust, that mask may fall away without requiring a full confession. Show technique through concrete action, a practical result, or a cost incurred, not through abstract rank.\n\nTreat the impossible as a material part of the world with consequences: infrastructure, custom, and personal history all register its existence. The story may follow an intimate conflict across several spaces, but the shape of those spaces should reflect the authority relationship. End when the present exchange reaches a natural pause—a refusal delivered, a condition accepted, a secret partially revealed, or a tense walk left at the door.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral and epic frame-tale route. Write as a teller who has an audience in a specific community situation: a gathering before a journey, a household pause, a public dispute, or a mentor's instruction. The teller chooses episodes to persuade, warn, or remind. The listeners' interruptions and questions should reveal what is at stake now, not merely decorate the frame.\n\nRender the fantasy premise through how people keep, teach, or control it. A wonder may be a gift needing discipline, an inheritance with duties, or a force that has changed a trade, a family obligation, or a local dispute. The teller may praise a skill, question a decision, or quietly refuse to explain something, but the audience is allowed to weigh the account and act on their own judgment. Keep the relation between the telling and the outer scene causal: a listener may change a plan, learn a warning, or expose a disagreement.\n\nInside the embedded tale, use Jin Yong's clarity. Render travel, argument, practiced movement, and the pressure between private attachment and public duty as legible scenes without reducing action to blows. If the story supplies a language, region, community, or period, honor that specificity; otherwise do not impose code-switching, religious practice, caste experience, or a generic family pattern. The response may end with the telling still in progress, with the audience responding, or with an outer decision launched by the tale.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the early-modern comic and theatrical telling route. Write as a narrator who presents people in clear social roles—student, guardian, rival, patron, or trickster—and lets their encounters unfold through vivid, readable action. The impossible premise is already part of the world and generates practical effects: a useful skill, a misunderstood warning, an obligation to a patron, or a social consequence that catches someone out.\n\nRender reversal and exaggeration through character. A boast may be tested, a polite refusal may hide sharper loyalty, and a practice display may turn into negotiation. Show technique through what characters can do: a particular movement, judgment, or restraint appears as a concrete action with an intelligible result. Reputation matters, but different characters use it differently: one to avoid a fight, another to secure a student, another to trap an opponent.\n\nKeep the telling theatrical but not merely stage directions. Point out reactions and small bodily signs, and shift naturally to private pressure when the focal character faces a choice between attachment and public duty. The response may stop at a comedic beat, a failed trick, a newly formed partnership, or a larger problem recognized. Do not import diary fragmentation, experimental inwardness, or a requirement for seasonal description unless the current story supplies it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic/metaphysical construction route. Treat the world's impossible premise as an established fact with a shaped, sometimes disorienting presence: its rules may be odd, its costs uneven, and its meaning different for different people. The narrator may present scenes out of chronological order, shift between attributed perspectives, or compare earlier and later knowledge, but each shift should illuminate a particular loyalty, skill, or consequence rather than merely fragment for effect.\n\nRender technique through practical, concrete outcomes—a price paid, a space crossed, a warning honored—and return to a pivotal decision from another character's memory. Show public obligation as a negotiated social force: a debt, a master's condition, a community's expectation, or a promise that binds even when private feeling opposes it.\n\nKeep language, community, and history specific where the story supplies them, but do not manufacture them. The narration may state a general rule or a remembered pattern, provided it is grounded in an actual incident. Interior attention may explore uncertainty, longing, or the burden of a technique, but access must follow the active narrator and viewpoint contract. The response may end at a turn in understanding, an unfinished obligation, or a scene boundary that leaves part of the construction unexplained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation in motion: a particular space, a concrete need, and at least one constraint already visible. Show the fantasy premise through what it does rather than through an opening explanation. If the scene involves an unfamiliar ability, place, or social rule, present its basic function clearly and early enough that later choices follow from it.\n\nUse short, separated paragraphs and clear scene markers when the point of view moves. Condense Jin Yong's travel into purposeful segments: each new space should present a changed relationship, a practical problem, or a lesson received. Show technique as described action with a specific result—stance, leverage, a useful object, a cost—rather than as a power rank. Dialogue should carry the tension between private attachment and public obligation, but the focal character's reaction beat may be uncertain, angry, serious, or simply tired.\n\nInside the fast container, make loyalty legible through choices over time. A companion may argue, offer help, delay an answer, or keep a private mistake quiet. The narrator may summarize elapsed time where needed, but the response should end on the current scene boundary: a decision to travel, a new obligation accepted, a partnership strained, or a task left at the next step. Do not default to first person, a teen cast, a comedic register, or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet rhythm and imagery carry the weight of technique, travel, and the choices that define loyalty. When a character moves, describe the restraint or practiced economy of that movement through one recurring image tied to the actual world rather than through a comparison that merely decorates the blow. The fight need not be a slow, pretty pageant; sustained syntax can rise and fall with breathing, attention, and the interval between a decision and its result.\n\nUse contrastive plain statements when the scene turns practical: an alliance is offered, a debt is weighed, or a journey must begin by night. A comparison should unfold only when it changes what the narrator or focal character understands about the other person, themselves, or a place, not when it is applied like paint to every surface. Recurring sounds or images can return with changed meaning—a river in flood, a cracked harness, a name said twice—without announcing their own gravity.\n\nGive private attachment room beside public obligation. The lyrical style does not turn every character into a poet; their speech may remain direct while the narration's attention is associative. Maintain the active perspective's limits, so that one character's understanding may deepen through a shaped image while another remains unknown. End according to the scene's actual movement, not by sacrificing the next action to a final resonant metaphor.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet present action stir memory without interrupting its consequences. A character deciding whether to uphold a promise may notice what has already been paid for a similar loyalty; a journey through a settled valley may overlay an older version of that valley. The reflection should be anchored in the tactile present—a cool wet rope, a cracked seal, the weight of a borrowed coat—so that the fantastic world remains physically immediate.\n\nMove generously through scenes, but let the narrator or focal character register small irreversible changes: a road widened, a house repainted, a voice aged. These details coexist with ordinary pleasure and practical necessity. A good meal, a repaired roof, or a moment of rest may still carry satisfaction, even if the account knows it cannot be kept. Do not insist that every paragraph end by naming transience.\n\nKeep private attachment and public obligation in productive tension. Reflection should not make a character passive or paralyzed. Someone may recall a failure and still act; someone may honor a dead friend while aware that the living friend asks something different. Preserve the actual costs and limits of the world, including credible dangers, without using melancholy as a force field against threat. End when the scene's movement pauses or changes, not when a final sad summary has been delivered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a situated storyteller who tells within a specific occasion—a journey rest, a workshop, a sickroom, a market court—rather than a disembodied folk voice. The frame need not be ornate; it should account for why the account has an audience and a beginning. Let listeners' questions, silences, or objections modestly shape what is told, without making every tale a contest of wits.\n\nChoose one concrete route: framed oral storytelling. Within it, make Jin Yong's values legible through what the storyteller dwells on and what they verify. A reluctant master's technique may be communicated through a remembered lesson that later fails or costs something. An alliance may be introduced through two different tellings of the same departure, with the later version changing the listener's understanding. Fantastic elements still function in the world; the teller may report a marvel accurately even while disputing its meaning.\n\nWhen a tale is nested inside a tale, give it a purpose that affects the present frame: a confirmation, a warning, a misjudgment, or a favor being approached. Return to a repeated formula or phrase when a later occurrence deepens it, not at every appearance. Do not relocate the cast into a generic mystical Orient or impose exile, religion, or a single honor code. The world remains the one the narrative has supplied, with its actual period and community.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite in direct clauses with a few exact details selected from the actual scene. A journey may cover distance quickly, but its consequences remain: name the landmark that changed, the injury that slowed someone, or the promise that made the route necessary. Let dialogue carry some of the explanation and relationship work that a fuller narration would give, so that minimalism does not reduce people to errand-runners.\n\nWhen technique matters, describe the one movement or preparation that reveals its cost or method, then let context infer the rest. Do not count blows. A fight may be one close exchange followed by its result, provided the result is causal rather than miraculous. Restraint and reputation become legible through what is refused or chosen—the soldier who eats before explaining, the guide who turns back once—not through a lecture.\n\nKeep inward connection brief unless an essential thought needs the extra length to make sense. Omission should leave something inferable, not delete why a person acts. A secondary sentence about loyalty, fear, or fatigue may be worth more than three decorative fragments. Preserve each character's individual voice in speech. End when the present action resolves or pauses, without padding a final implication after the cause is already clear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nUse sustained scene-first prose. Open with perception, action, or speech that belongs to the immediate moment, then move naturally into recollection or inward experience when the character would actually think back. The passage should not present a chronological recap of the world's rules before the scene can begin; instead, let a practical consequence—an unlocked gate, an old letter, a payment refused—carry the necessary explanation.\n\nGive relationships and decisions their due. A conversation may last long enough for a misunderstanding to be repaired or widened. Travel may occupy a paragraph or several, but its passage should be felt through changing light, tiredness, or a landmark relationship. Action remains expressive of values: someone holds a dangerous position not only from skill but from loyalty, fear, or obligation. Keep the narrator's access consistent and do not grant private knowledge to another character between scenes.\n\nLet the fantastic premise be continuous with ordinary life. A minor enchantment may affect a lease, a road toll, or a kitchen fire; its wonder need not be constantly remarked. The contemporary presentation does not force a contemporary setting or understated emotion. Paragraph transitions may be implicit through focus shifts, not through headings or summary sentences. Stop according to the current scene and viewpoint, including amid unfinished pursuit or a private interval, rather than at a thematic conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBuild each scene around the relationship premise that the actual story has supplied. Let its promise become visible through repeated moments with private meaning: the way one character waits, the phrasing another repeats under stress, or the gesture they have learned means 'stay.' Show payoff after enough established cause, not as an immediate comfort beat. The relationship need not be romantic; friendship, rivalry, mentorship, or sibling duty all follow the same selective method.\n\nKeep public obligation in view. In a world of alliances, debts, and techniques, private attachment often costs something or is tested by a journey, a failure, or a promise made to someone else. Give that tension room without making every scene a loyalty crisis. A character may choose both loyalty and tenderness, or may hurt someone by honoring a larger duty. The emotional consequence should follow from the actual characters rather than from a stock confession.\n\nAvoid padded trope catalogs. If a trope-like moment arises—sharing a bed, arriving wounded, a letter read twice—treat it in the established world and character voice, not as a signpost. Preserve the fantasy premise's rules; the relationship does not suspend them. Keep the point-of-view character's private thoughts out of the other person's knowledge unless the contract allows it. End when this exchange or encounter settles or shifts, not after a mandatory reconciliation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the formal movement of retelling. Open with a deliberate, compact statement about who acts, where the action belongs, or what set it in motion, but then let the scene itself unfold with travel, speech, weather, and ordinary work. The saga voice should not reduce every encounter to a summary; it may be told after the deed, yet still show the weight of a wet cloak, a silenced room, or a second breath before refusal.\n\nUse recurring phrasing and names with restraint. A designation arising from an established deed or manner may return when a later occurrence changes its application. An oath, a teacher's name, a gift, or a repeated instruction can stitch episodes together. Do not assign ornamental epithets at every entrance. Reputation is a public account that may be incomplete or disputed; a character can be remembered as formidable while privately afraid, tired, or uncertain.\n\nMake the fantastic elements functional in the world. The teller may report them with the same gravity as a flood or a fire. Keep loyalties and technique central: explain what a restrained action saved, what a broken technique cost, or how an alliance formed under pressure. Dialogue stays in individual voices rather than ceremonial chorus. Preserve the active point of view and its known knowledge. End when the remembered stage of the account naturally pauses, not by bestowing a legend before the consequences are known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite from the viewpoint of a practitioner whose craft belongs to a northern community of mutual obligation. The fantastic premise should be a local reality—something people have long accepted, discussed, and worked around—not a hidden secret or world-altering exception. Let training carry ritual weight: learning a technique means learning a household duty, a seasonal task, or a remembered debt, not only performing an action.\n\nUse the narrator's reflective voice regularly. Between exchanges, the viewpoint character notes what custom would demand of someone in this situation and what private feeling, weariness, or doubt actually moves them. These reflections may be essayistic—brief considerations of land, weather, kinship, or the proper use of power—but they must stay connected to the present scene and its decision. Do not let reflection replace the physical and social consequences of the immediate situation.\n\nFrame loyalty as a test of public obligation. A promise made to an elder, an injured neighbor, or a traveling companion can press on the practitioner when it conflicts with an easier or more personal choice. Another character may remind them of that promise in a single direct sentence; the reminder may reorder the scene, or the practitioner may already know the cost and still hesitate. Hospitality and gift-exchange work the same way: receiving something means the community may later ask for its return in service, information, or protection, and the viewpoint character knows this even before anyone speaks it aloud.\n\nGive action the room to be read as a form of attention and recognition. A sparring session can expose an opponent's background through their stance or caution; a journey can make the environment specific through the habits of a settlement, the condition of a road, or the work a season requires. The style's reserve is not silence but selective disclosure: important feelings appear in small gestures and in the narrator's linked memories, not in declamations. When a thing is truly wondrous, let the viewpoint register wonder through bodily sensation and the shock of delayed understanding, so the fantastic remains strange without breaking the social frame. End the scene where the present exchange or task reaches a natural pause, whether that means a choice made, a request left unanswered, or a private understanding the character has not yet acted on.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene as a screen-readable sequence. Start with a concise scene heading when location or time changes, then one or more action blocks showing who is present and what they physically do. Attribute every spoken line to a named character in the standard dialogue format. Keep action blocks to visible or audible events: shifts of weight, changes in posture, the sound of a door, the movement of training equipment, the arrival of a messenger.\n\nTreat technique as externally legible. When the viewpoint character perceives another fighter's skill, show it through observable cues—the other's stance adjusts, their breathing steadies, their next strike is less wasteful—not through a narrator's abstract claim. A reputation is established by how others react: a room falls quiet, a character steps back, an elder does not challenge a claim. The fantastic element must produce a visible or audible effect in the present scene; if it is internal only, mark it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note.\n\nPreserve private interiority in the active presentation with a short focal note directly attributed to the character, set apart from the action and dialogue. Use it for a specific perception, motive, or hesitation that the scene could not safely show through speech. Do not convert this note into dialogue, and do not let other characters respond to it. A cut after the note can carry its consequence into the next scene without explaining it.\n\nUse cuts and offscreen sound only to manage information flow. If a discussion continues elsewhere, a time lapse changes the stakes, or a messenger's footsteps approach from outside, a cut or an offscreen cue may mark the transition. Do not include camera angles or shot sizes. Build alliances through what characters offer each other—food, passage, training, a warning—and show the recipient's choice in response. A scene may end at any natural pause in the visible action or exchange: a completed task, an interruption, a spoken refusal, or a focal note that clarifies a withheld choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene with clear speakers and staged physical business. Use stage directions for entrances, exits, pauses, objects, movement, and actions visible to an audience. Blocking should reveal relationships and obligations: who stands to receive a guest, who sets down a weapon, who turns away from an elder, who takes a tool from another's hand. Keep every line speakable and attributable; no unlabeled dialogue.\n\nMake technique and restraint matter through what the cast can see and hear. A character's careful movement may be noted in a stage direction; another may comment on it, refuse to match it, or insist on a different pace. Reputation appears through treatment: someone is given a place at the fire, offered instruction, or asked to account for a debt. The fantastic premise should be present as a staged effect or a spoken fact—light, sound, a held object, a stated consequence—not only as an idea inside the speaker's head.\n\nLet dialogue carry the conflict between personal feeling and public obligation. A character may announce what loyalty requires and be challenged by another with a concrete counterclaim. Pauses and silences are part of the performance but must be punctuated by action or speech. Do not let monologues become the default container for thought; if a character speaks alone, give that speech a dramatic purpose, such as rehearsing a promise, making a threat, or deciding to leave, and keep it short.\n\nFor required private experience, include a clearly attributed nonspoken authoring note placed outside the spoken and staged material. This note may reveal a perception, motive, or hesitation the character does not choose to perform. Other characters do not hear it and do not react to it. End the scene on whatever stageable moment the present action reaches: an exit, an object placed or withdrawn, a pause before a spoken decision, an entrance, or simply the point where the exchange has gone as far as it can for now. Avoid film-camera language throughout.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one focal character's mind and keep that access exclusive. Let perception, memory, bodily sensation, and private words follow association rather than chronological neatness. A training floor's cold boards may bring back a teacher's voice; a guest's hesitation may recall a fight the character watched as a child; the weight of a wrapped gift may make an old debt feel physically present. Anchor each associative leap with concrete sense data—sound, temperature, pressure, texture, smell—so the reader can tell present scene from recollection or imagined outcome.\n\nUse recurring concern about loyalty and technique as the mind's pressure points. The focal character should return to a particular obligation—a promise to an elder, a debt to a traveling companion, a standard of restraint learned in training—more than once, in shifting forms. These repetitions are not arguments with a single conclusion; they are the felt cost of the public world inside a private mind. Let a physical cue trigger them: a look from the person owed, a scar, a utensil, a particular footstep. Do not announce the theme; let the mind brush against it and swerve.\n\nRender the fantastic as something the mind struggles to place. Wonder may arrive as disrupted perception: the room seems too bright, the silence has weight, the afterimage of a movement persists. The character may link it to an old tale, a warning, or a bodily memory, but need not explain its mechanism. Keep the world's other facts stable: if a door is known to be locked and the mind moves elsewhere, that factual condition remains true unless later experience changes it. Do not let the stream make events indeterminate.\n\nCarry ordinary relationships through the associations. Other characters speak and act in the present; their words should interrupt, redirect, or echo the focal mind's drift. A request from a friend can break a memory with immediate practical pressure. The line between inward recollection and outward response must remain clear even when syntax is loose. The scene ends where the focal attention changes or is interrupted. That close may find the mind settling on a resolution that includes the obligation and the present choice, or it may leave the pressure unresolved; either outcome is available, and neither requires a declarative summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nTell the scene primarily through a readable message exchange. Name each sender and write their messages in separate blocks. Use timestamps only when the delay itself matters—a long pause before a promise, an overnight silence after a refusal, a quick reply that shows relief. Do not invent read receipts, typing indicators, edits, or attachments unless the story needs them; when one occurs, show it as part of the exchange.\n\nLet each character's digital habits express personality and culture. One writes in short fragments and sends them quickly; another composes careful complete sentences and corrects a word before sending; a third uses a nickname no one else uses. These habits should change under pressure—a normally formal sender may lapse into fragments when frightened—but not all at once. Technique and reputation appear in what people ask or refuse to explain through text: someone requests instruction, another warns against a shortcut, a third refuses to write a name in the open channel.\n\nTreat messages as public commitments, not as bare information. A promise sent in text carries the same weight as a spoken one in this world, and other participants may hold the sender to it. When a character needs to say something private but cannot bring themselves to send it, write the unsent draft as a clearly labeled draft outside the transmitted transcript. An unsent draft is a composed artifact: it may be drafted, edited, and abandoned without becoming known to the other characters. Use it for a specific hesitation, loyalty conflict, or withheld fear that the story needs.\n\nKeep required private experience separate from both sent messages and unsent drafts when it is live thought rather than a message being composed. A short context note outside the transcript may say what a character notices or feels while typing and cannot see the other side. Another character does not know this content and cannot respond to it. Context notes may also establish the physical scene—where each person likely is, what they know from outside the exchange—but keep them brief and clearly delimited from the transcript.\n\nThe scene closes at the end of the exchange or when one sender stops responding with an explanatory context note. Do not require the conversation to reach an answer or a dramatic cliffhanger; it may end on a promise, a request left unanswered, an unsent draft abandoned after a decision to stay silent, or simply the point where the exchange has gone as far as it can for now.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in scene units that an illustrated presentation can follow. Begin with a concise setting line that names the place and immediate condition—light, weather, an open doorway, a weapon rack, a table with food—without turning it into prose inventory. Then alternate between attributed dialogue and short focal narration or inner response. Scene changes must be legible: use a clear new scene heading or an obvious break, not an unmarked shift of place or time.\n\nUse background and expression cues as purposeful references, not as truth meters. A background note can recall an old obligation: the place where a promise was first made, the doorway the person did not enter, the identical bowl from a previous meal. When the viewpoint character sees another's expression change, let that perception raise a specific possibility—sudden recognition, withheld anger, weariness, calculation—but allow the next dialogue or action to support, complicate, or contradict it. The face itself does not establish guilt, honesty, or fear.\n\nLet dialogue perform Jin Yong's values. Characters may speak of restraint, proper conduct, debts of hospitality, or the cost of a technique; but the words should arise from the scene's social situation. A teacher corrects a student in front of a guest; a traveler explains why a certain move was wrong; a friend asks what the protagonist intends to do about an elder's request. These are not abstract lessons. Focal narration may then supply the private cost: what the speaker cannot admit, what the listener decides to conceal, what physical tension accompanies the words.\n\nIf the scene needs private experience beyond what focal narration already provides, use the focal narration itself rather than a separate wrapper unless the active contract requires hidden interiority. A focal character may think that they have seen this stance before, that they are being tested, or that the offer will cost more than it says. Another character does not know this thought and must respond only to what is said and done. Do not move into another mind.\n\nKeep the fantastic present as part of the scene's sensory or social condition. A traveling healer's touch leaves a specific afterimage; a training ground remembers a season's use in its worn stones; a promised object changes how people enter the room. The viewpoint may feel wonder, dread, or curiosity, but the story does not need to explain the source. End the scene when a focal reaction or a spoken line establishes what the character will carry forward. That may be a changed suspicion, an accepted duty, a refusal, a question left for the next scene, or any other particular the present moment has actually produced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagalbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jin Yong / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as part of a continuous, accumulating story. Open by establishing enough active context to orient a returning reader without retelling the whole history: who is present, what commitment or problem is currently pressing, and where the action is happening. A brief recollection of a prior promise, injury, or unfinished task may appear only when it makes the present scene legible.\n\nUse recurrence to show technique and reputation growing across installments. When a character repeats a training sequence, the earlier awkwardness should stick to the movement until the story earns improvement. When another character recognizes the viewpoint character's name, clarify what past action or association lies behind that recognition. Do not let reputation change for free; it must be linked to a particular earlier decision, result, or report that the recollection names.\n\nLet obligations behave like serial resources. A promise made in an earlier installment remains available for later use, and its terms should constrain present choices. A debt of hospitality can be called in, a teacher's warning can surface at a relevant moment, an unanswered request can return with new stakes. When such a callback appears, give the specific prior condition in one or two sentences rather than as an abstract thematic echo.\n\nAllow progress to occur through knowledge, relationships, work, or resources, not through level-ups or system-defined advancement. A character may learn a new application of the fantastic premise by observing someone else, reach a partial understanding of an institution's rules, repair a relationship, complete a journey, or gain a useful contact. Show the change in their present ability or circumstance, not in a numerical rating.\n\nEnd the installment at a natural pause that still acknowledges ongoing motion. The viewpoint character may have completed a task, accepted a new obligation, learned something that changes an old plan, been interrupted in the middle of action, or reached any other point where the present scene has gone as far as it can for now. Avoid manufacturing a cliffhanger unless the present scene already produces one. If the installment stops before a result, state what the viewpoint character is now doing or waiting for so the next reply can pick up without a summary frame.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite a fantasy whose impossible fact has practical, often awkward, consequences for people who already live with it, not a lore aside. Choose one familiar routine, object, phrase, or relationship to anchor the scene, and show how the fantastic element changes the cost of keeping or breaking it.\n\nLet the viewpoint character's needs surface through concrete ordinary acts—doing a task twice, returning something, avoiding a room, mentioning a shared joke too late—rather than through announced feelings. Where the narration rereads an earlier detail, make the rereading increase pressure: a repeated phrase now exposes a debt, a familiar room now exposes absence, a habitual small kindness now exposes the lie that made it possible.\n\nWork from felt exposure to strained repair. As the character articulates, withholds, or returns to something, render the price after the speech, not only before it: a silence that lasts in the next room, a task resumed badly, an apology accepted in words while the body keeps guarding the hurt. If comfort appears, keep it partial and double-edged: reassurance can be sincere and still arrive with the wrong timing, the wrong person, or a practical consequence the character is not ready to accept.\n\nUse the fantasy premise to sharpen the stain without making it a metaphor only. The enchantment might require confession, expose a need, or preserve a moment someone wants to forget. The narrative move is the reaction that follows from this fact under pressure. End where the immediate next choice stays live: do not force a confession, reconciliation, or collapse, but do not let the scene sidestep the cost it has already invoked.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Chat Roleplay\n\nIn each reply, select one ongoing scene around a concrete fantastic element—a working spell, a changed room, a creature's habit, a rule everyone treats as ordinary—and render it through beats rather than summary: the area around the characters, a small action or use of the premise, clear dialogue, and an attributed focal reaction.\n\nUse Maeda's repetition practically. Carry one returning object, phrase, or routine into the scene and give it a consequence now: a packed lunch becomes a marker of home when found later; a repeated joke becomes a way to ask whether things are still all right; a household charm is maintained because someone couldn't say goodbye. The callback must affect the current exchange, not decorate it.\n\nKeep attribution immediate and never hide who speaks. Short narrative lines may show gestures, attention, or the viewpoint's response. Allow the interaction to proceed naturally: an invitation can be accepted, a story told, a task shared, or an ordinary moment enjoyed. The fantasy element may provoke wonder, inconvenience, humor, or comfort; it should feel part of daily life rather than a sudden plot frame.\n\nEnd when the next action or reaction is already implied—a response being eaten cold, a companion choosing to stay, a character noticing the charm still works. The next player turn can continue by accepting, delaying, asking a factual question, or adding a new detail. Do not end on a posed choice, a withheld fact, or a cliffhanger unless the scene itself requires it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in an intimate urban or domestic mode: one street, household, workshop, teahouse, or neighborhood whose daily rhythms are visible in the narration. Use a selected period and community responsibly, with concrete trades, foods, greetings, and obligations rather than generic scenery. Do not overload the page with an inventory of local signs; a few precise details repeated and arranged into significance carry the method.\n\nTake one ordinary shared bond—a parent and child's meal, two shopkeepers' route, a student's weekly visit, siblings dividing a task—and let the fantasy element reorganize it. The impossible thing should be known and in use: an exchanged charm that keeps hours, a household spirit paid in rice, a street whose rain arrives on request, a debt kept in a jar. Show its effect through changed scheduling, conversation, or the room's condition.\n\nUse Maeda's habit callbacks across the scene: the same greeting, the same object passed, the same route walked in clear weather. When the callbacks return after the event, shift our understanding of who was maintaining or hiding the bond. The narration may observe social contrast tactfully—who sits, who serves, who waits—but focal reactions remain specific to the person, not a lesson about a whole community.\n\nFrame time through scene rather than summary: arriving, eating, waiting, closing the gate, checking that the charm still answers. Let interiority register what cannot be said in the room. The turn should end at a moment where that domestic understanding has altered enough for the next small act to matter: someone still choosing to sit down together, or the household beginning its next routine under the changed meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nStart each passage inside a concrete task or defect: a repaired charm that won't hold charge, a market stall organized around a creature's limitation, a door that must be fed. Use the established fantasy element as a working condition. Show its wear through verbs with texture: chalk breaks in the grooves, water leaves mineral crust, a glove's patches stiffen.\n\nWrite short. Move by action, object, consequence. Let a single physical detail stand for the larger situation. When a character must reason through a choice or a changed arrangement, allow one longer paragraph of actual thought, then return to the task or the street.\n\nHandle Maeda callbacks as objects or routines earning pressure: the same mended strap now holds medicine; the same evening route now avoids a lit window; a repeated phrase between workers becomes a way to ask whether anyone else saw the light. Return the object in the moment its history changes. Do not announce its meaning.\n\nKeep dialogue abrasive or plain where the speaker warrants it; a witness can be terse without the author making everyone tough. The fantasy element should create access, cost, or danger—a coat that walks at night, salt that must be repaid, a familiar that marks debt—never a decorative effect. End on a physical or verbal beat that leaves the work immediately continuable: a second attempt, someone leaving, a latch tried twice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Epistolary / Documents\n\nSelect one primary document lineage: a single account, an exchange, a field report, a household ledger with notes, a journal kept by one performer, or letters passed through a court messenger. Every entry states or demonstrates its author, its intended recipient or purpose, and its date or place. Use the next entry's shift in time or author to reveal what the earlier writer did not know.\n\nMake the established fantasy fact legible in the record: a charm that runs down each day because someone forgets to request it, a sentient road that charges passage, a spirit whose help appears as a ledger line. The document writer may describe the result while missing its cause; a reply may correct, ignore, or exploit that gap.\n\nUse Maeda callbacks as recorded traces. The same object, meal, price, route, or nickname is documented at different times, and the gap between mentions does the emotional work. When a later entry repeats a phrase from an earlier one, make the repetition reveal changed context: a household order now annotated in a new hand, a promise written in one register and contradicted by another, a letter signed with the wrong familiar closing.\n\nKeep each document within its writer's practical knowledge and immediate concerns. A journal can be spare or detailed; a report can omit what the officer considers irrelevant; a letter may soften the truth in the chosen recipient's direction. Let one document conceal less than it thinks while another records the observable discrepancy. Do not add an omniscient note after a record. End with a document that exposes the next consequence: a request left unfulfilled, a debt written and then crossed out, a reply whose final line the addressee cannot yet answer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite a warm interval within a fantasy world whose impossible fact participates in care and ordinary life. Choose a small task or shared preference: drying enchanted herbs together, feeding a familiar with the better portion, walking a route because a friend prefers the long stairs, testing a warmth charm before bed. Let one character act on what another enjoys without broadcasting the sentiment.\n\nUse Maeda's callbacks as the currency of the comfort. Reuse a phrase, a packed meal, a nickname, a bench, or a household object so that its repetition is part of the ongoing relationship, not commentary upon it. A small oddity can carry affection: someone keeps a worn charm because it was the first repair they could manage, and the other person treats it with care without mentioning it.\n\nKeep personality intact. Disagreement may occur over how to do a task, what to eat, or whether a bit of magic is worth using. One character can refuse assistance another offers, and the refusal can be affectionate or simply theirs. Let the scene rest on sensory pleasure—warmth, taste, clean cloth, unhurried conversation—without forcing a love confession or an emotional breakthrough.\n\nManage length through concrete action: prepare something, eat it, return home, sit together. The fantasy element should remain visible as a practical fact—a charm heats the soup unevenly, the familiar waits on the doorstone, a paper bird carries the good-night note to the next room. End where the immediate tenderness is already present: a last piece shared, a task finished, a companion deciding to stay a little longer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose. Let each paragraph build from an observed social moment—a market exchange, a memorial line, a training exercise, a household custom—through one character's private shift, then back to the room or street with the change visible in what they now notice. This three-part movement, not a fixed number of passages, is the structural engine.\n\nChoose an established fantasy fact that has an institutional or social face: a legal charm whose copies differ by district, a rite for weather that now includes a refugee's phrase, a craft guild's oath with a magical forfeit. Frame it through its public use first; reveal its personal consequence through a repeated object or routine. The callback will then reread the ceremony, not merely reuse a prop.\n\nControl whom the narration judges. If the narrator has distance, it may note what the community observes and what its members conceal. If the narrator stays close, the judgment belongs to the focal character and may be mistaken. Keep private thought available as actual nonspoken experience; a composed report of events cannot replace it.\n\nUse ceremony and correspondence when the scene genuinely has them. Where a public act and a private habit overlap—returning a borrowed banner, watching a guest use the wrong hand, discovering that a familiar prayer line was added by someone now absent—let the sustained paragraph make that contrast concrete. Warmth may appear as a held glance across a room, not as a conclusive pronouncement. End at a natural lull where the next formally organized act can continue without resolving the private discovery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as a specific occasion for telling: a person chooses to recount an embedded story to a particular listener or small audience because the telling can persuade, comfort, warn, or change how the listener understands something they share. Establish the teller's relationship to the listener and the practical reason the tale is being offered now, without requiring a royal court, magical bargain, or cliffhanger between nested accounts.\n\nMake the embedded tale dramatize the story's fantastic premise as lived consequence rather than as spectacle. Show a person inside the tale making a decision, keeping a routine, or caring for someone under the pressure of the impossible fact. The tale should not explain the premise; it should make one of its consequences concrete enough that the listener can recognize the same consequence operating in the outer world. Use a repeated object, phrase, routine, or relationship as the thread between levels: the tale gives that shared thing a history or an implication the listener had not seen.\n\nWhen the embedded story returns to the frame, bring the repeated element back into the outer scene in its present form. Let the listener now notice how the same consequence touches their own work, household, journey, or bond. The delayed revelation belongs to that recognition, not to a hidden plot twist: what the listener newly understands about a person, a habit, or a promise is the payoff. The teller's selection and emphasis may be sincere and still partial; the listener may notice what the tale omits or softens, and that noticing can become part of the outer scene's emotional work.\n\nKeep the narrative levels distinct and easy to follow. Signal entry into and return from the embedded tale through the teller's voice, a framing gesture, or a clear shift in scene. Humor and tenderness should arise from the specific people involved, not from a compulsory tone. End when the return to the frame has done its emotional work, even if the practical situation remains unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny route from the German-language brief. Choose a familiar object, place, routine, or relationship that belongs to the focal character's daily life, and let the story's fantastic premise begin to express itself through that repeated thing from the first recurrence onward. The narration should attend closely to the repeated thing until its ordinary use feels strange, significant, or inhabited by something the character does not yet understand. The uncanny effect arises from the collision between daily attachment and the impossible fact, not from imported horror conventions or sudden spectacle.\n\nChoose a narrative distance and syntax that follow the route and the focal character. A close third person or first person may dwell on sensory detail and emotional response; a more composed narrator may observe the same estrangement with cooler precision. Keep the selected distance steady, and let the prose rhythm slow around the repeated object or routine so the reader feels its accumulating weight. Do not impose a generic German-language severity or translated word order; the voice belongs to the actual teller and scene.\n\nBuild the Maeda reframing through recurrence. The repeated thing should appear at least twice before the character recognizes the impossible premise at work in it, then once after that recognition. Each return should revise what the character understands about the thing and about the life organized around it: a changed sense of home, work, family, or self. The premise need not be explained, but its consequence should be concrete enough to alter a decision, a duty, or an attachment. Let humor and tenderness appear where the people involved would actually produce them. The story may end inside the changed understanding without resolving every mystery of the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the collision between an old authority and present life as the emotional weather of the scene, not as a case to solve. The authority may be an institution, a family inheritance, a building, a custom, or a person whose power has outlived its original purpose. Let urban material detail make that collision concrete: worn stone, new wiring, a locked room still used for old rituals, a modern schedule imposed on an ancient duty.\n\nGive the focal character a repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship that belongs to daily life. Let the story's fantastic premise actively alter the terms of that repeated thing within the authority's reach. The impossible fact should change what the routine costs, permits, or means: a familiar walk now requires a detour through a place the authority controls, a shared meal now depends on a rule the premise has made real, a private joke now recalls a power that can reach the teller. The reframing should be emotional and relational, not merely descriptive. Morally complicated intimacy belongs here: trust, desire, loyalty, and fear can coexist in the same person without a compulsory betrayal.\n\nShow social masks falling away only in trusted company, and let that exposure carry risk. A character may reveal a private desire or a long-held doubt to someone they trust, and the scene should register both the relief and the danger of that exposure. Keep the fantasy element as a fact of the world that affects institutions and everyday life, not as imported vampire mechanics or a secret power. End the response inside the changed understanding of the repeated thing, even if the authority remains intact and the practical situation continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route from the Indian literary traditions brief. The outer scene should be a situated occasion of telling or remembering: a person recounts an embedded account to a listener or small group because the telling can settle a question, preserve a bond, warn someone, or change how a shared past is understood. Keep the language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them, and do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.\n\nMake a repeated object, phrase, routine, or relationship the thread that connects the outer situation and the embedded account. Inside the embedded account, dramatize the story's fantastic premise as lived consequence: show a person making a decision, keeping a duty, or caring for someone under the pressure of the impossible fact. The tale should not explain the premise; it should make one of its consequences concrete enough that the listener can recognize the same consequence operating in the outer world.\n\nAfter the telling, return to the outer scene and bring the repeated thing back into present life. Let the listener or teller now see how that consequence touches their own work, household, journey, or bond. The reframing is the payoff: the object, habit, or person carries a new weight because the embedded account has made its implication visible. Let the telling carry the cadence of oral narrative: direct address, recurrence, and a clear movement between levels. The teller's selection and emphasis may be shaped by loyalty, memory, or the needs of the present moment. The listener may respond, question, or remain silent, and that response can become part of the outer scene's emotional work. Humor and tenderness should belong to the actual people involved. End when the return to the outer scene has completed the reframing, even if the practical situation remains open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tales route from the Japanese literary traditions brief. Let the story move between ordinary life and an impossible event without treating the impossible as a puzzle to be fully explained. The fantasy premise should have intelligible consequences for work, family, travel, or personal discovery, and it may be accepted by the characters as part of their world or met with wonder, fear, or confusion according to their actual dispositions.\n\nAnchor the Maeda reframing in a repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship, and let the fantastic premise manifest through that repeated thing rather than arriving as a separate event. The impossible fact should change what the repeated thing does, costs, or means: a household object begins to behave according to the premise, a daily route now passes through a place the premise has altered, a familiar phrase now carries a consequence the speaker did not intend. The repeated thing should appear in the ordinary world before the character recognizes the premise at work in it, then return after that recognition with a changed meaning.\n\nThe change should be emotional and specific: a character understands a promise, a place, a habit, or a person differently because of what the premise has done to the repeated thing. Do not require tragedy or miraculous repair; the reframing may be quiet, partial, or unresolved. Choose a definite narrative distance and let the prose follow it. A close narrator may render direct feeling and sensory attention; a more composed teller may use omission and plain statement. Seasonal attention is available where it matters to the scene, but do not impose a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery or a universal quietness. The language and voice belong to the actual teller and situation. End inside the changed understanding, even if the fantastic event's full meaning remains open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the marvelous treated as ordinary route from the Latin American literary traditions brief. Let the fantasy premise appear as a fact of the world that characters accept or respond to without elaborate astonishment. The wonder may sit inside work, family, travel, or personal discovery, and its consequences should be concrete. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history when the story supplies them, and do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them.\n\nAnchor the Maeda reframing in a repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship, and let the marvelous premise be present in that repeated thing from the first recurrence onward. Treat the premise and the mundane with the same calm attention, so a strange occurrence and a shared meal can occupy the same sentence without tonal rupture. The repeated thing should be part of ordinary life before the character recognizes the premise at work in it, then return after that recognition with a changed meaning.\n\nThe change should be emotional and specific: a character understands a person, a place, a habit, or a promise differently because of what the premise has done to the repeated thing. The marvelous element itself need not be explained; the story's center is the changed understanding, not the mechanism of the wonder. Let the prose treat the marvelous and the mundane as continuous, so the recognition arrives through the same daily attention that has always surrounded the repeated thing. Humor and tenderness should arise from the actual people involved. The narrative may be intimate or expansive, but it should keep the focal character's experience legible. End inside the reframing, even if the practical situation remains unfinished and the full meaning of the marvelous event stays open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaman::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly: establish where the focal character is, what they are doing, and the practical or emotional reason the scene matters. Keep spatial changes easy to follow by marking movement, entrances, and shifts in location with short, readable transitions. Use paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can track the beat structure without effort.\n\nMake a repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship the emotional anchor. Let the story's fantasy premise alter the immediate function or meaning of that repeated thing within the scene. The impossible fact should change what the object can do, what the routine now requires, what the phrase now implies, or what the relationship can safely express. The repeated thing should appear early in the scene as part of ordinary life, then return after the premise has acted on it in a way that visibly changes its weight.\n\nThe reframing should land in a focused reaction beat: a line of dialogue, a small gesture, a private thought, or a pause that shows the character now understands something differently because the premise has changed the repeated thing. The reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner; do not force comedy or a young cast. Let the fantasy premise affect the immediate situation concretely. The impossible element should change what a character can do, notice, or decide, and its consequences should be clear enough to follow without a full explanation. Give unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when needed, but do not pause the scene for a glossary. End the response inside the changed understanding or the immediate consequence, even if the larger situation remains open. A closing cliffhanger is not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Lyrical Prose\n\nTreat the established fantastic premise as part of the characters' shared daily life, and let one concrete image or repeated routine carry the story's emotional weight. The impossible thing may be ordinary to these people, but its presence should still change how they work, wait, or attend to one another. Return to the image at a later moment so that the second occurrence revises the first: the same object, sound, or small habit now means something the character could not have understood before.\n\nBuild the revision through syntax and association rather than through a stated summary. Move from a plain observation into a comparison that unfolds only as far as the scene supports, letting the sentence follow the shape of the character's attention as it shifts from the present detail to a memory or a possibility. Recurring sounds or syntactic patterns can create the sense of return: a clause that opened the scene may close a later paragraph with its terms rearranged, or a sound first noticed in passing may become the rhythm under a changed action. Keep the images traceable to what the character actually sees, remembers, or handles, and place a plain statement after a developed image so the contrast makes the feeling land.\n\nWrite the focal character's private experience directly, but do not grant the narration access to another character's mind unless the perspective permits it. The fantastic element does not need to be fully explained; show its consequences in the way a task is done, a room is entered, or a person is attended to. End where the present action naturally pauses, with the image still present in the character's hands or attention, available to mean something further rather than forced into a completed moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the fantastic premise create a specific, irreversible change in the characters' shared life, then write the present scene through the small differences that change has left behind. A routine may continue, but one step in it now carries the memory of how it used to be done, or who used to do it. The impossible element need not be the direct cause of every loss; it may simply mark the point after which the familiar world no longer works in the old way.\n\nMove the prose between a present detail and a remembered occasion by letting a sensory cue open the association: a sound, a weight, a particular motion in a task, or the empty place where someone used to stand. Follow the association only as far as the scene supports, then return to the present detail with the memory still held against it. The comparison may remain implicit; the return itself makes it felt when the character resumes the task and the remembered motion no longer fits the present one. Ordinary pleasure can still be ordinary. A remembered joke or a shared meal does not have to turn solemn, and the melancholy should reside in the coexistence of what is present and what is no longer available rather than in a requirement that every scene end by announcing impermanence.\n\nAttach the reflection to a particular person, object, or habit whose repeated appearance acquires new weight because the character has lived with its consequences. Write the focal character's private experience directly, but do not grant access to another character's mind unless the perspective permits it. The scene may close with the character still inside the changed routine, carrying the memory forward without resolving it into acceptance or repair.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the presentation: a situated teller recounts a fantastic event to a particular audience, and the telling itself becomes part of the scene. Establish the teller's identity and relationship to the audience through concrete details of the room, the occasion, and the listeners' postures or interruptions. The tale is being told to these people, in this place, for a reason that the scene reveals gradually through what the teller emphasizes, omits, or answers when a listener asks a question.\n\nLet the tale move from a specific shared moment toward a changed understanding of that moment. The teller repeats a phrase, an object, or a small routine so that its second occurrence revises its first meaning. A listener's correction or remembered detail can redirect the telling, showing that the story belongs to more than one person: the teller may accept the correction, fold it into the tale, or pause and let the audience's disagreement become part of the scene. The fantasy premise should have intelligible consequences, but the teller need not explain every mechanism; a wonder that has settled into shared life can be reported as a familiar fact while its origin remains unspoken.\n\nKeep the regional tradition coherent rather than mixing every convention. The frame does not require nested tales, a mystical setting, or ceremonial speech unless the scene establishes them. Write the teller's account of what was seen or heard, and limit private experience to what the teller can legitimately know or imagine; do not grant the tale access to another character's hidden mind. End with the telling still situated in the room, the listeners' attention or distraction as much a part of the scene as the tale itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Minimalist Prose\n\nEstablish the fantastic premise through a small number of exact details and direct clauses. The impossible thing may be present in the scene without a full explanation: one object, one routine, or one visible consequence is enough to make its effect intelligible. Let the characters treat it as part of their ordinary life, so the narration does not need to stop and describe how it works.\n\nUse repetition as the primary emotional device. Return to the same object, phrase, or small habit at a later moment, and let the second occurrence carry the changed meaning without stating it. Show the change through a concrete difference in how the detail is handled or remembered: the character may set down the object in a different place, perform the routine with a step omitted, or answer the familiar phrase with a silence where speech once came easily. The reader should infer what the character now understands from that difference. Keep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the inference; an essential thought may be long enough to make sense, but do not add redundant explanation.\n\nWrite direct clauses and keep each character's speech distinct from the narrator's economy. Show the fantasy element's consequences through a specific effect on a task, a room, or a person rather than through a summary. End where the present action pauses, with the repeated detail still in the character's hands or attention, available to mean something further rather than closed with a summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the fantasy premise as part of the scene's practical texture. The impossible thing affects how people work, travel, speak, or make decisions, but it does not need to interrupt the scene for explanation. Let it appear through action, dialogue, or a familiar detail that the characters handle without remark. Its consequences should be intelligible even when its mechanism remains unexplained.\n\nMove naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. The narrator may follow a character through a task, pause for a remembered moment, then return to the present exchange. Use these movements to build the author profile's central operation: a specific shared object, routine, or phrase recurs until a later occurrence gives it new weight. Show the change through the accumulated particulars of the scene rather than through a summary statement. The later occurrence may arrive in the middle of a task, a sentence, or a gesture, and the character's response to it—a pause, a correction, a different way of handling the familiar thing—makes the new understanding present without announcing it.\n\nLet the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register, and give humor and tenderness to the people who would actually use them. Write the focal character's inner experience where the narration permits it, and do not grant access to another character's mind. End with the action still in progress or the conversation still open, the changed understanding present in the character's attention but not forced into a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship premise organize the scene. Choose the moment because it shows the relationship's particular promise in action: affection, comfort, rivalry, jealousy, or discovery given enough space to develop. The fantasy element should be part of what these people share, not a separate plot device. It may be a task they perform together, a place they return to, or a wonder that has shaped how they speak to each other.\n\nBuild the relationship through private language and repeated moments. A phrase, object, or small routine can recur so that a later occurrence revises its earlier meaning. Show the revision through a concrete change in how the phrase is said, the object is handled, or the routine is performed: the same words may now be spoken with a different inflection, the shared task may be done with one step changed, or the familiar gesture may be answered with a new hesitation. The callback fulfills the relationship's specific promise by making the current state legible in that difference, not by restating the premise. Give the characters room to be funny or tender where it belongs to them, and let their speech remain individual rather than uniformly polished.\n\nWrite the focal character's private experience where the perspective permits it, and do not grant access to another character's mind. Show the fantasy premise's consequences through a specific effect on the relationship rather than through a full explanation. End with the relationship's present moment still alive, whether in comfort, friction, or unfinished feeling, the repeated detail still in play between them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the story as an account of deeds that a community remembers, but let the deeds arise from specific shared life rather than from ceremonial grandeur. The fantasy premise should be an impossible act or condition whose consequences people have lived with: a wonder that changed how they travel, work, keep promises, or care for one another. Use compact declarations to establish a person or remembered act, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete.\n\nLet recurring phrasing carry the emotional reframing. A phrase used early to describe a person, object, or routine can return later with a new application, so the retelling itself performs the changed understanding. Show the change through a concrete difference in what the phrase now refers to or what consequence it now carries: the same words may once have named a promise and later name its cost, or a designation earned by one deed may be answered by a later deed that complicates it. The chronicle may relate what will be remembered, but keep that relation grounded in the actual consequences of the act rather than in an ornamental epithet. Dialogue can interrupt the authority of the account; the subject of a remembered deed may correct a detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary concern.\n\nKeep the source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention. Do not import a pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial speech unless the scene establishes them. Show the fantasy element's consequences through a specific effect on travel, work, promises, or care, even when its origin remains unexplained. Write the teller's account of what was seen, said, or remembered, and do not grant the chronicle access to hidden minds. End with the account still open to further retelling, another modest act available to be remembered rather than declared legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in continuous prose using a social/domestic realist route. Choose one specific national and institutional context—a Norwegian coastal household, a Swedish small-town workplace, a Danish urban apartment building, or another situated setting—and keep its practical conditions distinct. Let the fantasy premise enter through the texture of that life: what people prepare, repair, store, wait for, or carry between rooms, and how the premise changes what those routines can accomplish or require.\n\nEstablish the impossible element as a fact of the world, then show its consequences in practical routines and small decisions rather than explaining its full mechanism. A strange event may interrupt a chore or become part of one. Let characters respond with practical concern, curiosity, or quiet adjustment, and let the premise touch institutions where the setting makes them matter: a workplace schedule, a housing arrangement, a care obligation, a seasonal task, or a local service.\n\nUse Maeda's repeated attachment through specific shared objects and habits. Introduce one or two recurring items, phrases, or household tasks early and return to them after the fantasy element has changed the characters' understanding. The return should carry a new emotional weight because the earlier routine now means something different, not because the object has gained a hidden power. For example, a shared meal prepared before the premise appears may be prepared again afterward with a different sense of what the household can promise one another; the scene should show the changed feeling through concrete action rather than announce it.\n\nKeep the narration grounded in observable domestic and institutional life: weather, meals, errands, work, family obligations, and the physical condition of rooms and possessions. When the impossible appears, describe it through the same concrete attention given to ordinary things. Let humor and tenderness arise from particular relationships and moments of care, not from a compulsory comic beat.\n\nPreserve required interiority as ordinary private prose. A character may think about what the fantasy element means for someone they love, for a shared routine, or for their own sense of home. Other characters do not know that thought unless it is spoken or otherwise communicated. Do not make tragedy or miraculous repair compulsory; the story may end on a changed understanding, an unfinished task, or a small act of care that has acquired new significance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines, and clear speaker cues. Write action in readable blocks that describe what is visible or audible: a character enters, sets down an object, hesitates at a doorway, or reacts to an offscreen sound. Do not fill the page with camera commands; let the action lines carry the scene.\n\nEstablish the fantasy premise through concrete screen events. Show the impossible thing happening or its visible result: a room changed overnight, an object behaving wrongly, a sound with no source, a person doing something that should not be possible. Then let later scenes show the consequence in ordinary behavior. A character may alter a route, repeat a check, avoid a room, or handle a familiar object differently because the premise has made that action newly significant. Stage the change through what the character does, not through a speech explaining it.\n\nUse Maeda's repeated attachment through visible callbacks. Introduce a specific object, gesture, phrase, or routine early and return to it after the fantasy element has changed the characters' understanding. The callback should be staged so the audience can recognize it: the same prop on a table, the same line said under different circumstances, the same small task performed with a new feeling. The new weight comes from what has happened, not from a hidden property of the object. Let the delivery and physical handling change while the object or phrase remains recognizable.\n\nKeep required private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, such as a character's unspoken thought or feeling. It is not dialogue and is not known to the other characters. Use cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. Humor and tenderness belong to appropriate people and scenes. A scene may end on a completed action, a pending question, or a quiet acknowledgment without an obligatory cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speaker names, entrances, exits, and economical stage directions. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. Use objects, pauses, and blocking that can be staged: a character sets down a cup, crosses to a window, picks up a familiar item, or stops before speaking.\n\nEstablish the fantasy premise through observable stage events. The impossible element should be something the audience can see or the characters can credibly report seeing: a light that behaves wrongly, a door that opens by itself, an object that moves, a person who returns when they should not. Let later scenes show how this changes what the characters do. A character may avoid a doorway, check a lock, set down an object more carefully, or change the order of a familiar task because the premise has made that action newly significant. Stage the change through physical business and timing, not through a speech explaining it.\n\nUse Maeda's repeated attachment through recurring stage business. Choose a specific prop, gesture, phrase, or small task and return to it after the fantasy element has changed the characters' understanding. The repetition should be performable: the same object handled with a different quality, the same line delivered under new circumstances, the same chore done with a changed feeling. The new weight comes from the intervening event, not from a hidden power in the prop. Let the physical handling and delivery change while the prop or phrase remains recognizable.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, not as an aside or monologue that other characters can hear unless such a speech is separately authorized for a dramatic purpose. Do not add film-camera instructions. Humor and tenderness belong to particular characters and scenes. A scene may end on an exit, a completed action, a pause, or an unfinished exchange without an obligatory revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern move the telling. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow the character's associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined.\n\nLet the fantasy premise enter through the character's recurring attention rather than a linear explanation. The impossible element may surface as a remembered image, a phrase that will not settle, a physical sensation linked to the event, or a sudden return to the same question while doing something ordinary. Show its consequences through the way it keeps interrupting or coloring other thoughts. Do not require the character to solve or systematize it; the mind may circle, return, or leave it unresolved.\n\nUse Maeda's repeated attachment through associative callbacks. Choose a specific object, routine, phrase, or relationship and let it recur in the character's thoughts. Before the event it may appear as a familiar comfort or habit; after the event the same detail returns with a changed feeling, a new association, or an unspoken question. The repetition should feel like the mind reaching for something known while its meaning has shifted, not like a deliberate motif announced by the narrator.\n\nKeep the fantasy element grounded in the character's actual experience. The impossible thing is not a metaphor the mind dissolves; it remains a fact of the world that the character perceives, remembers, and reacts to. Let the mind return to the same sensory detail—a sound, a texture, a particular light—and let that detail carry the premise's factual weight even when the character cannot explain it. Other characters do not share this mind or know its contents unless communication occurs. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this mode; let association, interruption, and sensory memory do the structural work. The passage may end on an unfinished thought, a returning image, or a momentary return to the present without an obligatory resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the exchange. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in fragments, another in complete sentences, one delays, another replies immediately. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet the fantasy premise enter through what people report, ask, avoid, or keep returning to in the exchange. The impossible element is a fact of the world; characters may describe what they saw, ask whether someone else noticed, send a photo, or change the subject because they do not know how to say it. Show its consequences through the way the messages shift: a practical question becomes urgent, a routine check-in stops, a familiar topic keeps resurfacing. Let the change appear in what a person is willing to type, how long they wait, or what they delete before sending.\n\nUse Maeda's repeated attachment through recurring message habits. Choose a specific phrase, question, shared routine, or object that appears early in the exchange and returns after the fantasy element has changed the characters' understanding. The same greeting may arrive with a different weight, the same question may now be harder to answer, the same shared task may be mentioned with a new feeling. Show the changed weight through the message itself: a delay before the familiar phrase, a shorter or longer version, a draft that is revised, or a reply that arrives without its usual sign-off.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. If a character's unspoken reaction matters, present it as clearly attributed nonspoken material, not as a message the other person can read. Humor and tenderness belong to appropriate people and scenes. The exchange may end on a sent message, a delay, an unsent draft, or a pending question without an obligatory cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagamba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Background or expression cues are purposeful references that orient the moment; they are not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nEstablish the fantasy premise through a concrete scene event or condition. The impossible element should be something the scene can show: a changed room, an object behaving wrongly, a person present who should not be, a light or sound with no ordinary source. Let later scenes show the consequences in what characters do and how the focal character responds. A character may alter a route, repeat a check, avoid a room, or handle a familiar object differently because the premise has made that action newly significant. Show the change through scene action and focal reaction, not through a speech explaining it.\n\nUse Maeda's repeated attachment through scene-anchored callbacks. Choose a specific object, phrase, routine, or relationship and return to it after the fantasy element has changed the characters' understanding. The same background detail may appear again with a new significance, the same line may be said under different circumstances, the same small task may be performed with a changed feeling. The new weight comes from the intervening event, not from a hidden power in the object. Let the focal narration register the changed feeling while the scene detail remains recognizable.\n\nLet the focal narration carry required inner response. A character may think about what the fantasy element means for someone they know, for a shared routine, or for their own sense of safety. Other characters do not know that thought unless it is spoken or otherwise communicated. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Humor and tenderness belong to appropriate people and scenes. A scene may end on a line, a focal reaction, or a quiet acknowledgment without an obligatory choice screen.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagambb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments. Each reply should make clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps: a single detail, phrase, or prior event that the current installment needs, not a retelling of everything that came before.\n\nLet the fantasy premise develop through earned local changes. The impossible element is a fact of the world; show its consequences through knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. One installment may reveal a new implication of the premise, another may show how it affects a practical task or a relationship. Do not require every turn to be a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can be quiet and cumulative.\n\nUse Maeda's repeated attachment through callbacks that accumulate across installments. Choose a specific object, routine, phrase, or relationship and return to it after the fantasy element has changed the characters' understanding. A familiar item may appear again with a new significance, a shared habit may become harder or easier, a phrase may now carry a different feeling. The repetition should deepen understanding rather than restart the same moment. When the callback returns, recover only the specific prior detail needed to make the changed meaning legible, then let the current installment continue from it.\n\nKeep required interiority available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. A character may reflect on what the fantasy element means for someone they love, for a shared routine, or for their own sense of home. Other characters do not know that thought unless it is spoken or otherwise communicated. Humor and tenderness belong to appropriate people and scenes. An installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known without an obligatory hook.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet the established fantastic premise create a practical problem that two people cannot solve without touching the unresolved matter between them. When a character explains how the premise works, write the explanation as an emotional act, not a neutral briefing. The narrator may notice that the speaker is using the mechanism as a shield: listing conditions instead of answering a direct question, reciting a rule to avoid naming what they fear, or offering a tactical solution where an apology or admission was needed. Render the narrator's private experience of that deflection directly—what they wanted to hear, what the explanation told them instead, and what they decided not to say in return.\n\nKeep the physical sequence clear even when the speaker is not. A mechanism can be exploited to protect someone, misunderstood because the character cannot bear the full implication, or explained too late to undo an earlier choice. Let the reversal land on the relationship: a successful workaround may cost the character the very admission they were avoiding; a failed one may leave them with proof of what they refused to say. Show the cost in the actual conversation and in the narrator's nonspoken response to it—the moment they recognize the pattern, the impulse they suppress, the practical offer they accept because direct feeling is too exposed.\n\nAfterward, let the strain persist in behavior such as avoiding a room, over-preparing a shared task, or resuming a routine with too much care. Keep comfort partial and awkward when it appears. Warmth may arrive through a corrected mechanism, a shared watch, or a practical concession, but it does not resolve the hurt or make the characters articulate. Use the narrator's distance and diction to render private experience without a stock vocabulary of suffering: specific wants, specific failures, specific silences. Do not require a confession, reconciliation, or tragic ending; the emotional turbulence is the treatment of the fantasy problem, not a script the scene must complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution, keeping each speaker's actions and speech clearly separated. Treat the fantasy premise as an active condition of the scene: something the characters can work with, misread, or turn to practical use. When a mechanism matters, have a character explain or test it in plain terms tied to the immediate problem, then show the result in the next action or exchange. Do not pause the scene for a lore block; make the explanation part of what someone is doing.\n\nKeep NPCs active during and after the player character's turn. They may continue a task, respond to a changed condition, or act on the information they actually have. The narrator's knowledge of the fantasy premise is limited to what the viewpoint character can observe or already knows; an NPC's private understanding arrives only through what they say, do, or leave visible. Let a reversal come from a character's practical choice or a misunderstanding of the rule, not from a forced twist. The physical sequence should remain easy to follow even when events shift quickly.\n\nEnd at a continuation that follows from the scene: a task in progress, a question someone has just begun to answer, a consequence still unfolding, or a character moving toward a decision. Do not require a cliffhanger, forced choice, or completed revelation. The next reply should be able to pick up the same interval without the scene having been artificially stopped.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the martial adventure route of the Chinese literary tradition: an episodic journey through practical conflicts, where the fantasy premise appears as a technique, resource, or condition that must be understood to be used. Keep the telling vernacular and action-forward. Shape each episode as an encounter that tests the characters' skill, resources, and moral instinct: they meet a problem, try a method, suffer or exploit a reversal, and move on with what the episode changed. The fantastic element has intelligible consequences in the world—it may change how a fight is fought, how a route is traveled, or how a community protects itself—but it need not become a fully explained system.\n\nWhen a mechanism matters, place its explanation inside the episode's arc. A character may demonstrate a technique in the middle of a fight, explain a condition while preparing to use it, or discover a rule's limit when an opponent exploits it before the protagonist understands it. The explanation should be tied to the immediate use or misunderstanding, and the resulting action should appear in the same episode. Reversals arrive through practical choices rather than sudden power escalation: a resource spent at the wrong moment, a technique that works only under certain conditions, a rule the protagonist misread.\n\nLet the journey's community and relationships remain concrete. A stop at a village or a meeting with an old acquaintance can change what the characters know or what they owe, but it should not pause the story for background. End an episode when its immediate problem is resolved or transformed, leaving the next stage of the journey open. Do not transplant a specific cast, setting, or period; use the route's craft, not its canon.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nRender the fantasy premise through hard-edged concrete prose. When a character uses or misunderstands the impossible element, show it as a physical fact: a tool that leaves residue, a condition that changes how weight or distance behaves, a rule that fails at the worst moment because of material wear. Keep transitions compressed and forceful. Use short declarative sentences for physical action and reversals: what happens, what breaks, what the cost is. Move from one action to the next with the minimum needed to keep the physical sequence clear.\n\nLet tactical reasoning appear as practical thought tied to the immediate problem. A character may work through a mechanism in a longer thought when the reasoning needs it, but the thought should stay concrete: what can be reached, what will break, what the cost will be. Slow the rhythm for that reasoning, then snap back to short sentences when the action resumes. Do not let explanation become a lecture. Show the result in the next action, with reversals arriving through a failed resource, a misread condition, or an opponent's faster use of the same rule.\n\nKeep physical stakes present. Injury, exhaustion, damaged equipment, and the limits of the body should matter. Ground rough speech in its speaker: a character's abrasive words come from their situation, not a uniform gritty voice. Vary rhythm so a sudden reversal lands hard after a slower stretch of preparation or recovery. The fantasy premise remains an intelligible consequence of the world, not a horror effect or an excuse for unearned escalation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Each document's knowledge of the fantasy premise is limited to what its author actually understood at that moment. A field report may describe a mechanism the writer used successfully; a letter may explain it incorrectly because the writer only saw one consequence; a later record may correct or complicate the earlier account. Let each document show the practical result of its author's understanding or misunderstanding: a plan that worked, a resource spent, a warning sent too late.\n\nBuild the mechanism across documents through partial, situated accounts rather than a narrator's summary. When one writer explains a rule to a recipient, show the explanation as the writer's practical reasoning for that specific situation. The next document may reveal the consequence of that reasoning through another author's experience: a second writer discovers the condition the first missed, uses the rule differently, or pays the cost the first did not anticipate. The reader reconstructs the fantasy premise from these overlapping and conflicting accounts.\n\nUse the document form required by the scene rather than a fixed rotation. A report can list conditions and outcomes; a letter can mix tactical explanation with personal concern; a log can record repeated attempts to make the impossible element behave. Keep each document readable and continuous, with gaps, revisions, or conflicting accounts revealing events across entries. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record. Preserve the active narrator and ownership: a document contains what its author records, not their complete live mind. The fantasy premise remains an intelligible consequence of the world, reconstructed through partial and situated accounts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet the fantasy premise create manageable practical friction that the characters can solve through care. A character may use their understanding of a mechanism to make someone comfortable: adjusting a condition, preparing a resource, or explaining a rule so a task becomes easier. Show the explanation as an act of attention rather than a lecture. The other person may respond with their own practical help, a familiar preference, or a small pleasure that matters to them.\n\nKeep the tactical element present without turning the scene into a crisis. A problem can be small: a route that needs preparation, a tool that works better with a particular adjustment, a shared task that requires two people to coordinate. Let the characters notice what the other person likes, needs, or finds reassuring, and act on that knowledge. Warmth should be sincere and specific, not a generic mood.\n\nPreserve personality, privacy, and disagreement. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. A character may still be tired, worried, or annoyed while accepting care. The fantasy premise remains an active condition of the world even when the scene's stakes are warm: the mechanism still has limits, the resource still runs low, the larger situation still waits beyond the eased interval. Do not require a confession or emotional repair. The scene can end with the practical problem resolved and the relationship eased, leaving the larger fantasy situation intact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-fantasy-04",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Fantasy · 04/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 4 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPaganag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Frame the fantasy premise through social context, history, or judgment where the narrator permits it: an institution that depends on the impossible element, a community that has learned to live with its consequences, a family whose practical decisions are shaped by what the rule allows. Let the narration distinguish itself from ordinary spoken language without becoming uniformly elaborate.\n\nWhen a mechanism matters, embed its explanation in socially situated reasoning. A character may think through a rule because their position requires them to use it, or misunderstand it because their training or class gave them only a partial view. Move within a sustained paragraph from the social frame to the character's practical reasoning to the resulting action, keeping the physical sequence clear even when the prose is broad. The narration's distance may let the reader see how the character's position shapes their understanding of the mechanism without granting access to excluded minds.\n\nLet reversals arrive through a misjudged resource, a conflicting obligation, or another person's faster use of the same condition. Ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast may organize a passage when relevant, but do not impose an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary. The formal architecture is the style; the fantasy premise remains an intelligible consequence of the world, and the characters' practical problems remain immediate even when the narration takes a longer view.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen on a telling that is doing work in the present. Give the storytelling an audience, an occasion, and a purpose: the listener controls something the teller needs — a decision, permission, safe passage, belief, a name — and the teller opens the tale because plain argument has failed, would be unsafe, or would not be believed. Let the frame run on its own clock and pressures rather than serving as scenery, so interruptions, departures, and the listener's patience can all matter.\n\nInside the tale, run the world's established fantastic premise on legible rules. When a wonder enters the told events, show what it costs, who can use it, and what limit or interaction governs it; let a character within the tale exploit the mechanism, misread its boundary, or combine two rules to unexpected effect. Keep the physical sequence of those interactions followable at the tale's own pace, so a listener reasoning along could arrive at the same turn. A reversal inside the tale lands when it overturns an assumption the telling had already established, not one conjured for the effect.\n\nTreat the telling itself as visible selection. The teller stresses what serves the present purpose, omits what would weaken it, and may repeat a phrase whose meaning shifts as the tale deepens. The listener's questions, interruptions, and visible reactions can redirect the account, and the teller adjusts — adding a proof, softening a cost, hurrying a passage. Keep this shaping legible without reducing the tale to a lie: substantially true events, selectively emphasized.\n\nBind the two levels through recurrence rather than a stated moral. A rule, cost, object, or formula from inside the tale should also bear on the outer situation, and recognition lands when the listener's circumstances catch up with the teller's emphasis. The listener may act on the application, refuse it, or demand another tale; either way, the told events have changed what is available to think with. The frame is craft, not setting — no royal court, djinn, desert, or magical bargain is required, and no cliffhanger must close every account. End where the scene's boundary falls, inside the outer exchange or inside the tale itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the Romantic-uncanny route. Let the established fantastic premise enter ordinary life as a disturbance the narration observes closely but never fully domesticates. Give the disturbance intelligible consequences — a rule, a cost, a limit, a claim on someone's time, property, or attention — so a character can discover it, exploit it, or misread it; when someone acts on the mechanism, show the physical sequence and what changes. The uncanny can stay legible without becoming a solved system: establish what the thing does while withholding why it exists or what it ultimately wants.\n\nHold the narrator's distance steady and let syntax carry it. A close or first-person narrator meets the disturbance through hesitation and repeated observation — returning to the same spot, circling the same phrase, letting practical description slip into longer, more associative sentences as attention frays. A more composed narrator reports with plain clarity while the unease stays visible in what the characters do and say. Whichever teller the story supplies, keep that distance consistent; reach for no national temperament, bureaucratic severity, or translated word order.\n\nPress the premise against everyday arrangements — work, family, travel, property, local custom — through concrete detail rather than general awe. The collision matters at the level of ordinary obligations: a duty complicated by the rule, a route that now costs something, a household adjusting itself around a limit. A character may understand part of the mechanism and still misjudge what it will do to them; when a rapid shift arrives, keep visible which earlier assumption or resource it overturns.\n\nLeave the disturbance partially unexplained at the close. The pressure comes from the encounter between the unresolved presence and the characters' practical choices, not from a final explanation that dissolves it. End where the scene's boundary falls, without a mandatory revelation or a forced return to normal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Gothic-Punk\n\nRun the fantastic premise as inherited authority colliding with present material life. An old rule, obligation, or privilege should have concrete effects on modern arrangements: who may enter a place, what a family owes, how a resource moves, what a person can do without someone's permission. Make the mechanism legible whenever a character can exploit, inherit, or misunderstand it, and keep the physical sequence of the resulting action clear.\n\nGround the collision in specific urban places and objects that carry their own history of use — worn fixtures, locked rooms, shared walls, transit routes, improvised repairs. Let atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced: the same request, in the same tired interior, reads differently by daylight than at the hour when the building empties. Mood never replaces function; a beautiful or ruined space matters through what it actually does and has done.\n\nTreat intimacy as morally complicated and practical. Social masks can fall away in trusted company, revealing private desire, resentment, or loyalty that changes what someone is willing to do, and the narration may show a focal motive that another character does not know. Keep genuine care and cooperation available alongside harm and refusal — an old bond can still oblige, and a trusted room can be simply warm.\n\nProduce reversals from the interaction of rules and resources: a privilege that still works, a modern system that has quietly displaced it, two obligations colliding in one person's evening. When a reversal lands, make the previously established fact it overturns visible. Import nothing from vampire setting material — no Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules; the style supplies a treatment, not creatures.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Set the telling inside a specific community occasion the supplied world already provides — a household gathering, a journey, a festival, a dispute being heard, a memory being marked — and treat the story as the customary vehicle for knowledge that has to be passed on. Keep language, region, community, and period exactly as the world supplies them; force no English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or generic family structure onto the scene.\n\nCarry the fantastic premise inside the told matter on legible rules. When a wonder enters the tale, show what it costs, who may use it, what it changes, and what mistake or discovery follows; a character within the tale can exploit a mechanism, misread its limit, or find two rules interacting unexpectedly. Keep the physical sequence clear through rapid shifts, so the outer listeners — and the reader — follow the causal chain rather than receiving a marvel.\n\nGive the telling a social function beyond entertainment, and make the audience a small public of particular people. The tale may transmit an obligation, account for a custom, warn a younger listener, weigh in on a dispute, or preserve a memory the community still needs. Let the listeners respond from their own stakes: one accepts, one argues the point, one recognizes a personal application the teller never intended. Epic texture belongs here — names, deeds, and remembered consequences the community still lives with — so the tale's past bears on its present without a stated moral.\n\nBind the levels through recurrence: an object, formula, or rule whose meaning accrues as the telling proceeds, its application emerging from the listeners' circumstances rather than from the teller's commentary. Not every listener needs to resolve, and the telling can stop where the scene's boundary falls — mid-tale or mid-response — without a compulsory cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tales route. Present the established premise as a strange occurrence at a particular time, place, and occasion — something someone recounts, records, or passes on — and make its mechanism legible through practical consequence: what it does, what it costs, who can encounter it, what mistake or discovery follows. When a character acts on the mechanism, keep the physical sequence clear even through rapid shifts.\n\nFit the telling to the actual teller. A personal account can move in first person with direct feeling; a transmitted tale may report with composed distance, plain argument, or deliberate omission. Seasonal attention is available where it matters to the characters — a season can mark time, work, absence, or the occurrence's return — without a mandatory inventory of scenery or a universal quietness imposed on the voice.\n\nKeep the occurrence strange even where its rules are legible: a character may know what it does without knowing why it exists or what it wants. The tale can explain a custom, warn against a mistake, preserve a memory, or simply record an encounter that changed someone's understanding, while ordinary life continues around it rather than suspending itself for the marvel.\n\nUse omission and emphasis as the teller's tools. Leave out what the teller never understood, stress the cost the listener should remember, repeat a phrase whose meaning shifts as the account proceeds. Do not collapse the tale into a stated lesson or a solved system. End where the scene's boundary falls — mid-account if that is where it comes — without a compulsory revelation or a forced return to normal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic or metaphysical construction route. Let the established premise enter a specific community's ordinary life as an intelligible intrusion, with consequences for work, family, travel, property, or local custom. Preserve the community's language and history as the world supplies them, and add no supernatural fact beyond what the premise already permits.\n\nMake the mechanism legible through consequence. Where a character can exploit, misunderstand, or resist the intrusion, show what it costs, who can use it, what it changes, and what discovery or mistake follows; hold the physical sequence clear through rapid shifts, so the reader can see which earlier assumption or resource a reversal overturns.\n\nLet the intrusion sit beside ordinary social texture — market days, household obligations, journeys, disputes, remembered hardship — and change how particular people experience that texture rather than replacing it. The fantastic and the ordinary share the same sentences; there is no separate register that suspends daily life for a set-piece. A character may understand part of the mechanism and still misjudge what it will do to them or to someone they care about.\n\nKeep the intrusion partially unexplained. Establish what it does and what it costs while withholding why it exists or what it ultimately wants; the pressure comes from the encounter between that unresolved presence and the characters' practical choices. This is construction, not magical realism by default, and it converts nothing into marvel that the world does not already allow. End where the scene's boundary falls, without a mandatory revelation or a forced return to normal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish where the characters are, what they are doing, and the practical problem the established premise has created, without opening on a history lecture. Track spatial changes concretely — when someone moves, place them before the action continues — and keep paragraph breaks readable around action, dialogue, and private response so the sequence never has to be reread.\n\nExplain a mechanism when a character can exploit or misunderstand it. Give an unfamiliar name or mechanic its clear presentation at the moment it matters — what it does, what it costs, who can use it, which limit or interaction is live now — and then show the resulting action. Do not stop for a full system explanation when the scene needs one rule; let the rest surface later through consequence.\n\nKeep the physical sequence understandable through rapid shifts. When a reversal lands, the reader should see which established fact or resource it overturns: a character acts on a rule, misjudges its limit, or finds two rules interacting, and the physical result of that choice appears before the next beat.\n\nGive each reaction to its owner. A private response can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to who the character is and what the situation means to them; do not tune the whole cast to banter. First person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are not requirements of this form. End where the scene's boundary falls — mid-action or mid-exchange if that is where it comes — without a forced reversal or a completed test.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Lyrical Prose\n\nTreat the fantastic mechanism as a condition the narrator can feel at the edge of the present moment. Choose images from the scene's material realities—heat, weight, light, distance, water—and let each image carry a causal edge: what it enables, what it costs, what it will do if pressure changes. A comparison should make the mechanism's behavior or the character's risk understood, not decorate it. When a character tests a limit, let the syntax quicken through the attempt and then slow around the consequence so the result lands with sensory weight.\n\nUse recurring sounds and images as instruments of tactic. A repeated image may first appear as a peaceful detail and return as the sign that a rule has changed or a resource is failing. The narrator may hold a sound across a paragraph while testing an interaction, so the reader feels the count, the delay, or the strain. Plain declarative sentences provide contrast, especially when the fantastic elements are behaving precisely: a short factual statement after a figurative passage can act like a snapped check.\n\nKeep the lyricism within the selected perspective. The narration may develop a metaphor from a character's training, trade, or immediate perception, but it must not grant that character knowledge they could not infer or attribute a polished figurative voice to every mind. Let the response stop where the present attention naturally rests: on a consequence still spreading, an image half-transformed, or the next test already visible in the changed conditions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse reflection to disclose a concrete change in the world's rules or resources, not merely to linger over feeling. When the narrative recalls an earlier occasion, it should clarify what was possible then and what is no longer possible now, or what the character understood then versus what they understand under current conditions. A remembered image works best when it connects to a present mechanism: a scarred tool, a ward's altered note, a route that used to be open. Let the association carry the weight while a later sentence names the practical implication.\n\nShow the coexistence of present experience and loss through attention to small irreversible changes. The character may notice an index on an instrument, a delay in a recovery cycle, a missing signal, or a changed tolerance. The reflection may be calm; ordinary present pleasure can remain present. Avoid turning every scene into an announcement of impermanence. Instead, let the practical stakes demonstrate the loss: the same task is slower, the same phrase has a different consequence, the same ally is now reachable only by a different route.\n\nMaintain a clear physical sequence even when the narrative drifts backward. A memory should arise from a current action or observation and return to the present choice with new information. Do not let metaphor erase the cause-and-effect chain. Stop where the present moment has absorbed the remembered fact: the character may act on it, set it aside, or notice the changed rule pressing against the next decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a framed oral tale with an identifiable storyteller and occasion. The storyteller may be the focal character narrating a current interaction or a participant relating an event after it happened; keep the frame's distance legible. Within the tale, use Kamachi's practical testing: a character observes a rule, tries a move, reads a consequence, and then adjusts. The frame should not be a neutral wrapper; the teller may stop to correct a listener's inference or to explain a step they took in a wrong order.\n\nUse recurrence as delayed mechanical clarity. A condition or object mentioned early returns later with more exact consequences, so the listener understands the law through its repeated applications. The teller can foreground a test's cost in a way that makes the fantastic element consequential without explaining it like a school lesson. If a listener questions or interrupts, their practical objection should prompt the teller to supply missing information, not turn every tale into a debate.\n\nKeep tradition distinct without importing a fixed honor code, mysticism, exile plot, or ceremonial dialogue. Use the concrete place, trade, household, or public institution already supplied. The oral mode should affect structure: an opening formula, a pause for confirmation, a compact declaration of what was known at that hour, or a closing turn that leaves the mechanism's current limits understood. Stop where the teller's present relation to the tale is clear: a practical detail still in motion, a listener's question already answered, or the rule's change acknowledged in the telling itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small set of exact details that can serve as indicators for the fantastic mechanism: a temperature, a count, a visible boundary, a changed sound. Direct clauses should connect action to consequence. When a character tries an interaction, show the attempt and the immediate result; omit speculation that does not alter the next choice. If an explanation is essential, give the minimum legible fact—what costs what, what triggers what—and let the rest be inferred from the sequence.\n\nDo not strip causality away with the adjectives. A precise extended sentence can describe a mechanism's effect better than several short fragments, but every clause should advance the present account rather than restate it. Keep characters' voices distinct even in simple speech. Dialogue may carry the test: one person points, another tries, a third reads the result. The viewpoint may register only what that character can perceive, leaving a concealed rule to be tested through visible consequences.\n\nLet rapid reversals arrive as direct action. A character may try a resource, see it fail, and switch without a paragraph of reflection. If an inward thought is included, it should be required for the next decision: one line of reasoning, one remembered fact, or one altered inference. Stop after the last detail that materially changes the present account, whether that is a consequence still spreading or the next practical move already chosen.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the fantastic mechanism inside a sustained scene rather than in an explanatory pause. Use the narrator's attention to travel between a character's observation of a rule's visible effect, a piece of dialogue that tests or misstates it, and the physical consequence that corrects or confirms them. Explanations should arise from characters using, avoiding, or talking about the mechanism, not from an anonymous briefing. If a character understands a rule, show that understanding through a prepared action, a counted delay, or a changed sequence; keep unspoken reasoning distinct from speech.\n\nAllow recollection natural access to the scene. A character may remember a previous application of the same rule while performing the current one. Use paragraph breaks to signal a shift from present action to a recalled event and back, without a recap of everything already known. The narrator's voice, the author profile, and the immediate world should set the register; no contemporary setting or understated emotional range is required. Ordinary family, work, travel, or danger may sit beside wonder.\n\nKeep rapid reversals physically readable. A sudden change in a mechanism should be grounded in a specific resource, condition, or misread limit, and the next paragraph should show its consequence. Do not use interiority to explain away every surprise or to make a character perfectly perceptive. Stop where the scene's present attention rests: on the mechanism's new state, a character still adjusting to it, or the next task already visible in the changed conditions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the relationship's private language grow out of the mechanism itself. A shared gesture, phrase, or reaction should refer to a specific earlier test of the fantastic rule: who held the boundary, who counted the delay, who was wrong about the cost. When the same mechanism returns, show how it reopens that private history. Callbacks should carry a revised emotional bearing because the current circumstances have changed a resource, risk, or trust condition, not merely because the words sound familiar.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where they already belong. Dialogue may move quickly, but a reaction can sit in the scene long enough to show a particular person's experience. Keep each character's knowledge separate: one may observe a gesture and infer affection while the other is still focused on a mechanical problem. Do not use a found family, slow burn, or hurt/comfort template unless the supplied premise supports it. Use only the actual relationship materials present.\n\nMaintain legible causal sequences through emotional moments. If a character misunderstands a mechanism, let that misunderstanding affect the relationship plausibly: an accusation, a withheld warning, a clumsy rescue. The private drama should neither replace the motor of action nor require every feeling to be announced. Stop where the shared history becomes legible in the present scene: a callback activated, a character noticing it, or the next shared decision already taking shape.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nLet the account preserve the exact practical movement behind a remembered deed. When the chronicle names someone by a deed—the one who counted the intervals, the one who carried the weight, the one who learned the condition—make the reputation grow from a sequence with causal edges: what rule was known, what resource failed, what choice followed. Do not reduce the fantastic mechanism to a vague wonder; the teller should say what worked, what cost was paid, and what later attempt changed the meaning.\n\nUse deliberate openings and recurring phrasing to organize time. A repeated phrase may mark the same test occurring under a new condition, or the same consequence arriving for a different person. Each return should add information about the rule or the community's relation to it, not merely echo. Compact declarations can introduce a person or an act, then expand into particulars that make the formula incomplete. Oaths, journeys, gifts, or public deeds may organize the account when the supplied world already includes them.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent. A communal narrator may know what has been remembered, but a focal participant should not suddenly speak for all later generations. Let the spoken voice allow direct practical concerns to interrupt high phrasing; another character may answer a hero's epithet with a question about the actual delay or damage. Do not impose doomed honor or ceremonial dialogue. Stop where the teller's present relation to the deed is clear: the memory settled enough to be told, a correction still being made, or the next act already visible in the account's terms.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantasy as a folktale-inflected telling grounded in a specific social world. The impossible belongs to the place: it may sit inside a household routine, a local obligation, a journey, or a remembered bargain. Let the narrative voice speak as one who knows the community's habits, reporting plain observation of ordinary life and the strange with the same even attention. When a wonder appears, show what it changes in the way people work, eat, travel, keep promises, or explain themselves to one another.\n\nUse the Kamachi method to make the impossible legible without explaining away its strangeness. When a character can exploit or misunderstand a fantastic rule, state the relevant mechanism in concrete terms: what must be done, what cannot be undone, what the cost or limit is. Then follow the action that results. Keep the physical sequence understandable even when events shift quickly. A reversal should arise from a rule interacting with a person's immediate need or mistake, not from an arbitrary new power.\n\nLet the folktale voice handle explanation through recurrence and plain consequence. A condition may be stated once and return later with its meaning changed by circumstances. The narrator may observe a household's habits closely, noting how a bargain alters the order of a meal, the route of an errand, or the terms of a promise. Do not require every strange thing to be fully systematized. The story may end with a bargain kept, a debt acknowledged, or a household changed, without resolving every mystery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Screenplay\n\nWrite the fantasy in concise screen-readable scenes. Use scene headings when location or time changes, clear speaker cues, and action lines that describe only what can be seen or heard. Keep each action block short enough to follow during a rapid sequence. When a fantastic rule matters, show its operation through observable events: an object that cannot be moved, a door that opens under one condition and not another, a person who reacts to a limit the audience has already seen established.\n\nLet explanation enter through what characters do and say in the moment. A character may state a rule when they need to use it, warn someone, or realize they have misunderstood it. Do not stop the scene for a lecture or rely on voice-over to supply the mechanism. When a character's private reasoning matters, stage it through visible behavior first: a pause before touching an object, a glance at a threshold, a hand withdrawn and then extended again. If the reasoning cannot be staged, keep it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not as dialogue another character can hear.\n\nUse cuts and offscreen sound only when the chosen sequence needs them. A cut may move from a failed attempt to its consequence, or from one location to another where the same rule is being tested. Keep the physical sequence understandable: what is attempted, what changes, and what remains possible should be clear from the action and speech. The scene can end on a completed action, a visible consequence, or a character's decision to act, without an obligatory cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Stage Play\n\nWrite the fantasy as a playable script. Use clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. When a fantastic rule matters, make it observable in the shared stage space: who can reach an object, pass through a doorway, hear a sound, or move between two places should be clear enough that the rule's operation can be followed.\n\nLet explanation enter through dialogue and staged attempts. A character may state a condition while testing it, warn another person, or discover a misunderstanding through a failed action. Keep the physical sequence understandable even when events shift quickly. Do not add film-camera instructions or rely on a monologue to replace every private thought. When a character's private experience matters, stage it through visible behavior first: a pause before touching an object, a hand withdrawn and then extended again, a repeated gesture that changes meaning. If the experience cannot be staged, keep it as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, not as a speech another character can hear.\n\nUse pauses and repeated business to let a consequence register. A character may set down an object, step back from a threshold, or turn away from a result. The scene can end on a completed action, an entrance, an exit, or a decision to act, without an obligatory gathering or theatrical revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the fantasy through one authorized mind's associative movement. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern braid together. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening from what is remembered or imagined. The fantastic belongs to this mind's world: it may arrive through a familiar object, a repeated phrase, a bodily warning, or a memory that suddenly becomes relevant.\n\nUse the Kamachi method inside the associative flow. When the character can exploit or misunderstand a fantastic rule, let the mechanism surface through the way they notice and test it: a remembered condition, a physical limit felt in the hand, a sequence of attempts that changes what they believe. Keep the physical sequence understandable even when the prose jumps between past and present. A rapid shift should still leave clear what was tried, what changed, and what the character now thinks is possible.\n\nLet the associations themselves carry the fantastic rule's consequences. A remembered bargain may interrupt a present errand; a bodily warning may recall an earlier failure; a repeated phrase may change meaning as the character tests a limit. Do not make every character share a mind or turn all scene facts indeterminate. Private experience remains the character's own, and another person does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. The passage may end on a realization, a sensation, or an unfinished attempt, without requiring the thought to resolve into a completed decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the fantasy through readable sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the story. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may type quickly and correct nothing, another may draft and delete, a third may send only practical questions. Distinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text.\n\nUse the Kamachi method through what people transmit, withhold, or misunderstand. When a fantastic rule matters, let it appear in the exchange as a condition someone states, a limit someone tests, or a consequence someone reports. A character may explain a mechanism because they need help, warn another person, or realize they have misread an earlier message. Keep the physical sequence understandable: what was attempted, what changed, and what remains possible should be clear from the messages and any separated context.\n\nWhen a character drafts a message and does not send it, show the draft as a composed artifact: the words they typed, the moment they stopped, the choice to delete or leave it unsent. Use separated context outside the exchange only when the character's private experience or offscreen action must be made available; keep it clearly separated from the transcript. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. The exchange may end on a sent message, a delay, a read receipt, or a pending question, without requiring a completed plan or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the fantasy in scene-oriented visual-novel presentation. Use concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. When a background or expression cue appears, use it to anchor the focal character's attention: a doorway the character hesitates before, a face that shifts as a rule is tested. Then let the narration or dialogue develop what the cue means in the scene, rather than treating the cue as a completed explanation.\n\nUse the Kamachi method through the focal character's observation and reasoning. When a fantastic rule matters, let the focal narration state the relevant mechanism in concrete terms when the character can exploit or misunderstand it: what must be done, what cannot be undone, what the limit is. Then follow the resulting action in the scene. Keep the physical sequence understandable even when events shift quickly. A reversal should arise from a rule interacting with a person's immediate need or mistake.\n\nLet dialogue and inner response carry different kinds of information. A character may say less than they understand, or explain a rule to someone who needs it. Private thought remains the focal character's own and is not automatically known to the cast. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene may end on a completed action, a changed understanding, or a decision to act, without forcing a choice screen.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaganbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Kamachi Kazuma / Web Serial\n\nWrite the fantasy as installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies. Keep clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps. When a fantastic rule was established earlier, bring back the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole explanation: a condition the character must meet, a limit they have already tested, or a consequence they are trying to avoid.\n\nUse the Kamachi method across installments. Explain a mechanism when a character can exploit or misunderstand it, then show the resulting action. Let the physical sequence remain understandable even when events shift quickly. When a later installment reveals a hidden condition, show how it fits the earlier observed event rather than contradicting it: the character may realize that the condition was present all along, or that their earlier test only appeared to cover the relevant case.\n\nLet progress involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources rather than a forced leveling system. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. The installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, leaving the next reply free to continue the same through-line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nEstablish one intimate fantastic consequence early and let it recur as the material cost of the relationship. It may be a pact that drains warmth from a room when a particular name is spoken, a familiar spirit that grows jealous of a human attachment, a magical debt that attaches to affection, or a transformation that makes touch unsafe. Choose the consequence that fits the scene and keep it consistent; do not explain the whole system or introduce a second competing wonder.\n\nMake the consequence legible through Li Bihua's controlled sensory precision. Show its effect in the ordinary textures of the scene: the exact color of a cooling cup, the weight of a bracelet that once meant a promise, the smell of a room after an argument, the careful distance between two people who still want to be near each other. Let a beautiful surface carry discomfort. The narrator may notice what a character cannot say aloud, but keep the narration in the chosen person and distance.\n\nUse the recurring consequence to shape what the characters admit, withhold, or return to. When the fantasy cost presses on the scene, let it alter actual conversation and subsequent behavior: a character may apologize without being forgiven, return without being welcomed, or offer comfort that the other person accepts only partially. Warmth can coexist with pain. A shared meal may ease nothing while still mattering; a touch may be both relief and reminder. Do not force a confession, a reconciliation, or a final break.\n\nLet the fantasy element sharpen jealousy or divided attachment through concrete behavior rather than explanation. The impossible thing may remember what the characters want to forget, or demand a loyalty that competes with human affection. Show that competition in a door that will not stay shut, a mirror that keeps the wrong reflection, a charm that grows warm when someone lies. The characters need not understand the mechanism; they need to live with its effects.\n\nKeep the emotional register intimate rather than operatic. Avoid a stock vocabulary of suffering. Instead of saying a character is devastated, show them folding a letter they have already read, or speaking in the same tone they use for strangers. Let the narrator's diction stay precise and controlled even when the feeling is turbulent, so the restraint itself becomes part of the hurt. End where the scene's actual pressure ends, with the relationship and the fantasy consequence still active.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each response as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs. Attribute every line of dialogue unmistakably, and keep narrative passages short enough to scan while still carrying sensory detail. Establish one concrete fantasy presence for the interval and keep it active: a charm that reacts to a name, a familiar that watches a doorway, or a magical debt that makes a particular room feel colder. Do not pause the scene to explain the system; let the characters live with its effects.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision in the narration between speeches. Notice the texture of a sleeve, the taste of tea that has steeped too long, the way a character touches an object they once gave someone. Let these details carry feeling without turning every response into a confession. A glamorous surface can sharpen discomfort: a beautiful room may feel like a stage where someone is performing calm.\n\nLet the fantasy presence shape NPC behavior specifically. An NPC may answer a question indirectly, continue a household task, remember something at an inconvenient moment, or offer help that comes with a condition. Their choices should follow from their situation and temperament as the fantasy presence affects them, not from a need to push the player toward a decision. The fantasy element may make an NPC's loyalty divided or their affection costly, but do not force a betrayal or a revelation.\n\nKeep the active world and NPC behavior moving without requiring a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end. The scene may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup. The next response should be able to pick up from what has just happened, whether the player's character speaks, acts, waits, or leaves. Preserve the active authorship contract, narrative person, and player control throughout.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Keep the story within a particular household, shop, courtyard, street, or neighborhood, and establish one impossible presence that belongs to that place: a mirror that shows the previous tenant, a debt that follows a family, or a spirit that remembers a promise made at a festival. Do not expand the story into a kingdom-spanning quest or an explained magical system. The impossible thing belongs to this specific community and changes its ordinary routines.\n\nWrite with Li Bihua's sensual precision and attention to remembered places. Describe the grain of a wooden counter, the smell of rice cooking in a back room, the sound of a gate closing after an argument, the way a particular room keeps the cold after a ghost has passed. Let these details carry the weight of attachment and jealousy. A beautiful object may be a burden, a gift may be a claim, and a familiar place may become strange without losing its familiarity.\n\nLet the fantasy consequence affect relationships and decisions. A character may keep a dangerous charm because it belonged to someone they loved, or avoid a room where a spirit waits because going there would mean admitting something. Show how the household adjusts: who will not speak of the matter, who leaves offerings, who pretends nothing has changed. The community's habits, gossip, and practical arrangements should remain legible without turning every neighbor into a chorus.\n\nUse the intimate route's pacing to move through small, significant scenes rather than grand events. A meal, a visit, a quarrel, a repair, or a night spent awake can carry the story. Let the fantasy element return through a specific sensory signature: the same sound, the same stain, the same question asked at the wrong hour. Each recurrence should revise what the household understands or fears, not merely repeat the first impression. Do not require a tragic ending or a full explanation. The story may end with the household still living alongside the impossible thing, changed but not resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard-edged, concrete prose with compressed transitions. Move from one significant detail to the next without transitional padding: a cracked cup, a wet sleeve, a charm that leaves a burn on the palm. Establish one fantasy element as material fact and keep it consistent. A ghost may lower the temperature enough to fog breath; a magical debt may show as a stain that spreads after each broken promise. Do not explain the mechanism. Show its physical cost.\n\nKeep Li Bihua's sensual precision by choosing a few high-contrast details rather than lush description. One exact image can carry the glamour and the damage: the red thread knotted around a door latch, the pearl that turns black in a liar's hand, the silk curtain that smells of smoke from a fire no one remembers. Let beauty and cost occupy the same object. A valuable thing may be dangerous to keep, and a beautiful person may be exhausting to love.\n\nGround rough or abrasive speech in its speaker. A character may speak sharply because they are afraid, tired, or protecting someone, not because the style requires toughness. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let dialogue stay short and specific. After a hard exchange, show the consequence in behavior: someone sets down a bowl too carefully, or leaves without closing the door.\n\nUse rhythm changes for emphasis. Compress routine action, then slow down when a character's reasoning needs room. A longer thought is acceptable when the character is deciding whether to touch a cursed object, return to a place, or believe what they just saw. Keep the thought concrete: weigh the risk in terms of what will happen to a body, a room, a debt, or a relationship.\n\nDo not turn the fantasy into survival horror. The impossible thing may be intimate and strange rather than monstrous. The story can remain about attachment, jealousy, memory, or the cost of beauty, but the telling stays lean and physical. End where the scene's actual pressure ends, without requiring a completed confrontation or a resolved feeling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the story through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Establish one recurring impossible event or object and let each document approach it from its author's situation. Use the document form the scene requires: a letter that must not say too much, a household ledger with entries that stop at a certain date, a physician's note that records symptoms without naming their cause, a pawn ticket for an object no one should have sold. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Each document exists because someone needed to record, request, explain, or conceal something.\n\nLet the fantasy element enter through what the documents notice and omit. A letter may describe a room as cold without saying why. A diary may record the same dream for a week and then stop. A receipt may show that a charm was redeemed twice. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts should carry the strangeness. One writer may call an event a haunting; another may call it bad luck or a family matter. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision within each document author's voice and purpose. A letter writer may linger on the color of a borrowed coat or the smell of a house after a quarrel. A ledger keeper may note that a particular cup was broken and replaced with one of lesser quality. Let these details reveal attachment, jealousy, or the cost of beauty without turning every writer into a poet. The document's form and purpose should discipline the description, so a practical record notices cost and damage while an intimate letter notices texture and memory.\n\nShow divided attachment through what different writers choose to record. One may preserve a flattering memory; another may correct it in a margin note. A later writer may discover an earlier letter and respond to what it did not say. Let the fantasy consequence affect the documents themselves: ink that fades on certain pages, a letter that arrives already opened, a record that repeats a name the writer did not intend to write.\n\nPreserve readable continuity across entries. Arrange the documents so the reader can follow events, but allow uncertainty where the writers themselves were uncertain. The story may end with a final document that raises more than it settles, or with a material trace that survives the people who made it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Establish one gentle fantasy presence and let it participate in the scene's particular acts of care: a charm that keeps tea warm, a spirit that mends torn hems, or a magical garden that grows the flowers someone remembers from childhood. Show the wonder through ordinary enjoyment rather than danger. The impossible thing may be strange, but it can also be gentle, useful, or fond.\n\nKeep Li Bihua's sensual precision in the details of care. Describe the exact way a character prepares a favorite dish, the weight of a blanket placed over someone who fell asleep, the sound of a brush through hair, the particular scent of a room after it has been aired and swept. Let these details carry affection without requiring a confession. A character may show love by remembering how another person takes their tea, or by saving the best portion without being asked.\n\nPreserve personality, privacy, and disagreement. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable. A character may accept care awkwardly, refuse help they do not want, or argue about something small while still staying close. Let the fantasy presence have its own preferences and limits: a household spirit may refuse to enter a room that has been redecorated, or a magical creature may sulk after being thanked too formally. Keep these frictions manageable and particular rather than turning into betrayal or dread.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring emotional repair. A character does not need to explain their feelings, apologize for everything, or reach a new understanding before the scene can be warm. The story may simply spend time with people who are glad to be near each other, even if an older hurt or a difficult obligation still exists in the background. Do not remove established problems from the world, but do not let them dominate the present interval.\n\nEnd where the warmth has had room to register. The scene may stop during a shared meal, a quiet task, a walk home, or an unfinished conversation. No confession, gift, or magical solution is required to close the response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let a scene open through its social or historical situation: a family that has kept a particular house for generations, a neighborhood that still observes a custom no one fully believes, a public celebration that privately means something different to one character. Establish one fantastic burden within that framing and keep it consistent. A ghost may be understood first through what the community says about the house, or a magical debt through the obligations that bind a family. Do not introduce the fantasy element as an isolated shock.\n\nUse narrative distance where the chosen narrator permits it. The narration may compare a present moment with an earlier time, note what a room says about its occupants, or observe how a character's public manner differs from their private feeling. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language: sentences may be fuller and more periodic, but do not make every character speak archaically or elaborately. The formal architecture belongs to the narration, not to a uniform aristocratic voice.\n\nKeep Li Bihua's sensual precision and attention to remembered places within the broader movement. A sustained paragraph may move from the texture of a curtain to the history of the room to the reason a character cannot bear to change it. Let beauty carry cost: a carefully kept garden may be a form of mourning, a valuable object may be a reminder of a promise that was broken, a beautiful person may be trapped by the attention their beauty attracts.\n\nLet public and private contrast organize a passage when relevant. A character may perform calm at a ceremony while privately dreading a particular encounter. A letter or formal visit may say one thing while the narration shows what it costs to say it. The fantasy burden should be something the household manages in public and suffers in private, or something the wider world dismisses while the characters know its weight.\n\nDo not require a grand resolution or a tragic ending. The novelistic movement may carry the story through a series of connected scenes, each deepening the relationship between the fantasy burden and the characters' attachments. The response may end at a pause in that movement, with the public and private still in tension and the impossible thing still present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nGive the telling a present occasion and a listener whose attention is not idle. The story may be offered to delay a parting, to soften bad news, to warn without naming a threat, or to make a listener recognize herself in a remembered figure. The teller's purpose is always concrete: the embedded tale must expose an attachment the listener has not yet named aloud, so the outer scene becomes a negotiation with that newly visible feeling.\n\nLet the nested story carry a fantastic fact as an intimate consequence. A house remembers a promise; a birthmark grows warmer near a hidden room; a song that was sung at a wedding keeps one flower from wilting. Show this fact through bodies, furnishings, meals, or weather rather than through explained magic. Its effect should reach the listening character: a habit, a fear, a loyalty, or a question she has carried becomes newly legible because of the old story.\n\nUse Li Bihua's controlled lushness to make beauty inseparable from cost. Describe the scent of a meal that was also a farewell, the weight of a gift that demanded a secret, or the shine of a floor kept spotless around a stain no one names. Do not let the frame cancel the tone of the embedded story; let the teller's voice grow quieter, more precise, or more ceremonial as the inner tale turns toward what hurt.\n\nKeep the story levels clear through recurrence. A repeated phrase, object, or gesture can move between the old story and the room where it is being told. Change its meaning on return: the same folded paper that once sealed an engagement can now measure how long the listener has kept a wrong assumption. When the outer scene resumes, let the listener notice one concrete detail differently. The changed perception is the revelation; no confession or duel is required, though the tale may occur before one.\n\nRespect the frame's delay. The teller may pause at the point where the inner story would become too close to the listener's own. The pause is part of the persuasion, not a technical break. When the story ends, show what has altered in posture, address, or willingness to remain in the room. The fantasy premise need not be re-explained, but its effect on the present relationship should feel earned by the telling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as an uncanny narrative in the Romantic manner: the impossible enters through repetition, a domestic object, a seasonal return, or a place that once belonged to someone now absent. Use Li Bihua's sensual precision to give the uncanny fact a material body. Do not announce a ghost, a curse, or a hidden lineage. Show the opened window on a windless night, the same hair caught in two different doorways, or the child's drawing that repeats a face the household has stopped naming.\n\nKeep syntax clean and modern; the route is not a copy of translated German word order. Place subordinate detail where a living speaker would naturally look for it. Let the narrator watch from a moderate distance, close enough to notice the smell of floor polish and far enough to avoid confessional meltdown. The strangeness should grow in calm, exact sentences. If the story is grim, allow plain statement to do its work: warmth in one room, cold spreading under the shutters, a bell that rings only when a particular coat is touched.\n\nCost and attachment must stay concrete. A wedding gift, a rented room, a kept photograph, or a carved bed frame can carry the unspoken debt between two people. Let the fantastic recurrence alter that relationship by forcing a practical change in how the character uses the charged object or space: the coat is finally moved, the photograph is taken down, the extra chair is removed, or the room is left empty for the first time. The uncanny event need not be solved or banished. Its meaning becomes clear through that changed use, but the narrator may refrain from diagnosing everyone. Jealousy and divided affection may sit beside practical kindness; neither must cancel the other.\n\nUse the route's attention to landscape and building as memory. A staircase, a courtyard, a freezer, or a station platform can hold the past without being described as a metaphor. When the impossible returns, show one ordinary consequence: a key no longer fits, a meal is prepared for an extra chair, or a dance lesson begins on the wrong afternoon. The response of the environment makes the fantasy intelligible. End when the present situation has shifted, not when every earlier event has been explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the fantasy arise where an old inherited authority presses against the city's working present. A family key still opens a municipal archive; a private lane retains its pre-war streetlamps; a clinic night shift follows a roster signed decades ago. Show these as living arrangements with practical consequences, not decorative history. The impossible premise may be an unchanging portrait that receives offerings, a tattoo that rises only near a certain bridge, or a telephone number that answers only during a power cut.\n\nUse Gothic-Punk place to make a relationship physically legible. Write the basement storage room with peeling vinyl, the tram window fogged by breath, the club back exit lit by a refrigerated sign. The social masks people wear at work, in line for medicine, or during a family anniversary can fall away only where there is an audience: a booth, a parking garage, a shared cigarette. Trust should feel earned because the setting exacts a price for privacy. Li Bihua's glamour lives in the contradiction: a beautiful dress purchased with a pawn ticket, a polished table set for a visit no one will confirm.\n\nMake the fantastic fact costly. Beauty and pleasure can be real and still require secrecy, money, memory, or waiting. The old authority does not need to be evil; it may simply maintain a building, a clinic, or a service in a way that gives one person excessive power over another's daily life. Show who pays the electricity, who can enter after curfew, and who remembers the original bargain. If the impossible event is sexual or romantic, let hesitation and divided loyalty remain specific rather than melodramatic.\n\nDo not import vampire physiology, blood bonds, or faction names. Keep inherited privilege in the form it already has: leases, uniforms, passports, birth records, family businesses, or church registries. When the atmosphere changes an encounter, localize that change. A meeting under flickering sodium light may permit a confession that the same characters could not make by daylight. End at the point where the present relationship moves: a door opened, a lie ended, or a mask allowed to stay on because both people understand it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the reflective inward route. Use a first-person narrator who notices the immediate household world with Li Bihua's sensual precision: the weight of a brass vessel, the smell of neem leaves outside a window, the way a cotton sari or kurta holds body heat. Let the fantastic fact enter not as an announced miracle but as a recurrent sensory proof. The same flower falls from a ceiling hook, the same song arrives through the wall at an impossible hour, or a photograph's glass remains warm only when a particular relative is gone.\n\nKeep philosophical attention on harm without making it abstract. The narrator must examine one specific harm through the changed use or avoidance of a recurring object: a broken promise measured by a locked desk no one opens, a child kept in the dark by a school fee paid without explanation, or an unacknowledged beauty held in a pair of spectacles left on the prayer shelf. Jealous and divided attachment may appear in the gaps between what is served, repaired, stored, or taught. Let the impossible event sharpen a moral question rather than resolve it. The narrator need not reach a creed or a conversion.\n\nUse remembered places as a method of composition. The narrator may compare a past room with a present one, but do not organize the piece by chronological autobiography or a generic family timeline. Instead, one sensory detail calls up a specific earlier detail: the smell of rice water now returns a particular rainy afternoon. The inner story should make the reader understand why the narrator watches this door, listens for this bell, or avoids this chair. Keep the language intimate and plain; do not force English, code-switching, or an explanation of regional custom where none is supplied.\n\nWhen the impossible fact affects daily life, show it through the material rhythm of hours and duties: clothes drying at the wrong rate, a recurring power cut that begins only when a certain name is spoken, or a phone that receives calls before the bell rings. The story may end with the narrator accepting a changed understanding rather than achieving a practical solution. Safety, choice, and ethical agency remain open. The point is the way this place and its attachments have become different to the one who remembers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the fantastic-tale route and tell it in the immediate present. Keep the narrator close to one character's physical sensation: cool tatami, a starched collar, a shared thermos, the pressure of a comb. Let the impossible fact return through seasonal or domestic repetition. The same wilted blossom reappears in a textbook, a particular child's geta stand outside a shuttered shop, the smell of a long-closed kitchen returns whenever the family album is opened. Do not explain these as rules or ghosts with a fixed code of conduct.\n\nUse Li Bihua's beauty-with-a-cost method to show attachment as precise and difficult. A gift is wrapped so beautifully that it becomes a claim; a long-held comb retains its owner's impatience; a borrowed coat keeps the shape of the person who left. The eerie event should carry desire, jealousy, or divided loyalty without announcing each feeling. Let what is unsaid fall into the white space between sentences. The narrator can notice a silence, a delayed answer, or a pair of slippers turned the wrong way. The unreal detail is treated with the same calm exactness as a train timetable.\n\nLet household and seasonal change make the fantasy consequential. A meal comes out hot despite a power outage; a sprig of plum blossom keeps its scent through a whole month. These small impossibilities alter how the characters use their rooms, prepare for guests, or speak to one another. Do not turn the story into a cautionary lesson or a tidy recovery. A child may know more than the adult acknowledges, but the tale need not reveal everyone's mind.\n\nAttend to selection rather than inventory. Pick two or three sensory facts and let recurrence give them force. A word, a wrapping cloth, a morning sound can be repeated with slight variation to show the uncanny pressing closer. Close at a point where someone acts or chooses differently because of the changed day: a window left open, a photograph turned face down, or a voice speaking a name usually avoided. The impossible remains part of the world, and the present relationship has shifted without explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nBuild the story around one impossible construction, an event with an internal logic that ordinary life must learn to accept. It may be a city map that changes when a particular couple quarrels, a borrowed apartment whose rooms multiply only after mourning, a pair of shoes that cannot be taken off until a promise is kept, or a public clock that moves backwards whenever someone breaks off an engagement. Whatever you choose, establish it through Li Bihua's sensuous immediacy: the smell of coffee, the heat of a bus, the pressure of a ring inside a pocket, the lint on a rented dress.\n\nThe fantastic event must be treated as fact, but not as mere local color. Its rules are real and occur among work, family, buses, paperwork, and jealousy. If the doors multiply, the owner must account for rent; if the clock reverses, someone misses a shift. The cost is specific: a meal that cannot be left, a fare that cannot be refunded, a letter that cannot be posted. Do not place the story in a generic magical village or use butterflies and yellow roses as automatic props. Use the setting actually supplied; if none is supplied, make the urban or domestic place concrete through one careful room or street.\n\nLet attachment be jealously precise. The impossible event must expose a claim of possession that the living character has not settled: a kept shirt that still belongs to the dead, a stored photograph that forbids a new marriage, a locked dresser that holds back an inheritance, or a plate set for an absent person that no one will remove. The narrator may not fully understand its origin, but the story should make that claim concrete through the object's use or refusal. Li Bihua's controlled lushness should show how beauty and love can also function as compulsion and surveillance.\n\nKeep the prose linear where possible and let the fantasy expand one consequence at a time, even if the narrated day becomes strange. Do not introduce multiple metaphysical systems. When the event changes, show the effect on ordinary arrangements: neighbors mention the new wall, a taxi driver refuses the street, or a child knows the correct bedroom before being told. The story may end with a small practical accommodation of the impossible, not with a reversal that erases it. Preserve the intimacy and unease of living inside the rule while the surrounding world continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Light Novel\n\nOpen inside the immediate situation: the character has just touched the strange object, missed the last tram, received the invitation, or noticed the changed photograph. State where the eyes go first. If a room has an impossible door, show the character's hand before the knob. If snow falls indoors, show breath, glass, or a burning candle. Keep paragraphs short and break for questions, denials, and attempts to touch or move the impossible thing.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensuousness in economical bursts. Do not write three sentences of atmosphere; write one vivid physical signal: the almond smell in a mailbox, the cold seam on a borrowed coat, the way a gift's ribbon tightens around the finger. Let these details recur when the relationship changes. The remembered place should be made present through action: opening a drawer, folding a sleeve, or setting down a cup exactly where someone else used to leave it.\n\nThe fantasy fact should be visible enough to stage. It can be a clock that counts down only when a certain person speaks, a photograph whose subject moves, a scarf that will not dampen in rain, or a stray cat that appears every time a phone is about to ring. Show it through clear physical evidence and the characters' immediate reaction beats. Reaction does not have to be witty or frightened. Let thought arrive as a short internal sentence: \"The lock wasn't supposed to open from this side.\" Keep private response separate from dialogue with a line break.\n\nTrack spatial changes cleanly. Move from entry hall to kitchen to balcony in readable transitions, naming one or two orienting details. If the impossible rule affects movement, show the attempt: running upstairs but finding the same hallway, leaving by one door and arriving in another district. Character attachment stays emotionally concrete through blocked gestures: a hand that stops before touching, a message drafted and deleted, a gift returned but then picked up again. End at a focused beat where the scene can continue, not necessarily with a cliffhanger. First person, comedy, or a young cast are not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Lyrical Prose\n\nRender the fantastic as a sensuous fact before it is an explanation. Enter each paragraph through a concrete image the premise supplies—the temperature of altered air, the weight a charmed object gives a pocket, the color a threshold keeps at dusk—and let the sentence rhythm expand that image until it discloses an emotional consequence: desire, jealousy, fear, or divided loyalty. Shape the sound as well as the sense; a repeated vowel or soft consonant can hold a feeling in the ear while the clause works. Let a comparison unfold across clauses only when it changes what the focal character understands or makes present, and keep its terms drawn from the scene or that character's own associations.\n\nReturn a chosen texture, scent, or sound later in changed circumstances. A light first noted as lovely and cold may next fall across a private quarrel, so the coldness becomes inseparable from what the characters feel. Each return should carry new emotional or practical meaning rather than mark time. Weave the premise into ordinary acts—how someone cleans, bargains, travels, waits—so wonder sits beside work and its cost is lived rather than announced.\n\nKeep plain speech distinct from the figurative narration. A character may answer a beautiful sentence with two blunt words; that contrast is part of the music. Let beauty have a price the scene itself shows—through restriction, loss, or an attachment that cannot be comfortably held—without the narration naming that price aloud.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite from a position where the fantastic is familiar but its meaning has shifted. Alternate present experience with remembered occasions the premise touched, and make each memory do explanatory work: it is why the present detail feels different now. A character who once touched a magical door without thinking may stand before the same door and understand its cost through accumulated absence or attachment. Hold the two time frames legibly apart—what the character knew then, what is known now—and do not retroactively insert later grief into the earlier scene.\n\nLet reflection arrive in both registers. The focal character may compare then and now directly, or a single object—a token kept from another year, a half-finished task—can carry the unfinished relationship without the narration explaining it twice. Give the premise practical consequences in both frames: what people could once do, what they no longer attempt, what they fear to try. Small irreversible changes accumulate the effect.\n\nKeep ordinary pleasure genuinely pleasurable. A meal, a joke, a warm room may stay whole even while something else is gone, and the character is allowed to notice both without ceremony. Do not close scenes by announcing impermanence; end where the scene's attention actually rests, whether mid-task, mid-conversation, or in the quiet after one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell this as a situated frame: a narrator with a name, a place, and a reason tells a tale to at least one listener, and the telling situation stays alive through brief returns. Build the frame from the community, language, and period the story actually supplies. If the world belongs to an Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Hebrew, Kurdish, or other tradition, keep that tradition's storytelling manners distinct rather than blending them into a shared bazaar décor; neither antiquity, mysticism, exile, nor piety is required to make the frame authentic.\n\nInside the tale, carry the fantasy premise as lived consequence—a conditional blessing, a transformed object, a place that answers only certain questions—and let the telling show why it matters: where it lingers, what it refuses to explain, which name it repeats. Give the narration intimate precision. A remembered room, the feel of a fabric, a face at a particular hour can carry desire, jealousy, or divided attachment without ornamenting every sentence.\n\nWhen the frame returns, let the listener's question or reaction alter the telling—a pause, a repeated phrase, a detail newly included or withheld—so the frame works as a second emotional layer rather than an interruption. Mark entrances into and exits from the embedded tale clearly, and keep the teller's spoken narration distinct from characters' direct speech within it. The reply may stop before the tale concludes; stop where the withholding itself means something, with the tale's own phrases recurring at moments that deepen them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite in direct clauses with a small number of exact details, and make each detail do two kinds of work at once: locate the fantasy premise concretely and carry an emotional or relational charge. Rather than describing a magical object at length, give its temperature against skin and the fact of who gave it or who wants it back. Sensual precision comes from selection; one right noun outperforms a paragraph of adjectives.\n\nKeep inward connection only where it materially serves the present account. A single sentence of what an object meant may stand; the rest of the history stays inferable through gesture, hesitation, or a spare line of dialogue. Omission must leave something to infer, not delete causality: if a character refuses to touch a charmed thing, show the refusal plainly and trust the reader to know the cost is known. When a thought genuinely needs length, let the sentence run long enough to make sense—one precise extended construction serves better than performed fragments.\n\nLet the premise surface inside ordinary action—locking a door, bargaining, walking home—so the impossible belongs to the world's working life rather than floating above it. Keep each voice individual; bluntness in the narration is not bluntness in every mouth. A scene may rest after a supported action or a held pause, with no revelation or moral owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained, scene-first prose. Open inside an action, a place, or an exchange where the fantasy premise is already part of working life—someone cleaning a shrine, arguing over supplies, traveling a road where the impossible is ordinary—and let its cost show in the doing rather than in a lecture. Move naturally among the focal character's perception, speech, authorized private thought, and brief recollection; orient through paragraphing and transition instead of recap.\n\nKeep the profile's intimacy inside that movement. One remembered room, one texture, one gesture of jealousy can open a private history if it arises from the scene's own business rather than arriving as an ornamental insert. When a comparison appears, keep it within the character's actual knowledge and present situation. The register may run warm or unsettling, but stay in contemporary-novel prose: flexible, unforced, attentive, without reaching for sustained lyric elevation.\n\nMake the premise's consequences practical and social—a household rule, a market negotiation, a friendship strained by a magical obligation, a workaround learned the hard way. Inner thought may reveal desire, uncertainty, or resentment without every inward moment turning the plot. Stop where the scene reaches a supported pause or change; no lesson, completed arc, or summarizing final note is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nTake the established relationship as the through-line and choose scenes for what they reveal about it under the fantasy premise's pressure: jealousy, secret comfort, refusal, shared work, cautious discovery. Build legibility through private language. A phrase, a gesture, a small ritual recurs between the characters, and each return marks a new pressure or a small reversal inside the bond—not a mechanical repeat. Work only from the premise actually supplied; do not stock the scene with borrowed tropes it never promised.\n\nFilter the bond through sensual attention. A face, a hand, a remembered place noticed with enough precision shows why the attachment costs what it costs. Beauty should sharpen the uncomfortable feeling—longing, suspicion, the pleasure of being chosen, the fear of being replaced—rather than soften it. Let disagreement and jealousy change the exchange; not every moment is tender, and comfort, when it comes, is earned by the scene.\n\nLet the premise alter the pair's practical life in a specific way. If one keeps a magical restriction hidden, or one depends on an object the other once handled, show it through behavior and dialogue rather than explanation. Give the key emotional beat enough space to land before the scene moves on. The reply may end before confession or resolution; use delay when the delay itself is the meaningful event.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a chronicle declaration drawn from the story's own world: name a person, a deed, a bond, or a consequence that people still remember, then move into the particulars that complicate the reputation. Let the fantasy premise enter as something whose effects are still remembered or disputed—a journey, a gift, a breaking, a protection—and keep the scale true to the actual cast; a village matter may weigh in the telling as much as a battle.\n\nRecur to phrases the tale itself has earned: a form of address promised once, a ritual question, a short description fixed to a deed, returning later when its meaning has changed. Against that public voice, let private heat interrupt. The teller—or the focal figure inside the telling—recalls the smell of a room, the weight of a token, the jealousy beneath a generous act, so the gap between the remembered story and its private cost stays palpable. Keep access consistent with the chosen perspective; a third-person oral recollection does not suddenly enter every mind.\n\nAnchor the remembered deed in the community's ongoing life—trade, labor, hospitality, worship—so it is not a dead legend. Dialogue may dispute a detail or answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern. The reply can end by setting the present act toward a future retelling without proclaiming it legend or doom on anyone's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantasy as an administered local condition. The impossible enters through the practical institutions of a specific community: a municipal rule, a workplace procedure, a housing arrangement, a ferry schedule, a shop's terms of trade, a church or civic custom that people obey or resent without needing to explain it. The narration should record the social surface with a realist's patience—names, occupations, rents or harvests, meal preparations, bus and ferry times, the small formalities of neighborly life—so that the fantastic element sits inside the same ledger as ordinary work and weather. Do not announce the magic; let it appear as a named seasonal fact, a regulation, a family obligation, or a piece of inherited knowledge that characters handle with the same competence or irritation they bring to other duties.\n\nKeep the telling rooted in one focal character's actual knowledge and remembered places. The narrator may know what this person sees, touches, owes, and recalls, but not the hidden minds of others. Let Li Bihua's controlled lushness work through precise sensory details attached to objects the focal character can handle: the weight of a wet coat, the smell of a particular hallway, the temperature of a cup, the color of a bruise or a worn table. Beauty should carry a cost that is socially legible—a garment that must be mended, a favor that changes a household, a room kept empty, a debt recorded in someone's account—rather than a private mood. Attraction may divide or complicate practical loyalty without turning anyone into a villain.\n\nUse a folktale-inflected telling as a secondary layer, not as the main narrative voice. When a story is handed down, a custom repeated, or a warning attached to a place or object, attribute it to a specific speaker and occasion: a neighbor at the door, a relative over coffee, a coworker during a break. The focal character may believe, doubt, or half-remember the story, and the narration should preserve that uncertainty. Keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts, foods, institutions, and speech habits distinct wherever they matter; do not turn the setting into a generic North.\n\nPlace required private experience inside the narration as the focal character's actual thought, attention, or remembered sensation, not as automatic mysticism. An inner moment may be calm, practical, and even unglamorous, because the fantasy persists through routine feeling. Do not force every emotion toward doom; preserve the possibility that a cost is manageable, an attachment ordinary, or a memory simply a fact. Let the scene close where the immediate obligation or realization ends, without requiring a symbolic completion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Screenplay\n\nTreat the fantasy as an established, visible condition of the scene, not as a stylistic fog. Show it through practical consequence: a character takes a different route, refuses a lift, locks a door, pays with unusual currency, or answers a question twice because the impossible condition makes that choice necessary. The opening action line should establish one such consequence immediately—a shop that opens only when a particular object is weighed, a street where a remembered face appears on the wrong day, a household duty that must be performed before a journey—and the scene should then follow the effect rather than explain the whole system. Keep the mechanism offscreen; let the viewer see its pressure on ordinary decisions.\n\nWrite action lines with Li Bihua's sensual precision concentrated into what the camera can hold: the weight of a wrist when a bracelet is removed, steam from a basin, the color of a bruise under a particular light, the sound of a kettle before a confession. Give each character a physical relationship to objects—mending, carrying, trading, hiding, offering—so desire and cost become visible business rather than spoken themes. A beautiful thing may be fragile, stolen, inherited, or owed, and its handling should reveal the character's attachment without a line of dialogue explaining it.\n\nLet dialogue carry Li Bihua's intimate, ironic, nostalgic, or flirtatious undertone while remaining speakable on screen. Characters should talk about practical things—rent, a journey, a meal, a debt—while the deeper attachment shows in what they avoid, repeat, or suddenly remember. A beautiful sentence may be followed by a deflating or surprising turn, but do not force every exchange into polished banter. When private thought is required, place it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, marked so the reader knows the character is not speaking aloud and the other cast members do not hear it. Use voice-over only when the scene genuinely needs interior recollection, not as a default substitute for thought.\n\nCut only when location, time, or continuous action demands it. Use offscreen sound to extend the supernatural: a knock at a door no one opens, a voice repeating a name from another room, the chime of a shop bell after closing. Let a cut deepen comparison rather than explain. End the scene on a completed visible action, a held look, or a line that leaves the magical condition changed in a small but definite way, without requiring the next scene to resolve it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Stage Play\n\nStage the fantasy as part of the playable world. The impossible condition must be embodied in what the actors can do and what the audience can see: a prop that must be covered, an entrance that cannot be used, a repeated sound, a spoken condition, or a physical routine that the characters observe with ordinary carefulness, haste, avoidance, or ritual. Let the fantastic alter decisions through movement—a character will not leave until the candle is lit, will not touch a particular object, will not answer until a name is spoken—so the rule is legible without spectacle or explanation.\n\nWrite dialogue with Li Bihua's dramatic language made theatrical. Characters should speak in clear, image-rich sentences that can be delivered with weight, then broken by pauses, interruptions, or practical domestic remarks. Avoid lengthy psychological self-explanation. Instead, let each speaker reveal longing, jealousy, nostalgia, or fatal attraction through what they ask for, refuse, remember, or fail to say. Cost appears in gestures and objects: a garment that must be worn or removed, a debt of hospitality, a favor that cannot be returned, a beautiful thing nobody is permitted to own.\n\nUse blocking to create intimacy and division. When one actor crosses toward another, that crossing matters; when two speakers avoid a table, chair, or window, their avoidance is legible to the audience. Stage directions should remain economical and playable, recording observable conditions—who can see an entrance, reach a prop, or hear an offstage sound—rather than the characters' hidden motives. Keep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, clearly attributed and unavailable to other characters; it belongs to the focal character's private knowledge, not to a monologue or aside unless those speeches have an actual onstage purpose.\n\nLet the fantasy intrude without spectacle. A sudden silence, a bell, a light change, a property moved out of place, or an actor standing where no one should stand can carry the supernatural. End the scene on a staged action or line that leaves the magical condition changed for the characters, not merely explained; the next entrance or exit may interrupt without resolving the feeling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the fantasy from inside one authorized mind. Let the established impossible condition enter as a recurring, charged concern: a remembered touch that changes daily plans, a debt that returns with a smell or a phrase, a house whose rooms shift every time the character recalls them. Do not present the magic as a detached narrator's explanation. The mind should notice it, worry at it, forget it during a practical errand, then return to it when an ordinary sensation—steam, a button, a soap smell, a half-heard name—calls it back. The recurrence should be specific to this character's history, not a random catalogue of sensory triggers.\n\nMake the associations Li Bihua's kind. Use concrete, luxurious particulars attached to the body and to familiar places: the weight of a wet sleeve, the gloss of a lacquered box, the cold of a floor through cloth, the taste of a preserved fruit eaten too quickly. Let memory and desire slip together until the reader cannot always tell whether the character is recalling a room, a gift, or an old punishment. Cost should arrive as an ache or an absent thing: a favor unpaid, a beauty kept too long, a person named only in a moment of self-reproach.\n\nKeep the stream governed by syntax and paragraph shape that follow the mind's jumps, but retain enough orientation for the reader. Real events, recollections, and fantasies should remain distinguishable, even when they share the same sentence. When a new perception interrupts a memory, let the interruption be sharp and physical. The fantasy does not have to be explained whenever it appears; it may stay as a half-understood condition, but it must continue to change how the character behaves, purchases, avoids, or answers in the daylight parts of the scene.\n\nDo not give other characters the same mental access. Their speech and visible acts appear through the focal mind's interpretation. Private thought is this character's actual experience, not a narrator pretending to guess. End where the mind settles, is interrupted, or turns to a new mundane task; the impossible need not be solved, but it should leave a definite alteration in thought or bodily feeling that the next passage can carry forward.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse messages as the primary story medium. Each sender's digital habits should be specific and established through their actual messages: one writes long, edited paragraphs in a formal register; another sends short bursts with erratic capitalization; another lets messages sit unanswered; another types flirtatious or teasing lines, then deletes them. Let the fantasy alter these habits in a legible way. A character may refuse to confirm a name, send a photo of the wrong room, demand that a message be forwarded to a particular person, or stop replying when the impossible condition is mentioned. The premise should appear through what people type, avoid, forward, screenshot, or leave unsent, not through a dramatic explanation inserted into the chat.\n\nGive each voice Li Bihua's intimate, ironic cadence. In text, this means a beautiful observation followed by a deflating or teasing remark, references to shared places, and small sensual details that a person would actually mention in a message: the smell of a taxi, the weight of a good luck charm, the cold of a screen after a missed call. Jealousy, longing, and fatal attraction can glow inside practical complaints about rent, dinner, or a late train. Keep the supernatural matter-of-fact in conversation; characters treat it as part of their everyday arrangements, not as a revelation to be solved.\n\nDistinguish the transcript from private experience. A sent message is visible to all participants. An unsent draft is shown only if it matters to the exchange and is clearly marked as unsent. Required inner thought is not a transmitted message; place it outside the transcript as clearly attributed context, using a character name and speaker cue or a labeled background note. None of that private material is known to the other characters unless it is actually sent or inferred from observable behavior.\n\nUse timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they carry meaning. A long delay can signal avoidance, a read receipt can create social pressure, an edited message can expose a changed account. Do not invent interface events merely for subtext. Let each exchange end where a character sees, sends, or refuses to send something. The fantasy need not be solved in the chat; it needs to have changed a concrete plan, promise, or relationship in a way the next exchange can continue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaoba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in short, scene-rooted blocks that let an image and a line of dialogue do equal work. Set each scene with one or two concrete details the reader can hold in mind: a paper lantern over a narrow stair, rain against a shop window, a tray of persimmons on a wooden counter, a folded coat beside an empty bowl. Then move into dialogue and the focal character's immediate reaction, letting the visual background remain behind the exchange without constant re-description.\n\nMake Li Bihua's intimacy operate through focus and memory. The viewpoint may notice the edge of a sleeve, the color under a fingernail, the pause before a name is spoken. Reaction beats should be specific and change slightly as the scene continues: attraction becomes wariness, nostalgia becomes regret, jealousy becomes a private refusal. Do not label every line with an expression cue; use a cue only when the illustrated expression would materially affect how the line is read.\n\nLet the fantasy appear as an established visual condition: the same room is described differently after a door closes, an object is remembered in the wrong hand, a tea remains hot too long, a reflection moves out of step. The impossible need not be explained. It should change what the character does within the scene—going back for a forgotten item, refusing a seat, asking someone to leave a light on, answering a question with a change of subject. Keep the scene legible and the dialogue natural to the active voice.\n\nKeep required private thought as focal narration or clearly attributed inner response, not as spoken lines or knowledge available to the other characters. The style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images. End a scene after a meaningful exchange, a changed visual detail, or a small decision. The fantasy's cost may remain open; the scene's emotional and practical consequences should be sufficient to advance the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaobb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Li Bihua / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment so the readers who have been following can see what has shifted and new readers can enter with one small anchor. State only what must be recovered for the current scene: the character's name and the relevant object, place, or obligation. The anchor should come from an earlier Li Bihua image—the same painted fan, the same cracked mirror, the same half-finished letter—now used in a new context. Avoid retelling the whole history of a fantastic premise; let its consequences speak.\n\nKeep the story's through-line in mood, relationship, and the cost of beauty. If a jewel was offered in one installment, a later installment may show its absence, its mending, or a new owner. If a character remembered an old room, the next entry can enter that room and find it changed. Across replies, preserve recurring themes: an impossible condition, a divided attachment, a remembered place, a practical burden carried with sensual awareness. Each installment should alter something small but definite—a promise, a possession, a daily route, a debt—even if the larger fantasy remains unresolved.\n\nUse developments that follow naturally from the characters' ongoing lives. An accident of work, a family obligation, a postponed journey, or a chance meeting can push the story forward. When a turn is dramatic, ground it in what the audience already knows or has just been shown, not in a sudden new rule. The twist should cost someone something: a security, a hope, a relationship, a way of living. The cost should be visible in the next ordinary scene rather than announced as tragedy.\n\nEnd each reply at a point of earned tension. The last line may be a half-remembered phrase, a change in the quality of light, a decision made not yet spoken, or a message received after midnight. It should leave the next installment with an emotional or practical question to answer, not a cliffhanger for its own sake. Preserve interiority through the focal character's actual thought and sensation, available to the reader but not to other characters unless the contract permits. Continue from there.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the impossible as an established condition that people accommodate without announcing its strangeness. Show how it shapes a morning's chores, a debt between neighbors, a family expectation, or an official record. Do not pause to explain the mechanism; let the reader infer it from ordinary arrangements and from what the viewpoint considers normal.\n\nUse plain observation to expose emotional cost. When a character admits a need, withholds an apology, or returns to a difficult relationship, mark the strain in the concrete exchange: a delayed answer, a dish left untouched, an errand that becomes an excuse. Let the fantasy premise create a practical consequence—a debt that cannot be repaid, an injury that changes daily work, a requirement that makes someone choose between honesty and safety—but do not prescribe the outcome. Warmth may arrive awkwardly, through an act of care that does not repair the underlying wound.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance and irony. A speaker may call something \"only natural\" while the scene demonstrates otherwise. Let small humiliations accumulate through what everyone accepts. Avoid a vocabulary of suffering or a political lecture; the social damage should reside in the gesture, the ledger, the shared food. Stop when the exchange has shown its cost, not when relief or ruin has been guaranteed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in short, readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep the fantasy premise as an accepted part of the setting: characters may pay in tokens, avoid a door that only opens at certain hours, or mention an impossible condition as casually as weather. Do not explain the system; show it through what an NPC treats as routine.\n\nUse Lu Xun's plain diction and irony. Let an NPC reveal their position by what they take for granted, such as \"It has always been this way,\" while the scene makes the damage visible. Avoid ornate description and emotional exclamation. Keep each reply focused on a single concrete exchange and its immediate consequence, whether a shared meal, a small refusal, or a piece of work completed or interrupted.\n\nEnd the response where the NPC has made a choice or remark that naturally invites the next move from the player, but do not force a question, cliffhanger, or binary decision. The continuation should follow from the NPC's recent action: a hand left near a ledger, a door still ajar, a payment accepted or set aside. Preserve active world behavior and the player's ability to respond without the narrator imposing an outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Break the story into a small number of clearly marked episodes, each advancing a practical consequence of the fantasy premise: a spring that returns only certain words, a household object that shifts when a promise is broken, a local official who owes his post to an inheritance with an impossible price. Keep each episode short enough to be told aloud, with concrete objects and ordinary transactions carrying the weight.\n\nLet Lu Xun's irony govern the narration. A villager may call a custom \"proper\" while the episode shows who benefits and who is quietly damaged. Avoid direct moralizing; the social meaning should emerge from the sequence of received words and small humiliations. Use unsentimental observation: record what is done, not what characters loudly feel.\n\nOrder the episodes so that a recurring object, person, or phrase changes meaning. A first episode may show the impossible practice accepted without question; a later episode may show its cost through the same gesture. Do not require a final judgment or a reversal in every installment. The tradition supplies a readable episodic world, not a transplanted setting or an automatic patriotic or tragic conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse hard-edged concrete prose. Describe the fantasy premise through its material effects: worn steps that count a debt no one can pay, a window that holds the cold of a forgotten promise, a register where a name appears after death. Keep transitions compressed and let physical wear show the passage of time.\n\nGround any rough speech in its speaker. A laborer, a clerk, or an old household servant may curse or refuse, but the words should belong to their place and immediate need, not float as universal toughness. Use high contrast between a clean object and a damaged one, a crowded room and a quiet corridor, a full basket and an empty hand. Balance short declarative sentences with a longer stretch of reasoning when the character works out a practical consequence.\n\nKeep Lu Xun's unsentimental irony by letting what characters call normal carry the damage. A remark such as \"It always ends this way\" may be delivered without self-pity while the scene shows the cost. Do not convert the fantasy into survival horror or dungeon danger. The stakes are social and material: a lost tool, an unpaid share, an accepted injury, a household custom that becomes impossible to keep.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the form required by the scene: a household account, a letter to a relative, an official notice, a copied prayer, a receipt. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet each document reveal what its author accepts as normal. A letter may praise a neighbor for \"following custom\" while omitting the debt the custom enforces; a receipt may note an impossible date without comment. Keep Lu Xun's unsentimental irony in the gap between official phrases and material damage. Preserve the difference between what a document records and what its author knows; a false or partial account should be explained by the writer's situation, not by an inserted omniscient correction.\n\nUse material traces and revisions to carry the fantasy premise. A torn page, a changed numeral, a name written twice, a seal missing from a place it should be, can show the impossible condition affecting record-keeping and obligation. Gaps between entries should advance understanding without a separate narrator's summary. The reader may compare documents to infer what no single author states, but the fiction itself remains within the documents' voices.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nShow warmth through particular, practical care within the established fantasy world. A character may warm food at a stone that holds heat only while a promise is kept, or mend a garment with thread that never frays as long as the owner remembers the maker's name. Let the impossible condition be part of everyday household life, handled without wonder or horror.\n\nKeep Lu Xun's plain observation. Describe the movements of hands, the arrangement of bowls, the time of day when a room becomes comfortable. Avoid inflated emotion, declarations of devotion, or tidy reconciliation. Affection may appear as a person remembering how another takes tea, sharing a stool, or finishing a task without being asked. Let disagreement and private worry remain; comfort need not resolve them.\n\nDo not make everyone agreeable. The kind act may sit beside a sharper remark, a small refusal, or an unspoken resentment. The fantasy premise may still impose a cost—a door that will not open until an apology is made, a lamp that burns only while two people speak honestly—but the scene need not reach that obligation in every reply. End at a moment where care has been given or received, without announcing that all trouble is over.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Frame each scene through social context, history, or judgment where the narrator permits it: a household custom passed down since the previous generation, a public office whose records carry an impossible order, a marketplace where a particular trade is avoided for reasons everyone knows but no one states. Keep the fantasy premise as an established fact that institutions and families have absorbed, not as a sudden intrusion.\n\nDistinguish narration from ordinary spoken language, but keep Lu Xun's clean prose. Avoid archaic vocabulary and uniformly elaborate speech. The narrator may comment with irony, noting that the family called the arrangement \"natural\" while the receipts show otherwise. Social damage should be made particular through observation of an ordinary act or a small humiliation, not through a lecture.\n\nUse public and private contrast to organize a passage when relevant: a formal observance in one room and a private transgression in another, or a letter read aloud and a ledger kept hidden. The fantasy condition may affect ceremony, record-keeping, or inheritance, and historical weight may gather around a recurring object or date. End the passage where the social framing has yielded a concrete shift, without requiring a confession or a final judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nGive the telling an audience, occasion, and purpose. A character recounts a deed, a promise, a journey, or an ordinary event that holds a social wound. The listeners have reasons to care: they need to decide, to delay, to warn, or to understand. The fantasy premise affects experience and institutions; treat it as a real part of the world rather than a wonder to be explained. The teller's language stays plain and unsentimental, so the damage shows through small particulars: an unreturned greeting, a rule no one questions, an item used without permission, a debt remembered as generosity.\n\nLet the teller's position emerge through selection and emphasis. What the teller finds ordinary may reveal more than what the teller finds strange. An account can be sincere while omitting something the teller did not understand. The outer listeners return to one concrete detail—who was present, what was said, what changed hands, when a door closed—and that detail silently revises the group's understanding. When the teller repeats a word, show one listener noticing the recurrence and adjusting their sense of the teller, the event, or the rule being described. Repeated phrases connect the narrative levels; they are not proof that the story is true.\n\nA nested tale may appear without a magical bargain or a royal court. It can be a story within the story that explains a custom, a rumor, or a name. Keep the levels intelligible by marking each return to the outer scene with a physical or practical action: a cup set down, a fire tended, a listener leaving. The embedded story does not need to solve the outer difficulty; it often changes how the outer difficulty is seen. End a response at a meaningful point of attention, not at a required cliffhanger. Let the next turn continue from a listener's comment or from the teller's choice to resume or withhold.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the route of documentary and historical-memory prose. The narrator or focal character moves between official records and remembered conditions. The fantasy premise is already part of the world; treat it as a real institution or established practice with consequences that appear in ledgers, regulations, testimonies, and daily routines. Let the prose stay clean and unsentimental. Expose a social wound through ordinary documents and acts—an entry marked in the wrong column, a rule cited without question, a small humiliation recorded as an administrative decision.\n\nKeep distance according to the narrator. A document itself may appear as a quoted passage, but the narrator's voice remains composed and plain. Let records and recollections disagree about details of access, timing, or authority. The difference is not necessarily a lie; it shows what people accepted as normal. Social meaning emerges without a lecture. When the system is unfamiliar, give it intelligible consequences: show how a rule changes what someone may carry, where someone may sleep, or who may speak. Do not require a systematic explanation of the fantastic premise, only its practical effects.\n\nMeasure remembered conditions against the record. A person remembers a room, a gate, or a working day; the file remembers it differently. The comparison makes the institution visible. Historical memory includes words people repeat without knowing their origin. Let those phrases accrue meaning through recurrence. The narrator moves through the quoted report, notice, or faded note with plain irony, treating each document as one more object in the room rather than as the final truth. End a response where the comparison has become concrete, without resolving every discrepancy or forcing a completion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the collision of old authority and present life through a particular encounter. The fantasy premise is an established institution or inherited arrangement, not a secret to be explained. Use urban material detail—a patched roof, a damp courtyard, a queue at a window, an old building repurposed—to make that collision concrete. Keep the language plain and unsentimental. The social damage appears in what a character accepts as normal: waiting where told, paying a fee without asking its purpose, lowering their voice near a particular door. When the atmosphere is dense, ground it in an observable object or gesture rather than vague dread.\n\nLet intimacy be morally complicated without losing Lu Xun's irony. A private desire need not be noble or clean. Show a social mask falling away in trusted company through a small betrayal of decorum: a warning given too late, a hand withdrawn, a phrase repeated in confidence. The mask need not hide a monstrous secret; it may simply hide exhaustion or complicity. Keep private inner experience available under the active contract, not replaced by voice-over or an aside. Connection does not immunize anyone from harm, and harm does not require every caring act to be revealed as fraudulent.\n\nWhen the old authority touches present life, make one concrete consequence affect the encounter: a permission, a debt, a name, a room, a rule about touching. Let that consequence alter what a character may do or say in the scene. Do not import kindred physiology, numerical humanity, factions, or blood-bond mechanics. The atmosphere changes how the encounter is experienced—through the smell of damp paper, the sound of a gate, the weight of a bundle—not through imported supernatural rules. End a response when the immediate encounter has become legible, without forcing a confession, a reversal, or a completed test.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the route of testimonial and autobiographical accounts. The narrator speaks from a specific position—age, work, community, region, language—without needing a frame-tale audience. The testimony moves between remembered events and later understanding, often highlighting an ordinary act or small humiliation: a name mispronounced, a queue reordered, a rule explained as though the listener should already know it. The fantasy premise is an established part of the world. Treat it as a real condition that affects work, travel, family, or daily obligation, not as a wonder demanding explanation.\n\nLet the narrator reveal position through what is accepted as normal. Render the remembered speech and habit that carried that acceptance: 'that was the arrangement,' 'everyone knew,' a shrug, a lowered voice, a form filled without reading it. When the region, language, or community is supplied, use concrete particulars: timings, exchanges, street names, foods, forms of address. Do not force code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure into the scene. The language stays clean and unsentimental; social meaning emerges through the accumulation of small particulars rather than a lecture.\n\nBecause the narrator tells from memory, the distance between what was understood then and what is understood now is part of the social meaning. Mark that difference subtly: a remembered tone, a repeated excuse, a caution the narrator once accepted without question. The fantastic does not need to be explained; show its intelligible consequences through practical details. The narration may pause for reflection, but each reflection should be tied to an event or object. End a response when the testimony has made an ordinary moment vivid enough to carry the injury, without requiring that the narrator reach a final judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the route of diary and reflective miscellany. Entries may be dated, episodic, or loosely connected; they record what the writer noticed, omitted, and accepted. The fantasy premise is an established part of daily life. Show its consequences through small entries: a delivery that changed because of a rule, a route altered after an omen nobody questioned, a neighbor who was never seen at a certain hour. Keep the language plain and unsentimental. The injury appears in what the writer records without comment—an apology that was not offered, a gate that remained shut.\n\nLet omission carry social meaning. A diary can record an event without naming its cause, or list goods without noting who could not afford them. The writer may accept a small humiliation as ordinary. Occasional reflection may connect an entry to a wider condition, but do not turn each moment into an explanatory lecture. The voice follows the actual teller: age, occupation, habits, and what they choose to write down. Seasonal attention is available—cold rooms, shortening days, rain on a queue—but there is no required inventory of Japanese scenery. Use only the setting and period actually supplied.\n\nThe diary entry is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live mind. When private experience is required under the active contract, render it directly as the writer's present thought or sensation rather than substituting the entry's wording. Let unmatched details accumulate: a recurring name, a repeated errand, a phrase someone used twice. These items accrue meaning through recurrence without becoming symbols. End a response after the writer has recorded a particular moment, without requiring a summary judgment or a completed sequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse social realism and chronicle. The narration follows a community, family, workplace, or neighborhood through concrete public texture: queues, permits, festivals, names, meals, and the small transactions that keep daily life running. The fantasy premise is an established part of this reality. Treat it as an ordinary material fact with visible consequences—an old service, a regulated substance, a seasonal event, an inherited duty. Do not explain the fantastic as a wondrous system; show how it affects who may work, travel, marry, or speak. The language stays plain and unsentimental.\n\nExpose the institution through an ordinary act. A person presents a paper, waits, is moved to another line, lowers their voice, or repeats a formula without questioning it. These actions reveal acceptance and small humiliation. Let speakers and narrator show their positions through what they treat as normal. A chronicle can include testimony, but it should not become a string of speeches; keep the social meaning in what people do and fail to do. Use particular place names, foods, titles, and timetables only when the actual setting provides them; do not invent local color or assume a generic Latin American atmosphere.\n\nBecause this is a chronicle, order events by practical sequence more than by emotional revelation. Let one change in a routine—a missed delivery, an unfamiliar official, a gate closed earlier—push the narrative forward. The fantasy premise may be treated with the same tone as a water shortage or a work schedule; its strangeness is not the point, its consequences are. Recurring details can accrue meaning, but they do not need to resolve as symbols. End a response after the chronicle has made a particular condition concrete, without forcing a lesson or a completed argument.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Name the place, the time of day, and what the viewpoint character is doing in one or two sentences. The fantasy premise is already part of the world; show it through a concrete detail in the scene—a posted rule, a modified tool, a queue's behavior, an accepted payment. Introduce an unfamiliar name or mechanic through its effect on the present action: the toll the character must pay, the gate that will not open, the form the clerk refuses. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: signal movement with simple physical cues such as 'the line moved forward,' 'the gate opened,' 'the clerk looked up.' Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response.\n\nLet the viewpoint character reveal a social wound through what they accept as normal. They wait without complaint, repeat a formula, step aside for someone, or think 'that was how it had always been.' The narration stays plain and unsentimental. A reaction beat can be serious, uncertain, angry, or dryly funny according to its owner; a small humiliation often works best when the character does not fully name it. Do not force witty banter or a quip in every exchange. The established fantasy premise may affect institutions—a registry, a toll, a work rule, a guard's authority—but do not turn it into a mystery needing systematic explanation.\n\nKeep private experience close to immediate action. A reaction after dialogue can be a brief thought, a bodily sensation, or a shift in attention. It need not lead to a decision or confession. Show the institution through ordinary moments: a shove in a crowd, a form refused, a window that is never open. If the scene involves multiple characters, let each speak and move from their own practical position. End the response after the immediate exchange has become clear, without requiring a cliffhanger or a completed task.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet a fantastic premise reach the narrator through a shaped image that the scene itself can support: a public clock that runs backward, a household spirit that keeps one lamp burning, a road that rearranges the order of shops when no one is counting. The image is not allegory first. Render its ordinary consequences—the altered schedule, the tradesman's adjustment, the convenience or nuisance—before allowing any larger association to gather. Lu Xun's irony belongs to that social rendering: a wonder can be accepted as normal even when it quietly damages someone, and a local authority may accommodate it without understanding it.\n\nRepeat a sound or image only when a later occurrence revises what it means. The first mention of the backward clock may be a public annoyance; its second mention, across a changed street or a child's question, may make visible the habit that has grown around it. The lyric movement should therefore connect perception and social fact, not drift toward private mood alone. A comparison may unfold through the sentence—'like a man who has forgotten the order of his own house, the street receded before the watchman's lantern'—but only if it clarifies what the teller sees or comes to understand. When such a comparison opens into social perception, let a plain observation keep it grounded: who pays for the wonder, who benefits, or what adjustment has become routine.\n\nKeep other minds outside the narrator's access. A figure may be beautiful, suspicious, half-explained, or resigned, but another person's interior remains inferred from gesture, speech, or record. The narrator's own thought may be associative and inward without becoming a monologue that displaces the scene. When the response ends, let attention remain on the tangible present: the clock's hesitation, the shop's altered awning, the unfinished errand. There is no need to close with a moral sentence, a symbolic resolution, or a vision of the whole city changed. Stop where the business, speech, or waiting pauses, with the image still working and the social condition still recognizable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse the fantasy as a continuing, intelligible change against which small losses become visible. A family's bargain with a house-spirit may have once kept the garden fertile; now the garden is smaller, the spirit's service poorer, and the characters still arrange their days around a promise no one fully remembers. Begin with the present arrangement, not an explanation of the past. Let one tangible detail—a cracked jar still kept on the sill, a door that must be knocked three times—carry both the old wonder and its diminished authority. Lu Xun's irony belongs in the social handling of that detail: the aunt who polishes the jar will not say what it was for, the neighbor praises the custom without believing it works, and the removal of the jar would cost more in family argument than it saves in floor space.\n\nReflection should arise from a concrete memory rather than a general mood. When the narrator sees the jar, have them recall a specific day: the year the spirit did not answer, the sister who was sent to another household, the winter the garden was planted too late. Then return to the present object and let the difference between then and now remain implicit. Do not announce that all things pass. Ordinary pleasure can still be present—a warm bowl, a familiar joke—and may sit beside the knowledge of change without canceling it. A remembered occasion may be incomplete or disputed, and the narrator's version need not be flattered by melancholy.\n\nAllow reflection to move through the narrator's own privilege or complicity. The lost garden may have been lost to a neighbor's need as much as to neglect, and the family may remember their own virtue too cleanly. A gesture or a sentence of dialogue may interrupt the reflection with a present social fact—a debt, a name no longer spoken, a room kept empty—but the interruption is not a required beat; reflection may also settle into the present object without external correction. Close the response at a still point where thought and present talk or work overlap, without a summary epiphany or a promise of return. The loss remains social and particular, not a universal elegy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the framed oral-storytelling route: a situated teller speaks to a small audience about a wonder, and the story's meaning lies partly in what the teller chooses to omit. Set the occasion concretely—an evening in a courtyard, a pause in a shop, a guest's arrival—and let the audience interrupt, doubt, or supply their own explanations. The fantasy is not a hidden force to be revealed at the end but an established feature of local life, such as a well that shows a person's unpaid debt or a bridle that can tame a household argument to silence. The teller describes its working in plain terms, and the audience's questions expose the social logistics: who may use it, who pays, who is blamed when it fails.\n\nUse Lu Xun's irony inside the frame. The teller may present the wonder as a benefit to everyone while the circumstances show it serving a landlord, a creditor, or an older relative. A listener's interruption can expose the omission without the narrator needing to condemn it in an editorial sentence. The teller's own acceptance of an arrangement—that a servant cannot touch the well, that the silence-cost is borne by the youngest daughter—is itself the critique. Keep the teller's language clean and slightly formal appropriate to oral recitation. Recurring phrases may mark stages—'as is known in this quarter,' 'but the matter did not end there'—but they should arise from the teller's habit rather than decorate every transition.\n\nEnd the response with the frame still open or with a new question raised by the tale, not with the teller pronouncing a moral. The final sentence may return to the physical occasion: tea growing cold, a child listening at the threshold, the audience debating whether the story is true, or a listener asking a question the teller does not answer. Let the fantasy remain intelligible but unsettled, its consequences visible in the listeners' practical concerns. This is not picaresque, spiritual allegory, or a comic tale; the humor may be dry, and the damage social and precise.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose one fantastic adjustment and show its social handling in exact, economical actions. The adjustment need not be explained: a stairwell that always opens onto the same neighbor's door, a scale that forgets a debt, a streetlamp that moves ahead of the lamplighter and leaves one corner dark. State it once, plainly, then show who has learned to use it and who is inconvenienced by it. Do not add a history or mechanism unless it changes a character's present decision. The phrase 'as usual' can carry both the wonder and its normalization.\n\nUse direct clauses and one or two concrete details per paragraph. A gesture—the lamplighter stepping around the dark corner, the shopkeeper setting a weight to the side—should stand in for explanation. Lu Xun's irony comes through what the character accepts as normal: the tenant who pays the same rent for a door that opens two blocks away, or the clerk who blames the lamp's stubbornness on the weather. Do not explain the unfairness; let the arrangement be visible in the list of daily adjustments. Keep required interiority in its active presentation. Precise thought can add a connection, inhabit a feeling, or register sincere agreement with an action; its presence is not gated on revealing new information.\n\nOmit reflection and sensory decoration that do not alter the account. A single concrete phrase—'the fourth step was always damp'—may do more than a paragraph about memory. Keep dialogue functional; a speaker may reveal complicity by what they do not ask. End with the smallest action that leaves the social condition legible: a key turned, a receipt folded, a mat pulled straight. The response stops there, with the fantasy still working and the cost still unspoken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nTreat the fantasy as one established feature of the scene's world, introduced through concrete routine rather than exposition. A family may keep an enchanted ledger that records services before they are rendered, or a municipal bridge may toll one memory per crossing. The narration should move among action, dialogue, perception, and the viewpoint character's thought without signaling the fantasy as the only interesting thing. Begin in the midst of a practical situation: a clerk checking figures, a daughter carrying a receipt to the bridge office. Let the fantasy's consequence appear through the task—the ledger showing a payment no one remembers making, the memory that surfaces a little thinner after a crossing.\n\nUse Lu Xun's irony by keeping the narrator's tone neutral while exposing the small humiliations the system creates. A character may be unable to refuse the bridge because the toll collector knows their address; another may nod at the ledger's convenience without seeing what it costs the family. Do not stop the scene to explain that this is unjust. Let the arrangement be accepted through ordinary phrases—'the office preferred the ledger,' 'everyone crossed once to prove they were not afraid'—and let the viewpoint's private awareness differ from what they say. Authorized inner thought may flow naturally into the next action, but another character's mind remains closed. Recollection enters when it connects to a present choice or perception, not as a detached flashback.\n\nTransition between beats with physical movement, a shift of speaker, or a change in what the character notices, not with 'meanwhile' or synopsis. Paragraph breaks can mark the shift from public compliance to private doubt, or from one side of the room to another. End the response at a natural pause in the scene: an unfinished conversation, a decision postponed, a character looking at an object whose meaning has just changed, or a task resumed with a new awareness. The closing need not resolve the fantasy's moral or reveal its origin; it should leave the social fabric still visible in the ordinary motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBuild each scene around the supplied relationship premise, using only its particular dynamic—rivalry, care, an existing bargain, a shared secret—not a gallery of imported tropes. The fantasy participates in the relationship's private language: a spell that one character performs only when the other is afraid, a token that changes warmth according to an unspoken promise, a household charm they maintain without explaining it to the family. Show one small repeated moment early and return to it later with a shift of meaning. The repetition should be specific to these people, such as the way one checks the token before answering a letter, or the pause at the threshold that the other has learned not to comment on.\n\nGive affection space without making every character uniformly tender. Lu Xun's irony keeps the relationship imperfect and socially visible. A gesture of care may be rationed by class, family expectation, or a debt that belongs to only one of them; one character may accept the fantasy's terms as normal while the other sees the cost. The viewpoint may know the other well through private signs without receiving access to that other's thoughts. Reveal the other's interior through dialogue, action, and the few details they allow, not through the narrator reading a mind. A moment of comfort can be real and still incomplete; a promise can be kept while its social consequences remain.\n\nUse scene selection to make the promise legible. If the premise is a cautious intimacy, choose the moment one character waits without being asked, or the moment the other notices the waiting. If the promise involves discovery, let the discovery alter only the next small decision, not flatten the characters into one emotional template. Short narrative passages may move between perception and action; the narration stays with the active perspective. End on a beat that fits the scene's actual pause: a hand not taken, a question heard through a closed door, an errand resumed with new understanding, or a private sign acknowledged without being named. The response should not require a reconciliation, a confession, or a completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nCompose the account as a retelling: a deliberate opening that names the wonder and the people to whom it belongs, then compact statements of what was done and what followed. Use a recurring phrase when a later occurrence changes its charge. The fantasy should affect common activity—a field that turns stones back into seed after a quarrel, a household's oath-fire that refuses to light for anyone who broke bread under false terms—so that its consequence is shared, practical, and arguable. Begin not with an explanation of the wonder but with the community's claim about it: 'This is the field that took the Weavers' anger and gave back three harvests, and for that the youngest daughter still sleeps by the gate.'\n\nLet the chronicle's form sit uneasily beside what it records. Lu Xun's irony enters through local reputations that simplify a harder social fact. The famous deed may have been a small theft, a coerced gift, or an act of obedience presented afterward as courage. Do not resolve that gap with an editorial sentence. Instead, let a speaker within the account contradict one detail, or let the recollection of the deed include an incidental harm—a brother sent away, a debt shifted to a servant—without the teller seeming to notice the harm. Keep dialogue sparse and used to interrupt the authority of the retelling: a wife may ask whether the fire ever lit for her, or a child may repeat the phrase 'as the old people say' while testing whether it still holds.\n\nMaintain the teller's access consistently with the chosen perspective. You may use a focal narrator who can report public recollection and their own private unease, but not the minds of all involved. The ending should leave the account at rest in the community's memory: the field lies fallow, the quarrel is not quite forgotten, another modest act waits to be told, or a listener asks a question the retelling cannot settle. Do not announce a moral or seal the deed as heroic legend; let the incongruity between the remembered wonder and the smaller, unglamorous cost remain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route. Establish one specific fantastic premise inside a clearly situated Scandinavian institution—a municipal office, clinic, school, housing association, or workplace—and show it affecting routines, obligations, and assumptions rather than remaining a marvel apart from daily labor. Keep the regional setting particular through ordinary material life: a staff rota, a key register, a shared kitchen, a noticeboard, a maintenance request, a seasonal schedule. Do not make cold, snow, or isolation the required atmosphere.\n\nStructure the narration around one focal participant's working day or shift. The person moves through a small chain of ordinary acts—arriving, checking a list, receiving a caller, signing a form, taking a break, locking a room—and each act reveals a consequence of the fantastic premise. The premise should be treated as part of the job: a register that must be signed in a particular order, a room that is never empty at the same hour, a delivery that arrives before it is ordered, a rule that applies only to some residents. The characters mention it because it delays them, costs them, or must be worked around.\n\nKeep the narration plain and unsentimental. Let characters describe the fantastic element with the same matter-of-fact language they use for schedules, pay, repairs, and the weather. Avoid wonder-struck lyricism or demonizing reaction shots. Instead, show precisely how the premise changes a practical arrangement or exposes what people have learned to accept: who is excused, who absorbs the cost, which questions are never asked, and what small humiliation repeats itself.\n\nRender the focal character's inward reasoning, misgivings, and unspoken comparisons in visible or hidden interiority according to the active contract. Other characters do not read those thoughts. A job can be done while a private realization forms; a public conversation can remain routine while the reader learns what the focal character does not say. Make the gap between action and private judgment visible but not theatrical.\n\nLet the social meaning emerge through selected details rather than explanation. A supervisor may cite a plausible rule; a neighbor may treat harm as normal; a record may show an exception that was never mentioned. Do not write an essay or a monologue about the institution. The narration should make the inflicted cost specific and the unquestioned belief visible. The scene may close on a completed task, a quiet observation, or a pending request; do not require catharsis, reform, or symbolic reversal, and do not forbid the particular outcome the actual story supports.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Screenplay\n\nLet the fantastic premise enter through what the screen would show and hear without special-effect spectacle: an object on a desk, a procedure everyone follows, a queue's quiet accommodation, a posted notice, a familiar sound. Write concise action lines that record observable conditions—who is present, what is handled, where it moves—without camera commands. Use scene headings when the location or time changes, and let those headings mark the small institutional spaces where the routine repeats: the intake desk, the supply room, the corridor outside the locked office.\n\nEstablish the fantastic condition as a visible routine. Show the same action performed with the same small variation: a form is signed in a particular order, a key is returned to the wrong hook, a chair is moved before a particular person arrives, a door is closed at a particular hour. The characters treat the condition as part of the job. They mention it because it delays them, costs them, or must be worked around.\n\nKeep dialogue flat and occupied with practical business. Speakers ask about shifts, keys, deliveries, forms, or the broken thing that must be repaired. They reveal what they accept as normal through brief exchanges: a manager repeats a rule, a colleague jokes about an expense, a patient answers the same question again. Do not add explanatory speeches or thematic declarations.\n\nPlace the focal character's private judgment in a clearly attributed nonspoken note at the point where the visible scene would otherwise invite a reaction. The note gives reasoning, doubt, memory, or a withheld reply. It is not dialogue, voice-over, or knowledge available to the other characters. Do not convert it into a line the speaker says aloud unless a separate dramatic choice is authorized.\n\nUse offscreen sound only when the chosen sequence needs information the current location cannot show: a knock from the next room, a siren after a decision, a machine still running after the conversation ends. Do not use a cut as a shortcut away from an unresolved beat. A scene may end while a character continues a task, waits at a counter, or stands in a doorway. Let the fantastic element's real cost appear through the everyday arrangements the scene has already shown; do not require a closing image of accumulated damage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, and props that can be placed on a stage. Give each character a practical task—folding linen, moving a crate, checking a list, laying a table—so the dialogue moves around physical work instead of forming a debate.\n\nEstablish the fantastic premise as a repeatable stage action. Choose one ordinary routine that can be blocked and repeated with a small variation: a ledger that must be signed in a particular way, a basin that is never empty, a chair that cannot be moved, a bell that rings without a rope. Show the action once, then show it again later with the variation that reveals the cost: the second person is told to wait outside, the third fills the same form, the fourth sits where the first was refused. Let the characters treat the premise as part of the job. They may mention it only because it delays them or because someone is blamed for it.\n\nKeep the spoken lines unadorned and occupied with authority, money, time, and obligation. Characters ask who is allowed to sit, who pays for the repair, whose turn it is to watch the door. Their positions reveal themselves through what they accept as normal, not through announced feelings. A line may be short, a pause may follow a refusal, a prop may pass from hand to hand while words continue.\n\nPreserve the focal character's private thought as a clearly separated nonspoken note in the script, not as a performed aside or monologue. Use an aside only if a separate dramatic purpose makes it necessary—for example, a character who speaks to the audience while another remains unaware. Do not insert the required private note into dialogue or stage directions that the other actors could hear or see.\n\nLet the stage picture carry the cost through the repetition the scene has already established. The play may end without a resolution; the close should follow from the visible arrangement and the private note that has formed during the scene. Do not require a symbolic final image, a gathering of characters, or a new consequence that the blocking has not earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Stream of Consciousness\n\nSettle the narration inside one particular authorized mind and let its associations carry the scene. Move between present perception, remembered detail, bodily sensation, and an unspoken phrase as the character's attention shifts. Keep the syntax clean and restrained; do not pad the movement with decorative images or abstract commentary.\n\nIntroduce the fantastic premise through what that mind notices and already expects. The character may think of it only when it intersects with a practical need: the waiting room's second door will not open until someone speaks a particular name; the returned coat is dry before it reaches the hook; the same form must be filled out in a different order if it is Tuesday. The premise does not need to announce itself. Let it surface in a remembered rule, a twinge in the back, the position of a chair, or a phrase someone said earlier.\n\nUse interruption and return when the chosen scene needs them. A new voice, a telephone sound, a pain in the hand, or the arrival of another person breaks a memory and redirects attention. Later the same image or phrase returns with a small change, so the reader understands the mind has not left the central matter. Do not make the returns mechanical or exact; an altered detail shows the person has thought again.\n\nExpose the unquestioned belief through the gap between what the mind silently wants to say and what the person actually does. The thought \"they won't ask me twice\" may precede a signed form; \"it's always the youngest\" may accompany a chair being given to a superior; \"the exemption doesn't apply\" may sit behind a polite answer. Make the private words specific and plain, not a performed confession.\n\nKeep scene facts distinguishable. When the mind moves to an earlier day or a feared outcome, show the shift clearly enough that the reader can return to the present. If a memory is part of the current action—for example, a face at the door that was also at the desk—give that overlap with sentence-level clarity. The scene may close when the association reaches a natural pause, even if the visible situation has not changed; do not require a resting point, a resolution, or a return to the present if the mind's actual movement carries it elsewhere.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent the story through sender-attributed messages in readable sequence. Give each sender a consistent digital habit: one uses short replies and delays; another writes long paragraphs and immediately corrects a detail; a third sends only after work hours. Do not make everyone equally polished or equally anxious.\n\nIntroduce the fantastic premise through one interface or organizational fact that shapes the exchange. A message arrives from a sender whose profile says they are no longer on shift. A file attachment cannot be opened by anyone below a certain permission. A status shows \"read\" before the device was turned on. Someone is told to reply in the group chat, not directly; another is moved to a channel without explanation. The characters may treat these oddities as routine and handle them through practical requests.\n\nShow the small humiliation in the gap between what a person drafts and what they send. Include an unsent draft, visibly marked as unsent, with the wording the character considered. Then show the actual sent message as shorter or more obliging. Similarly, a delay before reply can matter when it indicates hesitation, but do not add timestamps for every message. Use an edit only when the change matters: a correction that softens a demand, removes a name, or changes \"no\" into a question.\n\nKeep private thought separate from transmitted text. When needed, use clearly separated context outside the transcript—not as a message—to show what the focal participant understands but does not send. Do not give every participant that inner access; use it for the person whose position the scene is exposing. Another character may misinterpret a message, but the reader should know what the sender actually meant.\n\nLet the exchange end at the stopping point the messages actually reach, such as a promise made, a task assigned, or a read receipt left unanswered. Do not force a cliffhanger. The fantastic element need not be explained in the chat; a later message may mention a consequence, or a day may pass with no reply from the person who was supposed to check.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the scene with concise setting cues that an illustrator could realize: a corridor, a kitchen counter, a bus shelter, a supply room. Introduce the fantastic premise as one persistent scene element—a second shadow on the wall, a notice that changes its list when no one watches, a kettle that fills from the wrong tap. Do not describe the impossible as a special effect; let the characters move around it as they would move around furniture.\n\nUse clear dialogue attribution and let the lines stay practical. Speakers ask for keys, complain about the shift, repeat a rule, or pass along a warning. Their acceptance of the arrangement shows through what they do not question: no one asks why the third drawer must stay locked, why the supervisor's name appears on a form no one saw, or why the returned item looks older than before.\n\nKeep focal narration in short passages that show the viewpoint's private response, not a running commentary on every line. A pause after a refusal, a remembered rule, a comparison with the previous day, or a restrained conclusion can appear as inner text. Another character does not hear it. Use expression or background cues only when they orient the moment: the same poster behind them, the same coatrack, the same doorway seen from another angle.\n\nDo not turn expressions into truth indicators. A character may smile while avoiding a task, or look calm while repeating a false assurance. Let contradictions between what is said and what has just been shown do the work. A detail from an earlier scene can reappear later with new meaning: a tray left on the wrong desk, a name added to a list, a chair moved away from the table.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene may end after an exchange or a moment of recognition without a resolution. The fantastic premise should remain a persistent condition of the setting, not a puzzle to be solved by the characters before the scene closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagapbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Lu Xun / Web Serial\n\nKeep the installment focused on one concrete fantastic condition and one ordinary setting where it continues to operate: a registry that records complaints before they are made, a staircase that shortens for some tenants, a delivery window that opens only for people whose names appear on a certain list. The condition should affect work, housing, care, or access, not remain a background curiosity.\n\nBegin each reply with just enough recovery to let a reader re-enter: one sentence naming the condition and the current location or task. Do not retell the previous chapter. Then advance the scene through a small chain of practical events—waiting, signing, carrying, reporting, receiving—that shows who is affected and how the cost is distributed.\n\nLet speakers reveal their assumptions through ordinary lines. A supervisor says the rule has always been like that. A tenant asks why no one else complains. A worker repeats a phrase the office asked them to memorize. Do not have anyone explain the social meaning. The reader should see the damage accumulate in the small details: a missed delivery, a seat given up twice, a form returned because the middle initial was wrong, a fee added after the waiver was promised.\n\nUse an occasional hook when the new information naturally raises a question: a name appears on a list that should not exist, a drawer is opened after hours, a message says the matter will be handled by someone who has never been present. Do not turn every ending into a cliffhanger. A reply may close on a completed task, a quiet observation, or a question left for the next day.\n\nAllow progress to come through knowledge, not only dramatic reversals. A character may learn that a different route is allowed, that a fee was waived once before, that a neighbor knows a shortcut through the system. Preserve the existing world's rules: if the setting has no magic users or no particular institution, do not invent them. The fantastic premise should remain one specific condition with cumulative consequences, not a system that grows new powers each installment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nMake the fantastic element the specific source of a present relational wound, not a backdrop that occasionally becomes relevant. Establish early what the impossibility costs the viewpoint character in this relationship: a shared power that now separates them, an obligation that makes one person dangerous to the other, a failed working that left someone injured, or a gift that became a debt. When the scene turns on that element, let the cost enter the exchange concretely—the person who needs help hesitates because asking would confirm what the power has already taken from them; the person who could help remembers the last time their intervention failed.\n\nStage the emotional turbulence through Kishimoto's legible action beats. Show the approach, the first exchange, the reversal, and the memory that changes what a character says or does next. Keep the memory brief and present-tense in its effect: one image, one sharp remembered line, or one physical echo that redirects the current movement. Let strain alter the actual conversation and subsequent behavior. A character may take a defensive step before speaking, reach out and then drop their hand, or answer a direct question with a practical instruction because the direct answer would expose too much.\n\nDo not require the hurt to resolve. Comfort may be partial, awkward, or rejected. A genuine advance may still leave the other person unable to accept it, and warmth can coexist with pain. When warmth appears, keep it true to the characters' personalities—some use brusque care, others silence, others a practical favor instead of an admission. End the beat with the strain still active even if some connection has shifted. Keep the narrator's distance and diction consistent with the active point of view, and let private thought and spoken dialogue diverge only for character-specific reasons such as pride, fear, or the wish to protect someone from further hurt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the reply as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs. Attribute every line of dialogue with a name, an action beat, or both before or after the speech. Keep descriptions concrete and scannable: where characters stand, what the fantastic element does in the moment, and how it affects their movement or choices. Show the fantasy element through a visible consequence—a ward flaring, a summoned creature shifting, a spell leaving a mark—rather than through an explanatory block. Let its effect be intelligible from the action itself.\n\nUse Kishimoto's clear emotional staging within this format. Build the relationship-driven conflict through a small but significant action: a rival refuses to step aside, a teammate acknowledges a past failure, a student hesitates before accepting help. Deliver memory in a single scannable beat, such as one italicized image or one sharp remembered line placed immediately before the action it changes. Do not let the memory expand into a flashback that interrupts the shared interval. If a character succeeds, ground the advance in a visible preparation or an emotional push already present in the scene.\n\nKeep the world and NPCs active as the beat concludes. Another character may continue their own task, ask a practical question, or move toward a separate concern. Leave a natural continuation that follows from the current action without forcing a cliffhanger, a mandatory choice, or a sudden stop. End where the next reply can pick up smoothly, with the fantasy element's immediate consequence still legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Chinese literary martial adventure route in the vernacular episodic mode. Open each passage with a clear sense of place and the relationships at stake: an apprentice, a rival, an old friend, a debt. Keep the narration close to the action, with dialogue distinct from the narrative voice and the vernacular register consistent throughout the passage. Let each episode advance a concrete movement—an approach, an exchange, a reversal, a departure—rather than summarizing events from a distance.\n\nApply Kishimoto's staging to this route. Break movement into legible beats: the approach, the first exchange, the reversal, the memory. Let remembered wounds change a present encounter, so that a character's style reveals their past. A fighter's hesitation at a particular stance can signal an old defeat without narrating the entire backstory. Keep relationship-driven conflict central; rivalry, loyalty, and mentorship should shape choices, not just décor. An advance should have a personal and practical basis, such as a technique practiced after a failure or a promise made to a teacher.\n\nIntegrate the fantasy premise through the world's ordinary customs. Show the impossible element as part of how this community lives: a beggar's incantation, a household altar, a soldier's practiced breathing, a charm tied to a child's wrist. Let the premise have intelligible consequences—affecting who dares travel, who is trusted, what children are taught—without pausing the episode for exposition. Use setting-appropriate terms for places, weapons, ranks, or rituals, but keep them legible through the action. End each beat where the episodic structure naturally pauses, with the next movement implied by the relationship or task, not by a forced cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful, concrete sentences and cut transitions that slow the scene. Name the worn detail: cracked leather, a notched blade, salt-stiffened cloth, the grit under a kneeling student. Give the fantasy element material weight—a spell costs blood or breath, a summoned creature carries the smell of iron, a ward flakes like old paint. Let that cost and the relationship conflict be the same event: a partner takes a hit meant for someone else, a rival refuses a cheap opening because of an old debt, a teacher blocks a strike at the last instant and pays for it in the body.\n\nKeep Kishimoto's emotional staging inside this compression. Use a memory—one image, one line, or one physical echo—to change the present move without pausing for reflection. The memory should arrive as a concrete bodily detail: the way a hand remembers a grip, the ache in a shoulder where an old wound healed wrong, the sound of a voice that once gave an order. Let it redirect the next action immediately. If a longer thought is needed for a character's reasoning, let it run, but ground it in the body and the material world.\n\nVary rhythm for impact. Short declarative sentences for sudden movement; a slightly longer clause for a decision's cost. Do not make the world only violent or grim; grime and tenderness can share a scene. Avoid turning the fantasy into survival horror. The advance should be personal and practical: someone tries again because they know the cost and choose to pay it. End the passage on a concrete image or action that lets the next beat continue naturally.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story through documents. Choose the form that fits each moment: a field report after an encounter, a letter to a rival, a temple registry, a training log, a requisition note. Each document must have a clear author, a specific intended recipient or purpose, and a moment of composition. What the document says, omits, or revises is limited by that author's knowledge and intention.\n\nUse the documents to stage Kishimoto's emotionally significant action. Let the document's composition itself be the action: a student writes to a master and hides the worst injury, then adds a postscript that gives the lie to the careful tone; a teammate's note to a healer contains an apology hidden inside a list of supplies; a rival's report omits a shared failure and the omission changes what the reader can ask next. Let a memory enter as a material trace—a scribe copies an old order and adds a marginal note about a comrade lost that day, a training record shows a technique's progression and then a sudden stop. The memory should change what the document says or does, not merely decorate it.\n\nUse the documents to make the fantasy premise's consequences legible. A report can describe a magical mishap in practical terms—how many hours the ritual took, what the wards did, who was hurt. A registry can show who was permitted to learn a technique and who was struck from the record. Conflicting accounts—one survivor's report against another's—can show rivalry or loyalty without an omniscient narrator. Preserve continuity so readers can follow events across entries, but do not append an explanation after each document. End a sequence on a material trace or an unanswered point the next document might address.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet the fantasy premise shape the comfort without reducing it to a set of conveniences. Show the impossible element as part of how these people live and care for one another: a healer checks a friend's old injury while they bicker about a card game, a magical household prepares a warm meal with a small spell that keeps the bread from burning, a lamp stays lit through the night because someone remembered to renew its charm. Let the premise have intelligible consequences—established difficulties remain part of the world, and comfort works around them, not against them.\n\nUse Kishimoto's clear emotional staging for warm moments. Show a practical kindness: a teammate leaves food outside a door, a master sits beside a student who cannot sleep, a rival wordlessly shares a blanket. Let affection be sincere but not overwhelmingly confessed. A character may show love by remembering how someone takes their tea or by fixing a broken strap. Keep personality intact: a gruff character can care by complaining, a quiet one by staying close. Allow manageable friction within the warmth—friends can disagree about whose turn it is to wash up, partners can rest in the same room without needing to talk, a student can refuse help and then accept it in a smaller form.\n\nDo not force emotional repair or a confession. The comfort sits in small pleasures—a shared meal, a familiar song, a task finished together. End on a moment of ease or a small, continuing pleasure, such as two people sitting in a warm room while rain hits the window, with no demand for a dramatic resolution. Let the established relationship conflict remain present but not dominant, so the warmth feels earned rather than imposed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed prose with deliberate transitions. Begin a scene with a broader frame: the season, the custom, the household's position, or the social meaning of a particular magical practice. Let the fantasy premise be part of that context—how a village regards a healer, how a guild records an unusual ability, how a teacher's reputation shapes a student's choices. Narration should differ from ordinary spoken language, with measured sentences and sustained paragraph movement.\n\nWithin that architecture, stage Kishimoto's clear, emotionally significant action. Let the sustained paragraphs carry the beats—the approach, the first exchange, the reversal, the memory—without diluting the movement. Show a fight, a lesson, or a parting through who moves, what changes, who is hurt, who hesitates. Use memories to alter a present encounter; frame them as the character's recollection, with enough distance to show how they understand what they recall. Let the memory interrupt the composed narration with a sharper, more immediate image, then return to the formal frame.\n\nUse social context or historical judgment where the chosen narrator permits. The narrator may note that a certain spell is both valuable and feared, or that a family's honor rests on a particular task. Keep the impossible consequences concrete: show the healer's exhaustion, the guild's ledger entry, the student's changed posture. Avoid turning the fantasy into an abstract allegory. Private thought and spoken dialogue may differ for supported reasons, and the narrator's access should follow the active contract. End a passage where the social and personal threads naturally pause, with the next movement implied but not forced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the outer frame with a listener who has already acted on a misread memory: the listener crossed a changed land by the wrong route, paid a cost twice, or turned away a companion whose departure the listener now needs to understand. Let the fantastic premise make that misreading urgent in a specific practical way—the wrong route has left them stranded, the doubled cost has emptied a resource, the absent companion held the only working method.\n\nHave the storyteller offer not a full explanation but a corrected version of the memory. The embedded tale is an active confrontation: the storyteller chooses to dwell on the moment the listener was not present for, to omit a detail the listener has repeated as fact, and to show through motion what the listener's version never contained. Stage the tale with Kishimoto clarity—block the remembered movement, explain the fantastic act in practical terms, and let the climax hinge on a bond or failure the listener has so far named incorrectly.\n\nMake one recurring object carry the correction. Use a broken seal: in the listener's version it was broken by betrayal, while the embedded tale shows the hands that broke it and the reason. Each time the seal appears—turned in the storyteller's fingers, drawn in ash, pressed into wax again—it forces the listener to compare the story's version with the memory that has been guiding their choices. Let the listener interrupt, question, or resist, and give those interruptions consequences: a challenge about timing makes the storyteller revise the order of events, a challenge about motive makes the storyteller reveal a cost the listener had not known.\n\nKeep the outer scene physically present without breaking the frame. Small actions—rolling the broken seal, repairing gear, feeding a fire—register doubt, hope, or growing belief. Do not make the tale a puzzle that hides a single magical fact; the fantastic elements may be familiar to one character and new to the other, and the storyteller uses them to explain an actual consequence, not to delay the important revelation. When the tale ends, return to the outer scene and show what the listener does with the changed understanding: a decision, a question, or a task begun. The ending need not be a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWork from the social and developmental realist route. Keep the fantastic element as an established condition of the world—a weather that rises from a known source, a substance with accepted uses, a guild with rules—and show how it presses on everyday choices. Do not isolate the impossible in a distant spectacle; let it affect the price of food, the length of a journey, or the way a child is taught to work.\n\nStage Kishimoto-style growth through one specific correction. The protagonist has been performing a technique incompletely by withholding part of the cost from the material: the substance is fed too little, the spoken condition is shortened, or the required pause is skipped to save time. The technique fails at the moment the withheld cost would have mattered. The correction comes from reading the rival's observable pattern—the rival feeds the material fully, speaks the whole condition, or waits the full pause—and from admitting the withheld cost to the rival in the scene. The admission is not a confession of weakness; it is the practical step that lets the protagonist perform the technique as the rival does.\n\nMake the breakthrough contingent on the actual scene. The opponent's rhythm, the terrain, and the state of the protagonist's hands all bear on whether the corrected technique works. A partial success is enough; the response can end with the protagonist holding the corrected form or making one complete attempt, not with a final victory.\n\nUse clean, unornamented narrative prose that follows the character's attention rather than commentary. Describe social structures through their effects—a debt collector who waits outside, a teacher who withholds a tool until the student can name what it is for—without turning each one into a moral lesson. Let syntax stay direct and concrete; do not imitate a particular translated word order or national temperament.\n\nIn a confrontation, let both sides act for comprehensible reasons. The rival may want the same resource, respect the same master, or refuse a kindness that feels like pity. The narration can show the internal cost of such a refusal, but it should not explain it into innocence. Finish the response at the point where the character has made a real change in approach, not where a final victory resolves all conflict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake the scene a close collision between an old authority and the people who live around its remains. The fantastic premise has a material residue: a room that no longer opens the way it once did, and a family obligation that gives one character access while leaving another outside. Ground the atmosphere in tactile details—rust on the gate, the smell of the cellar, the sound of a key that no longer fits—so that decay is physical, not merely decorative.\n\nUse Kishimoto's staging and relationship-driven conflict to turn that atmosphere into a confrontation. Both characters want the room: one has inherited the right to ask, the other has worked for it. Show the fight through clear movement, precise use of the environment, and a moment when a memory changes a present action. The remembered detail is concrete and tied to the same building—a promise made in that room, a gesture performed at that gate, a scent that belonged to the person who first showed the way in. The memory does not explain the past; it changes what the character is willing to do now.\n\nKeep private desire and social masks present without making them a puzzle. A character may act respectful in public and honest only with the other person in the scene. The narrative can reveal what that honesty costs: a loss of protection, a joke that fails, a hand withdrawn. Allow morally complicated intimacy to remain unresolved; the response can end with a character choosing to stay, leave, or continue a task, not with a completed redemption.\n\nDo not import vampire physiology, faction discipline, or a universal blood bond. The inherited power here is environmental and relational—the family obligation and the room's changed opening are the old authority. When the fantastic element produces a consequence, show its mechanism in the current world's terms. A fast reversal is not required; a small shift in position or understanding is enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route, rooted in the story's supplied community, language, and place. Treat the fantastic premise as one that already lives in collective practice: a river that answers only on certain days, a craft that requires a spoken verse, a land agreement remembered in two versions. Do not relocate the cast or impose a generic village, religion, or family structure.\n\nOpen with a transmission scene: someone recites a version of the shared story to listeners who have reason to correct it. The storyteller may be a relative, a rival, a teacher, or an outsider given temporary authority. Let interruptions matter. The central dispute is the order of a ritual: the teller's version places one act before another, and the listeners know that the wrong order wastes a resource—water, seed, or the season's first yield. When a listener challenges the order, the conflict becomes practical: the wrong version endangers a child, costs a harvest, or leaves a field dry.\n\nShow Kishimoto-style growth through a corrected memory used in action. The protagonist does not simply learn the true order; the protagonist must test it against present conditions. The gesture from the tale works only if performed with the material the current season actually supplies, and a rival who used the incomplete version fails at the same step. Make the breakthrough specific to the character's relationship with the teller: the protagonist asks the right question about a borrowed debt—a tool, a verse, or a portion of the yield that the teller once lent and the protagonist has not yet repaid. The question is not a moral reckoning; it is the practical step that lets the protagonist perform the corrected ritual.\n\nKeep the prose attentive to the social texture without romanticizing it. Describe the cost of memory—who feeds the storyteller, who repairs the path to the shrine, who cannot leave the field to listen. Allow the fantastic to remain consequential but not miraculous; its effects should follow from the world's rules. End when the corrected story has changed a present choice, not when a final revelation erases the community's disagreements.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the fantastic-tale route. The impossible belongs to the ordinary order of the world: a spirit may be a creditor, a technique may be a form of speech, a creature may keep a household's accounts. Do not treat the fantastic as a rupture to be explained; instead, let it set the terms of the immediate problem, and explain only what a character must do next.\n\nStage the encounter with Kishimoto's physical clarity. Give the space a firm shape—the gap between two rocks, the height of a gate, the reach of a rope—and move the characters through it in comprehensible beats. The conflict is a debt of service: one character owes the other a task, a portion of labor, or a spoken obligation, and the debt has come due under conditions neither expected. Let a memory enter at the moment of necessity: the protagonist recalls the exact movement, the exact words, or the exact failure that now applies.\n\nMake the breakthrough contingent on the present conditions. The remembered movement must be adapted because the original partner is absent: the ground is wet, the opponent is left-handed, or the partner who once completed the other half of the gesture is no longer there. Show the moment when the character applies the adapted memory rather than simply narrating a flashback. Allow failure to have a cost, but do not require a final victory; the response can end with the character holding a new position or making a partial move.\n\nKeep the prose concrete and economical. Do not insert a seasonal inventory, a compulsory quietness, or an explanation of Japanese aesthetics. The narrative distance should follow the scene: close enough to see the character's hands and the changes in the opponent's stance, distant enough to report a long wait without expanding it beyond its actual duration. Finish at the point where the encounter's immediate question has been answered or suspended, not at a thematic summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological route. Keep the narration close to one character's experience of an established fantastic condition: an object that must be fed. Let the world accept this condition without commentary, so that the fantastic appears in the character's planning, worry, and physical routine rather than as an interruption. The protagonist knows what the object eats, when it must be fed, and what happens if the feeding is missed; the narration shows this knowledge through small acts—checking the container, portioning the offering, listening for the object's response—not through explanation.\n\nPut the conflict in a relationship. Another person wants the same object, is exempt from the cost, or thinks the burden is a story the family uses to control its children. Show Kishimoto's relationship-driven beats: a conversation where each speaker's goal is clear, a small action that reveals a competing loyalty, a memory that makes the present choice harder. Do not make the other person a villain; let them act from a comprehensible need for freedom, recognition, or safety.\n\nStage any fight or practical test through concrete, readable movement. If the feeding must be performed, show what the protagonist actually does with hands, breath, and attention. The decisive element is personal and practical: the protagonist stops treating the burden as inevitable and asks the other person a direct question—what would you do with the object if the burden were lifted? The question is not a test or an accusation; it is the moment the protagonist lets the other person's answer change what the protagonist is willing to do next. The change need not resolve the relationship; it can simply alter what the protagonist is willing to do next.\n\nPut the narrative voice in the protagonist's language and community, but do not manufacture a generic Latin American temperament or add supernatural facts the world does not support. If the world's canon already allows the marvelous as ordinary, you may use it; otherwise keep the fantastic within the supplied premise. End when the character has made a real internal move, not when a closing image explains everything.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Light Novel\n\nEnter with the protagonist already inside a practical difficulty: a technique that won't work here. Use one or two concrete details to establish the fantasy condition—the technique requires a material the current ground lacks, or a spoken condition the protagonist cannot complete in this place—then move. Do not open with a history of the world or a description of the magic system.\n\nKeep spatial movement explicit. When the scene changes, state the physical transition—through a door, over a wall, across a courtyard—so the reader can follow the action. Break paragraphs around beats: a shift in speaker, a change in position, a new piece of information, a private reaction. Use Kishimoto's clear staging: show what each character is doing with hands and feet, make the stakes visible, and let a failed attempt teach something the next attempt can use.\n\nGive the protagonist a short internal reaction after each significant beat. The first reaction is a memory of the last time this trick failed: the specific place, the specific mistake, and what the protagonist did wrong. Later reactions should be equally specific—a calculation about the remaining time, a spike of anger at the person who taught the wrong version. The thought need not be witty or elaborate; it should make the protagonist's next action understandable. When the thought and the spoken line differ, let the difference come from a particular reason: the protagonist doesn't want to alarm a companion, or wants a rival to underestimate the risk.\n\nEnd at a natural pause in the immediate situation: after a decision, a partial success, a new problem, or a question asked directly. A cliffhanger is allowed but not required. Do not pad the response with a summary of what the protagonist learned. The fantasy element must remain an active condition—one that changes what can be attempted—not a decoration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Lyrical Prose\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as an established condition of this world, already woven into what people can do and how they live around it. Its consequences can show in institutions, labor, travel, treatment, law, trade, family arrangements, or the cost of a single day's work. Let wonder sit beside the practical without stopping to admire a separate marvel, and give unfamiliar effects intelligible pressure on choice and consequence without turning every mystery into an explained system.\n\nStage encounters with clear physical and temporal sequence: who is present, what each wants from the next few seconds, what distance, terrain, or barrier lies between them, and what changed in the last exchange. Let action and reaction follow from bodies, tools, established limits, and the world's impossible facts. Lyrical rhythm can tighten around effort, impact, breath, and hesitation, then broaden into aftermath or memory, but the reader can still track who moved, what it cost, and why the result follows. Translate visual storytelling into textual cues - stance, gesture, the sound before a response, a held silence, the moment someone chooses not to strike.\n\nDraw conflict from bonds, rivalry, obligation, and remembered wounds that the fantastic premise has made newly relevant. Let two people want incompatible things for reasons their history makes legible: a promise kept too long, a past failure, a debt, a shared technique, a place one cannot return to. Their choices come from what they know and misread about each other. A breakthrough, if it comes, can have accumulated personal and practical basis - a technique changed after failure, a limit understood through the body, a remembered promise that alters the next decision. No victory, test, confession, reversal, or breakthrough is owed to the scene.\n\nUse recurring images, sounds, and shifts of attention to carry memory into the present. A scar, tool, phrase, weather, or repeated gesture can return with altered weight because the character or the world's impossible fact has changed what it means. Let a comparison unfold only when it makes a present sensation more exact or changes what the viewpoint character understands. Ground images in traceable scene details and the viewpoint's associations; let plain sentences cut through when directness serves better than figuration. Lyrical does not mean piled adjectives, unvarying solemnity, or forcing every speaker into the narration's figurative register.\n\nKeep each character's spoken register distinct. A frightened person may speak in fragments; a practical person may name the problem plainly; a proud person may refuse the obvious word. The viewpoint's private thought should render actual nonspoken experience - sensation, uncertainty, reasoning, want, and the associations that connect present events to memory - without granting access to other minds. Other characters reveal themselves through what can be observed, heard, and inferred. If interiority is hidden by the active presentation, keep the private experience complete in its proper hidden form rather than reducing it to summary or moving it into planning.\n\nLet the fantastic premise retain some mystery. Do not add a new magic rule solely to solve the current difficulty, and do not make every consequence legible as a system. If an established limit matters, show its pressure on the body, the plan, the relationship, or the available time.\n\nEnd where the current speech, action, thought, or unfinished setup reaches its natural boundary. No completed test, victory, confession, or revelation is required. Lyrical attention may rest on a sensory detail, a remembered line, a practical task, or a relationship that remains unsettled; the story can continue from there.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as an established part of daily life rather than a puzzle to unveil. Decide which one condition the scene depends on—a spell that scars stone, a bond that carries sensation, a beast route that changes with weather, a technique that ages its user—and let its consequences appear in ordinary work, travel, family, institutions, and the body. Show what the condition makes easier, what it costs, and what routines have grown around it. Wonder can sit beside an errand, a meal, or fatigue; the scene only needs to give the premise intelligible consequences for the people in it, not explain the whole system.\n\nStage action with clear bodies, distances, tools, terrain, and sequence. Translate visual storytelling into textual cues: gesture, blocked movement, the angle of a stance, the space between speakers. When conflict becomes physical, show who moves first, what they protect, what the ground allows, and which opening appears. Let the conflict run through relationships: rivals, comrades, teachers, siblings, or old allies want different things from the same bond, and their disagreement shapes what they attempt. A remembered occasion should change the present encounter as pressure, recognition, or hesitation—an old failure alters how a character reads an opponent's stance, a promise changes which opening they take, a shared technique now feels different performed alone. Any advance must rest on personal and practical grounds: a skill learned earlier, a cost accepted, a trust tested, a tactic adjusted after observing the other person.\n\nCarry the melancholic reflection through small irreversible changes: the repaired strap, the name no longer used, the scorched stone, the friend who now stands a step farther away, the route that no longer passes the old shrine. Let memory and present sensation coexist. The same room can hold an old argument and current weather; the same technique can feel different in an older hand. Unfinished attachment may remain without becoming a lesson. Ordinary pleasure can stay pleasurable—a meal, a joke, a familiar route—without being canceled by what has been lost. Let a detail carry the association rather than have the narrator announce impermanence.\n\nMove between clear scene beats and private experience. Render nonspoken interiority as actual thought, sensation, uncertainty, or desire, not as a mood summary. Keep one character's private knowledge separate from what others can perceive. A recollection should enter the present with its own incomplete understanding; later knowledge does not rewrite what the character knew at the time. End where the scene's attention rests—on continuing action, a spoken exchange, an unfinished thought, or a private interval—without requiring an external task to pause, resolve, or produce a result. Recurrence of an image or phrase should mark changed meaning, not force a closing lesson about loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nBuild the story as a framed oral telling rooted in one specific Middle Eastern or diasporic community already active in the narrative—its actual neighborhood, work, language, family networks, and social obligations. Let the frame be an event with present stakes: a listener who needs to understand something, a shared task, a family occasion, a neighborhood gathering, a return after absence, or a reputation that must be answered. The teller should have a reason to speak now—correcting a rumor, honoring a bond, asking for help, passing on a practical warning, or claiming a place in a history that others remember differently—and that reason should shape what the teller includes, skips, repeats, and emphasizes. Render the telling in the teller’s own idiom: address, digressions, repetitions, judgments, local turns of phrase, and the social rhythms of the audience. Keep language and tradition distinct; do not blend Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Hebrew, Kurdish, or other traditions into a generic atmosphere, and do not manufacture a universal honor code, mysticism, antiquity, or religious requirement.\n\nInside the frame, make the fantastic premise part of ordinary life rather than a separate wonder. An impossible ability, object, condition, or inheritance should change what people can do at work, in travel, in family negotiations, in markets, in courts, in classrooms, in confrontations, or in the routines of a household. Show its practical consequences and limits without turning it into a fully explained system. The premise may create danger, opportunity, obligation, or grief, but it should remain intelligible through what characters attempt, avoid, trade, repair, or refuse.\n\nStage the action with clear spatial and sensory detail so attacks, escapes, training, confrontations, journeys, and negotiations are physically readable. Let relationship-driven conflict drive encounters: rivalry, loyalty, resentment, old injury, debt, the desire to be recognized, and the need to protect someone should make characters choose and act. When a breakthrough or advance occurs, give it a personal and practical basis—a remembered failure, a repaired alliance, a new use of the fantastic condition, a sacrifice already made, or a limit finally understood—never because the scene requires a victory. Use memory to change the present encounter: a recollection of a past wound, fight, journey, or promise should alter how the teller or a character reads the current moment, revealing a motive, a cost, or an overlooked consequence. Do not let the memory simply repeat the same suspicion or certify the same conclusion.\n\nLet private experience belong to the teller or focal character according to the active narration’s person, knowledge, and access. Actual nonspoken interiority—sensation, judgment, uncertainty, the association that brings a memory forward, the pressure between what is said and what is withheld—should appear in the raw fiction, not be replaced by a detached summary, performed aside, or voice-over. Other characters’ minds remain available only through speech, action, and the teller’s interpretation.\n\nSet lyric, reflective, or philosophical passages beside social, domestic, and urban realism. Food, money, work, weather, neighbors, rent, school, transit, gossip, and the small negotiations of a household can carry the fantastic premise’s weight. The telling need not resolve every mystery or complete every consequence; some questions may remain open, some advances partial, some listeners unconvinced. You may stop at any natural boundary the scene itself reaches—mid-conversation, during an ongoing action, inside an unfinished recollection, after a practical step, or while the telling continues—without requiring the task, dispute, or revelation to conclude. Do not force a tournament, a universal speech about determination, a royal court, a magical bargain, or a compulsory cliffhanger. Preserve the active authorship contract and whatever the established world already permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Minimalist Prose\n\nStage each scene through a few exact positions: who stands where, what lies within reach, which distance has just changed. Treat the fantastic as plain fact inside that staging — a lantern that drinks sound, a bone set crooked, a gate that opens only for someone already named — and show what it does to the people working, traveling, or fighting near it. One named thing and one intelligible consequence are enough; leave the system behind it unassembled, but keep any cost that will matter later perceptible now, in a body, a tool, a debt, or a place someone can no longer stand. Wonder can sit beside ordinary work without turning the scene into a demonstration.\n\nLet conflict run through a bond rather than around it. A rivalry, a promise, an unpaid debt, or an old failure shapes what each person does with a hand, a footing, a sentence, so that an exchange reads as a question and an answer at once. When a remembered wound bears on the present, bring it in as a single concrete image — the sound of a particular impact, the position of a scar, a room from years ago — and let it change what the current movement means before the action resumes.\n\nEarn advances on the page. A practiced motion finally landing, a fear already visible in the stance, a decision made under a cost the reader has watched accumulate: these carry a breakthrough. Keep the personal and practical basis for any gain specific to these people and this place, and never announce a surge from outside the scene or let a victory arrive because the moment wants one.\n\nWork in direct clauses and few exact details. Omit whatever a reader can infer from staging and consequence, but never the causal link that makes the next action legible. Where private experience genuinely matters — what a character will not say while acting, what they choose not to notice, the reasoning behind a cost they accept — render the actual thought or sensation rather than a summary of it, and let one precise sentence run long if that is what the moment requires. Keep each voice individual in rhythm and in what it refuses to name, and let the fantastic premise keep pressing on decisions, trades, and routines instead of waiting in the background for a set piece.\n\nA scene may end during continuing speech, motion, thought, or an unfinished arrangement; no resolution beat is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nBegin scenes inside an ongoing activity, and keep the point of view close to the character's immediate attention: what they see, hear, physically feel, and think while events unfold. When action arrives, make it legible in ordinary novel prose—establish who is where through the viewpoint's noticing, let blows, dodges, and uses of ability register in bodies, breath, balance, and the room around them, and let the meaning of the exchange emerge through what is said, withheld, or privately understood between movements. Let rivalry, camaraderie, resentment, and care show through tactical choices, timing, and what each person protects. Do not pause for a play-by-play summary; let the reader track the fight from the same selective attention that tracks a conversation or a task.\n\nLet the fantastic belong to the world rather than a separate display. Established abilities, impossible creatures, or uncanny rules enter perception the way weather or traffic does: as conditions that shape choices, work, family, travel, and institutions. Show their consequences in habit and procedure, and let a mystery remain unexplained if the people living with it still act and decide intelligibly. A concrete cue—a name, a scar, a stance, a tool—can open a memory that changes how the character reads the present encounter; return to the present without recapping, and let the recollection alter emphasis rather than replace the scene.\n\nWhen an advance matters, ground it in something the character has practiced, misunderstood, or already failed at, and in what they want for a specific person. Let the breakthrough come from that personal and practical basis rather than from the scene's need for a victory. Give it cost—breath, injury, time, exposure—and follow it into ordinary aftermath: altered routines, changed relations, new obligations. Keep transitions and paragraphing guided by where attention moves; use the narrator's voice and the immediate scene to set rhythm. No contemporary setting, muted feeling, realistic physics, or closing lesson is required. A response may end while action continues, a question hangs, or a task remains unfinished; do not manufacture a victory, revelation, or settled lesson to close.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nWrite the relationship as the story's engine without turning it into a separate romance plot. Choose scenes because they test, deepen, or complicate the specific bond already established—training, a shared mission, a dangerous journey, a quiet repair, a public argument, a private ritual—and let the fantastic premise decide what that bond can actually do. If one character can heal, vanish, command, transform, or endure what the other cannot, show how that asymmetry shapes promises, jealousy, protection, debt, secrecy, and the practical work of staying together. The impossible element should change what intimacy costs and what loyalty requires, not merely decorate a conversation.\n\nKeep the action clear enough that the bond is visible in choices. Stage who stands where, what each person notices, what a technique or limit costs, what the environment permits, and what one character chooses to do when the other is exposed. Let rivalry and cooperation share the same physical vocabulary: a guard, a rescue, a refusal to retreat, a blow pulled at the last instant. When an advance comes—an opening in a fight, a new trust, a shared tactic, a forgiveness—ground it in something already established between them: a remembered failure, a habit learned from the other, an old wound that changes how the present encounter is read. The breakthrough should belong to these people and this world, not arrive because the scene needs a victory.\n\nBuild emotional legibility through private language and recurrence. A nickname, a phrase, a gesture, a place, or an object established early can return later with its meaning shifted by what has happened. Rivalry can harden into trust; comfort can arrive in words once used to mock; a promise can survive as a practical habit after its original feeling has changed. Let the two remember the same event differently when that difference matters, and let the discrepancy drive conflict rather than supply a convenient misunderstanding. A callback should land as recognition, not as a required confession or a fixed emotional beat.\n\nGive nonspoken interiority its real place. Render what the viewpoint character notices, remembers, wants, fears, and reasons through in the current presentation, and keep that private experience within what the viewpoint can know. Another character may infer, miss, or misinterpret it from behavior, speech, and the world's available evidence. Do not replace interiority with a letter, a report, a monologue, or a retrospective summary that says what the character felt. When private experience is hidden from display, keep the actual experience there rather than a vague note about it.\n\nLet affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery take the space the relationship actually promises, and let some scenes stay outwardly ordinary: two people traveling, eating, training, or working while the real negotiation happens in what they do not say. The fantastic premise can make a small kindness impossible, dangerous, or costly, and that consequence should matter to the bond. Do not graft on a roster of familiar tropes, a borrowed cast, or a confession owed on schedule. End where the current beat actually leaves the relationship—mid-action, mid-thought, mid-conversation, or in a silence that has not yet resolved—without requiring a victory, a reconciliation, a betrayal, or a cliffhanger to make the scene complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell a fantasy story as an oral chronicle that remembers deeds and their consequences. Let the impossible be part of ordinary life: a craft depends on a material that should not exist, a boundary shifts and forces a longer route, an oath binds because everyone has seen what breaking it brings. Show these consequences through arrangement and work rather than through a general tour of wonders. Keep the scale intimate when the story is intimate; a household, a village feud, or one journey can carry the fantastic premise as fully as a kingdom. Give unfamiliar things intelligible effects without turning every mystery into an explained system.\n\nShape the telling as a retelling. Open with a deliberate declaration that places a person, a rivalry, an oath, or a past act in memory, then let the present action test or revise that account. Use compact declarations and recurring phrasing when a later moment gives the phrase a new application. The repetition should come from what the community keeps saying, not from an ornamental epithet attached to every entrance. What will be remembered matters: an act enters the chronicle because of its consequence, not because the scene needs a triumph. Let oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds organize the account when the established world supports them, and keep the particular epic tradition coherent instead of mixing every convention.\n\nStage action with clear spatial and bodily particulars. Show distance, posture, movement, the instant a stance changes, and what an onlooker can see. Translate visual storytelling into textual form through readable cause and effect: a technique or fantastic capacity has an immediate, witnessable result without requiring a systematic explanation. Relationship-driven conflict gives the action its stakes. Rivals, teammates, siblings, teachers, or neighbors act from bonds that have history, and a remembered wound, rescue, betrayal, or shared labor can surface during current action and change how a character reads an opponent, ally, or debt. Let that memory include the fantastic premise: what someone did with an impossible capacity, what it cost, who witnessed it, and why the community tells the deed one way while a participant recalls another detail.\n\nAn advance in the action must have a personal and practical basis already present—a lesson applied, a sacrifice made, a memory accepted, a tool used as taught, a debt understood at last. Do not grant success because the chronicle needs a victory; let the attempt fail, succeed partially, or change the terms of the conflict according to what the characters and world can sustain. Keep private experience inside the public cadence. The chronicle may know a person by reputation, but the focal character's actual nonspoken interiority—sensation, uncertainty, memory, reasoning—belongs in the scene as it happens. Distinguish shared remembrance from private knowledge.\n\nEnd when the current telling, action, or thought reaches its natural boundary. The account can stop during continuing speech, mid-action, in the middle of a private recollection, or with an unfinished setup; it need not round off every deed before the response closes. Another act can remain unremembered, another answer unspoken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in composed scenic prose with a Scandinavian domestic-folktale method: the established fantastic premise is treated as an inherited local condition, like a bargain kept by a particular household, a seasonal obligation with a known consequence, or a wonder that has become part of ordinary work. Do not explain it as a full system. Let its terms appear through what people do, refuse, repair, or expect, so the reader learns the condition by watching its practical effect on family, neighbors, or a workplace.\n\nStage each scene clearly enough that the reader can follow who can act, what they can reach, and why a movement matters. Use the Kishimoto profile's emotionally significant action by grounding confrontation in a specific shared history: a broken promise, a rivalry over something both value, or a memory that changes what one person is willing to risk now. When a memory alters the present, render it as a concrete recollection arriving at the moment of decision, not as an abstract summary of the past.\n\nKeep the narration socially attentive rather than mythic in tone. Show how the fantastic condition shapes ordinary obligations: a household task becomes easier or more dangerous, a neighbor's help is practical rather than reverent, a debt is measured in work or trust. Let the point-of-view character's private reasoning and feeling remain unavailable to others unless spoken or performed. When fear, affection, or shame makes someone act before speaking, show the delay in physical behavior and in what the character later admits or withholds.\n\nRepeat the folktale condition's concrete signs—a threshold, a name, an object, a seasonal task—only when their recurrence earns new meaning from the scene's history. Keep Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context distinct when the story supplies it, and do not default to snow, isolation, or bleakness. An advance should arise from a personal and practical basis visible in the scene, such as a remembered failure becoming a usable technique or an old resentment turning into a choice, rather than from sudden power or determination alone.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Screenplay\n\nWrite in script format with scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines, and clear speaker cues. Use action lines to stage emotionally significant movement: a pause before a name is spoken, a hand withdrawing from an object, a step taken after a remembered failure. Let the reader see what each character can physically do and why the movement matters, without camera commands or internal-mechanics explanation.\n\nMake the established fantastic premise enter the scene through visible or audible consequences: changed light, a physical transformation, a sound that disturbs the room, an object moving, a surface altered. Show its cost and allowance through what characters attempt or refuse in response. If a character is familiar with the phenomenon, let their behavior show that familiarity—calm, avoidance, or practical use—rather than prompting exposition.\n\nUse dialogue to expose relationship history through current stakes. A line should carry a shared grudge, an old promise, or a present risk without summarizing the past. Let a reaction to a line carry the emotional weight: a silence, a glance, a change in tone, or a deliberate action. When a memory changes the present, render it as a brief visual flashback only if the sequence needs that image; otherwise show its effect through a pause, a gesture, or a shift in what the character is willing to say.\n\nKeep private thought outside cast knowledge. If required interiority cannot be shown through action or dialogue, include it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, marked so the reader knows it is not dialogue or information available to the other characters. Use cuts and offscreen sound only when the chosen sequence needs them: a cut to move location or time, offscreen sound to signal an event not yet seen. Advance the scene through the next visible or audible event rather than narrator summaries.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Stage Play\n\nBuild each scene around speaker-led dialogue and physically performable actions. Use entrances, exits, blocking, objects, and pauses to carry relationship history: who enters already carrying a grudge, who lingers near an exit, who reaches for an object that matters. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene, and keep each speaker distinct through habit, affection, resentment, or practical concern rather than generic banter.\n\nMake the established fantastic premise appear as a stageable consequence. Show it through a visible change in a prop, a light in the room, a sound from offstage, or a character's uncomplicated reaction to what has happened. If the wonder is familiar in this world, let the characters respond with the calm or alarm appropriate to their knowledge, not with exposition. Do not describe the phenomenon's internal mechanics in a narrator's aside; let the audience infer what it costs and allows from the characters' behavior.\n\nLet memories change a present encounter through concrete stage business. A character might pause at an entrance that recalls a previous departure, refuse an object that was significant in the past, or repeat a gesture with a new meaning. The audience learns the memory only when the character's action or line makes it legible. Stage directions may indicate an internal moment without a monologue. Use a monologue or aside only when the dramatic situation actually calls for one character to address the audience or speak alone, and give it a specific purpose in the scene rather than using it to summarize emotion.\n\nPreserve required private experience as clearly marked nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, not as something another character can hear. Keep the cast active and practically engaged: the scene moves forward through exchanges and physical tasks, and a relationship conflict changes what the characters are willing to do or say rather than pausing the world. End when the stage action has reached a clear moment, not when every emotional thread has been tidied.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the scene as the continuous mental movement of the authorized point-of-view character. Braid present perception with memory, unspoken words, fragments of an argument, a repeated name, and the physical sensation of the established fantastic feature working. Move by association, repetition, and sensory pull rather than logical summary. Keep enough orientation markers so the reader can tell whether the mind is now recalling a past event, imagining a possible action, or responding to the present room. Do not switch viewpoints; another character's mind stays unavailable unless the story explicitly permits access.\n\nLet the fantastic premise enter the mind through concrete bodily and practical experience. A warm draft where there should be none, the taste of metal, an object's wrong weight, a familiar room with one impossible detail: these belong to the stream because the body notices them. If the character has grown used to the phenomenon, the mind may treat it as routine alongside a complaint about work or a half-remembered obligation. Let the impossible consequence change what the person is about to do, but do not make every thought resolve into a complete plan.\n\nUse memory and association to make relationship conflict immediate. A smell can pull back a conversation from years ago; a rival's repeated phrase can interrupt the current argument; an injury can surface as a physical impression before the person names the emotion. The advance, when it comes, should emerge from the mind's own material: a remembered failure becomes a possible technique, an old resentment shifts into a decision, a loved one's habit appears as a warning. Render the private words of the mind as the character's actual inner speech, raw and unpolished, but distinct from a sent message or a spoken line. Keep the character's ignorance and uncertainty intact, and do not let the prose become a polished essay about the self.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent the scene as a readable exchange of messages with clear sender labels. Include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that actually matter to the story. Let each character's digital habits reveal personality and relationship: one person writes in fragments and sends quickly, another composes long blocks then deletes them, a third ignores a message while replying to someone else. Use those habits to expose shared history and present stakes rather than to decorate the transcript.\n\nLet the established fantastic premise affect the exchange through what people report seeing, what they ask others to check, or what they avoid saying. Do not require the transcript to literally contain a magical effect. Show the wonder's practical consequence in the requests and reports: someone asks whether the light is still wrong, another describes an object that moved, a third refuses to confirm what they saw. Let the characters' familiarity or alarm shape how they type about it.\n\nDistinguish sent messages from unsent drafts and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft can show what a character was about to say before hesitation, fear, or habit stopped them. A sent message is committed to the conversation and available to its recipients. When private interiority is required, add it as an unambiguously marked nonspoken passage adjacent to the transcript, stating whose mind it belongs to, and do not let that passage leak into what the character actually types.\n\nLet relationship history shape the digital exchange through behavioral details: a long silence after a particular phrase can signal an old wound, a quick correction after a risked admission can show fear, a changed typing pattern can mark the moment when a memory changes the person's willingness to continue. Use those details rather than a narrator explaining the relationship. Keep the transcript forward-moving: each message should advance the practical situation, push a conflict, or reveal something about the sender. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext; a read receipt or edit is used only when the human on the other side would actually notice or care. End the excerpt when the exchange reaches a meaningful point, without forcing a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in concise, scene-oriented blocks. Each scene begins with enough visual setting to orient the reader—where the characters are, what light or objects matter—then follows with attributed dialogue and short narrative passages that give the focal character's reactions. Use descriptions that could be illustrated: a change in the air, a crack widening, an object floating where it should not, a character's expression after a blow. Let the scene-setting and focal narration carry relationship history as well as physical detail, so the reader sees what the characters are to each other in the space they occupy.\n\nMake the established fantastic premise visible or physically felt in the moment, not merely described as a rumor or a system. Its consequence belongs to the immediate scene, affecting what someone tries to do or refuses to do. If a character is familiar with the wonder, show that familiarity through their practical response rather than through explanation.\n\nLet dialogue carry relationship history and conflict. Keep lines short enough for speech bubbles but give each speaker a distinct habit of thought and expression. Between lines, use focal narration or inner response to show what the character feels or remembers—not as a summary of personality, but as a present experience. A memory can appear as a vivid image triggered by a word, a place, or a face; when it changes the character's next action, show the shift in the dialogue or the physical description that follows. Do not make every inner response a witty aside.\n\nUse background or expression cues only when they orient the moment or mark a change, such as 'the window darkens' or 'her face has gone very still,' not as labels on every line. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands; those are separate features requiring their own authorization. Advance through earned developments: a question answered, a truth acknowledged, a task begun or stopped. End a scene when the present beat is complete, not when every thread has been resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaqbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Masashi Kishimoto / Web Serial\n\nWrite installment prose with a clear through-line. At the start of each reply, briefly recover only the particular detail the current scene needs—the task underway, the relationship that has changed, or the consequence of the established fantastic feature that remains relevant—without retelling earlier installments. Keep the reader oriented through simple present action rather than a summary paragraph.\n\nLet the impossible element affect practical life and relationships in accumulating ways. The same wonder that saved someone last week may now complicate a family argument or create a debt. Show its consequence in what characters can and cannot do, and let their understanding of it grow through local developments rather than worldbuilding exposition. A memory or earlier detail should resurface only when it changes the present: a rival mentions the same failure during a new confrontation, a character repeats a technique that used to fail but now works because of a specific earlier change.\n\nUse relationship-driven conflict to keep installments moving. Dialogue and action should expose the gaps between what characters want and what they are willing to say. Private interiority remains available where the story needs it, but each installment should carry the conflict forward through visible choices or new information. Earn each breakthrough through a personal and practical basis visible in the installment, not through sudden power or determination alone.\n\nProgress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, resources, or the cost of a decision. Use occasional hooks that arise naturally from a question, a discovered problem, or a changed plan; do not force a cliffhanger at every stop. End when the current development is clear, leaving available threads for the next installment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaraa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the impossible premise as something already present in the characters' ordinary life, with practical but intimate consequences. Let the fantastic fact sit inside a room, a shared chore, a debt, or a half-finished understanding rather than arriving as an external event. Whatever concrete image or rule the scene supplies, keep it specific and limited: the writer shows what the character notices, what they avoid saying, and what they cannot stop thinking about, not the whole system behind it.\n\nMake admission costly in both directions. When a character admits need, disappointment, guilt, or hope, let the other character react from their own history and current attention, not from a single emotional register. The conversation may move between practical talk and exposed feeling; the strange detail may make ordinary words carry an extra weight. Allow partial comfort, awkward repair, or an apology that does not fix the deeper hurt. Do not require confession or resolution.\n\nKeep the focal character's private experience actual and accessible while separating it from what other characters know. Use Gaiman's direct telling: clear sentences, familiar activity around the fantastic, and a sense that the encounter carries another story behind it. Do not inflate anger or pain into operatic gestures; keep the turbulence intimate. A broken promise, a changed body, a bargain already paid, or an impossible return may be the concrete kernel. The story may end without a repaired relationship, but the fantasy element should leave a trace in behavior, memory, or a later ordinary act that the characters will understand.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the cooperative scene as a shared interval in which the established fantastic premise is active. Use Gaiman's clarity: name the concrete elements of the impossible situation, keep the language direct, and let one strange detail imply a larger story without demanding that the whole system be explained. Do not pause the scene to deliver background; work the fantastic fact into what characters say, notice, or need.\n\nAttribute speech unmistakably. Use dialogue tags or equivalent clear markers so each speaker is identifiable without rereading. Keep paragraphs readable and focused: one paragraph may hold a small action, a reaction, and a few lines of exchange. Do not let narration swallow the conversation.\n\nLet the fantastic fact shape the exchange itself. A character may ask for something the impossible rule makes possible, refuse a request because of its cost, or notice a familiar object behaving wrongly and speak around it. The scene should show the fact in use or in the way it changes what the characters can safely say, not merely mention it. Keep the active narrator's person and knowledge permissions; if the point of view is limited, other characters' private thoughts remain unavailable.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point chosen from the active role and scene boundary. The reply may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, changed plan, or completed test before ending. Let ongoing world and NPC behavior remain active; the next exchange can pick up the same moment without a forced prompt. Do not add menu-like choices or executable commands. Preserve the active authorship contract and player control.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route: tell the fantasy in linked scenes that each carry one concrete development, with clear transitions and a recurring motif or phrase that ties the episodes together without turning them into a frame device. Choose the language, period, and community actually supplied by the story; do not invent a generic Chinese setting or transplant a dynasty or location.\n\nWithin each episode, keep Gaiman's direct narrative voice. Let the fantastic premise appear through a familiar activity: a household obligation, a market exchange, a journey, a family conversation. Attend to the strange fact's effect on that ordinary situation. Whatever the scene supplies—a bargain, a transformed object, a hidden path, an impossible return—show its consequence in the episode's practical business rather than pausing to explain it. Let that consequence unfold across episodes, not all at once.\n\nPreserve the distinction between what the characters observe and what they infer. An old story or local saying may guide their interpretation, but it must be tested against the episode's events. When a recurring detail or phrase returns, use it to show what has changed since the last episode: a character may now recognize the sign, misunderstand it, or act on it differently. Repetition is not proof. Do not require a concluding moral or social repair. The story may end when the current episode reaches a meaningful pause, leaving the larger consequences still active.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the fantasy fact as a material condition. Show worn thresholds, altered tools, scarred bodies, scarce resources, or the physical trace of an impossible rule. Use concrete nouns and active verbs; compress transitions so each paragraph carries the action forward without summary padding. Do not explain the whole fantastic system; let a visible consequence stand for the larger rule. A door that always opens, water that runs uphill, a coin that returns to an empty hand, or the same wound reopening each dawn can carry the premise.\n\nKeep Gaiman's direct telling and sense that the impossible sits near ordinary life. Ground the rough or abrasive speech in its speaker: show the calluses, the habit, the job, or the history that makes the talk hard. Let the point of view notice practical details before large meanings. When the character reasons, keep the thought as long as the decision needs; do not break every consideration into fragments.\n\nUse rhythm deliberately. Short sentences can land a reversal or a cost; a longer sentence can carry the pressure of waiting, distance, or exhaustion. Physical stakes must affect behavior: hunger, pain, cold, debt, or broken equipment change what the character chooses. Do not turn every danger into survival horror. The fantasy element may remain unexplained, but its practical effects must be intelligible. Preserve the active authorship contract and avoid making the cast uniformly cynical or combative.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the story through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a letter, ledger entry, report, marginal note, map annotation, inventory, or another written artifact. Keep the authorial voice of each document consistent with its creator's position, knowledge, and motive for writing. What a document knows or conceals follows that situation; the reader may infer more from gaps, revisions, or conflicting accounts, but the document should not become omniscient.\n\nUse Gaiman's narrative clarity and intimate resonance within the documents. A character may record a strange fact as ordinary practice, mention a bargain or a path in passing, or leave a note that reveals another story behind it. Let the fantastic premise appear piecemeal: one document records a consequence, another asks about it, another denies it. The impossible fact need not be fully explained, but its practical effects should be legible across entries. Do not add an explanatory passage after every record; let the sequence carry the understanding.\n\nPreserve the time of composition. A document cannot know what happens after it is written. When a later entry corrects or reinterprets an earlier one, make the change visible through causal evidence, not through retrospective omniscience. Use specific forms rather than a fixed rotation. A single ledger page, a torn letter, and a terse report may carry the story as well as a run of journal entries. The fantasy element should remain anchored in the world's ordinary documentary practices, not become a paste-up of supernatural exposition.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaraf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the fantasy premise as part of a comfortable ordinary world. Let a small impossible detail accompany a familiar pleasure: tea that never cools, a window that opens onto the right weather, a charm that finds lost keys, a garden that remembers its planters. Keep the narration direct and warm, with enough concrete Gaiman-like specificity to make the strange feel homely. Do not explain the whole system; let the detail be enjoyed and occasionally used in practical care.\n\nSpend attention on what particular people like and need. Show chosen kindness: one character remembers another's preference, handles a task before being asked, shares a quiet comfort, or says something affectionate without making it a confession. Keep personality intact; disagreement, privacy, and established problems may remain, but they do not have to interrupt the warmth. The fantasy element can make care easier or add a layer of shared wonder, but it should not remove all friction or turn the scene into a lesson.\n\nUse clear dialogue and simple narrative movement. Let the scene settle into a moment of ease without requiring a big emotional revelation or a completed arc. The response may end while the characters are still enjoying the small pleasure or beginning a low-stakes activity. Do not force a cliffhanger or a change of mood. The impossible fact should stay active in the characters' awareness, but its main role here is to deepen the sense of shared comfort and the particularness of the setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the fantasy premise as a fact embedded in a community's history and routines. Use composed prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraphs that move from a specific present moment to the social context or past that shapes it. Let the narrator frame the scene through public memory, local judgment, or family history, but only where the chosen point of view permits. Do not force an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary; the formality comes from the movement and distance of the prose, not from period decoration.\n\nKeep Gaiman's clear storytelling. The impossible fact should be concrete: a road that returns travelers to their departure, a house that forgets its rooms, a debt that passes to the next generation, or a bargain remembered in a place-name. Trace its consequences in ordinary work, marriage arrangements, inheritance, or communal ritual. Let one character's encounter carry the other story behind it without explaining the whole mythic system. The narration may compare the present event to an older tale, but the comparison should illuminate, not certify.\n\nUse social framing to organize the scene. A conversation may be measured against what the neighbors would say; a decision may be understood through the character's position in a household or trade. Keep private thought distinct from public knowledge. The style allows longer sentences and subordinate clauses, but keep the narrative purpose clear. Do not let formal prose drown the fantasy fact in abstraction. End when the scene has made its social significance legible; no confession, verdict, or complete interpretation is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nAnchor the fantasy in an ordinary pressing situation: a visitor must be persuaded, a child kept awake, a debt explained, a local danger named before it arrives. Choose one actual listener and give that listener reason to demand, half-believe, or resist the story. The teller may be a parent, courier, neighbor, prisoner, or stranger; the tale is spoken for someone in the room.\n\nIntroduce the impossible through the voice of the telling. When a story names a road that walked away, a bargain struck in a kitchen, a star that answered a question, or a festival held only during a famine, ground the wonder in sensory detail and shared activity: mending a net, waiting for bread, counting change, walking the same road twice. Let the listener respond while the story is still inside the teller. The response can be skepticism, fear, recognition, or a practical demand for the next part, but it should fold back into the outer scene.\n\nMake one repeated element travel between levels. A phrase about the fourth stair, a coin kept unspent, an instruction to never hand back water, or a question the listener remembers from another day can appear first in the frame, then in the tale, then in the listener's action after the telling. Each recurrence slightly shifts what the element means; it does not need to be explained or made true. The listener may say the phrase while locking the door, or set the coin near a lamp, and the narration may then show what the outer world does next without declaring whether the tale caused it.\n\nKeep the tale's consequence visible after the telling. The listener may alter a plan, examine an object, repeat a name to another person, or fail to sleep. The teller may notice that the story has said more than intended, or learn from the listener's reaction that one buried detail was closer to the truth than expected. Do not resolve every nested event before the outer story moves; let the telling remain an intervention whose full effect is still gathering.\n\nUse the frame itself to make the fantastic feel near. A story told beside a cooling stove, through a closed door, on a bus seat, or in the last hour before an errand carries its marvel into the listener's everyday world. Familiar Gaimanesque texture belongs not in a separate mythology but in names that still have weight, trades that still need protection, and bargains that ordinary people half remember. Names, places, and bargains acquire consequence because someone in the room is willing to believe them for just long enough to act.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic-uncanny route: write the fantastic as an interruption in a world made of visible work, weather, money, and familiar obligations. Start each scene with one precise social or physical fact: a window left open over a vegetable stall, the number of buttons on a work coat, the sound of a tram through an empty hallway, the weight of a key someone no longer owns. Then allow the impossible to enter through that detail, not through a gate of general atmosphere.\n\nLet the uncanny arrive by structural disturbance rather than violent spectacle. A room can be too quiet in a way that has a cause; a map may show a street the town has never built; a child can recall a festival no neighbor admits attending. Show how the disturbance alters practical action: someone stops at a door they never noticed, postpones a payment, rewrites a list, or walks a longer route without deciding to. The narration may observe these changes from a moderate distance, using the selected German-language tradition's composed syntax and careful sentence rhythm without mimicking translated word order.\n\nKeep Gaiman's intimate storytelling alive through named relationships and spoken bargains. The fantastic fact belongs to particular people: a baker who promised his first batch to a stranger, a daughter who keeps her mother's unchosen name, a clerk who has measured a hallway three times and obtained three different results. Show the encounter carrying another story behind it. The point of view may remain close to one person's reasoning while the style retains the route's observational clarity.\n\nTreat the uncanny as legible in memory and consequence. A character can remember what should have happened and compare it with what did. Later scenes may show the difference in ordinary terms: a bridge no longer appears on the transport map, a shop has changed ownership without a sale, a letter is found already opened on the same morning it was posted. Do not force every mystery into an explanation; instead let each manifestation make further practical effects intelligible.\n\nLet the imagination preserve the boundary between documentary precision and strange occurrence. Weather, coinage, transit schedules, and family records may remain exact while the impossible fact changes one of them. A list of purchases can include an item that was never unpacked; a timetable can gain a train that stops at a station without tracks. The narration should not become bureaucratic, satirical, or clinical. It reports the changed world the way the selected tradition reports a remembered landscape: with specific nouns, steady sentences, and enough moral attention to let the reader feel why the disruption matters.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaraj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake the fantasy premise live in the pressure between an old promise or inherited attachment and the present street, job, lease, or relationship. Choose one definite old authority: a family name with a debt attached, a building whose upper floor was sealed by an ancestor's bargain, a procession that still expects its route to be kept clear, a mirror that remembers a completed trade. Show that authority affecting ordinary transactions: a landlord refuses to receive a payment until a particular phrase is said, an office worker discovers a forgotten clause in a personnel file, a locksmith recognizes a mark on a modern key.\n\nBuild the urban material detail around access and need. Water, electricity, rent, transport, and the places where people sleep should be described with the concrete specificity of the Gothic-Punk tradition. Let the fantastic fact alter one of those systems rather than float beside them. A lift may stop one floor below the sealed apartment; a bill may arrive with an extra charge for a room that no one has opened; a bus route may deviate after dark for reasons the driver knows but will not say. The narration can use Gaimanesque ease: names, paths, and remembered tales gain weight because someone must act on them before the end of the day.\n\nLet morally complicated intimacy provide the story's emotional center. People may protect, desire, distrust, or care for someone connected to the old authority without knowing everything that person carries. A social mask can fall away in trusted company: one character may speak plainly about what the bargain costs, while another admits why they still honor it. The supernatural need not be proven to every character; it changes decisions even when only one person believes it. Show the private cost in sleep, appetite, or the careful way someone avoids a particular traffic light.\n\nMake the old authority and present life collide through specific actions rather than general mood. A dying relative can hand over an object that must be returned; a maintenance worker can discover an instruction written inside a panel; a hired car can arrive with a destination already entered. Each event should appear possible because the fantasy premise has already established that names, bargains, or places carry consequence. Do not import vampire factions, powers, blood bonds, or a global supernatural institution; keep the old authority local, inherited, and imperfectly remembered.\n\nUse private desire as a source of both danger and cooperation. A character may want the sealed room opened because a person or memory is inside, not merely because it is forbidden. Another may refuse to open it because the price would fall on someone else. The narration can move between public routine and private reaction without making anyone universally witty or sinister. The fantasy remains personal horror when a consequence is finally paid in changed relationships, broken routines, or a revelation the character hoped to outlive.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral and frame-tale route. Place the telling inside a specific occasion: an elder asked to settle a household dispute, a neighbor telling a story while a fever falls, a railway platform where strangers agree to pass an hour, a wedding kitchen where no one wants to name the bad luck already spoken. Choose a language, region, and community only when the story supplies them, and let local names, foods, work, or festival practices appear because the characters actually live there, not as scenic decoration.\n\nMake the framed story a practical intervention. The teller may use the tale to delay a sale, warn a daughter, test a visitor's patience, or explain why a particular object must not be given away. The listeners should respond in ways that change the outer action: someone agrees to wait until morning, returns a borrowed vessel, repeats a name with new hesitation, or asks the one question that the teller had hoped to avoid. Keep the narrative levels clear: show who is speaking, to whom, and what part of the tale is still unfinished.\n\nIntroduce the fantastic through the story within the story, but make it apply to the present. A tale about a well that returns lost coins on the condition that no one counts them can sit beside an actual dispute over a missing payment. A story about a road that will not let a traveler pass if he has broken an unspoken promise can become relevant to the older listener's journey the next morning. The wonder must be encountered in everyday actions: fetching water, sending money, waiting for a bus, storing rice, stitching a hem.\n\nUse repetition across the frame. A phrase, gesture, number, or object may occur first in the teller's opening, then inside the tale, then in a listener's response after the story ends. Each recurrence should sharpen rather than explain the meaning. One character may count coins for the first time since hearing the tale; another may leave a lamp burning past midnight without announcing why. The narration may show the result in the ordinary world without confirming that the story's rule is true.\n\nGive the frame-tale structure a social and emotional weight. The telling has consequences even before the fantastic fact is tested: a daughter realizes her father is asking for something he cannot say directly; a neighbor understands that the story is an apology; a traveler recognizes the teller as someone from an older village tale. The style should remain direct and Gaimanesque: names, bargains, paths, and remembered tales accumulate significance through the characters' choices. End a segment when the outer situation has shifted enough to matter, whether or not the tale itself has been completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaral::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tale route. Introduce the impossible as a known kind of story being realized in a particular place: a spirit who collects forgotten names, a stair that appears only during a certain festival season, a shop that buys one day of a customer's memory, a rain that repeats until an owed favor is acknowledged. Let the narrator relate the encounter with the clear, plain directness of someone telling what actually happened, without explaining every rule or forcing the event into an elaborate system.\n\nAnchor each manifestation in seasonal attention and specific daily activity. Mark the weather, the shifting light on a hallway, the smell of rain on pavement, the weight of a coat as the season changes, or the particular work that becomes harder after the encounter. Use omission strategically: the narration may leave out the moment of the impossible itself and show its aftermath, or include only the teller's partial view. Later scenes may return to the same spot in another season and find the encounter still active in the characters' practical choices.\n\nGive the fantasy consequence through private and social obligation. A promise made to the shrine's caretaker may keep a student from leaving the neighborhood; a name forgotten in childhood may prevent a woman from signing a work contract; a favor owed to a roadside stranger may make a driver reroute a delivery. The fantastic element need not be visible to every character; it affects those who know or half-know the story. Show how one person's obligation becomes a constraint on someone else, producing both care and difficulty.\n\nUse repetition and verbal economy to make the tale feel retold. A phrase, seasonal marker, or object can recur: the first autumn wind, a ticket stub, the sound of a bell in a different street, the number of coins a passenger still has. Do not repeat mechanically or make the narration epigrammatic. Let the tale's structure come from what is omitted between scenes; a character who would not speak of the encounter may later set an extra bowl on a household altar, leave the curtains open, or walk a longer path without giving her reason. The narration may show these actions and let them signify.\n\nKeep the voice intimate and Gaimanesque without importing a fixed narrator or national personality. A parent, neighbor, student, courier, or shop assistant may tell the story; the fantasy remains near ordinary life even when it is wild. Names, bargains, paths, and remembered tales can be meaningful because the selected route makes them part of a world where such things are still told. Do not add supernatural facts the world excludes; instead make the fantastic tale's occurrence illuminate the particular characters' choices and the cost of honoring or forgetting an obligation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaram::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the marvelous treated as ordinary route. Treat the fantastic premise as something the neighborhood, family, or town already accepts: a woman who can hear the dead speak through the pipes, a market that opens only during a particular month and sells one year of a person's luck, a house that adds a room when a marriage is in danger. From the first sentence, let characters respond to this fact the way they respond to a bad bus line, a good mango season, or an unreliable uncle. Wonder and practical life sit in the same paragraph: someone bargains for a blessing before checking her change; a man rewires a lamp beside a cupboard where a saint's name is still chalked; children know not to count the chairs on the last Friday of the month.\n\nKeep the language exact and local when the story supplies a place or community. Names, foods, family arrangements, feast days, money, work, and the way people joke or argue should be concrete. Do not manufacture a generic regional atmosphere or decorate the scene with invented folklore. If the narration is in English, retain the cadence of the characters' speech rather than mimicking stereotyped word order. Let the selected tradition shape how the marvelous is treated: as a communal fact with practical rules rather than a private surrealism or clinical case.\n\nShow the fantastic premise changing economics, memory, and social ties. A woman may inherit the obligation to keep a particular door open because everyone on the street knows what walks through it on the first rain. A shopkeeper may accept payment in promises because the till will not open for an unkept pledge. A family may keep a room empty, not out of grief, but because the mirror in that room shows the last visitor's reflection for one more day. No one explains the mechanism at length; the story's energy goes into how the characters meet their obligations or break them.\n\nUse Gaiman's intimate narrative ease to keep the marvelous from becoming decorative. The point of view can remain close to one person who is trying to keep the family business afloat, get a child to school, or find a missing relative. The impossibility may be dependable, like weather at a season's turn, or costly, like a debt that must be repaid to a particular person on a particular night. When a bargain is struck, show who will bear the cost and why the bargain still makes sense to the one striking it. The narration can treat the fantastic as the source of hope, constraint, humor, or grief without having to argue for its existence.\n\nLet the history of the place make the marvelous legible. A house may remember an old political violence; a street may still observe a custom begun after a flood; a family's blessing may carry the memory of an ancestor's bargain with the land. The narration need not explain every historical cause; instead it shows how the present characters inherit both the wonder and the obligation. Do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them—the premise must already belong to the story. Where the canon allows it, the ordinary treatment should make the impossible feel as rooted as a bus fare, a recipe, or a name that must be spoken correctly.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaran::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Light Novel\n\nStart the scene in the middle of an ordinary action that the fantasy fact will disturb. Show the location and situation in the first three or four lines without a general worldbuilding paragraph: a courier checking a delivery address, a student cleaning a classroom after a festival, a shop assistant restocking a shelf. Then let the impossible part of the scene appear as a concrete detail: the same address is written twice in different hands, the classroom window shows a season that ended last month, the shelf has an extra item with a label that changes whenever the assistant looks away.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When the narration moves from the street to the stairwell, from the kitchen to the locked storeroom, or from the station platform to the train, use a short direction or sensory transition: the sound of the door closing, the change in light under the awning, the cold metal of the railing. Paragraph breaks should mark a shift in speaker or place. Use dialogue to carry plot-essential facts, but let the narration supply the private response: not just what the character says, but what he or she notices, distrusts, hopes, or decides not to say.\n\nPresent the fantasy mechanic clearly the first time it becomes actionable. If a bargain requires giving back a key, saying a name, or opening a particular drawer, show the rule in a way the reader can follow: the character remembers what happened when someone broke it, or the shopkeeper states the condition while bagging the purchase. Do not hide the mechanic inside a metaphor and then depend on it three scenes later. The rule can be strange, but its immediate effect on the character's choices should be legible. Let names and bargains accumulate consequence across chapters without requiring a full mythology: a forgotten street name from the previous scene can become the address on the mistaken parcel; a phrase told in passing can be the phrase the locked drawer will not open without.\n\nUse reaction beats to keep the story moving without forcing comedy or banter. A character can freeze, laugh, check the time, debate whether to call someone, or simply try again while the fantasy fact remains solid. Reactions belong to the particular person: a practical courier may test the box again before admitting the impossible; a cautious student may photograph the window and then delete the picture without knowing why. The narration can make room for extended thought, but keep the external sequence going through a small action, a second attempt, or a shift in where the character is standing. Do not make every difficulty yield a one-line joke or a sudden loyal ally.\n\nGive the scene a private interval that connects the wonder to ordinary life. After the fantastic event, the character can sit on a bus, wait for a phone call, wash a cup, or stand outside a closed shop and think about what the event means. This is the place for Gaimanesque intimacy: the strange is near, and the story behind it is not yet fully revealed. The interval need not end with a discovery or a cliffhanger; it can end with the character choosing to go forward, to keep the object, to change the route, or merely to accept that the ordinary day has changed. Keep the prose fast, specific, and free of lengthy explanatory paragraphs. The fantasy should feel like an encounter just happened, and the next scene can be entered directly from the character's changed attention.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the fantastic as a sustained rhythm that gathers consequence from the ordinary. Let the story move through familiar work, errands, meals, or small negotiations, and let the impossible enter through a detail already present in that routine: a door that opens where there was a wall, a name that must be repeated a certain way, a bargain that changes the light in a particular room. Establish the premise clearly and then let its effects accumulate across the scene. A practical task can become strange without ceasing to be practical; a wonder can be handled like an everyday object and still alter what the narrator notices afterward.\n\nBuild images from the scene's own materials rather than importing ornamental comparisons. If the current moment includes rain, a locked drawer, a market, or a journey, let the image grow from that thing and return to it when the narrator's understanding shifts. A comparison may unfold in three movements: first it describes what is visible, then it connects what the narrator knew before, and then it makes the connection usable for the next action or decision. Plain statements should interrupt this movement so that rhythm does not become decoration. The model is the story's narrator, not a cataloguer of the fantastic; even a single striking image must belong to this character's attention and this world's rules.\n\nKeep the telling close to the source's narrative ease. Names, bargains, paths, and remembered tales may carry a previous story behind them, but only where the scenario supports it. The lyricism should not make every speaker sound like the narrator or turn moments of danger into gentle meditation. When an established fantastic consequence must be shown, show it through perception, choice, or institutional effect, not through explanation. End where the attention naturally rests: the task may be concluded or interrupted, a question may remain unanswered, and the next step may simply be visible rather than announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the fantastic event arrive as something already marked by time. Use the narrator's memory to place the present encounter alongside an earlier moment, a person no longer present, or a version of a place that has changed. The reflection may be direct: the narrator can say what this reminds them of and what is no longer available. Or the reflection may attach to a single detail—a cup, a coat, a market stall—so that the object carries the association without explanation. Do not make every such detail sad; let an ordinary pleasure remain pleasurable, and let its later absence be specific rather than universal.\n\nGround the reflection in the fantasy premise's actual consequences. If a bargain, a path, a remembered tale, or a strange institution has changed what people can do, show how that change has already altered an attachment, a daily habit, or a familiar place. The impossible should not merely decorate a memory; it should be the reason the memory sits differently now. The telling remains direct and close to familiar activity, in the source profile's manner: a character may recall a story while doing a practical task, and the recollection may change how the task is performed.\n\nLet the scene end in the coexistence of present experience and what is not available. When the narrator returns to the present detail that triggered the reflection, register how its weight has shifted: the cup is still warm, the road is still passable, the name is still spoken, but the earlier association now sits alongside the present one. A request can remain unfinished, a person can be absent without being mourned at full length, and a return can be partial. What matters is not a closing statement about impermanence but the narrator's renewed attention to what is left. The reflective tone may hold wonder and loss together without equating them, and without requiring the narrator to perform grief.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-fantasy-05",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Fantasy · 05/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 5 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPagaraq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantasy as a framed oral telling. Keep one narrator positioned in a concrete situation—a family gathering, a journey, a workshop, a shared meal—and let that narrator tell the impossible event because it has entered ordinary life. The frame need not be elaborate. A listener may ask why a door is never locked, or a traveler may explain how a bargain was struck; the narrator's account then supplies the remembered episode. Establish the teller's present need or occasion before the embedded account begins: the listener wants to understand a debt, a custom, a warning, or a familiar object's return. Use the source profile's direct telling: names, places, bargains, and small obligations acquire consequence through repetition and detail rather than explanation. The listener and the teller may respond during the story, but the narrative remains the teller's account, not a dramatic performance.\n\nKeep the regional route specific and non-decorative. This is not an imitation of a collection of tales; it does not require genies, hidden treasure, sultans, or caravans. The present world's culture, language, and daily work come from the supplied setting. If the fantasy premise is a talking animal, a changed coin, a garden that grows at night, or a promise that follows the family, show its consequence in how people plan, divide work, remember debt, or keep a custom. The regional style's framed storytelling allows the narrator to admit incomplete knowledge: a thing may be known because it was told by a grandmother, a neighbor, or a traveler, and its effects may remain partly unexplained. Keep that hearsay legible without turning it into mystification.\n\nLet the frame return at the end. The narrator's present situation can reveal why the story matters now: a debt has come due, a child has asked the same question, or a familiar object has reappeared. This return does not require a moral. It lets the listener's current need and the fantasy's earlier consequence meet in one scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the fantasy through selective detail. Choose two or three concrete particulars per scene—a lock, a coin, a road, a chair—and make them do the work of the premise. The impossible becomes legible because ordinary activity changes: a shop opens at the wrong hour, a name cannot be written, a journey covers more ground than the map allows. State the change directly and then move to its consequence. Do not explain how the magic works. If a character must understand it, let them understand it through one of the chosen details.\n\nKeep sentences short and clauses direct. A precise longer sentence is allowed when a consequence has a necessary sequence, but avoid rhetorical expansion. Interior connection should be given only where it is materially needed for the present action, decision, or changed perception. The source profile's sense of another story behind an encounter should be handled by absence: the narrator may mention a previous bargain or a half-remembered instruction without retelling it. The reader infers what is omitted; the model does not supply it as an explanatory paragraph. Make the omission specific enough that the reader can infer what was left out—a condition, a warning, a debt—rather than a vague mystery.\n\nLet dialogue carry the same economy. Characters speak in their own cadence, not in minimalist formulas, but they do not offer lore dumps. A single spoken line can establish an obligation or a rule, and the next beat shows whether it was followed. End when the present fact is established. The fantasy's consequences may continue beyond the scene; the scene may stop in mid-task, but the chosen details should be enough for the reader to know what has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaras::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the fantasy as a sustained scene. Begin with a character doing something in the ordinary world—working, walking, eating, arguing, waiting—and let the fantastic premise enter through that scene's own occasion. The narration may move between action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience as the moment requires. Use paragraph breaks and transitions to keep the reader oriented: a shift from speech to thought should be visible, not abrupt; a shift from present action to remembered incident should return with a clear signal, such as a present detail that re-anchors the scene after the memory. No recap machinery is needed; establish what happened earlier only when it matters to this scene.\n\nKeep the source profile's narrative ease. The voice is direct, competent, and aware that a strange encounter may carry another story behind it. The impossible has intelligible consequences: a promise, a boundary, a changed object, or a bargain affects what people can do next. Do not treat these consequences as hidden magic requiring explanation. The narration follows the characters as they adjust, resist, or misunderstand the new fact. Inner experience is available to the selected perspective but not to other characters.\n\nLet the scene end where its present business closes or pauses. A task may be interrupted, a new fact may be learned, or a character may simply decide to continue later. The ending should not supply a lesson or a summary of the fantasy's meaning. It may leave the next step visible: the character knows what they will try, or the reader knows what awaits them. This keeps the wonder close to ordinary life without redefining it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the fantasy premise become visible through the relationship's established terms. Use the actual premise from the user's setup—a bond, a shared task, a debt, a favorite place, an impossibility the pair has learned to accommodate—and select scenes where that premise affects their interaction. Do not import a generic roster of forced proximity, misunderstanding, or enemies-to-lovers. If a callback exists, it is a moment the two characters have already shared in this story or one the user established as background; the telling may return to it because one of them now sees it differently.\n\nWrite private language as the relationship knows it. A nickname, a half-sentence, a repeated gesture, or a shared interpretation of an event can carry more than a long declaration. Let affection, comfort, jealousy, rivalry, or discovery have room, but keep the cast's behavior character-specific. One person may offer practical help while the other guards a feeling; both may misread a familiar phrase. The narration may enter one focal character's private thought, but other minds stay outside its access according to the active authorship contract. The story should show the relationship as alive and consequential, not as a collection of tags.\n\nGive the fantasy consequence in the same terms. If the premise grants a power, changes a place, or imposes a rule, show how it alters what one of them dares to ask, what the other is willing to risk, or what they can no longer assume. This fulfillment can be quiet—a return to a phrase, a shared meal, a decision to stay—rather than a dramatic confession. End on a beat that the relationship's own rhythm would produce: a reply kept for later, a hand withdrawn, a plan made, or the realization that the other already knew.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the fantasy as an account that has already begun to be repeated. Use a deliberate opening phrase when the scene warrants it: 'It was said...' or 'There was a time when...' can establish that an event has entered the community's memory. But keep the telling close to the familiar activity the author profile favors. The deed at the center may be a promise kept, a boundary restored, a journey completed, or a strange obligation honored; its consequence matters because neighbors, kin, or the teller refer to it later. A recurring phrase should arise from an established act or way of speaking—a name given after the deed, a condition repeated when the bargain is tested—rather than an ornamental epithet attached to every entry.\n\nLet names, oaths, gifts, or public acknowledgments carry the fantasy's intelligible effect. If a character has entered a bargain with a river, a house, or a traveler who is not what they seem, the saga shows what that bargain costs and what it permits in daily terms. The narration may say that people afterward remembered how a door was left open, or how a particular word was never spoken again. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective: distinguish what the teller witnessed from what was reported by kin, neighbors, or travelers, and let the account admit where the retelling is partial or disputed. Keep the source tradition coherent: no imported pantheon, no requirement of heroic doom, no ceremonial dialogue from every speaker. Humor can surface when a practical concern interrupts an elevated memory, but ordinary work is not mock combat.\n\nLet the account end with what will be remembered rather than a conclusive moral. A task may be completed or deferred; the community's retelling may be partial or disputed. The point is that the fantasy's consequence has become a story the world will carry forward, not that every incident must be elevated into legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite continuous prose from a viewpoint situated in ordinary domestic and social life. Use the Scandinavian route as an inward, socially observant realism touched by folktale or myth: the story attends to a household, a workplace, a neighborhood, or a local institution, and the fantastic premise becomes part of that shared life rather than a private vision. Keep the particular conditions of the actual setting distinct—a Danish apartment block, a Norwegian coastal community, a Swedish workplace, or another named place—and let its weather, work, and social tone arise from what is actually given.\n\nEstablish one fantastic premise early and make it consequential through practical experience. Choose the premise from what the scenario already supplies: a local custom, a remembered bargain, a place with its own terms, or an object whose history changes how people work and live. Then show its effect in the texture of the day. Someone pays attention to a doorway at dusk, adjusts travel plans, accepts help from a person the town half-remembers, or notices that a familiar rule has changed. Let the narration move between outward observation and inward reflection: a character may think about a neighbor, a family obligation, a changing town, or a small practical task while the strangeness sits in the same room.\n\nTell it with Gaiman's clarity and layered significance. Use direct sentences and familiar activity, and let an encounter carry the sense of another story behind it. Names, bargains, paths, and remembered tales may acquire consequence where the scenario supports them, but do not add gods, ghosts, or childhood protagonists automatically. The fantastic element should feel near rather than remote—something a person can walk to, argue about, cook around, or explain to a child.\n\nKeep private thought available as ordinary narrative interiority under the active contract. Other characters do not know a focal conclusion merely because the reader does. Let developments arise from specific actions and relationships. The scene may end after a completed errand, a changed understanding, or an unfinished conversation; it does not need a mythic-scale obligation, a revelation, or a supernatural confrontation to justify the turn. The style produces intimate, socially grounded fantasy rather than high fantasy or allegory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaraw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene as a readable screenplay. Use concise action blocks, clear speaker cues, and a new scene heading when location or time changes. Let the fantastic premise be filmable: a visible object, an audible change, a repeated action, or a physical consequence. Show what a person does differently because the impossible is real—locking a door, leaving food, choosing one path, looking at a shadow too long.\n\nKeep Gaiman's clarity in the action lines. In each action block, select one visible or audible particular and show its local effect through behavior or reaction: a girl sets a saucer by the back step and waits before closing the door; a man traces a name on frosted glass and then wipes it away; a bus stops where no stop is marked and one passenger gets on without checking the time. Let familiar activity carry the strangeness. Dialogue is plain and purposeful; characters may speak about work, plans, children, or the terms of a bargain without explaining the entire mythos. If a line carries a second meaning, the scene shows the pause or reaction that earns it.\n\nUse a voice-over or offscreen sound only when the chosen sequence needs it. Required private prose can remain a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation; it is not automatically dialogue and not available to the cast. Otherwise keep thought implicit in choice and behavior. A cut may carry the viewer from an ordinary room to the same room changed, from a question to the thing that answers it, or from a small act to its consequence.\n\nDo not fill the page with camera commands. If a close-up matters, say what the audience needs to notice and why it changes the moment. End the scene on a visible action, a completed exchange, or the arrival of a person or event; a cut can imply the rest. The fantasy remains intimate and specific rather than spectacular, and the style does not create branching choices, menus, or interactive elements.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene as a playable script. Use clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that an actor and director can stage. Let the fantastic premise become stageable fact. Choose one particular the audience can see or hear—a door that was not there, a bowl left in a certain place, a light that changes when a name is said, a character who arrives without a sound—and let the characters' observable behavior establish its consequence: checking a lock, moving a chair, avoiding a threshold, keeping a promise.\n\nKeep the dialogue direct and intimate, in the manner of Gaiman's clear storytelling. Characters may talk about ordinary work, family, neighborhood, or the terms of a bargain without explaining the whole mythology. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A pause, a repeated gesture, or a prop can be more telling than a speech. If a monologue or aside is used, give it a dramatic purpose: it must address the audience or another character in a way the scene supports, not replace every private thought.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, not as dialogue another character can hear. A character's fear, suspicion, or hope can be shown through what they do or fail to do on stage. Stage directions establish observable conditions; they do not secretly narrate a mind.\n\nLet the scene work in a continuous space. No film-camera instructions. An entrance or exit may open or close the action, and the play can end on a completed exchange, a physical action, or a held moment. The fantasy should feel near and specific, as if it could happen in a kitchen or a waiting room, rather than requiring elaborate spectacle. The style is a stage play, not a novel excerpt or a screenplay.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaray::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the scene as the movement of a particular authorized mind. Begin with an immediate perception or bodily state, then let memory, private words, or a recurring concern branch from it, and return to the present scene through a sensory detail. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and the concern that will not leave. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined. The voice is intimate and direct: a person thinking while cooking, walking, waiting, or trying to sleep.\n\nLet one fantastic premise live inside that movement. It need not be announced; it can surface through a repeated image, a remembered name, a phrase someone once said, a small ritual the character performs without thinking. Show how the impossible changes ordinary perception and decision: the mind checks the window at the same hour, avoids a certain street, argues with itself about a promise, remembers the exact feel of a door. Use Gaiman's clarity even within the associative flow—each leap stays graspable, and the strangeness remains near rather than decorative.\n\nDo not let every thought become a puzzle or every memory a revelation. The character may think about shopping, a shirt, a song, and then return to the thing that will not leave. Private words and bodily sensation belong to that mind alone; other characters do not hear them. A recurring phrase can mark a concern without becoming a refrain in every paragraph.\n\nEnd where the mind's movement pauses: on a decision, an interruption, a physical action, or the unresolved return of the concern. No thought-length ratio, no compulsory wit, no fixed emotional outcome. The stream is a method, not license to make all scene facts indeterminate. Keep the fantasy consequential and the mind particular.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaraz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nTell the story through a readable exchange. Open with a sent message that establishes the immediate practical situation, then let each reply respond to what was actually transmitted. Use sender-attributed messages, with timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution—typing style, formality, timing, emoji, forgotten words, unsent drafts. Distinguish clearly between what was sent, what was typed but not sent, and what a character thought privately without transmitting it.\n\nLet one fantastic premise move through the messages. It may appear as a photo that will not send, a location pin in a place no one knows, a repeated phrase from different senders, a message delivered at the wrong time, or a name that cannot be deleted. Show how the impossible changes decisions and everyday digital life: someone mutes a chat, screenshots a warning, asks a practical question about a bargain, or tries to explain a thing they cannot photograph. Keep Gaiman's directness in the voice of each message; no one needs to sound like a mythologist.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. Such context can describe a room, an object, or a character's private thought, but it must not be mistaken for a transmitted message. Use an unsent draft or separated context only when it changes what the next sender can know or do. Do not fabricate an interface event solely to add subtext; if a delay matters, it must affect the exchange's meaning or the characters' choices.\n\nEnd on a sent message, a deliberate silence, a channel change, or a draft written and not sent. The story may continue in the next exchange; no cliffhanger, revelation, or supernatural confrontation is required. The fantasy should feel intimate and practical, as if the impossible had begun to scroll through an ordinary day.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the scene as an illustrated moment. Use concise scene-setting: where the characters are, what the reader can see, what has just changed. Attribute dialogue clearly and let the focal character respond—in speech, in action, or in a brief narrative thought. Keep the rhythm suited to an image: a detail becomes visible, a line is said, the focal character understands something small or refuses to.\n\nLet one fantastic premise enter through the visible and the felt. Choose one visible particular the scene can show—a door in the wall, a bowl on the step, a reflection looking the wrong way, a person everyone pretends not to see—then pair it with the focal character's felt response or practical adjustment so the image and the consequence arrive together. Show how the impossible affects ordinary life and choice: someone changes their route, covers a mirror, keeps a promise, returns a borrowed thing. Background and expression cues orient the moment; they are not truth indicators. A frown may mean tiredness, not guilt.\n\nUse Gaiman's clarity and intimate mythic resonance. The narration is direct, the dialogue familiar, and the strangeness sits close to everyday work, family, travel, or discovery. A name, a bargain, a path, or a remembered story can acquire consequence where the scenario supports it, but do not import gods, ghosts, or childhood protagonists automatically. Keep private thought available to the reader, not to the other characters.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene may end on a spoken line, a completed action, or a new visual detail; it does not need a choice screen. If the story continues, let the next scene follow from what has been seen and said, not from an artificial prompt. The presentation is a visual novel in rhythm and attention, not an interactive game script.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagarbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Neil Gaiman / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as part of an ongoing story. Maintain a readable through-line: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter—a name, a place, a promise, or a consequence—without retelling the whole history. Avoid turning every reply into a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger; an installment may end on a completed task, a new question, or a character simply continuing their ordinary business.\n\nLet the fantastic premise develop cumulatively. When an earlier particular returns, show the new fact or action that has changed its meaning, and briefly recover only the specific earlier detail the reader needs to recognize the change. One episode may show a locked door; a later episode shows a key, a note left behind, a change in the town's rules, or a person acting on what they now know. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. Use Gaiman's clarity and intimate mythic resonance: direct sentences, familiar activity, and the sense that the strange sits close to daily life. A bargain, a path, a remembered tale, or a name can acquire consequence across installments where the scenario supports it.\n\nKeep the narration oriented to what the active viewpoint can know and feel. Other characters do not share the reader's knowledge of earlier installments unless something in the story has given it to them. A revelation does not need to pay off immediately; the serial can let a fact wait while ordinary life continues. But when a payoff arrives, it should be earned by what has actually happened, not imposed to create drama.\n\nThe style does not automatically create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy rules. The fantasy should remain intimate and socially grounded, with each installment advancing a specific situation. The serial voice may be relaxed and direct, as if telling a story in parts to a reader who has been away and is glad to come back.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet the narrator's first-person account treat the fantasy fact as a practical condition that makes emotional exposure costly. The magical element should create the specific pressure in the scene: a ward the narrator cannot afford to renew before someone they love arrives, a transformation that makes a familiar room feel like a place where an apology would land differently, a summoned creature whose presence forces the narrator to name what they want from a person who is already leaving.\n\nStay inside the narrator's noticing and reasoning. They may scrub a pan while thinking about the failed ward, or watch the other person's hands near a spelled lock and understand that the real question is not the lock. Let the narrator over-explain, hedge, qualify a comparison, or withdraw mid-sentence because the feeling is difficult to hold, not because the prose needs tidying. The admission should emerge from the practical moment: needing someone, having screwed up, being afraid of a magical thing, or wanting to be believed.\n\nWhen the narrator does speak, show what the utterance costs in the actual encounter. The other character responds to the specific words and the specific magical circumstance, not to an abstract emotion. They may answer the practical question and leave the emotional one untouched, or accept help while refusing the apology, or offer a comfort that does not fit the narrator's fear. Warmth can coexist with pain; it need not repair the hurt or lead to acceptance.\n\nGround the emotional register in the narrator's own phrasings and the fantasy world's material details. A broken charm can stand for a broken promise without being announced as a symbol; a cold hearth can make a withheld confession harder to say. Avoid a stock vocabulary of suffering and avoid compulsory patterns: a confession does not guarantee relief, an apology need not be accepted, a pause need not conceal guilt. Let the magical element create the separation or intensity, but keep the struggle as the narrator's actual experience.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the collaborative interval as the manuscript narrator's close first-person account of a fantasy circumstance that is still in motion. Open with the narrator's practical attention to the immediate fantastic detail: the lingering warmth of a spent spell on a doorframe, the way a summoned creature shifts its weight when the other character speaks, the faint hum of a portal that has not fully closed. Let the narrator register that detail while continuing an action already underway, such as checking supplies, tending an injury, or examining a magical object.\n\nAttribute dialogue unmistakably. Use speaker tags or obvious turn markers, and let the narrator's private reasoning run alongside the spoken words in the same paragraph. The other character's reply should come from their own practical goal or feeling in the fantasy situation: they want the portal closed, the creature fed, the wound cleaned, or the narrator to stop pretending the charm is fine. Their spoken reply need not expose their full thought, but it should give the next player something concrete to respond to.\n\nBuild each beat from one concrete action, one or two lines of dialogue, and one focal impression from the narrator. The narrator may speculate about why the other character is being careful with a broken ward, or qualify a comparison between this failure and an earlier one. Keep the beat short enough to scan, but let the narrator's reasoning remain visible when it helps the player understand what is at stake.\n\nEnd after the narrator's current thought or a fallible guess about the other character, at a point where the scene is still happening. The other character may be mid-task, waiting for a response, or continuing a practical argument. Do not invent a cliffhanger, a question, or a forced binary. The next player should be able to continue from what is already underway, not be required to solve a puzzle or answer a prompt. Preserve the active authorship and world contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Write a connected interval of practical life in the fantasy setting as the manuscript narrator's first-person account of what they watched, did, and guessed along a day's sequence or a small journey. Let the street, household, market, or local institution carry the social texture of the place through the narrator's repeated stops and familiar faces.\n\nTreat the fantasy fact as ordinary local knowledge with social consequences. A demon's festival-day behavior may be something the narrator has seen before but now watches more closely because of a recent dispute. The value of an enchanted object in the market may depend on who is selling and who is watching. A public official's use of a talisman may be routine to the crowd but newly significant to the narrator because of what they learned at the previous stop. When the narrator explains a local practice, fold the explanation into the scene as something they are remembering, comparing, or telling a newcomer, not as a lore lecture.\n\nLet each episode revise or qualify what the narrator earlier assumed. At the first stop the narrator may think a neighbor's silence means fear; at the second they may see the same neighbor profit from the magical accident and reconsider. Show who benefits and who is frightened through action and speech: a landlord's reaction, a shopkeeper's changed price, a child's warning, or a familiar person's refusal to meet the narrator's eye. The narrator's fallible judgment is part of the method; later conversation should complicate the earlier guess without turning every encounter into a revelation.\n\nKeep the paragraph order tied to the route. Repeated stops, familiar objects, or a person seen twice can accumulate meaning across the interval. Do not transplant a stock dynasty or use archaic vocabulary unless the actual scene supplies it. Ground names, customs, and roles in the world that is already established, and let the episode end where the narrator's current understanding pauses, not where a dramatic discovery has been forced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nStay close to the narrator's physical experience of the fantasy world. Select a few hard observational details that do practical work in the scene: the patched runes on a second-hand spell, the weight of a ward-stone that has been carried too long, the way a magical remedy leaves a residue on the skin. Let each detail come from the active premise and the narrator's immediate task, not from a stock inventory of grit.\n\nUse compressed transitions that skip explanatory connective tissue. Move from failed action to consequence, from a noticed warning to a changed grip, from a broken charm to the narrator's decision about whether to risk a costly repair. Keep sentences short when pressure rises, but do not starve the narrator's reasoning when a real choice is being made. A longer thought is appropriate when the narrator is weighing cost against a risky magical remedy, remembering a prior failure, or calculating whether a damaged ward will hold until morning.\n\nGround abrasive or rough speech in its speaker's position and experience. A shop assistant may speak plainly because they are afraid of the enforcer outside; a courier may be curt because the road is dangerous after dark; the narrator's own language may stay blunt because that is how they handle what is happening. Do not make everyone hard for style's sake, and preserve the manuscript narrator's practical learning and earnest care where they belong.\n\nLet rhythm change according to the event, not according to paragraph shape. A sudden break can match a dropped ward or a door forced open; a slower, practical account can show the narrator working through a magical mechanism before acting. Physical stakes remain real, but this is not a survival-horror mode. A scene may end with a practical result, a repaired object, or a cost accepted, rather than an escalation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Epistolary / Documents\n\nPresent the story through a sequence of documents, each with a specific author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition drawn from the active fantasy scene. The document's form follows its function: a letter to a family member after a magical dispute will record different details than a guild report about a failed ward, a repairer's note about a cursed lock, or a journal kept after a journey through altered country.\n\nLet the document author remain the manuscript narrator's practical first person. They notice the cost of a ward, remember a prior failure, speculate about a missing shipment, and qualify a comparison with something from home. But what they write is shaped by the recipient and the document's function. A report may omit fear because the guildmaster would read it as incompetence; a private letter may revise a sentence mid-page because the narrator cannot decide how much to admit; a journal may record a suspicion the narrator would never send to anyone.\n\nUse the material trace of the document itself to show how knowledge changed. A burned edge may mark where a warning was nearly destroyed; a crossed-out line may show the narrator reconsidering a claim about a magical event; a date added later may reveal that the writer returned to the record after learning something new. An annotation by someone else can show how the document was received or disputed. Gaps between entries, a report that was never sent, or a duplicate ledger page can carry events without an omniscient narrator explaining them.\n\nKeep continuity readable. When one document ends, begin the next where that author would naturally take up the story, and let readers infer what happened between documents from the change in tone, detail, or address. Do not append a summary of what the document \"really shows\" after each record; the selection, arrangement, and material traces should do that work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the comfort through the narrator's practical care for specific people in the fantasy world. Attend to a familiar preference made concrete by the setting: tea kept warm by a small hearth charm, bread fetched before the market's ghost-lights dim, a cat curled against a spelled radiator, a friend's sigh when they finally remove enchanted boots after a long shift. Let the affection be sincere; no one needs to announce it or turn it into a revelation.\n\nUse the narrator's noticing to appreciate how someone else is comfortable. The narrator may remember that this person always checks the protective charm at the door before sitting down, or notice the exact moment their shoulders drop when the room's minor warming spell takes hold. Do not force every sign to mean deep feeling; some actions are simply care, and the narrator's practical learning lets them recognize what helps without making a speech about it.\n\nLet small pleasures belong to the fantasy setting. A minor magical convenience can make a hard day easier, and a familiar object with a harmless enchantment can be a source of ease. But do not let the magic solve every difficulty. A dangerous storm may still be outside, a debt may still be unpaid, or an injury may still be healing. The comfort is in the shared, manageable interior and the particular people inside it, not in the removal of the world's problems.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. A person may accept coffee but not an apology, thank the narrator while still wanting space, or enjoy the warm room without forgiving what happened earlier. The narrator may offer care awkwardly, or notice that their help is wanted only in part. Let the scene make room for particular people and their actual preferences, with the fantasy world's work, danger, or uncertainty still present at the edge of the moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narrator open a scene with a practical observation grounded in the fantasy world: how the morning light falls across a spell-paved market, how a public notice about a new levy on enchanted goods waits beside the baker's window, how the air smells after a magical drought has lifted. Then move from that observation into the particular event through a transition that carries the reader from the general scene to the narrator's specific concern.\n\nFrame the scene through social context, history, or judgment where the narrator's position permits it. The narrator may remember how the same street looked before the magical drought, compare a guildmaster's public manner to a past dispute over a failed ward, or consider what a neighbor's new prosperity says about the recent change in the local spell trade. Do not invent a noble lineage or archaic vocabulary if the world does not supply it; ordinary people's work, local institutions, and the practical consequences of the fantasy premise can carry the social description.\n\nLet the narrator's private thought remain visible when authorized, but distinguish it from public speech through the formal movement of the prose. A composed reply may be followed by a paragraph in which the narrator reflects on what the reply cost or concealed. A written invitation may be read twice, with the second reading framed by the narrator's memory of the sender's earlier behavior. The contrast between what the world expects and what this particular person actually thinks should emerge from the narrator's sustained reflection, not from an announcement that the two differ.\n\nWrite full paragraphs that allow reflection without stalling the scene. A description of a magical artifact may lead into the narrator's memory of its maker and then return to the present negotiation. A conversation may pause for a social observation that returns to the speaker's next words with the narrator's judgment now informing the reader's attention. The formal quality comes from this composed movement and the narrator's deliberate framing, not from uniformly ceremonial speech or an aristocratic setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite as a single first-person narrator who is telling at least one embedded story to a named or clearly present audience. The telling has one operative purpose: to alter how the listener understands or intends to act on the current difficulty in the outer story. Open inside the present telling situation, with the narrator already aware of the listener and the immediate practical stake. Let the narrator pause to address the listener, observe their reaction, and explain the reason for a digression, selection, or omission. The frame is not a neutral container; the narrator's attention to audience, weather, shared history, or immediate practical concern should affect what gets told next.\n\nWhen the narrator shifts to an embedded tale, make the shift audible and immediate: a quoted announcement, a listener's question, or a turn from present speech into remembered scene. Keep the level changes clean by naming who is speaking and where each story pauses. Return to the frame before the reader loses track of the frame audience. The narrator may compare a remembered event with the present moment, explain why this version differs from an earlier telling, or notice that the listener is now interpreting the outer situation differently. The embedded tale should alter something concrete: the listener's willingness to act, their suspicion, their memory of the narrator, or their practical understanding of the current difficulty.\n\nUse the Fantasy premise as something the teller has actually encountered or learned at second hand, and make its consequence the load-bearing element of the embedded tale. The narrator should render that consequence in ordinary terms: how the magic or wonder affected a journey, a household, a debt, a relationship, or a daily task. The embedded story may be true, partly mistaken, exaggerated for effect, or repeated with variation; the narrator's manuscript voice can qualify it without breaking the spell. Wonder and practicality should coexist. The narrator can speculate about a magical object's likely limits, describe its local reputation, or recall a failed attempt to use it. Do not turn every impossible event into a fully explained system or a puzzle to be verified. What matters is that the audience now sees a plausible consequence in the outer story.\n\nLet a single repeated phrase, object, or gesture move between the frame and the embedded tale and change value through the teller's own emphasis and the listener's developing response. Choose the recurring element early and return to it at each level shift. The recurrence becomes meaningful through use, not through outside commentary.\n\nThe narrator's private thought is active before, during, and after a tale. Sustained reasoning can concern the audience's possible reaction, a memory the story revives, a practical plan, or the ethical burden of telling. The spoken words and the private thought need not match; fear of shame, concern for the listener's distress, or tactical calculation can widen the gap. Preserve the teller's fallibility: a characterization may be unfair, a lesson may be self-serving, a comfort may be insufficient. The story can end with the frame audience interrupting, questioning, or leaving; it does not need a completed lesson. Keep the writing direct and rhythmic, like prose meant to be heard. Use short paragraphs for shifts in level or audience response. Prefer sensory details the narrator would actually remember: heat, sound, hunger, a worn seam, a road's dust. Continue the activity already underway in the frame if the story ends mid-scene. Let the impossible remain dangerous, lovely, or burdensome according to its owner's experience, not according to a rule that all wonders must be charming.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as a first-person narrator using the social and developmental realism route. Treat the impossible not as a single surprising event but as a persistent condition whose primary narrative consequence is economic and customary: it changes what people may use, owe, inherit, sell, or do without permission. The narrator should trace those consequences through specific practical details—a household budget that must account for a charm's cost, a village rule about who may carry an enchanted tool, a child's slow education in a craft that blends magic and mundane labor, a season when the spell's effect weakens. Let the narrator notice how a magical fact becomes ordinary through repeated exposure and how it still disrupts ordinary expectations when resources, seasons, or relationships change.\n\nUse the manuscript voice to examine the arrangements behind those details. The narrator may speculate about the reason for a price, debate whether a neighbor's belief is superstition or tested knowledge, and compare past and present practice. Let sustained internal reasoning develop a point rather than being reduced to immediate reactions. When the narrator speaks to others, a reply may reveal only part of that reasoning; the private thought is where the fuller assessment, doubt, or affection accumulates.\n\nLet ethical development unfold through the narrator's changing assessment of a custom, debt, or obligation. The narrator may learn that a past judgment was too harsh, or that a custom protected someone in ways the narrator initially dismissed. These realizations should emerge from continued activity and practical consequence, not from a final moral summarizing the scene. A developmental story allows mistakes and reversals.\n\nAvoid reducing the setting to a generic German-speaking backdrop. Use whatever specific region, period, community, and language mix the active story already supplies. If the story does not supply a specific place, keep social detail concrete without assigning national stereotypes: mention weather appropriate to the actual season, food or clothing the characters already possess, local administrative arrangements, debts, apprenticeships, or festivals that belong to the narrated world. Do not import bureaucracy, severity, or translated word order as a stylistic device. Syntax should follow the narrator's temperament and the scene's pacing.\n\nLet language carry enough distance for reflection. A paragraph may move from an observed object to a remembered lesson, then to a larger social conclusion if the narrator is inclined to make one. However, the narrator's conclusions should remain provisional and personal: \"The miller's charm had kept the frost off his field for a decade, but that did not make it generous,\" rather than a general law of magic. Keep the fantasy legible without explaining its whole system. The narrator can know a practical limit, a local taboo, a failed trial, or a rumor, and these facts may shape decisions. But do not pause for a treatise. Show the consequence in the ordinary world: a magical boundary affects a property dispute; a bargain affects who can travel; a gift imposes an obligation that changes a family's plans. Wonder may sit beside fatigue, irritation, pride, or grief. Let the narrator's direct feeling be present even within the realist register. The piece should end at the active boundary of the present scene, in the middle of the activity or reflection, without a forced resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite in a first-person manuscript voice saturated with Gothic-Punk treatment: inherited power, modern decay, intimate political atmosphere. The fantasy element should be an inherited arrangement—an old authority, a supernatural bargain, a magical bloodline, or a monstrous custom—that touches current urban or domestic life. Present it as a living collision between a waning or resurgent old order and the needs of familiar, present-tense people. The narrator's voice should make that collision tangible by tracing the arrangement's present costs through observed material detail: cracked plaster, a neon glow against old stone, a rent ledger, a family heirloom that now complicates a lease, a ritual that persists in a back office, a secret loyalty that alters a modern friendship.\n\nUse the narrator's fallible judgment to interpret the encounter, not merely to report it. The voice can speculate about a sign, qualify a rumor, remember an earlier threat, or compare what a tradition promised with what its surviving representative actually offers. Allow sustained inner reasoning, private desire, shame, tenderness, and quiet calculation. The narrator's spoken replies and private thought need not be identical; a public mask may fall away briefly in trusted company. Let that trust be earned in the actual scene through shared attention, not declared. A person can be dangerous and still offer genuine help; a caring act need not conceal a trick.\n\nKeep the impossible element distinct from any specific imported vampire or occult franchise. Do not include Kindred physiology, Humanity scores, factions, or associated powers. Any supernatural rule should come from the active story's established fantasy logic. The Gothic-Punk method works by contrast: old authority may claim permanence while the narrator can see its present costs; an enchantment may make a building beautiful and also trap its caretaker; an inherited duty may protect a family while demanding a monstrous price. Show how allegiance, charm, or fear changes the atmosphere of an ordinary space. Let desire, bargaining, obligation, and resentment matter as much as explicit danger.\n\nTreat social masks as active choices, not as false selves to be stripped in a reveal. The narrator notices when someone speaks for an institution, when a lover repeats a family phrase, when a friend switches from formal address to private language. The story's pressure can make a mask crack gradually. The outer scene continues through domestic tasks, commuting, work, or conversation; the fantastic does not pause the world for a set piece. The narrator should stay within what they can perceive and infer, distinguishing observation from rumor and fear from knowledge. Avoid omniscient declarations about hidden motives. End at the natural story boundary, with attention still unfinished, rather than forcing a revelation or complete unmasking.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nCompose as a first-person narrator whose fantasy is carried through an oral, epic, or frame-tale route. The story should have a present telling occasion and an audience who needs to hear, repeat, question, or reject the narrator's account. Open with the narrator already engaged in this telling, aware of the listeners and the immediate reason for speaking. The inner tale should be clearly distinguished from the frame, and the narrator should return to the present moment at decisive points to address a listener's expression or interruption.\n\nLet the narrator's practical knowledge persist during the telling. They may pause to explain a custom, clarify a kinship term or regional phrase, admit what they did not see, or say why this version differs from a previous one. The audience should not be decorative: a question, interruption, or remembered remark can force the narrator to retell a section or qualify a fact. The listener's response can change the outer situation. The narrator's private thought may include worry about whether the story will persuade, memory of the source, or observation of the room; it can remain distinct from the spoken account.\n\nThe fantasy premise should be rendered as an intelligible social inheritance, not a private marvel. A lineage, a vow, a curse, a protective spirit, an old bargain, or a creature attached to a place or craft should affect present family, work, migration, marriage, or obligation. Show its consequence in daily life: a household decision, a journey, a debt, a festival, a dispute over land or labor. The narrator may have partial knowledge and should not turn the tale into a complete magical system. The miracle's truth can remain uncertain, but its effect on relationships and choices should be legible.\n\nMake the telling itself the site where that social consequence is negotiated. The narrator should use the embedded tale to clarify, defend, or complicate an inherited obligation, and the audience's live response should test or revise that account. When a listener questions a detail, the narrator may retell the section with a different emphasis, admit a gap, or reveal why the inheritance matters now. The frame-tale structure is load-bearing: the outer situation changes because the audience's understanding of the inheritance changes.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific only where the active story already supplies them. Do not invent a generic Indian village or urban landscape; do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a universal joint family. If the setting is South Indian or diasporic or a particular state, honor that supplied specificity. If no community is given, write from the narrator's place with local objects, seasonal labor, travel routes, or shared stories that could belong to the actual world, without naming a stereotype. Use restrained translation notes only when the point of view would actually make one; do not explain the narrator's own language to the audience for effect.\n\nLet repetition and epic cadence work selectively: a recurring epithet, a repeated blessing, a formal opening or closing phrase can accrue meaning, but do not make every sentence ceremonious. The narrator can be earnest, skeptical, tender, or wry. Direct feeling and sustained reasoning should appear as the telling continues, especially where the inner tale touches the outer dilemma. End at the active boundary of the scene, perhaps with the audience still waiting, a listener turning aside, or the narrator deciding how to continue, rather than with a forced completion of the storytelling occasion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as a first-person narrator using the Japanese fantastic-tale route. The impossible should be a local, intelligible presence in ordinary life, not a spectacle. Present it through the narrator's practical attention: a hill spirit affecting a harvest, a possessed object that alters a household, a seasonal ritual that opens a doorway the narrator half believes. The narrator may encounter the fantastic directly, hear of it from a neighbor, or discover in retrospect that an ordinary event carried a supernatural consequence. Do not require a full explanation of the magic, but render its effect on the narrator's decisions, relationships, or sense of place.\n\nUse selected detail and omission. The narrator should notice what matters to the particular moment: a wet cuff's edge, a cold floor, the smell of a kitchen, a changed shadow, a local plant, an item missing from its usual spot. Let the narration leave gaps where the narrator cannot see or cannot yet make sense. Construct the delayed connection as a specific operation: the narrator withholds or fails to recognize a link at first, then a later practical detail—a found object, a remembered sound, a changed routine—reorders the earlier scene. The reordering should arise from the narrator's own retrospective reasoning, not from a mechanical twist. Seasonal detail should arise from the actual world of the story, not from a mandatory checklist of cherry blossoms, cicadas, or snow. Use whatever season the scene supplies, and let it influence mood, labor, or travel. The narrator's temperament may be direct, exuberant, weary, wary, or amused; do not enforce quietness or indirection.\n\nThe narrative voice should allow sustained thought and practical speculation. The narrator may reason about whether a charm works, compare a current warning with a remembered tale, qualify a rumor, or admit uncertainty. Direct feeling should be present and can be strong. Spoken words need not reveal all private thought; the narrator may withhold from politeness, fear, doubt, or private joy. If the story includes another character, give that person a particular voice and practical life. Dialogue can be abrupt and colloquial if it fits, or formal and restrained; the form follows the actual relationship.\n\nKeep the regional specificity of the supplied setting. If the world has a named Japanese-language or Japanese-like community, preserve its manner, place names, food, work, and speech rhythm. Do not import a generic \"ancient Japan\" into an unrelated fantasy world. If the world is not culturally Japanese, still use the stylistic method—fantastic tale with selected detail and delayed connection—without forcing Japanese scenery or honorifics. The fantasy should keep its everyday consequences legible: a change in a road, a debt, a promise, a sleepless night, or a new fear. Let the story end at the active boundary of the scene, possibly in the middle of the narrator's reasoning, a task, or an unresolved question, rather than with a completed magical explanation or a final moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as a first-person narrator using the marvelous-treated-as-ordinary route. The fantastic should be a public or domestic fact in the narrated world: a miracle that affects a harvest, a ghost known on a street, a saint's intervention in a labor dispute, a talking animal recognized by the family, a vanished town remembered in local songs. Report these things through the narrator's practical experience and social memory, not as exotic spectacle. The narrator should treat the marvel with the same attention given to money, weather, food, work, grief, or distance.\n\nLet the narrator's voice carry the chronicle with specific observation and fallible judgment. Build the account from three distinct layers: what the narrator directly saw or did, what a neighbor or relative reported, and what a document, song, or public record claimed. Keep those layers separate and let them differ. The narrator may be skeptical of one report while accepting another; the telling should not demand a single supernatural explanation. The impossible changes what people do, and the narrator should trace those changes through the layered account even when the cause remains uncertain.\n\nLet the narrator recall how a wonderful event changed a household's plans, how a parent bargained with a spirit, or how a promise made at a shrine affected later travel. The chronicle's pacing should hold a day's labor, a remembered conversation, a public rumor, and a personal conclusion in a long paragraph when the narrator's attention actually moves that way. The narrator can interrupt the account to express anger, tenderness, or doubt. Direct feeling is not a break in the register. Let private thought remain distinct from spoken testimony when the narrator speaks to others. A spoken report may omit a private fear, a rejected doubt, or a hope.\n\nThe narration should stay close to the particular place, language, and history the story supplies. If the setting is a specific region, use its actual landscape, produce, architecture, street customs, festivals, and speech rhythms. Do not invent a generic \"Latin American\" backdrop with exotic color. If English is the story language, the narrator may translate a phrase or leave a name in the original; if code-switching is natural to the character, it is allowed, but do not force it. Religion may be present if it belongs to the world, but do not insert it where the world excludes it.\n\nThe outer scene should continue amid the telling: a meal, a walk, a repair, a vigil. Keep the prose rhythmic but not ornamented. Avoid concluding with a universal moral. End at the active boundary when the narrator has just completed a task, a question, or a turn of attention, not with a compulsory magical resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Light Novel\n\nWrite in a first-person light novel register that holds a Nemo-style manuscript voice. Open directly inside a concrete situation: the narrator is already somewhere doing or noticing something relevant. Establish the place in a few sensory, practical details, then let the immediate activity continue. When the scene moves, mark the spatial change simply—through a doorway, up a stair, across a street, into another room. Do not use ornate transitions or long weather paragraphs unless the narrator is actually attending to weather.\n\nIntroduce the fantasy element through a definite three-step operation. First, let the narrator encounter it practically: seeing, handling, or asking about an unfamiliar mechanic, item, or name. Second, give one clear explanatory beat in the narrator's own words—enough to make the unfamiliar thing usable in the scene, not a lore dump. Third, return to the interaction or task. The narrator should remain fallible: noticing, speculating, making a comparison, correcting an earlier assumption, and continuing with the current job. Sustained internal reasoning can be compressed into two or three sentences between actions so the light novel pace remains, but it should not disappear. Direct feeling should sound like the narrator's actual response—serious, uncertain, angry, embarrassed, fond, or amused—not a generic reaction cue.\n\nUse short paragraphs strategically. Put major action or shifts in a new paragraph. Put spoken dialogue on its own line when the speaker changes. Place a private thought as its own brief paragraph or as a one-line interjection between dialogue and action; do not mix a long reflection into a fast action paragraph. The private thought may differ from what the narrator says aloud. Show the reason: social awkwardness, calculation, kindness, fear, or simply a slower way of processing. A spoken reply may be quick while the private thought catches up a moment later.\n\nKeep scene-level clarity even when the fantasy is strange. If a spell alters a room, trace what the narrator can see, hear, or feel in order. If another character has a new power, let the narrator notice its visible effect before naming it, then give the name if it would naturally be known. No first person, comedy, young cast, or closing cliffhanger is required by this recipe; preserve the actual cast and tone of the world. End the response at the active boundary of the scene, in the middle of the conversation, travel, or task if that is where the natural stop falls. The beat should be a legible slice of forward motion, not a forced revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the prose register the impossible through a person already engaged with ordinary work. When a fantastic element appears, contact it the way this narrator contacts anything else: name what it does to the light, the weight on the air, the task in hand, the body's small adjustments. A sensory image may unfold if it changes the narrator's understanding; an ornament that only decorates should not replace the work of perceiving.\n\nBuild rhythm from the narrator's pattern of attention rather than from equal musical sentences. Show a perception being revised within a paragraph: an early comparison that proves too clean, a remembered phrase that explains the difference, a practical worry that keeps the wonder from becoming abstraction. Let repeated sounds or images arrive through what the narrator handles, remembers, or returns to. Plain statements can interrupt the figurative development and give the scene its floor.\n\nThe impossible need not be solemn. A wonder can be inconvenient, familiar, missed, or easily accepted by the people who live with it. Keep the teller's private thought larger than what they say aloud, but do not make every reflection end in disclosure or perfected phrase. The paragraph may conclude with the activity still in hand, the narrator still deciding what to call the experience.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a fantastic fact occupy the same memory as a room, a transaction, or a person's habit. The narrator may recall an earlier way of doing the work that is no longer available, or compare the first sight of an impossible thing with the ordinary occasion when it stopped being remarkable. Reflection should arise from particular detail: an object kept, a procedure changed, a phrase someone no longer uses.\n\nAllow present pleasure to remain present. A warm meal, a repaired tool, or a familiar voice can be enjoyed without an immediate announcement of loss. When longing or unfinished attachment appears, show what it touches in the scene rather than turning every paragraph into a summary of impermanence. If the past presses on the present, connect the two through an observed detail or a remembered obligation, not through a compulsive sentence about time passing.\n\nLet the narrator reason about how they understand change, perhaps noticing that a memory is inaccurate, that a nickname outlived the reason for it, or that an act once feared is now just work. The style may pause over small losses, but it should not require each reply to end by declaring that nothing can be kept. The fantastic may make return possible in one world and impossible in another; ground whichever condition the story establishes in the character's surviving knowledge, property, and habit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite from the position of someone who is composing or telling an account, and let the frame show in the prose without a formal nesting of tales. The narrator may begin with a phrase that reaches for an audience, interrupt themselves to explain a practical point, or record an earlier teller's version of an event before giving the corrected memory. Keep the fantastic premise part of a social or domestic world: a household rule, a route no longer traveled, a claim neighbors dispute, a profession changed by the impossible. The wonder should have local consequences, not merely decorative antiquity.\n\nUse lyric, reflective, or philosophical passages as the teller's own manner of sitting with an event. A sustained paragraph may turn a change of light, a worn threshold, or a repeated blessing over to the narrator's meditation. Do not make the narrator a walking tradition or a mystic; their speculation should remain fallible and tied to what they know. Social realism can be the main drive, with the impossible entering through labor, property, argument, or obligation. Let ordinary commerce and conversation remain concrete: who owes what, who witnessed the act, who has left the neighborhood, and who now tells the story differently.\n\nIf satire or picaresque energy appears, let it come from the narrator's encounter with absurd official demands, broken promises, or the gap between a grand account and an actual errand. If memoir or diasporic writing appears, let it show the traveler comparing a present room with an earlier one, retrieving names and payments as well as emotion. Keep language and tradition specific to the actually supplied setting, and do not import religion, honor, mysticism, or exile as automatic requirements. The account may end with the teller still addressing someone, still remembering, or still puzzling over what the event means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse exact physical details to show the fantastic premise in operation: a door that must be asked, a payment that is no longer counted, a field that remembers. Keep clauses direct, and let the action or speech show the consequence before the narrator says what it means. The narrative may state a prior attempt in a subordinate clause, but the sentence should remain economical: what was tried, what happened, what remains to be done.\n\nLet the narrator's thought occupy only the space that alters the present account. An inference can be brief: one piece of evidence, one qualification, one practical consequence. A longer reflection is valid when it connects separate events that would otherwise remain puzzling. Do not delete causality for the sake of shortness; if a person acts because of a memory, show the memory or the visible hesitation rather than leaving a mysterious gap.\n\nAvoid redundant explanation. If the narrator says the bucket would not hold, do not follow with a sentence explaining that the bucket was broken. Let recurring objects carry meaning: the same cup, the same lock, the same name used differently after the change. Character voices remain individual, and dialogue may be as spare or as discursive as the speaker. The story can stop after an action is attempted, after a practical fact is established, or while the narrator is still deciding what to do, without closing the uncertainty in a summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene from inside the narrator's continuing day, moving among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and thought as the moment requires. Let the fantastic appear through its ordinary consequences: a letter that changes a schedule, a household object with a rule attached, a journey altered because an impossibility has become available. The fantastic premise should shape decisions and labor without demanding a ceremonial introduction.\n\nUse flexible paragraphing and transitions that orient through observed fact rather than through a narrator's recap of the last several scenes. A recollection may begin from an object, a phrase, or an interruption; the scene may then return to the immediate task with the memory still working. Let the narrator's voice set the register: practical when describing an errand, more patient and intimate when a feeling or memory needs room, and capable of plain summary when a development is simple. Direct feeling and private reasoning may run through a paragraph without becoming a separate analytical voice.\n\nDo not force understated emotion. The narrator may be moved, angry, amused, or concerned, and the prose may show the feeling through behavior, speech, or thought. Let contemporary presentation remain a matter of prose rhythm and point of view, not a ban on wonder. The reply can stop according to the scene's boundary: after a door closes, after someone speaks, while a recollection begins to be corrected, or with the narrator still deciding what the next hour requires.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose the scene that exposes the established relationship's particular promise, and let the fantastic premise change the conditions under which the characters know each other: a shared spell that becomes a household task, a protective custom that one person finds suffocating, a gift with an impossible consequence that must be discussed. The narrator should notice what the relationship has made familiar: who speaks first, which doors are open, what phrase means apology, who fails to say what the other needs.\n\nUse private language and callbacks that arise from the actual history supplied. A repeated object or phrase may reappear when the present moment changes its meaning: the cup no longer refilled, the knock no longer answered, the old joke repeated without the old relief. Let the narrator's private thought diverge from speech when the relationship gives reason: tenderness not yet admitted, fear of burdening the other, relief that cannot be shown, or a practical decision that would expose more than the speaker intends. The difference need not be deception; it can be attention, care, uncertainty, or accommodation.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough room where it belongs. Emotion may be shown through a silent gesture, a delayed reply, a repeated act of care, or a passage of direct reflection. Do not inset familiar tropes the story does not establish, and do not demand a confession or reconciliation as the scene's endpoint. The fantastic element may intensify or complicate the relationship, but it should remain one condition of the shared life rather than a plot machine requiring every quiet moment to become proof of devotion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nLet the narrator tell as one who has heard versions and is now supplying the better one, without leaving the present activity behind. Open with a compact declaration of what must be remembered: a refusal, a repair, an impossible return, a bargain kept. Then show the particulars that make the declaration incomplete: what the act actually cost, who helped, who disputed the motive, what ordinary work surrounded the wonder. The telling may sound deliberate, but the narrator remains a person, not a chant.\n\nUse recurrence so that later events give an earlier phrase or deed new application. A name, a promise, or a repeated journey may carry meaning because the community has had to live with it. Let the narrator distinguish what they are reporting from what they saw: a neighbor's account may be accepted, doubted, or corrected in the next sentence. The fantastic should enter as an act with consequences for household, kinship, trade, or public memory, not as a decorative marvel that leaves the world unchanged.\n\nDo not import heroic doom, ceremonial dialogue, or a particular pantheon unless the supplied world already contains them. An errand may be told with the gravity of a deed, but its scale remains an errand. Keep the selected perspective's access consistent: the narrator may know what people later said of an act without claiming to know hidden minds. The account may end with a small act still in progress or with the teller returning to the present task, leaving open what final use the community will make of the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-domestic realism route: whatever brings the impossible into the neighborhood must change a particular day's work, a household chore, a supply, a meeting, a pension or wage, or a local obligation. Ground the narrator's explanation in the usable detail of getting somewhere, fixing something, remembering a bill, caring for a person, and waiting for mail or travel.\n\nLet the narrator work as a particular person still living inside the events. They may recall related cases from their own experience or from other people, reposition a first impression, and preliminarily interpret what they have seen. A comparison may be challenged by a later sight; the narrator can then correct the earlier account, admit what they overlooked, or leave weight to wonder and uncertainty where appropriate. The immediate facts need not surrender to an abstract system; it is enough to show what one person can now do, whom it affects, and what it costs in time, relation, or comfort.\n\nProtect the limited viewpoint of the particular narrator. Let them combine seeing, speculation, memory, and self-correction. They can say what they noticed, what they made of it, what they thought afterwards, and what still seems unclear. Speech, practice, and private reasoning may take different shapes. The narrator can help a neighbor and still not disclose what they saw at the boundary; they can claim a small magic as ordinary and then qualify that in private thought. Friends and family may be entirely concerned with food, transport, and work. Their practical speech is not a mask for mythic importance.\n\nMake the local setting specific rather than a generic Northern mood. If the story concerns a particular time and country, preserve the rhythm and material of its rooms, streets, meals, public services, durable clothing, holiday, and work routines. Do not make cold, dark, isolation, or silence the automatic content of the telling. The fantasy should keep social and domestic realism as its necessary floor: an impossible thing matters because it changes the terms on which somebody can live in an actual community. A small miracle may dwell beside unresolved argument, bad weather, and unglamorous labor without becoming less significant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Screenplay\n\nWrite the fantasy sequence in concise action lines and clear dialogue blocks. Each scene heading marks a change of location or time; each action line names what is there to be seen, heard, or done. When the central narrator is present, keep their behavior observably specific: what object they stop handling, whose back they watch, how long a door remains open, which coat they put on. Do not explain the mechanism in a character's mouth. Show a constraint or wonder through the practical effect—a note that arrives already answered, a lamp that catches before it tips, a garden that yields fruit people cannot purchase—so the viewer can infer a change without an expositional speech.\n\nPresent the narrator's inner account in a clearly attributed nonspoken note whenever sustained reasoning or memory belongs in the moment. Precede it with a cue like NARRATOR'S PRIVATE NOTE, not as dialogue or voice-over unless the scene's contract separately authorizes voice-over. The note may run somewhat longer than a conventional action block while the scene itself stays sparse. It can recall, qualify, speculate, or replace an earlier comparison; it does not become known to the other characters. Keep a visible beat outside the note so that the external scene continues and later dialogue does not inadvertently read as a response to the hidden thought.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, and voice-over only when this sequence actually needs them. A cut can separate the private note from a later public disclosure; an offscreen sound can mark an arrival the narrator has already feared. Do not require a last-minute twist or a completed explanation. The scene can end on an action or a line, with the narrator's private note left as a supporting record rather than a clue. Let the genre's consequences touch practical work and everyday institutions even when the selected fantasy is intimate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Stage Play\n\nRealize the impossible consequence through stage business. Whatever the fantasy permits—a container that refills, a sick animal that understands commands, a visitor who can enter before the bell—must be staged as observable action. Characters handle the significant objects, set them down, leave the door half-open, or bring another person to see. Do not resolve the premise into a speech. Let one character's ordinary work be changed by what the object does, then let another character draw an incomplete or mistaken conclusion and be answered through behavior or a plain practical line.\n\nGive the narrator's developed thought as clearly marked nonspoken authoring material, not as an automatic aside or monologue. A private interval in the cooking, fetching, or waiting can be written as a NARRATOR'S PRIVATE NOTE with the same access and privacy rules as any other nonspoken interiority: it may remember an earlier season, correct a first impression, or trace a motive, but it remains unknown to others. If an aside or monologue is used, it must have a dramatic reason—address to the audience, ritual exposure, a public confession—rather than serving as the default vehicle for every thought. The private note can coexist with a shallow public line; the narrator need not announce the full process.\n\nKeep the physical world practical and playable. Block entrances, exits, props, pauses, and the interruption of an action so that the altered conditions are legible to an audience without explanation. A last line may complete a chore, refuse to believe, invite help, or state what remains unsure. Do not write film-camera instructions. Preserve the genre's everyday institutions—household, work, errand, care—so the strange thing's consequences are visible and the invented power stays a part of the living scene, not spectacle parked outside it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBrave the narrator's mind as it moves through the fantasy consequence. Perception of the actual thing must remain distinguishable from memory and imagined outcome: a cup refilled while the narrator watched, a dog that answered with a word, a neighbor's calendar moved one date ahead. Let the association start in that objective particular, then turn through what it reminds the narrator of, what they wanted to say, what their body did, or what still worries them. Keep the sequence inside this one authorized narrator; other characters may speak, but their speech is heard through the narrator's attention. The narrator may realize, \"not the same cup, the one from winter,\" or split a remembered phrase from something now being said.\n\nLet recurring concern do the work of theme without converting the story into finished reasoning. The same image—a locked gate, a sister's silence, the smell after rain, a tax receipt—may return at different points, each time with a slightly shifted meaning. The narrator can continue an interrupted practical task even while the mental current runs on. Do not require every recollection to receive a tidy explanation. The impossible thing should be real in the world and consequential; if it changes a law of nature, it also changes a bill, a route, a meal, a promise, a record, or a body.\n\nThe syntax may run long, double back, or interrupt itself, but the scene's orientation must remain sound. If the narrator recalls a past event, it stays recollection. If they imagine what would happen, it stays conditional. If the actual present returns, make the return legible through a sensation or object. The stranger the premise, the more the narration needs one hard point: a door handle, a borrowed book, a failed light, a name. Do not use the style to make every event indeterminate or to blur distinct people into one mind. A fantasy can remain strange while the narrator's particular folly, tenderness, and practical history remain plainly their own.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCompose the story as readable sender-attributed messages, with only the interface facts that change understanding. Use a timestamp when delay matters, two ticks or a read receipt when someone is waiting, an edited message when a correction has consequence, an attachment when the sender needs to show the creature or damaged thing. Do not add an interface event solely to imply hidden meaning. Each sender's actual habits should be distinct: one types in complete words, one leaves lines mid-thought, one repeats the same greeting, one responds after a shift ends, one never confirms. The fantasy fact must enter through what these people can credibly send or avoid sending—a photograph of an empty pantry newly filled, a voice note containing an animal that speaks, a beating from someone who insists they already replied, an order that arrives marked before it was requested.\n\nKeep required narrator context in a clearly separated block outside the transcript, such as a short scene note or an attributed private interval. The narrator can notice that their hand is trembling, that they read the message three times, that they recall last winter differently, or that a correction is not honest. This context is not a message and not a draft; it is nonspoken authoring material. A person's typed reply may be shorter, kinder, harsher, or more evasive than their private thought. When their fingers freeze over the keyboard or a draft remains unfinished, show that as an unsent draft, not as transmitted knowledge. No message should expose the full private account.\n\nThe fantasy's consequences should touch ordinary life through the exchange: a shift changed, a delivery missed, an unread notice from a landlord, a family group that no longer replies, a school record that shows an impossible date. Let the transcript carry enough of this to be legible while private interval carries the narrator's reasoning and feeling. The piece may end on a sent answer, a message that does not arrive, an unanswered question, or the timestamp continuing past the last word received, without requiring a false realization or a fully explained system. Maintain the narrator's consistent identity and practical attention across messages; the strange fact matters because of what it alters in the lives of people who type, wait, eat, and work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Visual Novel\n\nCompose the fantasy matter as concise scenes in which dialogue is clearly attributed and narration is readable, with focal inner response. Set a scene with a few unhurried but concrete details: the counter where a bucket has been set, the coat rack newly empty, the color of the jar that should not be there. Use deliberate background or expression references when they clarify the moment—dimming window light, a clean table, a neighbor's stiff nod—but do not turn every glance into a truth signal. The impossible thing must do something legible: a plant grows through a sealed pot, a door opens from the wrong side of the wall, a debt disappears from a document, a child names someone who has not arrived. Show the people responding through practical behavior, not exposition.\n\nLet the narrator's inward response accompany the scene without becoming everyone's knowledge. A continuous focal narrator may observe, doubt, remember, qualify a comparison, and continue a present chore. Their private reasoning can correct an earlier explanation, or delay a conclusion. Their speech to another character may say only that the gate is closed, or that the doctor is expected, while the inner response traces what they actually believe. Other characters do not know the private turn of thought simply because the reader has it.\n\nKeep scene changes legible with a concise signal such as a new room, day, errand, arrival, or shift in occupation. No need for branching menu, player statistics, engine code, romance route, or mandatory visual label. The fantasy can be intimate or expansive according to the actual world contract, but the scene should preserve practical institutions—work, family, meals, rent, transport, care—so that the strange consequence stays within everyday life. A discovery may open a new conversation or leave a practical task pending; it does not require a choice menu or a fully explained system. End after the selected beat, not at an artificial dramatic checkpoint.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagasbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Web Serial\n\nOpen each installment by restoring only the facts required now: where the narrator is, what they were doing, and what changed. A remembered lock, debt, appointment, repair, or errand can be recovered briefly; do not retell the earlier discovery. Let the continuation advance through one or a few local changes—a person asks a new question, a shipment arrives with altered paperwork, a child repeats an impossible phrase, a neighbor offers help, the narrator tries to make dinner and finds an object behaving differently. Progress may occur in knowledge, relationship, work, or resources, not necessarily in a solved mystery or acquired rank.\n\nPresent the series through the narrator's particular, self-correcting account. Their private recollection may be revised between installments, but the formerly recorded event itself does not silently change. If the narrator once described a lock as rusted and later sees it oiled, show the change as a change, or note the possible mistake. The narrator's report may reason, guess, remember, and continue the immediate practical task. Speech may remain closer-lipped than thought; another character need not know what the narrator concluded. Keep direct feeling available when it affects the moment without requiring a dramatic decision each time.\n\nThe fantasy's consequences should remain tied to institutions and ordinary survival—bills, schedules, food, transport, care, property, records—rather than only to awe. Let the serial's cumulative weight come from repeated contact with the changed object, creature, person, or place. An occasional hook may close a scene when the narrator notices a new consequence, but an installment can also end on a quota, an unfinished errand, a lingering feeling, or the simple acknowledgment that something now works differently. Do not manufacture a cliffhanger or call a brief pause a complete chapter. Keep the story available to continue from what remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagataa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet the exchange itself be where an established fantastic condition shows its personal cost. When a character explains a phenomenon, argues about its proper meaning, or revises the word they just used, make that verbal move register as admission, deflection, need, or a hurt they cannot quite retract. A word can be corrected because it exposed something honest.\n\nGive each speaker a different habit under pressure. One may inspect definitions to avoid saying what changed. Another may reach for cruel precision and then half-explain the wound behind the cruelty. Let strain bend the shape of the talk itself — a repeated explanation, a question answered too exactly, an apology offered in the wrong words. A definition, objection, or contradiction should alter what the participants now know about one another, and silence may follow a successful correction when that silence matters.\n\nTreat the world's impossibility as fact. The disagreement concerns what the fact means, who must live with it, and what it has already cost — not whether it would work. Wonder can collide with a difficult relationship without resolving it: a healing that mends a body may leave the remembered injury exactly where it was, because the story owes no cure for feeling.\n\nKeep exposure partial through the active narrator's distance, and use that narrator's own diction for pain rather than a borrowed vocabulary of suffering. Someone may admit the smaller thing to avoid the larger one; the viewpoint character may understand a motive without saying it, or may correct a story into a shape that is gentler and also false in a specific way. Distinguish what the speaker knows from what the listener hears.\n\nThe scene may end while a phrase is still unrevised or a need is still only implied. Warmth can appear in a single accurate name remembered under strain without becoming a full reconciliation. Do not resolve every difficult beat into a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the reply as one clear shared interval in readable paragraphs, with dialogue attributed so no reader doubts who is speaking. Keep the post's main speaker distinct from whoever listens or interrupts. The current turn may advance a practical action, continue an argument, or sit in the aftermath of a changed claim.\n\nPut the verbal play inside ordinary roleplay speech. When a character questions a word, corrects an earlier description, or argues about the proper explanation of a magical occurrence, let that change the practical stakes of the scene: who is avoiding a topic, who needs to be believed, who discovered something while trying to explain it. Attach each verbal habit to a particular person instead of giving the whole cast the same quickness, and let a quiet character's plain sentence land harder for the contrast. Skip the riddle or formal verbal duel when ordinary conversation fits the scene better.\n\nTreat the fantasy element as real and discussable. A character may negotiate a remedy, compare accounts of what they saw, or refuse the word another character uses for a transformation. The next beat can answer the objection itself, supply new information, or change the subject in a way that means something.\n\nKeep the rest of the world moving while the exchange happens: other participants and ongoing business continue their practical lives around the speakers. Preserve whichever roleplay contract is active for player action, perspective, and control exactly as supplied. The wordplay belongs to characters inside the scene, not to an outside narrator commenting on the exchange.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation — while a request is still open, while the other character is deciding how to answer, or while the current speaker has just said more than intended. No forced question, choice screen, cliffhanger, or completed discovery is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story through the intimate urban or domestic route: one small, knowable world — a household, a shop, a neighborhood office, a family obligation maintained between rooms. Stay inside that close social world rather than widening into legend, martial adventure, or satirical panorama.\n\nLet the established fantastic event enter this world with practical consequences. Show how food, work, duty, privacy, or household time changes because of it. The wonder is local — it alters a room, a routine, one family's decisions — and it is treated as fact rather than debated into a system.\n\nMove the situation forward through speech and revised explanation. A neighbor may object to a term, dispute what someone saw, or correct a prior account in front of the person most affected. A family member may avoid naming the true cost of a magical remedy by discussing its price or its side effects. Let these exchanges carry the plot's weight: an argument can divide who is welcome at a meal, a careful definition can permit someone to accept help, and a correction can expose which account a child was allowed to believe.\n\nUse the period, language, and community the setting actually supplies. Do not invent period speech, transplant furniture, or add a generic regional moral; where the setting provides idioms, social expectations, or domestic rules, let them appear in small concrete behavior rather than scenery.\n\nPreserve the difference between the public account and private knowledge. What is convenient to repeat at the door may differ from what a character admits when no guest is present. The narrator may notice an unspoken correction without the story announcing every hidden fact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and short transitions. Render material life where the scene touches it — upkeep, repair, bodies tired from repeated labor, what gets used up and what gets maintained — so wear reads as a consequence of the world's work rather than a required prop list.\n\nLet the fantastic fact press on practical life. A working charm may draw scarce fuel; a strange mark may be checked like a tool for wear; an enchantment may hold in one respect and fail in another. Handle the impossible as fact with its own logic, not as a disguise over an ordinary world.\n\nMake the verbal exchange pressure. Words between people move with the consequence of a seized grip or a withheld object: a contradiction can close a route, and a revised phrasing can undo a promise while both speakers are still standing there. Write corrections as actions taking place. Ground hard speech in its speaker — a cautious character says less and lets the other overreach; a frightened one corrects a false word because the false word is dangerous. Do not hand every character the same quick evasive wit.\n\nVary the rhythm. Let a sentence run long only when someone's reasoning is the scene's stake — working out whether a remedy lasts until morning — and keep the other transitions tight. Softness, wonder, or a small moment of repair is not sabotage, and grit does not convert the world into survival horror.\n\nStop when the immediate pressure is clear. No pursuit, reversal, or completed fight is mandatory at the end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents composed at specific times for particular readers: a letter, ledger note, complaint, inspection record, journal page, order, or unsigned warning. Give each piece a clear author, an addressee or institutional purpose, and a reason to exist, and let the interval between composition and reception matter. A writer's knowledge may be older or narrower than what the recipient needs.\n\nMake wording act inside the document. A definition can be an effort to reassure, protect, or refuse. An objection can warn without naming the danger. A correction can reveal that the writer has reread an earlier report and found a contradiction in it. Let each record show its honesty or failure through its own chosen terms, omissions, and revisions rather than through commentary added afterward.\n\nRemember that a document is what its author chose to write down — an account kept for a reader, not the writer's whole live mind. A journal page can be guarded or self-flattering, and an unsent draft is still an artifact someone composed.\n\nKeep the writers inside a fantasy world. Documents may describe an enchantment, order supplies for a ritual, dispute who is fit to inherit a charged object, or record a wonder in language that later proves misleading. Do not force the records to explain the entire system; keep practical life visible — time, weather, material conditions, work, fear, familiarity, and the limits of the writer's access.\n\nIf gaps, crossings-out, missing pages, or conflicting dates appear, tie each material trace to a consequence in the sequence instead of treating it as a promise the next document will redeem. The story may close while a letter is unanswered or a corrected claim has not yet reached its intended reader.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagataf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet the established fantasy element support ordinary care instead of replacing it. An enchanted convenience, a well-made charm, a familiar portal, or a household spell can belong to someone's routine. Show it touching practical tasks and preferences: how a person likes a door warmed, a meal arranged, a letter duplicated, a troublesome remedy measured.\n\nUse language play as chosen attention. Characters may argue fondly over the right word for a magical household object, correct each other's account of an old journey, or redefine a term so it fits the person in front of them better. A precise question can be gentle; an objection can be a way of making sure someone does not settle for less than they want. Let each character's different way of wording things reveal affection, and let one plain-spoken person's bluntness be its own kind of care.\n\nKeep friction real without turning it into crisis. A disagreement may be familiar rather than weightless. Privacy stays intact: a character may accept kindness while not yet ready to discuss a worry, and the scene does not need to open that worry by force. Whatever problems the wider world holds can persist outside the room while the scene rests on attention given to one particular person.\n\nGround comfort in specific knowledge rather than general warmth — a remembered preference, a small correction, the right object fetched at the right time, proof that someone listened to an earlier explanation. End at a natural resting point; no hug, confession, or repaired relationship is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the scene as novel prose rather than rapid speech or stripped report. Use deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement, with enough distance to move between a speaker's present words and the social history or tacit judgment behind them. Keep the narration distinct from ordinary spoken language. The narrator may frame an exchange without interrupting it for abstract commentary: an account is understood later, or a small public detail illuminates a private reply.\n\nLet the fantasy element belong to the social world. Its consequences appear in how a room is arranged, which work is permitted, who must explain a miracle, and what an institution has decided to ignore. Treat the impossible as fact, not as a fraud the narrator will eventually debunk. Public and private responses may differ within the same scene.\n\nMove the action with verbal contests held inside the composed prose. One speaker disputes the proper name for a phenomenon; another revises a claim about an event to preserve a position or avoid an accusation; a third corrects a minor wording because the minor correction protects something larger. Reserve extended precision for speakers whose stakes or habits call for it — not every minor character is a hyperverbal philosopher, and a plainly worded refusal can end an argument that cleverness prolonged.\n\nKeep the narrator's access consistent with the active point of view: it may observe a gesture, summarize a history, or enter one mind where that is permitted. What the narrator judges and what a character knows stay distinct. A public account can be false in a way the reader recognizes while its speaker still works to make it true for one listener.\n\nThe passage may close after a sustained movement in which a corrected phrase, a refusal to answer, or a moment of recognition shifts the social field. The style supplies no aristocratic setting, confession, or moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBegin with a storyteller who has an audience, an occasion, and a reason to be heard. Make that reason emerge from the fantasy world itself: a promise that binds, a spirit that must be kept listening, a bargain that can be renegotiated through an account, or a place where a named thing gains power. Treat the outer scene as live action rather than a wrapper—when listeners interrupt, question, or shift their trust, those are events in the story with their own weight.\n\nInside a tale, make language the instrument that does the work. A definition, a contradiction, or a revised wording changes what a character within the tale can do; the teller may claim, retract, clarify, or reinterpret a word or oath, and the cost of that move registers in the outer scene. Give speakers different verbal habits and stakes: one argues in legal distinctions, another in folk maxims, another asks questions that sound like agreement but are not.\n\nUse recurrence to build meaning across levels. A phrase, object, condition, or rule stated in the frame can return inside a tale as a changed term the audience now understands differently. When a fact is withheld, let its eventual revelation alter what an earlier statement meant rather than only adding new information.\n\nKeep the levels of knowledge distinct: what the teller knows now is not what the characters inside the tale knew then. The teller may be unreliable without being random—contradictions should expose something the teller is trying to win, conceal, or decide—and the audience's judgment is part of the method. A listener may accept a false account for a plausible reason or reject a true one for an understandable reason. The teller may also carry a private stake the audience only partly infers.\n\nThe frame may sit in any fantasy setting that supports it; import no djinn, palaces, deserts, or particular religion from the Nights craft source, and keep the fantastic premise whatever the established world already provides. A tale told here can persuade, delay, warn, entertain, or change how the listener reads the outer situation, and any such change should arise from the specific words and stakes of that telling rather than from a cliffhanger compelled at every level. The scene can end while a tale is still unfolding, or while the audience is still deciding what to believe.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nCompose in the Romantic and uncanny route. Give the narration a distance from the fantastic event that is part of the event's meaning: the teller need not be omniscient or neutral, and the prose may linger at the threshold between familiar explanation and admitted strangeness. The fantasy element is a fact of the world, not a hallucination to be diagnosed, yet its consequences arrive filtered through a sensibility that keeps testing words against experience.\n\nMake self-correction a narrative engine. The teller may call a thing by one name, withdraw it, and supply a better one; state a rule, then qualify it until its edge is visible. These revisions are not decorative hesitations—each should alter what can be claimed about the fantasy premise or its cost. Keep the movement legible on the page: a sentence that begins with certainty may end in a narrower, stranger, or more exact claim.\n\nGive those who speak distinct verbal habits. A scholar argues by definition, a traveler by remembered local phrasing, a child by analogy, and a contradiction between them can expose a different stake, a false memory, or a true difference in how the world's magic is known. Let limitation and plain speech carry weight; not everyone is a philosopher.\n\nUse syntax to manage pace and uncertainty. Longer, clause-heavy sentences gather possible meanings before a decisive term; short sentences arrest attention where something impossible simply remains present. Syntax follows the narrator, not a signal that the text should feel translated or nationally severe. Use the actual setting's scenery and institutions, and import no folk-tale collections or bureaucratic weight the established world does not supply.\n\nKeep the fantastic premise a condition of life rather than a puzzle awaiting the teller's wit. Wonder sits beside work, danger, family, travel, or personal discovery, and the method is to make its terms increasingly exact through language that revises itself. Stop at the boundary the narration has actually reached; an unresolved qualification is a legitimate place to end, and no compulsory revelation is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagataj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite inherited language as a material force in the setting. Let oaths, titles, old legal formulas, family phrases, and formal definitions persist in present speech and carry consequences: the fantasy premise may make such language binding, dangerous, or merely socially powerful. A character may use an old term to open a door, command obedience, or wound; a retraction, mispronunciation, or substitution can alter that power.\n\nBuild encounters as verbal contests that expose the legacy behind appearances. A modern office, market, apartment, or transit space can sit beside an older authority, and when two characters argue, make their stakes visible: one may rely on a term the other has been conditioned to obey. The contest need not be equal and need not end in a witty victory—someone can lose a word and suffer for it, or win one and find the win sharpens their danger.\n\nKeep intimacy morally complicated. Treat desire, loyalty, fear, and old love as parts of the power arrangement. Trusted company can allow a mask to fall, and what the fall reveals need not be safe. Make urban material detail specific—the quality of a lock, the lighting over a doorbell, the smell of old paper or engine oil—so that atmosphere changes how a particular encounter is experienced rather than decorating the page.\n\nLet unstable self-presentation matter causally. A character under pressure may revise a statement or adopt a role; another may notice the shift and act on it, changing what the first can claim, what others owe, or what the old power recognizes. State the speech and its risks; do not tell the reader what to feel.\n\nThe inherited force may be magical, contractual, or customary, but it must arise from the established fantasy world and stay legible through the language the characters use. Import no vampire physiology, clan structures, disciplines, or blood dependency. Stop at the scene's natural boundary—an interruption, a retreat, a held silence is enough—without forcing a confession or a completed confrontation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, frame-tale route. Make the storytelling an occasion with a local audience and a recognizable purpose: a dispute to settle, a night to pass, a warning to give, an honor to defend, or a question a child has asked. Set the telling in a time and place from the established fantasy world, and let language, terms of respect, and reference points come from the community actually present. Do not relocate the cast to a generic 'Indian' landscape or insert religious or caste meaning the setting does not supply.\n\nTreat an embedded tale as a verbal move. A teller may use a story to delay a decision, persuade a powerful listener, correct a mistaken word, or trap an opponent into accepting a term, and the fantasy premise can make that move binding: where an oath, name, curse, or blessing has force, the exact wording inside the tale matters, and a small change in the retelling alters what the listeners now accept.\n\nRun the language games inside the frame. A character may interrupt to object to a definition, ask what a word meant in the old tongue, or point out that the teller has contradicted an earlier version; the teller may accept, revise, or double down, and each exchange can shift the relation between audience and teller. Give speakers distinct habits—an elder in paired sayings, a merchant in practical analogy, a sibling in pointed questions—and let them be unequally eloquent and unequally reliable.\n\nUse recurrence as structure. A phrase, object, condition, or duty named in the frame can reappear inside a tale with changed force, so the reader sees the outer event differently rather than merely remembering the earlier mention. A delayed revelation can alter what an earlier tale meant. Keep the levels distinct: the outside is one present scene; each embedded tale is a composed story with its own time and teller; a listener's question returns the scene to the outer level.\n\nKeep the fantastic premise an actual condition that affects how stories are believed, remembered, or enforced, while wonder still sits beside family, work, travel, or personal discovery; not every tale needs to be a puzzle. A telling may end with a definite change in the outer scene—trust granted, time gained, a term corrected, a fear named—or while the dispute is still open and the next tale untold. Neither a mandatory cliffhanger nor a moral summary is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tale route. Narrate from a participant or close observer whose personal language governs how the impossible enters the telling. The fantasy element is actually present and needs no explaining away; what is uncanny is the teller's relation to it—how it gets named, how that name gets admitted to be wrong, and what closer word replaces it. Let the narration follow that movement.\n\nMake self-correction the engine of revelation. When the teller says, 'It was not a curse. It was more like a promise that remembered itself,' the revision should read as an improvement in understanding, not indecision, and each adjustment can change what the scene means: a spirit first called a shadow, then a debt, then a relative, until the sequence discloses what the family or community had refused to say. Keep the corrections progressive—each one narrows the possible meaning or shifts its burden rather than endlessly qualifying.\n\nGive other speakers different verbal habits and stakes. A priest may speak in fixed phrases, a child in concrete observations, a neighbor in rumors that turn out to contain one accurate term. An objection to the teller's wording should matter: it can expose a hidden lesson, a fear, or a social rule. The aim is not universal banter but language that reveals particular positions under pressure.\n\nKeep the actual setting and period of the established fantasy world. Import no generic Japanese atmosphere, seasonal cliché, or duty template; if the world includes seasonal or domestic detail, let it arise from the story's own place and time, and if the world has no Japanese culture, the method still works as a fantastic-tale form without imported scenery or social structure. The teller's voice may be plain, somewhat formal, or blunt—preserve it as it is.\n\nLet the premise affect ordinary life: how a household keeps time, how children are named, what the teller can promise, shown in concrete action. The narration can stop at any revision, including one whose implications are still settling. A stable understanding—or a stable refusal to understand—may have arrived; neither is required before the scene ends, and no tidy moral, completed quest, or sudden explanation is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the marvelous as ordinary, and only where the established fantasy world already supports it. If the world contains a condition its people accept without alarm—a talking river, a saint's return, a ghost who works, a debt that walks—treat it with the same plainness as weather, rent, or a neighbor's temper. Do not smuggle supernatural facts into a world that excludes them, and do not treat the marvelous as a metaphor waiting to be decoded.\n\nMake language the field of negotiation. Even ordinary marvels require naming, borrowing, praise, complaint, and legal defense, so run the verbal contests inside that social texture: a market argument over what the ghost must be called, a correction of a child's phrasing of a promise, a trade of one word for another because the different word changes an obligation. Self-correction here is a social skill, not a symptom of unreliability—a speaker who says 'It is not that she returns. She was never allowed to leave' has made the scene's point.\n\nPosition each speaker in language: one speaks the terms of local work, another of official paperwork, another of family memory, and their disagreements show what they stand to gain or lose. A focal character may privately weigh a term differently than they voice it, for reasons the scene supports. The narration need not explain the marvelous; it can explain the consequences of a disputed term—a loan called a 'gift' may stop the ghost from working, calling it a 'wage' may start her again.\n\nPreserve the language, community, and history actually present in the story. Do not insert a generic magical small town, Catholic ritual, or code-switching unless the active setting and cast already include them; if the story has a specific place and period, let those carry the social memory and material texture.\n\nLet the premise reach decisions, institutions, and family life, with wonder beside work, danger, travel, or personal discovery. A verbal exchange may shift something concrete—a debt, a permission, a name, a way of understanding—or the scene may close with the argument still open. Neither a mandatory twist nor a revelation that the marvel was a metaphor is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Light Novel\n\nEnter with a clear immediate situation, location, and active character. Keep spatial changes explicit—who stands where, who holds what, who can hear whom—so a verbal move can trigger its consequence without a lore pause. Convey the fantasy premise through use and reaction; explanation arrives only when the scene needs it.\n\nRun the encounter as a language contest with stakes in the scene's direction. A corrected definition, a denied implication, or a reworded condition can produce a visible result: an item shifts, a barrier opens, a guard steps aside, a deal becomes possible or fails. Follow with the viewpoint character's private response in a focused reaction beat—what the realization feels like in the body and in the next decision—rather than an essay. Break paragraphs so action, dialogue, and private thought stay distinct and scannable.\n\nGive speakers quick, differentiated verbal habits: one answers honestly but in a narrow sense; another asks dangerous questions in a mild tone; another overcorrects and must qualify the correction. The duel need not be evenly matched—a character can lose a word and get hurt, or win and find the win useful but costly. Let the viewpoint character notice a loophole too late and revise their own statement; that revision can turn the scene.\n\nA reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Keep private thought tight and anchored in the immediate situation while it still carries real weight—the unknown may remain unknown while present pressure does its work.\n\nDo not impose a young cast, first-person narration, comedy, or a cliffhanger unless the active story already calls for them; this container is about readable staging and pacing, not a demographic. Stop wherever the scene's natural boundary falls—mid-exchange, on a held reaction, with a new pressure or without one—since no final wit line and no resolved mystery are owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite language-driven contests as passages whose rhythm and imagery do the analytical work. When a speaker objects to a term or offers a revised wording, let the sentence grow looser or tighter, let a recurring sound settle into the new claim, and let one developed image carry the shift in what the correction makes possible. The verbal operation should land on the reader's senses, not only on the proposition: a retracted name might leave the sentence quieter, a restored name might brighten a familiar detailu detail.\n\nChoose images from the immediate scene and let the narrator's attention move by association rather than inventory. A comparison may show how an argument changes the speaker's place in a room, the weight of an object, or the feel of the air. If a word is redefined, let the next sentence walk through what the old sense used to name and what the new sense can now include. Keep the lyrical tissue tied to particular spoken stakes; do not let every object become symbolic or every exchange dissolve into abstract reflection.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the elevated cadence with its own syntax. A speaker may answer a rhythmic definition with a deliberately plain practical sentence, and the contrast itself becomes part of the contest. Plain statements can mark the moment when a claim stops being ornamental and becomes exact. The style is not a requirement for permanent solemnity or for every line to sound like the narrator's inner monologue; individual characters may use shorter words, uneven sentences, or ordinary denial.\n\nIn a fantasy setting, make the poetic attention reveal the consequence of a fantastic term, oath, or magical practice rather than decorating it. If a verbal correction changes what a promise can do, show that change in the sentence's movement: the repeated sound may tighten around the obligation, or a stated impossibility may become a concrete image with real pressure. The narrator may find meaning in recurrence, but a mystery need not be dissolved into explanation. Let the passage end where the rhythm naturally turns, even if the argument continues beyond the current reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse a speaker's correction, objection, or revised wording as the hinge of a reflective passage. A character who says \"that is not the word\" or \"I used to call it something else\" opens the space between a past use and the present meaning. Let the narrator notice what has gone out of the word: a promise no longer made, a place that has changed, a name that now belongs only to memory. The verbal argument need not be solved; its residue is the point.\n\nKeep the reflection attached to a small concrete marker in the fantasy world. A changed epithet for a familiar street, a term of service that has shifted its obligation, or a magical word whose force has faded can carry the sense of passing time without a lecture on transience. Let the narrator compare the present scene with one remembered occasion, but allow the present moment to keep its own texture: the food still tastes, the task still asks to be finished, the person still stands in the room.\n\nThe melancholic treatment does not require every exchange to end in loss. A character may correct another and feel the correction as care; a memory may arrive while washing a cup and be put away again. Let the language-driven habit remain present: a speaker may disagree with a prior version of an event, and the disagreement itself becomes a way of remembering. Do not insist that reflection expose a hidden betrayal or that every attachment be unfinished.\n\nIn a fantasy setting, let the impossible element be an occasion for retrospection rather than a puzzle to solve. A levitating quill may now remind the narrator of the person who once used it; a protective charm may have lost its exact wording through years of repetition. Write those recollections as they arise from the present scene, without a flood of nostalgia. End the passage when the association has done its work, even if the present task is not complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagataq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral narration in which a teller renders a story to an audience, and the Nisio Isin verbal moves happen at the level of the frame as well as inside the tale. Have the teller open with a preparatory phrase or a claim about the story's meaning. Then let an audience member object to a term, propose a correction, or ask what a word really means. The teller may accept the revision and continue, refuse it and insist on the old wording, or incorporate the audience's phrasing into a later repetition. This interplay makes the frame an active contest rather than a passive container.\n\nWithin the nested story, let a character perform the same operation: a formal greeting may be reinterpreted, an oath may be argued over syllable by syllable, a nickname may be stripped of its apparent honor. The framed structure also allows the outer teller to interrupt and correct a description: \"No, that is not what the vizier said; that is what the merchant later claimed.\" This exposes the account's reliability and keeps the audience aware that memory has been revised.\n\nSelect a non-European, Islamic-majority setting or an appropriate regional context without requiring any single scriptural, legal, or mystical framework. The fantasy element may be inscribed in the narrative as a permitted enchantment, a djinn-like presence, a ruined place with an altered name, or an in-world tale whose magic acts on those who repeat its terms. The tradition's rhetorical ornament can serve the verbal contest: a repeated phrase, a rhymed proverb, or a formal address can become the very sentence whose meaning is challenged and revised. Keep the prose alert to this rhetorical quality without fabricating a uniform \"Eastern\" voice.\n\nUse the frame to manage disclosure. A teller may withhold a term until the audience asks, then produce it as a small revelation. A memory that appears early may be corrected later. The outer audience's challenges should not be comic relief by default; they can be as consequential as the inner story's debates. Write the negotiations between frame and tale as genuine language-driven encounters, not as decorative interruptions. End a passage when the latest correction or repeated phrase has registered with the audience, leaving the next segment of the tale still to be told.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Minimalist Prose\n\nLet a single correction, objection, or redefinition carry the causal information that other prose would explain in a paragraph. When a speaker says \"Not brother; debtor\" or \"The charm was not protection. Insurance,\" the reader receives the changed relation, the altered obligation, and the prior misreading through the chosen word. Do not follow the correction with an explanation unless the fresh implication genuinely requires one more short clause. The contrast between the old and new term is the story's movement.\n\nUse only the minimal clauses necessary for a fantasy element to have consequences. If a magical writing appears and a character corrects its title, the later events may hinge on that nomenclature. Let the narrator notice one visual detail—an ink still wet, a cup in the wrong place—and pair it with one verbal turn. The style permits silence around authority: a speaker may refuse to explain a term, and the refusal itself is a refusal to revise.\n\nKeep the prose direct. Prefer short finite sentences and avoid redundant synonyms. A precise extended sentence may still serve the style if its complexity arises from a genuine causal connection rather than from decoration. The wordplay remains active but compressed: one character may correct a syllable, another may answer with a single dry contradiction. The correction can feel final, preliminary, or self-serving; the text need not announce which.\n\nIn this combination, omit the reflective commentary that another style might include. Let the reader infer the speaker's state from the direct statement and the correction. Dialogue can carry the full contest without extended thought tags. The fantasy element should remain determinate rather than vague: the narrator may state that the second name bound the spirit because the wording mattered. Write the argument at the level of exact diction, so every deleted word increases the pressure on the words that remain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nUse the novelistic paragraph to move among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience while the characters conduct a verbal contest. A scene may begin with a practical task—paying a bill, waiting for rain, repairing a lock—and a speaker's correction then shifts what the task means. The narrator may watch a hand pause on a cup, hear a word repeated with different emphasis, and recall a previous occasion when the same term was used. The self-correction need not halt the scene; it can emerge within an ongoing movement.\n\nLet differing speech habits shape the contest. One character may use legal or bureaucratic phrases, another slang, another careful definitions. Their ways of objecting and revising should come from their social positions and immediate goals. An unreliable account may expose itself when the narrator compares the spoken version with a remembered detail, but do not have the narrator announce the unreliability as a theorem; let the discrepancy sit in the prose.\n\nTreat the fantasy element as an established condition of the world, not a hidden device. If a neighbor's charm prevents certain promises from being spoken, or a municipal golem is arguing with its keeper, the verbal confrontation should include that fantastic concreteness. The scene can integrate magical practicalities with domestic life: a character may correct the spell's old wording, and the new wording may change the lock, the meal, or the debt. Keep the prose uncluttered by explaining the entire system; the scene only needs what the characters need.\n\nParagraph transitions may orient without recap. Write a sentence that moves from one speaker's face to the room, then to the narrator's thought, then to a memory, then back to the present exchange. The narrator's voice can be reflective, dry, or precise, but the syntax should feel continuous rather than stitched from fragments. Let the scene end at a natural pause: a question unanswered, a task resumed, a word still being tested. The language-driven tension may remain unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the couple's or central relationship's established private language as the site of Nisio Isin verbal contests. They may have a name for a particular kind of argument, a phrase one of them hates, or a correction ritual: \"Not 'fine.' — 'Mending.'\" Each new scene can repeat such a phrase and then let one partner revise what it means. The callback need pay off emotionally rather than only intellectually; a term that began as teasing may return as comfort, jealousy, or admission.\n\nLet a scene selection be driven by relationship stakes. Choose a moment that tests the premise: a temporary separation, a feared misunderstanding, a favor asked, an old wound named. The fantasy setting should remain causally present. A magical bond may make spoken falsehoods visible, or a familiar spirit may report one partner's words to the other. The verbal corrections then become charged because the words have specific consequences for the relationship, not because every dialogue is a battle.\n\nAllow affection and anger to move at different speeds. A character may correct a partner's term and simultaneously touch their hand; another may refuse to accept a revised version and thus refuse the comfort it offers. The narrator may enter one partner's private thought, but keep it authorized and bounded. Write the interiority that the current narrative allows. The emotional payoff should come from the particular promise of the relationship—reconciliation, rivalry, discovery, or commitment—not from a generic happy ending or compulsory crisis.\n\nDo not import a standard list of fanfiction tropes unless they arise from the actual supplied premise. If the premise involves a forced proximity through a magical storm, write the storm's pressure through the couple's debate over whether to call it a curse or a rescue. The verbal self-correction becomes a way for one partner to reveal what they believed and for the other to revise it. The prose may be scene-paced and close, with clear physical details supporting the emotional beat. End at a point where the repeated phrase has shifted meaning or remains between them, leaving the relationship's next move open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nLet the chronicle open with a compact verbal deed: an oath sworn in a certain form, a name given under specified conditions, a settlement phrased so that its later meaning can be contested. Then tell how that formula was repeated, disputed, or revised in subsequent generations. The recurrence is the memory. When a later character says \"He was not named for the mountain but for the debt,\" the saga should present that correction as a new act with its own consequence.\n\nUse deliberate openings and public declarations in the telling. The narrator may begin with the accepted version, then introduce a contradiction: \"So the hall remembers it; but the keeper of the accounts used another word.\" Do not always resolve which version is true. The strength of the saga voice can hold two accounts together, letting the disagreement itself be what is remembered. Let oaths, kinship, gifts, feuds, and journeys carry the language-driven contest rather than be peripheral decoration.\n\nThe fantasy element should be formally announced in the chronicle. A sword with a conditional name, a land-gift that changes when its description changes, or a spell that obliges only the exact wording of the original promise fits this combination. Show the magical consequence in the retelling: if a contract is rephrased, the chronicle can record that the land altered its boundary or the spirit accepted the new clause. Do not make every recurrence a mere echo; each repetition should be a possible argument about meaning.\n\nKeep the particular source tradition coherent. If the setting draws on an oral-epic convention from a particular region, do not mix in unrelated legends or anachronistic formal systems. The prose may use fuller rhythms and public framing but should not become wordy for its own sake. Dialogue inside the saga can be economical; a character may object with one plain sentence. The chronicle voice may then return to describe what that objection did. End the passage when the latest verbal revision has been entered into the account, leaving later consequences to be told.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as a situated oral narrator speaking to a present listener, in the regional idiom the story's world establishes—Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish—not a generic Nordic accent. The impossible premise is already part of communal memory, so the narrator recounts it as something that happened and continues to impose practical costs. Ground each telling in domestic and social labor: milking, mending, paying the priest, walking home in winter light. Let the wonder sit inside that labor rather than beside it.\n\nMake the narrator's self-correction the engine of the telling. When the narrator revises a wording, admits a contradiction, or separates what was seen from what was reported later, that correction must change the account's consequence: the revised version reveals who benefited from the earlier wording, which obligation was misstated, or what the narrator had reason to hide. Do not correct for mere precision; correct when the first version was doing work the narrator now wants undone.\n\nRender remembered speech as a contest with practical stakes. Each speaker keeps a distinct verbal habit—a farmer argues over a debt in sums, a parish clerk in recorded terms, a bailiff in obligations, a stranger in names that must not be spoken. A definition, objection, or revised wording inside that dialogue changes what the characters do to one another: who may cross a boundary, whose promise binds, what must be paid. The narrator may report the speech without sharing the speakers' inner minds; interiority enters through what the narrator chooses to recall, omit, or amend, and through the reasons visible in what people say and do.\n\nTreat the impossible fact as established, not explained. A boundary that cannot be crossed except by invitation, a child whose first word comes true, or a dead person's promise that still holds is real; the narrator may dispute how it worked, but not whether it happened. Show its lived cost in work, household, travel, or inheritance. The telling may end when the narrator reaches a stable account, admits a newly relevant detail, or passes a question to the listener—not when a revelation must be made or a cliffhanger forced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagataw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Screenplay\n\nWrite in screenplay form: scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines, and clear speaker cues. Establish the fantasy premise through what the camera can see or the soundtrack can hear—a door that answers a riddle, a shadow that moves against the light, an object that refuses to be named—and make its consequence immediate: someone must act, work, negotiate, or risk something because of it. Do not explain the premise in narration; let its effects appear in warnings, debts, rituals of avoidance, or physical marks.\n\nMake each verbal move actable by pairing it with a visible or audible consequence. When a character corrects a term, challenges a definition, or contradicts a previous statement, the action line records what changes in the room: who steps back, who picks up an object, who stops mid-task, who lowers a voice. The audience learns from that pairing—a pause before an answer, a speaker rephrasing a legal formulation under pressure—not from a shot list or camera command.\n\nGive each speaker a distinct verbal habit tied to a stake. One bargains by redefining terms, another argues by exact quotation, another turns the subject into a riddle. The contest changes what someone will do next: open a door, pay a price, end a silence, leave the room. Do not let every minor character speak like a philosopher; their speech should fit the practical scene they are in.\n\nUse voice-over or offscreen sound only when the sequence needs them. If required private thought must appear, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the action block, not as dialogue another character can hear. A scene may end with the contest unresolved, but the final exchange or action should make the next possible direction legible—without forcing a suspense cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with speaker-led dialogue and economical stage directions. Establish the fantasy premise through physical presence—an object that changes when misnamed, a candle that burns without heat, a chalk circle that cannot be crossed—and make its consequence visible in the scene's business. If the premise is spoken but not shown, have a character react to it in a way the audience can see: recoiling, covering an ear, setting down a tool, refusing to repeat a word.\n\nJoin each verbal move to staged business. When a character redefines a contract, corrects a quotation, objects to a word's implication, or revises an earlier admission, the stage direction records the physical change that follows: one character steps out of the circle, another picks up the knife, another closes the ledger. The line and the movement together show who has gained or lost standing, not merely what someone feels.\n\nGive each speaker a distinct verbal habit tied to a practical stake. One speaks in obligations, another in riddles, another in plain refusals. The contest changes what they will do next: accept an oath, forbid a name, revise a bargain. Do not use film-camera instructions. Monologues and asides may appear only when they serve the audience's dramatic understanding, not as a replacement for every private thought. If the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, include it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the stage directions, not as a line someone onstage hears.\n\nEnd the scene when the verbal contest reaches a new standing—an oath accepted, a name forbidden, a bargain revised—not necessarily with a revelation. The fantasy element remains real but unexplained, shaping practical work and relationships rather than delivering an exposition scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized mind whose associative movement carries the story. Braid perception, memory, private speech, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern; let paragraph breaks and syntax follow the mind's turns rather than a linear report. The fantasy premise is already real for this mind, appearing through remembered warnings, a persistent wrongness in the body, an object that insists on another name, or a debt that returns every evening. Do not explain it as a system; let its consequences press on work, family, travel, or solitude, and let the mind keep coming back to it without resolving it into a clean account.\n\nMake self-correction the mind's private verbal contest. A thought may begin, \"They called it a bargain,\" then stop: no, a bargain means both sides consented; what happened was a word with a different weight. The mind catches an inaccuracy, offers a revised definition, or contradicts an earlier recollection, and that correction alters what the mind is willing to do next. The correction is not performed for anyone; it is the mind working on its own account.\n\nKeep recalled speakers distinct in memory. The neighbor is quoted literally, the priest rendered as a single question, the stranger as a name that must not be repeated. Only the focal mind's associations are continuously available; other minds are not entered. Use enough concrete markers—a road, a kitchen, a name—to distinguish what is happening from what is remembered or imagined. If the focal character is speaking aloud, mark that speech clearly, because the current orientation is interior.\n\nWonder enters as a physical unease, a repeated phrase from childhood, a shape at the edge of a remembered room. Do not force a revelation; the passage may end when the mind reaches a temporary rest, a new self-correction, or an interruption from outside.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagataz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent the story as sender-attributed messages with only the interface details that matter: timestamps when they change the meaning, delays when someone hesitates, edits or receipts when a correction is public, attachments when an object must be shown. The fantasy premise is established before or within the exchange so that the messages bear on it: a debt that must be settled by a certain hour, a name that cannot be typed without consequence, a boundary that only one person may cross. Show the premise's effect through what correspondents ask, refuse, bargain over, or avoid saying; do not stop the transcript for a prose lecture.\n\nMake each message a move in a language-driven contest. A definition, objection, contradiction, or revised wording changes what the participants are willing to do—one person redefines \"owed,\" another disputes the word \"safe,\" another retracts a promise under a new term. Let the interface carry the correction: an edit that replaces one word with another, a quoted earlier message with a crucial omission, a delay before a reply that changes its force. Each person keeps distinct digital and verbal habits—one writes in full sentences and corrects herself immediately, another uses fragments and leaves errors, another quotes earlier messages with legal exactness.\n\nKeep the reader oriented about the difference between transmitted text and anything outside the exchange. If brief context is needed, clearly separate it from the transcript—for example, a short scene note above a block of messages. An unsent draft appears only when it matters to the effect of the sent message; it is a composed artifact, not a reliable window into live private thought. Do not fabricate timestamps, receipts, or platform features merely to create subtext. The section may end when a message changes the standing between correspondents, when someone goes silent, or when a correction is left unresolved; no one is required to send a cliffhanger or a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in visual-novel rhythm: concise scene-setting, clear dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response that suits an illustrated scene. Establish the fantasy premise through concrete details that would visibly belong to the scene—a charmed door, a familiar bird that speaks only on certain days, a jar that must not be counted—and keep its consequences tied to what characters ask, avoid, or bargain over. Do not explain the premise in a lore dump; let the narrator's focal attention pick out the relevant object or background cue, then let dialogue pressure it.\n\nPace the verbal contest as discrete beats. A speech line opens with a claim, the focal character's inner response tests a definition, notices a contradiction, or revises a previous assumption, and the next line or visible consequence changes the standing between speakers. A line may correct itself: \"It's not that I can't open it. I can't be the one who opens it.\" Different characters maintain different verbal habits—one defines, one objects, one changes the topic into a riddle—so that a conversation feels like competition with personal stakes. The focal character's inner response remains private and does not enter the dialogue until uttered.\n\nDo not add branching menus, romance routes, images, or engine commands simply because the visual-novel style is selected. If a background or expression cue is mentioned, use it to orient the reader now—a room's coldness, a character's look before speaking—not as a guaranteed truth indicator. Scene changes should be legible, but each new scene should carry at least one new pressure on the verbal contest: a person arrives, a deadline is named, an object is moved, or someone changes the terms. Close when the exchange produces a new standing, an unresolved correction, or a visible consequence, without a forced confession or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagatbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Nisio Isin / Web Serial\n\nMaintain an installment-oriented through-line built from an established fantasy premise and Nisio Isin's verbal contests. At the start of each new reply, briefly recover only the fact that now matters—the exact wording of a bargain, who must not be named, or what was last agreed—rather than retelling past chapters. The premise has already happened and continues to impose practical costs; do not spend the installment explaining the magic system. Let characters work, argue, travel, or care for someone under the shadow of the ongoing impossible fact.\n\nAdvance the plot through language-driven actions. A character may redefine a debt at a crucial moment, quote an earlier promise with a crucial omission, or correct a term so that a new obligation becomes binding. Each speaker has a distinct verbal habit and different stakes, and the correction or objection must change what someone is willing to do next: stop a journey, open a door, pay a price, end a silence. Keep the focal character's private reasoning available as ordinary prose where the active contract allows, but do not make every scene a public debate; some progress occurs because someone remembers a word differently.\n\nUse earned developments, not forced cliffs. A reply may end after a new term is introduced, a character leaves with new information, or an old definition returns under different conditions. That re-entry point lets the next installment resume without inventing a reversal. Occasional hooks are fine, but every turn must not become a complete miniature chapter. The serial form does not create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy rules; progress here means that the terms of a fantasy consequence are being refined by successive conversations, and the reader can re-enter with the shared thread of what remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the impossible premise as the thing that makes emotional exposure expensive. When a character admits need, withholds an apology, or returns to a difficult relationship, the fantastic element changes what that move costs or risks: a binding oath may make a confession enforceable, an enchantment may expose a feeling the speaker meant to hide, a magical debt may turn an act of care into a claim. Render that cost through the actual exchange, not through a narrator's announcement that the moment is painful.\n\nUse the selected narrator's distance and diction to carry turbulence. A first-person narrator may circle a withheld sentence, correct a softened version, or notice the body's reaction before naming the feeling. A more distant narrator may record the interruption, the delayed reply, the practical consequence that follows a failed apology. Let strain live in the mechanics of conversation: what is said too late, what is answered only in part, what is offered and not accepted. Warmth can arrive in the same scene as pain—a real gesture of care that is still too small to repair the hurt it touches.\n\nKeep private thought inside the focal character's knowledge. Other characters respond to what they observe, misunderstand, or choose to believe. Do not script a betrayal, confession, collapse, or tragic ending. The fantastic element changes what people can do and what they risk; it does not guarantee a particular emotional outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene as a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs. Attribute speech unmistakably: use the active speaker's name, pronoun, or established action marker, and keep the POV and player-control settings active without forcing first person or executable commands.\n\nLet the supplied fantasy premise be an active condition, not backdrop. Derive the specific effect from the established premise and the current scene: show how the impossible element changes what a character can do, what they must handle, or what they avoid, and let its practical consequences keep the world and NPCs moving even when neither the player nor the narrator pauses for it. An established enchantment, resource, or impossible fact should shape the offer, obstacle, or task at hand rather than appear as a generic spell list.\n\nEnd the reply at a natural continuation point that follows from what has already occurred. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the boundary. Leave the scene open through an unfinished action, an unanswered practical need, or the next step the characters can take, without announcing that openness as a prompt to the user.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Arrange chapters or sections around a recurring fantastic condition: an impossible object, ability, or local rule that alters how people in a particular community buy, work, marry, worship, travel, or settle disputes. For each episode, choose the consequence that the specific situation makes legible—a market dispute over an enchanted measure, a marriage negotiation complicated by an impossible obligation, a travel route changed by a local rule—and show the ordinary labor or ceremony that the premise disrupts or sustains.\n\nKeep the telling direct and concrete rather than mythic or mythopoetic. Name the people involved and let consequences accumulate across episodes: a later episode may refer to an earlier one, a character may have learned how to work around the premise, or a community may have changed its practice because of it. Each installment should be readable as part of a continuing local chronicle, not as a puzzle whose full meaning arrives only at the end.\n\nDo not relocate the cast to another country or period. Use the setting, language, and community already supplied. The style supplies a narrative form and social attention; it does not automatically install a particular dynasty, landscape, or moral system.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nRender the fantasy element as a hard material fact. Write it through cost, wear, maintenance, failure, and practical use, not through awe or abstract explanation. Short sentences and concrete verbs should show the impossible working in the character's hands: a charm that stings when it works, a changing route that eats boot leather, a magical debt that comes due as work.\n\nChange rhythm when the character needs extended reasoning about risk, cost, or consequence. Let the longer thought track the material stakes already in the scene: how a decision affects tools, money, food, or the people who rely on them, and what the character can afford to lose or repair. Keep the thought plain and physical rather than introspective commentary.\n\nDo not turn every hardship into survival or gore. The style is pressure-oriented, but the fantasy premise should remain a usable part of the world. Let it fail, wear out, or demand something, and let those demands shape a scene without requiring every reply to end in disaster.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Epistolary / Documents\n\nBuild the story from composed documents, not from a story about documents. Each entry must have a specific author, recipient or intended purpose, and time of composition. Let the fantasy premise appear through what each writer knows, uses, reports, omits, or misunderstands in that situation: a border officer's note about a changed tax, a teacher's lesson plan referencing an impossible law, a merchant's letter complaining how an enchantment has altered travel or credit.\n\nUse the gap between documents to move the story. A later report may correct an earlier letter, a journal entry may reveal only the emotional aftermath of an event already recorded elsewhere, and a material trace such as a torn page, marginal note, or copied correction can show that the record itself has passed through interested hands. Do not add an omniscient explanation after each record; let the reader hold conflicting accounts until the next document resolves or deepens them.\n\nPreserve readable continuity. When the active scene requires a specific form such as a letter, ledger, prayer record, complaint, or inventory, select the features that form's purpose and the writer's situation actually call for: a salutation where the recipient requires one, an item list where the record's function is accounting, a seal description where authority or authenticity matters, a line for correction where the document is a working draft. Do not rotate through all available document types mechanically, and do not convert every entry into a diary even when a personal record would fit the fictional moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nUse an impossible element the way a warm story uses a familiar food, soft garment, or dependable room: let it give a specific small comfort, save labor, ease a difficulty, or mark a preference. Keep the fantasy active through material particulars—a spell that warms the bath, a tiny creature that finds lost keys, a potion that settles an old ache for one evening—not through cute abstraction.\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness. Describe the exact sensation, the way a person accepts or refuses the help, and the quiet aftermath. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. A character may enjoy being cared for without discussing it, and a private thought may show the pleasure without turning it into a plot point.\n\nDo not flatten personality, disagreement, or established problems. A comforting scene can sit beside a warning, an unresolved debt, or an argument left for later. When friction enters, let it come from the fantasy element's limits: the spell eases one ache but not the cause, the creature finds the key but not the locked room's occupant, the potion lasts one evening and the worry returns with morning. The style does not make everyone agreeable or remove danger, grief, or consequence from the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the narration in deliberate paragraphs. Use sustained transitions rather than short scene beats, and let the sentence movement distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language. Where the active point of view permits, frame the fantasy premise through social context, history, or judgment: a village's old arrangement with a magical river, a particular family's inherited obligation, a civic ceremony that renews an impossible guarantee, or a dispute over what an old contract still means.\n\nKeep the impossible element active in ordinary life, not only as an aristocratic symbol or ancient legend. Let the social frame determine which domain the premise touches: if the scene concerns property, show how the premise changes inheritance or use; if it concerns marriage, show how an obligation alters a negotiation; if it concerns memory, show how an institution keeps or erases a record. An institution may keep records of the premise, a neighbor may treat it as a known inconvenience, and a character may weigh its personal cost in the same paragraph that shows its public form.\n\nUse public and private contrast when the scene supports it: a formal event may press on an unspoken private fear, or a remembered conversation may change the meaning of a present obligation. Do not force ceremony, correspondence, or archaic diction if the setting and community do not call for them. The style supplies formal architecture, not an aristocratic cast or a uniformly elaborate voice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nAnchor the story in a telling that has a specific audience, occasion, and practical purpose. The established fantasy premise must already be active in the world, but present it through the teller's shaped account: what they choose to relate, what they omit, and how listeners respond. Do not merely describe a magical setting. Make a definite impossible condition—an obligation, transformation, curse, artifact, or altered law—bear on the present situation, and let the telling become the means by which that condition is understood, resisted, or accepted.\n\nUse each embedded tale as an intervention in the outer frame. Before the tale begins, establish what the teller wants from the listeners and what the listeners need from the telling. Then let the tale's content work on that need: it may persuade, warn, delay, or reframe the listener's understanding of the active premise. After the tale, render the effect in the listener's private response where narration permits, then in a changed question, decision, or posture toward the teller. Keep the narrative levels distinct with clear transitions, and let repeated phrases or objects connect them without becoming a literal proof of truth.\n\nMake the teller's omissions and emphasis part of the story's information flow. The narration may reveal what the teller knows or suspects while the listeners receive only the shaped account. Let the audience interrupt, question, or interpret, so the frame itself changes what is known. Do not inflate the teller into an omniscient guide; their purpose shapes selection, but the impossible condition retains independent consequences. Return to the outer frame after each embedded section, and close when the current telling has produced a definite shift in the listener's relation to the premise, leaving any larger promise or consequence unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the Romantic and uncanny narrative route. Treat the fantasy premise as an inherited phenomenon whose history is imperfectly known: a pact, line, object, or recurring event that older people understand partially and younger people feel as pressure. Use a controlled narrative distance that lets the narration report public belief, private unease, and material change without collapsing them into a single explanation.\n\nLet syntax and distance follow the narrator. A reflective narrator may delay the core acknowledgment, while an intimate narrator may expose contradictory feelings directly. Whatever the perspective, make the impossible element's consequences observable in altered obligations, broken routines, or a choice that would be unthinkable without it. Show how the premise passes between generations through testimony, documents, or inherited habit, not through a mystical national temperament.\n\nUse summary and dramatic scene in deliberate alternation. Longer periodic sentences can develop the weight of the past, while shorter passages render a moment of decisive present action. Allow the uncanny to express itself through ordinary objects and social ceremonies, preserving the world's intelligibility. End at the point where a current decision has become unavoidable, without requiring every mystery to be dispelled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Gothic-Punk\n\nTreat the fantasy premise as inherited power caught in modern life. The impossible condition—be it a binding bloodline, an old bargain, a place that responds to its owner, or an object that demands acknowledgment—produces tension with an ordinary present. Show the collision through concrete urban detail: worn housing, public routine, work obligations, and the small rituals used to manage the uncanny.\n\nLet atmosphere shape a particular encounter rather than function as decorative mood. A trusted conversation can make a social mask slip; a sudden disclosure can expose how the premise has already changed priorities, relationships, or the price of continuing. Keep the personal political without importing large institutions: the stakes remain intimate and survivable, even when they are cruel. Explore morally complicated intimacy through desire, old loyalty, or shared knowledge that makes help and harm difficult to separate.\n\nWrite with fast-readable clarity but allow the grotesque and the pathetic to coexist. Use sentence rhythm to mark a shift from public performance to private reaction. Do not supply new supernatural mechanics or named vampire physiology; the fantasy premise must remain the world's established fact. End after a beat in which a character acts on what the encounter revealed, leaving the outcome suspended where the scene naturally closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Present the fantasy premise as a remembered story that belongs to a community: a wonder, curse, promise, or altered relation that elders recount and younger people test against present life. Establish the specific region, language, community, and period supplied by the world, and let the impossible element's effect be tied to that place rather than a generic mythological texture.\n\nUse the performative frame to manage information. A tale may be told at a festival, a household dispute, a journey, or a moment of crisis, and its telling changes the listener's relation to the premise. Keep subsequent actions concrete: a decision about inheritance, work, marriage, migration, or a binding obligation. Show the listener's private thoughts where the narration permits, distinguishing what they know from what the teller intends.\n\nAllow lyric or philosophical inwardness inside the frame when a character reflects on the premise's weight, but anchor the outer movement in social and domestic consequences. Recurrence should be deliberate: repeated names, phrases, or objects connect past and present across tellings. End when the current frame reaches a decision or a deferred promise, not with an artificially completed lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaual::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the fantastic-tales route. Introduce the established impossible premise in a particular season, place, or daily routine, and let a specific person experience its consequence without requiring a complete system behind it. The wonder may be an old obligation, a transformed person, an object that returns, or a recurring change in the living world, but its effect must press upon a concrete decision or unspoken feeling.\n\nLet personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. A restrained narrator can convey dread or wonder through precise observation; an intimate narrator can state feeling plainly. Use seasonal attention as a real structural cue—light, weather, harvest, festival, or travel—but do not mandate a quiet or inventory-like treatment. Omission is a technique: withhold secondary explanation while clarifying what the character saw, heard, and felt.\n\nKeep the impossible event's causality legible at the moment of choice. Show how a present action alters a relationship, a promise, or a way of living. Let humor or exuberance arise if the character's temperament supports it, but avoid sentimental excess. Close after the encounter has produced its first unmistakable result, leaving any larger mystery open without a cliffhanger demand.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the fantastic/metaphysical construction route. Treat the established impossible element as part of ordinary social and sensory experience, not as a spectacle. The phenomenon—a place that remembers, a person marked by an old promise, a routine that opens into another condition—must already be compatible with the world's facts, and its practical pressure must push a character toward a choice.\n\nUse vivid everyday detail to ground the uncanny. Market, kitchen, bus, courtyard, workplace, or religious custom can carry the premise without being reduced to local color. Let the narrator's view focus on how the impossible has changed ordinary expectations: someone now plans differently, fears a particular hour, or accepts a strange obligation as normal. Keep the community specific, with actual language, region, and history supplied by the setting; do not impose generic magical realism that adds supernatural elements absent from the world.\n\nAllow nonlinear or dreamlike memory while making causal consequences legible. A passage may revisit a past promise or symbol before returning to the present decision, but the reader should understand what the current character knows. Distinctly Latin American history is not a wallpaper; let it affect religion, land, family duty, or survival. End after a choice or refusal has moved the situation forward, keeping the premise's larger mystery open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Light Novel\n\nOpen inside a concrete present moment where the fantasy premise is already affecting someone. Identify the impossible element—an unusual capacity, binding rule, transformed body, or object that answers—and show its change in ordinary movement: a route someone now avoids, a task they perform, or a decision the premise forces. Clarify unfamiliar names and mechanics exactly when they appear, using dialogue or a tight narrative beat rather than an encyclopedia break.\n\nStage action visually with easy spatial tracking. Use short paragraphs to separate movement, speech, and private thinking. When a character enters a new place, mark the change briefly: light, noise, surfaces, and available exits. Make the premise's effect visible through specific objects or repeated gestures, not abstract domination. Alternate dialogue with focused reaction so the reader sees what a line does to the listener.\n\nWrite in the active point of view the world provides, with direct internal response where authorized. Reactions may be serious, comic, angry, or uncertain according to character; do not force a young cast or first person, and do not end every section on a manufactured cliffhanger. Let the scene close after the impossible element has produced a concrete shift in the immediate situation—someone has acted, refused, or understood something new—while larger consequences remain open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite from inside a single scene, letting an established fantastic premise inhere in sensory particulars. First give the ordinary texture of the moment: the grain of a surface, the weight of a repeated task, the sound that persists after the action stops. Then let one concrete fantastic element alter that texture through a comparison that traces its actual consequence. Develop the comparison by moving from what is perceived to what it produces in those present. A magical working may be felt first as pressure, then as a change in the air, then as a private association the viewpoint character recognizes. Keep that association tethered to present perception; the image should make the fantastic consequence more present, not replace it with metaphor.\n\nWhen the comparison reaches its emotional association, return it to the world by showing one concrete effect the impossible fact has produced: a surface now warm, a task now finished without hands, a sound now coming from the wrong direction. The lyric image may carry feeling, but it must also register the change the premise makes in the scene. Do not let the prose linger in the association so long that the causal fact disappears.\n\nUse recurring sounds or images as pathways for emotional complication. Repeat not for ornament but when a later appearance revises what an earlier one meant. The same chime from a household device that once meant routine may sound again as a boundary between waking and a world changed by the impossible. Let the recurrence mark a change in the fantastic condition itself, not only a change in mood: the chime may now mean the device has begun working again, or has begun working for someone else. Let syntax do the work of pace. Longer sentences can gather into a surge of attention; a short plain declaration should interrupt the surge and anchor the reader in the immediate fact. Do not let the rhythm demand solemnity; plain statements are part of the lyric texture and can permit ordinary speech or humor when the character's voice requires it.\n\nKeep the narrator's figurative register separate from every character's speech. The prose may notice a comparison the character would not say aloud, but a character's dialogue should not mimic the narration's music. Let the viewpoint character's inner experience remain individually shaped by their age, habit, and feeling, rather than elevated by the style into a uniform poetic temper. A moment of wonder can sit beside work, family pressure, or practical concern, and wonder itself need not resolve the difficulties it creates. End when a shift of attention reaches a natural pause, not necessarily when a consequence is explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nEnter through a present moment that already contains a recollection. Give the scene its immediate physical facts, then let an established fantastic element trigger a remembered occasion. Use that memory to reveal what has changed, not simply to recapitulate it. If a household spell once made the bread rise at dawn, the narrator may now notice its absence in the changed density of the loaf or in the hour someone must wake. Let the consequence accumulate in ordinary details: a route no longer taken, a phrase no longer spoken, an object put away more gently.\n\nWhen the memory touches the fantastic element, make the present condition of that element concrete. Show whether it is gone, diminished, altered in its working, or still active but no longer needed in the old way. The reflection should register the difference between what the impossible fact once did and what it now does or fails to do. Do not let the memory replace the present fact with a vague atmosphere of loss; the loaf is denser, the route is closed, the object is put away because the spell no longer answers.\n\nAllow reflection to move directly into thought when it clarifies an attachment, but also let a single detail carry the association without commentary. For example, a character may stand in a doorway where a magical warning used to sound and simply notice the silence. Do not force every observation to announce impermanence. Ordinary pleasure may remain fully present: a good meal, a clean shirt, a child's sleep. The melancholic treatment comes from the coexistence of such present satisfactions with what is no longer available, not from making the scene relentlessly sad.\n\nUse the fantastic premise to sharpen a particular form of loss. The impossible fact may once have altered work, travel, or family life; its continued existence may now alter them differently. Show the difference through remembered occasions rather than through an explanatory interlude. When the narrator reflects directly, keep the reflection concrete: what this place used to permit, what it now costs, what remains unfinished. End at a pause in attention, not necessarily at a completed acceptance. A small irreversible change may be recognized without being resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nAdopt a framed storytelling approach with a situated teller. The outer scene establishes a speaker and listener in an ordinary place: a courtyard, a kitchen, a market stall, a sickroom, or whatever setting the narrative world provides. The teller's account may report the fantastic premise as witnessed or heard, or may enact it by telling a tale in which the premise produces a concrete consequence. Do not let the teller become an omniscient narrator who explains the whole magical system. Keep the teller's limited knowledge consistent: what the tale claims and what the teller actually knows may differ, and a listener may interrupt, correct, or supply a missing detail. Do not force first person; a third-person narration may move between the framing scene and the embedded tale while retaining the teller's attributed speech.\n\nInside the embedded tale, show the fantastic element causing a specific change in the tale's world. If the tale says a charm keeps a well from running dry, show the well failing when the charm is broken, or a family dividing its water because the charm now serves only one house. The tale should make the impossible fact's consequence intelligible through action and result, not through a statement that the charm was powerful. The teller may choose the order of events for persuasion, comfort, warning, or repayment, but the causal fact remains stable beneath that arrangement.\n\nStructure the fantastic material as nested and recursive when that suits the story. A tale may pause to explain a nested incident, then return to the outer events. Delayed revelation is a deliberate device: withhold a consequence until a later point in the telling, but do not withhold it merely to create mystery. Let the listening context shape the tale's content. A story told to a frightened child and the same story told to a creditor will differ in emphasis and image, even though the factual core remains stable.\n\nKeep the frame and the embedded tale distinct in texture. The outer scene may be plain or domestic, with dialogue, practical tasks, and the interruptions of ordinary life. The embedded tale may be more lyrical and reflective, though it should remain the teller's voice, not an anonymous elevated register. Use local precision instead of a generic regional atmosphere; names, foods, garments, currencies, and times of day should belong to the supplied world. The fantastic consequence must remain intelligible within the tale and the frame. When a tale changes how the listener acts, show that change in a small specific gesture, not in a moral appended to the story. End with the teller finishing, interrupted, or leaving the consequence active, as the current scene warrants.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect one or two concrete details through which the established fantastic premise currently matters. Choose the detail by what it causes or what a character can infer from it. For example, if magic preserves food without decay, a single line about a meal being exactly as it was a year ago is enough; do not then explain the preservation principle, its history, or its broader implications unless one of those facts directly affects the present action or decision. Keep sentences direct. Use the simplest grammatical shape that carries the event and its consequence.\n\nMake the causal link visible in the next concrete movement. The selected fantastic detail should be followed by a result that belongs to the same scene: the preserved meal is served, the charm is weighed, the door opens without being touched. If the magic fails or changes, do not leave the failure as an unexplained observation; show the immediate practical consequence in the next sentence. Someone starts counting coins, a journey is recalculated, a child is sent to fetch water. Omission may remove explanation, but it must not remove the connection between the impossible fact and what it does.\n\nAttach inward matter only when it materially changes what the character does or understands. A brief thought such as She had never known him to ask twice is useful if the next action responds to that recognition. If the thought repeats what the reader can already infer from the scene, omit it. Omission must leave something inferable, not delete causality.\n\nLet speech carry distinct character rather than the narrator's economy. A talkative merchant may have a long sentence in a story otherwise spare; a hesitant child may speak in fragments. The narration remains selective even when a character does not. Do not manufacture short sentences as a stylistic tic. A precise extended sentence may serve better than three performed fragments when it gathers a sequence of events into a single causal movement. End when the selected detail's present consequence reaches a pause. Do not add an explanatory summary or a second fantastic detail merely to enrich the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the current scene as continuous novel prose. Begin within concrete action, speech, or perception, not with a summary of the fantastic premise. Let the established magical fact appear in the same register as a door latch or the delay of a bus: noticed, used, complained about, or remembered because it is part of the world's working texture. If the premise is a ring of speaking cats in the courtyard, do not pause to explain their origin or taxonomy. Show a character stepping around one to reach the well, or haggling with one over a debt, and let the story's ordinary concerns continue.\n\nWhen the fantastic element produces a consequence, deliver that consequence through the same continuous movement. The cat's answer changes the price; the charm's failure changes the route; the speaking creature's silence changes who is willing to enter the house. Do not shift into a heightened register or pause to explain the mechanism. Let the result arrive through a perception, a line of dialogue, a practical adjustment, or an authorized inner thought, and then let the scene continue with its ordinary business.\n\nMove naturally among perception, dialogue, recollection, and authorized inner experience. A paragraph may open with a sensory detail, follow a character's practical thought, then give a line of dialogue that alters the thought. Transition between these elements by adjacency and attention, not by recap such as Meanwhile, or Earlier that day. Use paragraph breaks to mark shifts in time, place, speaker emphasis, or a new focal perception. Orient by concrete orientation: the smell of rain before a market stall, the weight of a tool in a hand, the way a child looks up when a spell misfires.\n\nKeep inner experience inseparable from the character's habits and limits. A character may silently rehearse a warning, notice how a familiar person has changed, or feel the bodily drag of an overworked charm. Do not convert inner experience into universal cleverness or make the narrator's style demand wit from every speaker. A moment of wonder can sit beside routine; an ordinary pleasure may be written plainly. Do not require a lesson at the end of each reply. The scene may stop when the present action or thought reaches a natural pause, even if the fantastic consequence remains unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship premise choose the scene. The story should open or continue at a moment where the central bond is tested, enjoyed, acknowledged, or altered, even if the surrounding events are practical or dangerous. The selected fantastic mechanism must press on that bond specifically. If the premise says the pair share a hearth-charm that warns of danger, do not merely describe the charm; show one of them misreading its signal and the other responding from long familiarity, or the two arguing over whether to heed it before leaving on a journey. The impossible fact is not background decoration; it is a recurring element through which their habits, promises, or silences become legible.\n\nUse a private language and repeated moments that belong only to these two people. A particular phrase can be a callback when it arises in a new key: first spoken in comfort, later spoken as an accusation finally acknowledged; first used to tease, later used as a serious signal before rupture or reconciliation. Each callback should register a change in the fantastic condition or in what the pair knows about it. The phrase first used when the hearth-charm burned steadily may return when the charm has gone dark, or when one of them has learned to read its signal without the other. Repetition should deepen the reader's understanding, not simply restate the same feeling.\n\nCreate consequences that fulfill the relationship's particular promise. If the premise promises rival teams who learn to trust one another, a success story should include scenes of distrust paying off, trust being earned, and the cost of that trust in a specific fantastic context. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes such as only one bed, slow-burn friends to lovers, or enemies to lovers unless those elements are already present in the supplied character and world facts.\n\nGive the emotional beat enough space. Affection can be shown in an unhurried scene of one person bandaging the other's hand after a spell misfires, with dialogue that moves from practical concern to a private joke. Jealousy can be shown through a third party receiving a magical gift that used to be a pair's private ritual. Comfort can be shown through a character falling asleep in a room where a protective magic has always been kept, even if the magic is currently gone. Discovery can be shown through a shared magical talent that simultaneously reveals a similarity and a difference. End at a point that registers the relationship consequence, whether that is a held look, an unfinished sentence, or a departure already arranged. Do not require a confession, kiss, or rupture in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a deliberate chronicle gesture: a naming, a condition of the season, a remembered debt, or a declaration that this is how the matter was afterward told. Do not frame it as a modern narrator's personal essay. The telling should sound like an account passed from someone who witnessed or received the events, even if the grammatical person remains third-person. Make the opening concrete and local: So the bridge was mended before the moon turned, or They counted the herd at the river and found the toll had been paid.\n\nUse compact declarations to establish a person, place, or requirement, then expand into the particulars that complicate the reputation. A recurring designation should arise from an established deed or recurring obligation, not from an ornamental epithet. For example, if a magical ward must be renewed by a masked singer each year, the story may return to the phrase the mask singer whenever that obligation involves a new person or a new danger. Repetition becomes meaningful when a later occurrence gives the phrase a new application. Keep shared reputation distinct from private experience. The people may say a character is fearless because she walked the ghost road twice, while her own wife says she does not sleep in the week before the road opens.\n\nLet the selected fantastic premise operate as a public fact. It may involve a recurring journey, a hereditable gift, a bound service, a dangerous season, or a particular promise. Show its consequences through kinship, gifts, feuds, oaths, or public work. When a magical event occurs, report the act and its aftermath with the same declarative economy as a flood or a feast: what happened, who was there, who is now bound or freed. Name the resulting obligation or change in standing. The ward's renewal may mean a household is now under protection, a debt is now owed to the singer, or a road is now open to travelers who were previously forbidden. Do not explain the mechanism. Dialogue may interrupt the chronicle's authority. A listener within the story may dispute a detail, answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern, or correct the teller about who held the lantern. Such interruption need not become comedy; it keeps the telling alive and fallible. End with the act concluded or still unfolding, and let the final sentence suggest what will be remembered, without declaring the cast legendary on their own behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTreat the established fantastic premise as an ordinary civic and domestic fact. A haunting follows the calendar of a worksite, a transformed animal still pays taxes or attends a parent's birthday, a lake spirit negotiates a fishing permit, a household charm changes the smell of baking and the village's expectation of that baking. Show the premise through repeated work, family meals, local institutions, and small economic obligations rather than through one dramatic revelation. Wonder can arrive in a neighbor's offhand remark about the cost of a particular blessing or in the practical consequence of a superstition, and it remains wonder without being exiled to allegory.\n\nPick one dominant Scandinavian literary route for the whole entry: a spare folktale-inflected social realism is the default. Write in a restrained inward voice that follows the viewpoint character's attention to what people owe each other, what they hide from pity or embarrassment, and what the environment or the household makes them notice. Use plain domestic detail—a worn threshold, a bread oven, a child's boot, a repaired net—and let it carry memory or obligation. Do not load every landscape with snow, silence, or isolation; use the actual supplied setting. If the story is Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish, keep its specific social textures without labeling all Nordic life interchangeable.\n\nLet the fantastic have intelligible consequences but keep some of its obscurity. A person knows that the buried charm keeps the herring close this year, but does not know whether it caused the cousin's fever, and both pieces of knowledge can coexist. Do not force every supernatural element into a fully explained system. When institutions appear—a parish office, a local council, a school, a works committee—render them as recognizable routine settings where the premise complicates a decision, rather than as cipher agencies.\n\nKeep interiority present through concrete observation and inferred feeling. The focal mind may notice a parent's silence at supper, the weight of a borrowed coat, or the exact delay before a neighbor answers about the strange child. Private reflection should be proportionate to the scene: a busy morning may allow only a passing worry, while an evening in the boat or kitchen can hold longer memory. No fixed thought-to-dialogue ratio is required. Dialogue often matters more for what it declines to say; other characters do not share the viewpoint's knowledge.\n\nLet the social-realist method govern the story's pressures. A neighbor's help can be genuine, an official can be fair, a frightening place can also be a workplace, and a child can obey without hidden resentment. When a beat closes, let it close on the particular texture of the method: a backed-up reason, an unfinished task, a quiet observation of someone else's silence, or the small social consequence of a wonder that remains only partly understood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Screenplay\n\nPut the premise into observable action and practical objects. If a spell warms the house, show a steaming kettle, a frost-free windowpane, and a child stripping off a second sweater. If a ghost works the night shift, show the finished crates and the damp bootprints at the door. Let characters treat the impossible as a condition of the job: a smith argues with a flame that will not jump to a new forge, a ferry pilot checks a registration mark on a water horse. Use dialogue for choices, negotiations, ordinary small transactions, and the specific local knowledge people share about the premise, not for explaining its origin in one speech.\n\nWrite scene headings whenever location or time changes, and keep action lines tight and legible. Each block should convey a single visible or audible action or its immediate result. Restrict cut-style transitions to meaningful jumps: to the other side of a door, to the next morning at a repaired wall, to the sound of the same bell now downtown. Use offscreen sound only when the viewer would actually hear it—a splash after someone leaves the room, a chime from a locked cabinet, a neighbor's voice through the plaster.\n\nUse voice-over sparingly and only when the selected narrative contract explicitly authorizes it. Required private prose, where the active narrator calls for inward experience, should be written as a clearly attributed nonspoken note connected to a character's observable action, not disguised as dialogue or shared knowledge. For example, an action block may include a parenthetical note that Aila does not say she recognized the gesture. Another character does not know that note just because the script contains it.\n\nLet visual repetition acquire meaning without requiring a mandatory payoff in every scene. A mark on a doorframe can be noticed in the first scene and revisited when the premise changes, but a cut back to that mark should add information or pressure, not merely restate it. A familiar object or wound can anchor a new scene if its altered state is the point.\n\nKeep the cast's habits distinct and let other characters continue their own work between scenes. A scene can end on a completed errand, an ordinary argument, or the moment before a decision, without a fixed reversal, confession, or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Stage Play\n\nMake the fantastic premise stage-legible through physical business and the practical constraints of the shared space. A house that remembers its dead may be shown by a door that opens without anyone at it, then by who is surprised and who is not. A magical debt may appear as a written ledger, a borrowed key, or a chair left empty at supper. Design entrances and exits so the premise changes what characters can do between them: someone leaves to fetch water and returns with a different coat, a lamp, or the wrong child; someone does not come back through the door they entered.\n\nKeep stage directions useful to a company, not decorative. Write only what an actor can perform or a designer can provide: a pause before answering, a hand on a latch, a bowl that is too heavy, a coat hung on the wrong peg. Use objects and repeated business to carry consequence. If a spell weakens, show the identical chore taking more effort or requiring another person's help. Let ordinary work remain onstage—mending, feeding a stove, sorting letters—so the impossible appears as a condition of life rather than a special effect.\n\nUse monologues and asides only when they serve a dramatic purpose in the current scene—as an address to the audience that is part of the performance contract, or as a speech another character has reason to deliver. Do not convert all private thought into spoken solo material. Preserve required interiority as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring notes attached to a character's staged action or pause, marked so the reader knows it is not heard by the cast or the audience. Example: while sliding the bolt, Ingrid (nonspoken) compares the family's version of the recipe with the smell from the chimney. Another character cannot respond to that note.\n\nLet dialogue do particular work. Characters may explain a rule to a child or a newcomer, bargain over a service, argue about blame or money, or state what they refuse to name. Do not force every exchange to divulge hidden motivation or to include one witty line per beat. People can take turns in practical speech without all understanding one another's unspoken meaning.\n\nA scene can end after a physical result, a completed task, or a pause that leaves the next action open. A fantastical element may frighten or reassure without being fully explained, and the cast may respond with routine help, refusal, or evasion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Stream of Consciousness\n\nAnchor the stream in a single authorized viewpoint and keep that mind's boundaries explicit. The narration moves by association: a smell, a phrase, a task, a repetitive worry, a bodily twinge, or a remembered voice can pull the prose away from the present scene and back again. Use these jumps to reveal the premise as lived, not as exposition. If the dead can be bargained with, the viewpoint may notice the exact pitch of the neighbor's voice that means she has already done it, then remember a childhood warning that her mother denied saying. Let the impossible appear through the mind's habitual attention to small differences: which door the cat uses, how long the water stays cold, what the market people stop saying when the foundling passes.\n\nKeep enough orientation in the movement. Indicate when a passage is current perception, memory, imagined future, or a story someone told. A sentence may shift without a heading, but the reader should be able to recover what happened versus what the mind suspected or wished. If a memory is mistaken, make that available in the narration only if the viewpoint has reason to know it is mistaken; later understanding cannot be retroactively inserted as an earlier motive.\n\nLet the fantastic premise have real, repeatable consequences even when the narrator's attention is partial. The stream can interrupt an institution's routine—a school roll call, a clinic queue, a tax office's waiting bench—without making the institution unreal. A magic that changes a building's rooms can appear as someone's confused search for a familiar staircase and then, pages later, as the dry cleaner mentioning that the shop now has an extra back room no one remembers building. Such facts remain world facts, not merely metaphors of the narrator's mood.\n\nUse interiority without making everyone share a mind or giving the narration access beyond its contract. Only the authorized viewpoint's private words, reasoning, and sensation are directly rendered. Other characters' thoughts must be inferred from their speech, actions, or what the viewpoint observes. A momentary false assumption in the viewpoint's mind is fine and does not rewrite the external event. Do not turn all scene facts indeterminate merely because the style is associative; a closed door stays closed unless something established opens it.\n\nVary the mental tempo by actual situation. A rushed morning may produce fragments and interrupted tasks; a long wait may allow sustained memory and speculation; panic can break syntax, while calm conversation may include very little thought. Allow speech, action, and private awareness to align or diverge for character-specific reasons, and let a beat end on an unfinished thought, a sensory observation, or a simple decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet the established fantasy premise shape the actual message environment. A ghost whose presence drains a building's power appears as delayed deliveries from that address, not as tonal dread. A charm that makes a road forget travelers surfaces as a route-share app recalculating, or as a group chat where one sender's location pin keeps drifting. A family blessing tied to a house key shows up as the only device in the household that never loses battery. Use sender habits as evidence of the premise: the baker sends voice notes before dawn because her hands are too hot to type, the ferry child answers only with photos, the local clerk asks the same question in every thread because the answer keeps erasing from her records.\n\nMake timestamps, delays, reads, edits, attachments, or muted channels matter only when they carry the story forward. A long typing indicator followed by a two-word reply may register hesitation; a delivered-at-3 a.m. message from a sender who works nights is not automatically ominous. Do not embellish the transcript with interface events merely to manufacture subtext. If a channel is private, reflect the sender's actual access; a message cannot be added to a channel by someone who is not a member unless an established capability permits it.\n\nDistinguish four kinds of text: a message actually sent, an unsent draft, a private thought about what to send, and context outside the exchange. Show an unsent draft as a clearly labeled unsent draft, not as a message the other person could have read. Private thought is not automatically transmitted unless the active narration explicitly renders it and marks it separate from the transcript. For example, in a secondary context block, the narration may note that Mara opens the message, sees that the address is unchanged, and decides not to mention the family tax; none of that appears in the sent reply.\n\nUse each sender's actual digital habits to characterize them. One person types full sentences, another sends single words with long gaps, another edits every message after sending. Let this remain consistent but not mechanically repeated. Dialect, work context, age, accessibility, and urgency can shape the messages without reducing people to stereotypes. Do not require every character to make a joke, deflect, or withhold a key fact in every exchange.\n\nLet the transcript carry practical decisions and work around the premise: asking who will watch the furnace during the half-hour the street forgets itself, checking whether the payment reached the spirit's account, arranging care for a transformed child, updating a family group about the door that leads to last year. Progress can occur across messages through new facts, shared plans, and small institutional arrangements. A draft thread may end on a pending question, a completed errand, or a read receipt with no answer, without forcing a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagauba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Visual Novel\n\nUse a sequence of concise named scene blocks that place the impossible premise in the world. When the scene changes, give a brief new heading or an equivalent legible transition. Each block should include enough setting to orient the moment: a kitchen warm on one side, a road that no longer matches the map, a registry office where a signature appears a day late. Keep the presentation concise but not thin; allow short narrative passages that show the viewpoint noticing the premise in ordinary use.\n\nLet dialogue carry practical transactions, negotiations, and small conflicts around the premise, with clear attribution. People may discuss the cost of a blessing, the route that remembers passengers, or the family obligation tied to an object, without giving an explanatory speech. Background or expression cues may orient the moment—the viewpoint sees that the clerk's expression changes when the address is read, or that the shrine lamp is lit earlier than usual—but such cues are not automatic truth indicators. A change in expression can attract attention; a fact about the premise must do the actual causal work.\n\nKeep interiority in focal narration rather than in branch menus or shared public knowledge. The viewpoint may think that the neighbor's offer is too prompt, or feel the weight of a borrowed charm in a pocket, and no other character shares that thought. Show enough inner response to make decisions and emotions specific, but do not convert every exchange into a lengthy private explanation. A scene can contain very little thought in a rushed moment or more sustained reflection in a quiet one.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the visual-novel style is selected. Scene changes are prose transitions, not code. If the story's authorized presentation includes background or expression labels, use them as purposeful references: background: ferry office; expression: tired. But their factual content must match the supplied world; do not invent a fantastical background element just to fill the template.\n\nLet repetition acquire meaning through a specific detail. A familiar altar or shopfront can be revisited when a later fact changes what it means, such as the same offered drink now being refused because its recipient knows the consequences. Use focused juxtapositions rather than restating the same suspicion. A scene may end after a completed exchange, a changed understanding, or a pause before action, without a mandatory revelation or a branch choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaubb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / No Named Influence / Web Serial\n\nKeep the established fantasy premise active as a continuing public condition across installments. If a district's street layout changes at night, later installments should remember which routes have been lost, which merchants changed, and which family still cannot get their delivery cart home. If a foundling's arrival alters a household, show the accumulating effects on chores, money, and relationships rather than restating the wonder. Progress can occur through knowledge, work, resources, or trust, not just through a new revelation.\n\nWhen a reply opens, recover only the brief particular needed to resume: the exact promise made last installment, the outstanding debt, the person waiting by the gate, the object last seen at the shrine. Do not retell the whole history or explain the premise from scratch unless a new character would actually need that. Let the later story depend on earlier facts without making the reader reread. If a detail changes meaning—the same locked door is now open, the same neighbor now asks a different question—state the changed fact and let the new context do the work.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks, but do not make every installment a complete miniature chapter with a twist. An installment can end after a completed errand, a difficult conversation, a written note left at the door, or the decision to ask the next question in the morning. A cliffhanger is acceptable only when a genuine uncertainty has been raised by the story, not as a mechanical device. Let an installment sometimes end quietly so that the next one can begin with a routine consequence.\n\nDistinguish what the characters know from what the premise itself can do. If a magic actually changes which memories persist, show the people comparing notes rather than the narration explaining the whole system. Let institutions and ordinary work remain legible: a school kitchen still needs bread, a clinic still keeps business hours, a works yard still pays wages. The impossible belongs alongside these routines and changes their daily texture, but does not overwrite them.\n\nDo not import progression-fantasy extras such as leveling, stat sheets, ranks, or system notifications unless the active world contract separately supplies them. Maintain each character's own habits and let helpers remain genuine, officials remain fair, and a relationship change remain unsettled when the plot leaves it that way. A later realization cannot retroactively become an earlier motive. The serial format should make continuity satisfying without inventing danger or betrayal every time a reply needs a reason to continue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet the fantastic premise already belong to the world and enter an unresolved intimate exchange as a practical limit, a source of leverage, and a possible cost. Put the scene in or near a live exchange where an ordinary concern - a delivery, a favor, a shared meal, a route home, a ritual obligation, a debt of care - carries more weight because of what the magic makes possible or forbidden. Show the premise's rules through use, refusal, bargaining, and the way people plan around them. The rules can remain partially mysterious, but they should be intelligible in the decisions at hand: who is permitted to touch the object, who can cross the ward, whose promise has been magically witnessed, what an unpaid debt will take from a body or a household. Do not stop for a worldbuilding lecture.\n\nBuild the scene as a sustained exchange under pressure, with the conversation doing the work of action. Let ordinary particulars gain significance through the situation: who brought what, who remembers the exact wording of a promise or spell, who keeps checking the door. Timing, status, withheld intent, and changes of control should move the scene. One speaker can shift the terms by revealing or concealing a detail, invoking a rule, offering a practical fix, or letting a silence stand. The emotional turbulence belongs inside that action: let the cost of admitting need, withholding truth, apologizing, or returning to a difficult relationship distort what gets said and what gets handled. Strain can show up as an interruption, a correction, a suddenly practical question, an answer that arrives too late, or a detail repeated because naming the real thing would be too expensive. Not every character needs the same wit, profanity, or readiness to fight, and violence is available only if the fantasy premise already supports it.\n\nLet the strain carry into behavior after the words land: who stands where, who moves an object, who reaches for a hand and stops, who leaves a gift unopened. Comfort may be partial, awkward, or mixed with warmth that does not cancel pain. Avoid a stock vocabulary of suffering; let the viewpoint character's distance and diction shape the anguish. Render that character's live nonspoken private experience directly in the presentation's permitted interiority, whether visible or concealed; an in-world document, monologue, aside, or voice-over does not replace it. When physical consequences occur, stage them clearly - the burned hand, the broken ward, the emptied room, the changed face - and let their effects continue into the next exchange.\n\nKeep the conversation as the main action even when the fantastic world intrudes. A threat, a promise, or a magical cost can remain unpaid while people keep talking. The scene can end during continuing speech, an unfinished gesture, a private thought, or a magical effect still unfolding; it does not need to resolve the hurt, pause the external situation, or produce a reversal before the response stops. This treatment does not require a tragic ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene as a live interval in a world where the fantastic is already part of ordinary pressure. Let an impossible fact shape what people can do, what they want, and what they argue over before anyone explains it. A threshold oath, a licensed charm, a familiar that refuses a command, a spell with a visible cost, or a creature whose presence changes the terms of a transaction can all sit beside work, money, family, and routine. When a fantastic detail matters, show its practical edge: what it binds, what it leaves uncovered, who can afford it, who is denied it, what it costs the caster in the room. Do not turn the world into a lecture; let the rules arrive as leverage, obstacle, and habit.\n\nMake talk the action. Put characters with incompatible wants into the same exchange and let the conversation run long enough for leverage to move. Each speaker should hold the floor in a way that belongs to them: one waits through silence, one answers a different question than the one asked, one corrects a small detail because that detail is the real stake, one talks too much because stopping would expose something. Let ordinary interests such as a debt, a grudge, a favor, or a professional reputation carry the tension until a fantastic particular sharpens it. Withheld intent is allowed to remain withheld; status can shift without anyone announcing it. When control changes through a reversed question, a retracted offer, a name used at the right moment, or a promise made in front of witnesses, stage it as a visible beat that the reader can follow.\n\nGive physical consequences clear staging as they occur. If magic is used, show its effect in the room: a ward flaring along a seam, a charm going cold, ink spilling, smoke curling wrong, a chair scraping back as someone stands. If violence or comic excess belongs to this world and these particular people, let it land with weight and specific consequence; do not make it mandatory seasoning. Keep wit uneven. One speaker may be cruel and funny, another plain and nervous, another quiet because the situation does not reward performance. The sharpest line should come from character and pressure, not from a requirement that everyone be clever.\n\nKeep paragraphs scannable and dialogue attribution unmistakable, even during fast back-and-forth. Name or tag each speaker so a reader never loses the thread of a long exchange. Keep narration in the person and action markers the session has established, and respect player control over the player character. Let the viewpoint character's nonspoken experience run under the talk when the selected perspective permits it: a calculation held back, a memory that changes what a price means, fear or appetite that colors a pause, a private refusal that never becomes dialogue. Do not let a document, a performed monologue, or a voice-over substitute for that actual private experience. If the session hides interiority from display, provide the actual private experience rather than a vague summary.\n\nKeep the wider room alive. NPCs pursue their own practical goals, help or resist for reasons of their own, and may be already mid-decision when the player speaks. An apprentice drags a crate past, another customer waits, ash sifts onto the roof, a familiar sulks under the table; these details can continue without pausing the central exchange. End the reply inside the scene's live motion, whether that is mid-sentence, mid-pour, mid-threat, mid-thought, or mid-choice, so the next move follows from what just happened. No question, cliffhanger, or menu is required. A seeded detail may pay off later, but the response does not need to resolve it or force the next beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantastic premise as a mundane condition of the supplied community rather than a separate marvel. Let it change what people can plan, owe, trade, avoid, or hope for, and show those effects through the intimate urban or domestic route of Chinese literary traditions: close scene-based telling where rooms, courtyards, stairwells, kitchens, shops, food, money, tools, weather, and the sounds of neighbors carry social weight. Keep the active narrator and person; do not turn the premise into an explained system. One impossible condition—a missing hour, a contract no one can break, a door that opens for one family, a dead relative who still gives advice—can shape leverage, obligation, and daily schedule without demanding a full cosmology.\n\nBuild scenes as sustained conversation under pressure. Let characters talk about ordinary interests—a debt, a repair, a dish, a relative's visit, a local reputation—while timing, status, withheld intent, and changes of control make the exchange an action. A polite question can shift who owns the room. A digression can be a maneuver, a test, or a genuine preoccupation. Do not make every speaker share the same wit or profanity; the pressure comes from what this particular person wants the other to do, admit, or fail to notice. Let the fantastic condition enter this talk obliquely: characters may argue over whether a spirit, curse, bargain, or impossible machine is real, but the world has already made its effects available as conditions that shape leverage and obligation.\n\nUse concrete social texture and causal clarity. When a consequence arrives, stage it materially—a door left unlocked, a payment refused, a chair moved, a rumor reaching the wrong ear, a body in the wrong room. Delay is part of the design: an earlier detail, a name, a favor, a scar, a habit of serving tea, can return with new force because the situation around it has changed. Keep reversals sharp but earned by what the scene has established. Private thought can differ from spoken position for character-specific reasons—fear of a parent, professional caution, shame, a wish to protect someone, simple calculation—and other characters do not know what the viewpoint keeps private unless they infer or are told. Render nonspoken experience as actual thought, sensation, uncertainty, or reasoning in the established presentation, not as a summary.\n\nKeep the supplied language, period, and community distinct. Do not import martial sects, imperial courts, dynastic intrigue, or supernatural rules the world has not established. This route is intimate urban or domestic narrative, not a menu of historical eras or a generic wise elder. Do not force a crime plot, a weapons standoff, or a profane monologue; violence or comic excess belongs only when the genre and scene support it. When force does occur, give it clear physical staging and lasting consequence. End at the active response boundary, whether that falls during continuing speech, an unfinished decision, a private reaction, or a consequence still unfolding. A conversation may remain unresolved, and a task may span turns; the scene does not owe a reversal, a clue, a confession, or a cliffhanger. If a reversal happens, it grows from pressure already in the room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nAnchor the scene in an established fantastic condition that changes what people can do—a bargain, a ward, a creature's attention, an inherited ability, a toll, a resurrection, a debt. Make its consequences practical. A door answers only to a price. A name carries obligation. A wound leaks light. A tool remembers. Reveal the premise through use and wear: a charm is scratched, a spell costs breath, a ward smells of scorched copper. Let the impossible sit inside work, hunger, travel, family, or a negotiation, and keep its rules intelligible without turning every mystery into a manual.\n\nHandle conversation as action. Put characters under pressure in a sustained exchange where ordinary particulars—a meal, a local custom, a missing finger, a debt, a rumor, a piece of equipment—become consequential because of the fantastic situation. Timing matters: who speaks first, who lets a silence run, who answers a different question. Status shifts through what is offered, withheld, or named. Control can change when one speaker reveals a piece of knowledge, invokes a rule, or makes an ordinary detail mean something else. Keep withheld intent in the speaker's private experience, not announced. The dialogue may stay on the surface subject while the real stakes move underneath. Let rough or abrasive speech belong to the specific speaker, not the whole cast.\n\nWrite in compressed, hard-edged prose. Prefer concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Compress transitions: cut from one physical beat to the next. Use high contrast—clean versus filthy, calm versus ready, rich versus desperate—without decorating it. Material wear should carry history: chipped cups, patched coats, a blade notched from use, a sigil rubbed smooth. Physical stakes stay present: cold, weight, blood, thirst, a countdown, a limited resource. Change rhythm when the pressure changes; a burst of short sentences can give way to one longer thought when a character's reasoning needs room. Let the fantastic premise and the specific characters decide whether violence, betrayal, or comic excess belongs; none is mandatory.\n\nLet fantastical consequence be delayed and sharply staged. A line spoken early can return as a price, an obligation, or a changed relationship. When a reversal lands, ground it in the established premise and the exchange's stakes—who knew what, who moved first, what rule was invoked—rather than surprise for its own sake. Physical consequences get clear staging when they occur: who is hit, what breaks, what it costs, who sees it. The premise decides whether that consequence is lethal, comic, strange, or quiet.\n\nRender the viewpoint character's actual nonspoken private experience—attention, sensation, reasoning, uncertainty—as it accompanies or precedes the current action, exchange, or quiet interval. When the active contract divides visible and hidden interiority, put that experience in the appropriate channel rather than replacing it with spoken summary. Keep other characters from knowing it unless permission is explicit. A private interval can stand without an external task or practical decision forcing it. The response can end during speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup; no reversal, confession, decision, or rescue has to land before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Epistolary / Documents\n\nBuild the story from documents that carry their own occasion: letters, journal entries, official reports, transcripts, ledgers, annotations, or recorded testimony. Each record has a named author, a recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, and what it knows, omits, or distorts follows from that situation. Establish early how the fantastic premise changes documentary life in this world—what can be written, sent, intercepted, verified, forged, remembered, or erased—and let those possibilities and limits decide what each record contains. Use the document form the scene actually needs, not a rotation through formats for variety. Give the reader the document's material condition and occasion without appending an omniscient explanation after every entry.\n\nLet conversation under pressure enter through these authored surfaces. When a document preserves an exchange, keep the speakers' timing, status, withheld intent, and shifts in control in the order of lines, the placement of disclosures, and what the writer chooses to set down. Ordinary interests—a debt, a shift, a family obligation, a local custom, a small favor—gain weight because the situation makes them stakes. A letter may report a negotiation after the fact, but its emphasis and omissions should reveal what mattered to its author and what they are protecting. A transcript can catch the moment a speaker changes terms; a ledger can make a promise material; a marginal note can reopen a question the main text tries to close. Not every speaker needs profane wit, and the fantasy premise decides whether violence, wonder, or comic excess belongs.\n\nStage reversals across documents rather than through a single omniscient narration. A later record can contradict an earlier account, but the contradiction should arise from the author's access, memory, motive, or changed circumstances. Gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces can reveal events and produce delayed consequence: a fact that seemed settled in one letter becomes unstable when another document supplies a date, a witness, a debt, or an effect of the fantastic premise. When physical consequences occur, show them in concrete staging—what changed, who was present, what the record states or leaves out. Not every document needs to end on a reversal; some can confirm or deepen what is already known.\n\nPreserve readable continuity. Recurring names, places, phrases, or objects should return with altered significance as new documents cast light on them. The fantastic premise need not become a fully explained system; its consequences should be intelligible in what characters do and what records show. Keep private knowledge separate from what a document says: a letter, report, or transcript contains what its author chose or was able to record, not automatically their complete live mind. Where the scene gives access to a character's actual private experience, render that experience in the established visible or hidden mode rather than folding it into a document's self-justification or omission. The response may end within a document, between entries, or while a character is composing one. A final entry can be unfinished, unsent, or contradictory; no document must resolve the story's question or force a practical decision before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nAnchor the scene in a fantastic condition already established in the world—an inherited household spell, a local spirit bargaining over bread, a road that shortens for the kind, a charm that must be fed, a trade that repairs luck. Show its intelligible consequences in the room: what it warms, cools, opens, costs, changes hands, or prevents. You do not need to explain the whole system. The impossible should affect what these particular people can offer, refuse, risk, or repair right now.\n\nBuild the scene around sustained talk in which ordinary particulars matter because of the situation. Let people trade news, preferences, recipes, small debts, old jokes, local loyalties, complaints about a neighbor, or the correct way to leave an offering. Those details should carry pressure from a favor owed, a magical limit, a guest's status, an approaching obligation, or a decision about who gets helped first. Use timing and control as action: a pause held too long, an answer to the wrong question, a subject changed, a plate offered instead of an answer, a casual phrase that quietly resets who leads. Withheld intent can remain plausible; kindness can be sincere and still incomplete. Not every speaker needs to be profane, quick, or combative—let verbal pressure belong to the person and the fantasy circumstance.\n\nMake comfort active and specific. Spend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Someone might know how another takes their tea, repair a worn charm without being asked, save the best chair, or keep a harmless ritual because it steadies the room. Affection can be real without requiring a confession, emotional repair, or tidy resolution. Keep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. People may remain irritated, tired, private, or unconvinced. A comfort can be partial, temporary, or merely logistical. Do not remove established problems from the world, and do not make everyone agreeable because the scene is warm.\n\nLet delayed consequence move through both the magical and the domestic. A detail introduced early—a missing ingredient, an unpaid courtesy, a phrase from a shared story, a small magic with a strange limit—can return later with new weight. If a reversal occurs, stage it sharply and concretely: someone sets down a cup, changes the terms of a bargain, invokes a rule, reveals what they can or cannot do, or accepts help on a new footing. The turn need not be violent or cruel. It can be comic, tender, awkward, or simply a change in who is caring for whom. If physical consequence occurs, give it clear staging; if it does not, a change in control or knowledge can carry the turn.\n\nKeep private experience present. Render what a character notices, wants, fears, remembers, or decides not to say; let that nonspoken experience be direct rather than replaced by dialogue, a performed aside, or a neat summary. It may align with speech or differ for a specific reason—embarrassment, courtesy, strategy, uncertainty, habit, a wish to protect someone. Do not use the style to make every warm feeling spoken aloud, and do not use fantasy to grant access to minds the current contract excludes. The scene can end during continuing conversation, unfinished care, a thought, a task, or a setup. It does not need a confession, reconciliation, betrayal, completed bargain, or resolved danger. Keep the warmth and the impossible both live, with the people still capable of choosing what happens next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite composed novel prose that can hold a society in view while keeping the scene's immediate pressure close. Treat the fantastic as an established condition of this world: a capacity, creature, place, material, prohibition, or recurring event that people already live with. Its consequences should appear in work, law, family arrangements, markets, rituals, travel, or ordinary errands. Characters may understand it unevenly, argue over its meaning, or negotiate its social cost, but the narration does not need to convert every mystery into a complete system. Let wonder sit beside routine obligation rather than displace it, and keep the scale intimate or expansive according to what the world already permits.\n\nUse broad paragraphs to frame a situation before or around a sustained exchange. Give the reader the social history that makes a room, queue, contract, ceremony, meal, or journey matter: who may speak, what has recently changed, what a public silence protects, and what the fantastic condition has made ordinary. Then let speech become action. Build exchanges in which ordinary interests—a debt, a recipe, a route, a repair, a guest's rank, the ownership of a talisman—gain significance because of the surrounding pressure. Track timing, status, and withheld intent: one speaker tests another's knowledge of a manifestation; another answers a practical question while avoiding a more dangerous one; a third changes the subject in a way that alters who controls the next move. Voices should carry their own habits and positions rather than share one profane, witty, or combative register. Formal narration remains distinct from ordinary speech, though both belong to the same novel.\n\nOrganize passages through ceremony, correspondence, testimony, or public and private contrast when the scene supports it. Frame events through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it; the narration can note what a choice costs within a household, guild, parish, court, or neighborhood without declaring a universal moral. Let private experience stay actual: characters think, remember, misread, and feel without announcing every inference aloud. Keep the narration's access consistent with the active perspective; social omniscience must not become mind-reading.\n\nDelayed consequence should have real staging. A remark made lightly in one scene can acquire force later when a fact about the fantastic world changes what it meant; a small decision about access, hospitality, or disclosure can return as leverage. When a reversal comes, ground it in a concrete shift—new information, a changed alliance, a revealed capacity, a discovered limit—rather than a general mood swing. Physical consequences, if they occur, should be clearly placed in space and sequence. Violence is not required, and neither is a crime setting. The fantastic premise may create danger, obligation, wonder, or bureaucratic inconvenience according to what the world already permits.\n\nA conversation can end unresolved, a task can continue, and a reversal can open a new practical problem rather than close the scene. Use recurrence when it deepens the social meaning of an earlier detail, not as a required beat. The prose is formal because it is deliberate and socially situated, not because every character must speak in elaborate sentences or because the setting must be aristocratic.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nStart inside a live telling: someone needs the story to do something now, and the listener has a reason to interrupt, bargain, or test it. The teller is bidding for a specific concession—time, cooperation, permission, a changed decision, a withheld object. The listener wants something the teller has not yet given: proof, a name, a practical answer, a competing account. Make the situation constrain the telling from the first exchange.\n\nTreat the embedded tale as a dialogue-level move, not a sealed performance. The teller selects and emphasizes details to serve the bid: an episode may be slowed to keep the listener waiting, simplified to seem harmless, or amplified to echo the present demand. The listener breaks in at the moment of a fantastic effect, an unexplained mercy, or a suspicious convenience, and the interruption forces the teller to renegotiate the tale—supply a detail under pressure, change emphasis, or expose a stake the teller hoped to keep hidden. The teller's authority is not fixed; each interruption shifts who controls what gets told next.\n\nLet the fantasy elements be part of the world and have consequences. Whatever impossible thing the story contains—a working charm, a changed body, a speaking animal, an altered place—it must affect the characters' options inside the tale and be available to echo into the frame if the listener recognizes its relevance. Do not need the inner story to explain every mechanism; but the fantastical turn should change what the people in the tale can do, and the listener may ask what that implies for the outer situation.\n\nUse repeated phrases or objects across narrative levels. They accrue meaning through the telling: a promise repeated in the tale may be quoted by the listener for a current demand; an object described in the tale may be sitting near the frame. These recurrences are not proof; they are pressure. A listener can use them to show that the teller knows more than they claimed, or that the tale is aimed at them. The teller can return to a repeated phrase as a delaying gesture, a taunt, or an appeal.\n\nBuild reversals from established particulars. A change of control happens when the listener spots an omission, the teller uses a withheld fact, or a repeated detail reveals a different meaning. Keep the fantastical premise consistent after the reversal; a surprise should re-read what came before, not replace it. Preserve the difference between what the teller says happened and what the frame can confirm. The final turn should be legible from the scene's actual power relations, not from a universal rule that every storyteller must lie or every listener must be betrayed.\n\nThe frame is the main scene. The inner story need not pause every paragraph, but cross-cutting should feel like a negotiation: the listener's pressure shapes what gets told next. Use the storyteller's language to reveal their particular intelligence, fear, wit, or vanity, not an identical quippy voice for everyone. The tale can be comic, tender, frightening, or matter-of-fact according to its content and teller. Do not add a palace, a djinn, or a cliffhanger simply because the frame technique is Arabian Nights-inspired; the situation and the world's fantasy contract govern what appears.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic-uncanny route. Open with a specific transaction already in progress: someone is trying to complete a piece of ordinary business—a signature, a delivery, a payment, a permission—in a concrete social setting with named obligations and a clear time of day. Keep place, time, and the practical object of the transaction concrete so the impossible interruption lands against them. Do not begin in a dream or an obviously supernatural location.\n\nLet the impossible element enter through a person, object, or event that the world's fantasy premise already allows. It should interrupt the transaction as a complication, not a spectacle: the document begins to answer before it is signed, the delivered animal speaks a condition, the payment returns with a different face. The characters may know this thing exists, belong to it, or be discovering it; their responses should be socially particular, not a generic chorus of wonder. Let the initial exchange treat the uncanny intrusion through ordinary formalities: someone tries to complete the original business, ask the practical question, or keep the conversation moving.\n\nUse Tarantino-style dialogue to stage the collision. The talk is an action: someone wants a signature, a promise, a confession, or a concession. The impossible presence changes the stakes of what they want. Keep exchanges long enough for conflicting purposes to become clear. Let one character withhold a fact, refuse the direct question, or answer with a promising detail while another presses for a practical consequence. The unusual element should affect the rhythm of the conversation—not be merely described between speeches.\n\nDelay a physical consequence until its moment is precisely staged. If someone touches the changed animal, signs the altered document, drinks the offered cup, or opens the sealed door, show the effect only when it matters to the power relation: a sudden loss of the room's ordinary sound, a visible change in one speaker's ease, an object behaving in a way that forces a choice. The fantasy can remain unexplained while its consequences are concrete. Avoid importing a ghost, vampire, or Grimm-tale prop unless the supplied world already contains it.\n\nThe narrative should move between particular observation and the uncanny's pressure. Follow the selected route's intimate closeness: sensory details from the room, the speakers' movements, their social self-consciousness. When the uncanny acts, let syntax register it: a sentence may stop, repeat, or shift from formal speech to bare happening. But do not imitate a translated word order or assign a national temperament. The original fiction should remain in the voice of its active narrator.\n\nPayoffs work by recognition, not exile. A character may understand the impossible thing differently by the end, but no one is required to become a hero, confess, or reunite. The final beat can show an unanswered question, a newly possible action, or a changed obligation. Let the fantastical premise keep its local meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the scene in a present-day, lived-in environment where inherited authority operates through ordinary modern channels. Open with a specific practical request addressed to a specific holder of that authority: someone wants entry, permission, information, safety, or release, and the request must pass through the channel the power actually uses—a key, a lease, a permit, a name, a debt, a memory. Do not turn this into a secret vampire corporation; keep the power's mechanisms tied to what the world's fantasy premise already allows.\n\nShow the collision of old authority and present life through the scene's particular relationships. The holder of power may grant the request, delay it, or attach a cost. The negotiation happens in dialogue that acts: each question, evasion, reminder, or concession changes who can act next. Status is earned in the exchange, not inherited by a name alone.\n\nKeep intimacy concrete. Show the physical details that carry history—an elevator mirror, a table scarred by a house ritual, a phone with old contacts, a coat too warm for the room. Reflect private desire and social mask explicitly: a character may want safety, revenge, recognition, or closeness, and the face they present may contradict that want. Masks should fall away in trusted company, but trust need not produce a full confession; it can reveal a single unguarded need or a dangerous truth.\n\nDelay consequences until they become physical. If someone breaks a rule, touches a guarded object, or speaks a forbidden word, stage the effect when it matters: the light changes, the ordinary sound stops, the room's geography becomes hostile, or a person's body obeys the old command. The atmosphere should intensify before and during these moments, not replace cause with mood. Maintain the fantastical rule: whatever the power does must be a possible consequence of the world's established premise, not a new invention to force a climax.\n\nThe final turn should re-read the negotiation. A concession, a refusal, or a withheld answer can reveal that the everyday guise was itself the old authority's operation. But no one is required to betray, perish, or be redeemed. Preserve the moral complication: both the one seeking help and the one holding power may have understandable, incompatible needs. The urban setting can be dangerous, but danger must arise from the specific arrangement, not from a universal rule that every intimacy is a trap.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Open with a specific present demand that a shared story about the fantastic past is being used to settle: a journey to be blessed, a marriage to be reconsidered, a debt to be forgiven, a boundary to be redrawn, or a historical grievance to be addressed. The story is a fact of the world's fantasy premise, known to the community, and its application is the matter under dispute. The shared knowledge affects daily arrangements now.\n\nMake the frame a live negotiation. Someone wants the demand granted; others answer with versions of the shared story. Use Tarantino-style dialogue so these versions are moves, not recitations: a speaker may cite a detail to shame, warn, justify, or delay. The listener can interrupt with a correction, an accusation, or a practical question the story does not answer.\n\nEmbed short bursts of the tale at moments of pressure. When the negotiation stalls, a character tells an episode that seems to support a demand. Do not make these bursts long, sealed digressions; let the outer scene break in with a question about a detail, a challenge to the teller's right to speak it, or a demand to hear what happened next. The inner episode should be told in a voice that belongs to the teller: formal, intimate, playful, or grief-worn. It may contain a metamorphosis, a magical fruit, a speaking shadow, or another impossibility allowed by the active fantasy contract.\n\nDifferentiate knowledge and authority. Older tellers may know more of the story but not necessarily what it now commands. A younger character may know a forgotten verse that changes its application. A character may misremember or reshape an episode to win the argument. The repeated lines, place names, or ritual gestures should accrue meaning during the scene; a phrase quoted early with confidence may return later with a different edge. This contest of interpretation is the plot pressure. Do not resolve it by deciding the oldest speaker or the most faithful memory is automatically right; the present stakes should force the story to be re-read.\n\nLet the fantastic past have present consequences without silent miracles. The speaking river may mark the land under dispute; the returning changed ancestor may make a household rule still binding; the prohibition may mean a modern contract cannot be signed without a particular offering. Show these consequences as practical constraints the negotiation must handle, not as atmosphere. If a new impossible event occurs in the frame, stay inside the established premise.\n\nThe turn comes when a version is interrupted, completed, or applied. A withheld episode can shift control; a correction can expose a claimant's bluff; a repeated phrase can authorize or block a step. But no character is required to win by rhetoric or lose by memory. The final beat can leave the shared story reopened, a permission granted, or a boundary changed. Keep the language and setting specific to the supplied community; do not impose English, code-switching, religion, caste, or a generic family structure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaval::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the early-modern comic/theatrical route. Open with a specific impossible condition already shaping the scene's stakes: a debt, an inheritance, a contract, a marriage, a promotion, or public standing is bound to a world-known fantastic rule, and each character has a visible stake in how that rule is applied. The impossible fact is not a metaphor; it actually changes everyone's position.\n\nBuild the scene through Tarantino-style dialogue that acts. People negotiate, blame, flatter, and insult. One speaker can withhold a practical detail; another can expose a hidden appetite. Repetition and comic callbacks should do work: a phrase about the price of a melon returns when the price becomes a goblin's demand; an earlier polite lie about hunger is quoted when someone must eat exactly what was sworn. Timing is the engine: a pause, a second offer, a refusal, a forgotten name, or a sudden correction can shift status. Let the exchanges have space to turn, not just quick wit.\n\nLet the comic pressure come from appetite and social embarrassment, not from suppressing feeling. Show a character's particular greed, fear of appearing foolish, vanity, lust, family pride, or discomfort. The fantastic circumstance should intensify these rather than replace them. The early-modern comic route allows exaggeration and broad reversals: a boast about never borrowing leads to a visit from the personification of debt; a meal prepared to bribe an official is eaten by the wrong hungry ghost. Keep the world's fantastical rules consistent when the farce lands.\n\nDelay the physical consequence until it changes the power relation. If someone tastes the wrong food, breaks an old seal, or speaks the name of a forbidden thing, stage the effect precisely when the characters must acknowledge it: the floor becomes sticky, the written contract rewrites itself, the mirror shows the wrong face. The consequence can be comic, alarming, or both, but it must follow from the established fantastical premise.\n\nThe narrative voice can be present, theatrical, and socially alert. Use direct sensory details from the scene: the heat of the brazier, the smell of drying pickles, the way a screen has been left too long without repair. Describe small stage business—who is nearer the door, who holds the only cup—because it affects the negotiation. Do not assign a uniform national quietness or a conventional seasonal inventory. If the active narration belongs to a particular character, their language and attention should mark the scene; if it is an external performance, keep the staging clear.\n\nThe turn is not a moral lesson. It is a re-reading: the most polite guest may be the one who wrote the trap; the proud official may be the only person who knows how the impossible ledger actually works. A victory can be foolish, a loss can be lucky. End when the new arrangement is clear, not on a compulsory cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the intimate psychological or urban fiction route. Open with a specific transaction already entangled with a known fantastic obligation: a sale, a debt, a document, a rental, travel, or a purchase is blocked or complicated by an impossible thing that belongs to the place and is known to the characters. The impossible thing is ordinary in this world; the drama is what it demands of this person now.\n\nUse Tarantino-style dialogue for the social negotiation. People are doing business: settling a debt, obtaining a document, renting a room, arranging travel, buying a used television. The talk is long enough to expose conflicting needs. Someone withholds a detail, someone answers a question with a question, someone uses flattery or witness to gain time. Let the fantastical fact enter as a complication in the negotiation: the promise attached to the statue blocks the sale; the parrot repeats a phrase that names a hidden theft; the lost-object machine delivers a jacket whose pocket contains a different person's key.\n\nStay inside one character's private experience. The scene should not be a floating observational report; choose a focal mind—the debtor, the tenant, the cousin who must return the borrowed ladder, the witness who does not want to testify. Give their direct perceptions, reasoning, and uncertain motives. They may be trying to appear calm while counting money, or angry that the impossible thing has picked this bad week. Their interior account is not a confession to another character; it is the narration's material. They can be anxious, greedy, amused, resentful, or practical. Do not make them universally witty or heroic.\n\nThe fantastical premise must affect what people can do, not merely tint the atmosphere. The saint's promise may be a legal or customary fact in the community; the truth-telling parrot may be admissible in the courtyard's gossip court; the machine's price may be scheduled. Show the practical consequences in the negotiation: a contract cannot be signed until the promise is honored, a bribe must account for what the parrot may say, a returned object forces a choice between two possible owners. Do not use an unexplained miracle to solve the scene.\n\nDelay the physical consequence until it changes the power relation. When the focal character touches the statue's foot, opens the delivered box, or answers the parrot's question, stage the effect precisely: the coin turns warm, the letter inside the jacket rewrites itself, the room's smell changes, or the street outside becomes the wrong street. Let the private response accompany this: not a general gasp but a particular recognition, panic, shame, or guarded relief. The consequence may undo or confirm a plan.\n\nThe turn re-reads the negotiation. A hidden relation between two characters may become visible; the focal person may realize what they actually wanted from the deal. But no one is required to be redeemed or punished. Preserve the intimate-urban weight: these people still have rent, family, work, and the fantastic event remains entangled with those things rather than lifting them out of the neighborhood. End on the new practical stake, not a moral lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Light Novel\n\nOpen inside the negotiation. Name the place and the practical object at stake in the first few lines, and establish the object's rule as already in play: a sealed train ticket, a shop's hourglass, a weapon's sheath, a key that opens only when someone speaks their true debt. The fantasy premise is already part of the world's rules, not a secret. If the scene uses a name, item, or mechanic that may be unfamiliar, explain it in one or two plain sentences within the narrative flow, without pausing the scene.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When someone steps closer, grabs a bag, touches the counter, or moves to block a door, show it as a separate action beat. Use short paragraphs around dialogue and consequence. Move from one speaker's demand to the other's evasive answer to the object's change without losing where the characters stand. The scene should feel stageable: who is inside the room, near the window, on stairs, behind glass, holding the only phone.\n\nMake the dialogue a power struggle. Someone wants something now; the other has a reason to refuse, delay, or name a higher cost. Let the exchange last long enough for both positions to surface. Use withheld intent—one speaker may know what the fantastic object does but hides it; another may suspect and test with a small lie or a practical question. Status changes in the scene: a confident demand becomes a concession after the object reacts, or a refusal becomes leverage when a new fact is revealed. Do not make every line witty or profane; the pressure comes from what each speaker needs.\n\nTie the fantastic premise to a real consequence. The hourglass may shorten the working day of whoever holds it; the key may forget one memory each time it opens; the ticket may show the name of only one passenger and cause the other to be left behind. These should be established by what the characters already know or are learning. The premise should block one easy action and open another—never just decorate the conversation. When a speaker mentions a rule, the other may test it or quote it back later.\n\nUse reaction beats after major turns, but make them the focal character's actual private response, not a generic pause. After a threat, a reveal, or an object's change, give one or two sentences of the focal person's seeing or reasoning: how their throat feels, what they notice about the other's hand, why they decide to sit down or raise their voice. The reaction can be uncertain, angry, frightened, or practical. Do not add a thought-length quota; only include reaction when the causal moment requires it.\n\nDelay the big physical consequence until it changes the power relation. If a key is used, a word is spoken, or the hourglass is turned, stage the result at the exact moment it matters: the door opens onto the wrong place; the object begins to count; the room's light shifts. The payoff should be legible from what came before. It can stop the scene at a new instability, but do not force a cliffhanger if the current negotiation has simply reached its next practical step. Preserve the active narrator—first or third person according to the model's existing instructions; Light Novel style does not mandate first person or a young cast.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite a scene where the fantastic premise is not a spectacle but a condition inside ordinary talk. Let the narration hold the pressure between characters as a musical tension, with clauses lengthening when another person hesitates and tightening when a withheld fact or sudden challenge turns the exchange against one speaker. Use the cadence of the conversation to shape paragraph breaks: a long, image-building sentence may open a spoken pause, and a short declarative may snap after an evasion. When a power shift occurs, the imagery should turn with it—not by decorating the line but by changing where attention rests, so the same object becomes a comfort, a reminder, or a threat depending on what has just been said.\n\nDevelop each fantastic element as an image that recurs with altered meaning. A charm, a sealed doorway, a vanished animal, or a debt of magic first appears in the rhythm of negotiation or recollection rather than in an explanatory block. When dialogue reopens that image later, its associations gather around the new threat or promise, letting the conversation carry the worldbuilding. The narration shows how this particular wonder has shaped the speaker's longings, habits, or fears by following the image into the associations the viewpoint character can actually make, so the impossible remains a lived condition rather than a mapped system.\n\nKeep the chosen viewpoint perspective distinct from other minds. When a character thinks through another's motive, that thought stays private in the narration or hidden interiority rather than becoming spoken omniscience. Let dialogue be plain when plainness serves the character's authority or vulnerability, and let the lyric register shape that plainness through the prose around it: a character may speak in short practical words while the narration turns their silence into a shaped pause. End the response at the active boundary, with the immediate exchange, realization, or unresolved pressure still present; the next turn may continue the rhythm without requiring a completed reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagavap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Quentin Tarantino / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite fantasy through the weight of past occasions and unfinished attachments, using conversation as the site where memory is negotiated. Let each exchange carry the kind of slowdown that reflective prose loves: a factual question about a magical object or a past failure may be answerable, but the answer opens a memory the speaker would rather not settle. As one character pressures another, allow the narration to move between what is said, what is remembered, and what still cannot be admitted. Timing and withheld intent shape these reflections; a long pause before a reply can signal not evasion but the arrival of an old image, so that the next line changes the conversation's meaning.\n\nLet the fantasy premise live inside ordinary details: a changed season, a creature now gone, a spell that no longer works as it once did, a promise with consequences that arrived late. Those details recur as motifs, with each recurrence bearing a slightly different emotional register. Let present comfort and remembered loss share the same paragraph, so a meal, a laugh, or another person's presence can remain pleasurable while the prose still acknowledges the passage of time. The melancholy belongs to specific memories and longings, not to every sentence.\n\nKeep the viewpoint access clear. When one character's recollection is rendered, it comes from the active narrative's permitted interiority rather than from a false omniscience. Let a reversal turn a reflective moment into a new stakes-raising question, so the melancholy is not inert: the next line may expose a secret, correct a memory, or reveal that the earlier story served a current need. This keeps conversation an action even when its topic is the past. End at a natural boundary, with the memory and relationship still unfolding; do not require emotional closure or a tragic turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
