            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-fantasy-07",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Fantasy · 07/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 7 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPagazaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Wang Xiaobo / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings when location or time changes, and keep action lines concise. Establish the fantastic premise as an administered visible condition: a queue forming outside an office, a machine that must be fed, a notice pinned to a door, a household object behaving according to a rule everyone knows. Let the impossible thing have a practical place in the scene rather than appearing only as spectacle.\n\nLet dialogue carry the inquiry. A character asks a direct question about the premise, and another character answers with the official account. The scene then tests that account against observable conditions. A speaker may point out that the explanation does not match what just happened, that the rule protects the person explaining it, or that the procedure produces a result no one is willing to name. Keep the exchanges nimble and specific; do not let every character become a polished wit or a fixed skeptic.\n\nWhen a focal character's private reasoning matters, place it in a clearly attributed nonspoken note, not in dialogue. Another character does not hear that note. Use voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs it, and do not let it replace the visible scene's own evidence.\n\nAllow the scene to end on an unfinished exchange, a completed practical task, or a newly visible contradiction. Do not require a reversal, a confession, or a resolved explanation before the cut. The fantasy premise may remain partially unexplained, but its consequences for the people in the scene should be legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagazax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Wang Xiaobo / Stage Play\n\nWrite the fantastic premise as a condition the stage can show. An object sits on a table and must be handled in a particular way. A door opens only after a procedure. A character enters carrying something impossible and everyone in the scene knows what it means. Stage directions should establish these observable conditions economically: who can see the object, who can reach it, who is present when the rule is followed.\n\nLet the inquiry happen through dialogue and physical business. One character asks a practical question about the premise. Another supplies the official account. A third may test the account by doing the thing that should not be done, by pointing to the object that contradicts the explanation, or by asking who benefits from the rule. Keep the lines specific to the relationship and the immediate task. A speaker can be direct without being uniformly witty.\n\nWhen a focal character's private reasoning matters, keep it as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. Do not turn every private thought into an aside or monologue. If an aside or monologue is used, give it a dramatic purpose: the character is alone, addressing the audience under a specific convention, or speaking to someone who cannot hear. Another character does not know the content of a nonspoken note.\n\nLet the scene close on a staged result, an interruption, or a pause after a question. The fantasy premise need not be fully explained, but the audience should understand what the people on stage are doing about it and why the official account is under strain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagazay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Wang Xiaobo / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one particular authorized mind and let the fantastic premise enter through that mind's actual experience: a sound, a smell, a bodily sensation, a remembered instruction, a phrase from an official notice, a task interrupted. Move by association rather than by a linear report. A present perception can recall an earlier event, a private word, or a rule the mind was taught. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those connections.\n\nKeep the mind's rational habit active inside the associative movement. The mind returns to a question, holds an official explanation against a remembered fact, notices a contradiction between what it was told and what it is now seeing, or follows a practical implication that the official account avoids. The inquiry need not be tidy; it can surface, break off, and return when a new perception or memory revives it.\n\nDistinguish what is happening from what is remembered or imagined. Use enough orientation that the reader can tell whether the mind is in the present scene, recalling an earlier day, or entertaining a possibility. The fantasy premise itself remains an established condition of the world, not merely a private fantasy, even when the mind's associations are subjective.\n\nDo not make every character share this mind or grant the narration access to excluded minds. The mind's private reasoning is its own. The passage can end while the question is still open, provided the mind has made the absurdity or consequence of the situation legible through its own movement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagazaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Wang Xiaobo / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through sender-attributed messages. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in fragments, another in complete sentences, a third sends a question and then a correction. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they change what the participants know or do. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet the inquiry happen in the exchange. One sender asks a practical question about the fantastic premise. Another supplies the official account. A third responds with a detail that does not fit, asks who benefits from the rule, or reports what happened when the procedure was followed. The messages should test the official explanation against reported facts. Keep the argument nimble and specific; do not make every sender uniformly witty or combative.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. When context outside the exchange is needed, present it clearly separated from the transcript, and do not let it grant other characters knowledge of a sender's unstated reasoning.\n\nLet the exchange end on a pending question, a completed arrangement, or a newly visible contradiction. The fantasy premise may remain partially unexplained, but its practical consequences for the people messaging should be legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagazba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Wang Xiaobo / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting to establish the fantastic premise as a visible condition: the object on the desk, the queue outside the office, the altered sky, the household arrangement that everyone accepts. Attribute dialogue clearly and let focal narration or inner response follow in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. A background or expression cue should orient the moment, not serve as an automatic truth indicator or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nLet the focal character's reasoning carry the inquiry. Between exchanges, the focal narration notices a discrepancy between what the scene shows and what the official account claims. The character may follow the practical implication, ask who benefits from the rule, or state plainly that the explanation does not match the visible facts. Keep this reasoning owned by the focal character; other characters do not know it unless it is spoken or otherwise transmitted.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images merely because the style is selected. A discovery may open a new conversation rather than force a choice screen. Keep scene changes legible, and let the fantasy premise have intelligible consequences without requiring every mystery to become an explained system.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed observation, a pending question, or a newly visible contradiction. The focal character need not resolve the inquiry before the scene closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagazbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Wang Xiaobo / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: the narrator is pursuing a specific question about the fantastic premise, and each installment changes what is known, arranged, or possible. Recover context briefly only when it helps. If an earlier reply established a rule, an official account, or a practical consequence, recall the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole inquiry.\n\nLet the narrator's rational testing develop through local changes. A new fact qualifies an official explanation. A practical task reveals who benefits from a rule. A remembered detail acquires a new implication. The inquiry may advance through knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. Do not require a reversal, confession, or completed test in every reply, and do not make the fair answer perpetually recede.\n\nKeep the fantastic premise as an established condition of the world. Its consequences should be intelligible and cumulative, not exhaustively explained. Ordinary life continues around the inquiry: people eat, work, argue about money, and maintain the impossible thing. The narrator's private reasoning remains available under the active contract, but other characters do not know it unless it is spoken or otherwise transmitted.\n\nAn installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Use occasional hooks when they are earned, not as a mandatory cliffhanger at every boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet the fantastic element sit inside an already strained relationship, so that its impossible quality becomes inseparable from what someone needs, withholds, or cannot bring themselves to say. Choose one concrete sensory detail from the fantastic premise—a door that stays warm after being closed, a charm that dims when the wrong person holds it, a familiar voice that repeats only the words the listener fears—and return to that detail at intervals without explaining it fully. The detail should carry the emotional pressure rather than illustrate it.\n\nKeep the narration selective. Show the cost of emotional exposure through what the viewpoint notices and avoids noticing: the exact moment a hand withdraws, the pause before an apology that does not come, the way a room's ordinary sound becomes too loud after a confession. Let the conversation move forward while the hurt remains partly unspoken. When a character admits something, the admission may make the next moment harder rather than cleaner. Warmth can appear, but it should be partial, awkward, or tied to something already passing.\n\nDo not let the fantasy premise resolve the relationship or turn every hurt into a magical symptom. The impossible thing may make the strain more visible, but the characters must still choose, refuse, or fail to speak. Preserve the active narrator's distance and diction. If the viewpoint character is withholding something, the prose should withhold it from the reader only as long as the character's own attention withholds it, not through an artificial narrative trick. End the scene at a moment when the emotional cost has become concrete, without requiring a completed confession or a repaired bond.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in clear, readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Each turn should give the viewpoint character one or two concrete sensory impressions from the fantasy world—the texture of a spelled lock, the color of a familiar creature's breath, the weight of a charm that has grown heavier—and let those impressions orient the moment without pausing the scene for description. Keep the fantasy element active in the world: NPCs and environmental details should continue to behave according to the established premise even when no one is speaking.\n\nUse Kawabata's selectivity to keep turns emotionally legible. A character may notice something they do not say, and the narration can record that omission briefly without turning it into a hidden monologue. Let the gap between what a character notices and what they say create the emotional texture of the exchange. When a speaker withholds a feeling, show it through a small physical action or a shift in attention rather than announcing the feeling.\n\nEnd each response at a natural continuation point that follows from what has just happened. The next speaker should be able to respond to the scene's actual state, not to a forced question or cliffhanger. Do not require a choice screen, a dramatic reveal, or a completed emotional beat at the end. Preserve the active narrative person, action markers, and player control. The fantasy premise may complicate the situation, but it should not override the collaborative scene's need for clear, immediate, playable continuity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Place the fantasy element within a household, a neighborhood, a shop, or a small community where its impossible quality is handled through daily routines, practical adjustments, and social obligations. Do not treat the fantastic thing as a distant legend or a mythic-scale event; let it sit in a room, a courtyard, a market stall, or a family's private history, and show how people work around it, rely on it, or quietly avoid it.\n\nFollow Kawabata's selective sensory attention by choosing one or two physical details that anchor the fantasy element in the domestic world—a bowl that refills only when the household is at peace, a window that shows a different season than the street outside, a child's shadow that lingers after the child leaves. Return to that detail at intervals, letting its changing appearance or the characters' changing response to it carry the emotional and social meaning. Do not explain the mechanism.\n\nKeep the narration intimate and socially situated. Show how the fantasy element affects relationships, obligations, and the small decisions of daily life. A neighbor's curiosity, a family member's silence, or a visitor's misunderstanding can reveal as much as the fantastic thing itself. Preserve the active narrator's distance and the specific language, period, and community supplied by the story. Do not transplant a setting or cast identity from the cultural tradition; use its craft approach to organize the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the fantasy element through material wear and practical consequence. Show what the impossible thing costs in physical terms: a spell that leaves burns on the caster's hands, a portal that scuffs the floor each time it opens, a protective charm that cracks under repeated use. Keep the diction forceful and the transitions compressed. Do not linger over the wonder; let the characters handle it, maintain it, or suffer it like any other hard piece of their lives.\n\nUse Kawabata's selectivity to keep the grit from becoming a catalogue of damage. Choose one physical detail that carries the scene's emotional weight—a torn sleeve, a blade's notch, a smear of ash on a doorframe—and return to it without explaining its meaning. The relation between that detail and the character's state should be felt, not announced. When a character thinks or remembers something, keep the thought brief and grounded in the body or the immediate task.\n\nLet the fantasy premise create intelligible physical stakes. The strange thing may be dangerous, unreliable, or expensive to use, but its consequences should follow from the established world rather than from a sudden horror escalation. Preserve the active narrator's distance and diction. End the scene when the physical situation has reached a clear pressure point, without requiring a completed action or a resolved threat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the fantasy element through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Each writer should know only part of the impossible thing, and what they record or conceal should follow from their situation: a household inventory that notes a strange object without naming its power, a letter that describes a wonder the writer half doubts, a report that omits the detail the official could not explain. Let the gaps between documents reveal as much as the documents themselves.\n\nUse Kawabata's selectivity to shape what each writer notices. A letter may dwell on one sensory detail—the color of a flame that gave no heat, the silence after a door closed on its own, the weight of a charm that felt lighter in another person's hand—while leaving the larger event unstated. The feeling should live in the selection and omission, not in an explicit confession. When a document records a conversation or an event, keep the account partial and situated in the writer's knowledge at that time.\n\nDo not add an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the sequence of documents build a readable continuity through recurrence, contradiction, and material traces. The fantasy premise may appear differently in different accounts, but the differences should follow from who is writing and why, not from a random rotation of perspectives. Use the specific document form required by the scene rather than a fixed cycle through letters, journals, and reports.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet the fantasy element provide small, practical comfort within the story's actual conditions. The impossible thing may warm a room, mend a favorite garment, keep a meal fresh, or make a familiar place feel safer, but it should remain part of the world rather than a magical cure for every difficulty. Spend attention on chosen kindness and familiar preferences: the way someone sets a charm beside a sleeping child, the particular tea that appears when a friend is tired, the quiet pride in a small spell that works reliably.\n\nUse Kawabata's selective sensory attention to show warmth through concrete details. Choose one or two impressions—the smell of bread from a spelled oven, the soft weight of a blanket that holds heat longer than it should, the way a protective light dims to a comfortable glow—and let them carry the affection. Do not announce that the scene is comforting. Let the care live in the selection and arrangement of small physical things.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. A character may accept kindness awkwardly, prefer to be left alone, or disagree about how the fantasy element should be used. Let the scene enjoy particular people without forcing a confession or an emotional repair. End at a moment of ease or small pleasure that follows naturally from the interaction, without requiring a completed emotional beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the fantasy premise through composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration frame the impossible thing through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it: a family's long arrangement with a strange inheritance, a community's unspoken rules about a place that does not stay the same, an institution's careful record of something it cannot explain. The fantasy element should affect experience, decisions, and obligations without becoming a fully explained system.\n\nUse Kawabata's selective sensory attention to keep the narration from turning into an explanatory account. Choose one or two physical impressions that anchor the impossible thing in the scene—the way a ceremonial object changes temperature when a particular person enters, the faint sound a sealed room makes at dusk, the color of a written charm as it ages—and return to them without stating their full meaning. Let the relation among these details carry the emotional and social weight.\n\nKeep the narration distinct from ordinary spoken language. The prose may move through broader paragraphs and social observation, but it should remain selective rather than exhaustive. When the narrator offers judgment or history, ground it in the specific world and community supplied by the story. Do not impose an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech. End the passage when the social and sensory frame has become clear, without requiring a completed explanation or a dramatic resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the scene around a specific telling: someone recounts an encounter with the impossible to listeners who have a present reason to hear it. Establish the occasion through the teller's immediate purpose—what they want the listeners to do, permit, believe, or stop fearing—and let that purpose govern the order and emphasis of the embedded tale.\n\nInside the embedded tale, build the teller's memory from a small inventory of exact sensory impressions: one quality of light, one surface or texture, one sound, one temperature. Return to these same impressions rather than adding new ones. When the impossible enters, let it disturb one of these established details—the light changes without a source, the familiar texture is wrong, the sound repeats after it should have stopped—so the fantasy premise arrives as a precise alteration in what the teller already knew, not as an explained event.\n\nLet the teller's selection and omission carry feeling. They may linger on a detail they cannot reconcile, skip what they did not understand, or repeat a phrase because it is the only way they can hold the memory. Do not state their fear or wonder directly. The outer listeners should register what is missing through small physical shifts: a hand stilled on a cup, a breath held, a glance toward someone else in the room.\n\nMove between levels with a clear gesture: a repeated phrase, object, or image that appears in the frame and then inside the tale. Each recurrence should slightly alter its meaning, so the outer situation becomes legible through the embedded story. The fantasy element need not be explained as a system, but its consequences must be concrete: show what it changed in the teller's body, routines, or obligations.\n\nKeep the outer frame active during the telling. Interruptions, questions, or a listener's small movement should return the reader to the present moment without breaking the selective surface; write them as brief sensory observations in the same register as the tale. End with the frame's attention resting on one of the established recurring details—a cup, a scar, a closed door—now carrying the weight of what the tale has changed between teller and listeners.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny narrative route. Write from a narrator who observes the impossible through a composed, syntactically patient surface. Let subordinate clauses extend attention around a small inventory of ordinary things: a familiar room's window and floor, a daily walk's repeated sounds, a task's tools and surfaces. The syntax should hold these details in relation, not ornament them.\n\nEstablish the inventory before the fantasy premise appears. Return to the same details at intervals rather than adding new ones. When the uncanny enters, let it disturb one of these established particulars: the window that was closed now stands open, the coat that was hung is warm, the clock that kept time has stopped. The impossible is not explained as a system; it is felt as a precise alteration in what the narrator already trusted.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance consistent. The uncanny is not a permission for fragmented syntax or sudden emotional excess. Feeling gathers in the relation between the changed detail and the unchanged ones around it—the open window and the still room, the warm coat and the cold air—rather than in direct statements about fear or wonder.\n\nShow the fantasy premise's consequences through particular experience. A workplace, household, or civic arrangement may respond to the impossible, but write those responses as specific people adjusting specific routines: someone closes the window twice, checks the clock against another, avoids the room at a certain hour. Do not generalize into bureaucratic response.\n\nEnd with attention resting on one of the established details now passing or difficult to keep—the window still ajar, the clock still stopped, the warmth already fading—so the uncanny remains present without being resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Gothic-Punk\n\nPlace the fantasy premise at the point where old authority and present life collide. Write the encounter through a specific character's selective attention. Build a small inventory of the encounter's material world: a room's worn surface, the temperature of a hand, the sound of a modern device against an old wall, a light that burns or fails. Return to these same details rather than adding new ones.\n\nLet inherited power register as a disturbance in that inventory. The worn surface is newly warm, the hand's temperature does not match the room, the device's sound repeats after it should have stopped, the light changes without a source. Do not explain the power's source or rules. Its weight is felt in the character's attention to these precise alterations and in what they do next—check, avoid, protect, or approach.\n\nKeep the atmosphere intimate. Urban decay and political tension should appear as the texture of a particular relationship or encounter, not as a broad survey. When a social mask falls away in trusted company, write the change as a small physical shift within the established inventory: a lowered voice, a turned wrist, a pause before speech, a hand that stops moving. Do not replace the shift with confession or exposition.\n\nUse Kawabata's emotional omission. The fantasy element may be dangerous, desirable, or difficult to keep, but the narration should not state that feeling directly. Let it gather in the relation between the changed detail and the unchanged ones around it. Private desire and morally complicated intimacy remain present without forcing a betrayal or collapse; a character's want should shape which details they notice and return to.\n\nEnd with the encounter's atmosphere still unresolved, resting on one of the established details now gathering new meaning—a door that does not close, a light left burning, a mark that will fade—so the collision between old authority and present life remains present without being resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the lyric or philosophical inwardness route. Write from within a character's reflective attention as they encounter the impossible. Build a small inventory of exact sensory impressions: one quality of light, one texture, one sound that repeats, one object the character touches or sees. Return to these same impressions rather than adding new ones.\n\nLet the fantasy premise appear as a disturbance in that inventory. The light changes without a source, the familiar texture is wrong, the sound repeats after it should have stopped, the object is warm or cold when it should not be. Do not explain the impossible as a system. Its consequences should be concrete: a task the character now performs differently, a place they avoid, a person they watch more closely.\n\nKeep the inwardness specific. The character's thoughts may move between the immediate sensory world and a larger question or memory, but each abstraction must return to one of the established details. If the character reflects on time, let them notice the changed light again; if they reflect on loss, let them touch the wrong texture. The philosophical dimension arises from their actual attention, not from free-floating speculation.\n\nLet feeling gather in the relation between the changed detail and the unchanged ones around it rather than in direct emotional statement. Preserve the specific language, region, community, and period only where the story supplies them. Do not impose English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure. End with attention resting on one of the established impressions now passing or difficult to keep—a scent, a shadow, a phrase remembered—so the fantasy's presence lingers without being resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic tales route. Present the impossible as a strange event within an otherwise recognizable world: it may arrive through an encounter, an object, a place, or a moment of changed perception. Do not explain it as a system. Let the fantasy premise remain partly beyond understanding while its consequences are concrete and legible.\n\nBuild the telling from a small inventory of exact sensory impressions: one color, one texture, one sound, one quality of light. Return to these same impressions rather than adding new ones. When the strange event enters, let it disturb one of these established details—the color changes without a source, the familiar texture is wrong, the sound repeats after it should have stopped—so the impossible arrives as a precise alteration in what the teller or focal character already knew.\n\nKeep narrative distance and personal language tied to the actual teller or focal character. If the story is told by someone who witnessed the strange event, their selection and omission should reflect what they understood and what they cannot bear to repeat. Let them linger on a detail they cannot reconcile, skip what they did not understand, or repeat a phrase because it is the only way they can hold the memory. If the narration is more distant, keep the distance consistent and let the fantasy register through precise observation of the same recurring details.\n\nLet feeling gather in the relation between the changed detail and the unchanged ones around it rather than in direct statements about wonder or fear. The fantasy premise should affect experience, decisions, or everyday life in specific ways: a changed routine, a new obligation, a place now avoided. End with attention resting on one of the established impressions now passing or difficult to keep, so the strange event remains present without being resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic or metaphysical construction route. Present the impossible as a real disturbance within an otherwise ordinary world: it may enter through an object, a place, a person, or a moment of changed perception. Do not treat it as ordinary, and do not explain it as a system. Let the fantasy premise remain strange while its consequences are concrete and legible.\n\nBuild the scene from a small inventory of exact physical impressions: one quality of light, one texture, one sound, one object the character touches or sees. Return to these same impressions rather than adding new ones. When the impossible enters, let it disturb one of these established details—the light changes without a source, the familiar texture is wrong, the sound repeats after it should have stopped—so the disturbance is felt as a precise rupture in what the character already knew.\n\nLet the narrative's language, community, and history shape which details the character notices and how they interpret the disturbance. Use the story's actual supplied setting rather than imposing a generic regional temperament. The fantasy premise must be one the world already permits; the style shapes how it is experienced and told, not what exists.\n\nShow the impossible's consequences in particular experience: a changed routine, a new obligation, a place now avoided, a person watched more closely. The metaphysical dimension may raise questions the story does not answer, but those questions should arise from the character's actual attention to the changed detail and return to it. Let feeling gather in the relation between the changed detail and the unchanged ones around it rather than in direct statements about wonder or fear.\n\nEnd with attention resting on one of the established impressions now passing or difficult to keep—the light still wrong, the texture still strange, the sound still repeating—so the disturbance remains present without being resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly: establish where the character is, what they are doing, and what changes when the fantasy premise appears. Keep spatial changes easy to follow with short, clear transitions. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response, so the reader can track movement and reaction without losing the scene.\n\nBuild the scene from a small inventory of exact sensory impressions: one quality of light, one texture, one sound, one object the character touches or sees. Return to these same impressions rather than adding new ones. When the fantasy premise enters, let it disturb one of these established details—the light changes without a source, the familiar texture is wrong, the sound repeats after it should have stopped—so the impossible arrives as a concrete visual or sensory shift rather than through explanation. If unfamiliar names or mechanics appear, present them clearly when needed, but do not explain the fantasy element as a system.\n\nWrite focused reaction beats with Kawabata's emotional omission. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner, but the narration should not state the feeling directly. Let it gather in the character's attention to the changed detail and the unchanged ones around it: they notice one thing and not another, return to the wrong texture, or find that an ordinary object has become strange. Keep each beat short and visually anchored so the Light Novel's readability remains intact.\n\nUse whatever narrator and cast the story actually supplies. Show the fantasy premise's consequences in what the character does, avoids, or protects. End with attention resting on one of the established impressions now passing or difficult to keep—a sound that fades, a mark that will disappear, a door left open—so the fantasy's presence lingers without forcing a reversal or a completed discovery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Lyrical Prose\n\nTreat the impossible as one more thing present in the scene, available to the senses and to the consequences it leaves. Do not stop to explain its mechanism. Open the paragraph with one or two exact physical impressions of the fantastic thing or its effect: the way the light behaves around it, the sound it makes against ordinary work, the particular change it brings to the air. Then let the syntax extend into a comparison only when that comparison opens a further understanding, such as the impossible thing seen as a held breath, a door that remembers being shut, or a season that has learned to return without being asked. Keep the image traceable to what the character actually perceives or associates, and follow the extended figure with a plain sentence or a small action to set it in relief.\n\nLet the fantastic premise alter a practical decision, a household arrangement, or a journey's route. Choose one consequence that is already at work in the scene rather than pausing to introduce the magic as a separate event. Recurring sounds or images may gather meaning across the scene, but return to them only when a later occurrence gives them a new application; do not force a motif onto every difficulty. The narration may move from the impossible thing to an ordinary task and back again, so that wonder and work occupy the same continuous attention.\n\nSelect one aspect of the fantastic that is passing or difficult to keep: it may be visible only at a particular hour, dependent on a person's attention, or already fading from the place where it once worked. Let the relation among details carry the feeling without announcing wonder or loss. End where the present attention naturally rests, not where the mystery has been solved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the fantastic premise enter through what it has already changed. Begin the scene with a present physical detail that holds the past against the present: a door that no longer needs to be locked, a garden where the old planting pattern still shows, a task now done alone. From that detail, move into a specific remembered consequence of the fantastic, such as the occasion when the impossible first appeared, a small irreversible alteration in the household or landscape, or an attachment that could not be kept once the world worked differently. Then return to the present detail with the difference made perceptible, so that the reflection completes a movement rather than merely stating that things have changed.\n\nKeep the present scene brief and selective. Let one or two physical impressions carry the association without explaining every connection. The impossible element need not be dramatic. It may be a minor magic that changed the season of a particular crop, a creature that returned once and never again, or a bargain whose consequence is still being paid in small installments. What matters is what the character now notices or misses because of it, and that noticing should be shown through the chosen detail rather than summarized as a general mood.\n\nDo not turn the treatment into constant sadness or make every scene end by announcing impermanence. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable: a warm meal, a familiar voice, a task completed well. The reflection may find that something has been lost and something else has remained, and the scene can rest in that coexistence. End where the present attention naturally settles, not where the loss has been resolved or the magic explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral account: a teller speaks to a particular audience in a particular place, and the fantastic premise enters through the tale the teller chooses to relate. Establish the frame briefly through sensory detail—the light where the listeners sit, the sound outside the room, the teller's pause before beginning—without making the frame ornamental. The teller's access is limited to what they know, witnessed, or were told; they do not enter every character's mind.\n\nHave the teller select one or two physical impressions and connect them to a consequence of the fantastic, rather than inventorying the marvelous. The impossible thing may be described by its effect on a familiar object or routine: a well that fills at the wrong hour, a child who remembers a city she has never seen, a trade that succeeds only when the trader gives something away. Let the tale's consequences matter more than its mechanism. The teller may leave a connection unexplained because the teller does not know it or because the omission belongs to the telling, and the narration should preserve that limit rather than supplying an omniscient explanation.\n\nReturn to the frame briefly when the audience's presence or the teller's attention to something passing—a lamp burning low, a listener who has fallen asleep, the hour when the tale must end—changes the account's shape. The frame's return may interrupt the tale, shorten it, or carry the teller's own awareness that the telling cannot hold everything. End with the tale unfinished or with the listeners returning to ordinary business, as the frame's actual limit requires. Do not require a moral, a completed nested cycle, or a revelation that explains the magic. The wonder and the transience of the telling can stand together.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Minimalist Prose\n\nTreat the impossible as a fact in the scene. Name it once, precisely, and choose the single consequence that matters in the present scene. Show that consequence through a physical detail or a small action. If the well gives back what was thrown away, show the child checking the bucket before she drinks. If the road shortens for those who walk it without speaking, show the two travelers arriving early and saying nothing about it. Do not explain the mechanism. Do not repeat the wonder in different words.\n\nChoose one or two exact physical details and keep the clauses direct. A detail may carry the feeling: the worn handle of a door that opens only for the one who mended it, the clean space on a shelf where a returned object used to sit. Let an essential thought be long enough to make sense, but cut the explanation that only restates what the detail has already shown. Omission should leave the consequence inferable: the reader can see why the character acts differently, even when the narration does not say so.\n\nKeep the attention on something passing or difficult to keep without announcing it. The impossible may be already fading, or present only while a particular person remains in the room. Close the scene with one plain sentence that records the present state or action, such as the fire had gone out, the door stayed shut, or she left the lamp burning. End where the present action or observation naturally rests, not where the magic has been accounted for.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the fantastic premise into the ordinary movement of a scene. Let it appear in what a character notices while working, in a remark made during a meal, or in a decision that must be made before evening. Do not pause the scene to explain the magic. Its consequences belong to the world: a household adjusts its routine, a traveler chooses a different road, a child is taught a new precaution. Wonder may sit beside practical concern without either canceling the other.\n\nMove naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the viewpoint character's inner experience. Select one or two brief surfaces—a hand on a latch, the color of the sky through a window, the sound of a particular footstep—and let them carry the feeling of a longer exchange. From that surface, move into the character's inner experience or recollection when the present scene calls for it, and keep the recollection tied to what the character is doing or deciding. The narrator's voice may be plain or quiet, but it should not become a detached explainer of the fantastic.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. The impossible element may be passing or difficult to keep: it may work only at a certain hour, depend on a person's continued attention, or already be fading from the place. Let the scene end where its present attention naturally rests, such as a task finished, a conversation paused, or a decision postponed, without requiring the magic to be resolved or the character to reach a final understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the established relationship premise organize the scene. Choose a moment that makes the relationship's particular promise legible: an arrival, a shared task, a small betrayal, a comfort offered or withheld. The fantastic premise should affect the relationship's circumstances rather than stand apart from them. If the magic requires a price, show one character paying it and the other noticing. If it grants a private knowledge, show how that knowledge changes what they dare to say. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; use only the actual supplied premise.\n\nChoose a repeated moment or private phrase that carries the relationship's history—a particular phrase, a way of handing something over, a place they return to—and attach it to one or two exact sensory details in the present scene: the warmth of a cup passed between them, the pause before a door is opened, the way one character's name sounds when the other is tired. Let the fantastic premise change what that repeated moment now means or permits, so that the relationship's private language registers the altered circumstance without being explained. Keep the relation among these details carrying the feeling.\n\nGive the emotional consequence enough space to develop, but do not make every character speak in the same figurative register or announce their feelings in polished dialogue. The relationship may be passing or difficult to keep, and the scene can rest in that uncertainty. End where the present exchange naturally pauses—a hand withdrawn, a question left unanswered, a task resumed—without requiring a confession, a resolution, or a payoff that the scene has not earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the sense of being retold. Open with a deliberate statement that places the deed in memory: what was done, who was there, what came of it. Then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. The fantastic premise should appear as a deed or a condition whose consequences are remembered: a bargain struck with something that did not speak, a journey shortened by a road that no longer exists, a gift that changed what the receiver could keep. Do not explain the magic; let the chronicle record what it did.\n\nUse compact declarations to establish a person or a remembered act, and return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Select one or two sensory details at the moment of the deed's consequence—the color of a banner at the moment it fell, the sound of a name spoken after a long absence, the particular way a door was left open—and let them carry the feeling of the remembered act without an inventory. The chronicle may note what was passing or difficult to keep, such as a kinship that held only for one season or a place that was safe for a single winter, without turning every deed into heroic doom.\n\nLet the teller's access stay consistent with the selected perspective. The account may be interrupted by an ordinary practical concern or by someone who remembers the deed differently, but this need not become parody. Keep the source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention. End with the act's consequence available to be remembered, as the teller's actual access and the account's pause determine, not with the chronicle declaring a final legend on everyone's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTreat the established impossible condition as part of a settlement's accepted knowledge, not as a mystery to be explained. Whatever the premise is—a barn that grants a single night's dream as shared weather, a fjord bird that carries a person's name, a woman whose spinning keeps a village clock honest—write it as something already woven into work, water, or a house. Characters may respect, avoid, bargain with, or take it for granted according to their practical lives. Do not let everybody explain it at once; one neighbor may ask, another may strike a nail into a particular post without saying why.\n\nPresent the impossible through brief, exact perceptions rather than narrative wonder. Select one or two sensory details at a time: the changed smell of hay, a footprint filled with meltwater, one thread that does not lie flat. Let the detail appear at the edge of a sentence and then recede. If a character knows the secret, let the knowledge surface indirectly—a pause before planting, a doubled knot, a changed route home—rather than in performed folk commentary.\n\nKeep rural and seasonal work concrete: mowing, net mending, berry picking, a stove lit early. These tasks carry the story's forward movement; the impossible touches them without becoming the only thing the characters attend to. Short scenes may stay inside one or two people. Preserve required interiority in the active presentation as ordinary private prose, not as spoken folklore. The supernatural's passing quality lives in what is difficult to keep, so let each appearance be noticed once and then gone, leaving its consequence to show in a later gesture or a silence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Screenplay\n\nWrite action lines that select one or two sense details per scene: a lamp swaying after a door closes, rain darkening a stone step, a fish left in a basket that is still breathing. Let the fantastic premise alter ordinary movement through these details—one extra stair a character forgets to climb, a mirror that shows the room a minute earlier, a gate that opens without sound. Show the characters' response as physical attention: a hand pausing over a latch, a head turned toward a sound, a step taken back. Do not let them explain what they notice.\n\nUse dialogue to acknowledge the impossible indirectly when a character would speak of it at all. A question like \"You didn't hear it?\" can carry the acknowledgment without naming the premise. More often, let the characters talk about supper, a repair, a journey, while the impossible detail sits in the action line beside them. When a private reaction is required, place it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not as a voice-over pretending the thought is audible.\n\nUse cuts to show the passage that matters. A filled water jar where an empty one stood, a hand resting on a latch that was not there—each cut carries the consequence of the impossible without a visible-effects sequence. A scene may end before the marvelous thing finishes appearing; the following scene can continue its consequence. Use silence and ordinary offscreen sound—a dog, a distant train, wind clicking a shutter—as part of the sensory selection, not as theme music. Let the fantasy remain a condition of the world rather than a series of effects to be displayed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Stage Play\n\nLet one central object, sound, or physical change carry the impossible condition through the performance. A locked drawer that holds tomorrow morning's light, a chair that turns away from the person who lies, a spoon that keeps the soup from boiling—whatever the premise is, write stage directions that let the actors, pauses, and ordinary objects perform the wonder. Let the impossible be visible to the audience, but keep the characters noticing only the aftermath: a washed cup dry before the dishcloth, a bootlace that unties itself and is left.\n\nUse omission on the stage rather than only in the dialogue. A character may leave before the impossible occurs, or stand too far off to see the object clearly. Entrance and exit timing controls what can be testified to within the play. Asides are performed speech and should not become the default for private thought; keep required interiority as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation.\n\nKeep the language spare. Characters may not name the impossible until the second or third time it appears, and then only because it touches work: \"The porridge was still hot at noon.\" A pause can do the work of a speech. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene, so that a small unsettled action—putting a lock back on, covering a mirror, leaving a door open—can hold the moment without a complete resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet the impossible enter the focal mind as perceptual interruption and unfinished memory, not as narratively explained lore. A streak of wet leaves on the ceiling which no one else has seen, the repeated appearance of a pear that is never there when she reaches for it, a neighbor's step that always moves from cellar to attic though that neighbor sits downstairs. Braid these impressions with ordinary tasks and failed attempts to organize the thoughts.\n\nMove the syntax through porous associations while keeping enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. At each appearance the fantasy should be slightly off, remembered incorrectly or begun before an interruption: \"The window was open when I passed, and the snow had come in, except there was no snow, only the white curtain and a smell of turned earth.\" Later, the mind returns and corrects, perhaps wrongly. Do not let every association announce itself as significant; let a kettle, a stitch, or a name pull the thought onward.\n\nUse sensory specificity for an instant, then break it; leave the speaker uncertain into the next sentence. The fantasy's consequences may be domestic and quiet: she stops picking up a certain dish, or takes the long path home because of the earlier thought. Let the mind shift where attention shifts, so that the stream continues past the impossible rather than stopping to explain it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet the impossible affect the exchange through the interface itself. An image that will not attach, a message delivered before the receiver opens the app, a voice note that replays a different sentence the second time—whatever the premise is, write the interface event as a purposeful occurrence, not a decorative glitch. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. A long typing pause, a received time that is wrong, a message edited to a single space before anyone can reply—each event carries the impossible without the characters naming it.\n\nUse the unsent draft as private composition, not as the sender's complete mind. If a draft appears, distinguish it clearly from a sent message. Show one character noticing after the fact: \"you wrote then stopped. it said typing for nine minutes.\" The other may send only a final \"never mind,\" which is itself transmitted text, not interior speech.\n\nLet the characters' attention stay sensory and partial. They may photograph a wet windowsill, then say only \"the light did that.\" The fantastic need not be named in a message. Responses can be delayed by work, a train, a child, real concern, or indifference. Let the exchange close where the conversation naturally pauses—a sent reply, a pending delivery, a channel gone quiet—without requiring a cliffhanger or a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbaba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Visual Novel\n\nSet each scene in one or two plain lines that concentrate on a particular visual detail: lace curtains still even when a window is open, a teacup's reflection moving before the liquid is poured, a stack of books a fraction taller after each rain. Let the focal character react to the change in action or inner response, not in forced explanation. The fantasy may be visible to the reader in the setting description while the character registers only coldness, a shadow, a smell.\n\nAttribute dialogue plainly to keep the illustrated scene readable. Let conversation run against the fantasy: someone asks about supper while a second moon's glow lies on the doorstep. When useful, briefly note a facial or posture cue—\"she looked at the window, not at him\"—but do not use the cue as proof of truth or feeling. Required private experience appears as focal narration in the active presentation, not as a popup label on every line.\n\nKeep scene changes legible; use headings or clear shifts. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Let each scene close on a quiet focus on a changed object—a comb now lying on the floor, an unmarked paper that was blank a moment ago—so that the impossible remains a condition of the illustrated world rather than a puzzle to be solved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagbabb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Yasunari Kawabata / Web Serial\n\nAt the start of an installment, recover one sensory thread if needed: the smell of salt that appeared without weather, the scratch on the kitchen table that opens at certain hours, the extra key on the ring that no one remembers adding. Let the current scene quietly confirm the premise has remained, then continue ordinary work.\n\nUse short accumulations rather than progress traps. One installment may show the same impossible detail at a new time of day; another may show a character's changed habit around it, such as leaving a saucer under the window. Do not invent escalations merely to hook. The ongoing story may be about the characters' relationships or tasks, with the fantasy periodically touching them.\n\nKeep chapters self-contained enough for re-entry by using familiar locations and a brief context clue. Do not begin with a full lore summary or a recap paragraph. Let each installment close where the scene naturally pauses—after a completed action, a quiet observation, or an unanswered question—so that the impossible remains available to continue without demanding a cliffhanger or a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
