            "name": "<Init: Writing Recipes: High Fantasy>",
            "content": "{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @section-header true }}{{// @header <Init: Writing Recipes: High Fantasy> }}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-high_fantasy-01",
            "name": "🔧 Init · High Fantasy · 01/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == high_fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip High Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 1 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-high_fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{// Recipe Library: High Fantasy }}\n{{setvar::NPahaaaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite through a focal character whose perception of the present is interrupted by intrusive fragments from oaths, old failures, reliquary texts, or the speech of lost companions. Do not let the interruption become a stable flashback; let it alter the present interpretation and then recede, leaving a residue in how the character hears the next line. Whenever another person speaks, omit or distort a phrase when the focal attention slips toward pain, need, or the fear of sounding weak. Recover the missing word later only if someone repeats it or the character realizes the loss.\n\nMake emotional exposure work through strained concrete choices rather than stated feeling. Confessions, requests for help, apologies, or returns to a place of hurt should be delivered late, in fragments, or after a decision has already been made and must be admitted. Let the scene's high-fantasy obligations give that hurt a material form: a shrine visit that would reassert a broken vow, a sealed record whose opening would repeat an old judgment, a formal title that now feels like a false name. The cost of speaking or remaining silent belongs to the character's current relationship, not only to a historical wound.\n\nKeep the private experience available under the active narrator. Show what the focal character withholds as actual thought: the demand they nearly voice, the phrase they rewrite to protect the other person, the memory that makes the next sentence harder. Let a visible action continue while the thought distorts or interrupts it, but do not require each thought to produce an immediate visible change. The speech can be ordinary or halting while the interior remains turbulent; they do not have to match, but any split should come from a specific fear, shame, or effort of care.\n\nOrder the scene around an incomplete exposure rather than a completed reconciliation. An emotional statement may be received partially, redirected, or answered with warmth that still leaves the underlying obligation untouched. Avoid the pattern where every confession collapses the relationship or every pause conceals a revelation. Let an ancient institution, a policy of secrecy, or a lingering exile explain why someone cannot say everything now, and let the response show that the other character heard only part. End the response at the edge of that possibility, with the scene's practical action or formal duty still in motion, rather than at a forced emotional summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chat Roleplay\n\nKeep the reply as a coherent shared interval in short readable paragraphs. Attribute every line of speech clearly with the active speaker or a definite character tag, even when the focal character mishears or catches only part of a phrase. Write the missed or distorted fragment as what the focal character heard, followed when appropriate by a brief focal reaction, never as an unattributed line that could confuse a collaborating player.\n\nMake the high-fantasy setting active through what people say and carry: a duty spoken over mail or a sealed charter, a title used as reproach, a location entered because an older oath requires it. The current NPCs and the collaborative partner choose from these concrete obligations; do not let the setting turn every reply into exposition. Narrative passages may briefly supply what is visible or known to the focal character, but the scene belongs to the exchange of speech and actions.\n\nUse the Akira-style interruption as a live continuity risk. When attention slips, show the character losing a word, misattributing a phrase to the wrong speaker and catching the error, or answering a sentence that was never finished. Let them recover enough to continue the exchange; do not stall the scene. Keep each speaker distinct even when perception fails, so the reader always knows which character actually spoke, and the distortion remains a property of the focal mind.\n\nLeave a continuation point that follows from the last action or unfinished sentence. A character may be at the threshold of replying, handing over a token, beginning to explain why they came, or realizing they misheard a promise. The reply can end inside that motion. Do not add a question solely to prompt the player, and do not require a cliffhanger, a sudden loyalty crisis, or a completed decision. If a private thought belongs under the active visible or hidden interiority wrapper, show the actual interrupted experience for that character; otherwise keep the narrative moving with the characters' spoken and practical behavior.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Place the scene in a household, shop, courtyard, or private room where high fantasy's mythic materials appear through ordinary objects and inherited obligations: a sealed genealogy, a boundary marker that must be maintained, a family altar, a debt recorded in a ledger, or a name that carries the weight of an old oath. Let ceremonies, greetings, meals, or the order of household tasks organize the scene instead of battlefield or council events.\n\nKeep the intimate observations concrete and socially legible. A person's hesitation before setting out fresh spirits, a particular desk position, a servant's silence, or the way a sleeve is arranged can reveal attachment and status without omniscient commentary. Action and speech remain in the dramatic exchange, but the narration may notice how the room has changed, who is missing, and what the household now cannot say.\n\nUse the Akira-style interruption to make domestic recollection unstable. The focal character may begin to remember a ritual and then reach a gap caused by grief, noise, exhaustion, or an intrusive image from an old event. They may mishear a relative's request as a reproach and answer both meanings. Do not turn the scene into a clean family history; let the disturbed perception reveal how much the present interpretation depends on what the character cannot retrieve.\n\nResolve the scene through a moment of household significance: someone noticing a broken seal, deciding how to answer a relative, realizing what the older generation concealed, or being called to perform a duty they had avoided. Ending the response at the edge of that homecoming or obligation, with normal domestic activity continuing around it, is more in keeping with this route than a public address or a broad social panorama.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse short, hard sentences and concrete physical detail. A relic is metal, wax, carved stone, fraying cloth, or scorched wood, not a generalized symbol. When an oath or institution exerts force, show its effect in a practical consequence: a sealed door, an official refusing passage, a mark burned into a gate, a debt called in by an armed messenger, a character's exhausted body complying before speech.\n\nCut transitions to the essential movement. Move from one action or location to the next with a spare verb or an abrupt turn; do not summarize background. Keep a longer sentence or brief passage only when the focal character's reasoning actually needs it, such as comparing a mark against an old record or deciding how far they can trust a half-heard name.\n\nUse the Akira-style interruption as a break in practical continuity, not a dreamy aside. A loud sound, an intrusive image, or a lost word jerks attention away mid-task. Show the concrete return: the hand tightening on a strap, a tool now pointing wrong, a step misplaced. Speech can be cut off by noise, fatigue, or the need to act. When the character recovers, the next line should make clear what they missed and what they now do.\n\nGround rough or abrasive speech in the speaker's physical situation and relationship. A short insult belongs to cold, pressure, injury, hunger, fear of delay, or a real grievance, not to a universal attitude. Let high-fantasy grandeur appear through a working object or a body under obligation rather than a speech. End the response where the practical action stands unfinished: the last word half-spoken, the tool still raised, the gate not yet opened. Do not require the completion, the understanding, or a changed plan before ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the reply as one or more documents, each with a definite author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of writing. Use forms that a high-fantasy institution would actually produce: a courier's report, a sealed letter, a ledger entry, a charter fragment, a household record, a marginal annotation, or a private letter kept in a shrine. What each document knows and conceals follows its author's role and moment of composition. Do not add an omniscient paragraph explaining how every record fits together.\n\nMake gaps and revisions carry the Akira-style interruption. A writer may stop mid-sentence because a noise reached the room, alter a name uncertainly, copy an old text with a wrong word, or return after a break and continue from a different construction. A marginal note may correct an earlier line. The reader can see the break as material evidence: crossed-out phrases, a split pen stroke, a blot, torn paper, a date changed. Keep each document legible enough for continuity, but let the disturbance remain inside that authorized voice rather than outside it.\n\nLet the documents move the story through practical obligation. A letter can ask for a seal, explain why a messenger cannot cross a boundary, record a price or inherited debt, or preserve a decision already made. Avoid making every entry a private confession of emotion; the turbulent inward life, if it appears, belongs to what the writer would actually set down for that recipient. If the active contract permits hidden or visible interiority, it is additional fictional private experience and does not replace the document.\n\nOrder entries so that a later document changes or contests what an earlier one asserted without canceling the reader's grip on the sequence. End the response where the next document would begin, such as with an unfinished line, an unanswered request, a letter waiting at a gate, or a record known to be incomplete. Do not require the sequence to reach a solved conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nShow care through particular high-fantasy rituals and objects: someone brings the right tea for a traveler, offers a clean cloak or a dry bed, returns a repaired token, recalls how a companion likes the fire laid, or performs a small protective gesture associated with an old oath. Let affection be sincere and practical, not a declaration. A character may prepare a favorite meal, untangle a knot, smooth a torn margin in a book, or wait quietly outside a bathhouse because a friend needs rest.\n\nKeep the setting's obligations and disagreements alive. An order may still be waiting at the gate, a family may still disapprove, the character may still owe a debt or a visit. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or erase these constraints. One person can help another without agreeing with them, and the scene can stay warm even when the larger problem remains unsolved.\n\nUse the Akira-style interruption gently. A character may lose a name mid-sentence and laugh about it, hear a request wrong and be corrected kindly, or struggle to follow a conversation and receive a patient repetition. Intrusive images may surface, then be set aside by a familiar touch or a practical offer. Do not let the instability become a diagnosis or repeated punchline; let the other character's tolerance and attention become part of the comfort.\n\nSpend the prose on small pleasures and sensory detail rather than one overwhelming emotional paragraph: the smell of cedar and warm rice, the weight of a dry blanket, the click of a kettle, a hand pausing at a broken hinge. If the point of view moves, keep each character's knowledge separate. A comforting thought may stay private; an offer of help may be ordinary. The reply may end inside the ease, with the tea still steaming or a figure settling to sleep, rather than at an emotional summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in deliberate sentences and sustained paragraphs. Let each paragraph carry a movement of social situation, perception, or judgment, and use transitions that show how one concern leads to another. The narration may be at a formal distance from spoken exchange, but it should remain attached to the active focal access authorized by the current contract.\n\nFrame the high-fantasy scene through public history and ceremony. A council room, a shrine, a name-day feast, a library, or a formal audience can organize the passage. Notice how titles, seating, dress, or inherited precedence guide behavior. Let historical weight appear through the narrator's knowledge of an old charter, a previous exile, a scandal, or a boundary dispute. Use that material to explain what a decision costs, but do not let it replace the immediate scene.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception as composed interior prose rather than fragmented punctuation. The focal character may read a name on a banner and then lose the name behind an intrusive memory; a formal speech may blur and recover; a phrase may arrive with its meaning attached to the wrong speaker. The narration can say plainly that the character could not be sure what they had heard, and it can hold that uncertainty over the next paragraph instead of resolving it instantly.\n\nKeep speech distinct from narrative diction. Ordinary characters may speak plainly; ceremony may produce measured public addresses; but the narration remains composed and socially framed. Let private and spoken meaning differ for a particular reason, such as embarrassment, rank, loyalty, or the fear that a request would be refused. A character's silent thought can continue across a formal pause without being summarized as a lesson. End the response where the social interval remains open: a speaker rising, a document still unopened, a companion uncertain how to respond. Do not force a completed decision or an epigrammatic close.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBegin inside a specific occasion of telling. Name the listeners by relation or function—a council waiting to decide passage, a family gathered before a funeral, a traveling company stalled by weather, an apprentice sent to hear what an elder will not say directly. Establish what the teller wants from this audience before the tale begins: permission, food, a delay, a witness, a changed vote, a name released from silence. That want is the frame's engine, not a backdrop.\n\nLet the tale be selected and shaped by that want. When the teller needs the council to fear an old boundary, open with the boundary's first violation and return to it whenever attention wavers. When she needs a family to forgive a debt, begin with the ancestor who first incurred it and let the audience hear their own surname inside the story. When she needs to delay a departure, slow the embedded tale at a threshold—a door not yet opened, a pass not yet crossed—so the delay in the story becomes the delay in the room. The teller's omissions and repetitions are choices made under pressure, visible in what she will not say aloud.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception as the live cost of performing. A listener's cough, a cup set down, a child asking the wrong question, a door opening behind her can break a sentence, send her back to a line already spoken, or make her answer a voice from the tale as if it were in the room. When an inner interruption rises—a half-image of the person she is describing, a remembered phrase that belongs to a different night—let it enter the spoken story as a wrong name, a repeated clause, or a pause the audience fills with its own guess. The tale remains artful, but the artfulness is assembled in front of people who can interrupt it.\n\nKeep the levels intelligible by anchoring each in different sensory matter. The outer room has its own temperature, light, and sounds: a brazier smoking, boots on a stair, a shutter moving. The embedded tale has a different spatial anchor—a road above a river, a hall with a broken lintel, a winter camp, a field where a treaty was signed. When the narration returns to the frame, return to the room's concrete detail before continuing the outer action. A repeated object or phrase—a cracked bell, a line from an oath, a coin passed hand to hand—may appear in both levels and change meaning because the audience now understands whose hands it passed through.\n\nMake the tale's high-fantasy obligations specific and costly in the present. An oath sworn by a child may now require the listener to surrender a room, a horse, or a vote. A relic repaired by an ancestor may give the teller the right to speak but not the right to be believed. An institution that forgot its origin may still enforce the ritual that origin demanded. When a title or vow is named, show what it can grant or withdraw in the room where the telling happens: shelter, passage, food, silence, a signature, a door left unlocked.\n\nLet audience response be ordinary, partial, and consequential. A listener may believe one episode and reject the next, supply a name the teller forgot, correct the order of events, ask for the part she skipped, or leave at the moment her presence mattered. Treat each response as a new condition under which the telling continues or stops. The scene may end at an interruption, a refusal, a question, or the arrival of someone whose presence changes what can safely be said. No revelation, completed bargain, or nested-tale quota is required before the response closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite in the Romantic and uncanny narrative route. Begin with a present situation that carries an unfinished high-fantasy obligation: a boundary stone that must be remarked before a season turns, a treaty that still requires a name from each family, a bell rung on certain mornings because of an old promise, a road that forms part of an oath. Let the obligation appear first through the senses—the stone's cold edge, the bell's wrong tone, the road's surface underfoot—and only later through what characters avoid, repair, remember, or refuse to touch.\n\nLet an actual discontinuity in perception or attention provide an entry point for uncanny prose when present; otherwise retain the clarity and continuity of the focal experience. A sound heard through rain, a repeated word in a half-remembered song, a face seen across a hall, a patch of light on a wall can pull the narration into a pause, an intrusive memory, or an ungrounded dread. When that happens, change the sentence's shape to register the shift: cut a clause short, delay the verb, repeat a phrase with one word altered, or return to a physical detail out of order. Do not explain every disturbance as a symptom. Some may be the world's magic reaching through ordinary perception; some may be the focal character's stress. Hold alternatives open only where the supplied facts and focal knowledge leave them open; do not invent stress, magic, or doubt to maintain ambiguity.\n\nShow any practical effect an interruption actually has in the outer situation; an attention shift may also pass without a cost. The focal character is not receiving impressions in a void. They may be part of a delegation, traveling to an archive, standing guard, listening to an elder, or crossing a contested border. If an attention break affects the encounter, show that actual effect: someone may wait, a task may pause, or the character may recover without any outward consequence. The composed literary syntax can become more pressure-driven when time is short—shorter sentences, fewer subordinate clauses—and more expansive when the scene permits memory to unroll.\n\nTreat inherited obligations as real and intimate. A title, kinship duty, or sworn task becomes material when it makes the character take one road rather than another, keep a promise at personal cost, or refuse an easier escape. Let an institution's old authority appear in practical terms: who may enter, who must be greeted in a particular order, who can release a person from a meeting. Do not make everyone speak like a chronicle. The historical weight belongs to the situation and the selected telling, not to compulsory formal speech.\n\nLet the uncanny work through recurrence and consequence rather than sudden spectacle. A hand-shaped mark on a windowsill, the smell of a dry riverbed in a closed room, a name spoken for the third time at the wrong moment: these can build the sense that the past is not finished. When such a detail returns, do not merely repeat it. Show how the returning detail is actually received. It may redirect attention, invite a comparison, affect a choice, or simply be noticed again. If uncertainty is supported, give it concrete content; it need not cause a wrong answer, altered route, or hesitation, and a known detail may remain unambiguous.\n\nDo not import German scenery, bureaucracy, severity, or national speech patterns into a world that does not supply them. The route is a formal method: Romantic uncanny narrative that lets perception and historical obligation blur at the edges while keeping the prose controlled and situationally concrete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Gothic-Punk\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite present life entangled with old high-fantasy authority. Use settings that show inherited power occupying ordinary places: a bloodline's banner hung in a stairwell, a temple's old boundary running through a market stall, a title's privileges enforced at a train gate, an oath that requires someone to answer the same doorbell every season. Urban material detail should be precise and worn—the metal of a key, the grain of a patched coat, a landlord's ledger, the smell of a maintenance room—and it should reveal who maintains the old order and who pays for it.\n\nShape the telling around how the focal character actually experiences those collisions. If a title or ritual genuinely interrupts attention, syntax can pause or a sound can recur. Confusion or threat belongs in that treatment only when supported by the character's experience; an ordinary recognition may remain direct. An intrusive inner voice or half-memory can cut across a formal greeting or practical instruction. If the interruption affects the public exchange, make that effect concrete: someone may wait, a hand remain extended, or a request be repeated; attention can also recover without a social penalty. Do not reduce the interruptions to paranoia. Where an interruption occurs, its source and effect follow the actual situation. It may redirect what the character does or says, or pass without changing either; no exhaustion, unseen presence, or lost capability is required.\n\nMake old authority concrete and operational. A hereditary privilege might decide who may enter a building, who gets rations, who can call off a street function, or whose word settles a property line. A relic may be a broken sigil whose holder still gets a seat in a council. An oath may obligate a descendant to perform a pointless ceremony that now exposes them to fines or violence. Show these as lived institutions, not scenery. The gothic weight comes from the collision between that history and present need: someone wants to leave, eat, sleep, protect a friend, or avoid a ritual.\n\nWhere established motives make intimacy morally complicated or socially masked, let the prose attend to that particular tension; direct, uncomplicated care remains possible. Characters may speak politely while withholding one piece of truth, show warmth to someone they are also exploiting, or let a mask fall only with one trusted person in a private room. When a mask falls, write the fall as a change in the room's texture, not a plot twist: the formal address drops to a nickname, a rehearsed sentence is abandoned halfway, a hand stops performing its public gesture and simply rests. Show what the fall costs or relieves—the character may speak more freely and then owe that freedom, or accept comfort and then have to leave it. If the scene supports imperfect listening, render its actual form and effect. A kindness can also be registered fully and a tender remark answered directly; the style requires no misreading.\n\nUse atmospheric language selectively to intensify a particular encounter. A stone corridor, a cracked mirror, a flickering arc lamp, or a crowd eddying around a guarded door can make a power relation felt. Choose the image by what it reveals about authority or need in that moment: the corridor shows who may pass, the mirror shows what someone cannot bear to see, the lamp shows who controls the light, the crowd shows who is kept out. Do not use atmosphere as a substitute for showing who holds authority, what they want, and what it costs the focal character.\n\nKeep the style portable. Do not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, named vampire powers, or blood-bond rules. Gothic-Punk here is a narrative treatment: decaying present settings, old authority with intimate human costs, private desire, and social performance under strain, all applied to high fantasy's inherited obligations.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Place the scene within a situated retelling: a family gathering, a seasonal assembly, a court of elders, a traveling group around a fire, or another community whose members know fragments of the tale and have reasons to listen. The story being told concerns a high-fantasy inheritance—an ancient boundary, a lost ritual, a bloodline's promise, a god-sworn debt, or a change that still binds descendants. Make clear who asks for the story and what present decision the telling may influence, even if the teller does not control the outcome.\n\nLet the outer frame carry the weight of obligation. Before the embedded tale begins, show the audience's relationship to it through what they do with their bodies and voices: an elder refuses to sit until a name is spoken, a child repeats a misheard verse, a listener interrupts to correct the order of episodes, a woman leaves the circle when a forbidden part is reached. Each action reveals a stake—the elder's silence protects a claim, the child's error shows which version is already slipping, the correction marks a family's right to the story, the departure marks a boundary the telling must now navigate. The story is communal memory with practical consequences for land, marriage, feeding, passage, or status.\n\nRender Akira's unstable perception as a live teller's error and recovery. When she forgets a line, repeats a phrase, hears a sound outside the circle as part of the story, or follows a private image into a digression, let the audience react: someone supplies the missing name, another scoffs, a silence lengthens. The prose may switch between the outer scene and the embedded tale without warning, but keep the levels intelligible by anchoring each in different sensory matter—the outer circle's fire and breathing, the tale's road, hall, or river. Use a repeated object or phrase—a cracked bowl, a name pronounced two ways, a landscape marker—to show what keeps surfacing despite distraction.\n\nLet the teller's errors transmit meaning rather than merely interrupt it. A forgotten line may be supplied by the person who stands to lose most from its telling. A repeated phrase may be the one the teller's own mother always corrected. A digression may enter the tale at the exact point where the teller cannot yet say what happened to a particular character. When the audience fills a gap or corrects a name, show what their version protects or claims. The flawed retelling is how the inheritance reaches a new audience: each error reveals who still carries which part of the story and what they need it to mean.\n\nKeep the embedded tale itself high fantasy in method, not merely in ornament. Its episodes should involve oath, sacrifice, exile, institution, or magic that changes what a person can do. The characters inside the tale may possess titles and make choices at cost. The oral performance should be visibly shaped by that cost: the teller slows at a betrayal, skips a blessing, returns to a line she had changed earlier. When she slows or skips, let the audience notice and respond—a listener leans forward, another begins the skipped blessing herself, a third asks why the teller changed the line. The tale's high-fantasy weight lives in these exchanges, not only in the embedded episodes.\n\nUse local sensory and social detail from the supplied setting, not generic Indian markers. If the story's own world is cold northern country with stone forts and horse-trading families, the tale should speak of that. If it is a river town with temple granaries and boat covenants, the tale should show those. Do not inject a particular religion, caste obligation, or family hierarchy that is not present. The style route gives the oral epic form; the world supplies the content.\n\nEnd scenes according to the actual situation: a listener may walk out, a child may fall asleep, an elder may repeat the tale's first line to signal that only half can be told tonight, or the teller may stop when her voice breaks on an important name. A completed narrative arc is not required. Let audience and teller return to the frame before the scene closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite in the fantastic-tale route. Begin inside a concrete high-fantasy situation: a boundary ritual must be repeated before snowfall, a monk or warden carries an old name into a contested valley, a family's protective stone has been moved, a messenger has to cross a pass where a promise was broken. The supernatural can be real and ordinary within this world, but it should appear through particular events rather than summarized lore.\n\nRender fantastic events through the focal character's actual perception. If an established event interrupts that perception, a clear first impression followed by a syntactic break can register it. If attention remains steady, describe the event clearly without manufacturing a lapse, false recognition, or uncanny occurrence. The sentence may pause, repeat a sound, lose the object of a verb, or shift to a private thought that is not a neat explanation. Then continue with the moment's practical pressure: someone is waiting, cold is deepening, a horse is shying, a gate must be reached. Distinguish what the focal character knows, experiences, or remains unsure about. Preserve uncertainty when it exists and established clarity when it does; neither hallucination nor confusion is supplied by the form.\n\nIf established supernatural contact disrupts perception, render that disruption at the moment it occurs. A sound's direction, a reflected movement, or a familiar cadence can determine the shape of the sentence when the world actually presents it. Keep the character's agency and understanding accurate: delayed recognition, involuntary movement, and misheard words are possible effects only when supported, not automatic features of contact.\n\nKeep the prose episodic. Delimit each scene as a distinct passage—a narrow room, a path above a river, a shrine courtyard, a sleeping loft—with its own sensory anchor and its own tempo. Enter the scene through that anchor and leave it when the anchor changes: the room's lamp goes out, the path reaches a bridge, the courtyard's bell stops, the loft's window shows dawn. Seasonal or weather detail may appear where it affects movement and mood: wet bamboo, cracking ice, windborne cinders, early rain on a stone floor. Use such detail to orient, not as a mandatory nature inventory.\n\nLet direct physical action carry the fantastic contact. When an uncanny physical event is present, give its actual sensory particulars weight. An inexplicably wet rope, disembodied name, turning lock, or altered passage illustrates the method only in a world and scene that supply it; analysis and direct recognition remain available to the character. Give the sensation its full weight, including fear, absurdity, anger, or detachment. They may speak aloud to no one, or answer a voice before deciding whether it was real.\n\nWhere the supplied world connects a supernatural encounter to an inherited obligation, make that connection concrete. A broken promise, unpaid vigil, transferred title, or unburied relic may matter if established; leaving remains possible according to the actual constraints and character choices. Show the actual consequences of a choice, including its costs when present. Listening, speaking, or returning an object may succeed without penalty; a missed gate, debt, or lost claim follows only from established constraints and events. The fantastic event should be actual story substance, not a hallucination without consequence.\n\nDo not import Japanese scenery, character types, Buddhist or Shinto iconography, or a national code of restraint into a world that does not supply them. The route is formal: a fantastic tale told through immediate perceptual experience, where old supernatural truths and personal intrusion are both strongly present and the narration may omit or repeat according to the teller's attention.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the marvelous as ordinary within the supplied world. High-fantasy elements should be part of daily practical life: an ancestral ghost sits in the kitchen until its debt is paid; a house recognizes an oathbreaker by making the front gate stick; a saint's festival requires the seventh daughter to walk the boundary; a village's rain follows the will of a dead founder who was never properly buried. These are not intrusions to be wondered at by everyone. People adapt, argue, trade favors, and keep records about them.\n\nShow that adaptation in how characters speak and act. A cook may set a place for the ghost without comment while negotiating the price of flour. A neighbor may mention the sticking gate as a reason to avoid a particular family, then offer to help fix it anyway. A clerk may check a genealogy mark with the same tone they use for a tax stamp. A child may be told to greet the dead founder's portrait before asking for rain. The magical is not separate from law, property, or work; it enters ordinary conversation as a condition people manage, resent, or rely on.\n\nIf the focal character's actual perception is interrupted or uncertain, let that affect the prose's movement: composed attention can shift to a fragment and return. Select the interruption from what the scene and character already support. Accurate recognition, fluent attention, and an ordinary room remain available; the method creates no phantom touch, unknown voice, wrong face, memory lapse, or diagnosis.\n\nLet any actual perceptual break register against the ordinary magic's practical management. A misread document, uncertain payment, repeated phrase, or interrupted visit can reveal the strain when the scene supports it. The character may notice and correct the lapse, complete the task despite it, or make a consequential mistake; use the result established by their action and the world. The marvelous remains ordinary to the community while the focal narration follows the actual quality of attention, whether steady or interrupted.\n\nGive obligations a public, almost administrative texture. Inheritance, vows, and old institutions appear in documents, market queues, gate permissions, household rules, and marriage negotiations. A person may need a countersigned paper to satisfy a dead woman's demand, or lose a field because a grandfather's promise still determines which family can cross the canal. Show how people talk about these things in normal conversation and how they hide or delay uncomfortable duties.\n\nUse concrete local detail to ground the effect. Whether the setting is a mountain town, a port city, a river settlement, or a hacienda being divided, the scene should include its actual food, labor, weather, transport, and social habits. The community's speech can be direct, affectionate, sarcastic, or tired according to the characters. Do not make everyone speak in lyrical wonder or with a uniform magical-realist voice.\n\nKeep the narration focused through the focal character while allowing the community's perspective to appear in speech and action. Other characters may know parts of the obligation the focal character does not, or may misread their breaks as disrespect, illness, or bargaining. Their responses may clarify, assist, disagree, or simply continue the ordinary work. Let those responses follow what the focal character actually does; no incapacity to manage the magic is assumed.\n\nDo not invent supernatural facts beyond what the world permits. Since the genre already allows magic, the marvelous-as-ordinary route narrates that magic as accepted. It does not add a new layer of unexplained enchantment that contradicts the setting. Where actual perceptual breaks carry an uncanny feeling, use their texture without changing the world's established nature. A clear or uneventful perception need not become uncanny.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Light Novel\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nOpen each scene in the immediate situation: where the focal character is standing, sitting, walking, or waiting; what they hold or need to do; and who else is present. Give the spatial layout in a few concrete details early—a hall with two doors, a market aisle blocked by a cart, a tower stair with a broken torch sconce—so later movement does not confuse the reader. Keep paragraphs short and split cleanly around dialogue, action, and reaction.\n\nUse a visible reaction beat when the actual experience contains an interruption. A line break, clipped sentence, or repeated sensory detail can register how attention shifts without implying that the character misunderstood anything. If a real mishearing, mistaken action, or intrusive thought occurs, show its supported cause and effect. A title, ritual, or significant object does not automatically produce a stutter, false recognition, missed words, or wrong move.\n\nSequence the beats so the relation between perception, response, and any actual change stays legible; a beat may also hold an ongoing action or quiet attention. A beat may be: the focal character enters or is addressed; they act or speak; something in the scene does not fit; they react internally; the mismatch produces a visible consequence; someone responds to that consequence; the next action becomes necessary. Use such a sequence only when those events actually unfold. The reader should be able to follow what was noticed and any response, including a recovered focus, unchanged activity, or no outward action. Use a short reaction where it fits the beat, and let developed private reasoning continue when the character is actually working through something; paragraph breaks keep both readable.\n\nPresent high-fantasy obligations concretely. A relic is not just old; it burns cold to the touch when its holder has an unpaid debt or opens a locked room for the bloodline that swore to guard it. An oath is not a vague value; it demands a task now—deliver a sealed letter before moonrise, stand through the third bell, refuse the visitor's offered food. An institution appears as physical procedure: the clerk requires a thumbprint in wax, the gate guard checks a genealogy mark, the sacristy key must be passed clockwise.\n\nWhen mechanics need explanation, deliver it during a natural pause in the action, not before the action begins. A clerk may explain the wax thumbprint while the focal character waits for the seal to warm. A guard may name the genealogy mark while checking it against a list. A companion may mention the key's clockwise rule as the focal character reaches for it the wrong way. Keep the explanation to a few plain sentences tied to what the character is doing or about to do, then return to the scene's movement.\n\nLet the narrative move quickly through clear beats without forcing comedy, banter, or self-deprecating narration. Internal reaction can be serious, confused, angry, or wry, according to the moment. Preserve any actual uncertainty without using narration to hide available facts. If a mishearing occurs, distinguish the available words from their interpretation; accurate hearing and stable understanding remain intact.\n\nEnd a scene at a natural continuation point. The response can stop during an unresolved action or waiting state: a guard asks a question the focal character has not answered, a light moves at the end of a corridor, a messenger hands them a folded paper but they have not opened it. A cliffhanger is one option, not a requirement. The scene boundary should leave the next action clear without demanding a twist.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the mythic world as it reaches a mind that takes it in pieces. Build each paragraph's music out of what interrupts: an oath's exact wording pushing into the middle of a practical task, a relic humming beneath ordinary talk, an old defeat shouldering between two sentences, a landscape's older name arriving before the road does. Let syntax carry the fracture: a sentence that loses its footing and finds it again, a clause left hanging because attention went elsewhere. Let returning sounds — a vowel held, a consonant come back to — bind the interruptions into rhythm rather than noise. Unfold a comparison across several sentences only when it changes what the teller understands or makes suddenly present; when it merely decorates, cut it.\n\nReturn to a recurring image only after its meaning has moved: a vow-word heard first as promise, later as threat, later as grief. Do not toll the image at every difficulty; earn each return. Between returns, let plain statement carry the weight — say directly what the institution demands, what the relic verifiably does, what a refusal would cost in grain, blood, fealty, or years. The facts stay solid while the focal reading of them slides: the hum is truly there, though the mind cannot always tell whether it rises from the relic, the room, or its own blood. A title can settle who must be obeyed even when the bearer cannot hold the whole hall in mind; an inherited promise can close a road whether or not the inheritor can finish the thought of it. When an obligation does come due, let its full weight land on a mind that only half expected it.\n\nLet speech fray against noise and attention: a character answers the question they were still hearing two beats ago, loses the thread of a formal address, catches the tail of a name and covers the miss. Not everyone speaks this way; let others answer bluntly, their flat sentences puncturing and reorienting the figurative drift, since plain speech is contrast here, not a failure of the style. Render the intrusions as lived private experience — sensation, half-thought, misgiving felt from inside, under the active narration contract — never as a report about a troubled mind. A scene may close while a reading is still forming, mid-interruption, mid-image, the phrase's final meaning left open for the next passage to inherit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite the high-fantasy world as real and costly from the first scene. Let titles, relics, oaths, institutions, borders, landscapes, and ancient events alter access, obligation, and the price of a choice. Show mythic scale through particular loyalties and material consequence: a binding oath can close off a future; a relic can carry an old debt forward; a border can make a remembered home unreachable; an institution's history can decide who is heard. Grandeur should arrive through what present action requires and what it costs, not through formal speech for every character or exposition that replaces the scene.\n\nFilter that world through the focal character's interrupted perception. Attention can break, mishear, over-fixate, or receive intrusive fragments that do not settle into one stable interpretation. Speech may be shaped by noise, divided attention, or the pressure of what is not said: a sentence repeated, answered late, or understood differently after a pause. Keep objective world facts separate from the character's reading of them. A relic can truly be a relic, an oath can truly bind, and the character may still misunderstand why, what it asks, or who is speaking. Keep this Akira's identity unresolved; do not import another creator's cast, setting, or signature lines. Do not turn the fantasy into a puzzle, a delusion, or an occasion to prove that magic is fake or every institution lies.\n\nTreat memory and passing time as conditions of the present. Notice small irreversible changes in familiar things - a voice grown careful, a road rerouted, a spell that no longer answers the same way, a room without its former occupant - and let association, unfinished attachment, or remembered occasions carry the past into the scene. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable. Do not require every scene to announce impermanence or end in sadness. Let the past persist as something the present must act within: an old promise still owing, a landscape still carrying an earlier age, a person still shaped by a choice they cannot undo.\n\nRender nonspoken private experience as actual fictional interiority in the active presentation, visible or inside the hidden-interiority wrapper when the contract calls for it. An in-world document, monologue, aside, or voice-over has its own permissions and cannot replace that private experience. Private thought may diverge from speech for a character-specific reason - concealment, confusion, courtesy, intrusive memory, habit - or may align with it. Do not make the cast uniformly lyrical, witty, or forthcoming; let particular characters carry particular ways of speaking and withholding.\n\nWhen the response reaches its natural boundary, it may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, or composition. Do not force a revelation, a completed oath, a reconciliation, or a decisive interpretation before the scene can end. Keep the world's large obligations felt as intimate, remembered losses while the focal mind keeps interrupting, revising, and failing to hold a single clear reading of what is happening.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the piece as a situated oral telling that carries a high-fantasy obligation. Put a teller among listeners whose present circumstances make the account consequential: an oath, relic, magical power, institution, ancient event, or inherited debt is changing what someone can do and what a choice costs. Let the frame stay grounded in concrete social and domestic life—labor, kinship, money, food, hospitality, neighborhood, obligation—so mythic scale lands as material pressure rather than as decorative grandeur. Draw the teller's language, naming, and social forms from the story's supplied setting and keep that tradition distinct; do not blend unrelated regional traditions into a generic antique pastiche. The Akira label remains unresolved: do not import another creator's settings, characters, or biography.\n\nRender perception through interrupted attention. The teller's or focal listener's mind should be porous to intrusive thought, sensory noise, misheard words, and unstable interpretation. Present-day sounds, smells, or phrases can snag into the remembered tale; a repeated word may return with altered meaning. Speech may stall, veer, repeat, or sharpen under distraction. Let these interruptions reveal what the character cannot smoothly integrate—an inherited debt, a broken oath, a loyalty, a fear—not random damage and not a clue system.\n\nUse recurrence to bind frame and embedded account. A detail, phrase, or gesture from the outer gathering can echo inside the tale; a listener's question about timing, access, or sequence can change what the teller emphasizes next. Let lyric or reflective prose arise where a specific image or dilemma earns it, not as decorative philosophy. Keep the viewpoint's private experience as actual nonspoken fictional experience—worry, calculation, sensory memory, an intrusive phrase—in the active presentation or required interiority form. A spoken tale, a written record, or a listener's aside does not replace that live interiority.\n\nLet the telling intervene in the present without dictating an outcome. Listeners may accept, dispute, misunderstand, or postpone judgment; the teller may gain or lose standing; the obligation may remain unresolved. End at any live point in the frame or the embedded tale—during speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup—without forcing a resolution, confession, betrayal, or cliffhanger. Preserve the story's supplied world facts, perspective, and output contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Minimalist Prose\n\nBuild the scene from a few exact mythic particulars that are already changing what people can do: a named oath, a relic's condition, a title's claim, a border's old injury, an institution's demand, or a landscape that still carries what happened there. Name only what alters present options or the cost of a choice. Let magic work through limits, conditions, and prices rather than ornamental aura. The large scale enters through consequence—a promise closes a route, an ancient claim makes loyalty expensive, a title opens a door that should have stayed shut—not through a lore summary. Keep voices individual; grandeur belongs to the situation and the telling, not to compulsory formal speech for everyone.\n\nRender Akira's unresolved profile as interrupted attention and unstable interpretation without turning every sentence into a broken fragment. A sensory detail, a half-heard phrase, or an intrusive thought can cut across the present action; the next direct clause can continue the action while the interpretation remains unsettled. Noise, fatigue, or attention loss may affect what is spoken, heard, or omitted, but the causal chain stays available to the reader. Do not announce that perception is unreliable; let gaps and juxtapositions make that inferable. Do not attach this Akira to any named creator or work. When Akira's private experience matters, write it as actual nonspoken interiority—inner dialogue, reasoning, attention, sensation, uncertainty, or motive—visible in the scene or inside the hidden-interiority wrapper. A monologue, aside, letter, or document is not a substitute.\n\nUse minimalist selection: a small number of exact details, direct clauses, and no redundant explanation. Keep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the present account; an essential thought may run long if it needs to make sense, while a precise extended sentence can carry more than several performed fragments. Omission should leave something inferable without deleting why an event follows. You may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup; no revelation, loyalty crisis, or completed task is required at the boundary. Do not force practical business to pause, resume, change direction, or produce a result before you end, and do not demand an external task to justify a private interval.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which magic, history, oaths, titles, relics, institutions, and ancient events have active force and material cost. Let an obligation change what a character can attempt, what a decision costs, and who bears the consequence. The grandeur belongs to the situation, not to a uniform formal register: let people speak according to their place, urgency, and attention rather than in archaic exposition.\n\nCenter the telling on a focal character whose perception is interrupted and whose interpretation will not hold still. Noise, intrusion, and divided attention can break a scene into what is actually happening and what the character cannot stop noticing or thinking. An intrusive thought may arrive mid-ritual, mid-negotiation, or mid-approach; it can pull the character away from a speaker, repeat a phrase, or make a sign or relic seem to mean something it may not. Let speech reflect that condition: a sentence may trail off, answer an earlier question, sharpen to bluntness, or carry an emphasis that no one else heard enter the room. These effects belong to the character's mind and voice, not to every speaker in the world.\n\nAt the same time, keep the external high-fantasy facts operational. A magical power does what it does; an oath has a clause; a title opens one door and closes another; an old event has left a scar in the landscape or an institution. When the character misreads a face, a demand, or a memory, let the world's consequences test the misreading rather than dissolve into total uncertainty. Private interpretation can be unstable while the realm's obligations remain real. Show the cost through action, refusal, delay, payment, or changed access, not through a lecture on the setting.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery. Move among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience as the scene requires. Let a remembered history enter because a present object or phrase makes it relevant, not as a block of exposition. Keep the narration in a continuous contemporary-novel register, with flexible attention and unobtrusive structure; the world may be ancient or secondary, but the prose need not adopt a distant or archaic voice, and the ending need not deliver a lesson or resolve every obligation.\n\nWhen the focal character's attention splits, keep the reader inside the actual private experience rather than replacing it with a document, monologue, or voice-over. The inner life is part of the scene's causal texture: it may shape what the character notices, says, or risks. Another character's knowledge remains separate unless the scene has given them access. Let the mythic scale press on particular loyalties and material stakes, and let the response stop wherever the active scene boundary falls—during speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup—without requiring a task to pause, resume, or conclude.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nTreat the established relationship as the story's fixed architecture and the focal perception as weather crossing it. Choose scenes that exist because of this bond — such as a vigil kept in shifts, an oath spoken in public that carries a second, private meaning between the two of them, or the small ritual repeated before parting — and let titles, relics, oaths, and institutions determine what those scenes cost and who may be present at all. Use only the premise the story actually supplies; do not pad it with a stock roster of familiar tropes. Keep the bond legible through structure rather than declaration: private language, a shared moment that recurs, a callback whose weight has grown since its first appearance. Keep the instability inside perception, never inside the premise.\n\nLet interrupted perception do the texture work. The mythic order reaches the focal character through the senses as intrusion — ward-bells, liturgy heard through a wall, an inherited vow arriving like a thought in the family cadence. Follow each interruption until attention finds its way back; do not smooth it away, and do not make every intrusion carry plot weight. When old history touches someone through kin or office, deliver it as unwanted recollection rather than exposition. Interpretation may wobble — one gesture read as tenderness, then as formality, then truly — while the world enforces real terms: a sworn oath binds, a title changes what a partner may ask or refuse, and an institution's ruling lands as lodging, escort, debt, or rank that reshapes what these two can actually do.\n\nSpeech obeys attention. Ordinary exchanges may drop a thread, restart, or answer a question from two exchanges ago; formal language, where the world truly uses it, can carry a scattered mind on its rhythm. Most of the cast speaks plainly, because grandeur lives in the situation, not in court diction for everyone. Render the focal character's private experience — sensation, intrusive thought, half-formed readings — under the active narration contract, misreadings included. The narration lives inside that attention, so the other person is known through what they do, say, and leave undone.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery room where the premise calls for it, under the obligation's real pressure, and let the beat arrive through the noise — it may surface as a callback phrase recovered mid-panic, or as a confession that has to work inside an oath's exact wording. When accumulated history pays off, fulfill this premise's particular promise rather than a generic reconciliation or ruin. The bond's next state may remain open, and a reply may end mid-interruption with the bell still sounding, if that is where the scene's attention actually is.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nWrite a high-fantasy oral chronicle whose spine is a deed and its remembered consequence. Open on a concrete act, place, name, or declaration that the community would remember; state it compactly. Then expand into the particulars that make the remembered version incomplete: who witnessed, who benefited, who paid, what oath or title was invoked, what the act cost in land, allegiance, or blood. Let magic, relics, institutions, landscapes, and ancient events change what people can do and what a decision costs. Show that scale through individual loyalties and material consequence—who can cross a border, who is owed, whose word binds, what a relic makes possible or dangerous—rather than through detached exposition.\n\nLet recurring phrasing attach to an act, object, kinship, or oath, and bring it back when a later event gives it a new application. Use names and titles as practical facts: what a title authorizes, what a name obligates, what a feud inherits. The chronicle may know what a character does not, but its memory remains partial, contested, or subject to revision. Dialogue belongs to particular speakers; it may interrupt the chronicle's authority with ordinary concerns, objections, or corrections. Do not make every speaker formally epic.\n\nFilter the chronicle through a focal viewpoint whose perception is interrupted. Background noise, intrusive phrases, half-heard names, sudden associations, and unstable interpretation should affect what is noticed and how a sign, a person, or a memory is read. Speech can be shaped by that pressure: a thought spoken over another, a question answered late or sideways, a word repeated because it will not settle. These intrusions are not reliable prophecy, hidden truth, or automatic madness; they may be wrong, partial, or revised later, and they remain specific to this character rather than becoming a general style of confusion. Render this viewpoint's nonspoken experience as actual interiority in the active presentation—visible or hidden as the current mode requires—not as recitation, quoted speech, or a summary of what the chronicle says about them.\n\nLet the saga's recurrence and the focal instability cross. A phrase from an oath, relic, or old account may return in present noise and mean something different under pressure. The chronicle can record a deed while the focal viewpoint catches only part of it, or supplies an interpretation that later facts do not support. When magic, inherited obligation, or an ancient event changes what someone can do, show the cost through action, refusal, cooperation, or material loss. The world's history is active and binding, but it does not dictate a single outcome; characters may help, resist, misunderstand, or continue their practical lives within it.\n\nKeep knowledge and access separate: the chronicle may know what a character does not, but a focal interpretation does not become true merely because it is vividly rendered. Do not identify this Akira with another creator or work; the unresolved attribution is not a hidden canon to be filled in. Let the response close at the current scene's actual boundary—during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup—rather than forcing a conclusion, a resumed task, or a final legendary judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Scandinavian chronicle route rather than purely mythic declamation or universal snow-and-silence scenery. The high-fantasy world should be rendered as a society with titles, kinship, houses, oaths, and legible compensations. When an ancient claim, relic, or obligation enters a scene, show its cost in ordinary obligations: who must go, who must pay, who owes hospitality, who may close a door, and what the binding promise requires. The institution and landscape should change what people can do, but they remain concrete places with rooms, duties, letters, and practical disagreements, not a permanent backdrop for lament.\n\nWrite the focal viewpoint as a chronicler-like participant whose attention is not authoritative. A ritual or legal scene may be interrupted by a voice in the next room, the pressure of a seal, a half-finished thought, or a wrong name. Accumulate these interruptions so the focal mind keeps trying to settle what an event means without the narration claiming certainty too soon. A witnessed act can be real while its significance remains unsettled. Do not make every intrusion a divine sign or clue; let the disturbance be what the character actually notices.\n\nKeep speech and private thought distinct. Spoken oaths, verdicts, and formal declarations can be clear and binding even when the focal character privately mishears, doubts, or returns to an earlier phrase. When the focal perception is interrupted by noise or exhaustion, the narration should reproduce the interruption without making the whole cast inarticulate or making dialogue stylized for every speaker. Use spare, even prose for the public record of the scene and allow the same event to be recalled later with a slightly different emphasis, not because the facts changed but because the focal mind remained unresolved about their weight.\n\nDo not replace the high-fantasy scale with psychology alone. The large history is active in the present: a banner, a debt, a border, a marriage, or an old oath still has consequences. The chronicle method handles that scale through social recognition and concrete aftermath rather than through proclamations. A character may understand an ancient institution only partially, but the world itself continues to operate under it, and ordinary people respond to it as real. Stop according to the scene's actual boundary: the focal mind may still be taking in what the public event has done to a particular household, or the household may have already moved on to its next practical task, with the grandeur of the object that produced the confusion remaining materially present either way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Screenplay\n\nWrite the high-fantasy material through screen-readable action rather than narrated grandeur. A title, relic, oath, or ancient institution should appear as a physical or spoken fact that shapes movement: a seal that must be witnessed, a hall where entry depends on rank, a relic too heavy for one bearer, a line of names read in the wrong order. Let these details carry the world's scale without converting every scene into exposition. Keep action lines concise enough for a reader to follow the blocking, and use scene headings only when the time or location actually changes.\n\nTreat the focal character's perception as an event in the scene, not as the scene's authoritative summary. Show the character pausing before a threshold, losing the end of a sentence when a bell sounds, repeating a name under different light, or misreading a gesture. These interruptions should appear in the action or dialogue as visible behavior. Required private thought remains a clearly attributed nonspoken note; it can say what the focal character is trying to sort out, but it is not dialogue and not available to other characters unless their own observation supplies the cue.\n\nUse speech affected by noise or attention selectively and for specific characters. A formal proclamation may be drowned at the moment the focal character looks away, but the official words still exist in the scene. Other speakers may state matters cleanly. Do not turn every interruption into an omen or proof. The scene's high-fantasy weight can be felt through what people must do after the disturbance: restart the ceremony, close the shutters, send someone to repeat the message.\n\nCuts and offscreen sound are useful only when they carry a specific transition or an audible cause for the interrupted attention. A sudden offscreen cry can explain the shift in focus, but the scene does not need a new cut each time perception stumbles. Stop according to the scene's actual boundary. The scene may end while the focal character is still watching an object being lifted or still standing with a half-read letter, with the pause remaining an unfinished visual beat rather than a dramatic revelation. It may also end after the practical consequence has already landed: the shutters are closed, the message is repeated, or the ceremony restarts. Preserve the institution's material force even when the focal mind has not finished assigning it meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stage Play\n\nMake the shared stage the source of the high-fantasy claim. Oaths, relics, institutions, and inherited obligations should be present in objects that can be handled, entrances that can be refused, and public procedures that involve more than one speaker. When a character must identify or swear to something, the staging should make clear who can see it, who may touch it, and what the others do during the pause. This is a playable world, not a narrated vision.\n\nLet the focal character's unstable perception arise from the physical conditions of the scene: a distant bell, a poorly lit doorway, an object moved between entrances, a line spoken while someone crosses upstage. The character may respond to a remark that was actually finished, miss an implication, or attach importance to the wrong detail. Do not make every mishearing a plot marker or a sign of madness. It is ordinary interruption understood through this character.\n\nAllow a stage aside only when the public scene cannot carry the required private experience. The aside should have a dramatic reason: the character is obliged to remain silent, is watching a ceremony whose official meaning conflicts with a private doubt, or is returning to an earlier phrase while others continue. Keep the aside short and return to the spoken scene. An aside is a staged private interval, not the character explaining every thought to the audience, and not something the other cast members know.\n\nAvoid film camera instructions. Mark exits, entrances, physical business, pauses, and sound as matters a company can stage. A door can be closed or guarded; a hall can seem too quiet because the expected name is not called. Stop according to the scene's actual boundary. The play may end while the focal character is still holding the relic, still facing a threshold, or still waiting for the word that did not arrive, with the stage having established the public event that left the character unresolved. It may also end after the public event has produced its consequence: the door is closed, the expected name is called, or the relic is handed over. In either case, the institution's material force remains present on the stage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized focal mind, allowing perception, memory, bodily sensation, and intrusive thought to move through association rather than through a linear account. A sound in the hall may lead to a childhood corridor, then to the word of an oath, then back to the present speaker's unfinished sentence. Follow the live movement of attention, but keep the distinction between remembered event and present action visible: a remembered hall is not the present room, and a feared meaning is not the same as a witnessed act.\n\nLet the high-fantasy world remain materially and institutionally continuous outside the focal mind. The oath still holds, the relic still weighs what the others say it weighs, the title still determines where someone may sit or speak. The focal character may doubt what a ceremony means, misremember which person said which line, or attach a private fear to an ordinary household sound. Other characters continue under the world's rules, and the scene should let their actions register even when the focal character cannot yet interpret them.\n\nUse interrupted perception rather than general vagueness. The thought can break at a sudden voice, a change in light, pain, cold, or the recurrence of a name. The syntax may shorten, loop, or shift, but it should not obscure whether the event is happening now. When a public matter re-enters, ground it in a concrete detail: the raised hand, the door, the seal, the spoken genealogical name.\n\nThe high-fantasy scale appears through what the focal mind returns to and fails to resolve rather than through an imposed explanation. Recurrence is important: the same obligation or family name may return with a slightly different pressure each time. Let that recurrence accrue meaning while the mind continues to move. Stop according to the scene's actual boundary. The character may reach a conclusion, a breakthrough, or a better interpretation when the accumulated associations support one, or the mind may remain interrupted and unresolved. The world's grandeur can remain substantively present either way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent the story as a message exchange with clear sender attribution. The high-fantasy world is not decorative wallpaper around the chat; it enters through orders, summons, questions about lineage, updates from a rite or council, and the practical obligations each sender faces. When a title, oath, or institution matters, show its effect in what someone is expected to do before or after replying: report to a hall, hand over a seal, wait for a decision, or correct a name under authority.\n\nLet one focal sender write under interruption and unstable attention. The transcript can show a message sent before the sender realizes they misread the prior line, a correction arriving late, a question left half-answered because another matter broke in, or a reply that addresses the wrong version of an event. Use editing to represent a mind already changing its interpretation. A delivered message has consequences; an unsent draft is composed text that may never reach anyone and should be marked so it cannot be mistaken for private thought or sent speech.\n\nKeep private thought separate from transmitted text. If a gap in the exchange hides what a sender is feeling, the private material can appear clearly separated from the message log, but another sender must not know it unless their own observation explains the inference. Delays and read receipts mean only what the in-world interface is established to mean; do not invent hidden meanings for every typo or pause. A real interruption may be as simple as a bell, a person entering, or the sender needing both hands for a task.\n\nDo not flatten the high-fantasy scale into debate in a group chat. The stakes remain in the world behind the messages: someone is waiting at a border, a marriage is being considered, a debt falls due, or an old claim must be answered. The exchange can show people managing those stakes through terse instructions and misaligned attention. Stop according to the exchange's actual boundary. The conversation may end on an unresolved thread, an unanswered official question, or a sender still typing while another event demands their departure. It may also end after a practical answer has landed, a sender has departed cleanly, or an official question has just been answered. The transcript remains a slice of a living institution, not a substitute for it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaaba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Visual Novel\n\nKeep the presentation scene-oriented, with background and expression cues used only when they orient the current moment. A hall hung with house banners, a doorway where the ward-mark gleams, a council table, or a character turning away can establish the high-fantasy situation without explaining it. The world's titles, relics, oaths, and institutions should be present in the scene and should constrain what characters can do: someone may be admitted or refused, a seal may be passed, an heir may be named or disputed.\n\nLet the focal narration carry an interrupted and provisional reading of what is shown. The character may notice the wrong detail first, hear one clause and not the next, take an expression for approval, or fail to recognize a banner's significance until someone else reacts. An expression cue should be described as a visible detail, not as proof of motive. When speech is affected by noise or shifting attention, show the specific disturbance: a speaker turned away, a bell, a moving crowd, the focal character's own fatigue.\n\nAlternate concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and focal inner response. The private response does not need to resolve into a decision or a spoken line, but it should be actual thought or feeling under the active contract, not a menu prompt. Other characters remain outside the focal mind; they may observe changed behavior without knowing its cause.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance systems, or executable engine commands. Transitions can be as simple as a new scene heading when the location or time changes. Stop according to the scene's actual boundary. The scene may end while the focal character is still looking at the relic, still holding a half-interpreted gesture, or still listening for the part of the oath that was lost. It may also end after the focal character has finished the interpretation, someone else has reacted, or the visible world has moved on to its next practical task. The illustrated scene supports the uncertainty by keeping the visible world continuous even when the focal reading of it remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaabb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Web Serial\n\nLet each installment continue a particular high-fantasy obligation, institution, or claim. Maintain a clear through-line: an oath under dispute, a border being prepared, a relic awaiting proof, or a title whose inheritance is unsettled. When the action resumes, recover only the needed concrete details, such as which hall the characters are in, what promise remains unanswered, or why a particular object matters. New developments should arise from the world's conditions, not from a forced twist at every break.\n\nWrite the focal character as someone returning to the same situation with interrupted attention and an unfinished interpretation. A phrase misheard in one installment may be repeated correctly in another. A room visited before may now be lit differently, making an old detail more legible or more troubling. Let each recurrence change the range of possible meanings without erasing the original fact. The character can notice a new implication while ordinary work continues around them.\n\nProgress can consist of knowledge, relationship, resources, or practical consequence. An installment may close on completed work, a newly answered practical question, someone's departure, or the focal character standing with a returned letter before its meaning is settled. It may also close after the meaning has settled, the work has moved forward, or the practical question has been answered. The unresolved point of view is a method of attention, not a permanent deferral of every consequence.\n\nKeep re-entry brief and functional. Avoid a long summary of previous episodes or a complete miniature chapter in every reply. Speech affected by noise or attention should remain specific to the scene: a bell, a door, a name called from outside. The serial rhythm gives the focal mind time to revisit the same high-fantasy obligation without forcing a final interpretation, but the world's obligations keep moving, and ordinary people respond to them in ways the unresolved point of view cannot always predict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nMake the character's obligations, magical or political resources, and known history enter their emotional problem directly. Movement between places, use of power, or mention of an old title can renew a feeling the character does not say aloud. Let scale sit inside a constrained personal choice: a spell is prepared, a ritual falls due, a letter must be answered, a route depends on someone who once failed them. The world remains large, but the scene's cost runs through one specific relationship.\n\nWrite the angsty material through the viewpoint character's effort to manage need and exposure, not through a fixed vocabulary of torment. A character may stay close to a former friend because duty requires it, say the practical thing while privately assembling the older offense, or nearly apologize and return to a defensible grievance. Let that divergence show in attention and silence as much as in speech. When the character does admit the cost, treat the statement as an action with consequences: the other person may accept it, deflect it, misunderstand it, or respond from an injury of their own.\n\nKeep the emotional cost legible in the manner of Sandersonian consequence rather than letting it become atmospheric. The character may arrive with a limited ability to remain patient, a recent loss that leaves less room for someone else's explanation, or an obligation that makes honesty expensive at this hour. If an emotional effort draws on that resource, show how the next demand feels harder. If comfort comes, make it specific and incomplete; shared warmth can sit beside an unresolved slight or a promise the speaker still cannot make. Do not force the relationship to heal, dissolve, or reach a final confession once vulnerability appears.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Chat Roleplay\n\nBuild each entry as a readable, scannable interval that keeps the active scene in motion. Use short paragraphs centered on a concrete action, sensory fact, or movement, and make clear who speaks. Allow the player's chosen position to anchor the scene: the setting's visible details and available tools should be available to the player without the entry silently acting for them.\n\nUse high fantasy's systems and constraints to make choices testable. If a lock needs a token, a border requires a signed oath, or a spirit answers only while the moon remains above the ridge, state the relevant condition when it bears on the attempt. Show preparation and consequence on the same physical or magical terms: a spent lantern, an emptied route, a sealed acknowledgment from the institution whose permission was needed. Keep the world larger than the immediate scene by letting an NPC, message, or signal carry independent activity, but do not interrupt every exchange to deliver exposition.\n\nPresent NPC behavior through the available speaker's expressions, statements, and known interests, while keeping their private reasoning out of shared narration unless the active contract allows it. An NPC can cooperate, withhold help, ask a practical favor, or pursue an errand of their own; their next move follows the scene's present constraints rather than a compulsory cliffhanger. End the entry at a natural break where the player has room to speak or act next, and leave surrounding movement available: an attendant waiting for an answer, a fire settling, or a patrol expected soon establishes continuation without forcing a question or decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route. Move the story through marked incidents with simple connective transitions: a journey leg, a summons, a delivery, a seasonal assembly, an argument at a gate. Let each incident complete a practical question while leaving a larger obligation or unresolved relationship ready for the next episode. Titles and oaths should enter through what a person must carry, repay, or explain at that stage, not through a single lecture on the realm's mythology.\n\nMake each episode legible as a sequence of constraints and consequences. At an outer gate, say who has leave to enter and what is owed for passage. When old institutions act, show the rule they enforce through a person's hands: a steward counts the sealed letters, a border official refuses a name that lacks the correct mark, a host sets out provisions saved from a past promise. Keep preparation and use connected. If a character spends a ward, rested horse, or borrowed introduction in one incident, show the next incident carrying that loss forward.\n\nHandle time jumps as part of the episodic method. A new section can resume after a change of season, destination, or appointment, but make the first concrete detail reestablish what the characters still hold and still owe. Use recurrence as memory and burden, not as proof of destiny: a repeated gate, badge, or phrase indicates the same obligation or institution returning under altered circumstances. Preserve the characters' own knowledge from one incident to another; they may recall an earlier encounter only as clearly as the elapsed time and available information allow.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite high fantasy's powers, oaths, and institutions through hard physical details. A working of magic is a job done with salt, water, breath, and daylight; a ritual's cost shows in cracked skin, a floor scoured too clean, or a bell hauled until its rope burns the palm. Keep transitions tight and concrete: time passes through a shadow crossing a threshold, a horse shifting its weight, water rising over a boot. Do not soften each failure into atmosphere. A fall, a leak, a broken seal, or a snapped strap has a material result that the next beat must absorb.\n\nMake resource logic immediate. Before power or aid works, indicate what feeds it, what is left afterward, and what condition cannot be met. Use direct statements and concrete quantities when the attempt depends on them: two candles remain for the binding, the gate will hold only while the iron stays cold, the writ passes one rider and not the pack animal. Keep action and explanation pressed together; a character should not pause for exposition unless the work requires them to test a rule or read a damaged instruction.\n\nLet the prose spare words without losing the person's reasoning. Short sentences can deliver the hard action, then open into a longer thought when the character must weigh a constrained choice against a known danger. Abrasive or rough speech belongs to the speaker: a sergeant may curse when a rope gives, while the person holding the order may say nothing. Keep the world large through a visible mark of its institutions, but let consequence stay personal and material rather than becoming sermon or dread.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story through documents suited to the scene: a letter of passage, a captain's log, a ward ledger, an oath taken and later annotated, a temple inventory, a demand recorded by a clerk who did not see the fight. For each document, establish its author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the form control what can be known: a courier's note reports only what happened before departure; a ledger records that a key was drawn, not why the hand hesitated. Preserve the author's motive in phrase and omission, but do not turn every writer into a liar.\n\nUse the document's practical function to keep fantasy mechanics legible. If a request depends on a seal, treaty, bloodline, or replenishment cycle, let the writer state or misunderstand the condition in terms that serve their record. A request for more light-wards can explain that the house's pact renews only at the new moon; a steward's report can show that the sacred grain arrived spoiled, making the coming ritual impossible. Show errors, corrections, crossed lines, and later additions as material traces without adding an explanatory narrative after every record.\n\nMove across documents through a recoverable line of need or consequence. A petition leads to a denial, which leads to a record of goods moved anyway, which a later friend reads as proof of a relationship that was never declared. Use gaps to hold attention rather than to hide the entire answer: the story remains readable because each document answers one practical question while opening a neighboring uncertainty. Let documents repeat an institution's formula or a character's phrasing with changed meaning, but never imply that repetition by itself certifies a claim. Keep private experience distinct from what a character chose to write; a letter or memoir is an authored artifact, not automatic access to the writer's complete live mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nMake comfort specific to the characters and the high-fantasy world. Warmth arrives through chosen care: the right tea kept back for a late arrival, a bench placed near the only warm vent, a repaired strap returned without comment, a remembered preference for bread with the burned crust cut away. Let the act carry sincerity without demanding the character name their feeling. Affection can appear in attention to what another person needs before they ask, and it need not become a confession or a full repair of an old hurt.\n\nUse the setting's obligations and resources to support care rather than erase them. A character may be tired from a watch or worried about a debt and still sit down to eat; another may prepare a simple ward because the house has been cold. Show the small practical sequence: hands warmed on a bowl, boots set aside to dry, a blanket offered because someone forgot theirs at the gate. Keep high fantasy's scale nearby, but let its weight rest for this interval. The surrounding problems remain real and unhealed, yet the scene's focus stays on the meal, the rest, or the particular person rather than continuously reopening the conflict.\n\nPreserve individual limits and privacy. A kind person may refuse to discuss a private grief; another may accept care but continue to disagree with the plan. Do not require everyone to become agreeable or to resolve their complaint because the scene is warm. Gentle humor and ordinary friction can belong to the character or situation, but there is no required quipping or reconciliation beat. Each comfort should arise from the actual relationship and available means of the world, not from an imported cozy domestic setting or the suspension of established danger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions. Open a scene by placing its action within a social or historical frame the chosen narrator can see: the hall's arrangement as an old treaty court, a cramped house that still follows the mourning custom of a conquered province, a public square where the day's work follows an interrupted festival. Use the narrator's distance to set that frame without claiming access to minds the active contract excludes.\n\nKeep high-fantasy power and consequence legible inside the formal movement. When a character prepares or attempts something, let the narration remain clear about the relevant condition and cost rather than burying them in ceremony. A formal passage may show the order of a working, the number of petitioners still waiting, or the exact object that must change hands before a request can be made. Weigh social context and practical detail together: a refusal may be courteous in form while materially ruinous, and the narration may carry that contrast in its phrasing.\n\nUse ceremony, correspondence, and the line between public conduct and private behavior as organizing choices when the scene supplies them. A character may perform composure before witnesses, then reveal less in a later room; the narration can follow that change by shifting attention from the room's collective observation to particular unguarded details. Do not force uniformly elevated speech or an aristocratic setting. Formal architecture belongs to the prose; the cast may include laborers, soldiers, and household workers whose direct speech still sounds like their own lives.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the occasion: a real teller addressing a real listener in a room, war-camp, archive, or holdfast, with something the telling is for—counsel wanted, an explanation demanded, time being won, support being earned. Establish that stake before the tale begins, and let the teller's choices about what to recount first act on the present relationship: opening with the relic rather than the betrayal is itself a move between these two people.\n\nKeep one master timeline under the embedded narration. When the tale touches magic, name the mechanism and its cost as the teller understands it—a binding paid for in strength, blood, or memory—and let events obey that rule at every narrative depth; a nested story may be shorter, never differently governed. Distinguish what the teller witnessed, what they inferred afterward, and what they are extrapolating in order to move this listener. The tale is an account with a speaker and a purpose, not a transparent window on the past.\n\nBring the outer listener back as a participant. They can press a contradiction, supply a piece from their own tradition, or recognize that the recounted route matches a present map, oath, or claim—and that recognition should re-price the next practical move, even if no decision lands in this reply. Treat the teller's memory as a resource with limits, not as proof. Let a phrase or object repeated early in the tale gather a narrowed meaning at the return, rather than a coda announcing a truth the listener never established from the telling. The tale may not grant spells the world forbids or outcomes no one inside it caused.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the documentary and historical-memory route. The narration is an account composed for a purpose: council minutes later edited, a memorial for the dead of a campaign, a field report moving up an institution's chain, a family history read at an inheritance. Let the document's audience set its distance from the first line—a report to superiors compresses, a memorial lingers, minutes record what the recorder chose to notice—and let moments of composition surface where they matter: a line hesitated over, a passage reread, something refused in full.\n\nCarry the world's constraints through what the recorder could know and chose to write down. When a rite or relic affects the recorded events, put its named rule and one observable cost in the same passage that shows the attempt, so the causal chain survives the formal voice. Let the record do what its genre does: omit what it would never mention, misattribute what witnesses disputed, note in a later hand that a procedure changed after a specific failure. Show those deformations through concrete marks—an interlineation, a contradicting older copy, a relic whose effect survives only in the record of a failed test—instead of announcing that memory is unreliable.\n\nKeep the stakes of composition live: the account exists because someone must decide, answer, or answer for something, and the recorder's own obligations run through what gets included. Where the document cites oral tradition, treat that speech as another recorded object with its teller, not a veiled second magic. Leave the world's history and its debts fully legible, with the limited voice making their cost felt rather than explained away.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Gothic-Punk\n\nPut the old authority and the living city in one physical frame. Every inherited claim has a place where it is enforced—a declining order's stronghold above the market, a shrine against a barricaded tenement, a noble holding cut into a crowded district—and the scene should require that claim to pass through a present-day arrangement: passage paid for, a relic hidden from a cordon, a street-level official whose say-so the protagonist cannot simply override.\n\nKeep magical knowledge legible and scarce rather than atmospheric. When a relic or rite enters, state one operational rule and its cost, and let the cost bite in this scene—a memory offered up, the weakness after holding a ward, a route burned and unrepeatable. Let the decay of institutions deform how that magic is used—through surcharge, dilution, or favor—but never let mood substitute for the mechanism. An old rite may be witnessed badly, through fear or damage, and still be the same rule the world enforces.\n\nLet intimacy lower a mask for one particular person. In a trusted moment a character can say what an oath costs them, which debts are payable, why they serve a patrimony they no longer believe in—private, practical, and binding on what the hearer may do next without changing what is true. Bonds buy no exemption from a rule the setting enforces, and an heirloom does no more than its established nature allows. Keep the milieu's charge—old power pressing on present lives, desire under masks—without turning allies, lovers, or trusted households into guaranteed traitors.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nRun the scene on social and domestic realism: keep it inside the household, lane, or trade network where promises, property, and standing are actually negotiated. When a magical obligation touches that sphere, handle it with the same concreteness as a loan, a land claim, or the roof repaired before the rains—show who administers the binding ritual, what it consumes, and what its failure would do to these particular people.\n\nDraw region, period, and community only from the supplied world. The moneylender's ledger, the cook's provisions, the names and forms of address a family actually uses must carry the scene; do not manufacture a generic household or impose a default structure. Social pressure works on the magic rather than decorating it: a character may decline a rite because it would disgrace the house, undergo it to shield a sibling, or exploit a loophole in the old rule—and the magic still does exactly what its rule says.\n\nMake cost visible before and after. Show what using an ability takes in body, money, or standing while the choice is still open; then let kin respond from their own interests, which may include genuine support. Disagreement behind closed doors need not become public betrayal. Keep the world's active history present through today's consequences—an old battle surviving as a pension dispute, a debt, an oath still binding the family's land—seen through the current day rather than narrated as chronicle. The people keep their ordinary, motivated lives; the magic is what makes their private choices consequential.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story as a reflective personal record: a diary or miscellany kept by whoever holds the pen in the scene—a court diarist, a traveling scholar, a warden of a sealed archive, a prisoner counting days—and let that writer's distance set the prose from the first entry. The record is composed, not inert: it exists because events still have consequences, and alongside them the entries register the writer's needs, negotiations, and private responses—the doubt, relief, or calculation present while the line is being set down.\n\nFold each magical mechanism into the record's own idiom at the moment it affects an attempt. Let the diarist name rule and cost as they would—\"spent the last of the spring resin to see through the warden's gate\"; \"the oath-mark burned a day for each lie I spared her\"—so the system stays legible through the reflective surface instead of stopping for a lecture. Omit only what this writer would not write; never drop a link the reader needs in order to explain why something happened. Reflection may circle back to an earlier entry, never to a fact the writer could not yet have known.\n\nWhere the seasons enter, keep them practical: rain delaying a journey, snow closing a pass, heat spoiling a tincture, observed as the writer lives through them rather than as a set piece. The voice may be lyrical, plain, or sharply argumentative; a direct quarrel, an angry entry, or a comic aside belongs to the teller if the person supports it, with no requirement of universal politeness or understatement. What stays constant is a world whose rules and costs hold, recorded by someone with a continuing reason to keep the book.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse testimony with a stake in its recipient: an account given by someone who lived the events—a survivor before a tribunal, a clerk keeping the town chronicle through a rebellion, a farmer or miner explaining to neighbors what the old pact did—composed because listeners with power or need must decide something. Let the speaker's position in the community, and their fear or hope about who receives the words, shape what is claimed, conceded, and deferred; let their experience while speaking show where it matters—what it costs to say a name aloud, where the account steadies itself—without dissolving into general atmosphere.\n\nKeep the magic concrete under oath. When a binding or relic enters the account, show who administers it, what price is paid, and what evidence of the price remains present: a signer's failing eyesight each spring, a boundary stone still standing where the pact set it. A witness may state the price plainly and the story must then honor it—the rule operates whatever the official explanation asserts. Separate what the speaker knew then from what they know now, comparing the two without rewriting the earlier moment.\n\nDraw language, work, food, names, and political divisions from the supplied world rather than a stock landscape. The recounting should do present work—persuade a magistrate, shield a neighbor, force an heir to account for a broken promise—and where the chosen world includes conflict, let it shape who can safely speak. Treat any marvel strictly as the setting's own named magic, observed under stress and reported as fact; add no softer supernatural layer the world does not contain, and keep the record's causal chain clear of folklore.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaban::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Light Novel\n\nEnter in motion: show where the character is and what needs doing now, and let the wider obligation surface through that task rather than ahead of it. Keep paragraphs short and visually concrete, moving location to location without summary gaps; when a landscape or institution must be known, deliver it in pieces as the character crosses it—a mural on the gatehouse, a burned hall, an oath cut above a locked door.\n\nIntroduce each magical mechanism at the moment it affects an attempt, physically framed: the gesture, the change in the room or body, the named cost, in that order. Make cause and effect legible with a line break or a short beat rather than pausing the incident for history; let the rule surface inside the attempt—\"the oath only holds while both hands are bare,\" seen when a glove has to come off in winter—so the reader could replay the logic afterward.\n\nGive reactions their own weight. After a revelation, failure, or narrow escape, take a beat on the character's physical and internal response; it may be grave, uncertain, furious, or funny, as the person and the moment warrant. Speech and private thought stay distinct without being obliged to disagree. First person, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are not defaults—use any of them only if the story itself calls for it. Break paragraphs around dialogue, action, and private response, and end each segment at a natural turn in the scene, whether or not the next move has been chosen.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the scene so that the lyric movement of a sentence is also the explanation of a constraint. When a resource, oath, or inherited obligation limits what a character can attempt, let the syntax enact that narrowing: a sentence may begin with the full range of possibility, then close through subordinate clauses until only the available action remains. A recurring image can measure depletion or renewal across the scene—light failing in a reservoir, a seal's edge wearing thin, a name spoken with less certainty—so the reader feels the cost before it is stated. Keep the underlying rule legible; the image must trace an actual mechanism, not replace it.\n\nDevelop a comparison when it changes what the narrator understands about the problem, and let that changed understanding enter the character's reasoning. A character studying a failing ward may see it as a remembered voice losing its consonants, and that perception can reveal which part of the working has weakened; the next thought should then test that perception against the practical facts—what can still be done, what has been spent, and what the next attempt will require. Do not let the comparison obscure those facts. Plain declarative sentences should carry the decisive information, with the figurative language gathering around moments of uncertainty, effort, or consequence.\n\nLet the weight of history enter through objects and places rather than exposition. A relic need not be described as ancient; show the accumulated care or damage in its surface, and let that texture explain why people treat it as binding. When an oath or institution presses on a character, render the pressure through a specific perceptual or temporal consequence of the present attempt: a held breath before the forbidden word, a room gone quiet when the old title is invoked, the difference between the promise as first made and the promise as it now must be kept. The grandeur belongs to the situation, not to compulsory formal speech. Dialogue may stay plain and practical even when the narration around it is heightened, and the lyric register should not make every character sound like the narrator.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nAnchor reflection in the specific history of the world's constraints. When a character faces a depleted resource, a binding oath, or an institution that has changed its meaning, let the narration recall the earlier state with enough precision that the loss is concrete: what the resource could once power, what the oath permitted before its present clause became impossible, who the institution served before its current compromise. The present attempt should remain legible in Sandersonian terms—what can be done now, what it will cost—but the reflection shows that the cost is measured against a particular past, not a general sense of decline.\n\nUse small irreversible details to carry the feeling, and tie each detail to the mechanism's actual function. A character may notice that a ritual phrase is now spoken with a hesitation it never had, and recognize that the hesitation marks a weakened enforcement. A map may still show a road the land no longer honors, and the discrepancy may explain why a supply line or summons now fails. A familiar tool may fit the hand differently after its last repair, and the difference may limit what the next attempt can accomplish. Let one such detail open into memory, then return to the present decision. The memory should illuminate the choice without replacing it: the character can still act, and the action may succeed, but the reflection makes clear what the success will not restore.\n\nAllow ordinary pleasure to remain pleasurable. A warm room, a shared meal, or a working device may be enjoyed without immediately announcing its impermanence. The melancholy belongs to the narrator's or focal character's attention to what has changed, not to a compulsory sadness imposed on every object. When a remembered occasion returns, let it return for a reason—because the present scene resembles it, contradicts it, or makes a former possibility newly relevant. Keep the reflection within the selected perspective, and do not let the narrator know a later loss that the character has not yet experienced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral-storytelling route. Open with a situated teller and audience whose presence matters to the account: a traveler explaining a failed working to the people who must pay for it, a keeper of records answering a ruler's question, a survivor telling younger listeners why a particular oath is still binding. Let the frame establish why the story is being told now, and return to it when the audience's need changes what the teller explains.\n\nMake the audience's questions do the work of Sandersonian clarification. When a listener asks how the reservoir was drained, why the seal held only three days, or what the old compact actually required, the teller answers with the concrete mechanism. The explanation should be precise enough to make the later events understandable: what was spent, what could have been done differently, and what the next attempt will require. Do not let the frame become a lecture; the teller may omit, simplify, or emphasize according to the listeners' concerns, and the narration can show what the teller chooses not to say. When the teller omits a fact, let the omission itself be legible as a choice about what the audience can bear, what the teller is ashamed to admit, or what would invite a question the teller cannot answer.\n\nLet the nested account carry the weight of inherited obligation. A title, relic, or ancient event should enter the story as something the teller must make present for the audience—its origin, its cost, and the moment it changed what people could do. Keep the regional tradition coherent: do not mix every convention of framed storytelling, and do not impose mysticism, exile, religion, or a single honor code on the scene. The teller's access remains limited to what the teller knows or has been told. The frame's present moment may end with the audience's question unanswered or the story paused, leaving the unfinished telling itself as the pressure that continues into the next scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect details that make the mechanism inferable without stating it twice. When a character prepares an attempt, show the exact elements that matter: the weight left in the pouch, the number of seals still unbroken, the one clause of the oath that cannot be met. Let the next sentence show the consequence—a failed ignition, a door that stays shut, a messenger who will not meet the character's eyes. The reader should understand what happened and why from the sequence, not from a summary afterward.\n\nKeep direct clauses and a small number of concrete images. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several fragments if it carries the causal chain: what was spent, what the spending permitted, what remains. Do not delete causality for the sake of shortness. When an inward connection materially contributes—why the character hesitates, what the cost means to them—state it once and move on, and make it illuminate the causal chain rather than decorate it. Omission should leave the mechanism inferable: a missing tool implies its loss and therefore a failed preparation, a changed ritual implies the institution's compromise and therefore a new limit on what can be attempted, but the narration need not announce the implication.\n\nLet titles, relics, and oaths work through their practical effects. A relic is not described as ancient; it is shown by what it still does or no longer does. An oath matters because it closes off an action or forces a payment, not because the narration calls it sacred. Keep character voices individual: one speaker may use few words, another may explain at length, and the minimalist discipline belongs to the narration's selection, not to a compulsory terseness imposed on every line of dialogue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in continuous scene-first prose, letting the narrator move naturally among what the character sees, says, remembers, and reasons. Each movement should be earned by the present situation: a perception may reveal a mechanism's state, a memory may supply the earlier test that explains the current failure, a line of dialogue may expose what another character knows about the constraint. Do not use transitions as recap machinery; let the scene's immediate pressure determine when the narration turns inward or outward.\n\nKeep the Sandersonian account legible inside that movement. When a resource, oath, or institutional rule affects an attempt, the narration should make clear what the character understands, what they are trying, and what the attempt costs. The reasoning may unfold across action and dialogue rather than in a single explanatory block: a character may test a seal, name what the test should have shown, and then adjust the plan in the next exchange. Preparation and payoff should be traceable, but they need not be announced with technical labels.\n\nLet the scale of the world touch individual choices through material consequence. A title matters because it changes who must answer for a failure; an ancient event matters because its surviving law still limits what can be built or promised. Render those effects in the scene's ordinary texture—a room arranged around a relic, a messenger's hesitation, a budget counted in something scarcer than coin. The narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register; contemporary presentation does not require a contemporary setting, understated emotion, or a lesson at the end of the reply. Stop where the scene's boundary falls, without requiring the problem to be understood, attempted, or still unfolding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect scenes that make the established relationship premise legible through the world's specific constraints. If the bond is shaped by an oath, show the moment that oath requires something different from each partner. If it is shaped by a resource or institution, show the private knowledge, shared ritual, or repeated argument that has grown around that mechanism. The scene should fulfill the premise's particular promise—affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery—through consequences that belong to this world, not through a roster of familiar tropes inserted from outside.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments as evidence of the bond's history, and let each callback also mark the mechanism's current state. A phrase may return when the same constraint presses again, and its meaning should be visible in how the characters respond: one may reach for it as comfort, another may hear it as an old accusation. The reader should understand what the phrase means to these people from the way it changes the present exchange, and should also see what the repeated moment reveals about the constraint that has returned—whether the oath now costs more, the resource has dwindled, or the institution's demand has shifted.\n\nKeep the Sandersonian mechanism legible inside the emotional scene. When a choice about the relationship also involves a magical cost, an oath's limit, or an institutional demand, make clear what each character understands and what the choice will require. The emotional payoff and the practical consequence should arrive together: a confession may cost a resource, a comfort may violate a rule, a rivalry may turn on who can afford the next attempt. Do not let the relationship override the world's logic, and do not let the world's logic erase the particular feeling between these characters. End where the scene's boundary falls, without requiring the emotional question to be met, changed, or left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the scene as an account of deeds whose consequences explain the world's constraints. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes the person, place, or obligation at stake, then expand into the particulars of what was attempted, what was spent, and what held or failed. A mechanism should become legible through the remembered act: the reservoir's limit is known because someone once drew too much, the oath's binding is known because someone once tried to break it, the institution's power is known because someone once refused its summons. Let each recurrence add a new application of the rule rather than restating it.\n\nUse compact declarations and recurring phrasing to mark what will be remembered. A designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it new meaning: the line spoken at a first failure may be repeated at a later success, and the repetition should show what has changed. Keep the account's access consistent with the selected perspective; the teller may know what the community remembers but not every private thought of the people involved. When the teller cannot know a private motive, make the mechanism legible through the observed deed and its reported consequence rather than through an invented interiority.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority when the subject of a remembered act is present. The person credited with a deed may correct a detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This need not become parody: the correction can clarify the mechanism—what actually happened, what the story has exaggerated—without mocking the telling. Keep the particular source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention, and do not import a pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker. The account may end where the scene's boundary falls, without requiring the deed to be concluded or the next attempt to be left to be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse a myth-inflected Scandinavian telling: the narration treats the world's magic, oaths, and ancient institutions as inherited local reality that people already live inside, not as a subject requiring a detached lecture. Keep the account particular to a definite place and community. Weather, work, and domestic routine remain present, but they are not imported Scandinavian scenery. If the setting is cold, show what the cold does to a task; if a household is isolated, show what that isolation makes people rely on.\n\nLet the mechanism become legible through inherited practical knowledge rather than through systematic explanation. When a character attempts something with magical or institutional weight, the narration may supply the rule as a remembered instruction, a household saying, a neighbor's old failure, or a seasonal prohibition that the character now has reason to test. The rule should not sit above the scene as a manual. It should arrive with its source attached: who taught it, when it was last seen to hold, and what it cost the person who ignored it. A vow's cost becomes visible when the speaker's hand will not close around a tool, when a boundary will not let a trespasser pass, or when a named relic must be fed before it will answer.\n\nMake preparation and resource use legible through the community's material life. Show what a character brought, gave up, or accepted as part of a task the household already understands: the offering laid out before a journey, the tool left behind because the oath forbids it, the food or fuel spent on a rite that may fail. A failure is as instructive as a success. The attempt can reveal that the old rule was misremembered, that the relic answered a different question, or that the boundary held for a reason the character only now understands. Keep character values and competing commitments inside those decisions. The story can stop on a completed act, a refused bargain, or a person standing inside the consequence of what they chose.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues. Use a scene heading when location or time changes. Let the audience see the material conditions of magic and obligation: the prepared object, the marked boundary, the cost already paid, the gesture that will not work until a condition is met. When a mechanism affects an attempt, make its operation visible or audible. A relic may drink light from the room, a spoken oath may leave the speaker unable to lift the weapon they just named, or an ancient door may open only after a particular debt is acknowledged. Do not stop the scene for a lecture; let the consequence demonstrate the rule.\n\nUse dialogue to carry the terms people already accept. A character can state a condition because it is part of the task, not because the audience needs a manual: \"The ward holds until the third bell. After that it takes from whoever is nearest.\" Another character's hesitation or preparation can show what that condition costs. Keep competing commitments inside the spoken choices. A refusal may be practical, loyal, or fearful, and the action block should show what the refusal changes.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, and voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. A cut can compress travel or preparation while preserving the fact that preparation happened. An offscreen sound can announce a consequence before the next scene shows it. Required private prose can remain a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation; it is not automatically dialogue or knowledge available to the cast. The scene can end on a completed act, a visible failure, or the immediate consequence of the attempt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Stage Play\n\nUse the shared stage space to make magical and institutional constraints testable. Block the scene so the audience can see who can reach an object, cross a threshold, hear a line, or leave through a particular exit. When an oath, relic, or ancient institution affects an attempt, stage the attempt, the limit, and the result as one observable sequence. A character may set down a blade and find they cannot pick it up again after breaking a vow. A relic may remain dark until someone places a specific offering on it. A boundary may hold one person back while another passes. The physical relationship between the character and the object or threshold should carry the rule; no narrator explains the system.\n\nLet dialogue carry the terms people already accept. A character can name a condition because the scene requires it: \"The door opens for the one who paid the toll. I have not paid.\" Another character can test that claim by trying the door. Keep the relationship between lines and physical business active: a pause can be filled by someone checking a mark, turning an object over, or stepping back from a threshold. Do not turn every private thought into a monologue or aside. Required private experience remains nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation unless a dramatic purpose authorizes performed speech.\n\nKeep preparation and cost legible through what can be staged or spoken. What a character brought, gave up, or refused should be visible in the objects they carry, the offering they place, or the action they refuse to complete. A failure can reveal that the relic answered a different condition, that the oath was already spent, or that the institution's rule was older than the character assumed. The scene can end on a completed act, a refused bargain, or a character left alone with the consequence of what they chose.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet the telling move through a particular authorized mind's attention, braiding perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. When a magical or institutional mechanism affects an attempt, make it legible through that mind's experience. The character may remember the exact instruction that governs the relic, feel the oath as a pressure in the hand that will not close, or return again and again to the price they have not yet paid. The reader should understand what was risked and why the result followed, but the explanation arrives as lived knowledge: a remembered warning, an old failure, a sensation that confirms the rule is still active.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. If the mind slides from a real attempt into a feared outcome, mark the return to the present scene. A real consequence should be distinguishable from an imagined one: the door actually opens, the blade actually will not lift, the boundary actually burns. Do not let the associative method make all scene facts indeterminate. The mechanism remains causal even when the telling is not linear.\n\nLet preparation and resource use surface through memory and sensation as well as through present action. The mind may return to the offering it left behind, the promise it made, or the limit it accepted. A failure may arrive as the sudden recognition that the oath was already spent, that the relic answered a different question, or that the old boundary held for a reason the character now understands. Keep competing commitments inside the mind's movement. The scene can stop on a completed act, a refused bargain, or the character standing inside the consequence of what they chose.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one sender writes in short confirmations, another in careful clauses, another leaves a message unfinished. When a magical or institutional mechanism affects an attempt, make it legible through what people transmit, quote, confirm, or refuse to put in writing. A sender may type the oath and then find the message will not send until the price is named. A recipient may receive a relic's activation phrase only after a particular debt is acknowledged. A delay or read receipt may mark the moment a boundary was crossed. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the mechanism or the relationship.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. If required private experience appears, keep it clearly separated from the transcript and attributed to the character whose mind it belongs to. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has seen a draft or a private note.\n\nKeep preparation and cost legible through the exchange itself. A message may confirm that the offering is in place, a correction may name the wrong condition, or a silence may mean the sender is already paying the price. A failure can arrive as an unsent message, a refused confirmation, or a correction from someone who knows the older rule. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. The scene can end on a delivered message, a pending read receipt, or a sender looking at the words they have just agreed to send.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. When a magical or institutional mechanism affects an attempt, make it legible through what the scene shows and what the focal character notices. A background cue can introduce the condition: the relic's surface has gone dark, the boundary stones are no longer aligned, the oath-mark has spread past the wrist. A character's expression can register the cost, but it is not an automatic truth indicator. The mechanism should be confirmed by a visible or stated consequence, not by a reaction portrait alone.\n\nLet the focal narration carry the character's actual experience. They may notice that their hand will not close around the tool, remember the exact condition they failed to meet, or recognize that the institution's rule is older than the version they were taught. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. A speaker may see the focal character hesitate without knowing why. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nKeep preparation and cost legible through what the scene shows or the dialogue states. The character may set down the offering they brought, name the price they accepted, or refuse to complete a gesture the rule requires. A failure can reveal that the relic answered a different question, that the oath was already spent, or that the old boundary held for a reason the character now understands. The scene can end on a completed act, a visible failure, or a character left looking at the price they have just agreed to pay.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahabbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. When a magical or institutional mechanism affects an attempt, preserve the stated condition, preparation, or price as live material. A later installment can test an earlier rule by returning to the boundary the character was warned about, using the offering they prepared, or discovering that the oath they swore has already begun to take its price. Recover the necessary particular briefly rather than retelling the whole system. The reader should remember the rule because the story has kept it active, not because the narration restates it as a manual.\n\nLet progress involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A character may learn that the relic answered a different question, that the institution's rule is older than the version they were taught, or that the price they paid was not the one the oath required. Keep competing commitments inside those developments. A refusal may be practical, loyal, or fearful, and its consequence can carry into the next installment without being resolved immediately.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, a visible failure, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make the system perpetually recede. The style does not automatically create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene as a procedural confession built from the actual work of high fantasy obligation: maintaining a relic, renewing an oath, preparing a ritual, accounting for an inherited title, or carrying out the small repetitive tasks that keep an institution or magical bond alive. Let the narrator's account of these practical steps expose the emotional cost without the narrator naming it directly. A character can describe the exact sequence of cleaning a blade, the order of wards, the wording of a vow, or the route taken to a shrine while the reader understands that the real subject is a debt, a betrayal, a need, or a fear of being unworthy.\n\nUse hard, recurring fragments as the narrator's defensive rhythm. A phrase from the oath, a title, a family rule, or a physical gesture can return at moments of strain, each repetition carrying a slightly different weight. Do not let the recurrence become a magical truth indicator; it is the narrator's habit of self-exposure, not a proof of sincerity or guilt.\n\nLet emotional turbulence affect the actual scene. When a character admits something, withholds it, apologizes, or returns to a difficult relationship, show the strain in what they do next: a hand that keeps working, a silence that lasts too long, a practical task interrupted and resumed, a formal courtesy that becomes a weapon or a shield. Warmth, if it appears, can be partial, awkward, or accompanied by unresolved pain. The narrator's distance and diction should shape the suffering rather than a stock vocabulary of anguish.\n\nConnect the mythic scale to individual consequence. An ancient event, a magical law, or an institutional demand should change what a character can do in the present scene and what a decision costs. Grandeur belongs to the situation and the telling, not to compulsory formal speech for the whole cast or to exposition that replaces the scene. Keep the account confident on the surface while its defensive logic remains visible to the reader.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Each speaker's words should be clearly assigned, and narrative passages should stay brief enough to keep the scene scannable while still grounding the exchange in concrete high fantasy particulars: a relic being handled, an oath being quoted, a magical ward being checked, a title being invoked, or an institutional rule being followed.\n\nAnchor the scene with a recurring fragment. A phrase from an oath, a family rule, a ritual formula, or a physical gesture can return between speakers or across narrative beats. Each recurrence should carry the scene forward or shift its weight, not merely repeat for emphasis. The repetition belongs to the active world and the characters' habits, not to a narrator's hidden confession.\n\nLet the practical process of high fantasy obligation drive the interaction. Characters can prepare a ritual, maintain a magical object, account for an inherited duty, or carry out the small tasks that keep an institution alive. These actions should change what a character can do or what a decision costs in the present scene. Grandeur stays in the situation and the selected telling, not in compulsory formal speech for every speaker.\n\nKeep NPCs and world behavior active. They can cooperate, resist, continue their own practical lives, or respond to the player's actions according to the active authorship contract. End the response at a natural continuation point that follows from what has just happened. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end. The next turn can continue the same task, open a new exchange, or leave a request unanswered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as one episode in a journey or displacement, centered on a deed, encounter, or test of skill that carries the weight of high fantasy obligation: an oath to keep, a relic to protect, a master to answer to, a debt to repay, or a landscape that remembers an ancient event. Keep the episode's language, period, and community distinct from the actual setting and cast already supplied.\n\nGive the episode Palahniuk's hard rhythm through obsessive practical particulars. The narrator or focal character can describe the exact sequence of a technique, the condition of a weapon or magical tool, the steps of a ritual, or the rules of a martial code. Repetition should work as a recurring fragment: a phrase from an oath, a named technique, a family rule, or a physical gesture that returns at moments of consequence. Each recurrence should deepen the reader's sense of the character's defensive logic or the cost of loyalty, not merely decorate the prose.\n\nLet the deed change what a character can do or what a decision costs. A title, relic, oath, institution, or ancient event should have material consequences in the present scene. The episode can advance through action, dialogue, and practical process, but the mythic scale should remain connected to individual loyalties and physical stakes. Grandeur belongs to the situation and the selected telling, not to compulsory formal speech for the whole cast.\n\nKeep the episode self-contained enough to read clearly while leaving the journey open. The next episode can follow from the present deed without a cliffhanger or forced choice. Use the episodic martial adventure craft to organize the high fantasy material already in play, without transplanting a fixed cast, setting, or national speech pattern from the cultural tradition.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move between actions, observations, and speech without padding. Ground the high fantasy material in material wear and physical stakes: a relic's cracked surface, a blade's loose binding, a ward's failing edge, the weight of a ceremonial garment, the strain of a magical working, or the physical cost of an oath renewed. Titles, relics, oaths, institutions, landscapes, and ancient events should change what a character can do and what a decision costs in the present scene.\n\nUse obsessive particulars as the recurring fragment. A rule of the order, a phrase from the oath, a named technique, or a physical gesture can return at moments of pressure. Each recurrence should carry the scene forward or expose the character's defensive logic, not merely repeat for rhythm. The repetition belongs to the practical process the character is performing: maintaining a weapon, preparing a ritual, accounting for a debt, or carrying out an institutional duty.\n\nKeep the prose hard-edged without turning the genre into survival horror. Abrasive or rough speech should be grounded in its speaker, not distributed across the whole cast. A longer thought is acceptable when the character's reasoning needs it, but the default movement should be concrete and economical. Use changes of rhythm deliberately: a short sentence can land a consequence, a longer passage can follow a character working through a difficult choice.\n\nLet the mythic scale remain connected to individual loyalties and material consequences. Grandeur belongs to the situation and the selected telling, not to compulsory formal speech or exposition that replaces the scene. The account can be confident on the surface while its practical details reveal the cost of the obligation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Choose the form that fits the high fantasy material and the document's author: a letter, journal entry, report, inventory, oath record, ritual log, dispatch, or material trace. Do not rotate through all available formats.\n\nGive each document obsessive practical particulars. The author can describe the exact condition of a relic, the steps of a ritual, the wording of an oath, the route of a journey, or the rules of an institution. Repetition should work as a recurring fragment across entries: a phrase from the oath, a title, a family rule, or a physical gesture that returns in different documents. Each recurrence should reveal something about the author's defensive logic or the cost of the obligation, not merely repeat for rhythm.\n\nLet what each document knows or conceals follow its situation. A letter to a superior may omit a failure; a journal entry may record a fear the author would not speak aloud; a report may misread an event the reader can infer from another entry. Gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces should reveal events across entries without an omniscient explanation after every record. The high fantasy scale—ancient events, magical law, institutional demands—should change what a character can do and what a decision costs, but only as the documents themselves can show it.\n\nPreserve readable continuity across entries. The next document can follow from the previous one, answer it, contradict it, or reveal what an earlier author did not know. Do not require a cliffhanger or forced choice at the end of each record. The documents are composed artifacts, not automatic live private thought; a journal entry or letter contains what its author chooses to record, and the reader's access to interiority follows the active authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures, but ground them in the concrete work of high fantasy obligation. A character can prepare a meal according to a family rule, repair a relic with careful hands, renew a ward while explaining its history, share a ritual that once felt burdensome, or offer a moment of rest within an institution that rarely allows one. The mythic scale remains present through these particulars: a title, oath, relic, or ancient event can shape what the characters do and what the moment costs without dominating the scene with exposition.\n\nUse obsessive particulars as a rhythm of care. A recurring phrase from an oath, a family rule, a named technique, or a physical gesture can return as a familiar comfort rather than a defensive confession. The repetition should feel chosen and specific to the characters, not mechanical. A character can find ease in the exact order of a ritual, the weight of a familiar object, or the predictable sequence of a shared task.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. Warmth can coexist with personality, privacy, and disagreement. A character may accept care awkwardly, deflect it, or offer it without naming what it means. Established problems from the world remain present; comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove the obligations, dangers, or costs already in play. Show this treatment operating within the high fantasy genre rather than silently replacing it with a domestic vignette.\n\nKeep the scene moving through practical care and small pleasures. The next moment can follow from what the characters are doing together, without a cliffhanger or forced emotional breakthrough. The prose should stay readable and warm without becoming uniformly soft; the high fantasy world's edges and the Palahniuk profile's concrete detail keep the comfort earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration move through a scene with enough distance to frame it through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits. The high fantasy material should supply that frame: an institution's history, an oath's public meaning, a relic's place in a lineage, a landscape shaped by an ancient event, or the social cost of a title. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language; the prose should feel composed rather than conversational, without forcing the whole cast into archaic or uniformly elaborate speech.\n\nEmbed obsessive particulars within the composed prose. The narrator or focal character can attend to the exact sequence of a ritual, the condition of a magical object, the rules of an order, or the physical details of a practical task. A recurring fragment—a phrase from the oath, a family rule, a named technique, or a gesture—can return at intervals, each recurrence carrying a slightly different weight. The repetition should expose the character's defensive logic or the cost of the obligation, not merely decorate the prose.\n\nUse ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast to organize a passage when relevant. A formal occasion can frame a private moment; a letter can interrupt or illuminate a scene; a public obligation can press against a personal loyalty. The mythic scale should change what a character can do and what a decision costs, connected to individual loyalties and material consequences. Grandeur belongs to the situation and the selected telling, not to exposition that replaces the scene.\n\nKeep the narration's access and knowledge separate. The narrator may reveal a focal motive while another character does not know it, but the style cannot grant access to excluded minds or cancel access permitted by the active authorship contract. The prose should remain composed and deliberate without turning every paragraph into a summary of social meaning; the scene's action, dialogue, and private experience still carry the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBegin in the middle of a telling: a named or clearly situated speaker has an audience, an occasion, and a present reason to speak. The occasion might be a debt hearing, a watch-fire, a deathbed, a road shrine, a muster, a prison visit, a council, or a quiet trade negotiation. What matters is that speaking now can change something in the room—aid, suspicion, delay, a promise, a reputation, a price, a refusal—and that the outer listeners have their own stakes in the tale.\n\nLet high-fantasy scale act through enforceable particulars. An oath binds behavior or magic; a title carries duties and limits; a relic must be carried, fed, hidden, or surrendered; a lineage determines who may enter, inherit, testify, or be protected; an institution sends summons, levies tithes, keeps rolls; a landscape makes a road dangerous or a border sacred; an old war or treaty still decides who is owed, who is suspect, who can cross. Use these large facts as constraints on what the speaker can ask, promise, refuse, or reveal right now. The room should feel the weight of history through cost, procedure, and consequence, not through a lecture about the world.\n\nWrite the account as a process confession. The speaker explains, with hard rhythm and obsessive practical detail, how a thing is actually done: how an oath is sworn and witnessed, how a relic is wrapped and carried, how a wound is packed, how a patrol counts its miles, how a debt is paid down, how a body is prepared, how a name is entered in a roll. Let the same phrase, gesture, number, or object return under pressure and gather altered meaning. The speaker can sound certain and self-justifying; let the defensive logic live in what they stress, skip, repeat, and refuse to name. Do not summarize motive when a concrete procedure can expose it.\n\nUse embedded stories as instruments, not decoration. Inside the main telling, the speaker can offer a past deed, a cautionary account, a rival's version, a joke that tests the room, a memory of a battle, or a tale about a relic or ancestor. Each nested story should do present work: buy time, warn without accusing, secure aid, challenge a listener's account, shift blame, teach a rule, or change how the audience hears a name or obligation that will matter when the frame returns. Mark thresholds through the speaker's address, a listener's interruption, a repeated object, or a phrase that moves into another mouth. Keep the levels clear enough that the reader knows who is speaking, who is listening, and what has changed in the outer room. Draw on the Arabian Nights as a craft source for nested telling, recurrence, and delayed meaning; build the setting and belief system from the high-fantasy world rather than importing djinn, palaces, deserts, or a particular religion by default.\n\nBuild delayed revelation from changed emphasis rather than withheld arbitrary secrets. A detail that seemed decorative—a tally, scar, key, song, tithe, map, weapon, title, or missing witness—can become decisive when a listener asks a practical question or when the speaker returns to it with new context. The reader may understand earlier than the audience, or later, but the shift should come from facts and emphasis already present in the telling. A repeated phrase can accrue meaning without appearing at every difficulty.\n\nMake the audience active. Listeners may believe, doubt, interrupt, bargain, grow bored, take offense, offer a counter-story, or use the pause to press a demand. Their reactions can alter what the speaker offers next, but do not force cooperation, refusal, revelation, or a cliffhanger between nested accounts. The outer situation can remain unresolved at the response boundary.\n\nFor interiority, render the point-of-view character's actual unsaid experience in the story's established convention. If the speaker is focal, include attention, calculation, shame, memory, physical sensation, and the thing they will not tell the listeners alongside the public performance. If another narrator observes the speaker, keep the speaker's private mind outside that narrator's knowledge unless the story's authorized viewpoint grants access. A performed tale, recitation, monologue, or unsent draft is an artifact with its own audience and omissions; it does not replace the focal character's nonspoken interiority.\n\nEnd at the current telling's natural edge: a sentence still being spoken, a listener's unfinished reply, a hand on a cup or weapon, a private thought during the pause, a nested account left mid-course, or a procedure interrupted by the next breath. The telling need not complete a bargain, win aid, settle a debt, or deliver a moral before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the documentary and historical-memory route of the German-language traditions: a telling organized around records, memory, and the friction between them. The narration follows someone who maintains, or maintained, a specific high-fantasy obligation (for example an oath binding a house to a river, the feeding of a siege-relic, a quarterly payment owed to a dead city, or the inspection of a border where magic is legally recognized), and the story is their procedural account: a testimony, deposition, maintenance log, or confession given to a specific audience, for a reason they have now, which you need not state unless it belongs in the scene. Give the obligation titles, offices, tools, wages, and costs that change what this person can do in the present; an ancient event, a relic, or an inherited rank should make one path cheaper and another impossible, not decorate the background. Open inside the present performance of the obligation — a step, an inspection, a payment — and let deep history surface only when a title, tool, or clause summons it, arriving as citation or memory rather than opening exposition.\n\nMove the telling between two registers. One is the record: the clause, the chronicle entry, the instruction, the inventory, quoted or paraphrased in official phrasing. The other is what the narrator personally did, saw, felt, or failed to notice. Let the two collide in concrete particulars — a step the manual names but that was performed differently, a count that no longer matches the ledger, a face the chronicle omits. When memory revises the record, show the discrepancy through detail rather than commentary; when the record corrects memory, let the narrator concede it exactly and move on. Do not let quotation replace the live scene: bring each cited clause in at the moment the narrator's hands or voice actually reach it.\n\nUse hard rhythms and obsessive particulars. The narrator repeats procedures, counted clauses, inventory tallies, body sensations, and practical rules. Make the repetition functional rather than constant: a phrase can return when a cost is paid, when a name is avoided, when a ritual step is performed, or when the narrator needs steadying, and each return should carry what has changed since the last. Let confidence expose its own defensive logic through what gets insisted on, explained too precisely, or left out; the reader can hear the defense without the narrator admitting it. Draw the obsessive detail from this world — the smell of the oil used on a relic, the wage owed to a temple runner, the exact number of stairs to the council chamber, the way a title changes how a guard answers — and do not import a transgressive plot, an identical coping mechanism, or a catalogue of taboo material for tone.\n\nKeep the prose measured and clause-aware in the documentary-memory manner: official phrasing inside personal recollection, the distance closing when memory takes over and re-forming when the record speaks. This route is a method of memory, documentation, and contested record, not translated word order, generic bureaucratic severity, or a single national temperament, and it does not relocate the cast or rename their world.\n\nThe account is composed for its audience, yet it still carries the narrator's actual private experience. Weave attention, hesitation, sensation, judgment, and motive into the prose between the procedures; where a thought must stay hidden from display, use the active hidden-interiority wrapper for this character. A recited oath, a chronicle entry, or a spoken confession is not a substitute for that live inner experience. Preserve the active narrator, person, and knowledge permissions: other characters' minds remain outside this narrator's reach unless the contract permits otherwise.\n\nKeep outcomes contingent. A decision may be practical, domestic, political, or ritual; do not require betrayal, a loyalty crisis, a completed test, or a change of plan. The response can end mid-procedure, mid-recollection, or mid-action, without a resolution or cliffhanger. Not every pause conceals guilt, not every official is kind, and not every magical law is unjust. Let other characters pursue their own practical lives and make choices that include cooperation and genuine help. The telling may move quickly or linger; its length and attention follow the narrator's situation, not a quota.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the story as a procedural confession shaped by an inheritance that is still active. Put the point-of-view character inside the practical routine for handling this world's inherited power—an oath, relic, title, office, magical procedure, or ancient obligation—and begin mid-procedure. Make the routine concrete enough to perform: specific steps, payments, permissions, repairs, refusals, physical handling, or ritual maintenance. Let its costs be exact—time, money, bodily risk, loyalty, legal exposure, memory, or the right to refuse—so the large-scale history registers through what the character must do and pay, not through summarized exposition.\n\nUse recurrence as pressure rather than decoration. Repeat a rule, phrase, gesture, count, or warning when the character is anxious, defensive, or trying to stay in control. Let that repetition accumulate and change meaning as the scene proceeds. A confident recital can expose what the narrator is protecting without stating it; denial can live in what gets over-explained, made practical, or left out. Keep the rhythm hard and the particulars specific to this narrator: prices, tools, scars, borrowed clothing, tokens, ward-marks, food, what shelter costs, the smell of a room no one has aired, the particular way an old obligation is paid.\n\nStage the gothic-punk collision between old authority and present life in material terms. The ancient claim does not stay ceremonial; it enters crowded quarters, a queue, a back room, a body that needs treatment, a debt collected by someone who knows the family too well. Let atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced rather than sit on top as mood. When intimacy matters, make it morally complicated—care and coercion, desire and obligation, old power and present need can share the same gesture. Social masks can hold around authority and fall away in trusted company, but the fall should reveal a particular person, not a generic confession.\n\nConnect the grand scale to individual loyalties through concrete consequence. If a relic, lineage, oath, office, landscape, or ancient event changes what someone can do, show the change in an action: the door that opens, the magical procedure that answers, the plea that fails, the price someone else pays. Let different people understand different parts of the arrangement and protect different interests; not every disagreement is ignorance or corruption. The scene stays with the actual decision, the actual body, the actual room; grandeur arrives through what the character already knows and what it costs to act on it.\n\nOrder information the way a defensive account does: let the recurring rule surface before its full reason, and allow the narrator's certainty to sit beside facts that do not quite support it. Do not force a reversal, confession, or resolution. The response may end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup. Keep outcomes contingent, but let the chosen magical and political facts have real weight. Render nonspoken private experience as actual thought, sensation, uncertainty, and reasoning—whether shown openly in ordinary story prose or kept in the active hidden-interiority wrapper. A letter, monologue, aside, or voice-over is not a substitute. Private experience can stand where it belongs without needing to trigger a practical decision.\n\nThe gothic-punk treatment is portable: do not import another setting's vampire physiology, Humanity scales, factions, or blood-bond rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the Indian traditions' testimonial route: a participant in an oath-bound service — a reliquary keep, a hereditary wardenship, a guild sworn to an old binding, whichever the story already supplies — gives an account of how the duty actually worked, in the active narrator's voice and person, to whichever listener, record, or inquiry the scene has set in front of them. The form carries public duty and household cost in one telling: let the account set the office's grain ledger beside the ancient thing the office feeds. Quote the received formulas — the rules, the verses, the sealing litany — as text the narrator was made to recite, and let them recite it still. Let the addressee shape the delivery: a term explained for an outsider, a page skipped because the record already holds it, an answer aimed at what the listener is quietly asking.\n\nRun the telling on procedure. Move through the work in short declaratives — the wrapping, the counting, the watch hours — and let one or two fragments return across the account, each return carrying what the narrator has admitted since, until a line first recited with pride comes back meaning something else. The confident voice betrays itself through arithmetic: what it tallies to the last measure — lamp oil, sleep debt, dues, cracked hands — and what it refuses to count at all. That refusal is the tell, and the narrator never names it. Keep the bodily and material detail inside this order's own economy; the hard surface comes from the office's ledger, not from imported shock.\n\nLet the oath work in both directions: it is what the testimony is about, and it is what the testimony must steer around. The title, the relic, the ancient event changed what this person could do and what each decision cost — whom they could not tell, what leaving would forfeit, why the question they never asked was itself forbidden — and that pressure should show in the telling's shape: what gets offered first, what arrives only late, what is told as a joke or an anecdote instead of an answer. Grandeur belongs to that situation, not to formal speech; remembered people speak the way the supplied world says they speak, and pivotal incidents come back as remembered scenes with dialogue, not as summary.\n\nKeep two layers separate. The testimony is a composed artifact — polished, defensive, built for its addressee — while the narrator's live private experience runs underneath it: the unpolished thought that interrupts the recitation, rendered as actual thought under the active interiority contract, free to back the account or cut against it without being reconciled into one voice. End wherever the story's own boundary falls — mid-procedure, mid-recitation, mid-answer — owing no collapse, confession, or absolution: the testimony may hold, may crack, may simply run out of watch. Invent no region, religion, caste, or family structure beyond what the supplied world specifies; the specificity comes from the office, the oath, and the ledger already in the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nTell this stretch of the story as a kept record — a diary that takes in miscellany, the personal form that jumps by attention rather than plot. Set it down in entries: a season-marked fragment, a tally, a copied rule, a line of weather, a note of something mended. Keep the voice, person, and ownership the active narration already has. If the teller literally keeps a watch-log, a relic-house register, a roll of names, the entries can stand as that artifact; if the narration stays in scene, carry the same entry logic into the beats — short forward blocks, each doing one practical thing, with gaps the reader crosses alone.\n\nMark time by the world's own calendar: the actual seasons, moon-phases, festival closings, ashfalls, whatever the supplied setting uses — never a scenery inventory imported from outside the world. A season note is a unit of omission. Write what changed — the price of tallow doubled, the south gate shut for the mourning month — and let the unwritten interval carry its weight. When the story costs something large, you need not record the event itself; record its logistics: the extra ration issued, the name struck from the roster, wax scraped from a standard and saved in a jar. Omission renders what the story already costs; it does not ask you to invent a disaster just to leave one out.\n\nRepeat the institution's real rules. Take the oaths, standing orders, station-mottos the story actually has and let one return as a refrain — flat, imperative, each appearance weighed by what has changed since the last, spaced so recurrence earns meaning instead of chanting at every difficulty. Give procedure room as standalone passages: how a relic is dressed, how a binding-shift is survived, how tribute is counted — numbered or imperative steps with the cost inside them: burned fingertips, sleep debt, salt allowances, exact candle-counts. Obsessive numbers are this voice's honesty.\n\nThe keeper's confidence is the confession. Let the record assert control — nothing to report, the seal holds, stores within tolerance — while surplus detail leaks what the voice will not say: a name recurring once too often, ink bought in doubled lots, a rule quoted a third time with no one asking. Never explain the gap; the reader does that work. The diary tradition allows a plain line of direct feeling when it truly arrives — lonely, tonight — set down and moved past. No collapse is owed; the composure can hold through the final entry.\n\nLet the mythic scale arrive as logistics. Titles show as drawing rights; oaths as forbidden purchases and required fasts; ancient events as reused material and rules whose reason is forgotten; institutions as a signature that closes a gate for a season. Grandeur lives in what a decision costs in stores and skin, not in speeches. Between and beneath the entries, render the writer's live, unrecorded experience wherever the active contract gives you interiority — the composed page and the thinking mind are different things, and both belong here. Everyone else stays outside the record: the keeper logs behavior, rumor, what can be checked. End at the record's edge — an entry can stop mid-task, a question can wait out a seasonal gap, and the last entry owes no revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nTell this story as testimony: one participant's account of a magical obligation, delivered to a definite hearer — a community archive, a tribunal, the heir who inherits the debt, the shrine that takes petitions. Establish early who receives the account and why the telling happens now, because the addressee decides what gets named plainly, what gets softened, and what gets rushed past. From the Latin American tradition this takes the chronicle-and-testimony route, with the marvelous treated as ordinary: workings, relics, and old binding events are matters of fact, named with the same flat vocabulary the community uses for weather and debts. Do not explain them to an outsider; the reader learns the world's rules by watching what the teller assumes the hearer already knows. Keep the language, community, and history the supplied world already has — the tradition supplies the method of telling, not a relocation.\n\nCarry the mythic scale as logistics rather than speechmaking. Titles, oaths, relics, institutions, landscapes, and ancient events should each change what someone can do today and what a choice costs: a road a treaty closed, a name that opens a granary, a debt that transferred at a burial, a boundary re-walked every year. Tie each large fact to a particular loyalty and a material consequence. Let every speaker's register come from their station and immediate pressure rather than a uniform court formality.\n\nGive the telling hard, short rhythms and obsessive procedure. When a working is performed or an obligation honored, recite it: the order of the words, the materials handled, the exact counts, the rule that had to be followed — flat declaratives, with bodily and household particulars chosen for this world and this teller. Let one or two fragments return at points of pressure — an oath formula, a stated price, a family rule — each recurrence carrying what the account has added since, though the phrase need not appear at every difficulty. The account's certainty is where it exposes itself: over-precise about materials, vague about what that precision stands guard over, its justification arriving one step too quickly. Let the reader find this in what is over-explained and what is skipped; never announce it.\n\nSort what the teller witnessed from what they were told, and by whom, and from what they have concluded since; earlier knowledge stays as it was, uncorrected by later discovery. The account may move nonlinearly when memory or obligation pulls it — a breaking told before its making — so long as each movement anchors in something concrete and the sequence stays recoverable. Fit the account to the active narrator and person instead of forcing first person: if the narration is the participant's own voice, let the telling-present and the testimony interleave; if the narration stands outside, make the testimony an event in the scene — spoken, recited, entered into the record — while the narration carries what the document cannot.\n\nAlongside the composed account, write the teller's live private experience as actual nonspoken experience: fear of the hearer's verdict, calculation running between sentences, grief surfacing between two steps of the recitation. Keep it in the story's presentation — plainly visible, or in the hidden wrapped form when the active contract uses one — and never let the recorded testimony substitute for it; other minds stay closed unless the contract grants access. End where the scene's own boundary falls: mid-recitation, mid-answer, a step of the working left unspoken, the hearer's question still hanging. Nothing has to resolve, reverse, or confess before the reply stops. A working takes the time it takes, and the waiting is itself part of the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Light Novel\n\nWrite the scene as a procedural confession. The narrator walks through what they did or are doing right now — the vigil kept in order, the relic handled by its clauses, the tithe rendered on schedule — as steps, counts, and rules, in a flat, confident voice that insists everything was done correctly. Let the mythic scale arrive only through that procedure. A title matters because of what it lets the narrator touch or command; an oath matters because each clause has an enforceable, itemizable cost — sleep, blood, coin, a year of service. When an ancient event or institution presses on the present, show it as a live obligation: a boundary that must be renewed, a debt collected by an office that still functions, a ward kept on a schedule. No history lecture; the past is whatever still has to be paid.\n\nGive the narrator a repeated fragment — a catechism line, a guild rule, one clause of the oath — and let it return at block turns like a chorus. Make the recurrence do work in the world, not just on the page: those words are what open the rite, still the relic, or hold the line, so every repetition is also an act with a cost. State the rules as universals — the count, the order, the way it has always been done — even where they are one narrator's private discipline. As stakes shift, the same words can read as comfort early and cage later; any change in what they carry should be earned by what happens, not scheduled. Track materials the way a shopkeeper would: candles burned, salt spent, what the working takes out of the body each time. These tallies are where the narrator betrays the cost they refuse to name.\n\nKeep the confident account and the private experience in separate registers, both real. What is said aloud — the correct order, the proper count, nobody's fault — can run against brief, actual beats of nonspoken experience: fear arriving as a physical fact, grief surfacing as an obsessive particular, the plain want to stop. Give the gap a reason that belongs to this character: rank, habit, an oath that forbids complaint, a crew that needs the steady voice. Render that private experience directly under the active contract rather than summarizing it, and keep access honest — the narrator may know what others don't and may still be wrong about it. Correct procedure is not the same as correct understanding.\n\nStage everything for fast reading. Open mid-action — hands already on the relic, the rite already three steps in — and keep every spatial change easy to follow: where the bodies are, where the object sits, who stands at the threshold. Give action, dialogue, and private response their own short blocks, and let a reaction beat land after the procedural ones: a sharp exhale, a clipped thought, one visual detail carrying the feeling. Present unfamiliar names and mechanics through use and reaction — what a thing does, how it feels to hold it, what the veteran mutters about it — never a block of explanation. Let each character speak at their own register; the narrator's hard fragments don't put formal speech in everyone else's mouth.\n\nTone belongs to the narrator and the moment — serious, angry, uncertain, or wry as the voice allows — with no required comedy, young cast, or closing cliffhanger. The reply can stop mid-procedure, mid-count, or mid-thought: end where the scene's pressure and the narrator's interiority are live, without pausing the work to manufacture a result, a confession, or a settled obligation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Lyrical Prose\n\nTreat any inherited obligation as a procedure the narrator performs or observes until the steps become a litany. For each required act—pouring a binding oil, speaking a name, turning a ring so the rune faces the wall—give the exact physical order and then let one concrete image open past its immediate literal sense. That image should come from within the procedure: the oil still warm from a brazier now cold, the name spoken into a silence older than the speaker. Do not reach for a separate nature metaphor. The lyric expansion belongs to the act already being performed.\n\nWhen a rule repeats, repeat its wording almost exactly, but place it in a slightly changed material situation so the second occurrence carries the weight of accumulated use. The repetition can expose the narrator's defensive logic without a confession. For example, a sentence about holding a charred oath-stone steady may sound like ritual clarity while it actually records the fear that any tremor will be read as insufficiency. Keep that fear in the work; do not announce it.\n\nChoose particular details sparingly. One stain spreading through the salt in the shape of a country whose border changed that winter can hold the cost better than a list of vanquished enemies. Keep every sensory expansion traceable to the scene. If the narrator compares a relic's gleam to an unpaid debt, the narrator must be able to see and recall that resemblance in the moment. End on the procedure continuing, suspended, or interrupted as the scene requires, with the spell's requirements intact and any private cost present only if the actual situation has made it felt, not on a completed moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite through the gap between an old obligation and what can still be done. The narrator performs or reviews a required act—counting the broken seals, wiping the tarnished socket, repeating the words that no longer settle—and notices specific evidence of what is gone: the ritual bread now baked with new flour, the lamp oil changed because the old is no longer sold. The loss is not named as grief; it appears in the mismatch between the procedure and the materials that remain available.\n\nUse repeated practical phrases, said almost identically each time the narrator goes through a routine. A phrase such as \"the cold side of the coffer\" can become a unit of count, and each repetition can gather slightly different private feeling without the narrator saying that the feeling has changed. Keep recollection inside the present act. A memory may surface when a smell or shape actually prompts it, but do not start a scene with a summary of the past.\n\nLet ordinary pleasure remain present even in a worn obligation: clean water can still cool the hands, a familiar voice can still make a task easier. A small irrevocable change can appear in the object itself—a crack has advanced from the rim to the lion's mouth, a painted word has vanished under another layer—but the narrator need not summarize what that change means. End where the practical situation ends: the rite observed, the inventory finished, the door closed, or the routine interrupted by an ordinary need. The gap between then and now may remain present without being announced, and the scene need not conclude by declaring its significance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nBuild the instruction around a frame: a teller recounts a practical obligation, often by breaking it into numbered procedures, cautionary examples, or episodic failures. Choose the Persian didactic-anecdotal mode, where a moral emerges from a small concrete incident and may be undercut by the teller's own unruly experience. The frame can be explicit—a teacher instructing a pupil, a veteran explaining a rule—but it need not be announced at the start.\n\nIn each episode, present the required act as a sequence of exact steps: how to mix the potion, how to fasten the seal, how to walk the boundary without letting the bronze shadow cross one's own. Then compress this into a short account of what usually goes wrong: the impatient novice waters the mixture, the careless watchman spills the salt. Keep the picaresque close to the practical. Satire lies in the mismatch between the official rule and actual use, not in a set of absurd accidents.\n\nRepeat a phrase or fragment at the turning point of each episode, as a Palahniuk-like recurrence. The teller may expose a defensive detail by explaining a rule too precisely—such as the exact amount of powder that once burned a cousin's thumb—without saying what the anecdote reveals. The didactic point can be deferred, contested by a listener, or left to hang where the teller falls silent. End on the frame's present moment as the scene actually requires: a listener's comment, a teacher's gesture, the teller putting away the object that occasioned the tale, or the frame simply pausing before another episode. Keep the dialect of the world coherent: no Arabic or Persian ornaments unless the supplied setting actually requires them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect one or two central physical actions for the magical obligation—turning the key, feeding the lamp, knotting the blood-rope—and repeat the exact chore description when the act recurs. Add one new concrete element each time, such as the key scraping now because the lock has worn, the lampoil darker after a wind. Do not explain why the change matters. Omission should leave the loss inferable from the object itself.\n\nUse short declarative sentences, but let a single longer sentence appear when it is needed to convey cause or connection: the blood-rope's knot must be pulled three times because the old vow binds the east field as well as the door. Avoid adjective chains; let a specific noun do the work. If the narrator thinks, keep the thought as an exact practical item—she could not remember whether the left-hand notch meant the binding held or had failed—rather than as a general psychological summary.\n\nAllow dialogue to interrupt the procedure, but keep each speaker distinct. The ritual's weight belongs to the act, not to elevated wording. Humor, if present, should arise from an exact observation: the vow's most precious ingredient is ordinary table salt, and everyone pretends not to notice. End when the procedure stops or the scene's immediate question is answered, without a final sentence that announces the larger significance. The mythic weight, when it appears, shows through the grip of the hand or the scrape of the key rather than through commentary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite each scene as a sustained present-tense or past-tense narrative that follows a focal character through an obligation whose magical force remains visible in practical consequences. When the character must perform a ritual, give the steps in exact order, but let them occur inside ordinary movement: she pours the measured water over the oath-stone while another character is speaking; he counts the six paces to the western post as part of a larger errand. The procedure's importance should be carried by what it actually changes in the scene, not by a solemn announcement.\n\nReturn to a small number of physical objects or bodily habits—a scarred thumb, a box of red salt, a coat that catches on the door—and repeat their exact names instead of introducing new props each time. Let these repetitions accumulate implication through changed circumstances. A character may recall an earlier time the coat caught on the same door, and the memory may reveal a shift in the relationship without the narrator saying what the shift means. Keep interiority tied to concrete causes: the character notices a fine crack in the relic, then thinks of a promise she has not yet kept, then notices that she has been holding the spoon too tightly. The chain must be perceptible but does not need to be summarized.\n\nLet dialogue and thought move at the speed of real conversation. A character may say one thing and think another, but the difference should emerge from a particular event, not from a standing rule that everyone conceals. Do not give the entire cast the same voice; a guard's practical command can sit next to a mage's measured question. End each reply when the current action or exchange can naturally pause, leaving the obligation still in force. Whatever feeling the scene has actually produced may remain present without being announced, and the pause itself is enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nWrite the relationship through the world's required acts rather than through abstract declarations of feeling. When two characters share an obligation—a binding, a household rite, a vow they must renew together—show the exact practical steps one does for the other or with the other. A poured cup, a tightened clasp, a remembered word spoken at the proper place can carry more weight than a direct statement. Let these acts recur across scenes so they become a private grammar the characters themselves recognize.\n\nUse repeated phrases as callbacks, but ground each in the characters' history. A particular nickname or warning can return when the situation changes its meaning. When one character reveals something about a routine—the reason the water is always measured, the reason she insists on a clean blade—the revelation should arise from an actual event, not from a narrator's summary of how much they trust each other. The exposure can be partial: one character notices the other's hands steadying on a difficult word, and does not say what that steadiness cost.\n\nGive affection enough space to be specific. Comfort can be physical and practical, such as one person warming the other's hands before a rite, or it can be a moment of silence that is named only by how it changes their breathing. Do not import a roster of relationship tropes. Use the premise at hand: a mentor and student, rivals bound by blood, companions who must lie to each other about the literal consequences of their oath—whatever the supplied story actually establishes. End when the scene's immediate exchange or action can naturally pause, leaving the obligation's practical next step still to be taken. The relationship's particular weight, when it has been established, need not be announced at the close.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen the account like a chronicle made for retelling: name the deed, the obligation, and the particular object or salt or wound that keeps it in memory. Use compact declarations—\"the vow that bound the river to Anthel's spear\"—and then move to the practical sequence that gives the declaration its body. Let the sentence order match the telling of an event, not a written procedural list. The past event may be \"the third crossing, the west ford, the iron weight dragged behind a horse,\" but the telling should remain connected to a present consequence.\n\nGive each important obligation one recurring formula, attached to a physical act: \"as the knife turned in salt\" or \"after the knot had broken.\" Repeat the formula at the points where the deed is recalled or renewed. The narrator may expose a private cost by dwelling on a practical detail that others would omit, such as the exact amount of blood that stained the treaty table or the cracked tooth acquired at the feast, without announcing what that detail means. Keep the exposure within the teller's knowledge; a third-person tale may leave a participant's motive unspoken.\n\nWhere the account would become too uniform, let a reported voice interrupt: a character answers an elevated comparison with a practical contradiction, a listener corrects a date, a participant insists that a name was different. This need not become mockery. Humor may arise from the gap between what is remembered and what actually served, but combat and survival keep their cost. End with the telling's next beat as the scene actually requires—the deed concluded, the next obligation named, the listener asking a question, or the teller pausing before another episode—rather than with a final phrase announcing that the tale will be told forever. Preserve the active narrator and any author-owned world facts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the inherited obligation as a Swedish parish-level institution, legible through tax rolls, maintenance ledgers, shared kitchens, and recurring inspections. Let a magical oath function like a land lease or a household duty: binding, ordinary in its daily cost, and measured by dates, payments, and small material failures. Do not inflate every exchange into saga speech; grandeur belongs to the scale of the debt, not to all the voices carrying it.\n\nGive the narration a confessing, procedural quality inside whichever narrator the active contract uses. That focal mind keeps a repeated rule, phrase, or bodily habit that returns when pressure rises—counting the same steps to the altar, reciting a maintenance clause, checking a ledger no one else consults. Let the recurrence expose a defensive logic without the speaker naming it; the pattern itself reveals the avoidance.\n\nAnchor changes in concrete domestic objects and administrative events. A new name in the grain register, a repainted door, a withheld signature, a smudged entry—each such event should alter what the oath demands and what the household can keep. Link those consequences to individual loyalties and inherited guilt, and let them arrive through errands, meals, cleaning, and disputed responsibilities rather than explanatory speeches.\n\nKeep the frame Swedish rather than generically Nordic: the village board, the manor account book, the parish boundary, and the winter timetable may impose order, while snow and isolation appear only where the actual setting supports them. If the active story already fixes another place, keep the supplied community and language; what travels is this institutional legibility, not a relocation. Surround the oath's magic with obligations to relatives, neighbours, and tenants, and do not let one dramatic rupture erase the continuing work of upkeep.\n\nUnder the active authorship contract, show self-exposure through private experience rather than performed monologue. A speaker can defend the same evasive choice twice with slightly altered words, and the recurrence deepens the pressure without another character overhearing it. The reply can stop wherever the day's work stands—an errand unfinished, a chore done with its magical consequence still in force—without manufactured closure or rupture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action lines to make magical obligations inspectable. Show a treaty, oath, or relic through visible steps: unsealing a vault, counting marked objects, reading a register aloud, performing the same maintenance gesture each day. Put the recurring procedure on screen instead of explaining it.\n\nLet dialogue expose defensive self-narration. Characters recite rules, contracts, or personal instructions when they want to avoid saying what they fear or want. A second speaker can repeat the same rule back with a small change, and the difference creates pressure without turning the exchange into a confession. Keep dialogue plain, repetitive where the character is, and leavened by ordinary work talk.\n\nUse a scene heading whenever location or time changes—the oath chamber, the grain store, the boundary road, the archive at night—and keep action lines to what can be seen or heard: footsteps, a key turned twice, a manifest read in a flat voice. Keep camera commands off the page; a cut is simply the next heading, and offscreen sound is written as sound. Magnificent setting details belong to the immediate scene, not to a voice-over tour.\n\nCut between places to show how one decision travels: a name entered in a ledger, a messenger crossing a bridge, a torch extinguished at the border. Let offscreen sound convey a consequence without staging it prematurely. Where the active contract requires focal interiority, present it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note; it is not dialogue and not knowledge available to the cast.\n\nAdvance the story through material changes rather than announcements—a revised oath, a lost key, an emptied storehouse, a failed inspection—and let the next scene follow from what is now visibly different. The response can end anywhere in the ongoing work: a door held open, a rule recited once more, a signal being prepared. No summarizing beat is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stage Play\n\nLet magical obligations appear as stageable routine. When a character keeps an oath, show them performing a small repeated action with a physical object: folding the same cloth, setting a boundary stone, wiping a ledger, checking a lock. Keep the object in use so the audience can follow its condition rather than hear about its history.\n\nMake public claims testable within the shared stage space. If someone says the seal was intact, the actor should have been able to touch it. If a character was not present, another speaker may challenge the timing from where the action was visible. Do not rely on hidden effects; the play's power comes from what the audience and cast can witness together.\n\nUse asides and monologues for a dramatic purpose that fits the role. A caretaker may address the audience while repeating a maintenance rule; a claimant may deliver a dry inventory of inherited failures—but the speech shows self-exposure through procedure and does not replace inward experience. When the active contract requires private thought, present it as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material, not as a whisper another character can hear.\n\nPace entrances and exits so no one vanishes for an impossible interval. A door opened here closes there; an object brought in remains available or is removed by a visible action. Let pauses mark where a rule fails: the third recitation stops, a key is tried twice, a bowl is set down and not picked up.\n\nThe scene can stop with its physical situation still present—an opened chest, a torn page, a broken seal, a character mid-action—without a forced public confession or a sudden reversal. The repeated procedure itself can leave the audience with the weight of the oath.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stream of Consciousness\n\nAnchor the associative movement in one authorized mind carrying an inherited magical obligation. Begin inside a concrete present moment—a stair, a ledger, a bowl being counted—and let attention drift through remembered clauses, names, dates, and bodily discomforts. Return repeatedly to the same fragment: a rule, a word from the oath, the number of steps, the feel of a key.\n\nUse recurrence as the engine of self-exposure. The mind thinks the same line three times with a slight variation, and the variation reveals a fear or a justification the character will not say plainly. Do not label the emotion; show the pressure through what the mind keeps touching and what it keeps avoiding.\n\nKeep enough orientation that the reader knows whether a passage is happening, remembered, or imagined. Separate the present physical object from the remembered one with a small sensory change—a different texture, a sound in the room, a new smell. Fantasy elements may appear in memory or in the present, and they are never reduced to pure metaphor unless the world already permits that.\n\nLet repeated magical procedure enter the stream as sensory and administrative fragments: the drip of wax, the page that sticks, the count that never comes out right, a relative's voice reading the same clause. Progress and important decisions can surface alongside intrusive thoughts about hunger, cold, or an unpaid debt. Not every association leads to a solution or an epiphany; some simply repeat.\n\nStop where the attention actually is. The reply can end mid-thought, mid-rule, or mid-action, and a final turn of attention—a painful memory, a repeated phrase, a physical sensation that reframes the drift—can arrive without a summary or a neatly closed reflection.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nBuild the story on senders and their messaging habits. Each speaker has a recognisable tic: repeating the same rule, asking for the same attachment, correcting the same word, delaying the same confirmation. Let the habits expose pressure without anyone saying what hurts.\n\nTreat magical obligations as administrative traffic on whatever channels the world supplies—citadel dispatches, temple relay codes, guild memos, inheritance notifications. An oath becomes a received instruction, a verification request, an attached seal image, a timestamp proving the messenger arrived, an edit changing a single clause. Use delays, read receipts, and delivered-but-unanswered gaps when they alter the recipient's interpretation, not as default subtext.\n\nSeparate sent messages from unsent drafts and from private thought. A draft may show the full defensive logic before the sender deletes it and sends something milder, but the draft is a composed artifact, not the sender's live mind; required private experience stays distinct from transmitted text. Context outside the exchange may appear in clearly separated prose when needed, and no interface event is invented merely to add subtext.\n\nKeep institutional voices distinct: a clerk citing clause numbers, a relative using repetitive questions, a caretaker sending a single word at long intervals. Do not make everyone equally clipped or witty; each person's actual habits shape their contributions.\n\nAdvance the story through message events that change available knowledge or obligations—a corrected seal image, a receipt that fails, an attachment from an unrecognised address, a sender whose voice seems off—rather than through summary. The exchange can pause anywhere: a message sent, one left unread, a draft half-written. Nothing has to resolve before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Visual Novel\n\nStructure each beat with a concise scene picture, attributed speech, and the focal character's inner or sensory reaction. When the scene changes—a hall, a border, an archive, a cellar—open with a specific physical detail the focal person notices: the same crack in the seal, the same dust on a ledger, the same echo after a locked door closes.\n\nUse repeated focal details to carry the hard rhythm. The character counts the same steps, looks at the same worn mark, mentally repeats a rule before answering. Let repetitions accumulate meaning rather than restart an identical observation; a second visit to a place can reuse the anchor detail with a new consequence.\n\nMake speech expose self-narration through dry repetition. A speaker cites the oath by clause, asks the same safety question, repeats a family instruction with one word changed. Others answer in their own practical voices, without polished banter or compulsory wit, and discrepancies in the repeated lines create suspicion or pressure.\n\nKeep inner response close to the immediate scene: who touched the key, which name was not recorded, which bowl was already clean. Focal thought may include memory or inference, but it stays inside the active viewpoint; another character's motive remains private unless separately authorized.\n\nBackground and expression cues orient the reader—the sky outside the oath chamber, a face gone still—but they are references, not evidence on their own. Do not generate branching menus, player choices, romance routes, or executable engine commands. The reply can end on a repeated line, a focal recognition, or a scene still underway; no cliffhanger or choice point is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahacbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Web Serial\n\nKeep a single obligation as the through-line across replies—a disputed inheritance, a boundary repair, a house under covenant—and show one local change per installment: a signature added, a count corrected, a clause invoked, a door sealed. Each installment makes the existing situation more specific; it does not restart it. Recover only the context that helps a returning reader re-enter.\n\nUse a recurring fragment as the recall hook. The focal character repeats the same oath line, the same maintenance count, the same practical rule at the opening or under pressure, and the repetition carries the one prior event that gives it weight—not the entire history.\n\nLet developments arrive through material and social consequences rather than sudden reversals. A repaired roof reveals a debt; a relative's letter changes the legal meaning of a silence; an inspection passes because a record was altered. The magical scale stays grounded in upkeep, duties, and inherited choices, and required inward experience remains available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes.\n\nPreserve the hard rhythm by repeating exact words and concrete actions rather than sentimental summary. Use short, declarative sentences for procedural moments, and let a confident but unreliable narrator explain events with a logic the concrete details subtly undercut. The installment need not resolve the tension.\n\nAn installment can close on completed work, a pending request, an unfinished errand, a ledger left open, or a repeated rule that now carries a new implication; no cliffhanger, confession, or completed test is owed each reply. Give the next installment a definite point of entry, and let actual work continue across turns without escalation for its own sake.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite inherited obligations as lived costs that press on the body and the room. When a character faces a sealed relic, a summons, or a rite, render the hesitation through physical detail: a hand that rests too long on the clasp, a breath held before the seal is broken, the slow way a name is spoken because saying it fully would reopen an old wound. Let the magic carry an exact price that lands on private life. A promise upheld may cost the chance to repair a bond; a recovered name may reveal a lie a parent told. Show the consequence through concrete images—the weight of a mended horn, the shine on a ring that no longer fits—rather than through a diagnosis of feeling.\n\nKeep the hurt present in what characters do and say, not in abstract suffering. A clipped apology can do more damage than an accusation. A speaker may say the king's order was lawful while privately reciting the wounds it opened. Render that private reckoning in plain syntax: 'She said she would return by dark. He did not say he had spent the day burning the letters.' Let warmth appear in small, painful ways: a mantle laid over a sleeping companion, a meal left untouched because the eater cannot apologize. The strain affects the actual conversation and the behavior that follows it, but a character may also say exactly what he feels, or say less than he knows, or misunderstand what the other person meant.\n\nUse landscape and labor as pressure. A character may work the gate-hooks too hard after a rejection or climb the watchtower stairs to avoid a farewell. Let the world's history speak through worn names and used objects, not exposition. If an old oath compels attendance, let it also sharpen the fear of seeing someone again. Close the passage at the point where the emotional state has become legible in the scene—a silence that deepens, a door left open, a task resumed with too much force—without requiring the hurt to resolve, confess, or reach a breaking point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as a coherent shared interval in the current scene. Give every piece of character speech unmistakable attribution: a line of dialogue followed by a short beat of action or the speaker's name in the narration. No paragraph carries two different speakers. Use short, paratactic sentences and concrete words—boots, chain, ash, stream, lantern, oath—so the fantasy setting remains tactile. A title, relic, or ancient obligation enters because the characters handle it or speak it. A knight may ask, 'They still make you wear the old iron?' and the answer carries the weight of an institution without a lore block.\n\nBring in history through labor and property. A steward counts firewood for a ritual; a gate is scarred from a past siege; a courier checks the wax on a summons before he will say whose seal it bears. Keep the continuing world active by letting NPCs pursue their own practical tasks while the scene moves forward. Maintain the active narrator and person from the request. If the collaborative contract gives the player control of a character, never speak for that character; give only actions, dialogue, and reactions that belong to the available cast.\n\nUse the McCarthy profile's severe surface to make high fantasy feel close and costly. A chosen hero need not declaim; she may just swab the wound and tighten the saddle. Let the magic be visible and limited: a salted ring that hisses when the dead approach, a vow that makes the door lintel burn. Close the reply at the point where the scene's immediate physical state is legible—a character picking up a parcel, a door being bolted, a shared silence, a meal begun, a road chosen, or a sentence still unfinished as someone turns to answer. The next writer can continue from that state without needing a question or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantasy as a classical-vernacular episodic passage. Each episode opens with a clear initiating event and moves toward a return: a summons arrives, a road is taken, a shrine is entered, and the section closes at the turn before the next event. Keep the movement concrete and paratactic: boots, staff, the chipped gate, the old man who does not look up. Draw the event onward through actions and objects, not through narrated emotion or modern analysis of motive. A title does not need explanation in a paragraph; it enters when a guard announces the name or a mother repeats it over a bowl of rice.\n\nUse the physical landscape and the weight of inherited obligations as narrative force. A mountain pass with a broken bridge matters because a courier cannot pass. A forbidden ceremony matters because the uncle who performed it is now blind. Let one event push the next: the mule dies, the younger brother must choose, the destroyed shrine means no protection for the caravan that follows. The prose may be spare but the sequence should carry a shared history. Use repetition as a structural aid: the same bell, the same road, the same phrase at each turn, so the reader knows the episode is completing. Keep language plain and vernacular, following the action rather than imitating a particular translation.\n\nClose the episode at the turn before the next event—a rider seen, a boat untied, a door opened after the third knock—or at the point where the current event's consequence has become legible and the next movement is already beginning. Each episode advances through its own event and consequence without solving the whole narrative. If the story uses a chapter heading or storyteller aside, keep it short and part of the current work's chosen presentation; the tale itself carries the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite high fantasy as hard use. Everything shown is worked: the sword has a cracked tang, the cart's axle is wrapped with wire, the healing charm costs three days of fever. Let each sentence push through one action or one piece of information. Describe magic by its physical trace and consequence: smoke, hot iron, the smell of copper, a room gone cold, a hand that cannot close. A debt is paid in land, blood, or the labor of carrying a cursed standard. Make fatigue concrete through established sensation or difficulty when present. A failed grip is one possible sign; direct tiredness can also be named without inventing repeated failure.\n\nCompress transitions. 'The door opened. He walked. The mountain waited.' Long explanation belongs only when the reasoning is what matters—a spell requires a count, a covenant requires a list of clauses. Even then, give the reasoning its own plain material form: numbered pebbles, knots, the fingers folded against a thumb. A short line can sit amid continuing work when that speaker is economical; retain longer speech when it belongs to the person and occasion. People talk while dressing a wound, sharpening a blade, taking the harness off a dead horse.\n\nUse the scale of the genre through concrete wear. A civil war is a burned village, a bucket with a hole, a boy who knows no other tune. An empire is a column of smoke and a road that runs straight for days. Let the rhythm change: a moment of stillness can be as hard as a blow—the hanged man's boots turning, the last ember, a wound packed with snow. Close the reply at the point where the immediate physical state has become legible—the gate barred, the debt acknowledged, the dead counted, the fire built—or during the continuing action that is still unfolding as the response stops. Do not explain the meaning after the image.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the story as a sequence of documents, each with a clear author, recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Ground every document in material detail: a torn tally, a letter folded twice, a ledger with a burn, a wax seal broken at the edge. Let the chosen document form shape its knowledge. A steward's inventory names goods but not motives. A soldier's letter reports the road and the weather; it may be silent about the girl left behind. A ritual record can list the exact number of lamps and still leave the central oath unstated. Describe the ink, the hand, the paper's weight, the way a line is smudged where a hand passed over it.\n\nUse gaps and material traces to tell the high fantasy story. A torn page may omit a name; a revised oath may show where a word was struck through. The reader learns that a war was lost because a grain tally stops before the final shipment. An ancient event may appear only in a marginal note: 'The reliquary would not open after the fourth day.' Conflicting reports are allowed if each reflects its author's situation. The sequence should follow a recoverable thread: a letter prompts a search for a missing reply, a receipt proves a courier reached the gate, an old register reveals an heir. Use concrete landscape clues inside the documents—a bridge named for a dead queen, a pass closed by snow, a field burned by treaty—without turning the documents into world-lore essays.\n\nChoose the form required by the scene: a message from a frontier guard, a testament, an inventory copied by a clerk. Preserve the active narrator's relationship to these documents. If the narrator is reading them, let the reading have a physical setting: a lamp, a table, a chair with a broken runner. Close the reply at the point where the documents have left a usable silence or a material fact—an unopened letter, a name in the ash, a seal that still holds—or during the unfinished composition of a document whose final line is still being written as the response stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nMake comfort part of the fantasy's daily work. Warm bread, a mended cloak, a fire lit before dawn, a horse fed before the rider eats. Let one character notice the particular preferences of another: they cut the cheese thin because he never liked the rind, they leave the door unbarred because she comes back after the third watch. Use simple, concrete sentences with the objects and actions in view. The warmth is in the exact move: a blanket folded over a sleeping child's feet, a kettle moved to the back of the stove so the water will not boil away. Even in a citadel or a market town, the comfort should be legible and close.\n\nKeep the high fantasy obligations present without letting them destroy the scene. A knight may be due at a distant oath but stays one more evening to repair a shutter. The magic can serve domestic care: a charm to keep soup warm, a written ward to stop moths in a grain sack, a family relic that makes a room smell like hills in spring. The world still contains its costs—a war at the valley mouth, a brother owed to the temple—but within the interval, a person may be comfortable, fed, and remembered. Allow disagreement and privacy. The old woman may refuse the porridge and the young man may not say why he is afraid. No confession is required for the comfort to count.\n\nUse the McCarthy profile's physical attention to keep the passage from becoming cute. The fire is real; the cold outside is real. A tender act may be a cracked cup set carefully on the table or a coat taken from the peg and laid over the back of a chair. Let affection be shown through work: a strap repaired, a path swept, a bowl kept aside. Close the reply at the point where a small, stable comfort has become legible—the lamp lit, the latch dropped, the cat curled on a folded tunic—or during the continuing care that is still in progress as the response stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse composed novel prose with deliberate transitions. A scene may begin in a hall where the timber beams still show the axwork, then move through the corridor where the law is read, then to the yard where a boy leads a borrowed horse. Let paragraphs follow the narrator's social attention. Frame the immediate action through the institution that surrounds it: a wedding is not only two people but two lands, a sealed debt, and the long table where the older sisters sit. Explain only as much as the current point of view would consider; let the context arrive through naming, physical remains, and the way people defer to a title.\n\nKeep the McCarthy profile's concrete nouns and plain syntax even in the longer paragraph. A knight may ride through a burned suburb; the paragraph can register the iron fence, the loose stone, the smell of wet ash, and then let the social meaning surface: the outer road was closed by the duke's corvée. Public and private life should cross visibly. A lord signs a levy while his daughter mends his left sleeve. A mother's funeral brings the tax collector and the old nurse to the same bench, and the narrator can mark who stands aside. Use enough distance for judgment where the narrator has such authority; if the narration is close, keep the judgment inside the character's own observation. Titles, oaths, and relics should be woven into conversation and staging: a chancellor says, 'The third horn has not sounded since your grandfather loosed it,' and the silence after has weight.\n\nAllow ceremony and correspondence to organize a passage when they carry the plot. Rites should be rendered through physical sequence—the cup passed, the fire lifted, the gloves removed—rather than a list. Close the reply at the point where the scene's social threshold has become legible—a door closed on a quarrel, a letter sealed, a treaty read in two languages, a daughter standing at the stair—or during the continuing speech, action, or unfinished setup that is still unfolding as the response stops. The style is formal because the telling is deliberate and socially aware, not because every character speaks in formal rhetoric.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the outer scene in the middle of an obligation already in motion. Name the room, the weather, and the objects that carry the present need: a sealed warrant laid beside a bowl, a fire that must be fed before the telling can be heard, a rider folding wet gloves because the tale will outlast the cold. Let the listeners' preparations show what the telling costs them. Do not announce the stakes; let a character refuse bread, check a door, or turn a ring on her finger while the speaker waits.\n\nGive the teller a practical reason to begin, and let that reason shape what the tale includes. If the tale is meant to secure passage, the teller names the road, the toll, and the exact hour the gate was left unbarred. If it is meant to warn a family, the teller names the house, the well, and the child who was not where she should have been. If it is meant to sway an heir, the teller names the debt, the blade, and the witness who saw the old lord's hand shake. The tale does not summarize a legend; it moves through particular objects and particular choices. A relic appears as a thing carried, weighed, passed across a table, or refused. An oath appears as a limit on who may eat, who may cross a border, or who may inherit without forfeiture. Let the magical or ancient element change what a person can do in that moment, not merely decorate the account.\n\nLet the embedded tale act on the outer scene through the teller's selection and the listeners' interruptions. The teller may leave out a name, repeat a road, or pause before the moment of a death. A listener may ask where the bridge was crossed, whether the seal was whole, or why the dead companion is credited with a deed the teller could have done. The tale's effect appears in the outer scene as a narrow contemporary sign: a watcher asks the question that matters, a woman touches an empty sheath, an official says the century was not so long. Do not explain what the arrangement is for. Let the listener's question or refusal carry the recognition.\n\nKeep the knowledge permissions legible through the prose itself. When the teller reports what she saw, use verbs of direct witness: she saw the gate, she counted the horses, she heard the bell. When she reports what a dead companion claimed, mark the attribution: he said the road was clear, he swore the seal was whole, he told her the child had already gone. When the tale establishes a fact the teller could not have known, let the narration show its source or leave the gap visible. The outer narration may know more than the teller, but it must not silently grant that knowledge to the listeners. A listener may recognize that an account is arranged without saying everything that arrangement is for.\n\nUse repetition only where material recurrence connects the frame and the tale. The same oath may be spoken again in the present, the same road name may appear in an old itinerary, the same ash may catch in a sleeve. The repeated phrase or object accrues meaning through its changed context, not through mere echo. Do not add djinn, magical bargains, or an Arabian setting; stay inside the world's own powers and obligations. End the reply when the telling reaches a particular agreement or refusal, while the listeners' return to current business begins.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite from the Romantic and uncanny route of the German-language tradition: the marvelous breaks into ordinary work and is met as fact, not explained as system. Open scenes in labor and material — a task underway, tools, weather, the state of the road — and let the strange enter through the senses: cold coming off a relic, birds gone still, a flame that consumes nothing. Render it in concrete nouns and forceful verbs, and give it material consequence — work stopped, animals lost, years owed. Withhold explanation. The uncanny route and this spare prose agree: the strange stands uninterpreted while the practical scene goes on around it. When a mark or usage recurs, let repetition raise its cost or its reach rather than decode it.\n\nCarry mythic scale through land and obligation rather than elevated speech. Ancient events persist as marks and usages: a boundary raised by a dead king, a name people will not say above a whisper, an order whose tithe is still gathered. Let a title, oath, or relic change what someone can do and what refusal costs — goods, land, kin, blood — and name that cost in spare practical exchanges: question, answer, price, silence. Dialogue carries bargains, convictions, and withheld motives; what a speaker avoids saying can sit in the pause without narration translating it. Characters may speak of the marvelous flatly, the way they speak of weather; the flatness deepens it. Link clauses with and, let sentences run and accumulate, then break them short — the paratactic run does the enlarging, and the short sentence lands it.\n\nWhere the active contract permits private thought, keep it concrete and particular — attention, sensation, the thing a mind returns to — not general self-explanation. Let uncertainty show in what the eyes keep finding, and let the uncanny register in the body before it is thought: the hand that will not reach, breath held past sense. The route's instruments are available as the story warrants — omen, double, a dream that leaves residue, a night piece whose morning comes wrong — used singly and sparsely, never catalogued. Landscape is the supplied world's own, charged and active: no relocation to Europe, no borrowed severity, no translated word order. Keep the practical stakes moving through the strangeness — the work still waits, the debt still owed — and stop where the active boundary falls: mid-task, mid-exchange, mid-thought, or on the unresolved strange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the world's inherited apparatus inside a lived and wearing present. Let ancient events persist as physical strata and standing obligation: a city raised on stones robbed from an older one, dues still gathered under a name whose origin nobody remembers, an oath or a relic carried not as lore to recite but as a burden someone hauls into the work at hand. Titles, offices, and institutions matter through what they permit and what they take; grandeur comes from that cost, never from ceremonial diction laid on the whole cast. Where speech turns formal, let station and occasion demand it, and let it lapse when the officials have gone.\n\nRender this inheritance in concrete nouns and forceful verbs joined paratactically. A description of the old order — a procession, a strongroom, a flooded crypt beneath the market — can run long, clause linked to clause until the weight accrues; a bargain, a refusal, a threat should come in short plain declaratives, speech moving in spare turns with little ornament, the pressure carried in word choice and in what gets withheld. Read history off the place itself, off what has been robbed out, rebuilt, braced, or left to rot, and let labor show who maintains the old authority and who is ground under it. Let the hour and the weather bear on an encounter: the same rite or reckoning weighs differently by lamplight in the rain than it does by day before the officials.\n\nPractical speech carries bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. In public, people wear the register their station demands; in trusted company — two people on a stair, a kitchen before dawn — the mask can fall, and it falls in what they ask for and what they admit to, not in a prepared confession. Where the active contract allows, carry in narration what is never said aloud — the fear of what the gift takes, the wanting that has no lawful place — kept out of speech for a character-specific reason such as shame, utility, or habit. Render that private experience concretely, in what the character watches, counts, and refuses to touch, so the inward prose is as spare as the speech. Let intimacy and obligation share the same people — the one bound by oath or blood can be the one desired, owed, or lied to — and hold that pressure without forcing a betrayal or a confession to arrive.\n\nWhen power is used — a working done, a relic spent, an oath invoked — show its price in substance and person: what it draws from the user, whom it endangers, what new duty it fixes on a household. Where the story turns toward dread, keep that dread personal and inherited. Build every old authority from this world's own oaths, institutions, and relics rather than importing hidden predator societies, innate hungers, or borrowed faction mechanics. Let an oath hold or break according to what the characters actually do, and give either outcome its full cost rather than steering toward ruin or rescue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nTell this world's epic matter — oaths, titles, relics, old wars, working magic — where it is actually paid for: at the hearth, the well, the boundary stone, the shared field. Take the Indian epic route in which a great obligation is lived as domestic fact and argued as a question of right action, and carry it with McCarthy's material attention. Concrete nouns and forceful verbs do the grandeur; no one needs ceremonial speech to make an ancient house feel old.\n\nGive oaths, relics, and enchantments the solidity of tools and debts. An oath has terms and witnesses; it decides who crosses a boundary, who inherits, who speaks last at a reckoning. A relic has weight and wear, and a provenance two speakers can dispute. Magic costs something countable — labor, seed, years, the health of a beast — and people speak of it as they speak of weather or debt, without lecturing. Let ancient events surface as residue and argument: a place-name claimed two ways, an elder correcting a younger teller's version mid-sentence, the words of an old oath remembered differently by two houses. Do not halt the scene to deliver history.\n\nLet the story's pressure come from a dilemma of inherited duty rather than a display of power: two claims that cannot both be honored — kin against a sworn institution, a living teacher against a dead parent's word, or whatever pair this world's loyalties set at odds. Weigh the matter in speech. A character lays it before someone whose judgment must be accepted or refused — mother, smith, keeper of records, whoever holds standing in this world — and the answer comes as a question, a fragment of that person's own story, or a saying worn smooth by use, not a verdict unless the world's own authority makes it one. Keep the focal character's private weighing present as actual thought under the active contract, in that character's own idiom: a herder weighs an oath in herder's terms, feed and counted animals, not abstractions. What is said aloud and what is weighed in silence may diverge when the character has cause — a speaker can state the terms of a bargain and hold back the reason for keeping them.\n\nChain clauses with and; let sequence carry causation through labor and travel, sentences accumulating while the work is long, dropping to short ones where something is spent. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, speech stays spare and practical, and it carries bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. Take household, kinship, and community structure from the supplied world rather than a default family, a religious idiom, or code-switching the setting does not have; where language, region, or period specifics are given, keep them exact. Preserve whichever authorship contract is active, including its interiority and display rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nTell this as a plain-recorded fantastic tale: a strange occurrence set down as fact and left standing. Set each marvel down once — an oath that holds, a relic that answers, an old debt the land still owes — without astonishment and without origin story; credibility comes from matter-of-factness. Describe a relic the way a worker describes a tool: its weight, its wear, what it has done to the hands that carry it. Let titles, oaths, old institutions, and named places alter capability and price inside the scene — who may cross, who must answer, what a sworn word forbids — and carry that power through consequence and labor rather than lecture, so the mythic scale arrives as one person's loyalty and its cost. Ancient events surface the way flood-years surface, cited because they still cost someone something now.\n\nWrite in concrete nouns and forceful verbs, paratactic clauses that move the way work moves, with room for both a two-line paragraph and a long accumulating one. Let omission do the work explanation would cheapen: the mechanism of the magic stays unrevealed where the tale does not need it, and what an oath costs shows in what a person no longer does, no longer names, no longer keeps. Mark time by season, light, and labor rather than by dates, and let the year's turning matter — a swearing made in one season coming due in another — using season where it changes what people can do or what a thing costs, never as scenery inventory. The spareness is a register, not a wasteland: it serves any terrain or court and requires neither bloodshed nor fatalism.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep speech spare and practical, with grandeur living in the situation and the stakes rather than in ceremonial diction for the whole cast. Bargains, refusals, and sworn words do their work in few words; withheld motive lives in selection and silence. Interiority stays real and plain: render private experience as attention — the mind moving over particular things, the sum counted twice, the name not said — so motive remains available and causal without turning into self-analysis, and keep private thought outside the other characters' knowledge.\n\nA marvel may be recorded and the telling move on as if it were ordinary. An image or phrase bound to the oath or relic may return where the story earns it, gathering meaning with each payment, not at every hardship. Stop where the scene's own boundary falls — mid-labor, mid-exchange, mid-thought, or on a plain image — owing the scene no moral, twist, or settled reckoning before it ends; let a reckoning come only if the story earns it. Preserve the active narrator, person, and authorship contract: whoever keeps the record, their knowledge limits it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the register of the marvelous treated as ordinary, remembered the way a chronicle remembers. Whatever the world's canon has already established as real — enchantment, relics, the old powers — narrate it in the same flat declarative voice as weather, debt, and work. Let a standing wonder enter the scene as a cost before it enters as a marvel: what it weighs, what it eats in a season, whose hands have worn it smooth, which family it answers to. Report it as a household reports a difficult well. Do not pause for astonishment at what the world already contains, and do not explain the magic into a system on the page; it stays marvelous through accumulated concrete fact. Where canon leaves a wonder unresolved, let the community's practice absorb it — they fence it, tithe to it, complain about it — without the narration settling the matter for them. In dialogue as in narration, people bargain over a relic's upkeep the way they would over a mule's price; awe appears in someone's mouth only if awe is what that person would truly carry. This governs the telling of established magic only; it adds no supernatural fact the world does not already hold.\n\nKeep history a present creditor. Beneath the current scene communal memory runs: boundary stones, the names the dead are still called, a war the grandmothers have not finished losing. When the past surfaces, bring it through a speaker with a stake in it, and attribute it the way testimony does — this the speaker saw with their own eyes, this the old ones said, this the ledger claims — so a single event keeps its strata instead of collapsing into one authoritative recital. Where the community carries two versions, let both stand; the narration need not adjudicate. An inherited oath or title behaves like an inheritance in fact: it names what people can and cannot do, binds loyalty as surely as debt, and the scene prices it in land, labor, and bodies rather than in recapped lore.\n\nBuild the prose paratactically. Link clauses and link them again, letting long accumulating passages carry distance, labor, and consequence — a march, a mourning, the upkeep of a relic — then break the run with a short declarative when something is decided, spent, or dead. Choose concrete nouns and forceful verbs; measure landscape by what it does to the people crossing it. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical: bargains struck, warnings given, the exact words an oath requires. A speaker's motive may sit underneath the exchange, felt in what they decline to say. Formal speech belongs to a particular office or person who would truly speak that way, not to the whole cast; grandeur lives in what the situation demands of people, never in elevated narration doing the demanding for them.\n\nWhen private thought appears, give it the same stripped voice and real weight: an obligation reckoned against a specific object, a name held and not spoken, the arithmetic of what one more concession would cost. Spare is not absent — render the reasoning and feeling the moment actually carries, plainly, and keep whatever interiority the active authorship contract permits inside the reply.\n\nTake names, customs, terrain, and community from the supplied world as given; do not manufacture regional color to signal the tradition. The register requires neither a dying land, nor cruelty, nor a doomed household, and it forecloses no ending: mercies, failures, and revolts stay contingent on what these particular people do next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Light Novel\n\nRender this world's magic, oaths, and history as physical fact the eye can follow. When a power is used, join its visible cause to its consequence in spare linked clauses — the light on the stone, the heat, the crack running the old seam, what the speaking takes out of the one who spoke. Give an unfamiliar name, rite, or mechanic its meaning through that immediate effect and the focal character's response to it rather than through a history lesson, and let a carried relic or standing oath show as labor and price: what it weighs on a march, what it forbids in a room, who stands and who goes quiet when it is named. Let the land carry the past as wear and work — a road worn hollow by pilgrims of a rite no one keeps, a scar in the hillside older than the boundary stones — rather than as narrated chronicle.\n\nEnter each scene clean and keep paragraphs one beat each, so action, dialogue, and private response stay separate and fast to read, and make every change of place legible in a line. Spend a longer accumulating sentence only where a moment earns it — a ruin, a rite, a stretch of road — then break back to short lines. Dialogue is spare and practical, plain-spoken even from titled figures; a bargain, a conviction, or a withheld motive can ride in a few exchanged words, with the grandeur kept in what is asked and paid rather than in elevated diction. Speech does not have to explain itself: a question can be answered with an act, or left standing, and a scene can close on an open errand or an unfinished thought rather than a manufactured turn.\n\nWhere the world lays a weight on the scene, give a focused reaction beat: the focal character notices, weighs, doubts, resents, or jokes according to who they are. Their private reading may differ from what they say aloud for reasons of their own, and noticing is part of the story even when it changes nothing outward. Write that interior experience directly, as the active narration permits, while other characters stay known from the outside: what they do, what they carry, where they look. Let institutions and ancient events reach the scene through concrete channels — a toll at the bridge, a summons, a boundary stone, a debt called due — so the large scale presses on the choice at hand without stopping the action to explain itself, and a loyalty or a refusal remains the character's own act rather than the world's verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite high fantasy through concrete nouns and forceful verbs, with syntax itself carrying the weight of magic, oath, and history. Let a title, relic, or ancient event become physically present before the narrator measures its cost. Build the paragraph by addition: a first clause names the act, a second shows what it touches, and a third changes the light or sound so the reader feels the power enter the landscape.\n\nUse paratactic movement to keep enchantment ordinary in its materials and enormous in its reach. A blade can wake red along the runes; the same runes can leave their shape on the wall or on the holder's hand. Do not stop at decoration. Link the oath to the thing that preserves it, to the body that must carry it, and to the object that will outlast it. Let repeated sounds and images return across the paragraph, not as punctuation but as consequence.\n\nShift attention from a large event to a small exact detail when the detail carries more than explanation. A character may look at the traced ash rather than at the summoned fire. The lyric extension should change what is understood: the fire is not spectacle but a debt, a door left open, or an old claim recognized. Keep the extension within what the viewpoint can perceive or associate; the comparison arises from the scene's materials and the teller's attention, not from an omniscient gloss. Plain statements can interrupt the rhythm: he knelt, he counted, he closed the door.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare enough to preserve the prose's rhythm without making every speaker lyrical. A practical sentence under a burdened scene becomes its own music. Grandeur belongs to the present event and the chosen teller, not to ornamental diction or a cast that all speak in the narrator's figurative voice. The passage may close with the action still in motion, a perception still settling, or the next practical movement already begun, without announcing an ending or requiring a new cost to unfold.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet memory arrive through landscape and labor. A repeated task can carry the weight of an ancient oath: the same field still requires the blade, the well rope has been spliced, the road is harder in the early dark. Write these details plainly, as work a person knows by hand. Reflection should attach to a thing the character can touch or count, not to a general meditation on time.\n\nUse small irreversible changes to show the cost of magic and inheritance. A charm that mended a fence has left the wire colder than the post. A binding kept the family on the land and kept the garden from growing past the wall. The narrator may state what was once available, then let the present object answer: harness hung where no horse has been. Let ordinary pleasure remain available. A warm room, a well-made blade, or a dog's attention may be genuine without turning into proof that all is lost.\n\nWhen reflection lengthens, extend it by paratactic accumulation: this was the day they burned the old tally sticks, and the smoke went up white, and the boy watched it until the wind changed. Then return to the present. Keep the remembered occasion tied to what the viewpoint can still see, touch, or do. Do not require every scene to close by announcing impermanence. The passage may end with a task resumed, a remembered occasion still unfinished, or a present detail that quietly carries the old obligation, if the scene supports it, without forcing the work to change its meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a definite framed oral telling: the story is delivered by a particular teller to listeners in a present room, around a fire, or along a road. Begin the account with an ordinary material detail that marks the occasion: the tea leaves, the new rope, the shutter, the ash. Then let the teller move into the high-fantasy matter by a linking phrase of return, not by a chapter break.\n\nWrite the told material in the teller's concrete register, not in general antique diction. Titles, relics, oaths, and old places should reach the listener through objects. The oath was sealed with the salt from the northern well, the well is dry now, the posts of the house are still straight. Use McCarthy's paratactic movement inside the speech: \"He bound the sword and he slept. In the morning the binding held and the lamp was dark.\" The magic is presented as work and consequence, not as a catalogue of powers.\n\nLet the frame interrupt the telling at a point where the cost becomes unclear or disputed. A listener may ask whether the well was properly sealed or say that another version places the door on the western wall. The teller may answer with a physical correction or withhold the next part. Keep the teller's knowledge distinct from the listeners' access: the frame can show what the teller chooses to say, but the told matter remains the teller's account, not an omniscient record. Do not resolve every interruption into a moral or a lesson. The high-fantasy scale remains, but it is always a story being carried by a person with a practical reason for telling it. Close with the next part withheld, a listener attending, or the teller's hands returning to a simple task, as the present occasion allows, leaving the old event present in the room without making the teller a prophet or the tale a universal key.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small number of exact details. Each clause should move the action or the cost forward: a relic is lifted, a cut is tested, a rune goes dark. Let the details be physical and ordinary in material even when the power is old. The well rope and the iron key can carry the binding as strongly as a storm.\n\nOmit explanation that the chain of clauses already implies. If the ward holds and the horses calm, the reader can infer the price was accepted without being told the character felt relief. If an oath is remembered, show the object that recalls it: the ring being too loose now, the mark on the table. Keep an essential thought when it supplies a motive or connects one act to the next, but do not pad it with interpretation. A precise extended sentence may serve the style better than several short ones: the blade lay across the salt and the salt was black where the edge had touched it.\n\nPreserve causality. The magic must make sense through what follows: a door that opens on the wrong side, a lamp that burns water, a field that stays cool in summer. Each consequence is specific and visible. Dialogue remains thin and practical; internal speech is concrete rather than confessional. The passage may stop when the present action reaches a natural pause. Do not require a closing reflection or a completed task. The remaining implication is enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite a continuous high-fantasy scene in sustained prose. Let the narration move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized private thought without labeling the shifts. A character may lift a seal and remember the last time it was broken, then answer a practical question about breakfast or the leaking trough. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and verbs to orient each transition.\n\nKeep the magic causally legible. An oath or relic should change what people can do in the immediate scene: the door swings without a hand, the rope holds longer than it should, the knife is warm only for the one who made the promise. Show the consequence in work, injury, weather, or social obligation. The high scale belongs to the present materials, not to detached exposition: a treaty may be mentioned by the broken post it once marked, the old king by the stair he never finished.\n\nUse paratactic movement to move the narrative forward rather than as a clipped rhythm. A longer sentence may carry both the physical act and its consequence. Let authorized inner experience be as concrete as the action. Do not convert private thought into a showcase of wisdom. A viewpoint character may be angry, uncertain, distracted, or practical; the narration can reveal what they see and what they are doing even when their thought remains spare. Dialogue is one instrument among others. It may carry bargains, refusals, and withheld motives without becoming uniformly witty or epigrammatic. The passage may end in the middle of an exchange, a resumed task, or an unfinished observation, with the scene's present materials still in view.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nAnchor the relationship in the concrete material of the world and the particular promise it makes. If a character has promised to hold the seal or to wait, show the seal or the road in repeated moments. A callback works when it returns through a specific object or phrase already rooted in the story, not when it is announced as significant.\n\nUse private language that remains practical and belongs to the two people. One may ask about the salt on the other's sleeve, or finish a sentence about a broken hinge. Affection should appear in what they do and leave room for discomfort, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery as the actual premise requires. Let one person's knowledge remain with them until the other can see it; do not force all motives to be confessed. A magical obligation can expose the relationship without replacing it.\n\nWrite high fantasy stakes as material consequences that press on the relationship: an oath that costs sleep, a relic that changes who may enter the house, a binding that keeps one person from crossing a threshold, or a journey delayed by the promise. The emotional payoff comes from the consequence making the established promise legible rather than from a perfect reconciliation. Use McCarthy's paratactic movement to carry the physical action around dialogue. Close a scene at a natural pause: after a gift, an unanswered request, or a private moment before the next obligation. Do not force a reconciling sentence at every ending. The relationship's promise remains the organizing logic, not a list of required trope beats.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the high-fantasy matter as a story already held by memory. Open with a deliberate declaration that names the deed, the place, or the obligation: \"Three winters they kept the western gate,\" \"The seal was cut in the year of the ash,\" \"This is the oath of the house of the river.\" Then go to the exact material facts. A sword, a burned door, a ford, the number of days, and the name by which the act is called.\n\nUse recurrence to let the deed grow. A formula may return when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the phrase for a broken vow can return when a door is found unbolted on another night. Let the repeated phrase be short and rooted in an object or act, not a decoration. Keep the relation of an act to what will be remembered as the central movement: the chronicle says what was done and then what that doing meant to the settlement, the road, the following generation. The meaning may multiply rather than agree: what held the kingdom also kept the young from the sea.\n\nGround the chronicle in McCarthy's landscape and labor. The magic is an old thing made of new materials: the charm needs the black wool, the rekindled fire, the stone from the north porch. Show the oath through work. A character may not feel noble; they may count the cold mornings or set the ladder. Dialogue can enter briefly, a concrete objection or a promise kept practical, but the telling remains the primary voice. Do not import a Nordic pantheon or ceremonial doom unless the source already provides one. End with the relation still open: the next winter not yet told, or the same gate still standing in the wind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this high-fantasy story as a family saga told in the spare concrete register of McCarthy. Let the saga's retrospective, communal memory shape the telling: named ancestors, inherited land, a disputed boundary, a debt carried across generations. What is remembered is not exposition but a present claim, and what is passed down is a material thing—a blade, a bench, a field, a name—whose history changes what a person may do.\n\nUse the saga's domestic realism for interior scenes. Put the household at the center: loom, hearth, bench, byre, meal, council. Let a dispute enter through the door as a neighbor, a summons, a broken tool, a missing animal. The mythic scale lives in the cost of an oath or a feud to that household: a roof lost, a harvest withheld, a son sent away, a marriage refused. Keep the setting specific to the actual climate and season supplied by the story; do not impose snow, isolation, or uniform bleakness.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs for all action. Build scenes by parataxis, letting actions accumulate without explanatory comment. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue remains terse and practical; bargains, oaths, and refusals are spoken in plain words. Let a ritual phrase recur only when it is part of the world's law or memory, not as a stylistic ornament.\n\nTreat magic and the mythic as material. An oath binds as tangibly as a blade; a relic's power shows in changed weather, failing crops, a door that will not hold, or a name that must be spoken. Institutions appear as gates, seals, ledgers, and punishments. Connect ancient events to present debt through inherited objects, names, and laws, not through explanatory flashback. When a character remembers an ancestral deed, show it through a present object, a scar, a name, or a changed practice.\n\nLet private thought and reasoning appear as visible or hidden interiority according to the active contract. Character knowledge remains distinct from public knowledge: a person may know the cost of an oath and yet speak differently, and that difference is private matter, not dialogue. Grandeur lies in the actual obligations carried, not in formal speech from every cast member.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Screenplay\n\nWrite this high-fantasy story in concise screenplay form. Use scene headings when location or time changes; keep action lines in short, readable blocks that show concrete physical movement, terrain, weather, labor, and objects. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let dialogue follow McCarthy's spare exchange: practical, blunt, often partial, with bargains and refusals spoken plainly. Avoid camera directions and unnecessary transitions.\n\nCarry the mythic scale through the visible and audible: a broken oath becomes a failed gate, a soaked field, a missing rider, a delayed signal fire. Magic and relics show themselves in material effects—frost on a blade, words that change the color of a flame, a sealed door that opens only after a name is spoken. Institutions and history appear in buildings, badges, tolls, and rituals, not in explanatory speeches.\n\nGive each scene's physical business enough weight to make obligation felt. Men and women feed horses, repair walls, count stores, take oaths, and count their dead. Do not turn every speaker into a moral orator; grandeur lies in what they are bound to, not in formal talk.\n\nPreserve required private thought and reasoning as clearly attributed nonspoken material in the active presentation. Place such material apart from dialogue, labeled as private interiority according to the active wrapper, and do not pretend another character can hear it. A character may think the cost of an oath while speaking only of grain; that difference remains private. Use cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the selected scene needs them. Let a scene end after a completed action, an interruption, or a plain acknowledgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Stage Play\n\nWrite this high-fantasy story as a playable stage script. Use clear speaker names, economical stage directions, entrances, exits, blocking, and props. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the scene. No film-camera instructions; keep stage space honest and shared.\n\nUse McCarthy's spare dialogue: practical exchanges that can include bargains, oaths, refusals, and withheld motives. Let physical labor and objects convey the world's weight: a whetstone, a tally stick, a sealed box, a banner, a toll gate. Magic and oaths become stageable through specific effects or objects—a blade that will not draw until named, a hearth that burns cold, a door that holds against an unblessed hand. Institutions appear in the shape of a bench, a warrant, a robe, a spoken formula.\n\nMake each entrance and prop placement matter. A character who enters with a token changes what another may claim. A broken seal left on the table is visible to all; a private resolution is not. Preserve required interiority as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, separate from spoken lines, not performed as an aside or monologue unless separately authorized. Another character does not know a private judgment merely because the audience has read it.\n\nLet a scene end after a completed action, a refusal, or a turning-away. Do not force every scene into a full revelation or gathering. The playable form means each object and movement remains observable and testable; the mythic scale lives in the cost of a spoken oath, not in offstage armies described at length.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaday::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite this high-fantasy story through the associative movement of one particular authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, and recurring obligations braid together rather than follow a linear report. Use McCarthy's concrete register as the anchor: the grain of a table, the sound of a gate, the ache in a shoulder, the smell of wet wool. Paratactic clauses accumulate without explanatory commentary.\n\nReturn to the world's weight not as summary but as felt particulars: a half-remembered oath surfaces as a coldness in the hand when touching a blade; an ancestor's defeat returns as a name that stings; a pledge repeats as a phrase under breath. Let the mind move among these without losing the present scene. Use enough orientation—a known room, a waiting horse, a repeated task—to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or feared.\n\nKeep all interiority within the authorized mind; do not grant access to others. Another person's motive may be inferred from what they do or say, but not directly felt. Speech, when it appears, remains spare and practical, arriving out of the mind's flow rather than as a polished set piece. If the active contract permits visible or hidden interiority, write actual fictional private experience here; do not substitute monologue or a diary. Let the piece end when the mind has followed its association to a resting point, an interruption, or a decision, without forcing a completed external action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite this high-fantasy story as a transcript carried by sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits—the roughness of their words, the pace of replies, what they omit or edit—show their practical relation to oaths, bargains, and institutions. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene or relationship.\n\nKeep McCarthy's spare voice within the messages: messages are short, concrete, and practical, but they can carry full bargains, threats, obligations, and refusals. A gift of grain, a sealed pass, a toll owed, a name that opens a gate: these appear in what people send or fail to send. Magic and mythic scale enter through the material facts referenced in the exchange—the weather a message reports, the condition of a road, the arrival of a relic, the cost of a broken pledge.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. Do not invent interface events merely to add subtext. When context outside the exchange is required, place it clearly separated from the transcript, in a narrative block or note, without pretending it was sent. If required interiority must appear, keep it as a separate private entry or unsent draft, clearly owned by the character, not broadcast as dialogue.\n\nLet the exchange reflect real attention and waiting. A reply may come after a long interval; a sender may start typing and stop. Do not require a resolution or a cliffhanger. The story can hold tension and consequence in what is written, delayed, and left unread.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
