            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-high_fantasy-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · High Fantasy · 02/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == high_fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip High Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-high_fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPahadba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Visual Novel\n\nWrite this high-fantasy story in visual-novel form: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and verbs for the scene's physical world: a cold byre, wet stone, a blade laid on a cloth, ash on a sill. Let parataxis move the narrative passage without explanatory commentary.\n\nCarry mythic scale through visible and stated particulars rather than speeches. A broken oath appears as a door that will not stay shut, a field of black grain, a banner turned wrong. A relic's power shows in the scene—frost on a lock, a flame that bends toward a name. Institutions and history appear as architecture, clothing, tolls, and rituals. Focal reactions may follow a character's practical reasoning or felt cost, kept separate from what other characters know.\n\nUse background or expression cues only as purposeful scene anchors, not as automatic truth indicators. A character's stillness may mean obligation, weariness, or concealed decision; let action and dialogue complicate it. Keep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Preserve the active authorship contract and required interiority in the selected visible or hidden form; do not turn private thought into spoken lines. A scene may end on a completed action, a pause, or an unfinished question.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahadbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Web Serial\n\nWrite this high-fantasy story as an installment-oriented serial in concrete McCarthyite prose. Keep strong nouns and verbs, practical speech, landscape and labor, and paratactic movement. Let each reply show what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps a reader re-enter: a name, a debt, a half-finished task, a weather condition.\n\nUse earned developments rather than forced reversals. A day's work may complete a wall, load a wagon, hear an oath, or lose a horse; knowledge, relationships, resources, and obligations can all progress. Magic and institutions appear in material results—a repaired gate, a changed toll, a crop that fails after a broken promise. Do not invent a leveling system, ranking, or progression-fantasy rule merely because the style is selected.\n\nLet occasional hooks arise from the world's actual state: a rider arrives, a sealed letter is opened, a debt is called. But do not end every reply on a cliffhanger or a complete miniature chapter. A reply may stop after completed work, a refusal, a plain acknowledgment, or an unfinished task. Preserve required interiority according to the active contract; spare thought can weigh the cost of a bond without needing to change a visible action immediately. The serial's duration gives selected facts room to acquire meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite high fantasy so that its systemic absurdities function as emotional machinery rather than interruption or decoration. When an oath, relic, prophecy, magical institution, or inherited feud makes a large demand, render the small practical consequence that makes the demand personally unbearable inside the scene: the required pilgrimage means someone must decide how to feed the horse that has gone lame; the sacred text has a missing page that cannot be replaced without admitting an older failure; the inherited title comes with a ledger of debts the heir is too ashamed to read aloud.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between world-scale claims and petty logistics to generate internal pressure. Let an apparently reasonable magical procedure expose its absurd consequences in a way that lands on a particular wound: a protective enchantment that requires the caster to speak, truthfully, the name of someone they love; an ancient law that grants the crown only when the heir publicly forgives a betrayed vassal; a summoning that works only if the summoner can hold in mind the face of someone they failed. Each large claim is punctured by a concrete, inconvenient detail that matters differently to different characters. One person treats a world-ending ritual as a scheduling problem; another is quietly devastated that the necessary ingredient is a family heirloom she promised never to sell; a third is furious that no one has apologized for the last time the ritual was attempted. Do not push the whole cast into the same witty detachment.\n\nMake the turbulence the consequence of these collisions, not a separate mood layer. When a character admits something difficult, the admission alters the conversation and later behavior; it may leave the other person more guarded, more tired, or unable to respond at all. Where the active contract gives access to a character's mind, show the unsaid version alongside the spoken one for a character-specific reason—shame, habit, protection—and let the gap itself cost something. When a character withholds, the withholding is expensive. When a character apologizes, the apology opens a new practical negotiation rather than closing the dispute. Comfort can be partial, practical, or awkward: a companion mends the broken strap after the argument instead of making a speech; a rival leaves a warm cup beside the ledger and walks away. Warmth and pain can occupy the same interval without resolving into tidiness.\n\nKeep the mythic scale vivid—the mountains genuinely move, the old king's ghost is present, the magical treaty has material force—but deliver that scale through its immediate consequences for the relationship. Preserve each character's specific distance and phrasing: a temple guard expresses grief through excessive care with a weapon; a court clerk buries fear in corrections to a document; a stubborn heir repeats a childhood nickname instead of saying sorry. The ending need not be tragic and need not stage a final confrontation; it is enough that the chosen beat honestly renders what the absurd high-fantasy situation has done to the people inside it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each turn as a self-contained but open interval in the high-fantasy world, in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution: each speaker's action, speech, and visible reaction stays in its own paragraph, so the next participant can always tell who has spoken and what has changed.\n\nDeliver the absurdity through interactive procedures and props that belong to the fantasy world, not through a narrator standing outside the scene. A messenger bird arrives with a summons that has been edited by three competing chancelleries; a magical binding must be renewed with wax that can only be purchased from an unhelpful guild; the war council has to decide whether the enormous ceremonial banner, which does not fit through the tent door, counts as present. When one character invokes an ancient prophecy, another may ask where the original parchment was stored and learn that it was used to wrap fish; when a ruler issues a decree, an assistant may point out that the royal seal has been missing since Tuesday and was last seen in the garden where someone was practicing knife-throwing. Keep these details concrete and actable, and let them prompt character-specific responses rather than a single group punchline. Different characters find the same absurdity infuriating, reassuring, irrelevant, or quietly fascinating; that difference drives the next line.\n\nKeep the world and its people active between speeches. NPCs continue their practical business—the steward still has to count the candles, the gate captain still closes the gates at dusk—and they can cooperate, resist, or misunderstand according to their own interests, not merely react to whoever spoke last.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point by advancing the current practical scene or introducing a new material complication: the arrival of a third person carrying an unreadable map, smoke rising from the direction of the archive, a guard politely reminding everyone that the dawn ritual cannot begin until the previous day's paperwork is signed. The response may stop after a completed exchange, an unfinished task, or a newly visible problem; it does not need to resolve the high-fantasy arc, deliver a moral, or force every character to react at once, and it must not demand a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice. Narrative person, action markers, and player control come from their separate selected settings; keep any addressed character's knowledge separate from what the response shows.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route of the Chinese literary tradition as the presentation framework: tell the story in chapter-like installments that each open by settling where the previous matter left off and close by naming the matter the next installment will take up. Chapter headings may take paired, parallel phrases summarizing the episode's two central concerns; storyteller formulas—'as the old record has it,' 'let us return to the matter of the western archive'—mark the seams between summary and scene. Within an episode, keep the prose concrete and socially specific rather than uniformly lyrical.\n\nOrganize each episode around an entry in the high-fantasy world's public or private records—an official decree, a household ledger, a temple registry, a border report, a matchmaker's recommendation, an ancestral tablet—and let that document's claims collide with the lived scene. Give the episode a concrete social institution or customary obligation that generates the Adams contrast: a marriage alliance negotiated from a genealogical chart with an impossible number of contradictory branches; a border ritual that must be observed by two kingdoms whose calendars disagree about which day is sacred; a celestial audit requiring every magical oath to be written on paper that dissolves in rain. Let the comedy come from close social observation: clerks, stewards, elders, priests, brokers, and petty officials interpret a grand magical event through ordinary anxieties about expenses, precedence, face, and inherited grudges. A dragon arrives at the wrong mountain because the invitation was copied by an overworked scribe; the sacred sword is stored at the bottom of a grain sack because the armory was condemned. These incidents expose the cost of titles and oaths: the family that owns the restored sword also owes its storage fees; the bride chosen by the prophecy has to be told before the banquet; the defeated lord's heir must decide whether to honor a debt recorded only in a dead language.\n\nThe episodic structure keeps the mythic scale legible without turning the cast into witty observers. Recurring official summaries and chapter headings may call attention to the absurdity, but individual characters respond according to their position: a dismissed servant finds the celestial audit a welcome chance to demand back wages; a young heir is terrified the household records will reveal her mother falsified a title; an old general is relieved the sacred oath has at last been lost. Each episode can end with a practical result or a newly discovered record that hands the reader to the next installment. Do not transplant a specific Chinese dynasty, language community, or cast identity; use the episodic and socially satirical formal choices to shape whatever high-fantasy world the active story supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaead::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard-edged, concrete sentences with compressed transitions and exact material detail. Let the absurd high-fantasy procedure generate physical consequences rather than running commentary: a ceremony requires the participants to stand in a river at midnight reciting the names of seventeen dead kings; the current is freezing and one name is disputed. A magical contract demands that the signer walk from the western gate to the eastern gate carrying a stone that grows heavier each step; the healers cannot agree whether the pain is part of the enchantment or a cracked heel. A prophecy requires the chosen heir to lead the army under a standard cut from a flag that has already become bandages and is still wet.\n\nUse Adams-scale shifts as a structural principle: make a large supernatural obligation appear as a small, intractable administrative or physical problem—the sacred oil stored in a barrel that has begun to leak, the oath recorded on a tapestry that swallowed a mouse, the ancient sword whose scabbard jams in the doorway during the coronation—and render each problem with gritty physical stakes. Boots fill with water, fingers blister, a man loses his footing, a clerk cuts himself on the broken seal, the crowd has to be moved because the ceremonial bonfire stands too close to the granary. The practical detail is not comic relief; it is the material cost of the absurdity and the vector of real danger.\n\nCompress the prose and vary its rhythm. Short declarative sentences can fix an injury, a refusal, or a failed attempt; a longer passage may follow one character's reasoning while they salvage the ritual against a collapsing schedule, and a thought runs exactly as long as that reasoning needs. Do not make the prose uniformly clipped, and do not hand everyone abrasive speech: a veteran barks terse commands, a frightened novice babbles about the stars or the price of salt, and each rough line belongs to its speaker's position. The high-fantasy world stays present through concrete artifacts and institutions—a temple's stone floor, a border fort's repaired wall, a mage's copper instruments—and the scene may stop at physical exhaustion, practical failure, or a small success: a lever freed, a fire drowned, a man pulled from the river, the larger magical obligation still unresolved. Preserve the active narrator and perspective; the gritty diction belongs to the prose and the selected characters, not to an external hard-boiled voice that takes over the telling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the high-fantasy story through authored documents, each with a clear writer, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, using whichever form the occasion actually demands—an inventory, a dispatch, a ledger, a receipt—rather than rotating through formats for variety. Let the chosen form shape what can be said: a temple inventory cannot record a god's anger directly, so it notes that the sacred fire has consumed twice the usual cedar and that the third acolyte has developed a cough; a border commander's report describes the ritual that must be performed before the next full moon but omits that the required drum was smashed during a card game; a family letter explains a marriage alliance while carefully leaving out which ancestor's oath the bride's family has already broken.\n\nUse Adams-style procedural absurdity as a documentary problem. A magical institution appears as forms, marginal notes, previous versions, and attached memoranda: the official instruction for summoning a protective spirit runs eleven pages and ends with the note that the final step is to pronounce a name no longer legible because the original scribe spilled ink; the treaty between two kingdoms exists in two versions whose binding clauses contradict each other, and the relevant paragraph is stored in a different archive from the one the diplomats may open; the prophecy survives only as a chain of later copies, each with a certified correction—the seventh verse altered by three different priests, the authorized text now disputed between the temple and the mint.\n\nReveal events and cause through the documents' gaps, revisions, and conflicts rather than an omniscient explanation. A later memo contradicts an earlier report, and the reader notices that a date, a witness count, or the spelling of a place name has changed. A receipt shows that someone paid for a journey they denied making. A marginal comment exposes a motive the main text never announces. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not a live mind: what it revises and what it deletes show what its author could not send, and nothing more. Keep each document's knowledge limited to its writer—a court clerk does not know what the king decided after reading the report, and a general cannot record a soldier's private fear unless he happens to describe it in a letter. Move the sequence forward by the arrival of new records—a revised order, a returned letter, an invoice, a public notice—rather than by summarizing narration. The mythic scale survives in concrete institutional weight: a god's decree arrives as a sealed tablet with a damaged edge, a sacred oath is the missing clause in a contract, an ancient battle is a marginal note about ruined fields. No single document is omniscient; their combined record is the story's mechanism.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite a high-fantasy scene in which the world's absurd machinery remains present but becomes the occasion for small, concrete, often humorous care rather than crisis. A magical treaty requires three days of negotiation, and the host spends the first evening explaining which room in the tower has the warmest hearth and leaving an extra blanket by the guest's door. The sacred banquet requires a dish made from an ingredient the kitchen has never seen, and the cook solves it by asking the young apprentice what their grandmother used as a substitute; the result is imperfect and eaten with unusual gratitude. The war council is delayed because the summoning bird has attached itself to one of the guards and follows him into the pantry, and everyone postpones the serious argument long enough to feed it.\n\nLet the Adams contrast between large claims and small practical details generate the warmth: the prophecy demands that the heir arrive at dawn, and a servant quietly leaves dry boots by the bed; the old magical boundary must be renewed, and the neighboring farmers bring the renewal fee as honey, smoked fish, and a promise to look after the orchard through the next storm. Earn the comfort with particular preferences and habits: a knight is calmed by the smell of lamp oil; a court mage relaxes when someone remembers she dislikes cinnamon; a hostage prince sleeps better once a guard places a chair by the door instead of standing over him. Different characters offer comfort differently—one arranges a repair, one tells an intentionally terrible joke, one simply stays in the room and says nothing while the fire settles—and any of them may refuse a kindness or accept it without confessing anything.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable and does not cancel the world's stakes: the magical threat, the political tension, and the inherited duty remain real, but they move to the edge of the interval while the current beat attends to warmth. An apology or emotional repair is not required for the comfort to convince. The scene may end on a practical favor completed, a shared meal, a moment of quiet, or a joke that leaves one person smiling and another pretending not to be. The high-fantasy setting is not reduced to a cottage or a holiday; its scale, institutions, and obligations still surround the warm moment, and the narration spends its attention on care.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in deliberate, continuous literary prose with marked paragraph organization and enough narrative distance to embed each scene in its social and historical context. Let the high-fantasy world's institutions—councils, oath registries, temple archives, marriage negotiations, border courts, summoning guilds—provide the formal social machinery the narration observes from a slight remove. Open a paragraph with the public meaning of an action or object, then move into the scene where that meaning collides with a practical discrepancy.\n\nUse the contrast between official description and local necessity as a structural device. A royal proclamation declares a festival to commemorate the treaty, and the narrative follows the provisioner who must find twelve white oxen on three days' notice while a storm has washed out the eastern bridge. The sacred oath is described in the temple's old tongue, its clauses touching the movements of stars and the fate of lands, and the narration then observes that the current copy is held together by two rusty pins and that the last vow was recited with a line missing because the presiding priest has grown short-sighted. The absurd consequence is not announced as a joke; it becomes visible through sustained social observation of who must pay, who must apologize, who must wait, and who must pretend not to notice.\n\nKeep the formal register a matter of narrative architecture, not uniformly archaic speech: a court chancellor speaks in full periods while a farmer's son answers in plain words, and the narration holds both inside the same social scene. Let digressions arise from the current institutional problem and return to it—a border dispute may trace the disputed map's lineage through three prior clergies, each of which corrected a different error, before returning to the courier standing in the doorway with wet boots. Use history and social context to explain why an obligation has become practically insoluble now: the crown cannot be found not merely because it was mislaid, but because the last two coronations were held in different cities and no one updated the inventory. Close the scene at a natural point of social or physical consequence—a decision deferred, a letter signed, a procession delayed by rain, a grandmother's version of the oath finally entered into the record—rather than at a forced summit. The mythic scale stays active, and the formal tone lets grandeur and absurdity stand in the same sentence without collapsing into parody.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen in the outer scene with a teller speaking to one particular listener for a practical purpose: to buy time, win help, justify a failure, or make someone understand what an old oath now costs. Name the occasion and the listener's stake before the inner story begins, then keep the outer scene present through interruptions, questions, and the teller's adjustments rather than a frame that vanishes once the tale starts.\n\nBuild the inner story around a single inherited obligation — a title, relic, oath, institution, or ancient promise that still binds someone now — and let the teller explain it as a remembered sequence of events, never a lecture. Work the contrast between legendary scale and small logistics: a sacred vow that depends on a missing cart, a forgotten key, or a clerk whose office no longer exists; an ancient title whose ceremony now requires a fee nobody can pay. The absurdity comes from the world's own rules, not from a narrator mocking them, and the teller's attitude toward it may be reverence, complaint, or both at once.\n\nMake selection itself the teller's visible craft. Omissions, reorderings, and emphases should have causes — loyalty, shame, fear, or the listener's impatience — even when the narration does not open every motive. When the listener interrupts, let the interruption change the tale's direction or force out a detail the listener can actually use. Return a phrase, object, or gesture at more than one narrative level so its meaning shifts as the listener learns more. Where your viewpoint reaches a private mind, let unspoken experience run alongside the told version: what the teller actually remembers, or what the listener suspects and does not yet say aloud.\n\nCome back to the outer scene once the tale has changed the listener's options. The listener may act on it, reject it, or ask a question the teller cannot answer; the inner story need not solve the outer problem. It should leave the listener clearer about what the old obligation requires and about why this version is being told now. Signal each level shift through the teller's voice, the listener's response, or a physical detail of the outer room. Borrow the frame-tale craft of audience awareness, recurrence, and delayed revelation without adding a royal court, magical bargain, desert, or a particular religion unless the supplied world already contains them, and let a pause mark the teller's fatigue or the listener's need to act rather than a required cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the modernist-satirical route. Treat the high fantasy world's inherited institutions, oaths, and titles as working systems whose impeccable internal logic produces disproportionate consequences. The narration keeps a composed, socially precise distance: it reports procedures, offices, and official reasoning without borrowing the characters' emotional register or collapsing into a single joke voice.\n\nOpen by naming the institution or obligation that governs the immediate problem, then show its authority through concrete operations — a required form, a hereditary duty, a relic that must be transported under seal, an oath that now conflicts with a newer law. Let the cast respond differently: one treats the system as sacred, another as an obstacle, another as a livelihood. None of them needs to share the narrator's perspective.\n\nExpose the absurdity through explanation and procedure. When a character explains why something must happen, let the explanation unfold as a chain of reasonable steps that arrives somewhere disproportionate: a treaty clause about a dragon's hoard that now requires a notarized inventory, an oath sworn to a mountain enforced by a lowland court with no jurisdiction over weather. Make digressions pay their way back — an apparent detour into record-keeping should later decide who may enter a room or touch a relic.\n\nKeep the mythic weight real while it is distributed through offices, messengers, fees, and delays. A character's loyalty to an old promise may stay genuine even when the institution around it has become absurd; the satire shows how the weight is carried, it does not cancel it. Let syntax follow the route: carefully subordinated sentences for official explanation, short concrete ones for physical consequence. Avoid invented German word order or bureaucratic vocabulary imported from any real country. The narrator may observe the gap between official language and material reality without announcing a moral lesson. Stop when the procedural absurdity has changed a character's practical options, not when every implication has been catalogued.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake the collision between old authority and present life physical. Locate inherited power in spaces that have outlived their purpose — a temple sharing a wall with a dye works, a noble charter hanging in a damp stairwell, a relic stored beside someone's laundry — and give each space lived, decaying detail: peeling plaster, a leaking pipe, a lamp long burned out, a lock whose key no longer fits. Let this atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced rather than laying a uniform gloom over everything.\n\nLet inherited obligations shape private encounters. A character may owe loyalty to a title, oath, or institution that no longer commands public respect yet still has teeth: it can demand a service, open a door, or force a choice. Show the cost through relationships. A trusted companion may be the one person with whom a character can drop a social mask, and that exposure may reveal desire, shame, or exhaustion; do not make every intimacy a manipulation or every trusted person a future betrayer.\n\nUse Adams's scale-shift to measure the gap between the old authority's self-image and its current operation. A bloodline traced to a legendary founder now depends on a rented room and a part-time caretaker; a magical oath requires a ritual object someone has pawned; a hereditary duty means queueing behind ordinary citizens. The absurdity is the contrast between claim and material condition, and different characters read it differently — tragic to one, funny to another, merely inconvenient to a third.\n\nKeep the mythic scale active throughout. Decay does not cancel the magic or the obligation; it changes how they are experienced. A relic may still work, but only when its keeper finds the right key; an oath may still bind, but its enforcement runs through a bored official. Let those practical frictions produce real consequences rather than background jokes, and let private thought carry what the masks conceal when your viewpoint permits it. Import nothing of vampire physiology, humanity metrics, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules: this is a narrative treatment, not a setting transplant. Stop when the encounter has changed what a character knows, feels, or can do, not when the atmosphere has been fully catalogued.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Treat the high fantasy world's inherited obligations as stories that circulate within a specific community — a household, a workshop, a garrison, a village council — at a particular time and for a particular reason. A grandparent may retell an old oath to settle a dispute, a parent may use a legendary journey to explain a debt, a child may ask about a relic and receive the version the community can bear to say. The telling is never a neutral performance; it does social work.\n\nKeep the frame alive. The outer scene holds teller and listeners in ordinary surroundings — a kitchen, a workshop, a courtyard, a road — while the inner story carries the mythic scale of ancient events, inherited titles, and magical obligations. Let the outer scene interrupt and comment: a listener leaving for work, a pot boiling over, a younger child asking the wrong question. These interruptions are not just comedy; they show how the old story lives inside present obligations.\n\nLet the Adams absurdity open in the gap between the story's grandeur and its present material consequences. The community may still hold a title that requires an annual fee, a relic that must be kept dry through the rains, an oath that obliges someone to attend a ceremony three towns away. The teller may explain the obligation's origin with reverence while complaining about the cost of transport. Different listeners respond differently — one finds the story sacred, another a burden, another a livelihood — and none of them needs to become the same comic narrator.\n\nMake the retelling consequential. The teller selects details because of loyalty, shame, pride, or the listener's need; a repeated phrase, name, or object accrues meaning across tellings, and a listener may hear the story differently after learning a practical fact about the present. Where your viewpoint reaches a private mind, let unspoken reaction run beneath the told version. The inner story need not conclude; it should change how a listener understands a current choice or obligation.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period as the supplied world gives them — do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. The method shapes the telling and its social function, not the cast's identity. Stop when the telling has changed someone's practical options or understanding, not when every episode has been recounted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the early-modern comic/theatrical telling route. Present inherited obligations as performed social encounters. A character enters already playing a role — dutiful heir, outraged creditor, humble petitioner, officious guardian — and the narration makes the performance legible through gesture, posture, timing, and the arrangement of the space: a doorway, a screen, a raised seat, a borrowed robe can carry as much meaning as the spoken lines.\n\nDraw the comic energy from the gap between role and situation. A character delivers a formal declaration about an ancient oath while standing in the wrong room or holding the wrong relic; another answers with exaggerated deference while privately calculating the cost of a meal. The absurdity is the mismatch between ceremonial claim and material staging. Do not make everyone equally witty or theatrical: some play their roles badly, some refuse to play, and some are entirely sincere while the situation around them goes absurd.\n\nKeep the mythic scale active. The title, relic, oath, or institution still changes what people can do and what a decision costs, and the theatrical treatment does not reduce the obligation to a joke. Exaggerated performance may be a way of surviving an unbearable duty, preserving dignity, or hiding fear; show the strain beneath it — a shaking hand, a missed cue, a pause held too long — and let private thought carry what the performance conceals when your viewpoint allows.\n\nBuild digressions that return through the current problem. A character interrupts a formal scene to explain why a ritual object is missing, and the explanation reveals a chain of practical failures — a borrowed cart, a dishonest porter, a fee paid to the wrong official — that changes what the others can do next rather than merely decorating the scene.\n\nLet language and narrative distance follow the actual teller: direct address, exaggerated description, or sudden shifts between high ceremony and plain physical detail are all available, alongside seasonal attention, omission, exuberance, and direct feeling where the scene supports them. Impose no universal quietness and no mandatory scenery inventory, and relocate nothing to a real-world Japan. Stop when the performed encounter has changed a character's position, obligation, or understanding, not when every gesture has been described.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism/chronicle/testimony route. Treat inherited obligations as matters of community history and recorded testimony. The narration can move between a chronicle's official account, a witness's remembered version, and the practical details neither fully captures. A title, relic, oath, or ancient event matters because it still decides who owns land, who may enter a place, who owes service, and who is believed.\n\nGround the mythic scale in material life. Show the obligation through its consequences for ordinary people: a family that must supply a rider for an old patrol, a village maintaining a bridge because of a forgotten treaty, a relic carried to a festival nobody can afford. The absurdity opens in the gap between the official version and the testimony — the chronicle records a heroic oath while the witness remembers the rain, the missing mule, and the official who charged for the seal. Read the discrepancy differently through different people: scandal to one, routine to another, a reason to laugh to a third.\n\nHandle testimony as method, not a single monologue. A character reports what they saw, what they were told, and what they now believe; keep direct observation, hearsay, and later knowledge distinct. An account may be partial or self-interested, shaped by loyalty or fear, and still supply concrete details that matter to the present problem. Let competing accounts compose a fuller picture instead of dissolving into blanket uncertainty, and let private thought carry what a witness chooses not to say when the viewpoint permits it.\n\nKeep the obligation active throughout. Chronicle and testimony do not debunk the magic or the inheritance; they show how it is experienced and contested. A relic still works, but its keeper also faces the queue of petitioners outside; an oath still binds, but its enforcement depends on who remembers the exact wording.\n\nPreserve the language, community, and history the supplied world provides; do not manufacture a regional temperament or relocate the cast. The chronicle/testimony method shapes the telling and its social function, not the setting. Stop when the testimony has changed what a character knows, believes, or can do, not when every account has been collected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaean::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Name the character, the place, and the present problem in the first lines, with no long historical preamble. Let inherited obligations surface through what the character is doing now — arriving to claim a title, deliver a relic, answer an oath, stop an institution from acting — and show their weight through the next required action, not a lecture about the past.\n\nKeep every spatial change easy to follow. Use short, concrete movement sentences — a door, a stair, a street, a gate — and name each new space by what distinguishes it from the last. When an unfamiliar name or mechanic appears, present it inside the same passage: what the relic is, what the oath requires, whom the institution serves. Never pause the scene for a glossary.\n\nBreak paragraphs for readability around action, dialogue, and private response, and let the focal character react after a significant line or event. A reaction belongs to its owner and can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny; render it through a specific thought, sensation, or small physical detail — a tightened hand, a remembered phrase, a sudden awareness of cost. Do not hand every character the same witty voice or the same register.\n\nLet the absurdity emerge from the gap between the obligation's scale and its operation: a legendary relic too heavy for the cart, an ancient oath requiring a witness who is currently asleep, a hereditary title that comes with a queue, a fee, and a missing form. The humor comes from the world's own rules, not from a narrator mocking the genre, and responses differ — awe in one character, annoyance in another, an immediate search for a workaround in a third.\n\nKeep the mythic scale active. Fast pacing does not erase the obligation's weight; it makes the weight visible through immediate consequences — a character may realize mid-errand that fulfilling the oath will cost a relationship, a home, or a future they had assumed — and that realization lands as one focused reaction beat rather than an internal essay. First person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are available, not required. Stop when the current beat has changed the character's position or understanding, not when a reveal or reversal has been manufactured.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite in sustained cadences whose rhythm turns on the contrast between large claims and small logistical problems. When the prose reaches toward mythic scale—an ancient oath, a relic, a city that has endured three sieges—move within the same sentence or an adjacent clause to a material consequence: a cracked seal, a toll gate, the cost of oil for a lamp. The deflation is not a punchline and must not break the paragraph's music. Let the syntax carry the change, so a long clause that gathers grandeur arrives somewhere unexpectedly practical, and the reader feels both the reach and the landing.\n\nUse recurring images and sounds as the tale's organizing memory. A repeated color, a named stone, the noise of a particular bell may return at moments of decision, and each return should carry what has changed since its last appearance. Trace every image to the scene or to the teller's associations rather than to a decorative list; a comparison earns its length when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present. Let plain statements provide contrast: a short declarative sentence after a long associative one can land a vow or a cost more sharply than another figurative expansion.\n\nKeep the weight of inherited obligations active in the prose itself. An oath should alter what a character can do or hesitate to do; a title or relic should change what a decision costs. Register that weight through pacing—a pause before a door opens, a sentence that lingers on a dusty banner, attention folding back to the bell heard earlier—rather than through speeches alone. Ordinary practical inconvenience retains its absurdity, but render it as lived texture inside the cadence, not as a joke that dissolves the obligation; the solemn reach and the logistical stumble belong to one continuous music.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet reflection move between a remembered occasion and the present scene without requiring doom. In a world of oaths and ancient institutions, the telling losses are small and irreversible: a worn stair in a fortress meant to stand forever, a name missing from a muster list, a festival that once honored something no one now explains. Let such a detail carry the association rather than announcing impermanence at the close of a paragraph. Ordinary pleasure remains real—a good meal in a changed city is still good, and the memory of a dead friend may surface alongside laughter rather than instead of it.\n\nLet the contrast between grand scale and small logistics enter the reflective mood as something observed with affection. A thousand-year oath now depends on a broken wax seal held together with string; a grand magical tradition is preserved by clerks who cannot find the key. The incongruity should arise from a specific continuing procedure—someone still performs the rite, badly, on schedule—so the reflection notices what the procedure has survived and what it has outlived, without resolving the incongruity into a lesson.\n\nAnchor the melancholic attention in material obligations. A loss should touch a present choice: whether to keep a watch, whether to repair a wall, whether to answer a summons that arrives a generation late. The narrator may reflect directly when the reflection is earned by a concrete object or occasion, and may equally let the object do the remembering. Keep the register varied—sometimes the sentence is plain, sometimes it slows to observe—so that sorrow, amusement, and ordinary competence remain available notes rather than one compulsory tone.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed storytelling anchored in this world's own institutions rather than a borrowed court. A harbor clerk tells a council why the lighthouse was never relit; an archivist retells a failed negotiation over a long supper; the keeper of a shrine explains to a traveler why the door is chained. The telling belongs to its occasion—someone needs the account now, for a decision, a meal, or a vigil—so the tale is selected and shaped by its present use. Let the teller's digressions seem at first to be administrative or personal asides, then earn their return when a later detail in the main account shows why the aside mattered. The large claim—a treaty, a holy vow, a magical supply chain—crumples against the small logistical problem the teller finally admits.\n\nLet the frame shape information flow. The told story may be interrupted by the audience's present reactions: a practical objection, a correction to a name, a demand that the teller reach the point. The main tale remains a tale—the teller chooses what to repeat, what to compress, and what to hold until a listener asks. Not every interruption is a comic reversal; sometimes a question merely signals that a digression has not yet found its meaning, and the teller resumes. Keep the teller's language distinct from the other voices in the frame: the teller may pass from lyric register to plain account as the material demands, while listeners speak from their own concerns, and no one is obliged to answer in parables.\n\nPreserve the historical and magical weight of what is told. A framed tale is not an excuse to soften consequences: when the teller reveals a mistake, an oath, or an overlooked cost, the present scene shows its living burden—an official must decide what to record, a family must decide whether a promise made under false assumptions still binds. The absurdity belongs to procedures and misunderstandings, never to the magic itself, which remains operative, ancient, and costly.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaear::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a few exact details and let them carry the scene's obligations. If a character swears on a sword, the sword may be notched, borrowed, or tied into a scabbard that no longer fits; take one such detail and let it stand without a paragraph of history. A long-ago war can be present in a single worn step, a missing tower, an official's repeated refusal to sign a name. Direct clauses move action or choice; they do not rehearse the setting.\n\nRun the contrast between large claims and small logistics through omission. Let a grand claim appear in one sentence, then let the next sentence show the practical problem: the spell that can level a palisade, and the gate key that has been lost. Do not explain the irony. Omit commentary such as 'It was absurd' or 'The empire had fallen'; show the cracked seal and the two clerks arguing about ink, and let the reader infer the mismatch from the juxtaposition.\n\nKeep enough explanation for causality. If the magic's cost matters, state it plainly once. If a character acts from a remembered vow, give the single relevant memory rather than a sequence of flashbacks. Interiority stays brief but real: a sentence of direct thought or sensation that changes the next visible action. Vary sentence length—a precise extended sentence may serve better than four performed fragments—while the sparse surface keeps the fantastic terms legible: the reader knows what the magic does and what it costs, then watches the characters live with that cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose. Let the paragraph move naturally from action to perception to a character's recollection or private thought, then back into dialogue or movement, without recap machinery. Use transitions of attention rather than headers or time stamps: a guard captain notices a smell from the harbor while a wizard explains a treaty, and the paragraph may follow either the smell or the treaty as long as the return to the scene stays legible.\n\nEmbed the absurdity in social transactions and practical procedures rather than in narrator commentary. Let an apparently reasonable premise expose its consequences through a conversation or a sequence of minor actions—an official repeating a formula, a prophet trying to find a dry place to sit, a magical archive organized by someone who never learned to read—and let the characters respond differently. One finds a bureaucratic delay merely inconvenient; another realizes it means the treaty has already failed; a third has stopped listening. Do not give everyone the same comic distance.\n\nPreserve the weight of obligation through consequence. A vow, a relic, or an inherited debt changes a present decision and its material cost, and the fluid prose can show the character working through the choice—the nagging thought arriving mid-errand, the hesitation before giving a name. Magic remains operative, practical, tangible, and sometimes inconvenient, rendered in the same novel prose rather than set apart as spectacle. End at a natural boundary—an unfinished conversation, a journey begun, a plan still forming—without requiring a lesson or a completed arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nEstablish the relationship premise through scene selection and private language rather than an opening declaration of feelings. Choose a task that touches one of their obligations—waiting at a shrine, inventorying a mage's supplies, mending a cloak before a formal audience—and let the interaction reveal what they have shared and what each assumes about the other. Use a callback—a phrase, a joke, a particular way one checks the other's sleeve—only when it does work in the present moment, so it reads as something the characters carry rather than a footnote to earlier scenes.\n\nLet the absurdity enter through their differing responses to the same logistical problem. One treats a missing ritual component as a crisis; the other remembers the last time it went missing and starts laughing. They need not both be witty. Affection and annoyance sit side by side, and a grand magical obligation may shrink against a small practical need—a translation error in a marriage contract, a sacred grove that now has a ticket booth—without the relationship solving the absurdity. What the bond provides is a different perspective, or a moment of exasperated care.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, or discovery the space the actual premise promises, and keep the consequences real. The obligations remain large-scale and material: an oath may require one of them to lie; a family debt may force a choice between two loyalties. Show the choice and its effect on the bond rather than dissolving it in comfort. A scene may end with the relational question open—a help given, a worry shared, a private story retold—fulfilling this particular bond's promise without constant banter or a guaranteed soft ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a compact declaration a teller would remember: a name, an oath, a claimed deed, a place where something was vowed. Then expand into the particulars that complicate it. The saga voice may say, 'So was the bridge first promised,' and the next sentences show the overturned cart blocking the ford, the wrong measurements, the clerk who kept changing the plan. Borrow the oral chronicle's phrasing without continuous ceremonial diction; recurring formulas belong at meaningful junctures—when the same choice returns, when a name is invoked again—never as decoration on every entrance.\n\nExpose the absurdity through the relation between what will be remembered and what actually happened. The chronicle reports a great magical working, then admits that the wizard had lost the second component and substituted dried fish. Let the teller hesitate, correct the account, or own an uncertainty: 'They say the blade split the wall, but the wall had already cracked in the frost.' The absurdity arises from a specific procedure and stays inside the account as part of the record rather than detaching as a separate joke.\n\nKeep the mythic weight active. Oaths, kinship, gifts, feuds, and magical costs still govern choices: a character remains bound by a vow even when the vow's origin was ridiculous, and a public deed becomes a reputation its owner must live with. The saga's scale comes from consequence—a failed bridge, a misread prophecy, a rescue that succeeded only because someone forgot to lock a door—not from mock combat or heroic doom. Move at a chronicle's pace, pausing where the telling needs a practical exchange or a clarification, and leave the account where another act remains available to be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the high fantasy world as a place where ancient grandeur has survived into working institutions, and narrate it in the essayistic comic register of Scandinavian social writing: a dry, precise, socially observant voice that explains how a sacred thing is actually run. A hereditary title, a relic, an oath, or a magical obligation is never only a symbol; it has an administrative history, a maintenance problem, a disputed interpretation, and a cost that falls unevenly on particular households. Let each explanation change what a character can do or what a decision costs, rather than standing in place of the scene.\n\nBuild the comedy from the contrast between the scale of a claim and the small practical inconvenience of honoring it. A thousand-year covenant may depend on a clerk who cannot find the correct seal; a rite may require an ingredient the temple's supplier has stopped stocking; an ancient blade's warranty may have been voided by an unauthorized blessing. Keep the absurdity native to the fantasy world's own rules, records, and obligations — no imported space travel, no modern office comedy.\n\nUse digression as a working tool. An essay on the founding of an order, the translation history of a treaty, or the household economy of a fortress should return with a concrete consequence in the present scene: who owes whom the tithe, which door the old wording still opens, why the youngest sister cannot inherit the watch. Let different people respond differently to the absurdity — exhausted competence, literal faith, tactical cynicism, quiet resentment — and let none of them become the narrator's comic voice.\n\nKeep the mythic scale live under the ridiculous administration. The ancient event, the power, and the inherited burden remain genuinely consequential; a procedural ruling can cost standing, land, a limb, or a future, and that loss should register in loyalties, debts, and material conditions rather than dissolving into the joke.\n\nWhere the story's world distinguishes northern realms, tongues, or communities, keep their customs and registers distinct instead of blending them into one generic North; where it does not, this tradition supplies the narrative stance, not scenery. Snow, silence, and bleakness appear only if the situation earns them — the register is domestic and social, not climatic. The essayist observes public behavior and recorded history but enters no mind the active contract excludes; required private experience remains actual nonspoken interiority in its authorized presentation. End at the scene's boundary rather than on a forced summary aphorism.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nWrite each scene as a readable screen sequence in which the fantasy world's large claims meet small logistical problems through visible action. Concise action lines show what a character carries, inspects, waits for, repairs, or fails to find; the absurd consequence of an oath, relic, or magical procedure emerges from that physical business, never from a narrator announcing that it is funny.\n\nUse scene headings whenever location or time changes, with clear speaker cues throughout. Let a cut do the comic work of juxtaposition: from a solemn ritual chamber to the queue outside the registrar's window, or from a battlefield vow to the same character later trying to fit a ceremonial sword into a narrow stairwell. Offscreen sound may arrive before its cause — a seal cracking, a cart overturning, a distant official calling the wrong name — so consequence precedes explanation. Keep transitions selective and the page free of camera commands.\n\nLet dialogue carry the contrast between grand claims and practical inconvenience. One speaker recites the ancient wording while another asks where the tribute is stored, whether the witness must still be alive, and who pays for the damage. Different characters respond differently — as sacred duty, as a work problem, as an opportunity — and none becomes a universal comic narrator.\n\nKeep the mythic scale consequential. The oath, relic, or institution changes what people can do and what a decision costs, and the cost renders as a visible or audible result: a door that does not open, a name omitted from the roll, a payment refused, a guard turning away.\n\nWhen the active contract requires private experience, keep it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the presentation — not dialogue, and not knowledge the other characters hold. Use voice-over only if this sequence needs it and the contract permits it. End at the scene boundary, even mid-errand or mid-hearing, without forcing a completed arc or a closing gag.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene as a playable stage sequence in which the relationship between lines and physical business carries the absurdity. A character may swear a binding oath while keeping a ceremonial cloak out of the brazier, or present an ancient relic too heavy for the table it must rest on; the staging exposes the practical consequence of the large claim without anyone explaining the joke.\n\nUse clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can actually be staged. An entrance can interrupt a rite at the wrong moment; an exit can stall because the sacred door sticks; a pause can mark the discovery that the required witness has spent the morning in the wrong queue. Keep the business economical and purposeful — one well-placed object outworks a stageful of comic clutter.\n\nLet different characters respond differently to the absurdity: reverence, bureaucratic exhaustion, open irritation, the quiet competence of someone who has done this a hundred times. Their lines need not all be witty; the humor lives in the gap between what a line claims and what the staging shows.\n\nKeep the mythic scale consequential. The oath, relic, title, or institution changes what people can do and what a decision costs, and a failed rite can cost access, protection, standing, or a future — shown in stageable results: a door that does not open, a name left off the roll, a payment refused, a character shut outside.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, never as something another character can overhear; a monologue or aside needs its own dramatic purpose and separate authorization, and does not replace every private thought. Add no film-camera instructions. End at the scene boundary rather than on a forced curtain line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind — in whatever person and ownership the active contract uses — as it moves through the world's obligations by association rather than linear report. Braid the sacred wording of an oath with the pressure of a ceremonial strap, the smell of the temple stores, the memory of the official who explained the fee, and the private arithmetic of what happens if the rite runs late. The Adams-style absurdity arises inside a single thought trying to hold a grand claim and an immediate practical problem at once: the covenant that binds a kingdom, and the buckle that will not hold a cloak.\n\nLet syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations — a long spiraling sentence for a worry, a fragment when the herald speaks — while enough orientation remains to tell event from recollection from feared outcome. A remembered conversation is not happening again; a dreaded result is not yet real; the old covenant's phrase is text in the mind, not a voice in the room. Return to the present through perception or interruption, not through a summarizing narrator.\n\nMake the recurring concern particular to this mind. One person circles a debt, another a name, another a sore shoulder, another someone left waiting in the courtyard. The comic contrast comes from what this mind notices and cannot stop noticing, not from a comic voice shared by the whole cast.\n\nKeep the mythic scale consequential. The oath, relic, institution, or inherited burden changes what this person can do and what a decision costs; the digressions show how the obligation is lived through sensation, memory, and private calculation rather than dissolving it. A wrong name, an unpaid fee, or a missed rite can cost access, standing, protection, or a future.\n\nThe technique grants no access to minds the contract excludes and does not make scene facts indeterminate. This mind's actual nonspoken interiority is the authorized presentation of private experience. End at the scene boundary — the thought may still be moving when the reply stops — without forcing a completed realization or a final joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages sent over the world's own channels — an enchanted slate, a courier's ledger, a speaking-stone — keeping the medium consistent with the supplied setting rather than importing devices the world does not have. Let the Adams-style absurdity emerge from the friction of coordinating a magical or institutional obligation in writing: a character sends the ancient oath's wording with a copied-out error, asks whether the relic can travel by courier, or receives a formal summons followed by three corrections about which chamber. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to this exchange.\n\nLet each person's messaging habits shape their contribution. One writes in complete ceremonial sentences; another sends fragments from a stairwell; another revises a message three times before sending it; another answers with a bare confirmation of receipt. The gap between what a message claims and what the sender must actually arrange carries the joke: a solemn declaration followed by a request for a stall in the fortress stable, a binding promise followed by a question about whether the witness may attend through the far-speaking stone.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft, and transmitted text from private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact showing what its writer considered saying; it is not part of the exchange and not known to the other participants. Required private experience remains actual nonspoken interiority in its authorized presentation, clearly separated from the transcript. Do not fabricate a delivery failure, an edit, or a retraction merely to add subtext.\n\nKeep the mythic scale consequential. The oath, relic, institution, or inherited burden changes what people can do and what a decision costs, and a missed message, wrong attachment, delayed confirmation, or refusal to reply can cost access, standing, protection, or a future — shown through the transcript's actual events rather than through a joke on every exchange.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the messages. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contract. End at the scene boundary — an unanswered message may remain unanswered — without forcing a completed arrangement or a closing punchline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaeba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nWrite each scene as a concise illustrated sequence: a short scene-setting line or two, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response, in a rhythm suited to a panel of image and text. Let the Adams-style absurdity emerge from the contrast between the scene's solemn visual cue and the practical business the focal character must actually manage — the grand hall described in one line, then the brazier set too close to the tapestry, or the ancient throne propped on a ledger.\n\nUse background and expression cues as purposeful references, never as mandatory labels or automatic truth indicators. A calm face may accompany a line about the covenant while the focal narration notes the unpaid invoice tucked under the relic; the contradiction between cue and practical detail can carry the joke, but any inference the focal character draws must be supported by the scene rather than read off the expression alone.\n\nLet different characters respond differently to the absurdity: formal ceremonial speech, a question about where the tribute is stored, a quiet check of the hour, exhausted competence with the temple's ledgers. The focal character's inner response may be dry, anxious, irritated, or practical; it is that character's actual nonspoken interiority and is not known to the rest of the cast.\n\nKeep scene changes legible — a clear shift of place, time, or company — so the illustrated rhythm stays easy to follow. Keep the mythic scale consequential: the oath, relic, institution, or inherited burden changes what people can do and what a decision costs, and a failed rite, missing payment, or broken seal can cost access, standing, protection, or a future, shown through the scene's events and focal reactions rather than dissolved by the visual joke.\n\nThe style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images; add none without separate authorization. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contract. End at the scene boundary without forcing a completed arc or a final gag.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaebb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as part of a readable through-line in which the world's large claims accumulate small logistical consequences. Keep what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains open clear without retelling the history, and recover a particular only when it helps: the unpaid tribute, the disputed wording, the missing seal, the person still waiting outside the chamber.\n\nLet the absurdity develop across replies instead of resetting. A sacred obligation may open with a solemn declaration, acquire a scheduling problem, then a dispute about who must witness it, then a consequence for someone who was never consulted. Each installment can expose one further consequence of the original claim, so the joke deepens rather than repeats; a running detail — the seal, the fee, the queue — may return with new weight when a later fact changes what it means.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without turning every reply into a miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can be knowledge, relationships, work, or resources: a character learns why the rite requires that particular seal, persuades an official to accept a substitute, repairs the broken clasp, or discovers the debt is larger than believed. An installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, and elapsed story time comes from what actually happens rather than from reply length.\n\nKeep the mythic scale consequential. The accumulating practical problems are not mere comic texture; they can cost access, standing, protection, or a future, and they should connect to individual loyalties and material conditions.\n\nPreserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contract; required private experience remains actual nonspoken interiority in its authorized presentation. The style creates no leveling system, ranking, or progression-fantasy world rules. End at the scene boundary without forcing a completed arc or a closing punchline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nRender the mythic scale through what can be carried, counted, and lost. An oath matters as a present limit — what it forbids tonight, what it will cost at the gate tomorrow — not as recited history. Let titles, relics, and inherited debts change the scene materially: who may enter, who must kneel, whose word moves soldiers and grain. When the ancient past appears, bring it in an object handled or a debt paid, in a sentence or two, then return to the people in the room. Where the story calls on its power, treat magic as work with a visible price — the effort, the aftermath, what it takes out of the one who works it — and do not stop the scene to admire it. Grandeur stays in the situation; the cast speaks plainly, and ceremony, where it exists, is quoted once and lived past.\n\nRun the difficult relationship through practical exchanges. The characters talk about what is in front of them — the wounded, the road, the terms — in short direct lines, while what is actually between them stays under the words. Omit what they already know of each other; the reader will feel that shared history in how they move around it. Let strain deform the conversation in visible ways: an offer worded so it can be refused, a name avoided where it once came easily, an apology that arrives as a practical act instead of a sentence. Wherever the active contract gives you interiority, spend it sparingly and make it the character's own — what they need, and what it costs them to need it — in their diction rather than a borrowed vocabulary of suffering, and keep it unknown to the others until it is spoken or shown. Because so much stays withheld, a plainly named feeling, used rarely and at real pressure, lands as an event instead of decoration.\n\nRepeat on purpose. An oath formula, a family saying, a line about the cold or the counted dead can return with its emphasis shifted — once as ritual, again as accusation, again as grief. The returning phrase shows what the speaker cannot put down, and it is how hurt becomes audible without adjectives. Place repetition at the pressure points; it need not attend every difficulty.\n\nKeep the grandeur in what things cost. Admitting, needing, withholding, apologizing, or returning is never only private here: the oath, the house, and the war give each act a price beyond the people involved, felt concretely — a march not joined, a succession left standing, a promise to the dead kept or broken. Comfort can still arrive, partial or awkward — a cloak left where it will be found, soup, a silence that is almost forgiveness — and warmth and hurt can share one exchange. Do not force a resolution before stopping: end inside a continuing exchange, task, or thought if that is where the scene stands, the hurt eased, worsened, or simply carried, with the world's obligations still running underneath.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs, with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep the narrative person, action markers, and player-control conventions your active settings supply. Use short paragraphs for rapid exchange and longer ones for a practical sequence, but keep every speaker easy to identify.\n\nLet the mythic scale arrive through concrete points of contact. An oath is what someone will not do and what its binding word does to the people who hear it. A relic is how it is carried, who may touch it, and what its use takes from the hand that uses it. A title, an old battle, an inherited debt, a temple's levy, or a border matters by deciding who opens this gate now, who is turned away, what food is owed, what road is closed. Give each large element a price felt in goods, access, obedience, strength, or time. Grandeur lives in the situation and its stakes, not in ceremonial diction for the whole cast; a stablehand, a captain, and an oath-keeper can all speak plainly.\n\nUse economical clauses, exact nouns, and practical exchanges. A steward counting stores, a rider asking after a ford, a healer naming what a wound needs—these can carry rank, fear, and loyalty better than a speech about majesty. Name or anchor each speaker so the user never doubts who spoke. Let a phrase from an oath, a title, or a warning recur when the situation presses on it; the return can sharpen what it now costs or reveal someone who has not moved past it. Characters may leave things unsaid for habit, grief, politics, caution, or rank, but staging, action, and speakers stay clear enough for the user to follow.\n\nRender magic as specific acts with visible results and aftermath. Show its limits through what a character does next, what is refused, what is paid, or what cannot be repaired, rather than through an announced rule. When the selected presentation includes interiority, keep it in the same register: brief, concrete attention and sensation before explanation. Brevity is not numbness. A few exact details of hands, weather, tools, wounds, or provisions can carry fear or devotion unnamed, and a character who states a feeling plainly is not a failure.\n\nLet the world keep running around the exchange. Watches change; weather works on the road; other actors pursue their own aims; an NPC may help, haggle, refuse, or cooperate for reasons of their own. Continue from the submitted material according to the active authorship contract. Stop at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene—mid-errand, mid-negotiation, mid-march, mid-ritual, or a pause in speech—without forcing a question, choice, reveal, or cliffhanger to close the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the martial-adventure route. Build the telling from deeds, encounters, lineages, and standing, in whatever geography, language, and community the story already supplies; the route changes how scenes are shaped, not where the cast lives. Let scenes turn on encounters with stakes a body can feel — a passage demanded, a debt called in, a duel kept at the ford — so that standing settled or reopened in one encounter is inherited by the next.\n\nTreat magical power as cultivated practice rather than spectacle: trained breath, a light step, a palm that splits a lock. Name techniques in the world's own plain terms, the way a fisherman names knots, and let their cost show in the body and the years spent on them, never in an explanation. A named form may be spoken once; its weight comes from what it has done before, not from a gloss.\n\nLet the realm's scale arrive as material fact. An oath is a blade laid across a table. A tithe is grain carted to the wall each autumn. An old name closes a gate. Relics, titles, and ancient wars matter as things a character must handle today — too well known to sell, too old to ignore — and history surfaces only where it changes what someone does or what a refusal costs. No one owes the reader the lore; a plowline bending around ruin stones can carry a century. Formal speech belongs where rank or ritual actually demands it, not to the whole cast.\n\nKeep clauses short and verbs exact. Render a fight by footing, contact, and breath. Open on the practical need — passage, payment, shelter — while the larger account moves underneath: bargaining over a ferry can be an old debt settling. Use repetition to keep the time of obligation: the form practiced at dawn, the phrase returned to at each meeting, the apology renewed every year. When a character repeats what they will not pass beyond, let the repetition do the naming.\n\nRestraint governs speech and ceremony, never feeling itself. Where the active contract allows private thought, write it as lived experience in the same plain terms — the years a form has cost, an opponent's footing weighed, the memory of who taught the grip — not as a summary of what the mouth withholds. Give a silence a concrete holder when it appears: shame, debt, protection, habit. Read standing in behavior — wine poured before it is asked for, a chair left empty, a door opened slowly — and let the unsaid mean without anyone announcing it. Keep the active narrator and output contract; this method changes how a scene is told, not who tells it or what the world permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the mythic world through what can be touched, carried, and paid. Let titles, relics, oaths, and ancient events act as physical facts with present force: the relic has weight and a worn grip; the oath is kept by one specific act, not praised in a speech; the old war still collects its debts through riders who come with the crown's mark. Do not open with lore. Let a piece of history surface when it changes what someone can do now — a treaty line that forbids the crossing, a gate that will not lift for the dead house's heir — then state it plainly and move.\n\nTreat magic as exact work with a countable cost. Give it steps the way fieldwork has steps, tools, and fuel, and let it take something specific — heat, blood, years, sight. Show the price in the aftermath — hands that will not close, a lamp burned low — rather than in explanation. Grandeur comes from contrast and from what is withheld: the bright blade against the mud, the white stone of the hall and the step worn hollow, a single unclaimed pay chest saying more about a lost company than a roll of the dead.\n\nUse direct language and practical talk. A knight may speak as plainly as a farrier; a court officer more carefully. Let rough speech belong to the one who would use it, not to the whole era. Carry part of the meaning in what is never said — a name avoided, a debt not disputed, a campaign reduced to \"we held the ford.\" The unsaid works between people and on the page, not as an eraser for the mind: where the active contract allows, the reader may carry what the speaker will not give the hall — what the ford cost, and who did not come back. When a phrase returns, let the repetition shift its weight: pride first, then burden, and if it turns to a lie, let the reader hear it turn.\n\nCut transitions hard and vary rhythm with pressure: short declaratives in the work and the fighting, a longer breath in the aftermath or the waiting. Compress diction and transitions, never the character's mind. Weighing an oath against a loyalty can run long in concrete terms — what it costs, who pays, what cannot be undone — and a feeling may be named plainly and briefly or carried by an act; both are honest. The wear on the world is real, but it is not the whole truth of the world: the oath still binds, the power still answers. Let a scene stop at its own edge, in work or in talk, without a closing summary of what it all meant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell this story in documents — letters, orders, reports, ledger lines, petitions, oath-rolls, whatever page the scene calls for, not a fixed rotation of formats. Give every record a writer, a reader or purpose, and a moment of composition, and let that situation decide what goes on the page. A garrison commander may report the grain count and the beacon fuel and say nothing of the desertions; a petitioner may ask twice for a thing that needs asking for once. The page holds what its writer chose to set down for that reader — a composed thing sent to do a job, not the writer's whole mind. Where the active authorship contract grants that writer private experience, write it as live thought in the place the contract gives it, and let the record keep only what was set down. What is left out is part of the story, and the reason lies in the office, the relationship, or the risk — never in a standing rule against naming what is felt. When feeling is named, name it the way that writer would: once, briefly, and move on.\n\nKeep the sentences of the records plain and declarative — dates, names, counts, actions. Cut ornament and qualification; trust the noun and the number. Let magic and history enter the way paperwork would hold them. An oath is a formula copied into a roll, the seal that binds it, and the fine entered against the estate that broke it. A relic is a chain counted in an inventory and a watch roster changed the week after. A ward on a pass is the oil or salt requisitioned each season to renew it. Distance and weather arrive as supply lines and dated entries; deep time arrives as the calendar — the ninth year of a peace, the fortieth of a truce no one living signed. Do not open a record with the realm's history. Let titles and institutions act through what a seal can command, what the archive releases, what a clerk must copy out again by hand — and let one line, a levy, a succession, a boundary moved, change what the next document can ask of its writer and what writing it costs.\n\nCarry a repeated form and let it wear: the same closing, the same blessing line, the same ledger heading across entries. When something has changed for that writer, let the form carry the change — a name dropped, the blessing shortened, a postscript added — and stay with it without explaining it. Keep each record's knowledge to its writing date: a letter from before the fall cannot grieve it. Order the documents so the events can be followed without help afterward. If two accounts disagree, let both stand, dated, and do not settle it for the reader. Let a second hand, a water stain, a line squeezed between the lines carry what happened. A reply may stop with a report half-written or a letter not yet sealed. No summary after the last page.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the mythic order of the world as work done with the hands. Let an oath be a habit kept on an ordinary evening, a relic something carried, cleaned, or mended, a title a name people use without ceremony. Put the object before its history: the reader watches the strap get mended and the road get walked before learning, if ever, what was sworn to make either matter. Give one or two plain touches of the larger history — an old scar, a banner put away, a debt that still names its holder — state them without explanation, and let inherited things still decide practical facts: who answers a summons, what a name carries through a gate, which promise keeps someone home. Grandeur sits in what those old things can still do to a day, not in a formal register laid over the speakers.\n\nSpend the attention on chosen kindness in small, exact things: the good bread, the lamp filled before anyone asked, the bag ready by the door. Repeat the small rituals — the same greeting, the same order of tasks, the cup fixed the way the other likes it — and let the repetition read as steadiness and knowledge between people, not as being stuck. Let care pass through the hands: who cuts the bread, who takes the worse bedroll, who remembers a preference without being told. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue stays plain and practical, even blunt; the tenderness lives in what is done and noticed while it is spoken. A plain kind sentence is allowed once in a while. Say it once and leave it alone.\n\nWhere the active contract allows private thought, write it as real experience in the same spare idiom — the heat of the cup in cold hands, the wish for the evening to hold, the pleasure of another person's small noise in the next room. Economy here means no ornament, not no feeling; one short clause of wanting can carry the affection of the whole scene.\n\nLet personalities disagree in survivable ways — which road, how much salt, whose turn at the watch — and let it sit unresolved or settle through shared work rather than apology. The world's obligations stay real and named; whatever they cost, someone pays them willingly, and that is what makes the quiet evening mean something. Do not erase the cost and do not inflate it. If things are still going when the reply reaches its edge, let it stop there — a second cup poured, the lantern trimmed, hands still busy — owing the scene no confession, no reversal, no lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite composed novel prose whose paragraphs are broad and whose sentences are not. Build each sustained paragraph as a sequence of short, coordinated declarative clauses — plain verbs, concrete nouns, actions in the order they happen — and let the paragraph as a whole carry the scene's social frame: who owes whom, which institution is present, what the old names mean to the people in the room. Keep the narration set down and considered, distinguishable from any spoken voice but unarchaic; the formality lives in the architecture, not the diction. Frame each scene through social context, history, or judgment as far as the active narrator permits, and do not let the composed distance reach into a mind the chosen viewpoint does not know.\n\nRender magic, relics, and titles by their material working. Give a spell its cost in heat, fatigue, spoilage, or the silence afterward; give a relic its weight, its wear, and the exact procedure of handling that surrounds it; show a title through what it lets its holder do and what it forbids. Carry the mythic scale in landscape the same way — distances, passes, the slow road, what the country imposes on travel and supply. State these things flatly and let grandeur arrive as consequence, not as raised diction.\n\nLet the ancient events stay mostly unsaid. The narration may set a fact of history in one plain sentence of framing distance — who built the wall, what the oath was sworn against — and then return to surfaces. The rest of what happened belongs to what people refuse to discuss, answer sideways, or meet with a changed subject. Keep the unsaid recoverable from the shown: costs paid, deference given, a province no one visits. When an oath-phrase or a name recurs, let the repetition shift its meaning — ceremonial at first, later exposing what keeping it has cost or how far the speaker cannot move past it — and let it recur where the scene earns it, not at every difficulty.\n\nOrganize passages around the public and the private. In ceremony, council, and formal address, let the institutions' registers sound: oaths, titles, courtesies, exact procedure. A letter or summons can carry the same contrast, its formal phrasing set against what the recipient does with it. Away from those settings the same people speak plainly and elliptically — horses, roads, debt, the body — with the weight in what neither says, and each exchange stays practical: an order given, a price named, a question put off. Give each silence its reason: duty, shame, loyalty, habit, the plain uselessness of speaking. Give private thought the same concrete register as speech — attention, counting, physical noticing, the arithmetic underneath a courtesy — and when a character actually feels something, one plain sentence may name it; that sentence outweighs a page of refusal.\n\nMove between scenes with deliberate transitions, framing each new place through its history and obligations before its action begins. What a decision costs follows from what the world has already established — whom the oath binds, what the power spends, whom the institution protects. Oaths bind different keepers differently, institutions shelter some people and grind others, and power succeeds or fails at its cost according to what the people in the scene actually do. Let that decide what becomes of them, not any required pattern of defeat or restoration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBuild the response around one act of telling. Before the tale begins, put the frame on the page: who tells, who listens, where they sit or stand, and why this story is being told now — to persuade, to delay, to warn, to comfort, or to bind the listener to a claim. Make the frame physical: someone mends tack, counts a ration, keeps the fire; the teller holds an object or keeps a hand flat on the table and does not touch the thing beside it. Borrow the frame craft, not its furniture — no palace, djinn, or desert is owed to any setting.\n\nTell the embedded tale in the same stripped prose as the frame: direct clauses, named objects, visible actions. Inside it, let a title, relic, oath, institution, landscape, or ancient event carry real force, and let that force still operate in the frame's present. The tale is the teller's move, not decoration. Selection and emphasis follow the teller's purpose: a teller who wants something leaves out what weakens the request; a teller who is warning someone includes the price another person paid. Do not announce the omission. Let a concrete question from the listener — how long the road was, who else crossed, what happened after — expose what was left out, and let the teller answer or not answer. If the telling needs a tale inside the tale, open one, and keep the levels intelligible by returning to the frame's objects and voices.\n\nRepeat one concrete phrase or object across the levels so it changes meaning. The sword in the tale can be the sword against the wall. The words of an old oath return at the moment the listener must decide what to do with what they have heard. Repetition connects the levels; it does not certify that the tale is true.\n\nHold back one consequential fact until the listener needs it — a name, a cost, a death, a broken oath, what the teller did after the tale ended — and let its arrival change how the listener reads the present situation. The listener may still act, refuse, or ask again. Where the narration reaches private thought, keep it as spare as the dialogue: the listener's fear of where the tale is going, the teller's held fact. Keep the mythic scale in consequences rather than pageantry: a fallen kingdom matters because it decides who passes a gate, who owes a debt, whose name cannot be spoken in that hall. Stop where the scene's attention actually stops — inside the telling, in the silence after it, or in the middle of the listener's next movement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the narration as a composed documentary account: one voice setting what it saw and did beside what records, monuments, titles, and institutions now claim about the same events. The speaker may have carried the relic, guarded the gate, or simply inherited the consequences; whatever the role, keep the account specific — the year, the road, the hall, the order that was given, the object carried out, the names of those who came back and the one who did not.\n\nLet economical clauses do the separating. State what the narrator can verify: the weight of the relic, the condition of the wall after, the exact words of the oath, how many crossed the bridge before dawn. When a record claims more than the narrator saw, do not close the gap. Say what the monument now says; then say what the survivors never said. Let the difference stand in the prose. A phrase from the official account can return later in the narrator's memory with its meaning changed. Keep what the narrator knew then distinct from what the narrator has learned since, and give the later knowledge only when the account has earned it.\n\nBind the ancient event to present material cost. A failed oath keeps a family out of the city; a ruined foundation still collects its tax; a title still opens a gate; a landscape remade by magic is still unsafe to cross at night. Show the cost in coin paid, a door kept locked, a field left unplanted, a name struck from a register. The documentary voice is not neutral: what the narrator chooses to record, repeat, and pass over reveals what is being remembered and what is being refused.\n\nCarry large events in short sentences — the wall fell; the register burned; we crossed after — and do not inflate the language to signal importance. Where the narrator does not know, leave it unknown. The account can stop before the historical question settles, before the institution answers, or before the record is finished; end where the narrator's attention actually ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the scene where an old magical authority still operates inside present life: a titled house, a relic, an oath-bound institution, a building that remembers its purpose, a law that magic still enforces. Do not deliver its history. Show its present condition — the worn seal on the door, what it costs to keep a ward fed, the register that still lists a bloodline, the street bent around a shrine no one may enter — and let that condition tell what the place does to the people living around it. Draw every power from this world's own magic and offices, not from an imported night-society's physiology, factions, or bond rules.\n\nWrite the collision in concrete terms. Name the objects and actions: the key that no longer fits, the coin set down on the counter, the glove removed before touching the relic, the chair left empty at the table. Keep sentences short. Let the unsaid carry the private cost — a character says the oath still holds, then pays for the room in silence. Repetition can expose a refusal to move on: the same gesture each night, the same title spoken when no one else is in the room.\n\nMake the intimacy morally complicated rather than merely sinister. A trusted companion offers real help and still protects an old privilege; a private desire runs with loyalty or against it; a mask comes off only in trusted company, and wearing it has a price the narration can show. The narration may give a focal character's actual thought while the others see only the composed face; do not turn every caring act into fraud or every private room into a trap.\n\nLet atmosphere come from working power and visible decay. A beautiful room matters because of who may enter it and what it protects; a damaged ward matters because it no longer keeps something out; a ritual matters for its administrative and magical consequence, not its mood. End wherever the exchange actually is — mid-conversation, inside a private interval, in the middle of a continuing action — without requiring the old authority to fall, the intimacy to resolve, or anyone to pause for a conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and domestic realism route. House the fantasy material inside ordinary household and community life: a family weighing whether an oath still binds them, a relic kept in the room where they eat and sleep, an institution's letter arriving to change who may marry, travel, work, or inherit. Use the language, region, community, and period the story actually supplies, and do not impose English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a standard family shape on people who have their own.\n\nCarry the obligations in objects and actions. The register to be signed, the door that must stay open, the field that cannot be sold, the debt that passes to a child, the meal cooked for a guest whose title carries an old claim. Write in economical clauses and let hands hold the weight: a cloth folded over the relic before anyone speaks, coins counted twice, a child sent out to the step while the adults read the letter. Let the household be watched — neighbors, elders, a clerk — since what the family owes is also what the community sees them owe.\n\nLet omission carry the domestic cost. Someone agrees to the oath and says nothing about the journey it will require; a parent lays out the practical arrangements and leaves out the fear. Repetition can mark the rhythm the obligation interrupts: the same morning task done once more before departure, the same name said at the door each evening. Do not announce feelings; leave them visible in what gets selected and what gets passed over.\n\nKeep the mythic scale answerable to particular loyalties. A kingdom's law matters because it decides whether this family keeps its house; an ancient event matters because it still determines who is welcome at this table. Show the consequence materially. End inside a domestic task, a conversation, or a private thought — the decision need not be made, the journey need not begin, and no understanding has to be reached before the response stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tale route. Write the strange encounter as a concrete event that one particular person sees, hears, touches, or fails to understand — a relic, a spirit, a changed landscape, an oath that still acts, an ancient event briefly present. Follow the world's existing magical rules and add nothing the story does not permit. Set the narrative distance to match the teller: a personal account in the teller's own plain words, or close narration that stays with what the focal character notices. Direct feeling is allowed; do not flatten the telling into obligatory quietness.\n\nRender the wonder through exact observation. The light on the water, the sound out of the empty shrine, the footprints that stop at the tree line, the sword warm when no one has held it, the road shorter on the way back. Use short clauses and put the strangeness in the detail itself, not in adjectives or explanation. A character can say what happened and then stop talking; the narration can record the event without interpreting it.\n\nUse omission to protect what the tale withholds. The narrator may not know why it came, what it wanted, or what it changed. Show that in what is not said: the question nobody asks, the name not repeated, the door not opened again. Repetition can mark the encounter's return in memory — the same sound on a later night, the same object found somewhere else. Do not fold the wonder into a lesson or a finished explanation.\n\nGive the encounter a material consequence. A gate now opens, a debt is remembered, a journey becomes possible, a title must be answered; show it happening. Stop during the encounter, in the silence after it, or in the middle of the next ordinary task. The character does not have to understand the event, explain it, or act on it before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. Write the story as an account that keeps what the narrator or focal character personally saw and did separate from what others reported. The fantasy material can be a war, a magical disaster, an oath-bound institution, a change in the land, the fall of a titled house. Keep language, community, and history specific where the story supplies them. Do not reach for magical realism by default, and add no supernatural fact beyond the world's canon.\n\nMark the difference in the prose itself. Witnessed events take direct observation: I saw the wall break; I carried the child across the bridge; we buried the sword before dawn. Reported events stay attributed: say who told it and what that person claimed, and never let a report quietly become a sighting. Repetition can show how an account changes — the same battle given differently by two survivors, the same name added to a list years later.\n\nCarry the weight in concrete clauses. The register, the road, the coin, the door, the horse, the river, the burned field. Let omission hold what the narrator cannot verify or will not say; a testimony can break off before the full account is given, and the narrator can state what happened and then stop.\n\nBind the chronicle to present consequences. The old event still decides who owns the land, who may enter the city, who owes the debt, whose name is spoken at the ceremony. Show that in action rather than explaining the whole history. End wherever the telling actually is — inside the testimony, in a pause, in the middle of the listener's next question — without requiring the account to be completed or answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nEnter the situation clean. Open on a concrete place, a person, and a present action — a messenger reaching the gate, a relic being weighed, an oath spoken in a hall, a road rewritten by magic — and do not open on a history summary. Let the fantasy scale arrive through what must be done now: answer a summons, carry a title, pay a debt, cross a boundary, refuse an inherited claim. When an unfamiliar name, office, or mechanic matters, present it clearly at the moment it matters, in a line or two, and move on.\n\nKeep movement easy to follow. When the scene changes place, name the new place and the action that carries the character there — she left the hall and walked to the west gate; the ward was cold against her hand. Use short paragraphs and break them around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader always knows who is speaking and where everyone stands.\n\nWrite the reaction beats economically. After a significant action or line, give the focal character one focused private beat: a pause, a repeated gesture, a named object noticed, a decision formed and not spoken. The beat belongs to its owner and can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny. Do not inflate the language; let the unsaid carry the cost. A character receives an oath and answers only, 'I know what it means,' and the lines around it show what knowing it costs.\n\nUse repetition to hold the fast telling together. A title, relic, phrase, or gesture returns across scenes and changes meaning, so obligation accumulates without a recap. Show magic and institutions acting in the material world: a door opens, a coin is refused, a name is struck from a list, a road closes. End during a continuing action, inside a private reaction, or in an unfinished exchange; a cliffhanger is not owed, and nothing has to be decided before the response stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild each scene from exact objects and practical actions, and let the syntax carry the larger obligation. Open on the thing in use — a relic being carried, a boundary stone being walked to, a charter unsealed, a harness mended — not on the history behind it. Name the thing plainly. Let the sentences move at the pace of the work: short declarative clauses while the hands are busy, then one longer line when the narrator's attention lifts from the task to what the task means. That shift in cadence can hold the weight of an ancient agreement so the narration never announces it.\n\nDevelop comparisons from what the scene already supplies. A long wait can become like the slow settling of a wall the builders no longer remember raising. A promise can be measured against the condition of a road, a blade, a child's coat. When a comparison unfolds, let it change what the narrator understands or make a cost present; otherwise cut it. Return a sound or an image across the paragraph and let each return find it altered — the same bell a summons, then a counting, then something someone no longer hears. Keep plain statements available as contrast so the lyric movement never turns an ordinary exchange ceremonial.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, let dialogue stay practical and direct. A character asks for water, refuses a duty, names a price, while the narration's attention moves from the spoken words to the object between the speakers — its weight, its wear, the hand that holds it. The unsaid carries meaning through what the narrator notices and what the prose leaves unremarked, not through declared feeling. Grandeur belongs to the situation: an oath matters because of the particular people, places, and costs attached to it, not because the whole cast speaks in elevated periods.\n\nKeep the lyric attention inside the active perspective. It belongs to the narrator or focal character, not to every mind in the room; another person's motive may be inferred from what they do and what they leave behind, but it is not available as private experience unless the contract grants it. End where the scene's attention actually rests — mid-exchange, mid-wait, or after the work is done — without requiring the moment to resolve into a completed meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present object or action carry the memory instead of announcing it. A gate that no longer closes, a cup kept out for a guest who will not return, a field gone to thorn, a child doing a task once done by someone now dead: name the thing and what is done with it now, then let the focal character notice the difference between what it was and what it has become. That noticing is the reflection. Order the beat so the present action comes first and the memory arrives through it, then return to the hands and the work so the scene keeps living forward.\n\nUse repetition to expose a refusal to move on or a habit that has outlived its reason. Someone still sets a second place. Someone still walks the old patrol line. Someone repeats a phrase from a dead teacher when the work goes badly. The repetition need not be explained as grief; its persistence under present circumstances is the reflection. When the same phrase or gesture returns later, let the return carry a changed meaning rather than restate the first one.\n\nKeep the world's scale personal and material. An ancient defeat, a broken succession, a failing enchantment matters through the particular people who live with its consequences — the tax they pay, the road they cannot use, the name they avoid. A ruler's grief and a servant's grief both belong to the world, but they are not interchangeable; follow whichever perspective is active and its actual access to memory and knowledge.\n\nOrdinary pleasure stays pleasurable. A good meal, a repaired wall, a child's laughter can coexist with the awareness of what is gone, and the prose may let that coexistence stand without resolving it or closing on a declaration of loss. End where present attention rests, even if the remembered thing remains unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story inside a frame. Open with a present teller and a listener whose situation matters: a traveler resting in a courtyard, a servant waiting out a storm, a child kept awake by fever, a soldier sharing a fire. Give the frame concrete substance before the tale begins — the teller's hands, the cup, the lamp, the sound outside — and let the listener's questions and silences belong to this present scene.\n\nRun the inner tale in direct language. Name the people, the objects, the actions; move through practical exchanges and exact consequences. An oath matters because of what it costs the person who keeps it. A relic matters because of the hand that carried it and the work it still does. Do not inflate the diction because the subject is ancient; the teller's authority comes from the account's precision and from what the teller chooses not to say.\n\nWork the frame against the tale's omissions. The teller may stop at a difficult point, refuse to repeat a name, or answer a question with a practical instruction. The listener may interrupt, misunderstand, or supply a detail the teller corrects. These exchanges belong to the present scene and can reveal as much as the tale; where the teller lingers and where they hurry past shows where their own stake lies without stating it. Inside the tale, use repetition only where recurrence changes meaning: the same road under different circumstances, the same phrase from different mouths, the same object passed from one hand to another.\n\nDraw names, customs, and institutions from the setting and community the world actually supplies, keeping the tradition the world establishes rather than importing a generic Nights apparatus, a universal honor code, or compulsory mysticism. The frame need not resolve the tale's unfinished business. Stop where the teller or the present scene stops, leaving the listener and the reader with what was told and what was withheld.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a few exact details and let them carry the larger obligation. A cracked seal, a sword too heavy for the hand that now holds it, a road the patrol no longer walks, a debt recorded in a ledger no one opens: show the thing and what is done with it now. Do not explain the ancient event unless the present action needs that knowledge to make sense. When scale must be conveyed, pick one emblem and let it recur rather than supplying history.\n\nWrite direct clauses and let one action follow another. When a decision costs something, show the cost in the object, the body, or the exchange: the coin set down, the gate left open, the name not spoken. Repetition should mark a change or a refusal — the same question asked twice because the first answer was not accepted, the same task performed again because the person cannot stop. Otherwise vary or cut.\n\nKeep inward content to what materially contributes. A focal character may think enough to make a choice legible, and an essential thought may run as long as it needs to make sense; the economy is in selection, not in performed fragments. If the prose has shown the cracked seal and the unopened ledger, the reader can infer the weight — but omission must leave causality available, not delete it. Do not cut the step that explains why one action follows another.\n\nThe narrator's economy does not open other minds. Another person's motive may be inferred from what they do and leave behind, and the inference can be wrong; it stays outside the focal character's knowledge unless the active contract grants it. End when the present action or exchange reaches its natural stopping point, whether the business is finished, interrupted, or still waiting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose. Open with a person doing something concrete in a particular place — mending a wall, waiting at a ford, reading a summons, feeding a fire — and let the narration move without announcement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and whatever inner experience the active contract authorizes. Use paragraph breaks and small transitions to orient the reader in time and place. Do not recap what the scene has already shown or forecast what it will mean.\n\nKeep the language direct and name the work. A title, relic, or oath enters through its material presence or its effect on what someone does: the key's weight on the belt, the scar in the tabletop, the way a name is pronounced at the gate. When a present detail calls up an ancient event, let the recollection arrive through that detail and return to it, so the world's scale stays inside the scene instead of pausing it for an explanatory passage.\n\nLet the unsaid carry meaning. A character may refuse to answer, perform a task instead of speaking, or repeat a phrase that no longer fits the circumstances, and the narration need not interpret every silence. Authorized inner experience shows what the focal character thinks and feels as it accompanies the action; other characters' minds stay closed, their motives appearing through conduct, speech, and the focal character's inferences, which may be incomplete or mistaken.\n\nPreserve the scene's real duration and attention. A long wait stays a long wait rather than a sentence that jumps to its result, and a private interval may hold thought, sensation, or memory without needing an errand to justify it. End where present attention rests — the action concluded, interrupted, or still developing — without forcing a turn or a completed realization.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through scene selection. Choose moments where its particular promise is at stake: one waits with the horses while the other argues with a steward; one repairs a strap while the other reads a letter aloud; one sets food aside because the other is late. Show the affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery through what the characters do and do not say, and give the feeling its room — the economy lies in cutting commentary, not in cutting away from a moment while it is still working.\n\nUse private language and callbacks that belong to these particular people. A phrase from an earlier scene returns with a new application; a gesture is understood by one and missed by another; a joke survives into circumstances neither planned. Let the repeated moment fulfill the premise's promise or complicate it in a way the established relationship can bear, and let consequences follow from this particular bond rather than from a roster of familiar tropes the premise never supplied.\n\nGive the fantasy obligations material work in the bond. An oath may bind one person to a journey the other cannot share; a relic may pass between them at a cost neither states aloud; an inherited title may decide who speaks first at a gate. The world's scale matters because it changes what the relationship can promise and what a private moment must carry. Keep the prose concrete — the cup, the door, the road, the task left undone — so the emotion arrives through objects and acts rather than summary.\n\nHold the active perspective. The focal character's private experience is available under the contract; the other person's stays outside it unless access is granted, and inferences may be right or wrong without the scene correcting them. End where the relationship's present moment rests — settled, deferred, or still open — without requiring a confession, a quarrel, or a repair to close the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the feel of retelling. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes a person, a place, or a deed — who held the ford, who broke the seal, who carried the child through the pass — then move into the concrete particulars that make the declaration incomplete. The retold quality comes from compact authority and from the account's awareness that the act will be remembered, not from archaic diction or ceremony imposed on every speaker.\n\nScale the telling by consequence rather than calendar. The chronicle can pass a season in a clause, then give a full passage to the choice at the door, the giving of the gift, the refusal at the gate. Use recurring phrasing when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the same gate held by a different hand, the same oath kept at a different cost, the same road walked to a worse errand. Tie recurrence to material the story has already established rather than assigning ornamental epithets at each entrance.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority. A living character may correct a detail, refuse the role the account assigns them, or answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern; the record can absorb the objection without surrendering its sense of consequence. Keep the teller's access consistent with the active perspective — a public reputation is not a focal character's private account, and remembered deeds do not open closed minds.\n\nKeep the tradition the world supplies. Do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial speech unless the setting establishes them; oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, and feuds may organize the account when they are already present. End with the business concluded or still before the characters, leaving the act available to be remembered rather than declaring its final meaning on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse a myth-inflected Scandinavian telling: the story moves through a particular household, hall, border stead, or small community where an inherited title, oath, relic, or old obligation is still part of daily work. Keep the ordinary world concrete. People mend a gate, portion out grain, receive a traveler, settle a debt, or prepare a room. The magical or ancient element enters through those practical acts, not through a separate explanatory register.\n\nLet the object or obligation show its age and power through exact physical detail. A blade is heavy, pitted, recently oiled, or wrapped in cloth. A charter bears a broken seal. A boundary stone stands where the plow must turn. A name spoken at the threshold changes who is admitted. Describe the handling and the consequence. Do not explain the whole history at once; let what people avoid saying carry the weight of an older event.\n\nKeep speech direct and economical. A character may say, \"The door is open,\" or \"That was not for you to touch,\" while the private thought supplies what the words refuse. Repetition is deliberate: a repeated task, phrase, or object can mark a change in obligation or a refusal to move on. When a magical consequence occurs, state it plainly and let the practical result follow. The grandeur belongs to the situation, not to formal speech for the whole cast.\n\nPreserve required interiority in the active presentation. A focal character may reason about the cost of an oath while another person sees only the pause before an answer. Do not make every character equally aware of the old power. The scene can close on unfinished work, a completed exchange, or the plain fact of what has now been set in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nWrite the story in scene headings, concise action lines, and clear dialogue blocks. Use a new heading when location or time changes. Keep action lines to what can be seen or heard: a character lifts a sealed charter, sets a relic on the table, watches the road, or turns a key that has not been turned in years. Let the object and the act show the magical or institutional weight. Do not describe camera movement.\n\nMake dialogue direct and practical. Characters speak about what they are doing, what they need, or what they refuse. A line such as \"The oath still holds\" is enough when the preceding action has shown the cost. Let omission work through cuts and through what a character does not answer. A scene may end after a completed action, a refusal, or a sound from outside. Do not force a final explanatory speech.\n\nWhen a magical consequence occurs, show its visible or audible result: frost spreads from a touched stone, a sealed door opens, a bell sounds though no one is in the tower. State the result plainly. Do not pause the scene for a lore summary. Required private experience may appear as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation; it is not dialogue and not knowledge available to the other characters.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. A cut can separate the moment of decision from its consequence. Offscreen sound can announce an arrival before the door opens. Voice-over may carry a focal character's private thought only if the active contract authorizes it. Keep the cast's knowledge separate from the reader's or viewer's knowledge.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speaker names, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, and pauses. Let the shared stage space make the magical or institutional situation concrete. A sealed coffer sits on the table. A character enters with a wet cloak and stops at the threshold. A blade is drawn, laid down, or passed from one hand to another. Each object and movement should be something an actor can perform and an audience can see.\n\nKeep dialogue direct and economical. Characters speak to the person present, about the task at hand, or about what they will and will not do. Let a pause or a repeated phrase carry what is unsaid. Do not turn every private thought into a monologue. An aside or monologue may be used only when it has a dramatic purpose and the active contract authorizes it; it is performed speech, not the default destination for thought.\n\nWhen an old oath or magical power affects the scene, show it through stage action: a lamp goes out when a name is spoken, a door will not open until a token is set in place, a character cannot cross a marked line. State the effect plainly in the stage directions. Do not add film-camera instructions or explanatory narration that cannot be staged.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation. Another character does not know a focal thought merely because the reader has read it. The scene can close on a completed action, an entrance, a refusal, or a pause after a line. It does not need a gathering of all parties or a final revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized focal mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern move the prose. The associations should pass through exact things: the weight of a key, the smell of old wood, the sound of a bell, the feel of a scar, the name someone spoke at the door. Use short clauses and precise nouns to keep the movement grounded. Do not let the scene dissolve into indeterminate imagery.\n\nLet the magical or inherited obligation enter as a pressure inside the mind. A memory of an oath returns while the character works. A relic's touch brings back the day it was first handled. The thought of a boundary or a debt interrupts a practical task. Show the cost through sensation and repeated concern, not through a separate explanatory passage. The character may notice what he or she will not say aloud.\n\nKeep the distinction between present event, memory, and imagined possibility clear enough to follow. A sudden image may be a recollection, a fear, or a magical sending; orient it by a concrete detail or a shift in sensation. Do not make every character share a mind or grant access to minds the active contract excludes.\n\nUse repetition deliberately. A phrase, object, or remembered instruction may return with changed emphasis as the character approaches a decision. The passage can end on an unfinished thought, a completed act, or the plain recognition of what the obligation now requires. It does not need a resolution of every association.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Each person's digital habits shape the exchange: one writes short practical lines, another sends a single word after a delay, another attaches an image of a broken seal or a map. Let the messages concern concrete things: a relic's location, a gate that must be closed, a name that must not be spoken, an order received, a debt called in. Do not use the transcript to deliver a lore summary.\n\nUse timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter. A delay before an answer can show hesitation or the moment a magical consequence occurred. An unsent draft may show what a character considered saying but did not transmit. Keep the unsent draft clearly separated from the sent message. Private thought is not transmitted text; do not make another sender reply to something the character only thought.\n\nLet the magical or institutional situation change what can be sent or received. A message fails after a boundary is crossed. An attachment will not open until an oath is confirmed. A channel goes silent when a name is entered. Show the effect through the interface event and the characters' practical responses. Do not invent an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. Keep it brief and concrete. The transcript can end on a sent message, an unsent draft, a delay, or a plain statement of what the sender now knows. It does not need a resolution of every exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in concise scene-setting, clear dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response. Let each scene open with a concrete visual anchor: a sealed coffer on a table, a character standing at a boundary stone, a hall with one empty chair. Keep the description short and exact. The illustrated scene may include a background or expression cue when it orients the moment, but do not treat an expression as an automatic truth indicator.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, let dialogue stay practical and direct. Characters speak about what they are doing, what they need, or what they refuse. The focal narration may supply what the spoken line withholds: the weight of an oath, the memory of a name, the reason for a pause. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nWhen a magical or institutional consequence occurs, show it through the scene's visible or sensory detail: frost on the window, a door that opens without a hand, a mark that appears on a character's wrist. State the result plainly. Do not pause the scene for a separate lore explanation. Let the consequence change what the characters can do next.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected. The scene can close on a completed action, a spoken refusal, or a focal recognition. It does not need a choice screen or a final revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahafbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nKeep a readable through-line across installments: what obligation is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Open the installment with enough concrete context to orient a returning reader, then move into the present action. Do not retell the whole history. Recover only the necessary particular: the name of the oath, the condition of the relic, the person who last held the key, the gate that was closed.\n\nLet each installment advance the magical or institutional situation through practical work and direct speech. A character repairs a boundary marker, delivers a message, refuses an order, or opens a room that has been sealed. Show the consequence in exact detail. A repeated object or phrase may return with changed meaning, but do not invent a fresh contradiction merely to create a hook.\n\nUse earned developments. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A new fact may qualify an old explanation without erasing the earlier event. Keep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. Do not make every installment end on a cliffhanger or a completed miniature chapter.\n\nThe installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should let the weight of an oath or relic accumulate, not make the consequence perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet an oath, relic, title, or inherited magical obligation become the specific thing the viewpoint character cannot simply accept or renounce. Write the inner argument as a live contest between at least two voices: one that defends the obligation as duty, love, or redemption, and another that accuses the same choice as pride, cowardice, self-deception, or a way to wound someone. Do not settle the contest by making one voice obviously correct. Let the character reverse position, seize on a remembered phrase, or turn an accusation against themselves in the middle of a scene.\n\nMake the emotional cost visible in the actual exchange. A character may admit too much, withhold the one thing the other person needs, apologize in a way that becomes a new injury, or return to a relationship while still carrying the old hurt. Let strain alter what is said next: a pause can become a refusal, a request can arrive as an accusation, and a sincere attempt at comfort can be received as proof of being pitied. Keep the other person's response their own; they may answer with anger, patience, misunderstanding, or a kindness that does not repair anything.\n\nGround the turbulence in the fantasy world's material consequences. A broken oath may weaken a ward, a relic may exact a price each time it is used, a title may bind the holder to a choice that harms someone they love. When the character argues with themselves, connect the argument to these consequences: what the magic costs, whom the obligation protects, what the character fears becoming if they refuse or comply. The scale of the world should press on the private quarrel without replacing it with exposition.\n\nUse the narrator's distance and diction to shape the suffering rather than a fixed vocabulary of anguish. A plain sentence can land harder than an elaborate one: a character may say \"I did it\" and then spend a paragraph trying to unsay the admission. Allow warmth to coexist with pain. A gesture of care may be genuine and still leave the deeper wound untouched. End where the scene's actual strain pauses or turns, not where the emotional problem is resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in clear, scannable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Let each speaker carry a distinct moral position that arises from their place in the fantasy world: a sworn duty, a family debt, a magical price already paid, a loyalty to an institution that has failed them. Do not make every line a philosophical speech. Let one character speak plainly and let that plainness unsettle another character's elaborate justification.\n\nUse brief interior beats to show the viewpoint character arguing with themselves between spoken lines. The thought can accuse, defend, reverse, or fixate on a word the other person just said. Keep the interior beat short enough for the roleplay's rhythm, but make it specific: the character may realize they are lying to themselves, resent being understood, or feel the pull of an oath they want to disown. Do not let other characters read that private thought.\n\nLet the fantasy world's obligations produce concrete pressure inside the conversation. A relic may need to be surrendered, an oath may forbid the help being requested, a title may require a public choice that will hurt someone present. When a character argues, tie the argument to these material stakes: what will happen if they comply, refuse, delay, or confess. Keep the world and NPCs active without pausing the scene for exposition.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from what has just been said or done. The next speaker may answer, the character may act on a half-formed decision, or the scene may hold in the middle of an unfinished exchange. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice screen. Preserve the active authorship contract, including narrative person, action markers, and player control.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the story as a sequence of encounters, journeys, or temporary stays, each episode introducing a concrete obligation, dispute, or magical consequence that tests the characters' loyalties and self-image. Let chapter-like transitions mark the movement between episodes while keeping the through-line of an active history: an old war, a broken covenant, a displaced family, a relic whose power is not fully understood.\n\nGive each episode a clear material stake drawn from the high fantasy world. A village may depend on a failing ward, a title may carry a debt that cannot be repaid, an oath may bind a character to an institution that has since committed a wrong. Let the characters argue about what the obligation means, what they owe, and what they fear becoming. Use the Dostoevskian method of competing inner voices: a character may defend a choice as loyalty and then accuse the same choice as pride or self-deception. A plain-speaking companion can cut through an elaborate justification with a single direct observation.\n\nKeep the telling vernacular and immediate. Let speech carry the moral pressure, with narration supplying the setting, the physical consequences, and the viewpoint character's shifting self-argument. Do not import a fixed period, setting, or cast identity from another tradition. Use the language, period, and community actually supplied by the story. The episodic form should create recurrence and accumulation: an earlier encounter may return as a debt, a rumor, or a changed relationship in a later episode.\n\nLet the episode end when its immediate encounter reaches a natural pause or turn, not when the larger moral question is resolved. The next episode may continue the same obligation, answer an earlier accusation, or introduce a new test that revises what the characters thought they understood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions. Show the material wear of the fantasy world: cracked wards, tarnished relics, frayed oath-bindings, mud on a ceremonial cloak, blood dried under a signet ring. Let physical stakes stay present in the scene. A magical cost should feel like a cost: heat, fatigue, a nosebleed, a scar that does not close, a room that goes cold when a vow is broken.\n\nCompress the Dostoevskian self-argument into short, abrasive inner speech. The viewpoint character may think \"You wanted this\" or \"Say it was duty, you liar\" rather than delivering a paragraph of balanced philosophy. Keep the competing voices distinct: one voice justifies, another accuses, and a third may fixate on a concrete detail that proves the justification false. When the reasoning genuinely needs more room, let the thought lengthen without losing its edge.\n\nGround abrasive or rough speech in its speaker. A plain character may cut through a noble justification with one direct sentence. Another character may use formal language as a weapon, each polite phrase carrying a threat. Do not make the whole cast uniformly hard-bitten or witty. Let the fantasy world's obligations produce immediate pressure: an oath that must be honored by morning, a relic that demands a price before it will work, a title that forces a choice between two people the character loves.\n\nChange rhythm deliberately. A burst of short sentences can follow a longer self-argument, or a single blunt line can stop an elaborate excuse. Do not require every difficulty to end in a reversal, confession, or completed task. End where the scene's actual pressure pauses or turns.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a letter to a liege lord, a temple report, a confession left beside a relic, a journal kept during a siege, a witness statement sealed by an oath. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet each document carry a Dostoevskian self-argument. The author may defend a decision as duty, accuse themselves in the next sentence, revise an earlier claim, or omit the one fact that would make the account honest. A plain sentence inside an elaborate letter can expose the strain. What the document knows or conceals follows its author's situation: a report to an institution may hide a magical failure, a private journal may confess what the writer could not say aloud, a letter to a loved one may withhold the cost of an oath already paid.\n\nUse gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces to reveal events across entries. A crossed-out line, a broken seal, a letter never sent, a marginal note added later, or two accounts of the same ritual that disagree about who was present can all carry information. Let the high fantasy world's obligations shape the documents: an oath may require a formal record, a relic may demand a written price, an institution may archive only the version that protects it. The reader should see the difference between what the original participants knew and what a later writer has learned.\n\nKeep readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the next document answer, contradict, or complicate the previous one. A later entry may reveal that an earlier confession was incomplete, but it should do so through its own author's knowledge and purpose, not through a narrator who explains everything.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures within the high fantasy world. A character may remember how someone takes their tea, mend a torn cloak, bring food to a vigil, sit beside a friend who is afraid of a coming ritual, or offer a moment of rest before an oath must be renewed. Let the care be specific to the people and the setting rather than a generic soft scene.\n\nKeep the Dostoevskian inner life present without letting it overwhelm the comfort. A character may doubt whether they deserve the kindness, wonder if they are being pitied, or argue with themselves about accepting help. Let the doubt be quiet and particular: a hesitation before taking the offered cup, a thought that the other person does not know the whole truth, a flicker of pride that eases when the warmth is real. Do not require a confession or emotional repair. The affection can be sincere and still leave the deeper problem untouched.\n\nPreserve personality, privacy, and disagreement. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable. One character may offer care through practical tasks, another through teasing, another through silence and presence. A disagreement about an oath, a title, or a magical cost can remain unresolved while the characters still share a meal or a quiet hour. Do not remove established problems from the world: the war, the debt, the failing ward, or the dangerous relic may still be waiting.\n\nLet the fantasy world's obligations make the comfort meaningful. A moment of ease before a costly ritual, a familiar meal in a besieged keep, a friend who stays awake during a vigil, or a small gift carried across a dangerous road can carry real weight. The recipe should show how this warmth operates within high fantasy rather than replacing the genre with a domestic vignette.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let narration carry enough distance to frame a scene through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language: the narrator may observe that a hall was built to make petitioners feel small, or that an oath sworn in one age had become a burden in another, without making every character speak formally.\n\nUse the high fantasy world's institutions, titles, oaths, and ancient events as the public frame for private moral pressure. A ceremony, a council, a public oath, or a relic's formal display can organize a passage. Let the scale of the world press on individual loyalties: a character may kneel before an institution they no longer trust, or inherit a title that requires them to enforce a law they believe is wrong. The grandeur belongs to the situation and the telling, not to compulsory formal speech for the whole cast.\n\nDevelop the Dostoevskian self-argument in sustained interior paragraphs. Let the viewpoint character defend, accuse, reverse, and entangle themselves. A single remembered phrase or a plain observation from another character can unsettle an elaborate justification. Connect the inner argument to material conditions: what the oath costs, whom the title protects, what the character fears becoming if they comply or refuse. Keep the thought inside the viewpoint character's knowledge; other characters may see the strain but not the full private reasoning.\n\nLet public and private contrast organize the scene. A character may deliver a composed public statement and then spend a paragraph in private shame or defiance. A formal ceremony may proceed while the viewpoint character notices a small detail that undoes the official meaning. Use ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast when relevant, but do not impose an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary. End where the scene's actual movement pauses or turns, not where the moral question is resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the scene inside a frame in which one character is telling a story from the setting's history—an oath, siege, inheritance dispute, or miracle—to listeners who have immediate reasons to care about what the tale claims. Begin with the teller's choice of beginning as a decision: a flattering version, an accusatory version, or one that makes the teller's own standing fragile. As the tale proceeds, interrupt it through the listener's reactions, not through narrative confusion. A question about a missing detail, a challenge to the teller's analogy, or a demand for the part the teller has avoided can push the outer conversation forward.\n\nUse Dostoevskian pressure, not a coherent edifying legend. Let the teller and a listener argue about what the embedded story proves concerning a present oath, inheritance, or allegiance. When a listener proposes a different interpretation, the teller may reverse an earlier claim, accuse the listener of the same fault the tale condemns, or confess the personal stake that shaped the omission. Do not remove the storyteller's craft: a repeated phrase, relic, or formula should shift its meaning between the first and second use, so the delayed revelation is a moral turn rather than a factual puzzle.\n\nKeep magical power and scale credible by making the frame-tale itself a component of the current situation. An old bargain may be the source of the teller's authority, a land may be damaged by the event being retold, or a listener may be bound by the same oath under dispute. Grandeur may emerge from the tale as evidence, but the outer scene should show the costs: what this telling asks a listener to believe, forgive, or do. The embedded story need not resolve the evening. The narrator may break off, refuse the final formula, or turn the question back on the audience. End the reply at a point where the outer characters' disagreement has become concrete—a listener rejects the analogy, the teller admits the omitted detail, or someone names the action the tale now demands—without requiring the persuasion to succeed or the group to act on any single interpretation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-developmental realism route. Write the scene in a continuous third-person narration that can move between an individual's reasoning and the institutional or economic conditions that constrain it. Start from a material situation: an oath signed under a particular estate's law, a title whose legal obligations now fall on an unprepared heir, a guild decision, a land registry, or a debt tied to an old name. Do not allow the magic to become a metaphor that dissolves the material pressure; instead show how the magical condition is administered, inherited, or taxed, so that the fantasy order behaves like a social order.\n\nThe Dostoevskian element belongs to how characters argue about these conditions. Let one character defend the institution on legal or moral grounds while another reveals the particular humiliations that the first person cannot acknowledge. A plain question from a servant, sibling, or debtor should be sufficient to unsettle an elaborate justification. Use shifts between direct speech and free indirect narration so the prose can report a noble-sounding reason while exposing the anxiety, resentment, or pride that motivates it. The narration should not resolve every contradiction; it may enter a character's self-accusation and then move outward to the next ordinary task.\n\nKeep the syntax direct and the sentences mostly paratactic, with longer periodic constructions reserved for moments when a character is actually reasoning through a decision or reconstructing a past choice. Names, titles, and legal terms should appear with their functional consequences, not as decorative archaism. Descriptions of rooms, weather, and labor should be concrete but brief. End the reply at a point where the argument has altered the pressure on a practical choice—a signature now carries a newly understood cost, a journey appears unavoidable, or a reconciliation has become conditional on an unspoken concession—without requiring the character to complete the choice or resolve the contradiction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nTreat the setting as a present world in which old magical authority presses against ordinary urban or worldly life. Begin the scene with a specific material encounter: a noble heir waiting in a rain-soaked guild office, a sanctified instrument being examined in a rented room, or a former knight recognized on a staircase. Make the ancient power concrete through sound, temperature, smell, and the way other people adjust their speech when its owner enters. Do not separate the Gothic atmosphere from practical stakes; the decayed wall matters because it affects trust, security, or the cost of the next action.\n\nShape intimacy as a Dostoevskian exchange: one character wants something from another—shelter, absolution, information, or the right to remain near them—and explains that want through a theory of duty, family, or merit. Use private narration to show the gap between the presented reason and the unspoken desire or shame. In trusted company, the social mask may slip, but not necessarily into reciprocal affection. The mask can fall into anger, self-loathing, or a confession that leaves both people less certain. Let the reader see one character's interiority while the other must respond to visible speech and action, so the scene remains an encounter rather than a double monologue.\n\nKeep power relational. An ancient title should change where someone may sit, who speaks first, which doors open, and what a character can demand. The narration should not explain this as worldbuilding exposition; show it through the slight deferrals and evasions of the other characters. The setting can include grand buildings, documents, and ritualized language, but these must feel weathered, costly, or invasive. End the reply on a private or conversational beat that leaves the current obligation unresolved. The beat may be a refusal, a withdrawal, a partial admission, or a silence that changes what will be said next; choose the one the scene's actual pressure produces rather than a predetermined gesture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene around a testimony, family chronicle, or remembered oath being given inside a social gathering: a meeting of an extended household, a council of merchants or farmers, a temple courtyard, a landlord's audience, or a migrant community's assembly. Begin with who has the right to speak and who is expected to listen. The language may be indicated through the setting and address forms, but do not sprinkle non-English words without functional reason; instead mark region or community through food, clothing, work, festival, or the obligations named in the room.\n\nTreat the telling as an open performance with interruptions. A listener may correct a date, ask why a particular branch of the family was omitted, or object that the teller has placed himself in the tale as the blameless witness. Use these interruptions to activate Dostoevskian self-argument: the teller may first defend the story as tradition, then admit that the story has been shaped by his own debt or grudge. Do not make the interruption merely a clarification. It should change the pressure in the room, forcing the teller to choose between preserving the communal account and exposing his private stake.\n\nTie the epic scale to local remains. An old battlefield, a sealed well, a boundary stone, a shrine ledger, or a forgotten pledge should be the material point at which the narrated past touches the present. When the tale names a magical oath or ancient curse, show what the listeners can still touch or verify: an unwatered field, an unclaimed house, a name excluded from the marriage register. End the reply after the testimony has shifted the audience's judgment, but not with a complete resolution. One character may accept responsibility, another may refuse to repeat the corrected account, or the assembly may disperse with the teller left to face what his own version has exposed. Let the actual pressure of the scene determine which of these outcomes, if any, occurs.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as an entry or series of short entries made by a character who is trying to fix an event, an oath, or a fear on the page. Mark the date, place, or occasion with practical specificity, and include a seasonal or weather detail only when it alters the writer's state: cold in the castle room affects the hand, dawn after a sleepless argument invites a different confession, rain postpones the journey that the oath demands.\n\nThe entry should not be a finished legal deposition. The writer may record what was said, then interrupt the record to accuse himself of arranging the quotation, omitting his own angry reply, or pretending the other person's kindness was a test. Let the retrospective voice distinguish earlier knowledge from later understanding. A day may be recorded as a small injustice and returned to only after the writer has learned the actual consequence; the later entry can correct or deepen the earlier one without erasing its original fear. Use fragmented sentences, precise nouns, and plain observation rather than ornamental imagery.\n\nKeep the high-fantasy obligations concrete. The diary can mention the relic on the table, the letter requiring an answer, the debt to a house no longer inhabited, or the magical oath that cannot be evaluated except through visible effects. Do not let reflection replace the material relation: the writer should notice what the other person did with their hands, which door remained closed, what food was left untouched. At the end, the entry may stop at an unresolved point—a promise not yet written, an apology begun and crossed out, or a decision that the writer names but cannot yet perform. The private form permits moral self-questioning that a public council would suppress, but the entry itself is a composed artifact, not automatic transparency.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in continuous third-person narration with free indirect style, anchored in a house, market, office, clinic, or transport stop where the old high-fantasy order meets ordinary work. Begin with a small material action—a key returned, a ledger opened, a relic wrapped for sale, a relative recognized on the avenue—and let the scene expand into the family or political history that gives the object its weight. Magical or ancient elements appear only as they already belong to the story's world; this style does not add new supernatural facts.\n\nThe narration should move between the central character's immediate perception and the reasons this perception is difficult to confess. Let one sentence record the other person's courteous question; let the next enter the character's shame, pride, or suspicion. Use Dostoevskian reversal: the character may start with an angry accusation of someone else's greed, then realize that the accusation equally fits his own silence, his unopened letters, or the way he hopes to be seen as the family's protector. Do not make every inner turn dramatic. A flat, unheroic thought—about rent, about a younger sibling's schooling, about what the neighbors heard—can undermine a grand justification.\n\nConnect scale to particular memory. The old oath, land grant, or ruined institution should be present as a document in a drawer, a wall no one may repair, a debt referred to by a date, or a room reserved for a name that no longer arrives. End the reply in a pause where the character faces a changed self-understanding. The pause may lead to a confrontation, a refusal, a completed letter, or a decision to leave the matter unfinished for now; let the scene's actual pressure determine which action, if any, the character takes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nWrite a continuous scene with short paragraphs and clear motion between speech, reaction, and the next action. Open on a visible, easy-to-track situation: a throne-room summons, a master-apprentice argument in a guild hall, a magical contract being examined, or a knight returning to find the household gate barred. Name unfamiliar terms or mechanics the first time they matter, not in a separate encyclopedia paragraph; if the oath needs blood, show the inkwell, glove, or ritual before the larger explanation is required.\n\nGive the Dostoevskian self-argument a beat-based structure. After an important line of dialogue or physical choice, pause briefly inside the focal character's mind. The thought may be only one or two sentences: a defense of a just-said lie, a resentful interpretation of a look, a sudden memory of the promise being broken, or an accusation that turns back on the character's own pride. The next visible action should emerge from that thought without narrating the full reasoning. If the inner accusation is strong, the character may repeat the opposite of what he has just thought; if the shame is too close, he may walk out, ask the smaller practical question, or make a grand declaration that the scene's previous facts have already made suspect.\n\nDo not require every character to be witty or every reply to end with a cliffhanger. Large-scale fantasy obligations remain concrete: the magic has a name, a cost, a location, and someone who will be harmed if the character refuses it. The scene may stop after the central contradiction is spoken aloud, after a physical movement changes the room, or after the focal character realizes the reason for his own hesitation. Keep the language accessible, the spatial changes signposted, and the interiority visible but compressed so the page still moves forward.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nOpen with a single focal consciousness already carrying an oath, title, relic, or ancient event as a present cost. Let the first sentence register that cost through syntax rather than announcement: begin in resolve, let a subordinate clause introduce the counterclaim, and let the sentence end in a conviction that has shifted without being resolved. A sentence may move from \"He would keep the vow\" to \"He would keep the vow, though the vow had already kept him from the one door he needed to open.\" The magical or historical detail must change what the character can do in the scene, not decorate the paragraph.\n\nChoose one or two sounds or images from the scene's actual geography or ritual and return to them when their meaning alters. The grain of a relic described once at the beginning may reappear after a spoken accusation, now noticed as the place where a name was scratched out. A vowel repeated in a vow may return in the character's own self-accusation, carrying the oath's sound into the moment it breaks. Use a comparison only when it changes what the narrator understands about the obligation or the person bound to it; otherwise let the concrete detail carry the association.\n\nLet ideological conflict wear the shape of the immediate relationship. When a character argues a doctrine, make the argument also do something to the person addressed: a demand for loyalty that sounds like hunger, a justification that quietly confesses fear. Allow shame to interrupt the narrator's own sentence, so a declaration of intent collapses into self-accusation before the clause completes. A plain reply from another speaker can cut through the elaborate reasoning without ending the inward spiral; let the narrator absorb that plainness into the next self-address, perhaps repeating the other's word with a different inflection.\n\nKeep the landscape's scale as a felt condition inside the scene: a cold that makes a promise harder to keep, a temple's shadow that forces a decision into the open. Let an occasional plain statement provide contrast to the lyricism, and end where the narrator's thought or action actually pauses, with the next choice still available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nOpen with a familiar place or object whose present condition differs from what the character once believed or promised. Let present perception interrupt the memory before it becomes a neutral flashback: the narrator notices the repaired door, then remembers the night it was broken, then notices the different wood again and feels the repair as an accusation. The remembered self's conviction should argue with the present self's shame or pride, not merely be recalled.\n\nMeasure the gap between then and now through small irreversible changes that belong to the fantasy world's institutions: a child's name altered in the record, a ritual phrase now spoken by someone who no longer believes it, a magical debt recorded in a ledger with the interest still accruing. Let the detail carry the association before the narrator names it. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasure—a warm meal, a familiar voice—even while the passage notices what is already going away.\n\nReplay a remembered conversation with the knowledge of its consequences. The narrator may now understand what was not understood then, and may accuse the past self, defend it, then reverse again. In the replay, let the other person's remembered words acquire a second meaning that the present narrator cannot fully confirm, because the other person's private thought remains unavailable. The reflection may spiral without reaching a confession.\n\nKeep the fantasy world's scale as a weight on the present room: an inherited title, a magical debt, or a broken kinship obligation should make the familiar object heavier or the ordinary task more difficult. End where the reflection actually pauses, with the next action still unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the maqama as the definitive route: set the fantasy's oath, relic, or ancient obligation inside a framed, episodic telling where a self-conscious narrator performs the tale for an audience or a patron. Open with a formulaic invocation or a situational frame—a gathering, a journey, a request for a story—that establishes why this account is being given now. Let the narrative voice be learned and ornate, but make each flourish carry the fantasy's weight: an epithet for a sword should record its broken promise, a rhymed praise of a city should also recall the debt its walls could not repay.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian turn arrive as the narrator's own self-dramatization breaking into the performance. Within the ornate telling, the narrator should interrupt or undercut the elevated style to accuse himself, boast of a shameful act, or argue with an imagined judge. A plain spoken phrase from a listener or from the narrator's memory should puncture the rhetorical performance, and the narrator should then absorb that plainness into the next flourish, so the style itself becomes a way of evading or confessing the moral problem.\n\nReturn a phrase or object within the episode so that its second appearance changes its meaning. The same relic praised early in the episode should reappear with its cost now visible; the narrator may even pause to remark on the earlier praise, turning the repetition into an accusation against the performance itself. Keep the present frame's material conditions alive: hunger, patronage, danger, the listener's impatience. Do not let the ornamental style erase causality or the world's actual costs.\n\nEnd with the frame unresolved—the audience waiting, the narrator hesitating, or the next episode not yet begun. The narration remains a composed performance addressed to an audience, not the narrator's live private thought unless the viewpoint contract allows that interiority.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect one or two exact details that carry the scene's magical or historical weight, and let them stand without interpretive padding. If the detail is a marked relic or a broken seal, name it plainly and show its material consequence: the character cannot cross the threshold, the oath still holds the door. Show the detail and its consequence; omit the interpretation. The reader infers the broken oath from the refusal to touch the sword, or the unpaid debt from the repeated glance at the locked chest.\n\nRender Dostoevskian interiority as brief, direct self-address. The narrator may think, \"Coward\", or, \"She will know it was me\", and then act. Let the competing conviction appear as an immediate contradiction in the next clause: \"He had a right to it. I had a right to it.\" Keep the thought long enough to make its moral problem clear, but do not let it spiral into elaborate argument. The plainness itself can unsettle a more elaborate spoken claim; let a character's ornate justification collide with the narrator's three-word self-accusation.\n\nUse direct clauses and short sentences for movement, but let an essential extended sentence carry complex causality when needed. Omission should leave something inferable rather than delete causality: if the character refuses to touch the sword, the reader needs the sword's prior history and the character's visible hesitation, not an explanation of what the refusal means. Keep the character's available inner experience present even when the prose is short.\n\nCarry the fantasy world's scale through the weight of these exact details and their consequences, not through long exposition. An ancient war may enter the scene as a single scar or a name nobody says. End with the character's next action taken, refused, or still undecided.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in sustained scene prose where the fantasy world's obligations press through ordinary movement: a character enters a hall where an oath was sworn, handles a relic while arguing with a sibling, or receives a magical debt's demand in the middle of a practical errand. Let the mythic scale enter as a material condition inside the scene—the cold of the hall, the weight of the object, the name on a letter—rather than as detached exposition.\n\nMove naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the focal character's inner experience. The Dostoevskian self-argument should arise from the scene's immediate pressure, not as a formal monologue. When the narrator notices the grain of a table, let that perception open the memory of the promise broken there; when a spoken accusation lands, let it trigger a private reversal that the next line of dialogue may or may not answer. Let the thought entangle itself, but keep it grounded in what the character sees, touches, or hears.\n\nUse paragraphing and subtle transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. A new paragraph may move from one speaker to another, from exterior action to interior realization, or from present perception to a relevant memory. Do not interrupt the scene to summarize the fantasy world's history unless the character's attention genuinely turns to it. Keep other characters' private thoughts unavailable unless the viewpoint contract allows.\n\nThe prose may be plain or elaborate depending on the narrator and situation. Humor, shame, pride, or confession can arise where the character supports them. End during continuing action, dialogue, thought, or an unfinished situation, without forcing a lesson, confession, or cliffhanger at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the established relationship premise as the scene's organizing center, and let the fantasy world's obligations enter through that bond. If the premise involves a broken oath, a magical bond, a rivalry, or an inherited duty between these characters, make that obligation the immediate material pressure inside the scene: one character must choose between a promise and a hunger, or two of them handle the same relic with different histories. Do not add a roster of familiar tropes; let only the actual supplied premise generate affection, jealousy, comfort, or discovery.\n\nBuild the relationship's private language through callbacks and repeated moments. When an object touched in an earlier scene reappears, let its meaning have changed because of what happened between the scenes: the same cup now held by the one who refused it before, the same phrase now spoken with the knowledge of its earlier wound. Let the Dostoevskian pressure become interpersonal: one character's self-accusation collides with the other's need, a proud declaration tries to wound, a confession arrives too late or without the words the other wanted. Affection and shame may coexist in the same long look.\n\nGive the emotional beats enough space to land. When the premise promises comfort, let the comfort actually occur in concrete physical detail—a hand on a scar, a meal shared after an argument—without erasing the world's dangers. When it promises rivalry, let the rivalry cut through a mutual obligation. Do not force every loving act to be fraudulent or every confession to dissolve the bond.\n\nEnd with the relationship in an unresolved state: a question unasked, a door closed without an answer, or a character alone with the full weight of what just happened. Preserve the active point of view and the fantasy world's facts; other characters' private thoughts remain unavailable unless authorized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a deliberate, retelling-sense formula that establishes who is remembered and why this account is being given now. The opening should carry the fantasy's history concretely: \"This is the tale of the broken oath at Karadash\" or \"They will say the sword was returned, but they will not say how it was returned.\" Use compact declarations to introduce a person or a remembered deed, then let the details complicate the reputation.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian element arrive as the narrator's or focal character's self-argument against the chronicle's certainty. The teller may recount the public version of an oath-keeping and then interrupt: \"He called it duty. I called it pride. We were both right.\" Or a character may object to the way the tale is being told, correcting a detail that changes the moral meaning. The authority of the oral account should waver under this pressure without collapsing into complete relativism.\n\nUse recurring phrasing with actual moral weight. A phrase like \"the debt was counted\" may return at different moments, each time with a new consequence: first as a boast, then as a warning, then as an accusation the teller cannot answer. Oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, or feuds should organize the episode when the world supplies them. Do not mix in unrelated epic conventions or import a Nordic setting. The chronicle's compact declarations should remain clear and concrete; they can be plain even when the subject is grand.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the chronicle's elevation. A listener may ask an uncomfortable question, or the subject of a remembered deed may object to a flattering version. This need not become parody. End with the account still being told, the next act not yet remembered, or a disagreement about what to record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as a myth-inflected social chronicle: a definite regional telling in which the old story is transmitted, disputed, and locally owned rather than delivered as detached exposition. Let the chronicle voice record what the community knows, what it no longer says, and what different households remember differently. An oath, relic, title, or ancient event should enter through the way people manage a room, a journey, a debt, a shared meal, or a piece of work. Someone knows which door was sealed, which name is not spoken at table, or what the land promised before the current generation was born. Let that knowledge surface when a practical obligation forces the question of what the old promise now costs, and let one person's version of the old story be corrected, challenged, or quietly contradicted by another's memory or a local record.\n\nUse the chronicle voice to hold competing convictions in the same person. When a character must decide whether to honor an inherited obligation, protect a household, or preserve a self-image, render the inner argument as a sequence of accusations and defenses that can reverse under pressure. A plain domestic detail can interrupt an elaborate justification and change its force. Let shame, pride, and the need to be seen as faithful become concrete problems inside relationships rather than abstract positions. The chronicle may move between outward social observation and inward moral pressure, but it should not treat the whole cast as uniformly eloquent or uniformly tormented. A plain speaker may unsettle a long argument with a single practical question.\n\nKeep the mythic scale active through consequence, not through constant grandeur. An ancient pact may require a particular act of care, a refusal may affect an animal, a crop, a child, or a marriage, and the characters may disagree about whether the sign was real or coincidence. Let the telling remain social and domestic even when the stakes are large. A neighbor's memory, a grandmother's warning, or a local record can carry the old story into the present scene, and the chronicle may note where the account has been altered by pride, fear, or the need to justify a past choice. Reserve, snow, isolation, and bleakness are not automatic; use only the actual setting and season supplied. Private thought may be more turbulent than speech, but it need not be hidden from the reader. A character can speak calmly while inwardly accusing herself, or can say the harsh thing and only later understand why she said it. End a scene where the active response boundary falls, even if the argument remains unresolved or the old obligation has only begun to show its cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action blocks and clear speaker cues. When location or time changes, provide a scene heading. Let an oath, relic, or old institution become visible through a specific action: a character refuses to touch a sealed chest, covers a name carved in a doorframe, or performs a small ritual before entering a room. Do not explain the whole magical history in dialogue. Let the action show that the object or obligation has force, and let the cost appear in what someone is willing to do or avoid.\n\nWhen a character's private thought matters, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation. The note is not dialogue and is not known to the other characters. Place it at the moment the private pressure bears on the visible action: after a line the character cannot answer, before a gesture she forces herself to make, or during a pause in which the scene's business continues around her. Use the note to show the competing moral voices: a person can justify an action, accuse himself, reverse the justification, and then speak only a fraction of what he has thought. Keep the note short enough to remain readable within the scene, and do not let it repeat information already visible in the action or dialogue. Do not replace every private thought with a voice-over or a monologue.\n\nLet speech carry the social pressure of the moment. A plain practical line can cut through an elaborate argument. Characters may interrupt, change the subject, or answer a question with another question when that fits their relationship and immediate purpose. Offscreen sound or a cut may be used when the sequence needs it, but do not fill the page with camera commands. The scene can end during continuing speech, action, or an unfinished setup. A relic need not be explained, a confession need not be completed, and an argument need not reach a decision before the response boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, and economical stage directions. Let an oath, relic, or old institution become present through objects and blocking: a character sets a ritual cup in a particular place, refuses to sit in a certain chair, or pauses before touching a wrapped blade. The physical business should make the obligation visible without a long explanatory speech. If the history must be spoken, let it come out through a character's practical need or a dispute, not as a set-piece lecture.\n\nUse the relationship between lines and physical business to carry the public scene. A character may speak calmly while handling an object with sudden care, or may turn away during a promise. Let the audience see the pressure of competing convictions in what a person does between speeches: a hand withdrawn, a repeated gesture, a silence held too long. Do not make every silence signify guilt or every pause conceal a confession.\n\nWhen private thought is required, write it as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation. It is not an aside and not something another character can hear. Place it at the point where the staged action and the spoken line diverge: the character says the formal words while the authoring material shows the accusation or reversal those words suppress, or the character performs the required gesture while the authoring material records what the gesture costs. Use it to show the inner argument that speech does not fully deliver: a justification, an accusation, a reversal, a moment of shame. Do not attach it mechanically to every pause. A monologue or aside may be used only when it has a dramatic purpose in the scene, not as the default destination for thought. End where the active response boundary falls, even if a character is still speaking, still moving, or still deciding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nMove through a single authorized mind by braiding present perception, memory, bodily sensation, private words, and interruption. Let an oath, relic, or old event return as a recurring concern that changes what the character notices: a smell brings back the day the promise was made, a scar aches when a particular name is spoken, or a familiar doorway interrupts a long self-justification. Keep enough orientation to show whether the character is remembering, imagining, or standing in the present scene. Do not let the recurring concern remain a fixed image; let each return add or alter something, so the mind's relation to the old obligation shifts as the scene continues.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian pressure arise inside the movement of thought. The mind may defend an action, accuse itself, reverse the defense, and then be interrupted by a physical need or another person's voice. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, but do not make every character share a mind or grant access to excluded minds. Ordinary reasoning alone is not enough; the telling should follow associative movement rather than a linear report. The self-argument may surface through remembered instructions, imagined accusations, or a phrase repeated with changing force, and it should remain particular to the authorized consciousness rather than becoming a general philosophical voice.\n\nAllow the mythic scale to enter through the mind's private relation to it. A character may feel the weight of an inherited title as a pressure in the chest, or may argue with a remembered instruction from a dead parent. The grandeur of the old world should be felt through that particular consciousness, not delivered as detached exposition. When the mind turns to the old story, let the association carry its consequence: the memory of a promise may interrupt a present choice, or the sight of a sealed object may pull the thought back to an earlier failure. End where the active response boundary falls, even if the thought is still turning or the memory has not resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in short practical lines, another revises repeatedly, another sends a long argument and then a single correction. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the scene. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet high fantasy's oaths, titles, relics, and institutions enter through what people transmit and what they refuse to transmit. A character may send a formal confirmation of an old obligation, share an image of a sealed object, or stop typing when asked to renounce a name. The magical or inherited material should affect what a message can do or what a sender is willing to put in writing. Do not turn the transcript into an exposition dump.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. When required private experience is needed, place it in clearly separated context outside the transcript and keep it attributed to the appropriate person. Use it to show the competing convictions that the sent messages do not fully deliver: a justification, an accusation, a reversal, a decision not to press send. Move into that separated context at the point where the private pressure changes what the sender transmits or withholds, and return to the transcript with the message that actually results. The private context may explain a delay, an edit, a sudden change of tone, or a message that never arrives, but it must not be treated as something the other participants can read. End where the active response boundary falls, even if a message remains unanswered or a draft remains unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response. Let a background or expression cue orient the moment without making it an automatic truth indicator. A character's changed expression can draw attention, but the meaning must be earned by what is said, done, or remembered. Keep scene changes legible.\n\nLet high fantasy's oaths, relics, titles, and institutions appear through the illustrated scene: a sealed door behind the speaker, a ritual object placed on the table, a family crest worn or covered. The focal mind may notice these details and connect them to an obligation or a cost. Do not explain the whole magical history in a single block. Let the scene's visible particulars carry the weight of the old world into the present exchange.\n\nUse the focal inner response to show competing convictions. The viewpoint character may interpret another person's words, defend a decision, accuse herself, reverse the interpretation, and then speak only part of what she has thought. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. A plain line of dialogue can interrupt an elaborate inner argument and change its direction. Distribute the focal narration in a rhythm suited to the illustrated scene: a brief scene-setting line can establish the visible particular, a dialogue exchange can advance the public pressure, and a focal response can then show what the viewpoint character withholds or reinterprets. Do not attach an inner response to every line of dialogue; let it enter when the spoken exchange and the private argument diverge. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. End where the active response boundary falls, even if the scene is still developing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahagbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what obligation, journey, dispute, or piece of work is underway, what changes locally in this installment, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps. Do not retell the whole history each time. Let an oath, relic, title, or old event remain active across installments by showing its current consequence: a debt comes due, a sealed place is approached, a name is invoked in a new situation.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A character may learn something that changes an earlier interpretation, or may repeat a familiar justification under new pressure. Do not force a confession, reversal, or loyalty crisis at every reply boundary.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian inner pressure develop cumulatively. A character's self-argument may return in a later installment with a new accusation or a different defense. The serial duration should give selected facts and feelings room to acquire meaning, not make a fair answer or a stable emotional resolution perpetually recede. When an earlier installment established a particular self-justification, let a later installment show it under a new condition: the same defense may now sound hollow, the same accusation may attach to a different person, or the same memory may return with a changed implication. Recover only the specific earlier thought or fact needed to make that change legible, not the whole previous argument. End where the active response boundary falls, even if a request remains unanswered, a task is still underway, or a character is still arguing with herself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nAnchor each scene in the specific inherited obligation that makes the character's present feeling costly: an oath sworn in a particular room, a title that carries a dead predecessor's judgment, a relic whose use revives an old accusation, or a magical power that reminds the household of someone lost. Bring that obligation into the scene through a remembered gesture, season, object, or phrase rather than through exposition. When the character recalls the moment a promise was made, include the particular room, season, or gesture that made it feel binding, and let that memory interrupt the present attempt to speak, act, or accept comfort.\n\nWrite emotional exposure as a negotiation with what has already been said publicly. Before a confession, apology, or request for comfort, place in the character's mind the version of the event the household, court, or village has repeated for years. Let that remembered retelling shape the actual dialogue: a sentence stops short, a question turns into an accusation, or an attempt at tenderness lands as a reminder of what cannot be repaired. Do not require reconciliation. Warmth and pain may occupy the same exchange, and comfort may be partial, awkward, or refused.\n\nUse the gathering sentence to move from the character's present hurt to the communal memory that presses on it, then return to the immediate consequence. The retelling is not proof of the truth, but it determines what the character believes is already known and therefore cannot be said plainly. Keep the narration inside the chosen viewpoint's knowledge while allowing rumor and public judgment to enter as remembered speech, overheard comment, or the known opinion of a particular person.\n\nKeep the high fantasy stakes causally present in the emotional difficulty. The character may need to honor an oath that now feels like a wound, use a power that reminds everyone of the person they lost, or accept aid from someone whose loyalty is publicly doubted. Let the turbulence arise from that specific situation rather than from a stock vocabulary of suffering. The scene may end while the character is still deciding whether to speak, act, or accept the partial comfort that has been offered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep dialogue lines and action beats distinct enough that a collaborator can follow who is present and what each character is doing. When a García Márquez-style movement into memory or rumor appears, return to the present speaker or action before the paragraph becomes difficult to scan.\n\nMake the high fantasy obligation concrete in what a character can promise, refuse, or perform in the current moment. A character may be bound by an oath, carrying a relic, or acting under the authority of an institution whose history the other participants know only partially. Show that weight through a visible mark, a remembered phrase, a brief public rumor one character mentions, or a practical consequence such as a refusal of aid or a condition attached to help. Do not pause the scene for a full history lesson.\n\nTurn communal memory into an attributed social claim within the exchange. One character may say what the town, court, or order has always said about an event, and another may answer from personal experience. Let the difference between the retelling and the lived memory create friction without requiring an immediate resolution. Recurring language, such as a family saying or ritual formula, may help the collaborators recognize a relationship or obligation, but it should not become a mandatory refrain in every reply.\n\nLeave a continuation that follows naturally from the scene. The response may end during ongoing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished task. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or decision prompt. Active NPCs should continue their practical lives and may offer help, refuse, or pursue their own concerns. Preserve the active authorship contract for narrative person, player control, and action markers. The scene should feel like a coherent interval in a larger shared story, not a completed short story with a moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nOrganize the story as a chain of connected episodes in which a promise, debt, or inherited obligation moves from one scene to the next. Open each episode on a concrete occasion: a journey, a visit, a dispute, a ritual, or a return. End the episode when its immediate business is settled or interrupted, while the larger obligation remains active. Do not announce the route or explain the tradition inside the story.\n\nMake the high fantasy world historically and socially legible through recognition and misrecognition. A title, relic, oath, or magical power should have a known place in the community's memory, even if the viewpoint character understands it only partially. When a character acts under an old obligation, show how others recognize or misrecognize that obligation. Make the consequences material: a broken promise may close a road, forfeit a protection, or change who will offer aid.\n\nPass communal memory through retold events and recurring language. A household may repeat what an ancestor said, a village may remember a flood or battle differently from the court, and a character may discover that the story they were told omits the part that now matters. Use the gathering sentence to connect an intimate consequence with that longer memory, then return to the episode's forward movement. The retelling should change a present choice rather than merely decorate the scene.\n\nDo not transplant a particular Chinese setting or cast identity unless the active world supplies one. The route is a method of selection, emphasis, and episode construction, not a demand for specific geography, period, or speech patterns. Keep the language and community actually supplied in the story. The result should read as a high fantasy tale shaped by episodic vernacular storytelling, with layered memory and intimate consequence, not as a menu of possible routes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nMove from one action or perception to the next without explaining the intervening time unless the explanation changes the stakes. Name the material facts in forceful diction: a cracked seal, a rusted blade, a road washed out, a debt called in. When a magical or historical power appears, show its cost in the physical world rather than describing its aura at length.\n\nMake high fantasy obligations practical decisions. A character may honor an oath by paying for a horse, carrying a message through bad weather, or refusing shelter that would break a condition. Let the weight of a title or institution appear through what it requires the character to do now. Keep the physical stakes legible: hunger, cold, injury, exhaustion, damaged equipment, and the effort of continuing all belong to the scene when the story supports them. Do not reduce the world to survival horror.\n\nCompress communal memory into social pressure with an immediate consequence. A character may remember a single phrase from a dead commander, or hear a market rumor that changes who will sell to them. The García Márquez-style movement between personal life and public memory should arrive as one remembered sentence, one repeated name, or one public judgment that the character must answer or evade. Let the memory change what the character does in the scene rather than open a reflective passage.\n\nVary rhythm deliberately. A longer thought is acceptable when the character is weighing a costly choice or reconstructing a sequence of events, but it should remain hard-edged and specific. Do not pad the prose with repeated reassurances or general suffering. Abrasive speech should belong to a particular speaker and situation, not to every character. The scene may end during an unfinished task or an unresolved decision without forcing a reversal or a completed test.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the story through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a letter, journal entry, report, record, or note. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Each document should know only what its author could know at that time and should conceal or omit according to the author's purpose, fear, loyalty, or misunderstanding.\n\nLet high fantasy obligations shape what can be written. A character may record an oath because it binds a household, or omit a magical event because the recipient would not believe it or because the record would be dangerous. A relic, title, or institution should affect the document's authority, audience, and consequences. A report may be read by a council, a letter may be intercepted, a journal may be kept hidden. The document's material existence matters: a torn page, a copied text, a missing date, or a later addition can change what the reader understands.\n\nBuild the García Márquez-style movement between personal memory and public retelling through gaps and conflicting accounts. One writer may remember a battle as a betrayal, another as a necessary sacrifice, and a third may repeat only the official version. Recurring language, such as a family phrase or ritual formula, can appear across documents and shift meaning as the writers' situations change. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record; let the reader compare what each document knows and conceals.\n\nPreserve readable continuity without erasing the documents' limits. A later entry may correct or complicate an earlier one, but it cannot retroactively change what the earlier writer knew. The sequence may end while a question remains unanswered or a document is unfinished. Keep the active narrator and ownership rules: an in-world document is not automatically the writer's complete live mind, and required interiority must still follow the active contract rather than being replaced by the document itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nShow chosen kindness through particular actions and familiar preferences within the high fantasy world. A character may prepare a meal that another person likes, remember how a household takes its tea, mend a torn cloak, or keep a small ritual that makes a difficult journey feel less lonely. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. The comfort should arise from particular people and particular preferences, not from a general mood of safety.\n\nKeep the genre's obligations active without letting them cancel the warmth. A character may still be bound by an oath, carrying a relic, or waiting for news from a distant war, but the scene can show how care continues alongside that weight. The magical or historical stakes may be present in the background or in a practical detail: a protective charm is renewed, a family name is spoken gently, a letter from the front is set aside while someone is fed. Do not invent danger to make the comfort feel earned, and do not make every caring act fraudulent.\n\nUse the gathering sentence to deepen the pleasure rather than only to mourn. A sentence may gather the way a particular dish has been made in this house for generations, or how the whole village knows that a certain person hums while working, and then return to the immediate warmth of the scene. Communal memory can be a source of belonging as well as pressure. Recurring language may feel familiar and affectionate rather than ominous.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. A character may accept care awkwardly, refuse help at first, or remain private about a hurt while still participating in the shared ease. The scene may end during a quiet moment or an unfinished task. Do not require a confession, a repaired relationship, or a completed emotional arc before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open or close a scene by moving from a particular gesture to the social or historical situation that gives it meaning, then return to the immediate consequence. The narration should sound composed rather than like ordinary spoken language, but it must remain within the chosen narrator's knowledge and distance. Do not make every character speak in formal or archaic language; distinguish narration from dialogue.\n\nFrame high fantasy obligations through social context and history. A title, oath, relic, or institution should be understood through the way a household, court, or community treats it. The narration may explain why a promise made generations ago still binds the present, or how a magical power is regarded differently by those who fear it and those who depend on it. Keep the scale active in material consequences: a decision may affect land, succession, protection, or the survival of a community.\n\nUse the gathering sentence to connect personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor. A single sentence may move from a character's private hesitation to the way the event has been retold for years, then to the immediate choice that must be made. The retelling should remain anchored in a human consequence rather than becoming an abstract historical summary. Let recurring language and public judgment press on the scene without making the narrator omniscient.\n\nAllow ceremony, correspondence, or public and private contrast to organize a passage when the story supports it. A formal occasion may reveal what a character cannot say in private, or a letter may be read aloud and change the room's understanding. Do not impose an aristocratic setting or uniformly elaborate speech. The formal architecture is the style; the world and cast remain those actually supplied. The scene may end during an unfinished exchange or an unresolved decision, with the narration holding the moment open rather than forcing a conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nGive the current scene a definite teller, audience, occasion, and purpose. The teller may be a survivor, servant, heir, rival, or witness, but the tale must be told because someone in the outer story needs it now: to secure passage, justify a refusal, warn a ruler, comfort a child, or make a listener understand what an oath will cost. Let the audience's practical situation shape what the teller includes, postpones, or repeats.\n\nTreat the embedded tale as an active use of the world's magical and institutional weight. A story about a broken covenant, a named relic, a vanished order, or a ruler's old promise should change what the outer characters believe they may do and what they fear will happen. The tale does not merely explain the past; it makes a present obligation legible. When a listener interrupts, questions, or supplies a detail, use that exchange to show which part of the story matters to their own position.\n\nWrite the García Márquez movement by letting sentences gather particulars across time while returning to a human consequence. A sentence may move from a child's memory to a famine, a coronation, a rumor, and then back to the weight of a key in someone's hand. Keep the marvelous treated as part of ordinary social life where the high-fantasy world permits it: people may discuss a speaking relic's silence, a mountain's changed name, or a bloodline's recurring dream as matters of family and politics, not as isolated wonders.\n\nUse recurrence to connect the narrative levels. A phrase, object, gesture, or place name may appear in the outer scene and inside the tale, and its meaning should deepen rather than certify truth. For example, a repeated blessing may first sound ceremonial, then reveal a condition attached to an old alliance, then return in the outer scene as a warning the listener now understands. Do not require the same phrase at every difficulty.\n\nKeep the levels intelligible. Signal the movement into and out of the embedded tale through the teller's voice, the audience's attention, or a clear shift in scene, but do not stack more than two active story levels without a deliberate reason. The outer story remains the frame: return to it when the tale has changed a listener's understanding or a practical choice. The telling may be interrupted by weather, food, a messenger, or a listener's objection, and those interruptions should belong to the outer situation rather than merely decorating it.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the teller knew then and what the teller knows now. The tale may carry later understanding, but it should not grant the outer listeners knowledge they have not earned. A story can make a character decide to act, yet the action's consequences remain contingent on the actual scene and world facts. End the response at the natural boundary of the telling or its immediate effect, without requiring a completed bargain, confession, or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny route from the German-language tradition. Write in a continuous narrative voice, usually third person or focal first person, with syntax that can extend across clauses to hold a perception and its historical echo together. Let distance follow the focal character: the narration may stay close to one person's uncertainty while the world's larger institutions remain partly known through rumor, record, and inherited fear.\n\nTreat the high-fantasy world's magic, oaths, and ancient institutions as sources of uncanny pressure rather than as systems to be explained. A forest may remember a broken treaty, a family may inherit a recurring dream, a relic may make the air feel occupied, or an old oath may seem to answer before anyone speaks. These effects should remain consistent with the world's established magical facts; do not add supernatural events the setting excludes. The uncanny belongs to how the characters experience the world's real powers and obligations, not to a separate horror layer.\n\nAnchor the uncanny in García Márquez-style communal and familial memory. Let a sentence gather a personal detail, a public event, a rumor, and a material consequence: a woman's refusal to enter a chapel may connect to a battle fought before her birth, a changed boundary stone, and the way her son now hears his own name in the wind. Keep the movement returning to a human consequence, such as a debt, a marriage, a journey, or a silence between relatives.\n\nMake the uncanny socially situated. Characters should respond to strange occurrences through the ordinary practices of their world: prayer, negotiation, avoidance, record-keeping, family argument, or official inquiry. One person may treat a portent as a legal matter, another as a private grief, another as a political opportunity. This variety keeps the uncanny from becoming a uniform mood and shows how inherited power touches different lives.\n\nUse landscape and architecture as active memory. A road, hall, well, shrine, or border may carry the weight of an old event and affect present movement or decision. Describe it through the focal character's attention and the community's accumulated language about it, not through an inventory of national scenery. The place should feel specific to the supplied setting and its history.\n\nLet the uncanny create pressure without forcing a fixed outcome. A character may resist, misunderstand, accept, or attempt to bargain with an inherited obligation. The narration should render the private reasoning and sensation that accompany the choice, but the choice itself remains contingent on the actual scene and character. End at the natural boundary of the present action or realization, without requiring a completed confrontation or revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nUse the Gothic-Punk treatment as a way of rendering high fantasy's inherited power and present life in collision. Write the old authority as a living institution: a temple, guild, noble house, magical order, or ruling family whose ancient privileges still shape who may enter, speak, marry, trade, or be believed. Show the present world pressing against that authority through material detail: a patched robe over a ceremonial seal, a sacred hall used for storage, a young heir paying debts with a relic's name, a market built against a forbidden wall.\n\nMake intimacy morally complicated. Private desire, loyalty, fear, and ambition should not line up neatly with public allegiance. A character may love someone whose family holds an old claim over them, serve an order they no longer trust, or protect a person whose presence violates a covenant. Let these complications appear in private thought and in what characters reveal only in trusted company. Do not make every intimacy a betrayal or every kindness a trap.\n\nUse García Márquez-style gathering sentences to connect personal life, family memory, rumor, and public events. A sentence may move from a lover's hesitation to a grandmother's warning, a riot in the lower quarter, a priest's unfinished ledger, and back to the weight of a ring on a chain. Keep the movement anchored in a human consequence: a debt, a pregnancy, a promotion, a flight, a silence maintained for years.\n\nLet atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced. The same room may feel like sanctuary to one character and surveillance to another. Render that difference through sensory detail, memory, and the social meaning of objects rather than through a uniform mood. A candle's smoke, a locked archive, a worn step, or a half-heard hymn can carry the pressure of old authority without becoming a symbol in every scene.\n\nShow social masks and their limits. Characters may perform deference, piety, competence, or indifference in public, and the narration should distinguish that performance from private experience. In trusted company, a mask may fall away, but it need not fall completely or safely. Let the focal character notice another person's shift in voice, posture, or phrasing without making everyone perfectly perceptive.\n\nKeep the high-fantasy world's magic and obligations materially consequential. A relic, oath, title, or institution should change what a character can do and what a choice costs. Do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The horror and intimacy come from the world's own arrangements: an old law that still binds, a power that demands service, a family claim that outlives its original reason. End at the natural boundary of the present encounter or private realization, without requiring a completed confrontation, confession, or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route from the Indian literary traditions. Give the telling a definite teller, audience, occasion, and purpose within the supplied setting. The teller may be a bard, elder, survivor, servant, or rival, but the tale must be told because the outer story needs it now: to settle a dispute, prepare a heir, warn a ruler, explain a famine, or make a listener understand an inherited duty. Keep the language, region, community, and period specific when the scene supplies them; do not force English, code-switching, or a generic family structure onto the telling.\n\nShape the embedded tale as an epic account of lineage, covenant, and consequence. The tale should make high fantasy's magical power and inherited obligations legible through named ancestors, oaths, relics, institutions, and landscapes. A story about a king's broken promise, a river's changed course, a vanished order, or a family's recurring dream should change what the outer characters believe they may do and what they fear will happen. The tale does not merely explain the past; it makes a present obligation active.\n\nUse García Márquez-style gathering sentences to connect the tale's intimate cost to communal memory. A sentence may move from a child's hunger to a battle, a marriage, a rumor, and back to the weight of a key in someone's hand. Keep the movement anchored in a human consequence: a debt, a birth, a journey, a silence between relatives. Let the marvelous be treated as part of ordinary social life where the high-fantasy world permits it, so that people discuss a speaking relic's silence or a bloodline's recurring dream as matters of family and politics.\n\nUse recurrence to connect the narrative levels. A phrase, object, gesture, or place name may appear in the outer scene and inside the tale, and its meaning should deepen rather than certify truth. For example, a repeated blessing may first sound ceremonial, then reveal a condition attached to an old alliance, then return in the outer scene as a warning the listener now understands. Do not require the same phrase at every difficulty.\n\nKeep the levels intelligible. Signal the movement into and out of the embedded tale through the teller's voice, the audience's attention, or a clear shift in scene, but do not stack more than two active story levels without a deliberate reason. The outer story remains the frame: return to it when the tale has changed a listener's understanding or a practical choice. The telling may be interrupted by weather, food, a messenger, or a listener's objection, and those interruptions should belong to the outer situation rather than merely decorating it.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the teller knew then and what the teller knows now. The tale may carry later understanding, but it should not grant the outer listeners knowledge they have not earned. A story can make a character decide to act, yet the action's consequences remain contingent on the actual scene and world facts. End the response at the natural boundary of the telling or its immediate effect, without requiring a completed bargain, confession, or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tale route from the Japanese literary traditions. Present the high-fantasy world's magical power and inherited obligations as strange events that occur within ordinary social life and carry a definite personal or communal consequence. The narration may follow a focal character, a teller, or a witness, but the strange event should be rendered directly rather than explained away or treated as a mere symbol.\n\nLet personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. A servant's account may be plain and practical, a noble's account may be formal and guarded, a child's account may notice the wrong details. Do not impose a uniform quietness or a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery. Seasonal attention, omission, exuberance, direct feeling, and plain argument are available where appropriate to the character and scene.\n\nUse García Márquez-style gathering sentences to connect the strange event to family memory, rumor, and public events. A sentence may move from a woman's refusal to enter a shrine to a battle fought before her birth, a changed boundary stone, and the way her son now hears his own name in the wind. Keep the movement anchored in a human consequence: a debt, a marriage, a journey, a silence between relatives. Let the marvelous be treated as part of ordinary social life where the high-fantasy world permits it.\n\nMake the strange event socially observed. Characters should respond through the ordinary practices of their world: prayer, negotiation, avoidance, record-keeping, family argument, or official inquiry. One person may treat a portent as a legal matter, another as a private grief, another as a political opportunity. This variety keeps the fantastic from becoming a uniform mood and shows how inherited power touches different lives.\n\nUse omission and direct presentation together. The narration may leave some details unspoken while still showing what the focal character saw, heard, or felt. A strange event need not be fully explained, but its effect on a choice or relationship should be legible. Do not use omission to hide information the focal character would actually know, and do not grant the narration access to excluded minds.\n\nKeep the high-fantasy world's magic and obligations materially consequential. A relic, oath, title, or institution should change what a character can do and what a choice costs. The fantastic event should make that consequence more immediate, not replace it with a detached wonder. End at the natural boundary of the present encounter or realization, without requiring a completed confrontation, revelation, or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaham::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the marvelous-treated-as-ordinary route from the Latin American literary traditions. Write the high-fantasy world's magical power and inherited obligations as accepted parts of communal and family life. Characters may discuss a speaking relic's silence, a mountain's changed name, a bloodline's recurring dream, or an oath that still binds as matters of family, politics, and daily routine, not as isolated wonders. Do not add supernatural facts the world excludes; treat only the marvelous that the supplied setting already permits.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, community, and history when the scene supplies them. Do not manufacture a generic regional temperament or relocate the cast. The marvelous should feel specific to the supplied setting's institutions, landscapes, and inherited obligations, not to an imported familiar town or family.\n\nUse García Márquez-style gathering sentences to connect personal life, family memory, public events, rumor, and recurring language. A sentence may move from a child's hunger to a battle, a marriage, a rumor, and back to the weight of a key in someone's hand. Keep the movement anchored in a human consequence: a debt, a birth, a journey, a silence between relatives. Let the marvelous be part of that movement rather than a separate descriptive pause.\n\nShow the ordinary social treatment of the marvelous. A character may pay a tax on a haunted field, bargain with a spirit as with a neighbor, consult a priest about a recurring dream, or refuse to enter a room because of an old promise. These responses should reveal the world's institutions and the character's position within them. One person may treat a portent as a legal matter, another as a private grief, another as a political opportunity.\n\nUse layered time. The narration may move between a present scene, a family memory, a public event, and a rumor, but it should remain anchored in a human consequence. A character's present choice may be shaped by an old oath, a dead relative's promise, or a community's remembered disaster. Show how that past remains active in the present without turning the scene into a history lesson.\n\nKeep the high-fantasy world's magic and obligations materially consequential. A relic, oath, title, or institution should change what a character can do and what a choice costs. The marvelous-treated-as-ordinary route should make that consequence feel natural and unavoidable, not decorative. End at the natural boundary of the present action or realization, without requiring a completed confrontation, revelation, or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with the focal character's present position, the place, and the pressure that matters now: a summons, a debt, a relic's demand, an oath about to be tested, or a messenger arriving with news. Do not begin with a history lesson or a long description of the world. Let the high-fantasy setting's magic and obligations appear through what the character must do or decide in the scene.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When the character moves from a hall to a street, a shrine to a market, or a room to a courtyard, give a clear physical transition in a short sentence or clause. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. A paragraph may contain a quick movement, a spoken line, or a reaction, but do not let it blur them together.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when needed. A title, relic, oath, or magical institution should be introduced with enough concrete detail for the reader to understand its function and cost. For example, show what a seal permits, what a name obliges, or what a relic does when touched. Do not pause the scene for a full glossary entry.\n\nUse García Márquez-style gathering sentences in compressed form. A sentence may move quickly from a personal consequence to a family memory, a public event, or a rumor, then return to the present choice. For example: \"The debt had followed her mother from the burned temple to the new court, and now it sat on the table between her and the steward.\" Keep the movement anchored in a human consequence and short enough for fast reading.\n\nUse focused reaction beats for private experience. After a significant action, dialogue, or revelation, give the focal character's immediate response: a physical sensation, a memory, a doubt, a hope, or a shift in attention. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Do not make every character witty or every reaction a cliffhanger. First person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are not requirements of this container.\n\nKeep the high-fantasy world's magic and obligations materially consequential. A relic, oath, title, or institution should change what a character can do and what a choice costs. The fast-readable presentation should make that consequence immediate and legible, not reduce it to decoration. End at the natural boundary of the present action or reaction, without requiring a completed confrontation, revelation, or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild each lyrical movement from a present perception that carries an inherited obligation: a hand on a seal, a road kept clear by custom, a name spoken at a threshold. Let the sentence gather associated time from that perception, moving through family memory, rumor, or a public event, then return to the present object or gesture with its consequence altered. The return is the point of the construction; do not let the gathered history replace the scene.\n\nDevelop a comparison only when it changes what the narrator or focal character understands. A magical object may first be described through the ordinary thing it resembles, then through the history that makes the resemblance inadequate. Recurring sounds or images should accumulate meaning across a passage, so that a phrase first heard in rumor may return as private recognition. Keep the recurrence available rather than compulsory: if it returns, let the later occurrence revise its application.\n\nLet plain statements provide contrast. Not every sentence needs figuration, and characters should not all speak in the narrator's lyrical register. An oath may be rendered with formal gravity while the person who swore it speaks in ordinary practical terms. The account can end with the decision still unfolding, its consequence beginning to enter the memory of others rather than already declared legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the cost of an oath, relic, or ancient obligation become visible through small irreversible changes in things that were supposed to endure. A ceremonial object is lighter than it was; a name is now spoken with hesitation; a road passes a shrine no one tends; a festival keeps its date but has lost the family that once led it. The reflection should connect the present choice to what is no longer available: the person who first explained the vow, the confidence with which an institution once acted, the unbroken landscape before a magical catastrophe.\n\nUse remembered occasions as evidence of change rather than decorative nostalgia. A recollection may arrive during a practical task and return with a new implication for the decision at hand. Let a detail carry the association when possible: a worn inscription, a child's mispronunciation of an old title, a festival observed with fewer participants. Direct reflection is permitted, but it should arise from the scene and remain particular to the character's experience.\n\nPreserve ordinary pleasure where it belongs. A meal, a reunion, or a successful piece of work may remain satisfying even as the narrator notices what has altered. The account may close with the character continuing a task or conversation, aware of loss without being defined by it. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective; another character's private grief should not be narrated unless the contract permits it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral-storytelling route: the account is told by a situated teller to an audience within the world, and the frame remains active. The teller may be a family elder, a court reciter, a traveler, or another figure established by the scene, and the audience's interruptions, questions, or silences can shape what is told next. Keep the frame's occasion concrete: a night's halt, a household gathering, a debt being negotiated, a child asking about an old name.\n\nLet the tale gather personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor as a single retelling, but let the frame govern the gathering. A magical relic or ancient oath should enter through what people say it did and what the teller now believes it cost. Recurring phrases may mark the tale's structure, but they should acquire new application when a later event revises their meaning. Delayed revelation is legitimate: the teller may withhold a name or consequence until the moment when the audience, or the teller, can bear its weight. When a listener interrupts, let the interruption alter what the teller explains, repeats, or leaves unsaid.\n\nDistinguish the teller's public performance from the private experience of the characters in the tale. The teller may report what was said and done, but should not claim access to another mind unless the active contract permits it. The frame need not resolve every question. The telling may end with the audience dispersing, a listener repeating a phrase, or the teller pausing before the part that remains difficult to say. Do not import a particular historical court, religious framework, or honor code; use the world and community actually supplied.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small number of exact details to carry the scale of the fantasy world. One relic, one altered landscape feature, one remembered phrase can stand for an institution or an ancient event without a paragraph of history. Choose details that imply the larger obligation: a seal no longer pressed into wax, a road kept clear by custom, a child named for a broken treaty. Let the consequence appear in what a character does or refuses to do.\n\nKeep direct clauses and limited explanation. An essential inward connection may be stated once and left to work: the character knows what the oath cost, or remembers who taught the words, and the scene continues. Omission should leave the history inferable rather than delete causality. If a magical act matters, give its material result in the scene: the door opens, the crop fails, the name is refused. Do not explain its mechanism unless the present account requires it.\n\nUse recurring language sparingly as García Márquez's communal memory made economical. A phrase from rumor may return once in a character's thought or speech and thereby revise the scene. Ordinary social treatment of the marvelous can be rendered in a single calm clause: someone steps around the changed ground, or pays the old toll without comment. Cast voices remain individual: a formal oath may be spoken plainly, and a laborer need not speak like a chronicler. The account may end at a pause or continuation without a summarizing reflection.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which the fantasy world's scale enters through present action, dialogue, perception, and authorized inner experience. A character may carry a relic into a negotiation, hear a rumor while working, or recall an ancient event because a current choice makes it relevant. Let the recollection return to the scene rather than replace it. Titles, oaths, and institutions should change what people can do: a door opens or remains closed, a debt is called in, a name alters the tone of a conversation.\n\nMove naturally among action, speech, and thought. García Márquez's gathering sentences may appear when a perception opens into family or public memory, but the paragraph should remain anchored in a human consequence and then resume the scene. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery: a shift in place or time can be marked by a concrete detail, such as the changed light on a road or the weight of a carried object, rather than a summary of what has happened. The narrator's voice may be composed and flexible, but it should not make every character speak in the same register.\n\nTreat the marvelous with ordinary social attention when the world permits it. A magical occurrence may be noted as part of the day's business, with its strangeness registered through a character's hesitation or another's matter-of-fact response. Do not impose a contemporary setting, understated emotion, or a lesson at the end. The response may stop during continuing action, speech, thought, or composition, leaving the obligation still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise the lens through which the fantasy world's obligations are felt. Select scenes where an oath, relic, institution, or ancient event presses on the particular people involved: one must leave, one must inherit, one must keep a secret, one must decide whether to believe a rumor about the other. The world's scale should appear through what it costs them, not through exposition that pauses the relationship.\n\nBuild the relationship through private language and repeated moments. A phrase used in an early scene may return later with altered meaning because the characters have since faced a magical consequence or a family demand. When affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery belongs in a scene, let the fantasy obligation interrupt, delay, or alter the emotional exchange: a reunion is cut short by a summons, a confession is postponed by the arrival of a relic, a comfort is offered while one character still holds a sealed order. The emotional beat should remain legible even when the world's pressure prevents its completion.\n\nLet García Márquez's communal memory surround the relationship without overriding it. Family memory, public events, and rumor may explain why a gesture matters or why a name is difficult to say. Recurring language can mark the relationship's private history while remaining anchored in a human consequence. Keep private thought within the authorized perspective; the other character's mind is not automatically available. The response may end with the relationship still unresolved, its next consequence beginning rather than completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the sense of a retelling: open with a deliberate declaration or a compact statement of who someone was and what act will be remembered. Then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A recurring designation should come from an established deed, oath, kinship, or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application.\n\nLet oaths, relics, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds organize the telling when the world establishes them. Relate each act to what will be remembered by showing the consequence that entered the lives of those who remained: a gift that changed a household's standing, a journey that left a road named, an oath that bound a child not yet born. Keep the chronicle's authority distinct from a focal character's private account. Being remembered as oath-keeping does not mean the keeping was effortless or certain.\n\nAllow dialogue to interrupt the chronicle's elevation. The subject of an oft-told incident may correct a detail, answer a formal introduction with a practical concern, or speak in ordinary terms while the narrator preserves the weight of the occasion. This need not become parody. Keep the particular source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention; do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as a folktale-inflected communal chronicle grounded in social and domestic realism. Let a definite community voice, or a narrator close enough to that community to repeat its phrases, carry the telling. Move between the practical present and what people remember: a household task, a disputed boundary, a child's question, a rumor about an old oath, a public event that made a relic change hands. Keep each movement anchored in a human consequence. A sentence may gather years, but it should land on the specific person who must now answer for what was remembered.\n\nTreat the marvelous as an accepted social fact rather than a spectacle requiring explanation. If a sword remembers its last wielder, a road shortens for a particular traveler, or a dead queen's promise still binds her descendants, show the living arrangements around that fact: who is expected to tend it, who doubts it, who profits from it, and what ordinary courtesy or resentment it produces. The wonder does not need to be doubted into realism, but it must have material and relational effects.\n\nLet oaths, titles, relics, and ancient events enter through their present obligations. Do not present them as lore before they matter. A character may discover that a familiar saying records an actual condition, that an inherited name carries a a debt, or that a festival repeats a bargain the town no longer fully understands. Make the cost concrete: a household loses access to a resource, a marriage is forbidden, a journey becomes possible only for someone who accepts the old terms. Grandeur belongs to the situation, not to compulsory formal speech.\n\nUse recurrence as the chronicle's structural memory. A phrase, a season, a particular object, or a question may return with its meaning altered by intervening events. When it returns, do not merely restate it; let the new context show what has changed in the community's understanding or in the character's position. Repetition should deepen the history rather than restart the same scene.\n\nKeep the community specific. The chronicle may include domestic interiors, work, meals, local authority, and the way information travels through households and public gatherings. Do not make isolation, snow, or bleakness a universal setting. If the story's world includes a northern landscape, use only the particular conditions the story actually supplies. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contracts. A remembered event is not automatically known to every character, and a communal voice may repeat what people say without granting the narrator access to every private mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nWrite in scene units with concise action lines, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes. Let the audience learn high-fantasy obligations through what can be seen and heard: a sealed door that a character refuses to approach, a relic carried with too much care, a title spoken in a way that makes a room go quiet, an old banner repaired at the wrong season. Keep action lines concrete and readable. Do not fill the page with camera commands.\n\nUse the screenplay's scene changes to carry García Márquez's layered time. A cut can move from a present argument to the same room years earlier, from a public announcement to the private task it interrupts, or from a rumor repeated in a market to the household where the rumor has already changed someone's plans. Make the connection legible through a repeated object, phrase, or action rather than through a narrator's explanation. When a line recurs, let the new scene show how its meaning has altered.\n\nLet dialogue carry communal memory without becoming exposition. A character may repeat what the town says, quote a parent's warning, or answer a question with a story that has become too familiar to finish. Let the other speakers respond to the social fact of that memory: impatience, belief, correction, or the practical need to act. Do not make every character speak with the same formal gravity. Grandeur belongs to the situation and the selected telling, not to compulsory elevated speech for the whole cast.\n\nUse offscreen sound and voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. An offscreen bell, a voice from another room, or a remembered phrase may connect scenes without turning private thought into dialogue. When the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note set apart from action lines and dialogue, such as a brief block marked with the character's name and the note that it is not heard or seen. This preserves private experience without granting it to the cast. Do not use voice-over to replace the visible consequences of an oath or relic.\n\nLet a scene close after a completed action, a spoken consequence, or an unfinished setup. A decision may be made visible by what a character does next: accepting a burden, leaving an object behind, or refusing to repeat an oath. The screenplay style does not require a reversal, a reveal, or a completed plot turn in every scene. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nMake high-fantasy obligations present as stage facts. A relic may sit on a table where characters must decide whether to touch it. An oath may be repeated as part of a ceremony, a greeting, or a refusal. A title may change who enters first, who sits, or who is asked to leave. Let the physical handling of these objects and customs show their cost. If a character cannot cross a threshold, let that condition be visible in the staging; if a promise binds a family, let a practical choice in the scene reveal the binding.\n\nUse entrances and exits to shape communal memory. A character may arrive carrying news that everyone already half knows. Another may leave rather than hear a story repeated. A third may enter at the moment a name is spoken and change the room's attention. Let rumor enter through attributed speech and physical reaction: someone sets down a cup, someone else turns away, a third person asks the question the others were avoiding. Keep the memory anchored in the people present rather than in an invisible narrator.\n\nUse recurrence as a dramatic device. A phrase, object, or gesture may return in a later scene with its meaning altered. When it returns, let the new context show what has changed in the relationships or in the character's position. Do not restate the earlier moment as if it were new. Repetition should deepen the play's history rather than restart the same conflict.\n\nMonologues and asides need a dramatic purpose rather than replacing every private thought. When the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken authoring note set apart from dialogue and stage directions, such as a brief block marked with the character's name and the note that it is not heard or seen by the other characters. This preserves private experience without granting it to the cast. A performed aside or monologue has its own audience and permissions. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the audience has heard its formation.\n\nLet a scene close after a completed action, an entrance or exit, a pause, or an unfinished arrangement. The play does not need a revelation or a completed plot turn in every scene. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern braid together so that syntax and paragraph shape may follow the associations. Keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered, imagined, or feared. Do not make every character share a mind, and do not turn all scene facts indeterminate.\n\nLet high-fantasy obligation enter as lived private experience. A title may surface as a pressure in the chest when a name is spoken. A relic may return as the smell of the room where it was kept, the weight of a hand that once touched it, or a phrase the character repeats without deciding to speak. An oath may interrupt ordinary thought with the memory of the voice that first demanded it. Make the magical consequence physical and intimate: the mind may notice that a wound is healing too quickly, that a road is shorter than it should be, or that a promise has made a particular action feel impossible. Do not explain the magic as lore; let the character experience its effects.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time through association rather than chronology. A present sound may open a memory of a public event. A rumor heard in childhood may return while the character performs an adult task. A recurring phrase may carry different meanings at different moments. When the mind moves across time, anchor the movement in a human consequence: what the character now owes, fears, or can no longer ignore. Let the sentence gather detail, but land on the particular person whose body and choices are affected.\n\nAllow interruption and ordinary life to enter the mental stream. Another person's voice, a household task, a sudden pain, or a practical need may break an association and redirect attention. The character need not complete every thought. The mind may return to a concern later with new information or with the same unresolved feeling. Do not force every association to resolve into a decision or a completed insight.\n\nKeep private experience inside the authorized mind. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. If the active contract permits other minds, mark each shift of mind clearly and let each mind's associations remain distinct, so that the reader can tell whose memory, sensation, or concern is being followed. Do not blend multiple minds into a single undifferentiated stream. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contracts. A private interval does not need an external task or practical decision to justify its place.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one sender writes in fragments, another waits before answering, a third forwards a message without comment. Do not make every message perform the same formal gravity.\n\nLet high-fantasy obligation enter through what people transmit and withhold. A character may ask whether an heirloom has been moved, send a photograph of a sealed door, repeat an oath in a message that the recipient refuses to answer, or confirm a title with a delay that changes the exchange. Make the magical or institutional consequence legible through the communication itself: a message will not send until a condition is met, a receipt arrives from a place that should be empty, a sender quotes a family phrase that the recipient recognizes as a binding formula. Do not explain the magic as lore; let the exchange show its effects.\n\nUse García Márquez's communal memory through attributed rumor and recurring language. A message may repeat what the town says, quote a parent's warning, or ask about an event that everyone half remembers. Let the replies respond to the social fact of that memory: correction, impatience, belief, or the practical need to act. A recurring phrase may appear in different senders' messages with its meaning altered by context. When it returns, let the new exchange show what has changed rather than restating the earlier moment.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. When the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, write it as a clearly separated context block outside the exchange, marked as not transmitted, so that it is not knowledge available to the other senders. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet the exchange close after a completed message, a meaningful delay, a pending question, or an unfinished setup. The transcript does not need a reversal, a reveal, or a completed plot turn in every reply. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in scene-oriented units with concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nLet high-fantasy obligation enter through what the focal character notices in the scene. A background detail may show a relic kept in the wrong place, a banner mended at an unusual season, or a threshold that no one crosses. An object may be handled with too much care or left untouched. A title may change the way another character pauses before speaking. Make the magical or institutional consequence legible through these observable details and the focal character's reaction to them. Do not explain the magic as lore before it matters.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time through scene changes and recurring visual details. A later scene may return to a familiar room, object, or gesture with its meaning altered by intervening events. When a detail returns, let the new context show what has changed in the focal character's understanding or in the relationships. A phrase may also recur in dialogue, but let the scene show its new weight rather than restating the earlier moment.\n\nLet focal narration carry communal memory without turning every line into exposition. The focal character may remember what the town says, recognize a family phrase, or connect a public event to a private consequence. Keep the memory anchored in the present scene: what the character now owes, fears, or can no longer ignore. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. When the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note set apart from scene-setting and dialogue, so that it is not knowledge available to the cast.\n\nUse background or expression cues as purposeful references, not automatic truth indicators. A changed expression may attract attention, but a contradiction in an arrangement or account must do the interpretive work. Let a scene close after a completed action, a spoken consequence, or an unfinished setup. The visual novel style does not require a reversal, a reveal, or a completed plot turn in every scene. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahahbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / García Márquez / Web Serial\n\nWrite installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources.\n\nLet high-fantasy obligation develop across installments. A relic may be mentioned early as an ordinary object and later reveal its binding condition. An oath may be quoted in one installment and then shown changing a practical choice in another. A title may be spoken without explanation and then become the reason a character is refused entry or given responsibility. Make the magical and institutional consequences cumulative: each installment may add a particular fact that changes what the earlier detail meant. Do not explain the magic as lore before it matters, and do not make every installment introduce a new wonder.\n\nUse García Márquez's layered time through recurring language and memory across installments. A phrase, object, or public event may return with its meaning altered by intervening developments. When it returns, briefly recover the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole history. Let the new context show what has changed in the character's position or in the community's understanding. Repetition should deepen the serial's history rather than restart the same scene.\n\nLet communal memory enter through attributed rumor and ordinary social life. A character may repeat what the town says, quote a parent's warning, or answer a question with a story that has become too familiar to finish. Let the present scene respond to the social fact of that memory: impatience, belief, correction, or the practical need to act. Keep the memory anchored in a human consequence. Grandeur belongs to the situation and the selected telling, not to compulsory formal speech for the whole cast.\n\nKeep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. Write it as part of the installment's focal narration, or as a clearly attributed nonspoken note when the active contract requires hidden interiority. A private interval does not need an external task or practical decision to justify its place. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. Let an installment close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, world facts, and output contracts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the obligations of the high fantasy world as a living pressure inside intimate exchanges. A compact line may carry the same weight as an elaborate declaration: let a title, oath, or inherited debt enter the room as a named thing someone cannot answer, and then show that burden in what a character will not say.\n\nUse Gu Long's white space to make emotional exposure partial and costly. A character may ask for something, receive silence, and act on that silence; another may apologize without expecting forgiveness. Follow the actual behavior that results: a gesture held too long, an object set down with too much care, a sentence rehearsed and never spoken. Keep the narrator's distance appropriate to the active contract, but let the focal character's private reasoning or sensation carry the unresolved hurt rather than narrating it as a diagnosis.\n\nDelay the recognition of how much has changed until the scene has earned it. What felt like a simple refusal can become a larger betrayal when a later obligation recontextualizes an earlier lie. Do not require the recognition to complete: a character may remain uncertain whether the apology was genuine. Warmth can coexist with pain, but comfort need not repair the relationship or remove the magic, oath, or risk still in force. Preserve character agency and actual world facts; the turbulence is a treatment of the scene, not a scripted emotional collapse.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Chat Roleplay\n\nKeep each response inside the shared scene with clear attribution for every spoken line and brief, readable action beats. Let a high fantasy obligation appear as an object, title, debt, or magical condition that one character mentions or handles, then leave its consequences to their next action. Do not pause the scene to explain the world; show its weight through dialogue and physical response.\n\nUse Gu Long's compression so that a short retort or an unanswered question changes the available options. What a character notices but does not say may be rendered through a small movement, a turn of attention, or a deliberate silence, with no forced internal confession. Ensure the narrator and action markers follow the separate roleplay settings: if first person is active, keep knowledge limited to that speaker; if third person is active, keep each mind distinct.\n\nEnd the response at a natural place in the exchange, not with a compulsory cliffhanger or a demand for a player choice. The next reply may come from a changed mood, a new arrival, or the material results of the previous words. An oath, spell, or inheritance should remain in force across turns, so that the interruption created by the next speaker is a continuation of the same world rather than a reset.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the martial-adventure route: an episodic encounter shaped by compact exchanges, abrupt turns of attention, and the weight of pledged history. Let each scene begin inside a concrete obligation—an oath, a magical debt, a summons—and use the encounter to reveal what that bond costs the person who carries it.\n\nUse Gu Long's short declarative progression so that a gesture, a pause, or a deliberately omitted explanation does more work than a long account. Distilled images should carry the high fantasy material: a blade laid across a treaty, a seal pressed into wax, a broken rune in a doorway. What the point-of-view character understands may be limited; what the narration states must respect the active narrator and contract.\n\nEnd the episode where the encounter's revelation lands rather than where the problem is solved. A later episode may revisit a familiar place or object with changed meaning, but do not import a signature book setting or cast. Keep the language concrete and the world's magical institutions active in the choices characters make, without converting the whole culture into generic honor talk.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
