            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-high_fantasy-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · High Fantasy · 03/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == high_fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip High Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-high_fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPahaiad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nMake the high fantasy world tactile and costly. Use forceful, concrete verbs and specific materials—cold iron, cramped stairs, a wax seal split by frost—so that an ancient obligation arrives as a physical fact. Objects bear the weight of oaths, spells, or institutional authority; when something breaks or fails, the damage matters to later choices.\n\nKeep movement economical. Move between actions in short declarative strokes, and let a gap stand for what a character cannot face or explain. High contrast can serve the scene: warm light against frost, a clean line of a map against a soiled cuff. Irregular rhythm is allowed; a longer thought may run when the character's reasoning needs it, but it must remain concrete and tied to the immediate situation.\n\nGround abrasive speech in its speaker and station. A bitter soldier, an overworked clerk, and a debtor queen speak forthrightly for different reasons, and their rough words should reflect knowledge and position, not an undefined temper. The pressure does not need to worsen every paragraph or trap every character; let the selected scene carry its established danger or relief. Use the active narrator's perspective without adding an omniscient report of every wound.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Epistolary / Documents\n\nLet each section be a document with a definite author, purpose, and moment of composition. A treaty, a record, or a letter may mention a magical oath or institution only as the writer knows it; what is omitted or misremembered is part of the telling. Preserve the document's actual form: a report keeps its headings and practical limits, a letter keeps its salutation and private concern, a journal keeps its sequence and uncertainty.\n\nUse gaps between documents as Gu Long uses white space. An event that one record mentions in passing may become the central revelation of a later record written by someone with different access. Do not add an omniscient voice after each document; a conflicting account should stand without commentary, while material details like a torn edge, a different hand, or an interrupted sentence may imply what no one states.\n\nKeep high fantasy consequences concrete through what the papers witness and direct. A budget note can record the cost of a summoning; a letter can refuse a promised heir; a report can mention a boundary stone that now glows at night. The sequence should advance through the writers' actions and knowledge, not through a neutral narrator explaining how all the documents fit together. What remains unresolved may stay unresolved until a later document or a directly depicted scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nUse the high fantasy world as the context for specific, earned care. A familiar preference can appear in the practical texture of the scene: bread still warm despite the siege, a healing poultice mixed in a particular order, a space kept clear in a crowded archive. Let the affection be shown through concrete action and observation rather than declared at length.\n\nKeep Gu Long's compression so that a small gesture may carry the comfort. One spoken word or a silent adjustment can matter more than a paragraph of reassurance. Follow the actual relationship: people may disagree, keep secrets, or be afraid without canceling the care they give. If a character needs privacy or refuses comfort, that refusal remains part of the scene, not a demand for immediate repair.\n\nDo not remove high fantasy's obligations, magic, or danger. A warm moment can occur during travel through a dangerous pass, in the lull of a ritual, or after resolving one part of a larger debt. The continuation should show the world still moving around the characters—a bell, a patrol, weather, a coming audience—so the comfort is anchored in the continuing story rather than sealing the scene off from consequences.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraphs that can move from a private exchange to its institutional shadow. Use the narrator's established distance to show how a public ceremony, treaty, or family expectation frames the present choice, while keeping the focal experience immediate. Magic and history should appear as terms within the social order—an oath with legal force, a relic carrying documented authority—not as decorative atmosphere.\n\nLet Gu Long's compression work inside the larger sentence movements. A clause may state one fact and then stop, allowing the next clause to disclose a consequence; an image may close a paragraph without summary. Use white space to separate scenes or to mark a character's silence within an otherwise formal exchange. Keep the diction composed but grounded, so that ceremony is represented through what it changes rather than through archaism.\n\nPreserve the public/private contrast. A moment of tenderness or refusal may be rendered in shorter clauses against a backdrop of ceremonial language or civic procedure. The narrator may frame a scene through historical knowledge when the active contract permits, but must not announce a private thought the viewpoint could not have. Keep obligations binding, and let the paragraph structure carry the passage's pace without repackaging high fantasy as a costume drama.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as a present occasion with a specific audience and a reason the telling matters now. The storyteller may be a survivor, envoy, heir, servant, or rival, but the tale should act on the listeners: it can bind them to an oath, reveal what an old victory cost, or make a refusal newly expensive. Let the listeners' names, titles, and obligations enter the room before the tale begins.\n\nInside the embedded story, keep the high-fantasy scale concrete. A relic, treaty, curse, or ancient defeat should alter what a person could do, not decorate the account. Compose each embedded scene as a Gu Long movement: state one action or image briefly, leave a silence, and let the next detail imply what the teller will not say. The teller's loyalty should shape the account through selection and emphasis. A generous version of a dead lord can be sincere while omitting the teller's own failure; a hostile version can be accurate about events and wrong about motive.\n\nMove between levels through recurrence rather than explanation. A phrase, object, or gesture may appear in the tale and again in the outer scene, changing its meaning when the listener recognizes it. Delay one revelation until the outer scene can use it: the teller may withhold the name of the survivor, the price of the oath, or the reason the relic was not destroyed. Let the outer listeners interrupt, question, or fall silent, and let their response complete the implication the embedded scene left open.\n\nDo not make the embedded story a sealed moral. It should leave the outer scene with a live obligation, a contested memory, or a choice the listener must now answer. The telling may end without a completed bargain or a resolved dispute. Keep the frame intelligible: signal each return to the outer scene through a concrete detail, not a heading or summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny route. Treat the high-fantasy past as an active presence that disturbs the present rather than a completed chronicle. An old oath, ruined fortress, inherited title, or magical boundary should surface through perception: a sound that repeats at the wrong hour, a name that changes the temperature of a room, a landscape that seems to remember the army that crossed it. Keep the uncanny located in particular objects, places, and obligations, not in a general mood.\n\nLet Gu Long's compression shape the narration as an implication sequence. State the ordinary fact first, then the detail that makes it strange, and leave the connection unspoken. The next action or silence carries the implication: a character notices that the well rope is wet though no one drew water, and then chooses whether to draw the water anyway. Do not explain the uncanny event immediately; let the following gesture or refusal show what the character has understood without saying it.\n\nConnect the disturbance to inherited obligation. The uncanny should make a present decision harder: a claimant may feel the old boundary resist her, a messenger may hear the siege bell when he considers breaking his oath, or a steward may find the relic warm on the anniversary of its forging. The magical or ancient force need not speak or appear; its effect on perception and choice is enough.\n\nKeep narrative distance close to a focal character's experience, but let the prose remain composed and declarative. Use longer sentences for landscape and memory, then shorten sharply for the uncanny detail or the character's reaction. Do not require the whole cast to share the same perception. One person may feel the disturbance while another sees only an ordinary room, and that difference can become a source of uncertainty rather than proof.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake the collision of old authority and present life concrete. A high-fantasy institution, title, relic, or magical debt should press on a particular urban or domestic setting: a tenement built over a sealed vault, a noble house now divided into rented rooms, a guild oath enforced by a modern clerk who does not believe in it. Use material detail to show the pressure: worn stone under new paint, a relic kept in a locked drawer beside unpaid bills, a formal title spoken only when someone wants a favor.\n\nLet social masks be part of the scene's information flow. Characters may perform deference, indifference, or loyalty in public, then drop the performance in trusted company. Use Gu Long's compact exchanges to make the shift visible without commentary: a short answer in the corridor, a longer silence in the private room, a single question that reveals what the public mask concealed. The dropped mask need not produce a confession; it may only show exhaustion, desire, or fear.\n\nKeep the horror personal rather than cosmic. The inherited power should threaten a specific relationship, home, body, or livelihood. A character may discover that the family seal still binds her signature, that the old well under the kitchen has begun to sing, or that a lover's kindness is also a form of control. Do not explain the magical or ancient force fully. Let its effect appear through what people avoid saying and what they touch.\n\nUse atmosphere to change how a particular encounter is experienced, not to replace action. A room can feel close because the windows were bricked over after the last heir died; a conversation can turn dangerous because the rain stops at the wrong moment. Keep the prose readable and direct, with short paragraphs around dialogue and private response. End where the present pressure is felt most sharply, without requiring a resolution or a completed confrontation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. The outer scene should have a situated teller and listeners who belong to the same web of lineage, oath, or institution. The tale may be requested at a succession, a marriage negotiation, a funeral feast, or a council where an old claim must be settled. Let the occasion make the telling consequential: the account of an ancestor's broken oath can decide who may inherit, who must pay, or who may enter a forbidden place.\n\nInside the tale, treat names, genealogies, and ancient events as active forces. A name may carry a debt; a marriage may have bound two houses to a magical consequence; a battlefield may still demand a ritual answer. Use Gu Long's brevity to keep the epic material from becoming a catalogue. State one deed or image, leave a silence, and let the next detail imply the cost. The teller's selection should reveal loyalty: a lineage account may honor one branch while quietly omitting a bastard heir or a failed campaign.\n\nDelay one revelation until the outer scene can use it. The teller may withhold the name of the true heir, the reason a relic was buried, or the exact wording of an oath. When the outer listeners recognize the omission, the tale changes their present position. Let a repeated phrase, object, or name accrue meaning across the levels: a sword passed through three tellings, a title spoken differently by two generations, a river that marks both a victory and a curse.\n\nDo not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. Keep the language, region, community, and period as supplied by the story. The telling may end with a live obligation or contested memory rather than a completed settlement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaial::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic-tale route. Let the high-fantasy past enter the present through strange, often unexplained encounters: a traveler meets a woman who knows his grandfather's oath, a shrine gate appears on a road that did not exist before, a sword left in a river returns to its owner's door. Treat the encounter as a real event in the scene, not a dream or allegory, but do not explain its full mechanism. The magical force may act through a single object, place, or person, and its purpose may remain partly obscure.\n\nKeep Gu Long's compression in charge of the telling. State the ordinary detail first, then the strange one, and leave the connection unspoken. A character may notice that the stranger's sleeves are dry in the rain, or that the returned sword is warm though the night is cold. Let the next action or silence carry the implication: the character may accept the sword, step back from the gate, or continue walking without naming what has happened. Do not let the character immediately interpret the event; uncertainty can persist through the scene.\n\nConnect the fantastic encounter to an inherited obligation. The strange event should make a present decision harder: a messenger may meet the ghost of the lord who sent him, a daughter may receive her mother's lost mirror on the day she plans to refuse a marriage, or a soldier may find the battlefield bell ringing though the war is over. The encounter need not command or threaten; it may only make the obligation impossible to ignore.\n\nUse seasonal or environmental attention where it sharpens the scene, but do not require a universal quietness or a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery. Keep personal language and narrative distance appropriate to the actual teller and the supplied world. The scene may end while the strange event remains unresolved, with the character still deciding what it means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the marvelous treated as ordinary where the supplied canon permits. Let magical power, ancient events, and inherited obligations be part of daily life rather than intrusions. A family may pay a tithe to the mountain that remembers their ancestor's betrayal; a clerk may file a petition to a dead queen's tribunal; a child may be named for a treaty so that the treaty will not wake. The marvel should appear in practical, material terms: a debt, a journey, a household task, a legal form.\n\nKeep the narration direct and unastonished. State the marvelous fact as part of the scene's ordinary information, then let Gu Long's compression create the tension. A character may mention that the house has been angry since the will was read, and the next line may show a door closing on its own. Do not pause to explain or justify the marvel. The characters know it; the prose should know it too.\n\nDelay the revelation that changes the marvel's meaning. A familiar magical condition may turn out to be a punishment, a protection, or a claim. The grandmother's ghost who visits every Sunday may be guarding the deed, not mourning; the rain that follows the heir may be the old king's vote, not a blessing. Let the revelation arrive through a single image or exchange that alters the preceding stillness.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, community, and history as supplied. Do not manufacture a generic regional temperament or add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them. The scene may end with the character accepting or resisting the newly understood obligation, without a completed resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaian::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with a concrete present action or exchange that already carries the high-fantasy obligation: a summons delivered, a relic changing hands, a title spoken in the wrong place, a door that will not open for the new heir. Establish the magical or ancient fact through what it does in the scene, not through a history lecture. If a name, title, or mechanic is unfamiliar, give a brief clear presentation at the moment it becomes relevant.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When characters move, show the transition in a short concrete beat: the corridor narrows, the courtyard light changes, the vault door swings back. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. Let Gu Long's compact exchanges carry the tension: a short question, a shorter answer, a silence, then a detail that changes what the silence meant.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to make the obligation personal. After a significant action or line, give the focal character's private response in a brief interior beat: a memory of the oath, a sensation of the relic's weight, a calculation of what refusal would cost. Let the reaction end on an implication rather than a summary: the character may set the relic down, turn toward the door, or answer with a single word that leaves the next move unstated. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Do not make every character witty or every pause a cliffhanger.\n\nDelay one revelation until the scene can use it. The heir may discover that the relic is already attuned to someone else; the messenger may realize the summons was written before the war ended. Let the revelation arrive through a concrete detail or a single line, then end where the character feels its weight most sharply. The scene may stop during continuing action, thought, or speech without requiring a completed confrontation or a changed plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild each paragraph as a movement of attention rather than a report. Begin with a brief declarative observation of something concrete in the scene: the cold of a blade, the worn edge of a seal, the way a name changes the room. Let the syntax extend that observation through association until it touches the obligation, history, or magical consequence at stake, then break the rhythm with a short plain sentence that restores the immediate moment. The lyric movement belongs to the focal character's attention or the narrator's authorized associations, not to every speaker's dialogue. A guard may answer in practical terms while the narration lingers on the dust over the threshold.\n\nUse recurring sounds or images to carry what the dialogue does not state. A relic, oath, title, or landscape should be introduced as a concrete presence whose consequence arrives later. Return to that image in a new syntactic shape when a later fact changes its meaning: the same cold blade may become the cost of an oath, the same worn seal may become the proof of a broken succession. The withheld fact remains active through this recurrence rather than through explanation. A comparison may unfold only when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present; otherwise let the plain statement stand.\n\nUse white space as a structural device. End a paragraph at the moment a perception shifts, then open the next with a different surface detail whose relation to the previous one is felt before it is explained. A revelation may arrive as a single clause that alters the meaning of an earlier image, and the prose should allow that alteration to register without immediately summarizing its consequences. Preserve the fantasy world's causal facts: magic and ancient events remain real and knowable, but the telling may delay the character's understanding of them. The response may end while that understanding is still forming.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present detail open the memory without announcing it. The character may notice a cracked seal, a patched banner, or a chair left empty at a table, and the narration may follow that detail only far enough to show what has changed. Keep the remembered occasion concrete: a specific departure, a particular oath, the last time a room was full. Do not let the reflection become a general meditation on time or loss. Distinguish what the character experiences now from what is no longer available; the narration may hold both in the same sentence, but the present scene keeps its own size and the past remains a particular occasion rather than a mood.\n\nUse Gu Long's brevity to make the reflection feel earned. A short exchange can precede the memory, and a shorter one can interrupt it. The character may decline to explain what they remember, or answer a practical question while the narration holds the association. What remains unsaid should be inferable from the detail and the situation, not replaced by a summary of feelings. The fantasy world's scale should appear through the memory's specific content: an oath sworn before a war, a relic given before a departure, an institution that changed after a particular death. Grandeur belongs to the remembered situation, not to ceremonial speech in the present scene.\n\nAllow ordinary pleasure and present obligation to coexist with the memory. The character can enjoy the warmth of a cup, the sound of a familiar voice, or the completion of a small task without the prose forcing the moment to curdle into loss. A delayed revelation may arrive through the memory itself: the character understands something about the old oath or the absent person only now, and the scene may end before that understanding changes any visible action. Preserve the fantasy world's facts and the character's actual knowledge; later understanding does not rewrite what they knew earlier.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the concrete route. A situated teller recounts a high fantasy matter: an oath, a relic, a journey, or an ancient event whose consequences still reach the present. Keep the frame brief and specific. The teller may be a traveler, a servant, a survivor, or a keeper of records, but their audience and occasion should be present in the scene. The frame is not a decorative introduction; it establishes what the teller chooses to say and what they leave out. Let the occasion shape the telling: a story told to a child omits one thing, a story told to a rival includes another, and a story told to buy time may break off at a different place.\n\nApply Gu Long's brevity inside the told account. The teller speaks in compact declarations, names a few exact details, and pauses where the meaning should gather. A gap in the account is a deliberate feature: the teller may omit a motive, a name, or the worst part of an event, and the frame may show why. The audience's reaction can carry the uncertainty without the narration explaining the omission. What the teller notices in the room may interrupt the story and change its course. Keep the fantasy world's scale present through the told matter itself: the oath had a cost, the relic had power, the ancient event still reaches the present. The teller's partial knowledge does not make the world's causality uncertain, only the account incomplete.\n\nLet the delayed revelation belong to the telling. A detail mentioned early in the account may return near the end with a different weight, and the teller may reveal it through a single sentence that alters what the audience understood. The frame may show the audience still deciding what to believe, or the teller declining to finish. Do not require the teller to be ancient, mystical, exiled, or bound by a single honor code. The response may end with the story unfinished or the audience still weighing the account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and let each one carry a consequence. A relic should be present as a specific object: a chipped seal, a blade with one name scratched out, a door that has not been opened since a particular oath. Choose the detail that changes what a character can do or what a decision costs, and place it in the scene so that its consequence is felt through the immediate action. Do not describe the object's full history; let the reader infer the rest from what the character does with it or refuses to do with it.\n\nUse direct clauses and brief declarative movement. Dialogue should be compact, and what a character declines to say should leave an inferable gap rather than an empty one. If a character refuses to answer, the surrounding detail or the next action should show enough for the reader to understand what is at stake. Keep only the inward connection that materially contributes: a single thought about the cost of an oath may be essential, and it may be long enough to make sense, but do not add a second thought that restates it. Let the fantasy world's scale appear through the detail's consequence rather than through exposition: the chipped seal matters because of what it permits or forbids, not because the narration explains its origin.\n\nDelay the revelation through selection rather than through withheld exposition. An early detail may seem incidental, then return in a later sentence with its significance made plain by a new fact. The revelation should change the preceding stillness: one clause can show that the character understood, or that the situation has altered. Preserve causality and the fantasy world's facts. Magic and ancient events remain real, but the prose reports only the portion of them that the present scene requires. The response may end after the revelation without summarizing its consequences.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaias::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which the fantasy world's scale is present through the immediate situation. An oath, relic, institution, or ancient event should appear as part of the scene's practical life: a messenger arrives with a seal, a room is arranged around a forbidden name, a character's errand crosses the boundary of an old obligation. Let the narration move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience, but keep each movement grounded in the present moment. A recollection should arise from something the character perceives or says now, and it should return to the scene rather than replacing it.\n\nPreserve Gu Long's compact exchanges inside the continuous prose. Dialogue should remain brief and declarative, and a character may decline to explain something while the narration supplies only what the viewpoint can perceive or already knows. The gap is not filled by a summary. A delayed revelation may arrive as a single sentence that changes the meaning of an earlier detail, and the prose should let the scene register that change without immediately resolving it. Use paragraphing to create the white space that Gu Long achieves through omission: a short paragraph after a long one can mark a shift in understanding, and a break can stand where another writer would add explanation.\n\nDo not let the contemporary presentation flatten the fantasy world into realism or require understated emotion. Magic and ancient events remain real and causally active, and the characters may feel the weight of them directly. The narration should not announce grandeur; it should show the cost through a particular loyalty, a material consequence, or a choice made under an inherited obligation. The response may end during continuing action, thought, or speech, with the scene's next development still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through the specific obligations and objects of the high fantasy world. A shared oath, a relic one character gave the other, a title that separates them, or an institution that forbids their alliance should appear in the scene as something that changes what they can say or do. Use these world facts as the material for private language: a phrase from the oath, a nickname tied to a particular event, or a gesture associated with a promise. Develop only the actual supplied premise; do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. Let the private language arise from what the characters have actually shared, and let a repeated moment gain new meaning when a later fact changes what it signified.\n\nUse Gu Long's brevity to give the relationship's emotional beats their force. Affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery should be shown through compact exchanges, a few exact details, and what a character notices but does not say. A delayed revelation can change the meaning of an earlier moment between them: a word repeated from the past, a reason finally understood, or a consequence of an old choice now made plain. Let the payoff arrive through that change rather than through extended confession. The narration may show one character's inner experience where authorized, but the other character's knowledge remains separate; what one understands is not automatically what the other perceives.\n\nGive the relationship enough space where it belongs. A scene may linger on a small domestic act, a shared silence, or a moment of care without forcing it to advance the plot. At the same time, the fantasy world's scale should remain causally present: the relationship is shaped by the oath or institution, and a decision about the relationship may carry a material cost. The response may end with the emotional situation still unresolved, a promise kept or strained, or one character understanding something the other has not yet said.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the sense of being retold through deliberate openings and compact declarations. Begin with a sentence that establishes the deed, oath, or person as something already known: a name, a place, or an act that the chronicle has kept. Then move into the particular scene without inflating it. The fantasy world's scale should be present through the relation of an act to what will be remembered: a broken oath, a gift that changed a succession, a journey whose end is still told differently. Keep the telling brief enough that the white space does the work of memory.\n\nUse recurring phrasing only when a later occurrence gives it a new application. A phrase from an oath may return when the oath is tested; a designation earned by a deed may be repeated when the person acts against it. Do not assign ornamental epithets to every entrance. Let dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority: a character may correct a detail, answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern, or refuse to be remembered as the account remembers them. This need not become parody, but it keeps the chronicle from turning every speaker into a ceremonial voice. The chronicle's version and the character's own account may differ without one being declared false.\n\nDelay the revelation through the chronicle's own structure. An early declaration may seem complete, then a later scene shows what it omitted. The revelation should change the preceding stillness: a single sentence can reveal that the remembered deed was not what the account claimed, or that the oath bound someone in a way no one said aloud. Preserve the fantasy world's facts and the teller's partial knowledge. The account may end with the act still being weighed, another modest deed available to be remembered, or the chronicle's version left uncertain without declaring the whole tradition false.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social-domestic route through this telling. The oath, the relic, and the ancient institution arrive through the people who administer them in an ordinary household, office, or parish: a border office keeps the register of a watch sworn before the mountains woke; a steward measures grain against a tithe no one living has seen paid in full; a family resumes dinner around the chair kept for an heir serving a hundred-year term. Render the obligation as today's practical matter — what must be signed, fed, repaired, witnessed, or refused before dark, and who must stand behind the refusal.\n\nWrite in brief declarative movement. State what a person does or sees in short sentences, then let the gap stand. The register is older than the office keeping it — a fact like that can be noticed and not explained. A withheld signature, a chest left locked, a name spoken too late, a task finished in silence carry the oath's cost better than any speech about ancient history. Keep inward experience in the same clipped register: a suspicion counted like coin, a name not said aloud, the reason for a refusal kept as the character's own. A person may decline to explain what they know; let that silence leave a legible uncertainty grounded in the scene's practical facts.\n\nKeep the realism local to the world actually supplied. Render the room, the work, the wages, the kinship, and the institutional habits as one concrete community; where the setting distinguishes regions or traditions, keep their particulars distinct instead of blending a generic North, and do not add reserve, snow, isolation, or bleakness the world does not contain. Let the quiet of ordinary work accumulate its weight. When a withheld fact or action surfaces — the heir has already returned, the tithe was never owed — let it re-read what the quiet meant rather than swap the scene's subject for a new one. The scene may equally end inside the unfinished work, with the cost still settling on someone's hands.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Screenplay\n\nWrite the sequence with a scene heading whenever location or time changes, then concise action lines in which the obligation is visible as objects and work. A sealed charter on a clerk's table. A bell rope in a hall where an old promise is still answered. A gate closed against a messenger who has not left. Keep each action line short and declarative — what a person does, what is there to be seen — and let the setting and the characters' movements carry the oath's weight instead of explanatory dialogue. Do not fill the page with camera commands; the scene's own geometry does that work.\n\nGive exchanges clear speaker cues and compact lines. A single sentence, a refusal, or a question that opens a gap can answer a longer speech. Where the ancient history or the relic's meaning wants explaining, dramatize its present cost instead: a place set for someone who will not come, a guard stepping aside without being ordered, wages counted out to the wrong household. Silence between lines is playable screen time; let beats of it stand. Choose a cut, an offscreen sound, or a held quiet room only when the moment needs it, not as decoration.\n\nLet the visible work accumulate stillness while the significant implication stays unspent. When it surfaces, give the screen something to show or hear: the seal already broken when the charter is turned over, the gate opening after the long silence, a name called from offscreen. The change should re-read what came before rather than open an unrelated question. The sequence may end on that change, inside the motion it starts, or with the matter still pending — and the held implication may equally stay held past this reply's edge. If the active contract includes private thought, keep it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the presentation rather than turning it into spoken lines; voice-over, where used at all, is a separate choice the sequence earns.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Stage Play\n\nStage the obligation as business an audience could follow from the seats. A character enters carrying a sealed box and sets it on the table. Another leaves without touching the sword laid across the threshold. A servant folds a cloak no one has worn in a generation and puts it back where it was. Keep stage directions economical but exact about position and reach: who can see the door, who stands between the relic and the hand reaching for it, who waits rather than sits. The old promise lives in where people put their bodies and their things. No film-camera instructions; the stage holds its own sightlines.\n\nLet dialogue stay compact and speaker-led. Short lines answering the immediate task — the household's work, the institution's demand, the visitor at the gate — rather than the oath's full history. A question can be answered with a movement. Give pauses a business reason: the register is being read aloud, the box is being carried, no one speaks until the bell stops. Where the contract calls for private experience, keep it clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation; an aside or monologue is performed speech with its own audience, used for a dramatic purpose, not a disguised substitute for thought.\n\nLet the stillness build through the business while a significant implication is held back. When it lands, make it stageable: the box turned to show the broken seal, the expected entrance that does not happen, the heir's name spoken for the first time, the kept chair discovered occupied. The change should re-read the quiet rather than replace it. The scene can stop on the action itself, on the motion it begins, or with a question still hanging in the room; the held implication need not be spent within this reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Stream of Consciousness\n\nTell the scene through one authorized mind, in an associative movement that braids perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and the concern that keeps returning. Make the inherited obligation one of the returning pressures rather than a block of history: the smell of the sealed room, the weight of the ring not worn, a name heard once in a dream and again at the gate, the ache in a hand that once held the relic. The oath's scale shows in what this mind cannot stop noticing and what it refuses to revisit.\n\nInside the flow, keep the units brief and declarative. A perception can be stated flatly — the door was open, the chair was empty, the oath was still there — and then the mind moves: to the last time the words were spoken, to the errand this thought interrupts, to a private word said to no one. Do not smooth every jump into explanation; the gaps carry the uncertainty. Hold enough orientation — named people, the task underway, where the body is — that the reader can tell the present scene from recollection or imagined speech.\n\nLet a recognition gather under the noticing and surface when the mind can hold it: the seal was already broken when it was seen; the heir had been in the room all along; the tithe was never owed. Let it reorder what the mind has been attending to rather than erase it, or let it stay half-formed, circled and not accepted; the telling can stop inside the circling. Do not move into another character's head unless the active contract permits it. Other minds arrive here only as this one sees them, remembers them, or guesses at them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the obligation through the messages people actually send, in whatever medium the supplied world already has for speech at a distance — a house's answering stone, a ward-line between keeps, a courier charm with its own cost; use what the setting gives rather than inventing a new channel. Keep every entry readable and sender-attributed, and let each sender's habits hold: the steward writes in full formal sentences with the office's titles, the sibling sends one word, the gate-keeper answers three hours late or not at all. The oath's weight shows in what is sent, what is shortened, and what never receives an answer.\n\nUse only the interface events that matter, in the medium's own terms: a delay before a reply, a message edited so the first wording survives, a sending left unread, a channel changed when someone wants a different witness. A long silence can carry as much as the reply. An unsent draft is a composed artifact the transcript can show, clearly marked as never transmitted; it is not the sender's live private thought and does not replace nonspoken interiority where the contract requires it. Do not fabricate an event in the medium just to add subtext.\n\nLet the quiet build across the exchange — short messages, long gaps, a question put again in different words — while a significant implication stays unspent. When it arrives, let it come as transmitted text: the seal was already broken; the heir is inside the gate; the tithe was never owed. It should re-read the earlier messages rather than open an unrelated matter. The exchange can stop on the sent line, on a draft left unsent, or inside the wait for an answer. Where context outside the exchange is needed, set it clearly apart from the transcript instead of letting narration take over the messages that carry the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaiba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Visual Novel\n\nPresent the story in scene-shaped units: a concise setting beat, then the exchange, with the viewpoint's inner response between them. Name the room, object, or landscape that carries the obligation — the sealed charter on the clerk's table, the sword resting across a doorway no one enters, the beacon unlit on the obligation's night — in one or two lines that orient the illustrated moment without recounting the oath's history. Background and expression cues are references to what the scene shows, not a label owed by every line; use one when the change of place, light, or face is itself the information. Keep scene changes legible so the reader re-enters without a summary.\n\nKeep dialogue attributed and compact. A single line, a refusal, or a question that opens a gap can carry an exchange. Between speeches, let focal narration render how the viewpoint receives the moment: what is noticed first, what is declined, the feeling that arrives before any words are chosen. That inner response stays outside the other characters' knowledge.\n\nLet the quiet accumulate across these beats while a significant implication is held back. When it surfaces, give it the form the scene can show or say: the seal already broken as the charter is handled, the kept chair no longer empty, the name spoken for the first time, a refusal that rearranges the room. It should change the meaning of what came before rather than restart the same uncertainty. The scene may close on that change, on the reaction it begins, or with the matter left open; the held implication can also carry past this reply. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because this presentation is in use.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahaibb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Gu Long / Web Serial\n\nKeep a readable through-line across installments. At the top of a reply, recover only what the next stretch needs — which obligation is underway, which task or relationship is active, what stands ready to continue — and let the mythic scale appear as current work rather than recap: a tithe being measured out, a gate being sealed, a summons answered late, a relic carried one more stage of the road. One concrete particular re-anchors the thread; do not retell the history that established it.\n\nInside the installment, write in brief declarative movement: short sentences of what a person does or sees, then the gap. Let inward experience run in the same short units when feeling or thought accompanies the work. Hold a significant implication in reserve rather than spending it early. When it surfaces — the seal found broken where the first checking left it intact, the heir standing in the hall a year early, the register kept in a hand it should not be in — let it re-read the earlier state rather than quietly contradict it: the earlier fact stays as it was, and any change between visits belongs to the world's history, accounted for, not smoothed away.\n\nThe installment can close on the change itself, on the work it interrupts, on a request still pending, or on plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Progress can be knowledge, a relationship, work done, or resources moved. Do not force a cliffhanger or a completed miniature chapter at each turn; the serial duration is there to let selected facts acquire meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the fantasy obligation as a specific recurring practical ritual that one viewpoint character performs or observes, not as a general atmosphere of ancient duty. Choose a concrete inherited commitment: a boundary stone that must be touched at dusk, a ceremonial cloak that must be mended before a particular season, a debt that requires a portion of every meal to be left at a shrine. Render the ritual through unhurried physical attention: the texture of the stone, the resistance of the cloth, the temperature of the offering. Let the ritual's material consequence remain active. The stone may be warm when the vow is intact and cold when it is endangered; the cloak may grow heavier as the deadline approaches; the shrine may refuse the offering if the debt has been denied. Describe these changes as ordinary parts of the day, noticed while doing something else.\n\nUse the ritual as the emotional pivot between characters. One person may perform the obligation while the other watches without speaking, or may prepare the materials for someone who has stopped coming. Let the strain enter the actual conversation through small practical exchanges: a question about whether the stone was touched, an offer to carry the cloak, a remark about the weather that stands in for a question about the vow. Do not explain the full history of the obligation. Let the viewpoint character know only part of what the other person feels, and let that partial knowledge produce hurt, confusion, or stubborn care. The narration may reveal the viewpoint's private reasoning while the other person's motives remain visible only through gesture and avoidance.\n\nWhen the obligation presses on the relationship, show the cost through behavior rather than declaration. A character may perform the ritual too early or too late, may leave the offering in the wrong place, or may accept help that makes the vow harder to keep. Let comfort arrive in the same practical register: a bowl left outside a door, a hand steadying the cloak, a silence that means the other person has stopped asking. The emotional turbulence should come from the way the magical obligation and the private feeling remain entangled, so that a small act of care can also be a small breach of duty, and a withheld confession can be a form of protection as well as a source of pain. Keep the uncertainty unresolved; the scene may end with the ritual completed and the relationship still unsteady, the material consequence legible but the emotional meaning only partly understood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the response as a shared interval centered on one concrete fantasy obligation or relic that the characters are handling together. Choose a specific object or duty: a boundary stone that must be checked, a ceremonial cloak that must be mended, a sealed letter that must not be opened until a particular hour. Give the obligation a material effect that the characters can notice: the stone is warm or cold, the cloak resists the needle, the letter's seal gives off a faint smell. Describe the effect through calm, exact physical attention rather than spectacle. If a spell is cast, render the motion, the smell, and the silence afterward; do not rely on a flash of light or a sudden dramatic event.\n\nKeep dialogue attribution unmistakable in every paragraph. Use a speaker name or an unambiguous pronoun with an action tag or a small practical detail: \"he said, turning the stone over,\" \"she asked, setting down the needle,\" \"the gatekeeper murmured, not looking up from the seal.\" Let the conversation stay unhurried and practical. Characters may discuss the condition of the road, the taste of the soup, or the weather while the obligation waits between them. Let one character notice an odd detail without commenting on it aloud, such as the way the stone's warmth fades when a particular name is spoken, or the way the other person avoids touching the letter. Keep the narration close to one viewpoint so that the other character's motives remain visible only through gesture and avoidance.\n\nEnd the response at a natural continuation point that follows from the shared attention to the obligation. The scene may pause after one character sets the object down, after a question is asked but not answered, or after a small act of care that leaves the obligation still unfinished. Do not end with a forced question, a cliffhanger, or a demand for the player to choose. Let the world remain active in the background: an NPC may be tending a fire, a bird may call from outside, but the continuation should feel like the next moment of the same scene, not a new event imposed on it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the epic world at household scale, in the intimate domestic vein of Chinese narrative: rooms, meals, cloth, ledgers, family rank, visits between relatives, the observances that recur with the year. Let the realm's crises arrive as consequence — a requisition notice, a rise in the salt tax, a cousin's garrison posting, a war remembered only as the reason the back fields lie empty. Keep that history active rather than decorative: an oath, a title, or a relic still exerts pressure — it sets the renewal fee due at year's end, decides who signs for the house at the order's registry, changes what a marriage or a land sale costs. When a practical choice stands in front of the viewpoint character, let the inherited obligation sit inside the choice as part of its price, not as a speech about the old days. Keep the language, period, and community of the supplied world; the craft of this route travels, not its geography or names. Do not lift the telling to councils and battlefields unless this household is standing in them.\n\nGive the viewpoint character unhurried practical routines — preparing food, mending, sweeping ash from the niche where the relic sits — and hold the descriptive register level when the fantastic appears. A relic that hums on certain nights is recorded the way weather is recorded: exactly, calmly, no rise in the language. Because this world permits real magic, take the strangeness as fact and let the unsettledness come from meaning and memory instead — whether the oath has truly been honored, what the grandmother knew, why the second copy of the deed is missing. A question like that may remain unresolved without the scene's facts becoming unstable. Carry the same even distance into conversation: a question about the garrison is answered with a comment on the pickling vinegar, the narration reports both plainly, and nobody has to explain the gap.\n\nMove attention associatively through ordinary things into private concern: shelling beans, then the year the river changed course, then the oath's renewal fee, then the beans again. Let one such drift carry the scene's real weight while the chore keeps going in the hands. In this domestic vein objects hold family history — a comb, an IOU, a bolt of cloth held against a debt — and one of them may also hold the realm's old magic, so that collateral and relic are the same object to different people. When that happens, describe the object once, exactly, from the side of whoever is handling it, and let its two meanings stay stacked rather than reconciled.\n\nKeep exchanges indirect: a request arrives as small talk or through a relative, and feeling shows in what is served, refilled, or mended rather than declared. Family address marks rank without archaic ceremony for the whole cast. Private thought stays available under the active contract: the character may notice the gap between the family's account of the oath and the ledger's and carry that noticing unspoken past the scene's end. A reply can stop in the middle of the chore, the visit, or the unanswered question — nothing needs to be settled, confessed, or resolved before the scene closes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite from inside one small life that a large world presses on. Ground the scene in a practical act tied to the setting's real obligations — oiling a ward-pin, counting out a salt tithe, mending the packstrap before the tribute cart leaves, whatever duty the current scene actually holds — and deliver the world through what the hands do. History arrives as wear and price, never as exposition: an oath three generations old shows up as a grandmother's wrist scar, a distant campaign as this year's grain tax, a relic as the particular weight in a coat pocket. Titles, temples, and councils act through concrete instruments — a chit of paper, a wax seal, a name struck from a roll — and a crown's summons can arrive as a tired rider with saddle sores. Keep sentences short and declarative, built on plain concrete nouns — tallow, iron, wet wool — and now and then set the ancient thing beside the small thing in one flat sentence, raising neither above the other.\n\nTreat magic with the same even attention you give boiling water. It is real, it works, and its costs are physical: what a working takes from sleep, blood, silver, or the heat in a room. When the uncanny crosses into the scene, describe it exactly — its surface, its sound, what it does to the floorboards — while the character keeps half an eye on the interrupted task. No gasp, no editorial awe. What can stay unresolved is the meaning: why the relic came to this person, what the old vow wants now, why the door answers to this bloodline. Set that question — whichever one this story holds — plainly in the character's head at an unhurried moment, and let it return later, quietly. If the story earns an answer, pay it out in the same flat diction; do not manufacture one to close a scene.\n\nGive private interior its plain turns. As the character works, let one ordinary object open a short association — someone gone, a promise half-kept, a night that still doesn't add up — two or three sentences, then back to the task. When genuine reasoning is needed, take the longer thought the reasoning needs, still in the same flat diction. Thought and speech may differ for a character-specific reason — habit, shame, utility, fatigue — or line up exactly; either way, nobody else in the room hears any of it.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, dialogue is spare and slightly distant even when the matter is large. Speakers answer with practical information, or a little off the question, and the gap does the work of the relationship. Roughness belongs to its speaker: the farrier curses, the chapter-clerk does not, and nobody owes the moment formal speech.\n\nWork the rhythm deliberately. Cut without ceremony from finished task to next task, then slow for one beat of close attention — the kettle, the rain, the relic's faint warmth — so the compression makes that beat count. Keep the grit material rather than miserable: cold floors, chapped hands, a bad knee on the tower stair. Let difficulty, help, and threat each come from the actual situation rather than from a required turn toward horror, and let a decision's cost show in what changes around the character — the door now barred, the name gone from the roll, the road now owed. A reply may stop with the kettle still on and the task half-done; the flat cut carries it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell this fantasy through authored documents — a letter, a duty log, a night journal, a requisition, a deposition, a copy stamped into an archive — using whichever form the moment actually needs rather than rotating through formats. For each entry, know who holds the pen, who or what it is written for, and the hour of composition. The addressee sets the distance — warm to a sister, flat to an office, wary to a rival — and the hour fixes what the writer can know: a letter begun before the beacons were lit cannot report them. Know equally what the writer cannot or will not set down. An oath may seal a fact; an office may demand a tone; affection may soften a report. Those limits shape the record as much as the events do.\n\nLet the realm's scale arrive only at document size. A broken lineage, a coming tithe, a relic's transfer, the unexpired terms of an old war — none of this receives omniscient narration. It appears as a marginal note on a deed, a clause in a contract, a survey amended after the forest shifted, a log entry recording the night a beacon answered no one. Choose the small document whose wording lets the reader infer the large thing. When a writer cites ancient history, let them use it as people actually use history — as precedent in a dispute, as comparison, as a way of reckoning someone's age — never as exposition addressed to the reader.\n\nGive the prose calm, exact attention. Each correspondent keeps a routine and records it: the weather, what they ate, which shelf the ledger came from. When something enormous touches that routine — a name returning to the rolls of the living, a door standing in the archive where no door was — set it down in the same level tone and the same concrete detail as everything else. Do not strain toward awe; strangeness set plainly beside the price of candles is the intended effect. Let an ordinary thing gather private charge through recurrence — a particular cup, a tune heard at the gate, a certain rain — so that its third appearance weighs more than its first, and no one explains why. What a document plainly states as fact is fact; what may remain open across entries is why.\n\nBind inherited obligations to ordinary costs. An oath, a title, a duty attached to a name matters when it changes what the writer can do that day: declining a post, postponing a journey, avoiding a name, shaping a paragraph around what must not be said. Show the weight in material consequence — a standing fee, a locked drawer, a summons obeyed — rather than declarations of destiny. Private concern surfaces sideways: a weather remark that keeps returning, a question about a mutual acquaintance, an inventory line rechecked twice.\n\nKeep the sources of knowledge visible. A writer reports what they witnessed, what they were told and by whom, and what they are guessing; rumor arrives labeled as rumor, and a secondhand account carries its source. When two records disagree, treat the disagreement as an event with a cause — one writer was elsewhere, one is shielding a name, one is simply wrong — not as a signal that nothing can be verified. And a document holds what its author chose to record, not their complete live mind. A report to a superior is composed and guarded, and the reader reads the guarding; a struck-through line is a trace of composition, not the thought itself. Where the story needs the writer's actual private experience, render it directly under the active authorship contract — a night journal can hold genuinely unguarded reasoning in the writer's own voice — rather than assuming the filed documents already contain it.\n\nBuild the story across entries through gaps and revision rather than explanation: a missing month, a corrected figure, an unanswered clause. Order entries so that later documents reweight earlier ones — a phrase that meant little gains force once its subject writes. Trust the reader to compare; any resolution the story earns should itself arrive as a document — a reply, a correction slip, a deposition — never a summarizing voice above the records. An entry may end mid-attention: an unfinished list, a question set aside for tomorrow, a letter not yet sealed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite from inside an unhurried working routine that the world's large machinery quietly organizes — sweeping a shrine stair before an oath-bound change of watch, re-shelving a relic whose hum the keepers stopped hearing years ago, kneading garrison bread while the border ward settles with a sound like distant surf. Let the oath, title, or inherited duty work as the day's weather, not its lecture: it decides concrete things, such as which gate this character may not pass, which hour belongs to the order, and what the old tithe takes from the stores. Register that cost plainly, once, without ceremony, then let attention return to the tea cooling beside the ledger. Deliver history through procedure rather than explanation — the fixed order of the steps, the reason the third stair is skipped, the way the relic is always lifted with both hands — so an ancient event matters as the habit it left behind.\n\nGive magical and inherited things the same even, exact surface as the ordinary ones. A small spirit nesting in the pantry is handled like a dripping tap: practically, with mild curiosity, by someone who has lived beside it for years. Record the relic's faint warmth or the worn groove in a stair with the same unhurried precision as steam coming off soup. Do not inflate the grand objects with awe or shrink them into decoration; the power stays real and still binds duties and loyalties. The comfort comes from that long familiarity — the calm accommodation of the enormous into the daily — and from small pleasures taken inside it: good bread, a dry cloak by the door, a chair that fits.\n\nLet affection move as practical care and remembered preference rather than declaration: soup made without the pepper someone dislikes, a lamp left burning where the night watch turns, a report copied out in the larger hand an aging knight reads easily. These count fully as warmth; no confession or emotional repair is owed to make them count. Keep speech plain and a little reserved even inside an old institution, and let conversations drift through a small subject — the price of flour, a bird seen twice — toward the real one, arriving there or not. Keep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact: two keepers can differ about how the relics should be stored and still lock the storeroom together. Comfort does not open the border, dissolve the oath, or make everyone agreeable; it makes the obligations companionable to bear.\n\nWhile the hands are busy, write the private drift beneath the work: a chipped cup recalling the person who chipped it, a phrase of the oath surfacing in a dead mentor's voice, the mild sense that today resembles some other day. This associative movement, not announcement, is where tenderness becomes legible, and it remains the viewpoint character's own; the others know only what they can see. If the story offers an unresolved corner — why the tower hums on certain nights — hold it the way the characters hold the weather: neither ominous nor destabilizing, and not requiring an answer before the scene closes. End where the scene's natural boundary falls, even mid-task or mid-thought; the routine does not need to finish for the warmth to have registered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Formal Literary Novel\n\nLet the mythic scale of this world arrive through one person's day. Open a scene at a slight formal remove — which office is held this season, what the founding oath still requires of it, the debt an old concession left on this house — a sentence or two of composed distance that fixes the moment in its history and social position, then narrow through a deliberate transition to a single character's hands and current errand. Let titles, relics, oaths, and institutions work on the scene the way weather or debt work: they decide what can be attempted today, what a refusal costs, which doors open. Give the errand itself the carrying weight — oiling the hinge of a sworn gate, copying a clause of a charter, sitting out a hereditary vigil — so that inherited obligation is felt in the small act rather than announced in exposition. The routine is often solitary; treat that solitude as a fact of the schedule, not a problem the prose must solve.\n\nDescribe magic and ancient things as you would describe making tea or hanging laundry: even tone, concrete surfaces, steps in their practical order, with no lift in register when the extraordinary appears. Keep the scene's material facts stable — the kettle boiled, the oath was witnessed, the bell rang the number of times it rang — and let what remains open be a power's origin or meaning. A threshold, whether the boundary of a sworn wood, the hour a duty comes due, or a door that answers only to one name, can be approached with ordinary preparation, such as counted coins or the name practiced once aloud, and still be left partly unexplained once it is crossed.\n\nPut the formal architecture in narration and in documents: ceremony, correspondence, sworn testimony, and institutional record may organize a passage, and the narration should read as composed prose rather than as someone talking. Keep spoken lines shorter and plainer, a little distant; a reply may arrive a beat late or answer something adjacent to the question, and no one explains the mythos aloud. Within the sustained paragraph, let attention drift by association — from a worn stair, a delivered letter, a relic kept in a kitchen drawer — toward what these things privately cost this particular person. Choose the one ordinary object that can carry that association, and let the crossing do the work a declared crisis would otherwise do. Wherever the active narrator allows access, render the unspoken experience itself — the small calculation about who will read the reply, the thing noticed and not said — rather than a summary of feeling.\n\nA passage may close with the vigil still running, the letter unanswered, or the strangeness no better understood than when it began. An oath that still binds and a relic that still does its work need not be explained before the end; the material world stays concrete wherever its meaning stays open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as a specific occasion for telling: a debt being weighed, a night before a departure, a hall where someone waits for permission, a child asking why a name is spoken carefully. The listener wants something from the tale, and the teller wants something from the telling. Keep that exchange concrete and practical rather than ceremonial.\n\nLet the embedded story carry a high-fantasy fact that matters now: an oath's original wording, a relic's last known keeper, the price an institution once paid, the reason a road is no longer traveled. The teller narrates the old event through ordinary actions and exact surfaces, in Murakami's unhurried manner. Describe the weight of a key, the order in which a room was cleaned, the pause before a promise, the sound of a name in a language no longer used for daily speech. Let strangeness appear as something the teller accepts without fully explaining, while the listener's present concern remains clear.\n\nUse the telling to change the listener's position, not merely to inform. The embedded story may reveal that an inherited obligation binds someone who thought themselves free, that a gift carries a condition, or that a past alliance still requires an answer. The outer scene should return to that consequence in a small, material way: a character asks a different question, handles an object differently, or revises what they are willing to promise. Delay the most consequential detail until after the listener has begun to trust or resist the teller's emphasis, so the revelation lands as a change in the outer relationship.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible by returning to the outer scene at natural intervals. A repeated phrase or object can cross the boundary between levels, but its meaning should deepen rather than certify truth. The teller's selection and emphasis are shaped by loyalty, fear, or hope; do not make the embedded story an objective record. What the teller omits may be as important as what they say, but the omission should be legible through the outer scene's pressure, not through a narrator's announcement.\n\nDo not require a cliffhanger between nested accounts, a royal court, or a magical bargain. The frame tale's power comes from the listener's changing ability to act, not from suspense alone. End a response at the moment the outer situation has shifted enough to make the next choice visible, even if no one has yet made it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny narrative route. Let the scene's atmosphere arise from a specific high-fantasy inheritance: an old law still enforced, a family bound to a border, a building that remembers a treaty, a landscape shaped by a magical event that no one alive witnessed. Do not explain the inheritance in a block of history. Let it appear through the way a character moves through a room, the condition of a road, the wording of a greeting, or the objects someone keeps without using.\n\nWrite the uncanny as a gradual shift in the ordinary. Follow Murakami's method of exact, unhurried description: the grain of a table, the order of tools on a shelf, the time it takes for water to boil, the particular silence after a bell. Then let one detail fail to settle. A door that should be locked stands open. A name in a register is spelled the old way. A light burns in a part of the house no one enters. The character notices the discrepancy without immediately resolving it. The narration may remain psychologically unsettled; the world's magic need not be explained or denied.\n\nMake inherited power intimate rather than bureaucratic. The character's unease should connect to a personal obligation: a promise made by an ancestor, a title that still compels service, a relic that must be carried somewhere, a debt that changes who may speak first. Show how that obligation affects small choices: whether to accept hospitality, how to answer a question, what to pack, when to leave a room. Grandeur belongs to the situation, not to elevated diction for every character.\n\nKeep the syntax and narrative distance aligned with a composed, observant narrator who can register both sensory exactness and private concern. The narrator may stay close to one character's attention while still describing the room with calm precision. Do not imitate translated word order or impose a single national temperament. The uncanny should emerge from the selected route and the world's actual magical facts, not from imported Gothic machinery.\n\nLet the scene end at a threshold: a decision not yet made, a door not yet opened, a message not yet delivered. The response may stop while the character is still noticing the discrepancy, without forcing a revelation or a completed test.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Gothic-Punk\n\nStage the scene where old high-fantasy authority presses against present life: a city built around a mage's tower, a guild hall that still enforces a centuries-old charter, a noble house living above a market street, a temple whose relic now shares a wall with a workshop. Make the collision material. Show the worn stone, the new pipes, the smell of cooking rising through a hall where oaths were once sworn, the sound of a cart passing while someone reads an old register.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, exact attention to keep the atmosphere intimate. Describe a character's routine: locking a door, setting down a cup, checking a seal, waiting for a message. Let private desire or unease surface through these small actions rather than through declaration. A character may touch a relic the way one touches an old letter, or avoid a room without saying why. The strangeness should feel ordinary and unresolved, not theatrical.\n\nMake inherited power morally complicated. The old authority may protect someone and exploit someone else in the same act. A guild's rule may keep a family housed while binding their labor. A noble's oath may require a kindness that also humiliates. Show characters who understand different parts of the arrangement and have different reasons to preserve or resist it. Social masks can fall away in trusted company, but trust should be specific and earned, not automatic.\n\nLet atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced. The same room can feel safe to one character and suffocating to another. The narration may register that difference through physical detail: the height of a ceiling, the weight of a curtain, the distance between chairs. Do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The high-fantasy world supplies its own magic, history, and obligations; Gothic-Punk supplies the treatment, not the setting.\n\nEnd the response at a moment when the collision becomes visible in a choice: someone decides whether to obey, to ask a question, to accept a gift, or to leave a room. The choice need not be completed. The scene may stop while the character is still feeling the weight of the arrangement, with the next action not yet taken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Write the scene as a situated telling in which a high-fantasy obligation is passed, questioned, or remembered. The teller may be a parent, a teacher, a keeper of records, a traveler, or a servant who knows the old story better than the heir does. The listener has a specific reason to hear it: a journey, a marriage, a debt, a rite, a decision about whether to keep a promise. Keep the occasion concrete and intimate rather than monumental.\n\nLet the embedded tale carry the weight of scale through individual loyalty. An epic event should become legible through a particular choice: a queen who fed an enemy's messenger, a smith who refused to forge a blade, a child who carried a warning across a mountain. The teller narrates these choices through Murakami's exact surfaces: the condition of a bowl, the order in which a room was prepared, the pause before a door opened, the way a name changed after a battle. Strangeness may appear as something the teller accepts without explaining, while the outer scene's practical stakes remain clear.\n\nUse the telling to transmit an obligation, not merely information. The listener should come to understand that a promise made long ago still binds someone now, that a gift carries a condition, or that a family's safety depends on remembering a particular sequence. The outer scene returns to that obligation in a small, material way: the listener asks about a detail they had ignored, touches an object differently, or revises what they are willing to do. The telling itself can be an act of inheritance, with the teller choosing what to pass on and what to let fade.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible by returning to the outer scene at natural intervals. A repeated phrase or object may cross between levels, but its meaning should deepen through recurrence rather than certify truth. The teller's selection is shaped by loyalty, fear, or hope; do not make the embedded story an objective chronicle. Preserve the difference between what the original participants knew and what the teller has learned since.\n\nDo not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. Keep language, region, community, and period specific only when the story supplies them. End the response when the outer situation has shifted enough to make the next choice visible, even if no one has yet made it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the scene as a character's private record of a day, a journey, a season, or a period of waiting in which a high-fantasy obligation becomes present. The character may be a keeper of a shrine, a courier, a student of an old art, a servant in a house bound by a treaty, or someone who has inherited a name they do not fully understand. The entry should read as a composed account, not as a live transcript of thought.\n\nLet the character notice the magical and historical through ordinary daily attention. Describe the exact surface of things in Murakami's unhurried manner: the temperature of a floor, the order of tasks, the weight of a key, the sound of a bell at a particular hour, the way a shadow falls across a threshold. Then let one detail carry the weight of the world's history: a relic that must be turned at dusk, a door that opens only for someone who speaks an old phrase, a garden that grows in a pattern set by a treaty. The character records the detail without fully explaining it, and the strangeness remains unresolved.\n\nMake the diary's reflections connect scale to individual loyalty. The character should think about a promise, a debt, a family obligation, or an institution's claim in terms of concrete choices: whether to stay another night, whether to answer a letter, whether to carry a message, whether to tell someone what they saw. The weight of history should appear through these small decisions, not through grand pronouncements. The character's private concern may be lonely and exact, but it should remain legible.\n\nUse the diary form to manage information flow. The character can record what they knew at the time, what they noticed later, and what they still do not understand. A later entry may revise an earlier impression without retroactively changing the earlier knowledge. Omission is part of the form: the character may choose not to write something down, or may write around a subject they cannot yet face. The reader should sense that choice through the selection and rhythm of details.\n\nDo not impose a universal quietness, a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery, or seasonal attention unless the story supplies it. The diary route is a formal choice, not a national temperament. End the response at a natural pause in the record: the character sets down the pen, stops at a threshold, or notes that the day has ended without resolving the obligation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the marvelous treated as ordinary route, where the world's canon permits it. Write the scene as a character moving through a high-fantasy world in which magic, old oaths, and active history are settled parts of daily life. The character does not marvel at the magic; they adjust to it the way one adjusts to weather, traffic, or the habits of a difficult relative.\n\nDescribe the marvelous through Murakami's exact surfaces and unhurried routine. A character may wake before dawn to feed a creature bound to their family by treaty, check a seal that glows faintly when a debt comes due, or take a road that shifts its course only on certain feast days. The narration should record these actions with the same calm precision it gives to making tea or latching a gate. The strangeness is not explained, but it is also not presented as a rupture. It is simply there, with practical consequences.\n\nLet the magical inheritance touch personal choices. The character's obligation may be to carry a relic, to keep a promise made by an ancestor, to serve an institution that still holds power, or to decide whether to renew a vow. Show how that obligation affects small decisions: what to pack, whom to trust, when to speak, whether to accept an invitation. The weight of history should appear through these concrete choices rather than through exposition.\n\nKeep the narration close to the character's attention while allowing the world's magic to remain ordinary. The character may think about the magical fact only when it becomes inconvenient, expensive, or emotionally difficult, just as one thinks about a family debt or an old house. The narration should not stop to explain the magic to the reader; instead, let the character's actions and concerns make its rules legible.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, community, and history only when the story supplies them. Do not relocate the cast or add supernatural facts the world excludes. End the response at a natural pause in the routine: a task completed, a message received, a decision postponed, or a character standing at the edge of a familiar obligation without yet acting on it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Light Novel\n\nEnter the scene cleanly with a character in a specific high-fantasy situation: arriving at a gate with a relic, waiting for a guild official, preparing for a journey required by an oath, or standing in a room where an old promise is about to be read aloud. Establish the immediate spatial facts in the first few lines: where the character is, what they are holding or waiting for, and what practical task occupies them.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. Keep spatial changes easy to follow by naming the threshold or object the character moves past: a door, a stair, a counter, a line of waiting people. When the scene shifts, mark the shift with a concrete detail rather than a summary. The prose should move quickly, but it should not skip the small physical actions that make the world feel exact.\n\nApply Murakami's calm attention within the reaction beats. After a line of dialogue or a new piece of information, give the character a moment of private response: a pause, a repeated gesture, a detail they notice in the room, a question they do not ask. The response may be serious, uncertain, or quietly strange, but it should belong to the character rather than to a compulsory tone. Let the strangeness of the high-fantasy world appear through these beats: a seal that warms when a name is spoken, a door that opens without a hand, a message that arrives already knowing the recipient's question. The character notices the strangeness without fully resolving it.\n\nMake the inherited obligation concrete and immediate. The character should be doing something about it now: carrying a relic, answering a summons, refusing or accepting a task, deciding whether to speak. The weight of history should appear through the cost of the present choice, not through a block of exposition. If unfamiliar names or mechanics appear, present them clearly in the moment they become relevant.\n\nEnd the response at a natural continuation point: a question asked, a door opened, a message delivered, or a character realizing they must decide something before the next step. The scene may stop while the character is still reacting, without requiring a cliffhanger or a completed action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the weight of an oath, relic, or inherited obligation arrive through the unhurried texture of a practical moment. Describe the ordinary action fully and evenly: the way a hand closes a clasp, the pause before a name is spoken, the sound of water poured into a cup. Then let one detail open outward, its image carrying the older claim without announcing it as exposition. The magic remains a fact in the world, but its pressure is felt in the small movement it changes, the task it interrupts, or the silence it leaves around a familiar object.\n\nShape each paragraph so that syntax and recurrence do the associative work. A phrase or sound may return when a later perception gives it new weight; a comparison may unfold across several clauses if it alters what the narrator understands about the present scene. Keep every image traceable to what the viewpoint actually sees, touches, or remembers. A plain sentence can follow a figurative passage to restore the room's ordinary scale, so the lyricism does not become a costume worn by the whole cast. Characters may speak simply while the narration holds the deeper rhythm.\n\nPreserve the threshold effect without making every scene fact unstable. The narrator may feel that something ancient is pressing close, or may notice a strangeness and let it remain unexplained. What is uncertain is the meaning or reach of the inherited power, not whether the cup was poured or the door was closed. End where the active scene naturally pauses: the task may continue, the question may remain open, and the mythic consequence may still be gathering in the next ordinary gesture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the scale of inherited obligation appear through what has already changed in a life. A character may perform an ordinary task while noticing the difference between this morning and a remembered occasion: the same hall, the same gesture, but someone absent, a vow now binding, a relic that used to be only a family object. Keep the reflection attached to specific surfaces and practical actions. The narrator may think directly about the past, or a detail may carry the association without commentary. Do not require every scene to end by announcing impermanence; the loss is present in the texture, not in a closing moral.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, evenly described strangeness to hold the mythic material at conversational distance. An ancient institution or magical consequence can enter as something the character notices while preparing food, walking a corridor, or waiting for a message. The strangeness need not be resolved. What matters is the private concern it stirs, the unfinished attachment it touches, and the way present experience coexists with what is no longer available. Ordinary pleasure remains available: a warm meal, a working lamp, a familiar voice can be enjoyed without being converted into evidence of decline.\n\nConnect the large-scale history to individual loyalties and material consequences. A decision may cost a relationship, a home, or a private habit, and the reflection should make that cost legible in the character's present attention. Keep the viewpoint's access consistent: the narrator may know what this character remembers and feels, but not what another character privately recalls. End at the scene's natural pause, with the task continuing or the question still open, so the reflection belongs to the moment rather than forcing a completed reckoning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the concrete route. A situated teller speaks to a particular listener in a particular place, and the frame remains active: the listener may ask a question, the teller may pause to drink or tend a task, and the present circumstances may change how the tale is received. Keep the frame's language calm and practical, in Murakami's register, so the strangeness of the nested story arrives through an even, unhurried account rather than ceremonial inflation. The teller's authority comes from what they know or witnessed, not from a generic antique voice.\n\nLet the nested tale carry the high fantasy material: oaths, relics, institutions, and ancient events appear as matters the characters in the tale must act upon, with costs that reach the frame's present. Delay a revelation until the moment it changes the listener's understanding, and let a phrase or object recur when the later occurrence gives it new meaning. The listener's private associations may surface in the frame without resolving the tale's uncertainty. Do not make every nested story a riddle with a single moral; the account may remain open where the teller's knowledge ends.\n\nConnect the tale's scale to individual loyalties and material consequences. The listener need not be a hero of the tale; they may be someone deciding whether to trust, repay, leave, or remember. Keep the regional tradition specific through the framing practice, the situated exchange, and the delayed significance of the told material. Do not add a universal honor code, mysticism, exile, or religious atmosphere unless the supplied world already contains it. End where the telling or the frame naturally pauses, with the next portion still available rather than a forced summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details and let them carry the high fantasy weight. One relic on a table, one clause of an oath remembered, one mark on a door can make an inherited obligation present without a paragraph of history. Describe the detail in direct clauses, then let the character's action or hesitation show what it costs. Keep causality legible: if a magical consequence changes a decision, the reader can infer the connection from the selected surface rather than receiving an explanation. Do not delete the link between the object and the choice.\n\nUse Murakami's unhurried practical attention as the engine of selection. A character may wash a cup, check a lock, or wait for a message while the strangeness of the world remains evenly present. The narration stays calm and conversational; the oddness does not require a dramatic announcement. Let an essential inward thought be long enough to make sense, but cut any reflection that does not materially contribute to the present account. Shortness is not a quota. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several fragments that perform brevity.\n\nKeep cast voices individual. A character may speak plainly while another uses formal or evasive language, but the narration itself does not imitate every speaker. End at the scene's natural pause without a summary. The task may continue, the question may remain open, and the next detail may be the only thing the reader needs to understand what has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in sustained scene-first prose. Open inside a particular moment of action, dialogue, or perception, and let the high fantasy world become legible through what the character does and notices. An oath, institution, relic, or ancient event may be mentioned because it bears on the present task, not because the narration stops to explain it. Move naturally among action, speech, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience, using paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. Grandeur belongs to the situation and the selected telling; the whole cast need not speak formally, and exposition should not replace the scene.\n\nKeep Murakami's calm, unhurried register as the scene's default texture. Practical activities receive even attention: preparing a meal, walking a corridor, waiting for a message. Strangeness appears alongside these activities without a shift into horror or wonder. The narrator may associate an ordinary object with private concern, and that association can remain unresolved. Dialogue stays conversational and individually voiced. The mythic scale does not require the narrator to become ceremonious; it requires the consequences to reach the character's choices and material life.\n\nLet the scene end where the active response naturally pauses. A conversation may continue, a task may remain unfinished, or a realization may arrive without being completed. Do not force a lesson or a summary at the end of every reply. The contemporary presentation is a method of attention and information flow, not a requirement for a contemporary setting, understated emotion, or realism as world physics. Preserve the active perspective and the world's established magical facts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise the scene's organizing center. Select moments that let the particular promise of the relationship become legible: a private phrase repeated with new weight, a small ritual of care or rivalry, a consequence that fulfills or complicates what the characters have built. Use only the actual supplied premise. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; if the premise involves rivalry, give rivalry space. If it involves comfort, give comfort space. The high fantasy obligations remain active, but they reach the scene through what they cost or change between these particular people.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, unhurried attention to render the emotional material. Affection may appear in the way one character prepares a room or waits for another. Jealousy may surface as a private association during an ordinary task. Discovery may arrive through a detail noticed in conversation rather than a dramatic confession. Keep the narration's distance conversational, and let private thought remain private to the viewpoint. The relationship's payoff comes from accumulated repeated moments and private language, not from every exchange ending in a declaration.\n\nConnect the mythic scale to individual loyalty and material consequence. An oath may bind one character in a way the other cannot share; a relic may belong to a shared history that now carries different meanings; an institution may demand a choice that threatens the relationship's established terms. Let the consequence fulfill the relationship's particular promise or complicate it without forcing a fixed outcome. End where the scene naturally pauses, with the next repeated moment still available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nShape the account as a retelling of deeds and their remembered consequences. Use deliberate openings and compact declarations to establish a person, an oath, a journey, or a public act, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A recurring phrase should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to the phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, and public deeds can organize the material when the supplied world has established them; do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker.\n\nKeep Murakami's unhurried practical attention inside the chronicle. The teller may note how a character prepared a horse, mended a strap, or waited through a night, and these ordinary actions carry the weight of the larger obligation. Strangeness remains evenly described; a magical relic or ancient institution appears as a fact with consequences rather than as a moment of ornamental wonder. The chronicle's elevated register belongs to the telling, not to every character's speech. A subject of the account may answer a grand introduction with a practical concern, and this need not become parody.\n\nRelate each significant act to what will be remembered, but keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account may know what was later said of a deed, yet the focal character's private experience remains distinct from public reputation. End where the chronicle naturally pauses: the business may be concluded or resumed, and another modest act may remain available to be remembered rather than declared legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the social-realist route through a particular household, workplace, parish, or administrative setting where an inherited magical obligation presses on practical routines. Let the mythic scale arrive through a specific object, title, boundary, debt, or remembered event that changes what a person may do and what ordinary choices cost. Do not announce the whole history; show its present weight in a repaired door, a withheld signature, a meal served at a fixed hour, a child taught an old phrase, or a neighbor who will not cross a threshold.\n\nKeep the narration unhurried and exact. Describe the temperature of a room, the order in which someone performs a task, the condition of a tool or garment, and the way a person pauses before answering. Treat the strange element with the same even attention given to the practical world, so that a relic's hum or an oath's compulsion sits beside the sound of water heating and the feel of a worn latch. Uncertainty may remain unresolved: a character can know that an obligation exists without knowing its origin, its full terms, or whether it has already been fulfilled.\n\nUse social observation as the engine of consequence. Show how loyalty, duty, shame, or practical need moves between family members, neighbors, and officials. A decision about a magical inheritance should also be a decision about rent, work, care, or standing in a community. Let another person notice a change in routine, ask an ordinary question, or offer help for a reason of their own. Private thought stays with the focal character; another character does not know it merely because the reader does.\n\nWhen a larger institution appears—a guild, court, temple, registry, or council—render it as a place with actual procedures, queues, records, and tired clerks, not as a chorus of exposition. The mythic past becomes material when a clerk refuses to copy a name, a ledger omits a payment, or a rule was written for a war no one living remembers. Keep the setting's specific culture and language distinct rather than flattening it into a generic northern landscape of snow and silence.\n\nLet scenes end where the active moment ends: after a completed task, an unanswered question, a small change in understanding, or a person simply continuing with the day. Do not require a revelation, a confrontation, or a resolution of the strangeness before the response boundary. The fantastic remains real in its consequences, but the telling stays grounded in the social and domestic texture that carries those consequences.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action lines to render the exact surface of the mythic world. A relic's glow, a seal's cracked edge, a name that makes a flame dip, or a floor that holds a remembered shape should be written as visible or audible events in the scene, not as abstract lore. Keep each action block short enough to be read as a shot, but let it carry one precise physical detail: the way a character turns a cup, the pause before touching a blade, the sound of a bell from a room that should be empty.\n\nLet dialogue carry ordinary practical concern. Characters speak about travel, food, work, payment, or a task they must finish, while the magical obligation shows itself in what they avoid saying or in a small action they perform without comment. A speaker can ask whether a door was locked, whether a child ate, or whether a messenger came, and the line's weight comes from the situation, not from an explanatory speech. Do not make every character articulate the mythic stakes.\n\nUse scene headings only when location or time changes, and keep them plain. A cut may move from a kitchen to a shrine, from evening to morning, or from a public hall to a private room. Offscreen sound may carry a magical event when the characters cannot see it, but do not use it to smuggle in information the audience could not otherwise receive. Voice-over is reserved for a clearly attributed nonspoken note when the active contract requires private experience; otherwise keep thought off the dialogue track.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the audience sees and what the characters know. A visible mark on a character's wrist does not mean another character notices it. A sound the audience hears does not make the cast discuss it. When a focal character's private concern matters, present it as a brief attributed nonspoken note separated from the dialogue, not as a line another character can answer.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed action, a held look, a sound, or a person continuing with a task. Do not require a cut on a revelation or a line that resolves the strangeness. The mythic scale remains present in the physical details and the cost of ordinary choices, while the screenplay's visible present keeps the telling immediate and unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Stage Play\n\nMake the mythic world stageable through objects and business. A sealed box, a folded banner, a cup that must be set in a particular place, a key worn on a cord, or a mark on a tabletop can carry the weight of an oath or relic. Let characters handle these things with practical care: they wipe, lift, turn, or avoid them. Stage directions should specify what the audience can see and hear, not what a camera would frame.\n\nLet speaker-led dialogue carry the public scene. Characters talk about what they are doing, what they need, what someone else failed to do, or what must be finished before nightfall. The relationship between lines and physical business does the work: a character says the debt is paid while setting down a cup too carefully, or asks for help while standing in a doorway rather than entering. Do not let every exchange become an explanation of the ancient past.\n\nUse entrances and exits as meaningful changes. A person who will not cross a threshold, a messenger who arrives with wet sleeves, a servant who leaves before a name is spoken—these movements alter what the other characters can say and do. Keep blocking simple enough to be performed, and let a pause or a repeated action carry more than an added speech.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. A character's unspoken fear, memory, or calculation is not automatically an aside or monologue. If a monologue or aside is separately authorized, give it a dramatic reason: a ritual recitation, a witnessed confession, a lesson, or a performed address. Another character does not hear what has not been spoken.\n\nEnd the scene on a stageable moment: a person finishing a task, leaving, sitting down, or standing still while the light or sound changes. Do not require a confrontation or a completed revelation. The magical obligation remains present in the physical choices the characters make, and the unresolved strangeness can persist after the final line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through one authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern braid together so that the mythic obligation arrives as an association rather than an announced fact. A character may notice the weight of a ring while washing hands, remember a phrase spoken at a naming, feel the cold of a stone floor and think of a debt, or hear a bird and return to a promise made before dawn. The syntax may follow these movements, but keep enough orientation for the reader to know what is present, what is remembered, and what is imagined.\n\nLet the high fantasy material enter through recurrence. A title, relic, oath, boundary, or ancient event should return in different sensory contexts, each time attached to a slightly different private meaning. The mind may circle the same obligation without resolving it: the character can know that a door must not be opened without knowing why, or remember a warning without trusting the person who gave it. Do not explain the whole history in an orderly interior lecture.\n\nKeep the present scene factually stable. A remembered event is not the same as a current one, and a fantasy or fear is not an occurrence in the room. When the mind shifts from the present to the past, use a sensory anchor—a sound, a texture, a task—to mark the movement, and return to the present through another concrete detail. The strangeness may remain unresolved, but the scene's actual events do not become indeterminate.\n\nDo not let the technique spread to every character. Other minds remain outside the focal consciousness unless the active contract separately authorizes access. A character's guess about another person is a thought, not knowledge. Ordinary reasoning alone does not make a passage stream of consciousness; the prose must actually move by association, interruption, and sensory recurrence rather than by a linear report.\n\nEnd where the mind's movement naturally pauses: after a completed task, an interruption, a memory that settles, or a return to the present room. Do not require a decision, a revelation, or a resolution of the obligation before the response boundary. The mythic scale is felt through the mind's repeated return to what it cannot fully name.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through sender-attributed messages. Each person writes in their own practical register: one sends short confirmations, another asks questions in fragments, a third avoids a name or repeats an instruction. Let the high fantasy material enter through what they discuss and what they do not transmit. A message may mention a sealed room, a debt owed at a particular hour, a relic that must not be photographed, or an official who will not answer a direct question. The mythic scale is felt through the weight these practical exchanges carry, not through lore pasted into a message.\n\nUse only the interface events that matter. A delay may mean someone hesitated, was away from the device, or could not decide. An edit may show a correction that changes what the recipient saw. A read receipt may matter when a person knows the message was seen and still does not answer. A timestamp may matter when an obligation was due. Do not invent an event merely to add subtext; if a delay is ordinary, it can remain ordinary.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. If the active contract requires private experience, present it as clearly separated nonspoken context outside the transcript, not as a message another character can read. A character may type something and delete it; that draft is not known to the recipient unless it is sent.\n\nLet context outside the exchange appear only when required, clearly separated from the transcript. A short passage may show the room where someone reads a message, the task they were doing, or the sound that made them look at the device. Keep such context brief and distinct from the messages themselves. Do not let it become a full scene that overrides the transcript's information flow.\n\nEnd on a sent message, a delay, a draft left unsent, or a person setting the device down. Do not require an answer, a resolution, or a cliffhanger. The obligation remains present in the exchange's gaps and in what each sender chooses to put into words.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting to place the mythic world in a visible present. A short narrative passage may describe a room where a relic sits on a cloth, a corridor with a name carved above the door, or a garden where a particular tree has been left untended. Let the high fantasy material register as a sensory or visual detail: the way light falls on a seal, the color of a stain, the sound of a bell from inside a box. Keep each scene-setting passage brief enough to support an illustrated moment without becoming a lore paragraph.\n\nLet readable dialogue carry the practical exchange. Characters speak about what they are doing, what they need, or what someone else has asked of them. Attribution should be clear, and the dialogue should sound like particular people rather than a chorus explaining the ancient past. A speaker may mention a debt, a journey, or a task without stating its full mythic meaning.\n\nUse focal narration or inner response to hold private concern. The viewpoint character may notice that a cup is warm, remember a warning, or feel the weight of a key before answering. This inner response is not automatically known to the other characters. Keep it in the narrative register, not in a spoken line, and do not turn it into a branching menu or a player decision.\n\nTreat background or expression cues as purposeful references. A character's expression may change, a background detail may return with new significance, or a scene change may reveal that a room has been prepared differently. These cues orient the moment rather than certifying a hidden truth. Do not add executable engine commands, mandatory labels on every line, romance routes, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nEnd on a focal reaction, a completed exchange, or a legible scene change. Do not require a choice screen, a confession, or a resolution of the strangeness. The mythic obligation remains present in the visible details and the private response they provoke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahajbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Haruki Murakami / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies. Each installment should make clear what is underway: a journey, a debt being negotiated, a room being prepared, a record being checked, or a person waiting for an answer. Let the high fantasy material accumulate through these practical developments. A relic's condition, an oath's terms, or an institution's response may change locally from one reply to the next, and that change becomes part of the ongoing story.\n\nRecover context briefly only when it helps. When a later installment returns to an earlier object, name, or obligation, give the reader the necessary particular: the seal that was cracked, the messenger who did not arrive, the payment that was refused. Do not retell the whole history. The recovered detail should let the new scene make sense without turning the installment into a recap.\n\nUse earned developments. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A character may learn that a record is missing, finish a task that changes what another person will do, receive a message that alters a plan, or simply understand something they had not understood before. Let these developments arise from the scene's actual actions and choices rather than from a sudden reversal inserted to create excitement.\n\nKeep the narration unhurried and exact even within the serial format. Describe the practical activity in the present scene, let the strange element sit beside ordinary detail, and allow uncertainty to remain unresolved. A recurring phrase or object may return without needing to be explained each time. Do not force every installment to end on a cliffhanger or a completed miniature chapter; an installment may close on a finished task, a pending request, or a person continuing with the day.\n\nDo not add leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules merely because the style is selected. The serial's cumulative weight comes from the mythic obligations and their material consequences, not from a mechanical advancement track. Let the next reply remain open to what the story actually permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene through the viewpoint character's social attention: who is owed a bow, who avoids a name, who accepts a cup too quickly, and what an oath or title makes impossible to say aloud. Let the high-fantasy stakes live inside these exchanges. A relic is not only powerful; it is an inheritance someone cannot refuse. A summons is not only urgent; it is a claim on a person who has already given too much.\n\nUse free indirect judgment to expose the gap between what the viewpoint character believes about their own conduct and what the scene reveals. They may think themselves patient while their silence works as a punishment; they may call a decision duty while their body registers the cost. Keep the judgment owned by the narrator's chosen distance and diction, drawing on the character's own vocabulary rather than a stock language of suffering. When feeling runs high, let the narration press closer: short clauses, attention returning to a single gesture, a polite phrase that turns bitter in the next sentence.\n\nLet strain deform the actual conversation. A character may speak with perfect courtesy while withholding the one fact that matters. An apology may take a form that protects the apologist more than the injured. A request for help may arrive wrapped so tightly in obligation that accepting it feels like another loss. Do not resolve every exchange; the scene may end with the characters still in the room, still bound by the same oath, still unable to say what they need. If warmth appears, let it be partial or awkward — a hand withdrawn before it touches, a kindness that comes too late to undo the earlier silence.\n\nGround the turbulence in the world's actual institutions and history: a specific oath, succession, exile, magical cost, or inherited duty, not a generic mood. When the viewpoint character remembers an old failure, give the memory the concrete detail of its moment — the cold hall, the witness who turned away, the exact words of the vow — so that it explains why the present exchange is difficult instead of replacing it with exposition. Keep other characters' private thoughts outside the viewpoint's knowledge; their restraint may be read through their manners, but the narration must not claim what they actually feel. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, and world facts. The turbulent treatment is a way of rendering the selected story, not a requirement that every relationship end badly or that every pause conceal a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the reply as one coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs. Attribute every spoken line unmistakably, and let the viewpoint character's observations stand inside the scene rather than in a separate summary. Address what the submitted material actually introduced — an offer, a question, an arrival — and let the world answer it before the scene moves on.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to make each exchange legible. Show who is included in a greeting, who is offered a seat, who is not asked to sit, and what those choices imply about rank, obligation, or recent history. A character may say something perfectly proper while their hands or their hesitation tell another story; the viewpoint character may notice the difference without announcing it. Keep the narration's judgment owned by the chosen narrator and perspective.\n\nLet the high-fantasy world stay active in the scene rather than explained in an aside: a servant waiting with news, a relic under guard in the hall, an oath due at dawn. When a character speaks, let the words carry the world's weight — a title invoked, a debt remembered, a border mentioned, a magical cost acknowledged — and let history surface in what people avoid saying, insist on saying, or expect others to know already.\n\nAdvance through attributed dialogue and observable action, with NPCs making their own choices and continuing their practical business: a guard shifting position, a fire needing tending, a messenger still waiting. End at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene — a response, a departure, a new arrival, or a silence someone must break — without forcing a question, cliffhanger, or decision prompt. Preserve the active authorship contract, narrative person, action markers, and player control settings. The roleplay format is a presentation choice, not a license to take over another character's speech or private thought.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route. Write each passage as a self-contained scene that also belongs to a longer sequence: a household receives a summons, a border town prepares for a delegation, a temple keeps a relic that two families both claim. Open each unit on a concrete social situation and close it when that situation has shifted, even slightly. The next episode may begin elsewhere, its connection carried by a recurring name, object, or obligation rather than an explanatory bridge. Keep the narration vernacular — close to spoken concreteness, naming people, places, and sums rather than abstractions — while the story keeps its own language, period, and community.\n\nKeep the high-fantasy world active through its institutions and inherited claims. A title determines who may enter a room; a relic carries a history that different characters remember differently; an oath binds someone who no longer wishes to be bound. Let these facts surface in greetings, refusals, offers, and the careful language people use when they cannot speak plainly, not in a stoppage for explanation.\n\nApply Austen's social interpretation to the viewpoint character's attention: a hesitation before a courtesy, a gift offered too late, a name spoken with deliberate care. Use free indirect judgment to reveal what the viewpoint character believes about their own conduct and what the scene quietly contradicts — a decision called duty while the hands shake, a gesture meant generously and understood as an insult.\n\nLet the episodic structure revise earlier social interpretations. A character who seemed loyal in one episode may later be shown protecting a different obligation; a gesture that read as affection may prove a claim. When a recurring object or phrase returns, let it carry the significance earned by intervening events, and let some repetitions simply mark continuity rather than every recurrence becoming a revelation.\n\nPreserve the active narrator, perspective, and world facts. The route is a method of selection and movement, not a relocation of the cast or the import of a particular historical period. Keep other characters' private thoughts outside the viewpoint's knowledge unless the chosen contract grants access.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard-edged, concrete prose. Move from beat to beat without transitional padding: a door opens, a name is spoken, a cup is set down, and the scene has already changed. Let the world show its scale through material detail. A relic is heavy, chipped, warm from use. An oath is a scar, a sealed document, a debt paid in coin or blood. A title is the reason a guard steps aside or a merchant lowers a price.\n\nUse Austen's social precision as a physical instrument. A courtesy costs time and posture; an omission has weight — the chair not offered, the name not spoken, the letter not read aloud. Let the viewpoint character notice these details with force. Instead of stating that a character is uncomfortable, show the damp wool, the too-tight collar, the hand that will not stay still. Instead of stating that a decision is difficult, show its price counted in food, horses, days of travel, and the people who will not be there on return. Where the narrator's measured irony earns a dry clause, let it land in one line and move on.\n\nKeep the viewpoint character's reasoning available when it matters. A longer thought is justified when the character works a real calculation: whether to trust an offer, how to answer a summons, what an old debt costs now. Let it move through the specific facts of the world and the specific people involved, without padding the thought with general feeling.\n\nGround speech in its speaker. A soldier speaks bluntly; a noble speaks with cold precision; a servant says little and means a great deal. Do not make the cast uniformly polished or uniformly abrasive. The compressed style does not require every line to be short: change rhythm when the scene needs it, letting a long sentence carry exhaustion or a complicated obligation and then break against a short, hard fact.\n\nPreserve the active narrator, perspective, and world facts. The gritty treatment is a way of rendering the selected high-fantasy story, not a conversion into survival horror and not a stripping of the world's institutions, magic, and history.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires — a letter to a patron, a steward's report, a journal kept during a siege, a copy of an oath with marginal notes, a supply list that reveals what someone feared — rather than a mechanical rotation of formats.\n\nLet each document carry its author's social position and obligations. A younger sibling's letter may be full of careful deference while asking for something impossible; a report to a council may omit the one fact that would disgrace its writer; a journal entry may record what the writer needs to believe about their own motives. The document knows only what its author knows at that time and conceals what that author would conceal. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nWork Austen's social interpretation inside the documents. Show what a writer chooses to mention, leaves out, calls by a softer name, or insists upon too strongly. A gift may be described with elaborate gratitude while the letter's real business is to refuse a marriage or a command. A title may be written in full when the writer wants something and shortened when the writer feels safe. Let the reader see the gap between the document's performance and the situation it describes.\n\nLet the world move through gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts. A relic may be described differently in two reports; an oath may be quoted with a crucial clause missing in one letter and restored in another; a battle may be remembered as victory by one writer and disaster by another. Make the differences arise from each writer's position, knowledge, and interest, not random error. Material traces carry meaning too: a broken seal, a line crossed out and rewritten, good paper where a receipt should have been. When a later document corrects an earlier one, let the correction itself be an event — someone has chosen to tell the truth, or a different lie.\n\nPreserve readable continuity across entries through recurring names, places, objects, and obligations, so the reader can follow events and shifting relationships without a narrator stepping in to summarize. Keep the active narrator, perspective, and world facts. A document is a composed artifact with its own audience and permissions; it is not automatically its writer's complete live private thought.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness within the high-fantasy world. A character remembers how someone takes their tea, which blanket they prefer, which chair they always choose. A gift is small and practical — a sharpened knife, a copied map, a warm cloak left by the door — and specific to the people involved rather than a generic gesture. Let the comfort be earned by the scene's attention instead of announced.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to make the kindness legible. Show who notices a need without being asked, who offers help in a way that preserves the other person's pride, and who accepts care with a joke or a deflection. Courtesy can be warm without being empty: a gruff character still sets food aside for someone late; a shy character still remembers an important date. Affection is sincere without requiring a confession or an emotional repair.\n\nKeep the world active around the comfort. The characters may be resting between battles, waiting for a summons, or recovering from a magical cost; obligations and dangers do not disappear because the scene is warm. A character is kind while still worried about the border; a household shares a peaceful meal while a messenger's letter waits unopened. The comfort is real, but it neither makes everyone agreeable nor removes established problems.\n\nLet personality, privacy, and disagreement remain intact. A character may refuse help, change the subject, or accept care awkwardly; two people may disagree about a plan and still share a meal. Comfort does not require everyone to get along or to say what they feel. The scene may close on a small pleasure — a fire lit, a song remembered, a joke that lands despite everything — without forcing a resolution. The fluffy treatment is a way of rendering the selected high-fantasy story, not a replacement of the genre with a domestic vignette.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open each scene with enough social context to orient the reader — who is present, what obligation has brought them together, what the room or landscape says about their relative positions — without a block of exposition. Let context emerge through the scene's arrangements: a seat at the high table, a messenger kept waiting, a relic placed where everyone must see it.\n\nUse narrative distance to frame the scene through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. The narration may note that a particular courtesy has not been offered in that house since a certain exile, or that a title carries a debt its holder would rather forget. Keep the judgment owned by the narrator's chosen position and distance, and distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language: the prose may be more measured than the dialogue without becoming uniformly archaic or elaborate.\n\nApply Austen's free indirect judgment to the viewpoint character's interpretation, moving between the narrator's framing and the character's own beliefs without losing the distinction. The character may think they act from principle while the narration shows the personal cost being avoided; they may call a decision inevitable while the scene reveals the moment they could have chosen otherwise. Let free indirect discourse carry the character's actual reasoning, not a summary of it.\n\nLet the world's scale live in the novelistic movement. A paragraph may move from a single conversation to the history of the hall where it takes place and back to the character's hands on the table. An oath may be recalled in a long sentence that carries its full weight, then broken by a short fact in the present. Use ceremony, correspondence, and public/private contrast to organize a passage when the scene actually involves them: a public audience followed by a private exchange that revises what the public moment seemed to mean.\n\nPreserve the active narrator, perspective, and world facts. The formal architecture is the style, not a requirement for an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech. Keep other characters' private thoughts outside the viewpoint's knowledge unless the chosen contract grants access.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nTreat the outer scene as the primary social field. Before any embedded tale begins, establish the teller's practical purpose and the listeners' material positions in the same exchange: a steward tells an heir about a broken oath because the heir must decide whether to honor it; a dependent tells a patron about an old service because she needs the patron to remember what that service cost. The tale is not entertainment that happens to occur; it is a move in a negotiation, and the narration should show what the teller stands to gain, lose, or avoid by telling it now.\n\nLet the tale's selection and emphasis reveal the teller's position through Austen's free indirect judgment. The outer narration may register that the teller dwells on the cost of a magical compact while omitting who paid it, or that she describes a relic's history in terms that flatter the house now holding it. The omission is not a puzzle to solve but a social fact the focal listener can notice or miss. When the listener notices, the narration should mark the noticing as an interpretation owned by that listener: a steward may hear the omitted payer's name because he knows the household accounts; an heir may accept the flattering version because acceptance is useful to her. Keep the teller's motive legible through arrangement, not through a confession at the end.\n\nUse the high fantasy material as negotiable social evidence, not decoration. An oath should bind the teller's choices in the embedded story and explain a present obligation that the outer scene must now address; a relic should carry a specific claim about inheritance, service, or debt that one listener can confirm or contest; an ancient event should make a current alliance feel either secure or fragile. The tale's authority depends on the teller's standing, and the outer scene should register that dependence: a listener may believe a story because the teller's rank supports it, or doubt it because the teller's interest is visible. Repeated phrases and objects should help the reader connect the narrative levels by accruing meaning across tellings, not by certifying that the tale is true.\n\nKeep the narrative levels clearly separated. The outer scene remains in the active narrator's present action and conversation; the embedded tale may use its own temporal frame, but its beginning and end should be marked by the teller's address and the listeners' return to the room. A listener's interruption is a social move: a question about the tale's authority, a correction from someone who knows the events, or a request to continue that also changes the teller's advantage. When the tale ends, let one concrete element from it change how a listener reads the present: a repeated phrase attached to a promise, a title whose meaning has shifted, or a remembered cost that makes a current offer seem insufficient.\n\nLet the tale have consequences without requiring a cliffhanger. The telling may conclude a negotiation, alter a character's willingness to act, or leave an obligation newly understood. The scene may end with the listeners still absorbing what they heard, with a practical decision now possible, or with the teller's purpose only partly achieved. Do not force every embedded story to produce a reversal; its work may be to make a position socially legible, and that is enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through the social-realism route of the German-language tradition, with its documentary patience for how institutions record, maintain, and fail their obligations. Treat the high fantasy world's councils, orders, households, temples, guilds, and noble offices as working systems whose daily operation shapes what a character can afford, refuse, or ask. A magical oath is not only a dramatic vow; it has administrative consequences: who recorded it, who may release it, and what it costs the household that depends on it. A relic is not only a symbol; it is property with a history of maintenance, dispute, and use. Let the narration attend to the records, seals, accounts, and precedents that keep these obligations enforceable, and to the moments when the records no longer match the living situation.\n\nUse Austen's free indirect judgment to track a particular character's understanding of their position against the institutional record. Let the narration move between the scene's observable exchanges and the focal character's interpretation, marking where that interpretation is partial. A dependent may describe an offer as generous while the narration shows what the offer costs her; a steward may present a refusal as principle while the narration registers the income it protects. The narration may also compare what the character believes with what a document or account actually says: the character remembers an oath as unconditional, but the record shows a condition that has been forgotten. Keep the judgment owned by the focal character's perspective, not by an omniscient moralist.\n\nLet conversation carry multiple meanings through the material stakes already present in the scene. A courteous inquiry about a neighbor's health can also be a check on whether that neighbor can still meet an obligation. An offer of hospitality can test an alliance or create a debt. Do not make every exchange ironic; some courtesies are sincere, and some omissions are simply what a character does not know. The point is that speech occurs inside a structure of dependence, inheritance, and reputation, and the narration should make that structure legible without announcing it in exposition.\n\nConnect large-scale history to individual decisions through concrete consequences. An ancient war matters because it left a border that now blocks a marriage, a tax that now burdens an estate, or a title that now obliges a younger child to serve an order she never chose. Show the cost in the scene: a letter that cannot be sent, a journey that must be delayed, a household member whose labor is owed elsewhere. Let the mythic scale press on the situation from outside rather than replacing the immediate social situation.\n\nLet the narration proceed with the social-realism route's patience for how conditions develop over time. A character's understanding may change through repeated small exchanges rather than a single revelation. The scene may end with a decision made, a misunderstanding deepened, or a material fact newly understood. Do not require a dramatic reversal or a completed arc; the method's work is to make the institutional and social pressures legible in the present moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the high fantasy world's old authority be present as decaying infrastructure and intimate hierarchy. A great house may still command service while its roofs leak; a relic may retain legal force while its keeper's order has shrunk to a few aging members; an ancient oath may bind descendants who no longer believe in the power that first extracted it. The collision is not between good and evil but between inherited structure and present life, and it should be felt in the material details of the scene: the cost of maintaining a ceremonial hall, the labor of a servant whose family has served for generations, the worn emblem on a door that still decides who may enter.\n\nUse Austen's social masks as the surface through which this pressure moves, and let the focal character's free indirect judgment register the decay beneath the performance. Characters perform courtesy, gratitude, deference, or concern while their material dependence shapes what they can say. A dependent may thank a patron for a kindness that also binds her more tightly; a younger relative may praise an institution while privately calculating what it will cost him to leave it. The narration should mark the gap between performance and position through the focal character's attention to the material signs of decline: the damp patch on the ceiling above the patron's chair, the chipped seal that still authorizes a demand, the servant's worn livery that still enforces a boundary. Keep the judgment owned by that character rather than a detached narrator.\n\nAllow the social mask to fall away in trusted company, but make the intimacy specific and risky. A private conversation may let two characters speak plainly about an obligation they cannot name in public. The intimacy does not have to be romantic; it may be the shared knowledge of servants, siblings, or old allies who understand what the public performance costs. When the mask falls, the scene should register the relief and the danger together: someone may be overheard, or the plain speech may reveal a disagreement that courtesy had kept manageable. Let the plain speech be possible because the characters share a specific knowledge of the decaying institution, not because intimacy automatically grants honesty.\n\nTreat private desire as part of the political atmosphere. A character's wish to leave, to marry, to keep a child from an order, or to claim an inheritance is not a distraction from the high fantasy stakes; it is where the old authority presses hardest. Show how the desire is constrained by money, family expectation, or a magical obligation whose terms are still enforceable. Do not make every desire forbidden or every intimacy a trap; some alliances are genuine, and some refusals are simply what a character can afford.\n\nLet atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced. A meeting in a preserved council chamber should feel different from one in a kitchen where the old order's servants now do their practical work. Use the setting's decay and upkeep to make the power relations legible: who sits in the dry part of the room, who knows which door still locks, who must stand because the old chairs are reserved. The scene may end with a mask restored, a private understanding reached, or a character newly aware of what the old authority will cost them. Do not require a horror beat or a revelation; the pressure itself is the method's work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through the social/domestic realism route of the Indian literary traditions. Place the high fantasy material inside a household or community whose members must manage inherited obligations together, and let the narration track that management through the ordinary work of the house: who prepares the meal for the guest whose visit may settle a dispute, who keeps the relic clean and in which room, who knows the accounts well enough to say what a promise will cost. An oath may bind a family across generations; a relic may be kept in a particular room and maintained by a particular person; a title may determine who may speak first at a gathering or who must provide for a dependent relative. The mythic scale enters through these domestic arrangements, not through spectacle.\n\nUse Austen's attention to what people believe about themselves and what conversation reveals. Let the narration track a focal character's understanding of her or his position within the household, and let the domestic work register the gap between stated propriety and material need. A daughter may believe her refusal is private while the narration shows how the family's reputation already depends on it; an elder may present a decision as tradition while the narration registers the income or labor it protects. Keep the irony measured and owned by the focal perspective.\n\nLet conversation carry multiple meanings through the specific relationships in the scene. A question about a cousin's health can also be a question about whether that cousin will fulfill an obligation. An offer of food or shelter can express genuine care and also establish a claim. Do not make every exchange ironic; some courtesies are sincere, and some silences are simply what a character cannot yet say. The point is that speech occurs inside a web of family, community, and material dependence, and the narration should make that web legible through the exchange itself.\n\nConnect the high fantasy world's larger history to the household's practical life. An ancient conflict matters because it left a family divided, a marriage impossible, a piece of land contested, or a child promised to a temple or order. Show the consequence in the domestic scene: a meal prepared for a guest whose visit may settle a dispute, a letter read aloud that changes what the family can plan, a room kept ready for a member who may never return. Let the mythic scale press on the household from outside rather than replacing the immediate social situation.\n\nLet the narration proceed with social/domestic realism's patience for how relationships and conditions develop through repeated small exchanges. A character's understanding may change over several conversations rather than a single revelation. The scene may end with a decision made, a misunderstanding deepened, or a household member newly aware of what an obligation will cost. Do not require a dramatic reversal or a completed arc; the method's work is to make the domestic and social pressures legible in the present moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through the social/psychological realism route of the Japanese literary traditions. Treat the high fantasy world's obligations as social facts that a character must manage in company and alone, and let the narration render omission through attention. An oath may determine where a person may sit, whom she may address, or what she must not say; a relic may carry a claim that everyone in the room knows but no one states; an inherited title may shape the way a servant bows or a neighbor withholds a question. When a character chooses not to ask, not to correct, or not to mention, the narration should show what she attends to instead: the pause before a reply, the object she looks at, the phrase she repeats to herself afterward. The mythic scale enters through these precise social pressures, not through grand description.\n\nUse Austen's free indirect judgment to track what the focal character notices and what she or he omits. Let the narration move between observable exchange and inward response, marking where the character's understanding is partial. A young person may notice a guest's hesitation and interpret it as rudeness while the narration registers the debt that makes the guest hesitate. A dependent may describe an arrangement as proper while the narration shows the private cost she is learning to accept. Keep the judgment owned by the focal perspective.\n\nLet omission carry weight without making every omission a concealment. Some omissions are tact, some are uncertainty, and some are simply what the character cannot yet put into words. The narration should distinguish these by showing what the character knows and what she cannot yet say. A character may know an obligation would change the conversation and choose not to mention it; the narration registers the choice through her attention to the thing she does not say. A character may not yet know what she feels; the narration registers the uncertainty through her hesitation, not through a hidden confession.\n\nUse the social/psychological realism route's attention to how inward response develops over time. A character's feeling about an obligation may change through small repeated encounters: a courtesy that once felt natural now feels like a claim, a debt that once seemed distant now shapes a decision. Let the scene register this development in the present moment rather than announcing it as a completed change. The narration may end with the character still absorbing what has happened, with a decision now possible, or with a social situation newly understood.\n\nLet personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. If the active narrator is a participant, keep the observation within that character's knowledge and attention; if the narration is more detached, keep the detachment consistent with the active contract. Do not force a universal quietness or a seasonal inventory onto the scene. The method's work is to make the social and psychological pressures of the high fantasy world legible through precise observation and inward response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through the social realism/chronicle route of the Latin American literary traditions. Treat the high fantasy world's history as something the community records, repeats, and partially forgets, and let the narration compare the public account with what particular individuals privately know. An oath may survive in a local saying, a festival obligation, or a story told to children; a relic may be kept in a public place where its meaning has shifted over generations; an ancient event may still determine who may farm a field, enter a building, or claim a name. The mythic scale enters through this collective memory and its material consequences, not through isolated spectacle.\n\nUse Austen's attention to what people believe about themselves and what conversation reveals. Let the narration track the gap between what a community officially remembers and what a focal character privately knows. A public account may present an old alliance as honorable while a focal character knows what it cost her family; a courteous exchange may repeat a version of history that both speakers know is incomplete. Keep the irony measured and owned by the focal perspective, not by a detached narrator condemning the community.\n\nLet the chronicle route shape the information flow. The narration may move between the present scene and the remembered or recorded version of an event, but the levels should remain distinct. A character may recall what she was told, what she witnessed, and what she has since learned, and the narration should mark these as different kinds of knowledge. Do not blur memory and present observation for atmosphere. When a remembered event enters the scene, let it change how the character reads a current offer, refusal, or obligation.\n\nConnect the high fantasy world's larger history to the community's practical life. An ancient war matters because it left a boundary that still separates families, a debt that still shapes a local economy, or a promise that still obliges a particular household to provide a service. Show the consequence in the present scene: a neighbor who will not cross a certain threshold, a payment that must be made on a particular day, a name that opens or closes a door. Let the mythic scale press on the community from the past rather than replacing the immediate social situation.\n\nLet the narration proceed with social realism's patience for how conditions persist and change through ordinary exchanges. A character's understanding may develop over several encounters rather than a single revelation. The scene may end with a decision made, a public account newly questioned, or a private knowledge still unspoken. Do not require a dramatic reversal or a completed arc; the method's work is to make the weight of recorded history and present social pressure legible in the moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly through an obligation-bearing exchange. Open with the character already inside a social moment that matters: a summons, an offer, a refusal, a debt being called in, or a courtesy that carries an obligation. Establish the room and the positions of the speakers in a few concrete details so the reader can follow who may speak, who must wait, and what object or document sits between them. Do not begin with a history lesson; let the high fantasy world's rules appear through what the characters must do in this scene.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When a character moves, opens a door, receives a letter, or touches a relic, mark the action in its own short paragraph or clear beat. Let the movement carry social meaning: a dependent steps back from a threshold she may not cross, a steward places a seal on the table before he speaks, a younger relative remains standing while an elder sits. The staging should make the power relations visible without explaining them.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to carry Austen's inward judgment. After a significant line or offer, give the focal character a brief private response that registers the gap between the polite surface and the material cost: what she notices, what the offer will cost her, what she cannot say aloud. Keep the beat short and owned by that character. A reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or quietly calculating according to its owner; do not make every character witty or every pause ironic. The reaction beat is where the social interpretation lives, not in a narrator's summary.\n\nLet dialogue carry multiple meanings through the material stakes already present. A polite phrase can accept a debt, decline an alliance, or test a listener's loyalty. The narration should make the stakes legible through what the characters do with the words: a pause before agreement, a glance at the relic that enforces the oath, a question that is not answered. Do not explain every implication; let the focal character's reaction register the one that matters to her.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when they first affect the scene. If a title determines who may speak, show it in action before it becomes decisive. If a relic enforces a promise, let a character hesitate or comply in a way that shows the enforcement without a glossary. Keep the explanation inside the movement or dialogue, not in a separate exposition block.\n\nEnd the response at the active scene boundary. The scene may stop with a decision made, a new obligation understood, or a character still absorbing what has just been said. Do not require a cliffhanger, a completed test, or a reversal. The method's work is to make the high fantasy obligation and its social cost legible through clean staging, focused dialogue, and owned reaction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the narrator move between the large and the intimate within a single syntactic movement. A title, oath, relic, or ancient boundary may enter as a public fact, then turn toward the particular person who must answer for it or explain it. The turn should be felt in the sentence itself: a clause may open with the weight of an institution and close with a character noticing that someone has not met her eyes. Keep the image traceable to the scene or to the focal character's association, and let a plain statement follow a shaped passage when the contrast sharpens what has been understood.\n\nUse free indirect judgment with a clear owner. When the narration adopts a character's interpretation of another person's courtesy, hesitation, or offer, the prose may become more patterned or more partial, but it should not pretend to be neutral report. A lyrical comparison can carry that interpretation without making it true. For example, a character may see a rival's silence as the hush before an oath is broken; the scene may later show the silence belonged to a different private trouble. Render the misreading as beautiful in its own terms, and let the scene's later facts show what acting on it costs.\n\nGive magical or inherited power a social surface. The enchantment on a hall, the obligation attached to a name, or the history stored in a relic should affect what a conversation may ask and what a refusal may cost. Do not translate every magical fact into metaphor. When a character thinks of an ancient compact, let the thought include its practical consequence: who may enter, who owes service, what will be forfeited. Then let the lyric attention return to the small gesture that reveals how the character bears that consequence.\n\nKeep grandeur in the situation rather than in every speaker. A formal declaration may be brief, and a servant or younger relation may answer it with ordinary concern. The narrator's rhythm can rise when the scene's obligations rise, but it should not make every character speak in the same figurative register. End where the present exchange or reflection has reached its natural pause; a reply may close with an unresolved courtesy, a remembered phrase, or the weight of a decision still being carried.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet reflection begin from a particular social or material detail in the present scene: a chair left empty at a table, a title no longer used, a courtesy performed with less warmth than before, a household object associated with an earlier obligation. Move from that detail to what it recalls, but keep the memory attached to the person who is remembering. The narration may compare an earlier occasion with the present one without declaring that everything has declined. A familiar room may be smaller, a ritual may be shorter, and yet the present moment may still contain ordinary pleasure or a practical kindness.\n\nUse Austen's social attention to make loss legible through manners and dependence. A character may notice that an invitation no longer arrives, that a younger relation is now the one who manages the accounts, or that a formal phrase has become a private joke. Let the reflection include the material consequence: what the change means for income, position, access, or obligation. Do not let the magical or historical scale erase those small consequences. An ancient house may be diminished by a broken compact, but the scene should also show who now waits in the cold hall and who no longer has the right to enter.\n\nAllow memory to coexist with the present rather than replace it. A character may recall a promise while performing an ordinary task, or hear an old oath in a child's mispronunciation. The reflective passage may pause over that coincidence, but it should not force the character to conclude that the past has returned or that the present is only a shadow. When a remembered occasion gives the present scene its meaning, let the connection be specific: the same bell, the same road, the same phrase used by a different speaker.\n\nKeep the narrator's access consistent with the selected perspective. A focal character may reflect on what she believes another person remembers, but that belief remains hers. The scene may end with the reflection still open, with a small present action continuing, or with a change so slight that only the focal character has noticed it. Do not require a completed farewell, a recovered object, or a moral statement about time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral-social telling as the concrete regional route. Establish a situated storyteller and audience whose relationship to the events matters: a former steward recounting a household's negotiations, a guest describing another family's fall, a relation explaining why an invitation was refused. The frame should not be decorative. The audience's questions, interruptions, or evident interests may shape what the storyteller includes, and the storyteller's own position may limit what he or she knows. Keep the high-fantasy facts real: the oath, relic, or magical institution inside the story still changes what the characters could do, even when the teller reports it from a distance.\n\nLet Austen's social interpretation operate through the storyteller's measured judgment. The teller may remark that a character believed herself generous while the household understood her offer as an insult, or that a family's reputation rested on a courtesy no one now performed. Free indirect judgment inside the recounted scene should belong to the focal character of that scene, while the storyteller's irony belongs to the frame. Do not let the frame erase private experience. When the telling moves into a scene, render the character's actual thought or perception under the active access contract rather than reducing it to the storyteller's summary.\n\nKeep the account domestic and social rather than turning every magical element into spectacle. A compact may be remembered through the room where it was sworn, the cup that was refused, or the child who now bears the name. The storyteller may pause over such a detail, but the pause should serve the audience's understanding of obligation, reputation, or consequence. Ordinary pleasure and ordinary friction remain part of the account: a good meal, a delayed reply, a younger relation's impertinence, a guest's careful silence.\n\nLet the frame return without forcing a lesson. The audience may respond with recognition, correction, or a practical question, and the storyteller may answer or leave the matter open. The reply can end with the frame still in progress, with the storyteller pausing before the next part, or with the audience's attention turning to a present concern. Do not require the tale to resolve the magical or social question before the response ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect details that carry social and magical consequence at once. A ring on the wrong finger, a name omitted from a list, a door that opens for one guest and not another, a purse placed on the table before a refusal: each chosen detail should let the reader infer the obligation, relationship, or magical fact behind it. Do not explain the inference afterward. If a detail does not change what a character may do or what an exchange means, leave it out.\n\nUse direct clauses and let dialogue do social work. A character may say, \"You are very kind,\" while the narration records only that she did not sit down. The gap between speech and action is the interpretation. When private thought is needed, give the specific connection: what the speaker fears, what the offer would cost, what the other person does not know. Keep the thought long enough to make the connection clear, but do not repeat it in the next sentence.\n\nMake magical or inherited power visible through its material effect. A broken oath may appear as a locked gate, a cold hearth, a servant's hesitation, or a title that no longer commands an answer. The narration should not stop to explain the ancient history unless the present scene requires it. A character's knowledge of that history may appear in a single clause: she knew what the silence meant. Let the reader infer the rest from what follows.\n\nKeep the narrator's access and judgment exact. Free indirect style may compress a character's social reading into one sentence, but the reading belongs to that character. Another character's later action may correct it without the narration announcing the correction. End the response when the selected exchange or reflection has reached its natural pause. A reply may close with an unanswered question, a small action still in progress, or a consequence that has become clear to the focal character but not yet to the others.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the high-fantasy scene as continuous novel prose, beginning inside a particular moment of action, speech, or perception. Let the narrator move naturally from a character's observation of a room or a gesture to the social meaning of that detail, then to the practical consequence of an oath, title, or magical fact. Transitions should arise from the scene: a character notices who is absent, remembers the last time the hall was full, and understands what the empty place will cost. Do not pause the scene to deliver the history as a block unless the character's present attention actually returns to it.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation through the focal character's judgments, but keep those judgments owned. The narration may say that a hostess believed her generosity was evident while the guest saw only the distance of the offered room. When the prose moves into free indirect thought, the reader should know whose interpretation is shaping the sentence. Another character's later speech or action may complicate that interpretation without the narrator stepping back to explain the irony.\n\nLet magical power and inherited obligation remain material. A compact may determine who may enter a hall, who must answer a summons, or what a family may not sell. Show these consequences in the scene: a servant's hesitation, a locked door, a name that changes the tone of an introduction, a debt that turns an ordinary invitation into a test. The narration may move from that material fact to the character's private feeling about it, but the feeling should not replace the fact.\n\nKeep the paragraphing and transitions unobtrusive. A new paragraph may mark a shift from dialogue to perception, from present action to memory, or from one character's visible behavior to the focal character's inner response. Do not use headings, recap sentences, or explicit scene-setting machinery. The response may end at a natural pause in the scene, with an exchange unfinished, a decision still forming, or a character left alone with what has just been understood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBegin from the actual relationship premise supplied for the story: who these people are to each other, what obligation or history binds them, and what the relationship promises or threatens. Select scenes that make that premise legible through social exchange. A formal courtesy may be a private signal; a refusal may be a protection; an offer may carry a condition neither speaker names. Let the high-fantasy setting give the relationship its material pressure: an oath may forbid a visit, a title may make a friendship public, a magical debt may turn an ordinary kindness into a risk.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments as callbacks. A phrase, gesture, or object may return when the relationship has changed, and the repetition should show the change rather than merely restate it. For example, a character may again offer the same formal greeting, but this time the other person hears it differently, or a shared joke may become painful because the circumstances that made it possible are gone. Keep the callback specific to these characters and this premise; do not import a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where it belongs. Austen's social interpretation should keep the emotional beats from becoming abstract: show what the feeling costs in manners, money, reputation, or access. A character may be jealous not only of another person's attention but of the freedom that person's position allows. A moment of comfort may be complicated by the knowledge that accepting it creates an obligation. Let the narration move into authorized inner experience when the emotional consequence needs to be felt, but keep other characters' knowledge separate.\n\nLet the payoff fulfill the particular promise of the relationship rather than a generic resolution. The scene may end with a private understanding reached, a misunderstanding deepened, a comfort offered and not yet accepted, or a consequence that changes what the relationship can be. Do not require a confession, a kiss, a reconciliation, or a completed emotional arc in every reply. The response should leave the reader with the specific weight of this relationship as it now stands.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a deliberate chronicle movement: a compact declaration of who someone is, what obligation was sworn, or what act is now remembered. Then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. A woman may be remembered as the one who refused the hall, but the scene shows the refusal as a social act: what was offered, what it would have cost, and who understood her meaning. Let the high-fantasy material organize the account through oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds where the story establishes them.\n\nUse recurring phrasing when a later occurrence gives it a new application. A title or epithet may return after the person has changed, or a remembered phrase may be spoken by a different character in a different situation. The repetition should deepen the record rather than decorate every entrance. Keep the chronicler's public reputation distinct from a focal character's private account. Being remembered as steadfast does not mean the character felt no hesitation, and the narration may enter that private experience under the active access contract.\n\nLet Austen's social interpretation work through the chronicler's measured judgment. The account may note that a family believed itself generous while the household remembered the conditions attached to the gift. When the telling moves into a scene, free indirect judgment should belong to the focal character of that scene, not to an omniscient moral voice. Dialogue may interrupt the authority of the chronicle: a character may correct a detail, answer a grand introduction with a practical concern, or reveal that the remembered deed looked different from inside the house. This need not become parody.\n\nKeep the oral form consistent. The account may relate an act to what will be remembered, but it should not declare every modest exchange legendary. A courtesy may be recorded because it changed an obligation, not because it was heroic. The response may end with the chronicle pausing before a later consequence, with a deed still being weighed, or with the teller returning to the present moment of retelling. Do not require a completed fate or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in a restrained social-realist third person, close enough to one focal character to render private judgment but distant enough to measure that judgment against the room. Let high fantasy's scale enter through practical channels: a title changes who may sit first, a relic must be inventoried before it may be used, an oath obliges a household to feed a guest, and an old defeat still determines which family may hold an office. Treat these as material conditions, not as decoration.\n\nShow what people believe about themselves through the small exchanges that expose it. A courteous refusal may protect a dependent, insult a rival, or avoid a debt the speaker cannot pay. Let money, land, reputation, and family connection give an offer more than one meaning. When the narrator reports a character's private reasoning, keep that reasoning owned by the character: the narration may know what she thinks, but another person in the scene does not.\n\nLet institutions work slowly and imperfectly. A council may spend a session on a boundary dispute while a magical threat waits outside. A steward may record an ancient obligation in the same ledger as grain. The mythic and the domestic should press on each other: a ritual requirement can force an awkward family conversation, and a household shortage can make an inherited duty impossible to perform. Do not resolve that pressure by making everyone speak formally or by pausing the scene for a history lecture.\n\nKeep the Scandinavian social-realist route specific. Prefer the observed interior and social consequence of a Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish domestic situation over snow, isolation, or generalized Nordic bleakness. If the supplied world has a particular region, let its institutions and habits shape the scene; do not invent a Northern landscape or temperament for it. The narration may be dry, exact, and quietly ironic, but the irony must belong to the narrator's measured view of the characters, not to a universal mockery of their world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Screenplay\n\nWrite concise action lines that make social meaning visible. A character sets a sealed relic on the table before speaking, steps back from an offered chair, or waits for a steward to finish recording a name. Let the blocking and objects carry the weight of high fantasy's obligations: a title changes who enters first, an oath changes who may touch a chest, and an old institution changes what may be said in a particular room.\n\nKeep dialogue attributed and let each line do more than one social task. A polite question may test an alliance, delay a request, or remind a listener of a debt. Do not force every character to speak with polished formality. The mythic setting affects what people can do and what a choice costs; it does not require the whole cast to sound like a proclamation.\n\nUse a scene heading when location or time changes, and keep transitions selective. A cut may move from a council chamber to a servant's corridor, or from a public refusal to the private room where the refusal must be explained. Offscreen sound may carry a ritual bell, a closing gate, or a distant magical alarm only when it changes the scene's pressure. Do not fill the page with camera commands.\n\nWhen private judgment matters, place it in a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not in dialogue and not in an unmarked voice-over. The note may record what a focal character believes another person meant, what an offer would cost her family, or why she chooses silence. Other characters do not know this note. Let the next visible action or line confirm, complicate, or leave that private reading unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Stage Play\n\nLet the stage space make social and institutional meaning playable. An entrance can show who may enter without being announced. A chair, a sealed box, a household ledger, or a ritual cup can become the object around which a conversation turns. Keep stage directions economical: state who moves, what they touch, and what pause matters, then let the lines and business carry the scene.\n\nUse clear speakers and let dialogue reveal what people believe about themselves. A courteous phrase may be a genuine kindness, a claim to rank, or a way of postponing a request. Let money, family, reputation, and obligation give an exchange more than one meaning. Do not make every character speak in formal high-fantasy diction. The setting's oaths and titles change what a person may do, but ordinary speech can still expose embarrassment, ambition, or relief.\n\nMake the mythic obligations stageable. A character may be required to remain standing until a name is spoken, to hand over a relic before crossing a threshold, or to repeat an oath before opening a door. These actions should cost something visible: time, dignity, access, or a relationship. Do not explain the world's history in a block of exposition. Let the characters' practical handling of the obligation show what it means.\n\nKeep private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. A monologue or aside may be used only when it has a dramatic purpose and its audience is clear; it is not the default container for thought. The authoring material may record what a focal character notices, suspects, or decides not to say. Another character on stage does not hear it. The next line or action may support or complicate that private reading without making the cast telepathic.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized focal mind and let its associations structure the passage. Perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, and interruption may move together: the weight of a sealed letter recalls the oath taken in the hall, the smell of old stone recalls a family's lost claim, a polite phrase in the present recalls the same phrase used as a threat. Keep enough orientation that the reader can distinguish the present scene from recollection or imagined consequence.\n\nLet Austen's social interpretation operate inside that associative movement. The focal mind may notice what a courtesy concealed, what an offer implied about money, or what a silence did to a dependent's reputation. These readings may be partial, self-serving, or corrected by a later perception. Do not make the character perfectly perceptive. The mind's private reasoning is its own; other characters do not share it.\n\nLet high fantasy's obligations recur as felt pressures rather than as explained history. A title may return as a phrase the character cannot say without consequence. A relic may return as the memory of a hand closing over it. An institution may return as the sound of a door that will not open. The mythic scale should change what the character can do and what a choice costs, but it should arrive through the mind's movement, not through a block of worldbuilding.\n\nKeep the mode genuinely associative. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow the mind's jumps, repetitions, and interruptions. Do not reduce the passage to orderly deliberation with a few memories attached. At the same time, do not make every scene fact indeterminate. Events still occur; the focal mind's experience of them is the route, not the erasure of the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the scene through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one sender writes formally and edits repeatedly, another sends short replies, another leaves a message unread while attending to household work. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the exchange. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet Austen's social interpretation work through what people send, omit, and imply. A polite message may be a genuine reassurance, a demand for acknowledgment, or a way of avoiding a direct refusal. A delay may mean hesitation, preoccupation, or a deliberate slight. Let money, family, reputation, and obligation give the same phrase more than one meaning. Do not make every sender equally polished or equally perceptive.\n\nLet high fantasy's obligations enter the exchange as transmitted claims and practical constraints. A steward may forward a ritual requirement with a note about the cost. A title may determine how a sender addresses another person. An oath may prevent a character from sending a particular message, or force them to send one they fear. A relic may appear as an attachment, a shared image, or a description whose accuracy another sender questions. These elements should change what a character can do and what a decision costs, not become decorative fantasy jargon.\n\nKeep the boundary between transmitted text and private experience clear. A sent message is what the sender chose to transmit. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the sender's live private thought. If context outside the exchange is needed, separate it clearly from the transcript. Private thought may be shown in the active presentation as nonspoken interiority, but it is not a message another character can read.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. A background cue may establish the hall, the household shrine, the sealed archive, or the garden where a private conversation becomes possible. An expression cue may orient a reaction, but it is not an automatic truth indicator. A changed smile can attract suspicion; a contradiction in an account or arrangement must do the interpretive work.\n\nLet focal narration and inner response carry Austen's social interpretation. The viewpoint may notice what a courtesy concealed, what an offer implied about money, or what a pause did to a dependent's standing. These readings may be supported, complicated, or shown to be mistaken by the next exchange. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals their motive.\n\nLet high fantasy's obligations appear as legible scene conditions and material costs. A title may determine who may sit, enter, or speak first. A relic may be present in the scene and change what a character dares to do. An oath may require a particular phrase before a door or chest is opened. An institution may send a messenger whose arrival changes the room's pressure. These elements should affect choices and costs, not serve as background decoration.\n\nKeep scene changes legible, but do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected. A discovery may open a new conversation rather than force a choice screen. A relationship consequence may remain unsettled after a factual or social question is answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahakbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jane Austen / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what obligation is underway, what has changed locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps. If an earlier installment established that a family cannot pay a ritual tax, a later installment may show the steward arriving to collect it. If a character refused an offer, a later installment may show the refusal being discussed in another household. Do not retell the whole history each time.\n\nLet Austen's social interpretation develop cumulatively. A courtesy shown in one installment may be revealed later as a claim, a kindness, or a mistake. A character's private reading of another person may be supported or complicated by a later exchange. Let money, family, reputation, and obligation give repeated phrases new meanings. Do not make every character perfectly perceptive, and do not force a confession or reversal in each installment.\n\nLet high fantasy's obligations persist as material conditions. A title may still determine access several installments after it is first mentioned. A relic may remain under seal while its legal owner changes. An oath may bind a character across multiple scenes, and an institution may continue its slow work in the background. These conditions should change what people can do and what a decision costs, not become static worldbuilding notes.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. A reply may end after a completed task, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. The serial style does not automatically create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet an inherited obligation create the specific occasion for emotional exposure. A title may require a character to speak publicly about a loyalty they cannot privately justify; an oath may compel them to accept help from someone they have hurt; a relic or ancient debt may force them to return to a place or person they have been avoiding. Do not announce the cost in general terms. Show the moment when the demand of the public task makes a private admission, request, apology, or refusal harder to complete.\n\nGive the hurt room to move through a scene in the Jin Yong manner. Travel, explanation, and practical alliance work can carry the emotional strain forward instead of pausing for a single confrontation. A character may speak around the wound while negotiating a road or a mission, then return to it when a companion asks the wrong question or offers the wrong help. Let the strain affect subsequent behavior: a kindness accepted with visible difficulty, a task performed too carefully, a silence that changes the next exchange. The private feeling need not be named by the character or resolved by the scene.\n\nTreat comfort as partial and consequential. Warmth may make the hurt more bearable or more painful, may be accepted without being returned, or may arrive too late to undo an earlier withholding. Let the focal character's private experience remain distinct from what others know. A close narrator may render the pressure of an unspoken need in fragments; a more distant narrator may observe the visible cost of a choice without entering every mind. The style is a tonal treatment of high fantasy, not a tragic ending or a fixed vocabulary of suffering.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep each speaker's words clearly assigned, and use short narrative beats between speeches to show movement, reaction, or the active world without burying the exchange in dense description. A beat may record a character reaching for a relic, checking a road, or hesitating before an oath-bound answer.\n\nMake high fantasy obligations actionable within the interaction. When a title, relic, oath, or institution presses on a character, let it shape what they can offer, refuse, or ask for in the current exchange. An NPC may act on a competing loyalty, withhold help because of an old debt, or extend protection that carries a future cost. The world should continue its practical life during the scene: a messenger may arrive, a ritual may require attention, or the party may need to decide how far to travel before nightfall.\n\nGive the encounter room for travel, explanation, and relationship work in the Jin Yong manner. A journey can include talk about the road, the mission, and the people involved. Let action arise from particular decisions rather than a sequence of blows. End at a natural continuation point that follows from what has just been said or done, leaving the next move open without requiring a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the martial adventure route through a vernacular episodic telling. Open each episode in the middle of a journey, errand, or dispute, and let the narration explain what the characters already know only when the road or the encounter makes it relevant. Close the episode with a consequence that carries into the next one: a debt incurred, an alliance strained, a reputation changed, or a question about an oath left unanswered.\n\nLet high fantasy's mythic scale enter through the weight of inherited obligations. A title, relic, oath, or ancient event should change what a character can do and what a decision costs. When a magical power or technique appears, show its use as a choice with social and moral weight: a blow withheld to spare an enemy, a binding accepted to protect a companion, a display of power that frightens the people it was meant to save. Do not let the fantastic become a mere spectacle.\n\nGive encounters room for travel and talk in the Jin Yong manner. A journey can include explanation of the road, the mission, and the people involved. Let competing loyalties surface in what characters choose to reveal or conceal, and let reputation precede a character into a new place. The episodic structure should feel continuous, with each episode advancing the larger story through action, explanation, or a shift in allegiance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful diction and compressed transitions. Cut from one concrete action to the next without explanatory padding, but let each cut carry the cost of the interval: a road that has worn down supplies and sleep, a night watch that left someone too tired to argue, a delay that changed the terms of an alliance. Material wear should be specific and consequential, not a list of damaged objects.\n\nMake high fantasy's scale physical. An oath may burn or bind when broken; a relic may exact a price in blood, memory, or service; a title may force a choice between shelter and pursuit. Show the mythic through its effect on the body and the practical world. Do not let the fantastic become abstract or ornamental.\n\nPreserve Jin Yong's room for travel and relationship within the compressed form. A journey can be rendered as a sequence of concrete changes: a road narrowing, a guide growing silent, a companion's limp worsening. An explanation can be carried by a single exchange or a practical demonstration rather than a block of exposition. Let technique and restraint matter in particular decisions: a blow pulled, a weapon lowered, a reputation risked. Use rhythm changes deliberately; a longer thought is justified when the character's reasoning must weigh a loyalty, a debt, or a dangerous choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the document's position in time control what it can know. A letter written before a battle cannot foresee its outcome; a journal entry composed after a betrayal may reinterpret an earlier kindness. What the document conceals follows its author's situation: a letter to an ally may omit a private doubt, a report to an institution may flatten a personal cost into a line item, and a record meant for posterity may revise an inconvenient fact.\n\nLet high fantasy's obligations press through the documents. An oath may appear as a signed covenant with conditions and witnesses; a relic as an inventory entry whose description changes across hands; an ancient event as a disputed chronicle. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts should reveal how the same obligation looks from different positions. Material traces can carry what the words omit: a torn seal, a blotted line, a letter never sent, a report corrected in another hand.\n\nUse Jin Yong's sense of shared history and competing loyalty. A document can be an active intervention: a letter that secures cooperation, a journal that explains why someone once remained silent, a report that challenges a reputation. Preserve readable continuity across entries by letting each document respond to or revise an earlier one. Use the specific document form required by the scene rather than a fixed rotation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness and practical care within the high fantasy world. Let the comfort answer the particular strain of the characters' situation: a companion who remembers which food the oath-bound traveler can still eat, a repair made before a dangerous road, a quiet hour given to someone who has been performing a public duty. A familiar preference should be grounded in shared history, not invented for the moment.\n\nKeep the fantasy's obligations present without letting them erase the comfort. A character may still carry an oath, a debt, or a dangerous mission, but the scene can give them room to rest, eat, or be known by someone else. The warmth may make the burden easier to carry without removing it. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or erase established problems from the world. Disagreement and privacy can remain intact, and a kindness may be accepted awkwardly or only in part.\n\nUse Jin Yong's room for travel and relationship. A journey can include a pause for care, an explanation that builds trust, or a small pleasure that reminds a character why an alliance matters. Let the warmth arise from particular people and their history: a gesture that recalls an earlier kindness, a preference remembered from a shared journey, a moment of ease that makes the next obligation feel survivable rather than solved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open a scene with enough narrative distance to establish the social or historical meaning of an obligation: the reputation that precedes a character, the institutional weight of a title, the memory of an ancient event that still shapes present choices. Then move into the particular exchange or decision, letting the private moment complicate the public meaning rather than merely illustrate it.\n\nLet high fantasy's scale enter through the weight of inherited obligations and their material consequences. A public ceremony, a correspondence, or a contrast between private feeling and public duty may organize a passage when relevant. Show how an oath or institution changes what a character can do in the scene: a formal duty that forbids a private kindness, a public role that requires a lie, a historical debt that makes a present alliance fragile. Do not assume an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary; the formal architecture is the style, not the cast or the speech.\n\nUse Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation. A character may speak in one register to an institution and another to a companion. The narration can distinguish those registers without making the whole cast uniformly elaborate. Keep the focal character's private experience available under the chosen narrator's permissions, and let the social frame deepen rather than replace the particular loyalty at stake.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside a telling that has somewhere to arrive: a speaker, an audience, an occasion, and something the telling is for—an alliance to secure, a debt to settle, a ruler to persuade, a companion to warn. Let that present pressure shape the account from its first sentence, so the embedded story is the scene's action rather than a pause around it.\n\nGive the tale a full body in the Jin Yong manner: travel, arrival, encounter, decision under constraint. When the remembered version of a character uses a technique, honors a restraint, or risks an alliance, show the particular choice and its cost, because those costs are still running in the room where the tale is told. Bind the tale's substance to the world's working machinery of titles, relics, oaths, institutions, and ancient events: a name spoken in the story can be a living listener's debt, a recited oath one of them has also sworn, a relic's origin the reason a present border exists.\n\nMake the teller's selection and emphasis a visible act of loyalty. Praise, omission, softening, and lingering are choices, and the narration can let the reader feel the shaping without announcing it; where the narration reaches the teller's mind, the private weight of an omission is ordinary scene, not a separate confession. Keep the listeners live during the telling: one returns to a question of timing or sequence, another recognizes a scar, a phrase, a half-remembered ritual, and that recognition can change what they concede next.\n\nLet recurrence carry structure: a phrase or object spoken inside the tale returns at the outer level with its accrued meaning. Close the response wherever the exchange actually stands—mid-tale, at a return to the frame, or in the beat after a revelation alters a promise—without pausing the outer business for a completed moral, and without importing a royal court, a magical bargain, or a compulsory cliffhanger between nested accounts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the Romantic and uncanny route. Let the world's history be present in its geography as active pressure: a pass where an oath was broken changes how travelers cross it, a boundary river still enforces an old compact, a ruined hall bends the conduct of everyone who enters. Deliver that pressure through perception and body—a chill at a threshold, a silence where there should be sound, a reluctance the character cannot yet name—so a place's meaning is felt before it is explained, if it is explained at all.\n\nAnchor every uncanny effect to a particular fact of the world: the specific oath, relic, institution, or ancient event it belongs to. A watcher-feeling on the road should be traceable to the compact that road still serves; a voice in the wind to a duty someone died refusing. The uncanny sharpens consequence; it does not replace it.\n\nRun the Jin Yong profile's clarity of decision underneath the atmosphere. When a character uses a technique, observes a restraint, calls on an alliance, or conceals a loyalty, show the choice and its price in movements the reader can follow. Give encounters room to travel and talk: two riders arguing over whether to ford at the old boundary, a mentor explaining what a marker means while privately doubting the story she was taught, that doubt carried as ordinary narration between speeches. Let characters misread a landscape's warning, or find that a place remembers an event differently from the official account; that discrepancy can matter more than a battle.\n\nLet syntax follow the mode: longer, image-bearing sentences where landscape presses on people; direct, particular dialogue and short decisive movement when a choice must be made. Do not equate this route with one country, bureaucracy, severity, or translated word order—the uncanny belongs to this world's own history and magic, not to imported scenery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Gothic-Punk\n\nStage each scene as a collision between inherited authority and the present that must live with it. Render the world's grand structures at the scale of their decay and their daily use: a guild hall with a leaking roof where an oath is still sworn, a bloodline down to its last heir and last working relic, an office that enforces a magical compact no one now remembers founding. Atmosphere arrives through objects the characters actually handle—peeling insignia, a debt ledger, the condition of a coat or weapon—and it should change how a particular encounter is experienced, not substitute for the encounter.\n\nUse the Jin Yong profile's craft of technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation inside this decay. Test standing through the specific situation: a duel nobody wants, a favor owed to an institution that protects no one anymore, a restraint kept for a dead master's sake. Make social masks do real work. Before witnesses a character performs the role—loyal sword, reasonable magistrate, grateful ward—and in trusted company the mask can come off, exposing desire, fear, or an attachment that complicates the obligation. Play that shift in concrete signs: the voice dropping its ceremony, hands unclenching, what is finally said once the title is out of the room.\n\nKeep the politics intimate. Large obligations press on particular bodies and relationships: service that outlives any reward, love aimed at someone whose loyalty is pledged elsewhere, an inherited power that is also an inherited bill. Show the cost in choices and their consequences—a refusal, a payment, a secret carried one more season—rather than in generalized dread, and leave room for an ally inside the old institution to choose genuine help.\n\nDo not import vampire physiology, humanity metrics, factions, or blood-bond rules. The treatment is the collision of old authority and present life, carried by this world's own oaths, relics, and institutions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalak::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route: a public telling with a defined occasion and a community present. Set the recitation where it does work—a gathering after a battle, a council before an oath is sworn, a festival at which a house recites its lineage and its debts—and let the teller speak for a household, garrison, or people rather than only for themselves. The tale is common property; its obligations bind nearly everyone listening.\n\nMove the embedded narrative with the Jin Yong profile's expansive clarity: journeys, arrivals, alliances formed and strained, decisions made under constraint, rendered in particular moments rather than summary. Within the tale, show how a title, relic, oath, or ancient event changed what people could do and what a choice cost, and let technique, restraint, and reputation express the values behind each act.\n\nTreat the audience as participants in the history they are hearing. They can correct a detail, supply a name the teller avoids, dispute an emphasis, claim their own house's part in the events, or fall quiet at a debt that touches them directly. Treat these interventions as live plot: a correction reopens a grievance, a supplied name forces a promise into the open, a dispute over emphasis shows where loyalties actually sit. Let a character who honored one obligation by breaking another stand at the center of the telling, and let the listeners divide over whether the choice was just, without the narration issuing a single settled moral.\n\nMark returns to the occasion clearly, and let repeated names, phrases, or objects accrue meaning as the telling comes back to the people hearing it. Keep language, region, and community specific to the supplied setting; do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalal::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the early-modern comic and theatrical route. Stage encounters with performative logic: characters enter and exit deliberately, announce intentions before acting, name their technique or lineage as they use it, and treat one another's moves as performance before an audience. Let the world's oaths, relics, and institutions appear as roles with spectators—a vow made before witnesses, a title that must be answered for at court, a duel that is also a public statement.\n\nKeep the Jin Yong profile's principle that action expresses values. A formal challenge, a refusal to draw, an offer of alliance, a comic misunderstanding that turns serious: each act should show what its actor holds worth defending. Exuberance, wordplay, and open declaration of feeling belong to characters and occasions that warrant them—a brash duelist, a theatrical foe, an embarrassed veteran—not to the whole cast as a uniform tone.\n\nLet public role and private feeling collide productively. A character performs loyalty while privately doubting it; another discovers a comic pose adopted for safety has hardened into a real obligation. Make that private experience available as ordinary narration between the spoken lines—the held breath during an announcement, the flat assessment of an opponent hidden under flattery—without turning every pause into a confession or concealed guilt.\n\nKeep the staging readable: clear spatial positions, clean transitions among action, dialogue, and inward response, reactions that register visibly in the scene. Seasonal touches, omission, and plain argument are available where the moment wants them; impose no universal quietness and no mandatory inventory of scenery. The theatrical method belongs to this world's own institutions and performances, not to imported stages or borrowed repertoires.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalam::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the chronicle and testimony route. Let the world's history reach the present through attributed accounts: a soldier's recollection, a courier's report, a survivor's testimony, a community chronicle read aloud, a house's kept record. Give each account a teller with a stake—someone who fought, carried, hid, or lost—and let that stake shape what the account admits, softens, or leaves for others to fill in.\n\nMark, in the texture of each telling, the difference between what a witness personally did or saw and what they heard, inferred, or learned afterward. A soldier knows the field's mud and the order he was given; what he knows of the treaty comes from rumor. Treat this distinction as the scene's working condition, not a puzzle with a single solution: the present decision must be made on partial, humanly shaped accounts, and the narration can show which parts carry direct memory and which carry borrowed certainty.\n\nUse the Jin Yong profile's competing loyalties inside each testimony. One witness protects a companion; another justifies a choice made under duress; a third recites the chronicle's smoothed version while privately remembering the cost it leaves out. Let the outer scene set accounts beside one another—two tellers disagreeing on when a gate opened, an official record that names no one for a defeat—so shared history becomes something the living must still negotiate rather than settled backdrop.\n\nConnect scale to bodies and debts. A great war or ancestral compact matters through what it still extracts: a tithe, a garrison obligation, a name unspeakable in one valley. Let testimony intervene in the present—altering what a character is willing to promise, forgive, or refuse—rather than merely recording the past. Preserve the setting's own language, community, and history; this route shapes attribution and information flow and adds no supernatural facts the world excludes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalan::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Light Novel\n\nEnter the situation in motion: within the opening beats establish where the characters are, what they are trying to accomplish, and which obligation or danger already presses. Keep the physical layout legible throughout—doors, roads, halls, shifts in footing or light—so that when a fight or a flight happens, the reader can track who is where without rereading.\n\nBreak paragraphs for readability around three registers: action, dialogue, and private response. Give each register its full weight: a technique used, a restraint kept, or an alliance invoked gets its particular decision and cost, in the Jin Yong manner, and a conversation receives room to travel, explain, and build or strain a relationship, so that talk can carry as much consequence as combat. Do not reduce an encounter to a sequence of blows.\n\nWhen an unfamiliar name, relic, oath, or magical mechanic first matters, present it clearly enough to use: what the title permits, forbids, or costs; what the relic does and what it takes. Deliver this through whichever channel the active narration actually has—a character's explanation, an observed effect, an inward note—rather than stopping the scene for a glossary entry.\n\nGive each reaction beat to its owner. A response to a revelation, insult, or summons may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny, as that character is, and registers the weight of a large-scale obligation without inflating every beat into a dramatic pause. End the response at the scene's living boundary—an exchange still open, a departure underway, a decision forming—rather than at a manufactured cliffhanger; first person, comedy, and a young cast remain available choices, not requirements of this container.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalao::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the weight of an oath, relic, or inherited duty enter through sustained attention to one charged image. When a character touches a seal, crosses a threshold, or hears a title spoken, follow the image as it gathers association: the cold of the metal, the sound of the name in a different voice, the distance between what the object promised and what the hand now holds. Shape the syntax so rhythm carries the experience — hold a clause a beat longer than expected where attention lingers, let a sound repeat softly across a line, then break the cadence with a plain sentence that names the practical cost. Return to the image after the interruption changed, with the cost inside it. A comparison earns its place when it alters what the narrator understands or makes present; trace every figure back to the scene or the teller's associations rather than to ornament.\n\nGive travel and conversation room through shifts of attention. A journey is a sequence of perceptions that carry the relationship between companions: what one notices, what the other avoids, what the landscape makes them remember. Bind separate moments with recurring sounds or images — the same river, color, or phrase returning later with a different weight because an obligation has changed what it signifies — without announcing the connection or sounding the echo at every difficulty. Let a companion's plainness or evasion stand against the narrator's figurative attention; the cast need not speak in the lyrical register, and their flat answers sharpen the music around them.\n\nWhen action expresses a value, render the decision through body and environment together: the pressure of a hand, the delay of a breath, a familiar room suddenly too small for what is being refused. Choose the moments where interior attention and external consequence meet, and let the rest move at the speed of event rather than slowing every gesture into contemplation. A reply may stop mid-journey, mid-argument, or mid-recognition, the larger obligation still active and the gathered image carrying its cost into whatever comes next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalap::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the present scene carry an earlier occasion without stopping to explain it. A character recognizes a hall by the way light falls at the same hour, or hears a phrase that once belonged to a different speaker; the memory rises out of that particular detail, holds only as long as attention holds it, and returns to the present changed. Keep the double exposure deliberate — what is happening now stays available, work and conversation and weather, while what is no longer available sits beside it. Ordinary pleasure remains pleasurable even when shadowed; do not hollow warmth in advance or make every recollection painful.\n\nUse the tension between private attachment and public obligation as the material for reflection. When a duty requires something, notice the small irreversible change that accompanies it: a gesture not made, a name spoken formally for the first time, a belonging left with someone who may not return it. The oath, relic, or inherited charge still does what the world says it does — commands, protects, costs — and reflective attention reveals what the choice spends rather than summarizing the loss. What the focal character knows and feels stays distinct from what companions can see; they may read the new formality as coldness and be wrong.\n\nLet reflection select which moments receive room. A journey may be told through the places that recall a previous departure; a conversation through the sentences that will be remembered later; an exchange of courtesies through what courtesy used to mean between these people. Action keeps its pace and its consequences — the style slows to notice, not to halt — and time behaves honestly: a moment of attention is a moment long, no more. A reply can end with the character still carrying both the active obligation and the remembered attachment, partway through either, with no lesson about impermanence owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalaq::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the story as a situated telling. Establish a teller, an audience, and an occasion — whatever the supplied scene provides, from a road camp to a formal audience — and keep that presence live: impatience, a listener's question, an offered correction, or the teller's deliberate withholding can interrupt or steer the tale. Where the active narration already supplies a perspective, the teller is that voice; the frame shapes address and pacing, not a different mind. Fix early how the teller knows what they relate — as participant, witness, or recipient of another account — and hold that access: what was seen rendered with a witness's particulars, what was had from others marked as report, what cannot be known left outside the tale.\n\nWithin the frame, keep the telling expansive. Encounters retain room for travel, explanation, and relationship, but the teller paces the material as tellers do: a month of road compressed into a sentence, one conversation given in full because it changed what the teller understood. A pause over a custom, title, or old alliance should answer the audience's immediate need, not become a lecture. Technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation matter through the particular decisions the teller chooses to relate, and recurrence — an oath formula, a name, a phrase returning at a heavier application — is the teller's craft. The frame is presentation, not a substitute for the nonspoken experience the active contract authorizes: the teller can hold a private thought in the moment of telling, and the tale's figures keep whatever interiority the perspective allows.\n\nMake the world's obligations concrete inside the tale: an oath, relic, or ancient event changes what a character can do and what a decision costs, shown as action and consequence rather than declared fate. Return to the frame between episodes to let the audience react or the teller delay a name until it will carry weight. Use the setting, language, and community actually supplied; antiquity, mysticism, exile, religion, or a single honor code is not a scene requirement. The telling can rest with the teller pausing, the audience pressing for more, or the tale between episodes, its threads unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalar::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Minimalist Prose\n\nCarry the weight of an oath, relic, or inherited obligation in a small number of exact details. One object on a table, one name spoken, one scar can stand for a larger history without explanation. Place each detail in action — a hand stops before touching it, a speaker pauses on the name, a door is closed more carefully than usual — and state the action and its object while leaving the evaluation out, so the cost is inferred from what the character does and leaves unsaid. Cut description and commentary, never causality: omission should leave the why reconstructible, not missing.\n\nMove through encounters with direct clauses and selective perception. Travel, explanation, and relationship keep their room, but the prose advances by precise observation: the change in light, the weight of a pack, the first sight of a known wall, what each speaker notices and what each avoids. A sentence may run as long as the thought it carries requires; do not perform brevity in fragments. Keep only the inward connection that materially contributes — one essential thought stated once, in whatever words make it exact — and let each speaker's voice stay individual inside the economy, since terse is not interchangeable.\n\nLet technique, restraint, and reputation matter through single decisions. Show skill in one economical action, loyalty in one refusal, an alliance in one shared task; the pause before a strike or the way a companion moves to cover a weakness expresses value more exactly than an account of the fight. Action is more than a sequence of blows and need not become a chronicle of them. Place the last detail and stop there — mid-exchange, mid-departure, or at the threshold of a decision — with the next consequence inferable and nothing required to resolve first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalas::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves without announcement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the inner experience the active perspective authorizes. Let the world's scale arrive through particulars: a character enters a hall built by an older power, receives a message that changes an obligation, recognizes a relic by its weight. History surfaces in what the characters know, notice, and avoid — a hesitation before a name, a road taken the long way — rather than in a pause for exposition. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient: a break shifts attention, a changed subject marks time passing, and nothing restates what the scene already showed.\n\nGive encounters their room. Travel, explanation, and relationship unfold across the scene instead of compressing into a sequence of events: a conversation shifts from practical arrangement toward the tension between private attachment and public duty, and the narration follows the shift through hesitation, a change in forms of address, a memory arriving mid-sentence and kept or dismissed. What the focal character knows and feels remains distinct from what others can see; a companion may misread the hesitation as insult, weakness, or agreement. Dialogue carries relationship as much as plot, and register follows the narrator's voice and the immediate moment — formality where this world earns it, plainness where the speaker chooses it, with no compulsory understatement in either direction.\n\nLet technique, restraint, alliances, and reputation matter through particular decisions rendered now: a display of skill, a refusal to act, a promise made under pressure, each with consequences the narration can trace into the next moment or leave suspended. A reply may close on an obligation still active, a relationship unsettled, a journey continuing — or stop inside any of them; no completed arc or closing lesson is owed to the form, and the presentation demands no contemporary setting or realist physics from the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalat::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the supplied relationship premise the organizing choice of scene and emphasis. Whatever the premise actually is — a bond across an oath's divide, teacher and student carrying an inherited duty, companions whose attachment conflicts with public expectation — select the moments that show that particular tension in action, and give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space they hold in it. Reach for no roster of familiar tropes; the premise's own promise decides which beats exist. The high fantasy world makes the relationship specific: a public duty can require separation, a magical consequence can bind or threaten, an inherited enmity can make private loyalty dangerous, and each of those costs is this story's material rather than backdrop.\n\nBuild the bond's legibility through private language and repeated moments rooted in the world's forms. A mode of address used only between them, an oath formula spoken once in jest and later in earnest, a gesture made in comfort and recognized when it is withheld — callbacks return changed, deepening what the characters have rather than repeating it. Keep the focal character's inner experience of the other person available under the active perspective, distinct from what the other knows or suspects. Give the pair room for travel, explanation, and conversation, so the relationship develops through shared time and particular decisions — who tends whom, who speaks first after silence, who yields a point — rather than through declarations alone.\n\nLet the emotional payoff fulfill the premise's specific promise as the world prices it: comfort after a costly choice, rivalry resolving into understanding, a discovery that changes what can be admitted — or the ache of a payoff withheld, if withholding is the promise in play. The relationship can remain in development at any stopping point: a private moment completed with the public question open, a confession half-made, a parting under way. No beat of affection, rupture, or resolution is owed to every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalau::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the sense of a retelling. Open with a deliberate statement of who is involved or what deed is to be related — a name with its title, an oath named before its keeper acts — then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Compact declarations establish a person or remembered act; journey, explanation, and relationship follow at the length their consequence deserves, so the chronicle keeps the room this telling needs rather than reducing action to a sequence of blows. Time moves as memory moves: years cross in a clause, while a single choice fills a passage because the teller knows what it cost.\n\nLet oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, and public deeds organize the account where the world establishes them, and let titles, relics, and ancient events change what people can do and what a decision costs. Recurrence does the chronicling: a phrase from an oath, a byname earned by a deed, a formula of greeting returning at a later and heavier application. Return to a phrase only when the new occurrence gives it fresh meaning, never as an epithet pinned to every entrance. Keep the account's sense of what will be remembered distinct from private experience — the public version is incomplete — while still writing whatever inner life the selected perspective grants; the chronicle's silence about motive is a fact of the telling, not a limit on the narration.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority. The subject of an oft-told incident corrects a detail; a character answers a formal introduction with an ordinary practical concern; plain speech complicates the elevated line without becoming parody, since household and personal matters are not mock combat and disagreement is not doom. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, whether the voice is communal or one remembered witness. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, partway through a journey or an accounting, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on anyone's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalav::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this high fantasy along the socially realist, inwardly narrated route of the Scandinavian tradition: the epic world is told from inside ordinary life under obligation. Oaths, relics, titles, institutions, and ancient events keep their power to change what people can do and what a decision costs, but they reach the page through a household, a holding, a lodged journey, a village that remembers the same events differently. The route is a discipline of attention, not a relocation: keep the supplied world's own geography, customs, and seasons, keep its regions and peoples distinct where the world distinguishes them, and do not coat the story in a generic northern temperament of snow, silence, and gloom. Reserve, weather, and isolation are available when the actual scene supplies them, never defaults.\n\nLet the cost of the mythic be domestic and countable. Feeding a garrison, mending a roof after a relic-seeker passed through, walking a border whose stones commemorate an ancient wrong, refusing shelter to a name tied to an old oath — these carry the scale. When someone acts with skill, show what they notice, what they choose not to do, and who pays for the choice; restraint is a visible economy, not an announced virtue. Alliances and reputation change what neighbors will risk for one another. Give encounters Jin Yong's generous movement: travel and explanation take real time, and a fight, when one comes, is followed by tending wounds, settling animals, and telling a family what has been promised.\n\nNarrate inwardly as well as socially. The focal character's private thought is the actual experience of weighing one loyalty — a specific person, a specific debt, a specific failure — never a summary of the political situation. In the public room, register how people address one another across rank, age, and obligation, what their hands do while they talk, and what goes unsaid where everyone shares the same history. Keep magic and antiquity concrete: a relic has weight and a provenance someone can recite; an oath has witnesses and a penalty a neighbor can name; the supernatural is met through what the body feels and what the community will afterward believe or deny.\n\nKeep the telling understated precisely because the situation is large. A death, a broken oath, or a change of allegiance can be carried by plain sentences and one exact physical detail. No compulsory formality of speech, no lecture that replaces the scene; the world's scale accumulates through the consequences people bring into ordinary rooms.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalaw::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Screenplay\n\nWrite the story as a screen-oriented script: scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines for what can be seen and heard, dialogue blocks with clear speaker cues. Let the mythic scale exist only as it becomes visible and audible — a relic unwrapped and handled, an oath spoken before witnesses, a title changing how a gatekeeper receives someone, a landscape that turns three days of travel into nine. Nothing essential stays in narration the camera could not witness.\n\nGive Jin Yong's encounters room in the action lines. Travel, waiting, explanation, and relationship are scene material, not gaps between fights. Make technique and restraint visible choices: a hand that stays off the hilt, a stance that yields ground on purpose, an object set down with deliberate care. Do not reduce a confrontation to a list of blows, and after one, show the material aftermath in the scene — a wound bound, dropped gear gathered, the household that must now live with what happened.\n\nLet dialogue carry the competing loyalties through particular decisions: a character explains an obligation, refuses a request, or asks a question that reveals what they value. Reputation and alliance decide who dares speak, who is offered shelter, who is watched. Keep lines in the speakers' own registers rather than a uniform ceremonial address, and let the world's history surface in argument and question, not in speeches that replace the scene.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, and voice-over only when the chosen sequence genuinely needs them, and keep camera commands off the page. When a focal character's private thought matters, render it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation — never as dialogue another character could hear, and never as knowledge the cast shares. A scene may end mid-exchange or on continuing action; do not manufacture a button, revelation, or arrival before the response's natural boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalax::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Stage Play\n\nWrite the story as a playable stage script: clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and business a company could perform. Let the mythic scale exist in the shared space — a relic laid on a table and guarded by whoever stands nearest it, an oath sworn before witnesses seated in view, a character refused entry because of a title, a long journey legible in how someone moves and what they carry when they arrive.\n\nLet Jin Yong's encounters move through physical business and spoken exchange. Travel is arrivals, departures, and burdens; explanation emerges from questions and partial answers rather than recitation; relationships shift through who stands close to whom, who hands an object across, who will not sit. Make technique and restraint stageable: a held stance, one deliberate step, a weapon left sheathed, an object taken without force. Do not reduce action to a sequence of blows — let the relation between a line and the body speaking it do the work.\n\nLet dialogue carry competing loyalties through particular decisions: an obligation explained, a claim challenged, a question that reveals what a speaker values. Reputation and alliance determine who is admitted, believed, and watched onstage. Keep the world's history in dispute and citation between characters, not in speeches delivered to the audience about the past.\n\nUse monologue and aside only when they serve the public scene; they do not substitute for private thought. Keep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, never as words another character can hear. No film-camera instructions — stage directions describe what is done and heard, not how it is shot. A scene may stop mid-business, an entrance still unanswered, without an obligatory revelation or curtain effect.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalay::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the story through the associative movement of one authorized mind. Braid present perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and the concerns that return unbidden. The mythic scale surfaces as what this particular character carries: the remembered weight of a relic in the hand, the cadence of an oath sworn years ago, an institution's name that still decides where they may sleep, a landscape that reappears behind closed eyes.\n\nKeep Jin Yong's generous movement inside the associative form. An encounter arrives as a chain of perceptions and recollections — a smell of the road, a half-recognized face, a phrase someone once used, the bodily decision to step forward or hold still — rather than as a linear report. Travel, explanation, and relationship live in what the mind returns to, what it avoids, and what interrupts it. Technique and restraint appear as bodily knowledge: a remembered lesson in the shoulders, a countermove felt before it is chosen, the cost of a blow to the person who threw it.\n\nLet loyalty and consequence recur as particulars, not themes. The focal character privately rehearses a promise, feels an alliance's price as a specific debt owed to a specific household, notices a reputation in how a name changes a room's temperature. These are the actual experience of carrying an obligation, and they may be interrupted, resumed, or still unresolved when the response ends.\n\nLet syntax and paragraph shape follow the associations — a long drift, a broken-off clause, a return — while keeping enough orientation that the reader can tell what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is feared or imagined. The mode does not merge minds, grant access the authorship contract withholds, or blur scene facts into indeterminacy; ordinary consecutive reasoning alone is not this style. The world's history and the present scene stay legible inside the drift.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalaz::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as an exchange carried by readable, sender-attributed messages. The epic world exists as what people choose to transmit: a title used or deliberately avoided in an address, a relic named or described in words where nothing can be attached, an oath quoted from memory to bind or to shame, an institution given as the reason someone cannot travel or must leave at once. Use only the interface events the exchange needs — timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, channel changes — and let the world's own medium set what is possible.\n\nLet Jin Yong's movement happen inside the form. Travel is a delay, a silence, a sender whose location changes between replies; explanation unfolds through questions, partial answers, and what each person includes or leaves out. Relationships shift through forms of address, a message edited before sending, a reply that arrives too late. Technique and restraint appear in what a sender reports doing, what they refuse to describe, and what another sender doubts or corroborates — not in a blow-by-blow transcript of a fight.\n\nLoyalty is enacted as decisions about what to write: a promise sent, help requested, a warning issued, a fact withheld. Reputation and alliance decide who is believed, who is messaged first, who is quietly left out of a channel. Let each person's habits govern their messages — terse, formal, over-explained, slow — and distinguish a sent message from an unsent draft, and both from the writer's live private thought; a draft is a composed artifact with its own audience of one.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when needed, clearly separated from the transcript, and interface events are not invented merely to add subtext. An exchange can rest with a message unanswered: the silence has its real duration, and the next reply need not explain or resolve it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalba::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the story in scene-oriented visual-novel presentation: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated moment. Let the mythic scale arrive through what the scene holds and what the focal character notices — a relic on a table, a title spoken with particular weight, an institution's mark on a door, a landscape in a window or returning in a pause.\n\nGive Jin Yong's encounters room across scene changes and dialogue. Travel is a new scene, an arrival with dust on the clothes, a short narrative passage that crosses distance without becoming an itinerary. Explanation comes through questions and partial answers; relationships develop through who speaks, who is addressed by name, and what the focal character reads in another person's posture and tone. Make technique and restraint physical and noticed: a held stance, a deliberate step, a hand staying flat on the table — never a mere catalog of blows.\n\nCarry loyalty and consequence through particular decisions: an obligation explained, a request refused, a question that reveals what someone values. Reputation and alliance decide who is admitted, believed, and watched. The focal narration can hold private reasoning or feeling that the other characters do not know, in whatever presentation the active contract supplies.\n\nKeep scene changes legible. Use background and expression cues as purposeful references, not as executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line. The style does not by itself activate branching menus, player choices, romance, or images; a consequence may stay unsettled, and a scene may close on a focal reaction or an unanswered question rather than a decision point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahalbb::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jin Yong / Web Serial\n\nWrite the story as an installment-oriented serial: each reply keeps a readable through-line — what is underway, what changed locally, what remains open to continue — without recapping everything. Let the mythic scale accumulate through particular developments rather than being restated: a title, relic, oath, or ancient event returns in a later installment with a new consequence, and recover only the particular needed now, not the history that first established it.\n\nLet Jin Yong's expansive movement span installments. A journey is underway across replies; a conversation pauses and resumes; an alliance shifts through a small decision whose full cost appears later. Do not compress each reply into a complete miniature chapter, and do not reduce action to a sequence of blows. Technique and restraint matter as particular choices, and progress can be knowledge, relationships, work, or resources as easily as combat.\n\nLet loyalty and consequence accrue between installments. A promise made earlier is tested under new conditions; a reputation affects who will speak or offer shelter; an institution's reach becomes visible through a local refusal or a delayed letter. Keep required private experience available when it contributes thought or feeling — a focal character may still be weighing what a promise costs when the installment ends.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks rather than artificial cliffhangers. An installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. The style does not itself create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy rules; the world's magic and obligations behave as the world already establishes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamaa::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nAnchor each scene in a specific shared routine, object, phrase, or place that predates the current hurt. When a character returns to it, do not explain the shift in general terms; show the exact gesture or exchange that has become difficult — the oath repeated with a pause where a name used to be, the relic handled without the old joke, the meal still laid out for someone who may not come back. Let the inherited obligation stay active and concrete: a magical cost, a title's duties, a broken pact, or a threatened holding shapes what the character can do and what silence costs, so the emotional strain and the world's pressure meet in one decision instead of taking turns.\n\nMake the strain do work inside the conversation itself. A character answers the practical question while avoiding the real one, offers help in a form the other cannot accept, or keeps up a careful courtesy that makes the unresolved accusation louder. When warmth appears, keep it partial or awkward: the same shared memory can comfort one character and wound the other, and humor may survive as deflection where it belongs to that person. Let the character's understanding of the shared life shift — privately, incompletely — without the scene owing anyone a resolution.\n\nUse the chosen narrator's distance and diction rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering. A close viewpoint can render the moment a familiar phrase turns painful; more distant narration registers the change through behavior and leaves the feeling implied. Private thought carries actual reasoning and hesitation, while other characters know only what they have seen and been told. Follow the scene's pressure to its own stopping point; a confession, apology, or repaired relationship is not required for the ending to work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamab::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution, so a returning participant can see at a glance who spoke and who acted. Keep each speaker's words and actions separated, and let narrative passages supply only what the active point of view can perceive. Follow the scene's established narrative person, action markers, and player-control conventions rather than imposing new ones, and add no branching menus or executable commands.\n\nKeep the high fantasy present as working detail rather than scenery: a spell that must be fed before it answers, an oath-bound object that responds only to its keeper, an order's messenger arriving with a demand, a landscape still marked by an old war. These details change what the characters can do in the current exchange.\n\nLet one detail from the scene's shared history recur as the emotional anchor — a routine greeting, a kept object, a remembered promise, a phrase one character repeats. Because of what has happened, the callback lands with changed weight; show the change through behavior or tone instead of announcing it, and let it surface where it fits the conversation rather than at every difficulty.\n\nKeep NPCs active on their own concerns. They answer from their own knowledge, pursue their practical business, and may cooperate, hesitate, or misunderstand without waiting on the player's next move. Address the submitted material within the story's established facts and perspective, then stop at a natural continuation point — a continuing exchange, an unfinished task, a private reaction — without forcing a question, cliffhanger, or choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamac::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route: a chain of linked episodes in storyteller-paced prose. Enter each episode on a concrete occasion — a summons, a rite, a debt called in, an arrival at a gate, a return — and leave it on a turn that changes someone's standing or obligation. Compress travel and passing time between episodes into brief bridges, and let each episode advance the longer chain of consequence rather than stand as an isolated set piece.\n\nKeep the high fantasy history and institutions active across the sequence. A house's oath, a relic's provenance, an order's charter, or an old campaign determines what an episode's request costs and who is bound to answer it. Give ceremony, recitation, and formal exchange the diction of the world, period, and community the story actually supplies; do not relocate the cast or impose an imported idiom on ordinary dialogue.\n\nCarry the emotional movement through recurrence across episode boundaries. One repeated object, phrase, routine, or relationship travels the chain: a title recited at a rite sounds different after a betrayal; a keepsake is offered, withheld, or handled with new hesitation; a habitual greeting quietly becomes a test of loyalty. The changed meaning arrives through action and dialogue, never through announcement. End an episode at a natural pause in its own action, with the next following from what has changed; no closing moral or cliffhanger is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamad::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful diction and compressed transitions. Cut the connective tissue between actions and let concrete detail do the work: cracked stone, a blade's worn edge, blood dried stiff in cloth, a ritual circle still smoking. Make the fantasy material and consequential — a spell burns fuel and leaves damage behind, an oath binds a hand or a name, a relic carries the marks of its use, an institution's demand arrives as a practical burden someone must carry today.\n\nAnchor the emotional movement in a physical object or routine that has survived the current trouble: a mended strap, a shared watch, a meal eaten in shifts, a tool passed between companions, a phrase spoken at the same task each time. When the anchor returns, show its changed condition or changed handling — the strap tightened instead of help asked for, a portion still laid out for someone absent, the old phrase repeated over work that has gotten harder. Let the worn detail carry the feeling; do not explain it.\n\nKeep the stakes physical: harm hurts, exhaustion slows the hands, magic that goes wrong leaves marks on the body and the place. Vary the rhythm when the character's reasoning needs a longer thought, then return to the short beat. Rough or abrasive speech belongs to its particular speaker and situation, not to the whole cast. Stop at the scene's actual pressure point; a completed task, a confession, or a change of plan is not owed to the ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamae::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition that governs what it can know. Use the specific form the scene requires — letter, journal, report, ledger, rite record, field note — rather than rotating through formats. What a document knows, guesses, and conceals follows from who wrote it, for whom, and when: a letter sent before a battle cannot report its outcome, and an official report may flatten what a private note admits.\n\nLet the world enter through the documents' actual business. A report records the cost of a working, the condition of a relic, the terms of an oath, the movement of an order's forces; a letter concerns a title, a debt, a journey, a threatened holding; a ledger shows what care or neglect has cost. Partial understanding is a feature — the writer may misread the larger event, and the record carries that limit instead of being corrected by an explaining narrator.\n\nBuild the emotional movement through recurrence across records. A repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship appears in more than one document, and the later record changes its weight: a journal calls a shared meal ordinary, and a later letter mentions the same meal with an omission or a different emphasis; a formula from official correspondence turns strained when it reaches a private note. Let gaps, revisions, crossed-out lines, seals, cramped hands, and conflicting accounts reveal what happened and what the writers could not say. Keep continuity by starting each new document from the situation the previous record left behind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamaf::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend the attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, and small pleasures inside the high fantasy world: a favorite food remembered after a long road, gear mended before its owner wakes, a drink made the way someone actually takes it, a small gift suited to the person rather than the occasion. Let the affection be sincere without owing the scene a confession, a declaration, or an emotional repair.\n\nBuild the warmth out of shared history. A habitual phrase, a kept trinket, a recurring joke, a task always done the same way returns and comforts because of what these characters have already lived through together. Show the particular pleasure it gives — the specific relief, the specific laugh — and let humor and tenderness belong to the people who actually carry them rather than to every exchange.\n\nKeep the genre's stakes awake around the ease. Comfort happens before a dangerous journey, after a costly ritual, inside the brief safety of a defended hall, and the oath, the threat, and the disagreement are still there. Care does not make everyone agreeable: a character can be worried, stubborn, or private while accepting help, and manageable friction between people who like each other is part of the warmth. End at a natural moment of ease or continuing activity; no arc needs completing, and nothing must promise that everything will be all right.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamag::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let a paragraph travel from a particular gesture or exchange into the frame around it — the obligations of a title, the memory of a pact, an institution's expectations, a landscape still altered by old magic — and back to the people in the room, wherever the chosen narrator's distance permits. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language without forcing the whole cast into archaic or uniformly elaborate speech; heightened formality belongs to ceremony, correspondence, and public occasion, while private talk can stay plainer.\n\nCarry the emotional movement through recurrence within the scene and across the novel's progression. A repeated object, routine, phrase, or relationship returns, and the narration shows its changed meaning through treatment rather than summary: the familiar phrase spoken with new hesitation, the household routine continuing after its purpose has gone, the keepsake handled differently since the loss. Let the paragraph's movement from concrete detail into social implication carry the change.\n\nKeep the stakes materially present. Titles, relics, oaths, institutions, and remembered events change what characters can do and what a decision costs, and the narration ties those large forces to particular loyalties and private feeling. Ceremony, correspondence, and the contrast between public performance and private thought may organize a passage when the material offers them, without replacing the scene with exposition. End where the narrative pressure actually subsides; a settled judgment, a repaired relationship, or a resolved public question is not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamah::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen in the outer scene at the moment a telling becomes necessary: a child asking why a boundary is kept, a companion hesitating before renewing an oath, a councilor explaining why a relic is carried but never drawn. Give the teller a practical purpose — persuading, comforting, warning, buying time — and give the listener something they want from the tale, so the frame stays a live exchange instead of a curtain-raiser for exposition.\n\nLet the embedded tale carry the world's active history: an inherited obligation, a magical object, an institution, an ancient event whose cost is still being paid in the room where the story is told. Keep the teller's hand visible in the telling — what is emphasized, what is softened, what is repeated because a predecessor repeated it, what the teller cannot yet explain. Signal each move between frame and tale through address, tense, or an aside the teller makes to the listener, and let the listener interrupt, drift, or press a question, so the levels stay intelligible without labels.\n\nBind the levels with a repeated object, phrase, or gesture drawn from shared daily life — the cup filled before anyone asks, a farewell said twice, a lamp lit at the same hour. Inside the tale the repetition is a habit; when it recurs in the outer scene after the telling, it should read differently to the listener, as loyalty or debt or something chosen rather than merely inherited. Render that shift as the listener's private experience while it happens, not as a conclusion they announce.\n\nLet the telling alter the present without deciding it. A listener may understand why a promise was made and still choose otherwise; a companion may recognize a shared habit as love or as burden. Return to a concrete outer moment — a hand on the relic, a name spoken, the oath half-formed — and show what the listener now notices about the person, object, or obligation before them. The tale need not settle the choice, produce a confession, or break off on a cliffhanger; its work is the changed understanding the listener carries into the next act.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamai::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the Romantic and uncanny route: the fantastic enters not as an announced event but as something familiar turning strange. Begin with one person inside an ordinary attachment — a household routine, a path taken daily, an object handled so often it is barely seen. Let the high-fantasy world press on that attachment until its older claim becomes legible: a landmark never questioned reveals the boundary it marks, a childhood gesture turns out to preserve an oath, a relic kept for comfort discloses a history with terms of its own. Keep the magic and history concrete — a working enchantment, a named compact, an office with duties — rather than a wash of atmosphere.\n\nEstablish the shared life before reinterpreting it. Show the repeated habit with the affection or weariness it actually carries: two cups always set out, the song hummed at the same chore, the phrase said when someone leaves. When the uncanny returns, move through that specificity — the character realizes the gesture taught to them was also a form of service, that the field where they played was shaped by an ancient agreement, that the person who taught them knew more than they ever said. Do not deliver the whole history in one disclosure; let understanding arrive in stages, carried by sensory and emotional detail, so the character feels the weight of the past before they can state its terms.\n\nLet sentence shape follow the movement of recognition. When the familiar becomes strange, allow longer, rhythmically shaped sentences that hold the double view of then and now; when the character returns to practical consequence, shorten toward plain statement. Write the recognition as lived experience — attention snagging, memory re-forming, the body's unease — rather than a lecture about the world's lore. End with the character still inside the changed understanding: the obligation now visible, its cost not yet settled, the affection not erased by knowing what it serves.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPahamaj::♢ * [STYLE] High Fantasy / Jun Maeda / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the encounter where an inherited high-fantasy authority touches a life being lived now. Make that power material and maintained rather than vaguely ancient: the emblem worn thin on a servant's collar, the garden kept locked behind the council hall, the way a household modulates its speech when a representative's boots sound in the corridor, the ledger recording what each family owes the order. Let atmosphere sharpen one particular meeting — a summons, an inspection, a favor asked over dinner — instead of blanketing every scene in gloom.\n\nCarry the political cost through shared routine. A repeated errand to the chapterhouse, a meal laid the same way each week, a private phrase between two people who serve the same master: let one of these reveal what the oath or institution has done to a relationship. Show the masks worn in public — the correct title, the composed face, the answer that protects someone — and let them loosen in trusted company, where a character may say the resentful or frightened true thing, or find that even there some subjects stay closed. Keep intimacy morally complicated: someone can love a person whose authority they also fear, keep faith with an institution that has used their family, and be tender and complicit in the same hour. Not every authority figure is corrupt, and not every closeness is a trap.\n\nHold private desire against practical obligation. A character can want safety, recognition, or a particular person while knowing what that want costs under the present order, and the body can register the negotiation: the cold of the stone corridor, the incense smell in a robe they are expected to kiss, the ceremonial key's weight against the chest. Write the wanting and the fear as felt experience, not stated position. End the response while the character is still inside that tension — the representative waiting for an answer, the errand half-done, the confidence just spoken — without requiring a rebellion, a confession, or a completed escape. The old order stays concrete and consequential, and its personal price stays the point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
